[#:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  The futon stack is infrastructure for knowledge work that persists, compounds,\n  and transfers—turning ephemeral problem-solving into durable, reusable artefacts."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Most knowledge work disappears. You solve a problem, write some code,\n    have an insight—and then it's gone, locked in your head or scattered across\n    emails and notebooks."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    you want knowledge work to persist and compound rather than evaporate"} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    current tools treat each session as ephemeral; insights are not captured,\n    patterns are not named, and reasoning is not checkable"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    build layered infrastructure where everything is addressable, agents have\n    principled perception-action loops, reasoning is checkable, and patterns\n    transfer across domains"} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    compounding requires persistence, and transfer requires abstraction—the\n    stack provides both"} {:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  FUTON1 provides graph memory where everything—text fragments, relationships,\n  annotations—lives as addressable nodes that can be queried and linked."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "you need a foundation for persistent knowledge"} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "scattered files and databases do not support linking and querying"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "store everything as graph nodes with typed edges"} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "addressable nodes are the prerequisite for all higher layers"} {:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  FUTON2 provides an active inference engine that gives agents a principled way\n  to observe their environment, perceive what matters, and act—this same engine\n  powers both the reasoning agents in futon3 and the cellular automata\n  experiments in futon5."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "agents need to act in changing environments"} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "ad-hoc agent loops lack principled grounding"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "implement observe-perceive-affect-policy-act loops with free energy minimization"} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "active inference provides a unified framework for perception and action"} {:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  FUTON3 handles the messy reality of human reasoning: arguments that are not\n  formal proofs but still need to be checked, patterns that recur across problems,\n  and the gap between how people actually think and what machines can verify."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "real reasoning is informal but still needs validation"} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "formal proof systems reject most human arguments"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "build flexiformal pattern checking that validates structure without requiring full formalization"} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "checked informal arguments are more valuable than unchecked ones"} {:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  FUTON4 provides the editing environment where documents, patterns, and\n  annotations share a common workspace—Arxana links fragments and scholia so you\n  can see how ideas connect and evolve."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "knowledge workers need to edit and annotate linked content"} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "traditional editors treat documents as isolated files"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "build a hypertext editor where fragments, links, and scholia coexist"} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "connected editing reveals structure that flat files hide"} {:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  FUTON5 collects design patterns for improvisation—not fixed procedures but\n  flexible moves that transfer across domains. Wiring diagrams give these patterns\n  a category-theoretic interpretation, and a transfer milestone proves that\n  patterns developed in one domain (cellular automata) work in another (ant\n  simulations)."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "you want reusable patterns for improvisation"} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "informal patterns lack compositional semantics"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "represent patterns as wiring diagrams with CT interpretation; prove transfer across domains"} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "compositional patterns can be developed once and applied many times"} {:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  FUTON6 builds a mathematics dictionary where informal reasoning patterns sit\n  alongside formal proofs, making mathematical knowledge navigable rather than\n  locked in textbooks."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "mathematical knowledge should be navigable and reusable"} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "textbooks are linear and proofs are opaque"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "build a dictionary with informal arguments, cross-domain patterns, and domain-specific constructs"} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "named patterns at multiple levels make mathematics teachable and transferable"} {:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  FUTON7 applies these ideas to economic life. Hyperreal Enterprises operates as\n  a seed institution testing transparent governance. Gravpad does to the web what\n  street art does to cities—adding commentary and accountability information\n  without requiring permission from those being made transparent. Scenario\n  simulations let you prototype civic and economic structures before committing\n  to them."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "economic and civic structures need transparency"} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "current systems obscure costs, relationships, and externalities"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "build transparency infrastructure that does not require permission from those being made transparent"} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "accountability should not depend on voluntary disclosure"} {:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  The layers connect through shared infrastructure: graph memory stores everything,\n  active inference provides the perception-action loop, pattern checking validates\n  reasoning, and wiring diagrams transfer between domains."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Cross-futon bridges make the stack cohere."} {:name "BRIDGES", :name-key "bridges", :slug "bridges", :text "    f1 ──graph memory──→ f4 (Arxana stores documents as graph nodes)\n    f2 ──AIF engine──→ f3 (fulab agents use observe-perceive-act loop)\n    f2 ──AIF engine──→ f5 (MetaCA uses same infrastructure)\n    f3 ──checked arguments──→ f6 (informal reasoning imports to dictionary)\n    f5 ──wiring diagrams──→ f2 (transfer from MetaCA to ant simulation)\n    f6 ──mathematical models──→ f7 (scenario simulations use growth models)"} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "layers must interoperate"} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "isolated layers become silos"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "define explicit bridges with shared infrastructure"} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "coherence requires connection"} {:name "EVIDENCE", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "  evidence[Seven futons with defined devmaps in holes/]\n  evidence[Shared infrastructure: graph memory (f1), AIF engine (f2), MUSN transport (f3)]\n  evidence[Transfer milestone demonstrates cross-domain pattern reuse (f5/P4)]\n  evidence[Hyperreal operates as living prototype (f7/P0)]"} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "  next[Validate integration points with end-to-end tests]\n  next[Document more transfer examples beyond MetaCA→ants]\n  next[Test stack with real knowledge workers outside the project]\n  next[Write tutorials for each layer]\n\n:success-criteria\n  pass[Knowledge persists across sessions]\n  pass[Patterns transfer between domains]\n  pass[Stack usable by people outside the project]\n  fail[Layers remain isolated silos]\n\n:psr-example \"Selected layered architecture because compounding requires persistence and transfer requires abstraction\"\n:pur-template \"Stack session, layers touched: {{layers}}, bridges crossed: {{bridges}}, artifacts persisted: {{count}}\""}], :conclusion "  The futon stack is infrastructure for knowledge work that persists, compounds,\n  and transfers—turning ephemeral problem-solving into durable, reusable artefacts.", :directives {:audience "outsiders, collaborators, future-you", :factor "Keen investigation (dhammavicaya)", :references ["devmap-coherence/prototype-structure-checklist" "stack-coherence/futon-bridge-health"], :sigils ["👓/本" "💬/工"], :title "The FUTON Stack — An Argument for Persistent Knowledge Infrastructure", :tone "formal-analytic"}, :id "futon-stack/argument", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["devmap-coherence/prototype-structure-checklist" "stack-coherence/futon-bridge-health"], :scores {}, :sigils ["👓/本" "💬/工"], :source-path "holes/futon-stack.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "The FUTON Stack — An Argument for Persistent Knowledge Infrastructure", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Every entry in an Agency state atom must have a defined lifecycle with explicit creation, bounded retention, and guaranteed cleanup; fire-and-forget futures are not cleanup.  A5-bounded: Every entry in every Agency state atom has a defined maximum lifetime. No atom may grow without bound during normal operation. Cleanup is deterministic: either lazy-on-read, periodic-sweep, or cascaded-on-parent-removal."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Agency maintains six state atoms: secrets, acks, rendezvous-state, connected-agents, local-handlers, and pending-whistles. Most use future-based TTL cleanup that races with concurrent operations. The acks atom has no cleanup at all and grows without bound."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    You store transient state (secrets, promises, connection entries, rendezvous tracking) in an in-memory atom."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Future-based cleanup (future (Thread/sleep ttl) (swap! atom dissoc key)) races with concurrent reads and writes. Between the sleep ending and the swap! executing, a concurrent operation may read the entry, begin work, and then find the entry gone. The acks atom is never cleaned. pending-whistles entries can be orphaned if the agent disconnects mid-flight."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "      - Unbounded growth: acks atom accumulates 10k entries/day with no cleanup\n      - Race cleanup: secret TTL future fires during concurrent ack validation; ack fails with \"unknown secret\"\n      - Orphaned promise: agent disconnects; pending-whistle entry sits until timeout future fires (or forever if future was GC'd)\n      - Late ack: rendezvous cleaned up; agent sends ack after cleanup; ack recorded in acks atom but not in rendezvous"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Every atom entry must have:\n    1. Created-at timestamp: when the entry was created.\n    2. TTL or expiry: maximum lifetime, after which the entry is invalid.\n    3. Cleanup mechanism: either (a) lazy cleanup on read (check TTL before returning), or (b) periodic sweep (scheduled task that removes expired entries), or (c) cascaded cleanup (when parent resource is removed, dependents are removed).\n    4. Bounded size: maximum number of entries, with eviction policy for overflow.\n    5. No fire-and-forget futures for cleanup: cleanup must be deterministic, not scheduled in a separate thread that races with operations.\n    Specifically:\n    - acks: clean up when the corresponding secret expires.\n    - pending-whistles: clean up on agent disconnect cascade, not just on timeout.\n    - rendezvous-state: clean up when all expected agents have acked or deadline passes, not deadline + arbitrary delay.\n    - connected-agents: clean up triggers cascade to pending-whistles and rendezvous-state."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Unbounded state atoms are memory leaks. Race-prone cleanup creates intermittent failures that are impossible to reproduce. A database table without a retention policy eventually fills the disk; an atom without cleanup eventually fills the heap."} {:name "EVIDENCE (repo history)", :name-key "evidence (repo history)", :slug "evidence-repo-history", :text "      - 2026-02-01 9ea1b06: secret storage endpoints introduced with TTL-driven cleanup, establishing the \"fire-and-forget future\" cleanup pattern for transient state.\n      - 2026-02-08 b14c9de: pending-whistles introduced as an atom with timeout cleanup via future (sleep + dissoc), creating an explicit orphan/timeout lifecycle that can race with in-flight operations.\n      - 2026-02-08 9acf154: rendezvous-state introduced alongside acks recording; the design makes acks meaningful, but also increases the need for deterministic cleanup (acks, rendezvous, and pending requests must not leak or race)."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - evidence: measure atom sizes under sustained load (entries per hour per atom)\n    - evidence: count orphaned entries (entries past TTL still in atom)\n    - evidence: reproduce race between TTL cleanup future and concurrent operation\n    - evidence-shape: {:atom/name :keyword, :entry/count :int, :entry/oldest-ms :int, :entry/ttl-ms :int, :cleanup/mechanism :keyword, :cleanup/race-window-ms :int, :orphan/count :int}"}], :conclusion "Every entry in an Agency state atom must have a defined lifecycle with explicit creation, bounded retention, and guaranteed cleanup; fire-and-forget futures are not cleanup.  A5-bounded: Every entry in every Agency state atom has a defined maximum lifetime. No atom may grow without bound during normal operation. Cleanup is deterministic: either lazy-on-read, periodic-sweep, or cascaded-on-parent-removal.", :directives {:audience "agency maintainers, futon developers", :keywords ["atom" "state" "cleanup" "leak" "ttl" "lifecycle" "resource"], :references ["realtime/listener-leases" "realtime/liveness-heartbeats"], :sigils ["⏳/终"], :style "pattern", :title "Every Resource Has a Bounded Lifecycle", :tone "technical"}, :id "agency/bounded-lifecycle", :keywords ["atom" "state" "cleanup" "leak" "ttl" "lifecycle" "resource"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["realtime/listener-leases" "realtime/liveness-heartbeats"], :scores {}, :sigils ["⏳/终"], :source-path "library/agency/bounded-lifecycle.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Every Resource Has a Bounded Lifecycle", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Every message sent through Agency must produce either a delivery receipt or an explicit failure; silent send-and-hope is not acceptable.  A0-delivery: No message send operation returns success unless the recipient has acknowledged receipt or the send has been confirmed at the transport layer. Failure to deliver produces an explicit error with agent-id, transport, and reason."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Agency routes messages (bells, whistles, pages) to agents via three tiers: registry, local handlers, and WebSocket. Callers currently receive a boolean or a best-effort result with no delivery guarantee."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    You send a message to an agent and need to know whether it arrived, was processed, or failed."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    send-to-agent! returns false on failure but callers often ignore it; bell to \"all\" returns {:ok true} even if zero agents received it; MUSN posts use throw-exceptions false and never check HTTP status; ack HTTP calls don't verify response codes. The system treats \"sent\" as \"delivered\" and \"no error\" as \"success.\""} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "      - Silent drop: message sent over WS but agent disconnected between send and receive\n      - False success: HTTP post returns 503 but caller sees no error because exceptions are suppressed\n      - Orphaned promise: whistle promise created but agent crashes before responding; promise leaks"} {:name "ANTI-PATTERNS", :name-key "anti-patterns", :slug "anti-patterns", :text "      - {:throw-exceptions false} without status check\n      - (catch Exception _ nil) in send paths\n      - Returning boolean from send operations (true/false is not enough context)"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Require a delivery receipt for every message type:\n    1. Bell: secret-based ack proves reception. If no ack within deadline, emit explicit failure event with agent-id and reason.\n    2. Whistle: response proves delivery. Timeout produces explicit {:ok false :err \"timeout\" :agent-id aid} with no silent fallthrough.\n    3. HTTP posts (MUSN, forum): check response status. Non-2xx is a failure, not silence.\n    4. send-to-agent! must return a result type, not a boolean. Callers must handle the failure path.\n    The delivery receipt is the analogue of a database write acknowledgment: the operation is not complete until the receipt is in hand."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Without delivery receipts, the system confuses \"sent\" with \"delivered\" and \"no exception\" with \"success.\" This makes failures invisible and debugging impossible. A telephone system without ring/busy/unreachable signals is not a telephone system."} {:name "EVIDENCE (repo history)", :name-key "evidence (repo history)", :slug "evidence-repo-history", :text "      - 2026-02-01 9ea1b06: \"test-bell-v2 with secret verification\" adds a closed-loop receipt mechanism (secret retrieval + verification) explicitly to prove message reception rather than assuming it.\n      - 2026-02-03 ad6bea1: \"bell ack flow\" extends the closed-loop idea into a broader bell/ack workflow, reinforcing that \"sent\" needed to become \"verified\".\n      - 2026-02-08 b14c9de: rename page -> whistle to clarify the sync request/response contract and introduce an explicit timeout knob (AGENCY_WHISTLE_TIMEOUT_MS), pushing toward explicit failure over silent waiting.\n      - 2026-02-08 9acf154: standup redesign notes \"no reception guarantee\" as a defect of the prior approach, and replaces it with a rendezvous + ack handshake."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - evidence: audit every call to send-to-agent! and classify caller handling of false return\n    - evidence: audit every HTTP post with :throw-exceptions false and verify status checks\n    - evidence: measure delivery failure rate (bells sent vs acks received) per transport\n    - evidence: track orphaned promises in pending-whistles atom over time\n    - evidence-shape: {:message/id :string, :message/type :keyword, :agent/id :string, :transport :keyword, :sent/ts :string, :receipt/ts :string, :receipt/type :keyword, :failure/reason :string, :failure/ts :string, :latency-ms :int}"}], :conclusion "Every message sent through Agency must produce either a delivery receipt or an explicit failure; silent send-and-hope is not acceptable.  A0-delivery: No message send operation returns success unless the recipient has acknowledged receipt or the send has been confirmed at the transport layer. Failure to deliver produces an explicit error with agent-id, transport, and reason.", :directives {:audience "agency maintainers, futon developers", :keywords ["delivery" "receipt" "ack" "bell" "whistle" "message" "guarantee"], :references ["realtime/rendezvous-handshake" "storage/durability-first"], :sigils ["📬/送"], :style "pattern", :title "Every Message Gets a Receipt or an Explicit Failure", :tone "analytic-foundational"}, :id "agency/delivery-receipt", :keywords ["delivery" "receipt" "ack" "bell" "whistle" "message" "guarantee"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["realtime/rendezvous-handshake" "storage/durability-first"], :scores {}, :sigils ["📬/送"], :source-path "library/agency/delivery-receipt.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Every Message Gets a Receipt or an Explicit Failure", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Never overload identifiers across layers; transport IDs (forum thread, MUSN session, WS connection) must not be stored or used as LLM resume/session IDs, and LLM session IDs must not be treated as transport addresses."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Futon3 passes many identifiers through Agency and peripherals: agent-id, forum thread-id, MUSN session-id, WebSocket session-id, LLM resume/thread id, bell secret-id, whistle request-id. Early Agency code used the same key (thread-id) for different meanings depending on call site."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    You add a new endpoint, peripheral, or bridge that must preserve continuity across tools and transports."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Overloaded IDs create \"accidental continuity\" (resuming the wrong thing) and \"accidental routing\" (sending to the wrong destination). Silent corruption follows: state appears valid but refers to the wrong layer. Debugging is hard because logs show an ID but not its type."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "      - Resume-id clobbered: forum thread-id overwrites LLM resume id, causing \"resume\" to start a new LLM context every time.\n      - Ghost continuity: a WS session-id is treated as an LLM session and stored, creating persistent but meaningless state.\n      - Debugging dead-end: logs show \"thread-id=...\" but you cannot tell whether it is a forum thread, an LLM thread, or a MUSN session."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    1. Define and enforce a typed identifier vocabulary:\n       - transport: forum-thread-id, musn-session-id, ws-connection-id\n       - LLM continuity: resume-id (Codex/Claude thread), llm-session-id\n       - protocol: secret-id, request-id, rendezvous-id\n    2. Store only LLM continuity identifiers in continuity state (e.g., :agent/current-thread-id).\n    3. Accept transport IDs only inside transport-specific payloads (e.g., under :forum or :musn), never as top-level \"thread-id\" that can be misread.\n    4. Log IDs with their type (key name) and the layer emitting them (transport/identity/invocation), so traces can be interpreted without guesswork."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Identity in a distributed system is a graph of references; if edges are mislabeled, the system becomes internally consistent but externally wrong. This is an Identifier Garden problem: without stewardship, identifiers drift and get repurposed, breaking provenance and continuity."} {:name "EVIDENCE (repo history)", :name-key "evidence (repo history)", :slug "evidence-repo-history", :text "      - 2026-01-31 466bc73 and 2026-01-31 b2fbb84: mission documents the concrete break: forum thread ids were used as LLM resume ids.\n      - 2026-01-31 17c87b7: introduces :resume-id as distinct from forum thread-id in the payload and state update path, an explicit fix for identifier overloading.\n      - 2026-01-29 0879d1f: peripheral spec draft makes \"session-id transfer\" a first-class hop mechanic, increasing the cost of ID confusion."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - evidence: audit Agency endpoints for the presence of ambiguous keys like thread-id used in multiple meanings; target is zero ambiguous keys.\n    - evidence: add a property test that generates payloads with forum-thread-id and resume-id and asserts they never swap or overwrite one another.\n    - evidence-shape: {:id/type :keyword, :id/value :string, :layer :keyword, :operation :string, :agent/id :string, :ts :string}"}], :conclusion "Never overload identifiers across layers; transport IDs (forum thread, MUSN session, WS connection) must not be stored or used as LLM resume/session IDs, and LLM session IDs must not be treated as transport addresses.", :directives {:audience "agency maintainers, futon developers", :keywords ["identity" "identifier" "thread-id" "resume-id" "session-id" "routing" "transport" "forum" "musn"], :references ["p4ng/identifier-garden" "futon-theory/agent-contract" "agency/state-atomicity" "agency/single-routing-authority"], :sigils ["🏷/别"], :style "pattern", :title "Keep Transport Identifiers Separate From LLM Session Identifiers", :tone "analytic-foundational"}, :id "agency/identifier-separation", :keywords ["identity" "identifier" "thread-id" "resume-id" "session-id" "routing" "transport" "forum" "musn"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["p4ng/identifier-garden" "futon-theory/agent-contract" "agency/state-atomicity" "agency/single-routing-authority"], :scores {}, :sigils ["🏷/别"], :source-path "library/agency/identifier-separation.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Keep Transport Identifiers Separate From LLM Session Identifiers", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Agency must uphold six invariants — delivery, identity, atomicity, error hierarchy, debugging, and bounded resources — analogous to the I0-I4 framework in futon1a; every feature built on Agency inherits these guarantees or is built on sand."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Agency is the messaging and coordination backbone for all futon agents (human and AI). It routes bells (async), whistles (sync), manages agent registration, and bridges to IRC/MUSN/forum. An audit revealed: 20+ silent error catches, three independent routing tiers with no consistency, unbounded state atoms, no delivery guarantees, and no specification. Features built on Agency (standup, PAR sessions, pair programming) inherit all of these deficiencies."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Operate an explicit move on the named axis (see substructure below); steward the pattern against the declared discipline."} {:name "INVARIANT-SET", :name-key "invariant-set", :slug "invariant-set", :text "      A0-delivery (agency/delivery-receipt):\n        Every message send operation returns either a delivery receipt or an explicit failure.\n        No send may return success without confirmation from the transport layer.\n        Proof: for every bell sent, an ack arrives or a timeout error is emitted.\n\n      A1-identity (agency/single-routing-authority, agency/self-attribution):\n        For any agent-id, exactly one routing entry exists at any time.\n        Agents speak for themselves; the server never posts under an agent's identity.\n        Proof: at no point do two tiers claim authority for the same agent-id.\n        Support: agency/identifier-separation (typed IDs prevent routing/identity drift across layers).\n\n      A2-atomicity (agency/state-atomicity):\n        Every state transition completes fully or reverts with an explicit error.\n        No transition may leave state partially updated.\n        Proof: rollover never produces a nil session-id; corrupted state files are rejected, not silently defaulted.\n        Support: agency/identifier-separation (continuity state stores LLM session IDs only; transport IDs cannot clobber them).\n\n      A3-hierarchy (agency/loud-failure):\n        Every error surfaces at the layer that caused it with agent-id, operation, and cause.\n        No exception handler may discard an error without propagation or explicit recovery.\n        Proof: zero instances of (catch Exception _ nil) in the codebase.\n\n      A4-debugging:\n        Any failure in Agency is diagnosable from logs within 10 minutes.\n        Every error includes enough context (layer, agent-id, operation, timestamp, cause) for diagnosis.\n        Proof: given a failure scenario, an operator can trace the cause using only Agency logs.\n\n      A5-bounded (agency/bounded-lifecycle):\n        Every entry in every state atom has a maximum lifetime.\n        No atom grows without bound during normal operation.\n        Proof: atom sizes remain stable under sustained load; no orphaned entries accumulate."} {:name "TRACEABILITY", :name-key "traceability", :slug "traceability", :text "      Each invariant traces to a pattern (abstract) and must trace to:\n      - Implementation: specific function(s) that enforce it\n      - Test: specific test(s) that prove it\n      - Evidence: specific metric(s) that monitor it\n\n      Supporting patterns (non-invariants) that reduce common failure modes:\n      - agency/identifier-separation supports A1 (identity) and A2 (atomicity) by preventing identifier overloading across layers.\n\n      Current traceability (to be filled as implementation proceeds):\n\n      | Invariant | Pattern | Implementation | Test | Evidence |\n      | A0 | agency/delivery-receipt | src/futon3/agency/http.clj: send-to-agent!, handle-ring-bell, handle-whistle | test/futon3/agency/invariants/a0_delivery_test.clj | TBD |\n      | A1 | agency/single-routing-authority | src/futon3/agency/http.clj: enforce-single-routing-authority! (watches over registry/local/ws stores) | test/futon3/agency/invariants/a1_identity_test.clj | TBD |\n      | A2 | agency/state-atomicity | src/futon3/agency/service.clj: load-agent-state, save-agent-state!, update-agent-state!, roll-over-state! | test/futon3/agency/invariants/a2_atomicity_test.clj | TBD |\n      | A3 | agency/loud-failure | src/futon3/agency/service.clj + src/futon3/agency/http.clj: status checks on :throw-exceptions false + elimination of (catch Exception _ nil) sites | test/futon3/agency/invariants/a3_loud_failure_test.clj | TBD |\n      | A4 | (derived from A3) | (derived) A3 enforcement + log context conventions in http/service layers | test/futon3/agency/invariants/a3_loud_failure_test.clj | TBD |\n      | A5 | agency/bounded-lifecycle | src/futon3/agency/http.clj: prune-acks/handle-ack; handle-get-secret TTL; kick-agent!/ws on-close cascades; secret cleanup cascades | test/futon3/agency/invariants/a5_bounded_test.clj | TBD |"} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Agency is not different from a database just because it uses WebSockets instead of disk. A message that might arrive is not a message. A session-id that might be correct is not an identity. An error that might be logged is not an error. The invariants make these guarantees concrete and testable."} {:name "EVIDENCE (repo history, why this spec exists)", :name-key "evidence (repo history, why this spec exists)", :slug "evidence-repo-history-why-this-spec-exists", :text "      - 2026-01-29 0879d1f and 2026-02-01 9a0f28d: peripheral specs and contract notes push toward routable, machine-readable invocation with explicit I/O and session continuity, which forces Agency to get identity/delivery/atomicity right.\n      - 2026-01-31 466bc73 and 2026-01-31 b2fbb84: mission and invariants for session continuity document real failures: identifier conflation (forum thread-id vs LLM resume id) and rollover semantics that erase session identity.\n      - 2026-02-07 fac9efb: \"memory bifurcation\" brief shows the split-brain failure mode when routing says \"connected\" but invocation says \"no invoke-fn registered\".\n      - 2026-02-03 ad6bea1 and 2026-02-01 9ea1b06: bell/secret verification work is direct evidence that \"sent\" needed to become \"receipt-proven\".\n      - 2026-02-08 169c4bc then 2026-02-08 9acf154: standup evolves from proxy collection to rendezvous + self-reporting, surfacing the identity and delivery invariants as practical necessities, not theory."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Fill traceability table as each invariant is implemented and tested.\n    - Write proof tests for each invariant in test/futon3/agency/invariants/.\n    - Add A0-A5 health checks to Agency startup (analogous to futon1a startup integrity gate).\n    - Monitor invariant violations in production via evidence-shape metrics.\n    - evidence-shape: {:invariant/id :keyword, :invariant/status :keyword, :proof/test-path :string, :proof/last-run :string, :proof/passed? :boolean, :implementation/files [:string], :violation/count :int, :violation/last :string}"}], :conclusion "Agency must uphold six invariants — delivery, identity, atomicity, error hierarchy, debugging, and bounded resources — analogous to the I0-I4 framework in futon1a; every feature built on Agency inherits these guarantees or is built on sand.", :directives {:audience "agency maintainers, futon developers, pattern reviewers", :keywords ["invariant" "specification" "agency" "guarantee" "contract"], :references ["futon-theory/agent-contract" "futon-theory/error-hierarchy" "storage/durability-first" "realtime/rendezvous-handshake" "realtime/liveness-heartbeats" "realtime/listener-leases" "p4ng/identifier-garden"], :sigils ["🏛/律"], :style "pattern", :title "Agency Invariant Set", :tone "formal-analytic"}, :id "agency/invariants", :keywords ["invariant" "specification" "agency" "guarantee" "contract"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["futon-theory/agent-contract" "futon-theory/error-hierarchy" "storage/durability-first" "realtime/rendezvous-handshake" "realtime/liveness-heartbeats" "realtime/listener-leases" "p4ng/identifier-garden"], :scores {}, :sigils ["🏛/律"], :source-path "library/agency/invariants.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Agency Invariant Set", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Every error in Agency must surface at the layer that caused it with enough context to diagnose; silent catch-and-swallow is never acceptable.  A3-hierarchy: No exception handler in Agency may discard an error without either (a) returning an error result to the caller with layer, agent-id, and cause, or (b) executing an explicit recovery action that is logged with the original error context."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Agency code contains 20+ instances of (catch Exception _ nil) or (catch Exception e (log! ...) false) across service.clj, invokes.clj, and http.clj. Corrupted state files load as defaults without warning. Subprocess parsing errors are skipped. Forum and MUSN post failures are swallowed. The log function itself swallows its own errors."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    An operation fails — network error, parse error, subprocess crash, file corruption, timeout."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    The current pattern catches exceptions at every boundary and converts them to nil, false, or logged-but-forgotten entries. Callers cannot distinguish \"succeeded with empty result\" from \"failed silently.\" Operators cannot diagnose issues because the error was absorbed three layers below."} {:name "ANTI-PATTERNS", :name-key "anti-patterns", :slug "anti-patterns", :text "      - (catch Exception _ nil) — the canonical silent swallow\n      - (catch Exception e (log! \"error\" {:err (.getMessage e)}) false) — logged but not propagated\n      - {:throw-exceptions false} without (when-not (= 200 (:status resp)) ...) — HTTP error suppression"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Apply the error hierarchy from futon-theory/error-hierarchy to Agency:\n    1. Layer 0 (transport/durability): HTTP, WS, and file I/O errors surface as transport failures with status codes.\n    2. Layer 1 (identity): Agent not found, session-id mismatch surface as resolution failures.\n    3. Layer 2 (integrity): State corruption, partial rollover surface as integrity failures.\n    4. Layer 3 (invocation): Subprocess timeout, parse error, empty result surface as invocation failures.\n    5. Layer 4 (validation): Missing fields, bad request surface as validation failures.\n    Every catch block must either:\n    a. Propagate the error with context (layer, agent-id, operation, cause), OR\n    b. Handle it with an explicit recovery action that is itself logged.\n    Catch-and-ignore is removed from the codebase entirely."} {:name "ENFORCEMENT", :name-key "enforcement", :slug "enforcement", :text "      - grep for (catch Exception _) and (catch Throwable _) — zero allowed\n      - grep for {:throw-exceptions false} — each must be followed by status check\n      - grep for (log! ... false) — log-and-return-false is not error handling"} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Silent errors make debugging impossible. When a standup fails and no agent responds, the operator has no trail: was the bell not sent? Was it sent but not received? Was it received but the agent crashed? Was the response generated but MUSN was down? Without layer-attributed errors, every failure investigation starts from zero."} {:name "EVIDENCE (repo history)", :name-key "evidence (repo history)", :slug "evidence-repo-history", :text "      - 2026-01-31 466bc73 and 2026-01-31 b2fbb84: the \"Make Agency Work Properly\" mission is, in effect, a loud-failure postmortem: multiple user-visible failures were traced back to swallowed/blurred errors and identifier confusion that did not surface at the right layer.\n      - 2026-02-08 9acf154: standup redesign calls out the prior approach as lacking a reception guarantee; that is a loud-failure requirement (explicit failure when reception is not proven), not just a protocol preference.\n      - current src/futon3/agency/service.clj: load-agent-state catches EDN parse errors and returns nil, which collapses integrity failures into \"empty state\" with no operator-visible signal."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - evidence: count all silent catch blocks in agency/ directory; target is zero\n    - evidence: classify each catch block by layer (transport, identity, integrity, invocation, validation)\n    - evidence: measure time-to-diagnosis for a sample failure before and after fix\n    - evidence-shape: {:error/layer :keyword, :error/operation :string, :error/agent-id :string, :error/cause :string, :error/recovery :keyword, :error/propagated? :boolean, :error/ts :string}"}], :conclusion "Every error in Agency must surface at the layer that caused it with enough context to diagnose; silent catch-and-swallow is never acceptable.  A3-hierarchy: No exception handler in Agency may discard an error without either (a) returning an error result to the caller with layer, agent-id, and cause, or (b) executing an explicit recovery action that is logged with the original error context.", :directives {:audience "agency maintainers, futon developers", :keywords ["error" "failure" "exception" "silent" "propagation" "diagnostic"], :references ["futon-theory/error-hierarchy" "storage/error-layer-hierarchy"], :sigils ["📢/声"], :style "pattern", :title "Errors Surface at the Layer That Caused Them", :tone "analytic-foundational"}, :id "agency/loud-failure", :keywords ["error" "failure" "exception" "silent" "propagation" "diagnostic"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["futon-theory/error-hierarchy" "storage/error-layer-hierarchy"], :scores {}, :sigils ["📢/声"], :source-path "library/agency/loud-failure.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Errors Surface at the Layer That Caused Them", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "An agent's output must be posted by the agent itself under its own identity; the server must never post on behalf of an agent.  A1-attribution: Every message posted to a shared channel under an agent's identity must originate from that agent's process. The server may not construct, transform, or relay agent output under the agent's identity."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "The first standup implementation had the server whistle each agent, collect responses, and POST them to MUSN as that agent. The server impersonated agent identities. This violated I1 (identity) from the agent contract: every change must be linked to the agent that made it, with no anonymous or proxy mutations."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    You need an agent's output to appear in a shared channel (IRC, MUSN, forum)."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    The server has access to agent identities and could post on their behalf, making the code simpler. But this creates false attribution: the message appears to come from the agent, but the agent never authored or approved it. If the agent's invocation returned an error, the server might post the error as if the agent said it. If the server's MUSN post fails, the agent has no way to know its update was lost."} {:name "ANTI-PATTERNS", :name-key "anti-patterns", :slug "anti-patterns", :text "      - Server collects whistle responses and POSTs to MUSN as each agent\n      - Server registers a context-free subprocess under an existing agent's identity\n      - Server reformats or summarizes agent output before posting it as the agent"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    The server's role is coordination, not content relay:\n    1. The server creates the rendezvous, sends the bell, tracks acks.\n    2. Each agent independently generates its output and posts it under its own identity.\n    3. The server may post as \"agency\" (its own identity) for announcements.\n    4. If an agent cannot post (no MUSN access, transport failure), the failure is attributed to the agent, not hidden by the server.\n    This applies to all output channels: MUSN, forum, IRC, CRDT documents."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Proxy posting breaks the audit trail. When output appears under agent X's identity but was posted by the server, you cannot distinguish between \"X said this\" and \"the server said X said this.\" In incident review, this ambiguity makes root cause analysis unreliable. A meeting transcript where one person takes notes for everyone is not the same as each person signing their own statement."} {:name "EVIDENCE (repo history)", :name-key "evidence (repo history)", :slug "evidence-repo-history", :text "      - 2026-02-08 169c4bc: /agency/standup initially implemented as \"whistle all agents and collect responses\", putting the server in the position to relay agent content as an intermediary.\n      - 2026-02-08 9acf154: standup redesign explicitly rejects the prior proxy approach as an identity violation and moves to \"agent self-reporting\": each agent acks, invokes itself, and posts to MUSN under its own identity.\n      - 2026-01-31 b2fbb84: mission notes define the first principle that Agency is a continuity layer, not \"the agent itself\", which aligns with self-attribution (coordination vs content authorship)."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - evidence: audit all MUSN/forum posts and verify posting identity matches originating process\n    - evidence: track cases where server-posted content was attributed to an agent\n    - evidence-shape: {:message/id :string, :message/channel :keyword, :message/author-id :string, :message/origin-process :string, :attribution/valid? :boolean, :attribution/proxy? :boolean}"}], :conclusion "An agent's output must be posted by the agent itself under its own identity; the server must never post on behalf of an agent.  A1-attribution: Every message posted to a shared channel under an agent's identity must originate from that agent's process. The server may not construct, transform, or relay agent output under the agent's identity.", :directives {:audience "agency maintainers, futon developers", :keywords ["identity" "attribution" "proxy" "self-report" "musn" "irc"], :references ["futon-theory/agent-contract" "agency/single-routing-authority"], :sigils ["🪪/名"], :style "pattern", :title "Agents Speak for Themselves", :tone "foundational"}, :id "agency/self-attribution", :keywords ["identity" "attribution" "proxy" "self-report" "musn" "irc"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["futon-theory/agent-contract" "agency/single-routing-authority"], :scores {}, :sigils ["🪪/名"], :source-path "library/agency/self-attribution.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Agents Speak for Themselves", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Each agent-id must resolve to exactly one routing path at any given time; the routing authority must be a single source of truth, not a priority cascade across independent stores.  A1-identity: For any agent-id, at most one routing entry exists at any time. The entry specifies the authoritative transport, session-id, and connection handle. No other store may independently claim to route messages for that agent-id."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Agency routes messages through three tiers: agent-registry (subprocess invocation), local-handlers (in-JVM Drawbridge), and connected-agents (WebSocket). These are independent atoms checked in priority order. An agent can exist in multiple tiers simultaneously with different session-ids, capabilities, and liveness states."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    You need to send a message to an agent and expect consistent behavior regardless of which tier handles it."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    The same agent-id can appear in registry (session-id \"S1\"), local-handlers (no session-id), and connected-agents (session-id \"S2\") simultaneously. Whistle routing picks the first match (registry > local > WS) but the others aren't consulted or cleaned up. Disconnect from WS doesn't remove registry or local-handler entries. The result: stale routing, session-id divergence, and messages going to dead tiers while live ones are ignored."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "      - Session split: registry has session S1, WS has session S2; whistle goes to S1 (stale)\n      - Ghost routing: agent disconnected from WS but still in registry; messages invoke dead subprocess\n      - Duplicate delivery: agent in both local-handlers and WS; bell sent via local handler AND WS"} {:name "ANTI-PATTERNS", :name-key "anti-patterns", :slug "anti-patterns", :text "      - (distinct (concat registry local ws)) as the agent list — hides conflicts\n      - Priority cascade without eviction — checking tiers in order without ensuring mutual exclusion\n      - Disconnect that only cleans one tier — WS on-close that ignores registry and local-handlers"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Establish a single routing registry that is the sole authority for agent resolution:\n    1. One atom, one entry per agent-id. The entry specifies: transport type (registry/local/ws), connection handle, session-id, registered-at, last-active, capabilities.\n    2. Registration replaces: when an agent registers via any transport, it evicts any existing entry for that agent-id. No silent coexistence.\n    3. Disconnect cascades: when a transport drops, the entry is removed. If a whistle is in flight, it gets an explicit failure.\n    4. Session-id is authoritative: one session-id per agent-id at any time. Updated atomically on registration or invocation.\n    5. connected-agent-ids returns only agents from the single registry, not a concat of three stores."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Multiple independent stores for the same identity create split-brain: each store has a partial, potentially stale view. Priority cascading hides the conflict rather than resolving it. A database with three independent indexes that disagree on which row is current is a broken database."} {:name "EVIDENCE (repo history)", :name-key "evidence (repo history)", :slug "evidence-repo-history", :text "      - 2026-02-07 fac9efb: \"memory bifurcation\" brief documents WS-delivered messages that never reach the live terminal session, because \"connected\" is not the same as \"invokable\".\n      - 2026-02-07 84ac0e0: unified agent registry introduced explicitly to address multi-tier routing and bring invocation under one authority.\n      - 2026-02-04 9a73783: /agency/kick endpoint added to disconnect stale agents, indicating stale routing entries were a recurring operational failure mode.\n      - 2026-02-04 92fe6a1 and 2026-02-04 bb2e3a7: session-id tracking for WS connections added and then fixed, highlighting that session identity was drifting outside a single source of truth."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - evidence: count agents that exist in multiple tiers simultaneously during normal operation\n    - evidence: track session-id divergence across tiers for the same agent-id\n    - evidence: measure time between WS disconnect and registry/local cleanup (currently: infinite)\n    - evidence-shape: {:agent/id :string, :routing/transport :keyword, :routing/session-id :string, :routing/registered-at :string, :routing/last-active :string, :conflict/tiers [:keyword], :conflict/session-ids [:string]}"}], :conclusion "Each agent-id must resolve to exactly one routing path at any given time; the routing authority must be a single source of truth, not a priority cascade across independent stores.  A1-identity: For any agent-id, at most one routing entry exists at any time. The entry specifies the authoritative transport, session-id, and connection handle. No other store may independently claim to route messages for that agent-id.", :directives {:audience "agency maintainers, futon developers", :keywords ["identity" "routing" "registry" "tier" "agent" "session" "authority"], :references ["futon-theory/agent-contract" "realtime/listener-leases"], :sigils ["🔑/一"], :style "pattern", :title "One Agent, One Routing Path, One Source of Truth", :tone "analytic-foundational"}, :id "agency/single-routing-authority", :keywords ["identity" "routing" "registry" "tier" "agent" "session" "authority"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["futon-theory/agent-contract" "realtime/listener-leases"], :scores {}, :sigils ["🔑/一"], :source-path "library/agency/single-routing-authority.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "One Agent, One Routing Path, One Source of Truth", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Agent state transitions (registration, rollover, session update) must complete fully or fail without partial effects; corrupted or partial state must be detected and rejected, not silently accepted.  A2-atomicity: Every agent state transition either completes fully (all fields updated, persisted, and confirmed) or reverts to the prior state with an explicit error. No transition may leave state in a partially-updated condition."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Agency persists agent state to .edn files. Rollover clears session-id to nil (documented bug). State loading silently returns defaults on parse error. Registration can succeed in one tier while failing to clean up another. These are all partial-state failures: the system is left in an inconsistent state with no signal."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    An agent's state must change — new session, rollover, registration, disconnect."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Multi-step state transitions can fail midway. Rollover currently: (1) summarizes context, (2) clears session-id to nil, (3) should create new session but doesn't. If step 1 succeeds and step 3 fails, the agent has lost its session with no recovery. State file corruption is treated as \"no state\" rather than \"corrupted state.\""} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "      - Nil session: rollover clears session-id but doesn't create new one; agent loses all context\n      - Silent corruption: state file has truncated EDN; load returns nil; agent starts fresh with no warning\n      - Tier desync: agent registered in authority but connection handle is stale; messages route to dead handle"} {:name "ANTI-PATTERNS", :name-key "anti-patterns", :slug "anti-patterns", :text "      - (swap! state assoc :session-id nil) as \"clear\" — nil is not a valid session state\n      - (catch Exception _ nil) on state load — corruption becomes invisible\n      - Multi-step registration without rollback — register in tier A, fail to clean tier B"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Apply all-or-nothing semantics to every state transition:\n    1. Rollover: summarize → create new session → update state atomically. If any step fails, restore previous state and emit error.\n    2. Registration: register in authority → confirm registration → begin routing. If confirmation fails, remove registration.\n    3. State persistence: write to temp file → fsync → rename to target. If rename fails, temp file is evidence of interrupted write.\n    4. State loading: validate structure on read. If validation fails, emit integrity error with file path and content hash. Do not silently fall back to defaults.\n    5. Session-id updates: swap! with compare-and-set semantics. If the agent-id has been evicted between read and write, the update fails explicitly."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Partial state transitions are the hardest bugs to diagnose because the system looks almost correct. A session-id of nil is not \"no session\" — it is \"corrupted state from failed rollover.\" Silently loading defaults after corruption masks data loss. A database that silently returns empty on corruption is worse than one that refuses to start."} {:name "EVIDENCE (repo history)", :name-key "evidence (repo history)", :slug "evidence-repo-history", :text "      - 2026-01-31 466bc73: mission \"Make Agency Work Properly\" documents two atomicity breaks: rollover wiping the active session id, and forum thread ids overwriting LLM resume ids.\n      - 2026-01-31 17c87b7: code change introduces :resume-id as distinct from forum thread-id and removes \"Agency completed\" prefixes; it also adds rollover pending flags, indicating state transitions were previously leaving the system in partial/inconsistent states.\n      - current src/futon3/agency/service.clj: load-agent-state catches parse errors and returns nil, which is silent corruption acceptance rather than an integrity failure."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - evidence: audit rollover path for atomicity; trace each swap! and verify all-or-nothing\n    - evidence: inject corrupted .edn file and verify system behavior (should fail loud, not silent)\n    - evidence: count nil session-ids in production state files\n    - evidence-shape: {:transition/type :keyword, :transition/agent-id :string, :transition/started-at :string, :transition/completed? :boolean, :transition/rolled-back? :boolean, :state/before :map, :state/after :map, :error/cause :string}"}], :conclusion "Agent state transitions (registration, rollover, session update) must complete fully or fail without partial effects; corrupted or partial state must be detected and rejected, not silently accepted.  A2-atomicity: Every agent state transition either completes fully (all fields updated, persisted, and confirmed) or reverts to the prior state with an explicit error. No transition may leave state in a partially-updated condition.", :directives {:audience "agency maintainers, futon developers", :keywords ["state" "atomicity" "rollover" "session" "corruption" "persistence"], :references ["storage/durability-first" "futon-theory/agent-contract"], :sigils ["🔒/全"], :style "pattern", :title "State Transitions Are All-or-Nothing", :tone "analytic-foundational"}, :id "agency/state-atomicity", :keywords ["state" "atomicity" "rollover" "session" "corruption" "persistence"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["storage/durability-first" "futon-theory/agent-contract"], :scores {}, :sigils ["🔒/全"], :source-path "library/agency/state-atomicity.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "State Transitions Are All-or-Nothing", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Exploration without limits drifts; any search needs a budget that forces commitment or escalation."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "An agent explores options—spatial locations, semantic regions, conversation branches, solution candidates."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The agent must balance discovering new possibilities against making progress on known paths."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Unbounded exploration leads to wander: the agent keeps looking without ever committing, exhausting resources without output."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Assign explicit budgets (time, steps, tokens, reads) to exploratory phases and define reset rules. When budget nears exhaustion, force a decision: commit to best-so-far, escalate to a supervisor, or explicitly expand scope with justification."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Budgets convert open-ended search into bounded decisions, making progress measurable and preventing silent resource drain."} {:name "evidence", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "`fuclaude` enforces `--explore-limit`, and `src/futon3/fulab/hud.clj` caps candidates with `pattern-limit`."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Identify unbounded loops in your agent (e.g., \"keep searching until found\").\n    - Add budget parameters with sensible defaults and observable countdowns.\n    - Design escalation paths for when budgets expire without resolution and log the choice taken.\n    - Add explicit time/token/tool-call budgets for fulab sessions and log budget exhaustion decisions in lab events."}], :conclusion "Exploration without limits drifts; any search needs a budget that forces commitment or escalation.", :directives {:audience "agent architects, workflow designers, futon developers", :sigils ["💰/分"], :style "pattern", :title "Budget Bounds Exploration", :tone "foundational"}, :id "agent/budget-bounds-exploration", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["💰/分"], :source-path "library/agent/budget-bounds-exploration.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Budget Bounds Exploration", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "How strongly to commit to a choice should depend on how certain the agent is."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "An agent must choose among options with varying degrees of uncertainty."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The agent wants to balance exploiting known-good options against exploring uncertain alternatives."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Fixed commitment strength fails both ways: always committing hard misses better options; always hedging prevents progress."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Maintain a commitment parameter (temperature, precision, confidence threshold) that modulates how decisively the agent acts. Couple it to uncertainty signals with explicit thresholds: low confidence -> explore or pause; medium confidence -> hedge or seek confirmation; high confidence -> commit and execute."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Variable commitment adapts behavior to epistemic state. The agent exploits when it knows enough and explores (or asks) when it doesn't."} {:name "evidence", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "`src/futon3/fulab/hud.clj` computes `tau` from candidate spread and `precision/prior`, and `dev/lab-session-report.clj` logs tau/prediction-error updates."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Identify how your agent currently commits to choices (is it always the same strength?).\n    - Add uncertainty tracking that can modulate commitment and log threshold crossings.\n    - Tune the coupling: what uncertainty level triggers exploration vs. exploitation vs. pause?\n    - Tie tau/confidence thresholds to explicit actions (ask/hedge/commit) and log crossings in lab sessions."}], :conclusion "How strongly to commit to a choice should depend on how certain the agent is.", :directives {:audience "agent architects, workflow designers, futon developers", :sigils ["👓/认"], :style "pattern", :title "Commitment Varies With Confidence", :tone "foundational"}, :id "agent/commitment-varies-with-confidence", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["👓/认"], :source-path "library/agent/commitment-varies-with-confidence.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Commitment Varies With Confidence", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Involving other agents or humans has overhead; factor it into decisions."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "An agent can work alone or involve others (other agents, human supervisors, external services)."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The agent wants to leverage external capabilities or get authoritative answers."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Coordination is not free: it takes time, consumes attention, introduces latency, and may require context transfer. Treating it as free leads to over-reliance or spamming."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Model coordination as an action with explicit cost (latency, tokens, human attention units). Include this cost in decision-making: sometimes working with partial local knowledge is better than waiting for external input, unless the risk of error is higher than the coordination cost."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Cost-aware coordination prevents both under-use (never asking for help when stuck) and over-use (constant interruption). It respects the resources of collaborators."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Inventory coordination actions your agent can take (ask human, call service, delegate to agent).\n    - Assign cost estimates to each and include them in action selection.\n    - Track actual coordination costs and update estimates based on experience.\n    - Add coordination cost fields to fulab session events (latency, token cost) and use them in action selection."}], :conclusion "Involving other agents or humans has overhead; factor it into decisions.", :directives {:audience "agent architects, workflow designers, futon developers", :sigils ["🎎/功"], :style "pattern", :title "Coordination Has Cost", :tone "foundational"}, :id "agent/coordination-has-cost", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🎎/功"], :source-path "library/agent/coordination-has-cost.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Coordination Has Cost", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Build environments that select for viability, not agents that hit targets."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are designing how agents will operate, learn, and evolve over time."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    You want agents that can adapt to changing conditions and improve through experience rather than being brittle to novelty."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Optimization toward fixed targets finds local maxima; agents become overfit to benchmarks and fail when conditions shift. The \"best\" agent for yesterday's metric may be the worst for tomorrow's reality."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Focus on creating environments where viable behaviors can emerge, persist, and be selected: provide trails that enable learning, budgets that create selection pressure, evidence requirements that filter confabulation, and handoff mechanisms that let successful patterns propagate. Let agent quality emerge from environmental constraints rather than direct tuning."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Viable environments enable open-ended evolution. Instead of one optimal agent, you get a population of approaches that can be compared, combined, and adapted. The environment becomes the product, not any single agent."} {:name "evidence", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "Fulab exposes a shared pattern catalog plus selection environment (`resources/sigils/patterns-index.tsv`, `src/futon3/fulab/hud.clj`) and PUR/PSR templates (`src/futon3/fulab/pattern_competence.clj`)."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Audit whether your agent design is optimizing for metrics or creating conditions for viability.\n    - Identify environmental features that create selection pressure (budgets, evidence requirements, scope bounds).\n    - Design for variation: can different agents or approaches coexist and compete in your environment?\n    - Track what emerges rather than what you planned; let the environment surface surprises.\n    - Persist pattern outcome metrics and feed them back into AIF priors to increase selection pressure over time."}], :conclusion "Build environments that select for viability, not agents that hit targets.", :directives {:audience "agent architects, workflow designers, futon developers", :sigils ["➰/本"], :style "pattern", :title "Environment Over Optimization", :tone "foundational"}, :id "agent/environment-over-optimization", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["➰/本"], :source-path "library/agent/environment-over-optimization.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Environment Over Optimization", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Escalate when expected error cost exceeds coordination cost."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "An agent must choose between acting locally and involving others."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    A decision could carry material risk or irreversible impact."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Coordination costs discourage escalation, while risk-blind escalation wastes time on low-stakes work."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Estimate risk cost (impact x likelihood) and compare it to coordination cost. Escalate when risk cost exceeds a defined threshold, and log the comparison basis."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    This preserves speed on low-stakes actions while protecting high-stakes decisions."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Define risk categories and default impact scales for your domain.\n    - Calibrate escalation thresholds with real incidents.\n    - Log escalation decisions alongside their risk and cost estimates.\n    - Classify fulab tool actions by risk (read/edit/network) and log the escalation decision before enabling `--allow-edits` or `--yolo`."}], :conclusion "Escalate when expected error cost exceeds coordination cost.", :directives {:audience "agent architects, workflow designers, futon developers", :sigils ["⚡️/平"], :style "pattern", :title "Escalation Cost vs Risk", :tone "foundational"}, :id "agent/escalation-cost-vs-risk", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["⚡️/平"], :source-path "library/agent/escalation-cost-vs-risk.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Escalation Cost vs Risk", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Claims require grounding; an agent that asserts without evidence cannot be audited or trusted."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "An agent makes claims about what it did, what it found, or what is true."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The agent's outputs need to be verifiable by other agents, humans, or future selves."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Assertions without anchors are indistinguishable from confabulation. Observers cannot tell if the agent is reporting reality or generating plausible-sounding noise."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Require every substantive claim to point to evidence: a file path, a trace event, a test result, a quotation. If evidence is unavailable, emit the claim as provisional with an expiry and a verification path. Make \"unsupported claim\" a detectable violation, not just a style preference."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Evidence transforms agent output from \"trust me\" to \"check this.\" It enables verification, correction, and cumulative trust-building."} {:name "evidence", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "Fulab PUR/PSR templates require anchors/certificates (`src/futon3/fulab/pattern_competence.clj`), and claim verification is enforced via `fulab-pattern-claim` + `fulab-pattern-check`."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Identify claim types in your agent's output (facts, completions, diagnoses).\n    - For each type, define what counts as adequate evidence.\n    - Add validation that flags claims missing their evidence anchors and records provisional claims separately.\n    - Auto-attach tool/file anchors from `lab/tool-uses` to PUR/PSR records and surface missing evidence in `fulab-session-report`."}], :conclusion "Claims require grounding; an agent that asserts without evidence cannot be audited or trusted.", :directives {:audience "agent architects, workflow designers, futon developers", :sigils ["📁/示"], :style "pattern", :title "Evidence Over Assertion", :tone "foundational"}, :id "agent/evidence-over-assertion", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["📁/示"], :source-path "library/agent/evidence-over-assertion.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Evidence Over Assertion", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "When passing work to another agent or future self, transfer enough state that the receiver doesn't restart from scratch."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "An agent's work will be continued by another agent, a human, or the same agent in a future session."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    Continuity matters: the work is incremental, context is expensive to rebuild, or progress would be lost."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Without explicit handoff, successors face cold starts: they repeat discoveries, miss constraints, or contradict prior decisions."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Define a handoff record that captures: current state hypothesis, decisions made and why, open questions, scope boundaries, next actions, evidence/trail anchors, and any provisional-claim ledger. Emit it at session end or when transferring to another agent."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Handoffs convert ephemeral session state into persistent project state. They enable distributed and asynchronous work without context collapse."} {:name "evidence", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "Fulab exports session context to `lab/raw`, `lab/stubs`, and `lab/trace` (`dev/lab-export-claude.clj`), plus summaries via `fulab-summary` and `fulab-session-report`."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Identify handoff points in your agent's lifecycle (session end, escalation, delegation).\n    - Define what context is essential vs. nice-to-have for each handoff type.\n    - Test handoffs by having a fresh agent resume and measuring ramp-up cost.\n    - Extend lab stubs/summaries to capture open questions, scope boundaries, and provisional claims for resumption."}], :conclusion "When passing work to another agent or future self, transfer enough state that the receiver doesn't restart from scratch.", :directives {:audience "agent architects, workflow designers, futon developers", :sigils ["📤/代"], :style "pattern", :title "Handoff Preserves Context", :tone "foundational"}, :id "agent/handoff-preserves-context", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["📤/代"], :source-path "library/agent/handoff-preserves-context.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Handoff Preserves Context", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Before investing effort in a proof path, sketch multiple complete proof architectures as\n  wiring diagrams with explicit node status (solid/open/blocked). Triage immediately to\n  focus effort on the most promising path."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You have a theorem to prove with multiple plausible approaches. Each approach\n    has several steps, some established and some open. The temptation is to start with the\n    \"obvious\" or \"natural\" approach and push until stuck."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      There are 3+ plausible proof paths, each with multiple steps. Some steps are\n      established results, some are open problems, and some may be blocked by known\n      obstructions. The risk profile of each path (number of open steps, their independence,\n      known difficulties) varies significantly."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      Sketching architectures is upfront cost. For problems with one clear approach, it's\n      overkill. Only invest in this when the proof landscape is genuinely multi-path and the\n      cost of pursuing a dead end is high (weeks of work, not hours)."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "        - Architecture bias: sketching only 2-3 paths and missing the best one. Cast the\n          net wide initially; narrow after triage.\n        - False \"blocked\" assessment: marking a path as blocked based on a surface reading\n          of an obstruction, when the obstruction has a workaround. Verify blocks carefully.\n        - Triage paralysis: spending too long comparing paths instead of working on the\n          best one. The triage should take hours, not days."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "      1. For each plausible proof path, draw a complete wiring diagram from hypotheses to\n         conclusion.\n      2. Mark each node as: solid (established), open (needs work), or blocked (known\n         obstruction).\n      3. Kill blocked paths immediately.\n      4. Compare open paths on: (a) number of open nodes, (b) whether open nodes are\n         sequential (each depends on the previous) or parallel (independent), (c) whether\n         any open node is a known hard problem.\n      5. Prioritize paths with fewer open nodes, parallel structure, and no known-hard\n         sub-problems."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      Sequential open obstacles multiply risk (if any one fails, the entire path fails).\n      Parallel open sub-goals reduce risk (any one success may suffice). A path with one\n      open node is strictly better than a path with three sequential open nodes, even if\n      the single node is harder, because success is binary: one hard question has the same\n      risk profile as three easy questions in series but is easier to focus on."} {:name "EVIDENCE", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "        P7 lattices with 2-torsion (futon6, Feb 2026): 5 hypothetical proof architectures\n        (H1-H5) were sketched for the S obligation. Two (H3, H5) were immediately killed\n        (codim-2 gap fails for reflections; Gauss-Bonnet kills Fowler in odd dim). One (H2)\n        was deprioritized (3 sequential obstacles with structural headwinds). The winner (H1,\n        rotation route) was not the \"obvious\" continuation but had the best risk profile:\n        one open question (lattice existence) with no known blocker, plus two parallel\n        S-branch options."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Sketch 3-5 complete proof architectures as wiring diagrams.]\n    next[Mark node status: solid, open, blocked.]\n    next[Kill blocked paths, deprioritize sequential-obstacle paths.]\n    next[Focus on the path with fewest independent open nodes.]"}], :conclusion "  Before investing effort in a proof path, sketch multiple complete proof architectures as\n  wiring diagrams with explicit node status (solid/open/blocked). Triage immediately to\n  focus effort on the most promising path.", :directives {:audience "proof engineers, mathematical agents, research coordinators", :factor "Keen investigation (dhammavicaya)", :keywords ["hypothetical" "architecture" "wiring" "diagram" "triage" "path" "blocked" "open" "solid" "proof" "kill" "sequential" "parallel" "obstacle"], :references ["math-informal/split-into-cases" "math-informal/technique-landscape-map"], :sigils ["🏗/乾"], :title "Hypothetical Proof Architecture", :tone "strategic"}, :id "agent/hypothetical-proof-architecture", :keywords ["hypothetical" "architecture" "wiring" "diagram" "triage" "path" "blocked" "open" "solid" "proof" "kill" "sequential" "parallel" "obstacle"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["math-informal/split-into-cases" "math-informal/technique-landscape-map"], :scores {}, :sigils ["🏗/乾"], :source-path "library/agent/hypothetical-proof-architecture.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Hypothetical Proof Architecture", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "A run should not start until the agent has restated and bound the intent and scope."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "An agent is about to begin a session or new turn."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The system needs a reliable record of what the agent believes it is doing."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    If intent only lives in the prompt or UI, it drifts: agents reinterpret mid-run without explicit renegotiation."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Require a handshake that echoes intent, scope, constraints, and success criteria, and record an acknowledgment. Changes to intent or scope require an explicit renegotiation event before further actions."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Binding intent makes the run auditable and prevents silent goal drift."} {:name "evidence", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "Fulab HUD prompts require an intent restatement (`src/futon3/fulab/hud.clj`), and `fuclaude --intent` passes intent into the HUD."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Add an intent-handshake event to the session start protocol.\n    - Block tool actions until the handshake is logged.\n    - Record intent changes as first-class events with rationale.\n    - Emit an explicit intent-handshake event in lab sessions and enforce it before tool calls."}], :conclusion "A run should not start until the agent has restated and bound the intent and scope.", :directives {:audience "agent architects, workflow designers, futon developers", :sigils ["👥/令"], :style "pattern", :title "Intent Handshake Is Binding", :tone "foundational"}, :id "agent/intent-handshake-is-binding", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["👥/令"], :source-path "library/agent/intent-handshake-is-binding.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Intent Handshake Is Binding", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Stopping to request clarification or expanded scope is a valid action, not a defect."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "An agent encounters ambiguity, uncertainty, or boundary conditions during execution."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The agent cannot proceed confidently without information or authorization it doesn't have."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Agents that never pause either over-commit (bulldozing through uncertainty) or silently drift (making up answers). Both erode trust."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Treat \"pause and ask\" as a first-class action with defined triggers: ambiguous requirements, scope boundary reached, confidence below threshold, resource budget near exhaustion. Emit pause events with context for resumption (reason, missing info, requested decision, scope impact, confidence/budget snapshot)."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Pausing surfaces uncertainty to those who can resolve it. An agent that pauses appropriately is more trustworthy than one that always \"succeeds.\""} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Define explicit pause triggers for your agent (don't rely on ad-hoc judgment).\n    - Design pause events that carry enough context for efficient resumption.\n    - Track pause frequency as a diagnostic: too many suggests unclear scope; zero suggests over-confidence.\n    - Add pause events to fulab sessions with reason/confidence/budget snapshots and a resume hook."}], :conclusion "Stopping to request clarification or expanded scope is a valid action, not a defect.", :directives {:audience "agent architects, workflow designers, futon developers", :sigils ["💤/不"], :style "pattern", :title "Pause Is Not Failure", :tone "foundational"}, :id "agent/pause-is-not-failure", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["💤/不"], :source-path "library/agent/pause-is-not-failure.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Pause Is Not Failure", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Unsupported claims must be tracked, timeboxed, and resolved."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "An agent must sometimes act before evidence is available."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The agent needs to continue despite missing confirmation."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Treating provisional claims as facts hides uncertainty and undermines trust."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Record provisional claims in a ledger with fields (claim, reason, evidence needed, expiry, owner). Require each entry to be resolved to confirmed or retracted, and surface unresolved items during handoff."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    A ledger turns uncertainty into actionable follow-up rather than silent drift."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Add a lightweight ledger store (file or trace event) for provisional claims.\n    - Define expiry windows and resolution statuses.\n    - Include the ledger summary in handoff records.\n    - Implement a fulab provisional-claims ledger with expiry/status fields and surface unresolved items in session reports."}], :conclusion "Unsupported claims must be tracked, timeboxed, and resolved.", :directives {:audience "agent architects, workflow designers, futon developers", :sigils ["📁/未"], :style "pattern", :title "Provisional Claims Ledger", :tone "foundational"}, :id "agent/provisional-claims-ledger", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["📁/未"], :source-path "library/agent/provisional-claims-ledger.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Provisional Claims Ledger", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Reduce a complex open problem to the smallest possible missing lemma with explicit\n  hypotheses, then record it as a named conjecture. The reduction itself is proved work;\n  the named conjecture becomes a precise handoff target."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You have been working on a proof and have closed most cases or steps. The\n    remaining gap is hard but may be expressible as a single statement with clear\n    hypotheses and conclusion."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      The proof is mostly complete: the structure is understood, most cases are closed,\n      and the remaining difficulty is localizable to a specific mathematical statement.\n      The statement can be formulated with explicit hypotheses that are already proved\n      or assumed from the problem setup."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      The reduction must be rigorous. \"It would be enough to prove X\" is not a reduction\n      unless you have proved that X actually implies the desired conclusion. Also: the\n      kernel should be genuinely minimal — if it contains unnecessary hypotheses or can\n      be split into independent parts, simplify further."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "        - False reduction: the kernel doesn't actually imply the conclusion. Prove the\n          reduction rigorously.\n        - Over-specified kernel: too many hypotheses, some unnecessary. Simplify until each\n          hypothesis is used in the reduction proof.\n        - Under-specified kernel: the kernel is true but insufficient. Check that the\n          reduction covers all cases."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "      1. Formulate the missing lemma with numbered hypotheses (H1, H2, ...) and a\n         conclusion.\n      2. Prove the reduction: show that the hypotheses are satisfiable from the problem\n         setup and that the conclusion implies the target theorem.\n      3. Check each hypothesis: is it already proved, or does it need work? Mark status.\n      4. Give the conjecture a name (GPL-H, L2*, etc.) and record it in the proof status\n         document.\n      5. Include any numerical evidence that supports the conjecture."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      A named conjecture with explicit hypotheses is qualitatively different from \"the\n      proof is stuck.\" It converts an open problem into a precise target that can be:\n      (a) worked on by a different agent with fresh perspective, (b) recognized by someone\n      who knows the relevant technique, (c) connected to existing results via the explicit\n      hypotheses, (d) empirically tested. The reduce phase (all the work that leads to\n      the kernel) is always tractable; the kernel phase may not be — but the reduce phase\n      is still valuable because it converts \"hard problem\" into \"hard problem minus\n      everything tractable.\""} {:name "EVIDENCE", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "        P6 epsilon-light subsets (futon6, Feb 2026): the full proof (650+ lines) was reduced\n        to GPL-H — a single operator-valued averaging statement with 4 explicit hypotheses\n        (H1: light edges, H2: core regularity, H3: barrier-valid prefix, H4: residual size)\n        and one conclusion (min_v ‖Y_t(v)‖ ≤ θ). A sharper reduction showed that L2* alone\n        (score control) implies the theorem, without needing L3 (drift control). Named\n        conjecture + proved reduction + 2353 trajectory numerical evidence.\n\n        P7 lattices with 2-torsion (futon6, Feb 2026): the S obligation was reduced to a\n        single lattice-existence question: do arithmetic uniform lattices in Isom(H^{2k+1})\n        contain order-2 rotational involutions? This is a number-theoretic question\n        independent of the topology/surgery machinery."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Formulate the missing lemma with explicit hypotheses.]\n    next[Prove the reduction (hypotheses → target theorem).]\n    next[Name the conjecture and record in proof status.]\n    next[Gather numerical evidence for or against.]"}], :conclusion "  Reduce a complex open problem to the smallest possible missing lemma with explicit\n  hypotheses, then record it as a named conjecture. The reduction itself is proved work;\n  the named conjecture becomes a precise handoff target.", :directives {:audience "proof engineers, mathematical agents, research coordinators", :factor "Keen investigation (dhammavicaya)", :keywords ["reduce" "kernel" "lemma" "conjecture" "formalize" "hypotheses" "bridge" "named" "handoff" "minimal" "explicit" "GPL" "L2"], :references ["math-informal/structural-obstruction-as-theorem" "math-informal/exhaustion-as-theorem"], :sigils ["🎯/坤"], :title "Reduction to Kernel", :tone "strategic"}, :id "agent/reduction-to-kernel", :keywords ["reduce" "kernel" "lemma" "conjecture" "formalize" "hypotheses" "bridge" "named" "handoff" "minimal" "explicit" "GPL" "L2"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["math-informal/structural-obstruction-as-theorem" "math-informal/exhaustion-as-theorem"], :scores {}, :sigils ["🎯/坤"], :source-path "library/agent/reduction-to-kernel.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Reduction to Kernel", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Declare the territory before entering it; unbounded scope makes progress unmeasurable."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "An agent is about to begin work on a task that could touch many things."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The agent needs to make progress that can be verified and bounded."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Without declared scope, \"done\" is undefined, side effects are unpredictable, and observers cannot tell if the agent is on track or wandering."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Before substantive action, state the scope: what files, concepts, or regions are in-bounds; what is explicitly out-of-bounds; what the exit condition is. Treat scope violations as requiring explicit expansion, not silent drift. Scope defines the territory; budgets and pauses govern what happens inside it."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Declared scope makes progress measurable, side effects auditable, and completion definable. It also protects the broader system from unintended changes."} {:name "evidence", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "Fulab HUD prompts require a pre-action plan (`src/futon3/fulab/hud.clj`), and scope boundaries are spelled out in `CLAUDE.md`."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Add scope declaration to your agent's initialization or planning phase.\n    - Log scope boundaries and check actions against them.\n    - Design a scope-expansion protocol for when legitimate work requires broadening.\n    - Record explicit in/out-of-scope fields in fulab session metadata and log scope-expansion events."}], :conclusion "Declare the territory before entering it; unbounded scope makes progress unmeasurable.", :directives {:audience "agent architects, workflow designers, futon developers", :sigils ["📐/内"], :style "pattern", :title "Scope Before Action", :tone "foundational"}, :id "agent/scope-before-action", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["📐/内"], :source-path "library/agent/scope-before-action.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Scope Before Action", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Structure every agent cycle as three distinct phases: gather input, reason about options, commit to action."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "An agent operates in an environment where it must repeatedly choose what to do next."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The agent needs to make progress on a goal while responding to a changing environment."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Rushing to act without sensing leads to blind thrashing; sensing without acting leads to analysis paralysis; acting without deliberation leads to incoherent behavior."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Enforce a rhythm: first gather relevant observations, then weigh options against goals and constraints, then commit to a single action before the next cycle. Make each phase explicit and bounded, and check scope, budget, and confidence gates at phase boundaries."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    The three-phase structure prevents both impulsive drift and endless deliberation, and makes the agent's reasoning inspectable at phase boundaries."} {:name "evidence", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "Fulab HUD prompts force deliberate candidate review before acting (`src/futon3/fulab/hud.clj`), and the session report tracks selection and outcome phases (`dev/lab-session-report.clj`)."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Identify where your agent conflates phases (e.g., acting while still gathering input).\n    - Add explicit phase markers to logs or traces so observers can see the rhythm.\n    - Tune phase durations based on task urgency and uncertainty.\n    - Emit explicit sense/deliberate/act markers in fulab session events and surface them in reports."}], :conclusion "Structure every agent cycle as three distinct phases: gather input, reason about options, commit to action.", :directives {:audience "agent architects, workflow designers, futon developers", :sigils ["🔃/工"], :style "pattern", :title "Sense-Deliberate-Act Cycle", :tone "foundational"}, :id "agent/sense-deliberate-act", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🔃/工"], :source-path "library/agent/sense-deliberate-act.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Sense-Deliberate-Act Cycle", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Treat internal state as a working hypothesis about the world, not ground truth."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "An agent maintains beliefs about its environment, progress, and goals."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The agent needs to act under uncertainty and update its understanding as new information arrives."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Treating state as certain leads to brittleness when observations contradict expectations; the agent either ignores evidence or fails catastrophically."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Represent state as revisable hypotheses with associated confidence. When observations conflict with state, update the hypothesis rather than defending it. Track what would falsify current beliefs."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Hypothesis-framed state makes the agent self-correcting: surprises trigger learning rather than denial or breakdown."} {:name "evidence", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "Fulab PSR forecasts in `src/futon3/fulab/pattern_competence.clj` and belief updates in `dev/lab-session-report.clj` treat state as revisable (prediction error, tau updates)."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Audit your agent's state representation for implicit certainty assumptions.\n    - Add a \"surprise\" signal when observations diverge from expectations.\n    - Design update rules that revise state proportionally to evidence strength.\n    - Emit explicit surprise/contradiction events when outcomes diverge from PSR forecasts and track hypothesis confidence."}], :conclusion "Treat internal state as a working hypothesis about the world, not ground truth.", :directives {:audience "agent architects, workflow designers, futon developers", :sigils ["🌀/计"], :style "pattern", :title "State Is Hypothesis", :tone "foundational"}, :id "agent/state-is-hypothesis", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🌀/计"], :source-path "library/agent/state-is-hypothesis.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "State Is Hypothesis", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Dispatch an agent as a student-explorer, not a junior-developer-verifier. Give them the\n  problem context, what's been tried and why it failed, candidate directions to explore,\n  and a structured report format. Let them discover rather than confirm."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You have a research problem with multiple unexplored directions. You could\n    hand each direction to an agent (Codex, another Claude instance, a human collaborator)\n    for parallel exploration. The temptation is to specify exactly what to compute and what\n    answer to expect — but this discards the agent's ability to discover surprises."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      The remaining work involves genuine exploration (multiple approaches, unknown outcomes)\n      rather than rote verification (check that X equals Y). The dispatched agent has the\n      capability to reason about the problem, not just execute a script."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      Unrestricted exploration wastes time. \"Go think about this problem\" is too vague.\n      The student needs: (1) what's known, (2) what's been tried and failed with explicit\n      reasons, (3) candidate directions with rationale, (4) what NOT to try, and (5) a\n      report format. The negative knowledge (what not to try) is as important as the\n      positive — it prevents the student from repeating dead ends that took the advisor\n      hours to discover."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "        - Too little context: student wastes time rediscovering known results.\n        - Too much prescription: student confirms rather than explores.\n        - No report format: student returns unstructured prose that's hard to integrate.\n        - Missing dead ends: student repeats the advisor's failed approaches.\n        - Overloaded dispatch: giving 10 directions when 2-3 is the right scope per agent."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "      Structure the dispatch as:\n\n      1. **Context**: The problem statement and what's been proved so far. Enough for the\n         student to understand the landscape without reading every prior document.\n\n      2. **Dead ends with reasons**: Approaches that were tried and WHY they failed.\n         Not just \"SOS didn't work\" but \"SOS is structurally blocked because the interior\n         zero forces all multipliers to vanish.\" This is where structural-obstruction-as-\n         theorem and exhaustion-as-theorem pay off — they compress dead ends into\n         transmissible theorems.\n\n      3. **Directions to explore**: 2-4 candidate approaches, each with a sentence on why\n         it might work. The student may pivot based on findings — that's fine.\n\n      4. **Report format**: What to come back with. Not pass/fail but structured findings:\n         what was tried, what happened, what surprised, what new dead ends were discovered,\n         what partial results were obtained.\n\n      5. **Success criteria**: What would constitute progress. \"Find a universal c_0 >= 0.01\"\n         is better than \"prove the theorem.\" Partial success is success."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      A verification dispatch (\"confirm X\") caps the return at 1 bit: yes or no. An\n      exploration dispatch (\"investigate X\") can return discoveries, new dead ends,\n      partial results, unexpected connections, and reformulations. The student may find\n      something the advisor didn't think to look for. The negative-knowledge transfer\n      prevents wasted work while the open-ended framing permits serendipity."} {:name "EVIDENCE", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "        P4 n=4 Stam inequality (futon6, Feb 2026): Codex was dispatched in verification\n        mode (\"confirm these eigenvalues, verify this identity\"). Returned pass/fail.\n        Separately, Codex was dispatched in exploration mode for PHCpack — \"find all critical\n        points of this system\" — and returned 12 in-domain CPs with full case classification,\n        discovering the mixed_volume=0 issue independently. The exploration dispatch produced\n        richer results per unit of effort.\n\n        P6 epsilon-light subsets: the Claude→Codex handoff note contained dead ends\n        (6 technique failures with reasons) and candidate directions (BSS greedy, expander\n        decomposition, structural analysis). This is the right format for student dispatch;\n        the missing piece was framing it as \"go explore these\" rather than \"verify my claims.\""} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Write the dispatch note: context, dead ends, directions, report format.]\n    next[Send one direction per agent if parallelizing.]\n    next[On return: integrate findings, update the dead-end list, re-dispatch if needed.]"}], :conclusion "  Dispatch an agent as a student-explorer, not a junior-developer-verifier. Give them the\n  problem context, what's been tried and why it failed, candidate directions to explore,\n  and a structured report format. Let them discover rather than confirm.", :directives {:audience "proof engineers, research coordinators, multi-agent orchestrators", :factor "Right effort (sammā vāyāma)", :keywords ["dispatch" "explore" "student" "grad" "investigate" "try" "report" "handoff" "negative-knowledge" "dead-end" "direction" "structured" "findings"], :references ["agent/handoff-preserves-context" "agent/scope-before-action" "math-informal/structural-obstruction-as-theorem"], :sigils ["🎓/令"], :title "Student Dispatch", :tone "collegial"}, :id "agent/student-dispatch", :keywords ["dispatch" "explore" "student" "grad" "investigate" "try" "report" "handoff" "negative-knowledge" "dead-end" "direction" "structured" "findings"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["agent/handoff-preserves-context" "agent/scope-before-action" "math-informal/structural-obstruction-as-theorem"], :scores {}, :sigils ["🎓/令"], :source-path "library/agent/student-dispatch.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Student Dispatch", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Leave traces that allow backtracking; an agent that can't retrace its path can't recover from mistakes."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "An agent navigates through states, decisions, or locations over time."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The agent may need to undo actions, revisit prior states, or learn from dead ends."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Without a trail, the agent cannot distinguish \"unexplored\" from \"explored and rejected,\" leading to repeated mistakes or inability to recover."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Record a trail of moves, decisions, and state transitions with minimal fields (time, state snapshot, decision, action, evidence, reversibility). Make the trail queryable so the agent (or its successors) can backtrack, detect loops, and learn from history."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Trails convert ephemeral navigation into persistent structure. They enable learning, debugging, and graceful recovery."} {:name "evidence", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "Fulab session exports write `lab/trace/<session-id>.org` and `lab/raw` (`dev/lab-export-claude.clj`), and `fuclaude` supports `--resume`/`--continue` runs."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Decide what granularity of trail your agent needs (every step? key decisions only?).\n    - Implement trail storage that survives across sessions if work spans multiple runs.\n    - Add trail-query primitives: \"have I been here?\", \"how did I get here?\", \"what failed last time?\"\n    - Add fulab trail query helpers (CLI or HUD) to fetch prior decisions during resume flows."}], :conclusion "Leave traces that allow backtracking; an agent that can't retrace its path can't recover from mistakes.", :directives {:audience "agent architects, workflow designers, futon developers", :sigils ["🚞/归"], :style "pattern", :title "Trail Enables Return", :tone "foundational"}, :id "agent/trail-enables-return", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🚞/归"], :source-path "library/agent/trail-enables-return.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Trail Enables Return", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  AI is most useful in this project as a probe for surfacing candidate\n  mid-level reasoning cues already present in mathematical exposition, not\n  as a generator of new proofs."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are defining the specific role AI should play in the AI4CI pipeline so it augments human annotation without inventing proofs."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    Worked mathematical solutions already mix formal derivations with\n    explanatory phrases that gesture toward motivations, subgoals, and\n    tactical decisions, and prior HDM patterns show that capturing such\n    outline-level structures keeps reasoning legible to humans and systems."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    These informal cues are not systematically linked to specific steps or\n    objects; without assistance, annotators must rediscover every linkage by\n    hand, and without structure, downstream tools receive only cleaned\n    events with little reusable explanation."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Use existing AI models strictly to propose candidate cue–step links and\n    outline-plus-informal alignments, attach provenance to each proposal,\n    and pass them to human reviewers for acceptance, editing, or rejection\n    before anything is treated as explanatory structure."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    This deployment keeps AI in the role of suggestion engine while ensuring\n    that every retained explanation has a traceable record and a human\n    steward, turning dispersed expository text into candidate reasoning\n    scaffolds that future training-oriented AI could build on."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Stand up the human-in-the-loop annotation pilot that uses AI to propose cue–step links and records reviewer decisions as structured provenance.]\n    next[Publish the acceptance/rejection log into the evidence ledger so other layers can see which AI proposals were trusted and why.]"}], :conclusion "  AI is most useful in this project as a probe for surfacing candidate\n  mid-level reasoning cues already present in mathematical exposition, not\n  as a generator of new proofs.", :directives {:audience "EPSRC reviewers", :factor "Keen investigation (dhammavicaya)", :references ["hdm/outline-first-detail-later" "hdm/human-machine-dialogue" "stack-coherence/evidence-ledger" "library/ai4ci/project-summary-core"], :sigils ["👯/未" "🗿/二"], :title "AI Incorporation — Core Function", :tone "plain-analytic"}, :id "ai4ci/ai-core-function", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["hdm/outline-first-detail-later" "hdm/human-machine-dialogue" "stack-coherence/evidence-ledger" "library/ai4ci/project-summary-core"], :scores {}, :sigils ["👯/未" "🗿/二"], :source-path "library/ai4ci/ai-core-function.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "AI Incorporation — Core Function", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Human validation of AI-proposed reasoning cues is required to identify\n  genuine structure and avoid false signals; the project treats AI as an\n  experiment whose outputs are graded and logged, not as an automatic\n  source of truth."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are defining the methodological guardrails that keep AI proposals subject to human validation and traceable review."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The HDM canon already assumes that outline-level proposals are only\n    trustworthy when human stewards can interrogate them and decide what\n    stands, what changes, and what is discarded."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    If AI-generated segmentations and glosses are used without explicit\n    records of where they were kept, edited, or rejected, the workflow\n    cannot demonstrate that it is learning anything about the boundary\n    between helpful and misleading explanations."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Use AI for first-pass segmentation and paraphrase, route each proposal\n    through a ready/blocked-style decision record (accepted, revised,\n    discarded), and treat staleness scans as alarms when drafts linger\n    without review."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Methodological validity in this setting depends on showing that human\n    judgement governs every explanation and that the resulting log reveals\n    which explanation types AI handles reliably and which remain beyond its\n    current competence."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Publish a reviewer rota and decision rubric so every AI-assisted explanation is triaged within a fixed window (e.g. 48 hours).]\n    next[Wire review outcomes into stack-coherence/staleness-scan so unattended drafts raise visible alarms and cannot silently accumulate.]"}], :conclusion "  Human validation of AI-proposed reasoning cues is required to identify\n  genuine structure and avoid false signals; the project treats AI as an\n  experiment whose outputs are graded and logged, not as an automatic\n  source of truth.", :directives {:audience "EPSRC reviewers", :factor "Keen investigation (dhammavicaya)", :references ["hdm/outline-first-detail-later" "hdm/human-machine-dialogue" "stack-coherence/ready-blocked-triage" "stack-coherence/staleness-scan"], :sigils ["👯/未" "🔦/公"], :title "AI Incorporation — Methodological Role", :tone "plain-analytic"}, :id "ai4ci/ai-method-role", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["hdm/outline-first-detail-later" "hdm/human-machine-dialogue" "stack-coherence/ready-blocked-triage" "stack-coherence/staleness-scan"], :scores {}, :sigils ["👯/未" "🔦/公"], :source-path "library/ai4ci/ai-method-role.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "AI Incorporation — Methodological Role", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  The usefulness of extracted reasoning cues must be tested through\n  collective understanding rather than model accuracy, because the target\n  outcome is shared clarity among diverse learners."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are defining evaluation in terms of shared clarity across cohorts rather than model accuracy."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    Value-flow and festival patterns show that gatherings enable collective\n    sense-making only when participants can relate their own understanding\n    to a visible shared artefact."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Standard AI metrics (e.g. step-wise correctness) say little about\n    whether undergraduates, postgraduates, and professionals can actually\n    use the annotated proofs to reason together; they hide where explanations\n    help, where they confuse, and where they leave gaps."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Run tiered seminars in which identical annotated proofs are reviewed by\n    different cohorts, log guided discussions using consistent prompts, and\n    treat clarity/confusion reports as primary evaluation signals, feeding\n    them back into ready/blocked-style triage so blockers are explicit."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Collective intelligence is evidenced when diverse readers reach a\n    workable shared understanding of the argument; only structured\n    discussion around common artefacts can reveal whether the extracted\n    reasoning cues support this outcome."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Schedule undergraduate, graduate, and professional seminar sessions using the same annotated proofs and capture transcripts for comparison.]\n    next[Pipe transcript-coded blockers into stack-coherence/ready-blocked-triage so explanatory weaknesses are visible to the whole project.]"}], :conclusion "  The usefulness of extracted reasoning cues must be tested through\n  collective understanding rather than model accuracy, because the target\n  outcome is shared clarity among diverse learners.", :directives {:audience "EPSRC reviewers", :factor "Keen investigation (dhammavicaya)", :references ["vsatlas/value-flow-constellation" "vsatlas/festival-model" "stack-coherence/ready-blocked-triage" "stack-coherence/stack-blocker-detection"], :sigils ["🎋/上" "🌄/工"], :title "Collective Intelligence — Evaluative Lens", :tone "plain-analytic"}, :id "ai4ci/collective-intelligence-evaluation", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["vsatlas/value-flow-constellation" "vsatlas/festival-model" "stack-coherence/ready-blocked-triage" "stack-coherence/stack-blocker-detection"], :scores {}, :sigils ["🎋/上" "🌄/工"], :source-path "library/ai4ci/collective-intelligence-evaluation.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Collective Intelligence — Evaluative Lens", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Shared reasoning scaffolds are essential because they allow diverse\n  learners to inspect, question, and refine mid-level reasoning cues\n  together, rather than relying on individual, opaque interpretations."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are explaining why shared artefacts are needed to test reasoning cues across cohorts."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    Peeragogical-infrastructure and “working where others can see” patterns\n    already show that collective reasoning improves when intermediate work\n    surfaces are visible and linked to concrete evidence."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Typical mathematical materials provide either a polished proof or an\n    informal commentary, not a structured artefact that makes the connection\n    between steps and explanatory cues explicit across cohorts."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Package dual-layer proofs (formal outline plus linked informal cues)\n    into templated seminar kits that include discussion prompts for different\n    levels and a simple evidence trail back to the underlying texts, reusing\n    existing ledger and bridge patterns instead of inventing new governance."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Collective intelligence depends on inspectable, cross-referenced\n    intermediate artefacts; without them, seminars slide back into folklore\n    and private interpretations instead of testing and refining the shared\n    reasoning layer."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Draft the cross-level seminar kit that presents outline-plus-cue structures and maps each cue to one or more discussion prompts.]\n    next[Integrate evidence-ledger entries so every shared artefact points back to source texts and annotations before piloting with students.]"}], :conclusion "  Shared reasoning scaffolds are essential because they allow diverse\n  learners to inspect, question, and refine mid-level reasoning cues\n  together, rather than relying on individual, opaque interpretations.", :directives {:audience "EPSRC reviewers", :factor "Keen investigation (dhammavicaya)", :references ["vsatlas/peeragogical-infrastructures" "corps/working-where-others-can-see" "stack-coherence/evidence-ledger" "devmap-coherence/prototype-alignment-bridge"], :sigils ["🌱/仁" "💬/白"], :title "Collective Intelligence — Communication Framing", :tone "plain-analytic"}, :id "ai4ci/collective-intelligence-framing", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["vsatlas/peeragogical-infrastructures" "corps/working-where-others-can-see" "stack-coherence/evidence-ledger" "devmap-coherence/prototype-alignment-bridge"], :scores {}, :sigils ["🌱/仁" "💬/白"], :source-path "library/ai4ci/collective-intelligence-framing.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Collective Intelligence — Communication Framing", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Dual-layer proof patterns are a feasibility question: can formal steps and\n  informal cues be captured consistently enough to matter?"} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Mathematical proofs contain both formal steps and informal cues\n    (exploratory and expository), but only the former typically survive into\n    print. Contemporary AI tools can identify symbols yet struggle to recover\n    motivations."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    We collect a modest corpus of worked graduate-level solutions and examine\n    both their formal expressions and the informal commentary surrounding each\n    step."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Off-the-shelf models misidentify or omit exploratory cues, confuse motivation\n    with justification, and cannot reliably map informal language to the exact\n    mathematical objects or subgoals referenced; without human oversight, outputs\n    drift or hallucinate."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Use AI only as a draft assistant whose every suggestion is logged, accepted,\n    edited, or rejected by a human annotator; link cues to specific steps and\n    objects; and run structured seminars across learner cohorts to test whether\n    these annotated proofs genuinely support understanding."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Human-validated cue logs and coded seminar transcripts provide evidence—not\n    speculation—about which pattern fragments are stable, which are fragile, and\n    what methodological constraints govern scalability."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Characterise which cue types are consistently recognisable; evaluate where learner groups converge or diverge in what helps them reconstruct a proof; define measurable failure modes; and specify what infrastructural or methodological changes would be required for a full-scale study.]"}], :conclusion "  Dual-layer proof patterns are a feasibility question: can formal steps and\n  informal cues be captured consistently enough to matter?", :directives {:audience "EPSRC reviewers", :sigils ["💬/二" "🌀/今"], :title "Dual-Layer Proof Patterns as a Feasibility Question", :tone "plain-analytic"}, :id "ai4ci/dual-layer-proof-patterns", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["💬/二" "🌀/今"], :source-path "library/ai4ci/dual-layer-proof-patterns.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Dual-Layer Proof Patterns as a Feasibility Question", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  A sub-£50k request is proportional because the work is confined to\n  methodological evaluation—annotation, AI-assisted suggestion and human\n  review, and seminars—built on existing infrastructure rather than new\n  platforms."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are justifying the budget scale by tying spend to concrete, auditable pilot activities."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    Job-argument and transformation-engine patterns already describe how to\n    cost short bursts of research that leverage existing institutional and\n    technical capacity."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Without a clear ledger of what the budget buys in terms of specific\n    activities (specialist annotation, light AI usage, seminar facilitation),\n    the request can look speculative or infrastructure-heavy."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Itemise costs around part-time researchers, limited compute, and seminar\n    delivery, and explicitly cite existing institutional infrastructure as\n    in-kind support rather than a target for new investment."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Reviewers need to see that the funds unlock a focused test of the\n    reasoning-layer hypothesis, not an open-ended attempt to build a new\n    platform."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Draft the budget sheet that links each cost line to a concrete artefact (annotation hours, seminar sessions, reporting) and cite it in the EoI.]\n    next[Attach evidence-ledger references so the use of funds can be audited post-award.]"}], :conclusion "  A sub-£50k request is proportional because the work is confined to\n  methodological evaluation—annotation, AI-assisted suggestion and human\n  review, and seminars—built on existing infrastructure rather than new\n  platforms.", :directives {:audience "EPSRC reviewers", :factor "Keen investigation (dhammavicaya)", :references ["repository-transition/job-argument" "repository-transition/transformation-engine" "stack-coherence/evidence-ledger"], :sigils ["🎎/匀" "👭/习"], :title "Funding Justification — Rapid-Response Pilot Scale", :tone "plain-analytic"}, :id "ai4ci/funding-justification", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["repository-transition/job-argument" "repository-transition/transformation-engine" "stack-coherence/evidence-ledger"], :scores {}, :sigils ["🎎/匀" "👭/习"], :source-path "library/ai4ci/funding-justification.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Funding Justification — Rapid-Response Pilot Scale", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  A six-month evaluation is feasible because the project is deliberately\n  scoped to test whether mid-level reasoning cues can be extracted and\n  validated, not to build new AI systems or long-term infrastructure."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are defending the six-month timeline by tying scope to specific, trackable activities."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    Repository-transition invariants and stack-blocker detection already\n    describe what infrastructure exists and how to keep scope aligned with\n    available capacity."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Without explicit scope fences, a project framed around “AI and\n    explanation” can drift into trying to develop storage, user interfaces,\n    or new models, making the schedule unrealistic."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Freeze scope to: proof selection, dual-layer annotation (AI proposals\n    plus human review), seminar-based evaluation, and reporting; track each\n    clause with explicit next-steps so diverging work is visible as scope\n    creep."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Dhammavicaya (keen investigation) in this context demands evidence that\n    the pilot answers a clear methodological question—whether a recoverable\n    reasoning layer exists—rather than attempting production-grade systems."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Map the proof sample, annotation sprints, and seminars onto a simple timeline and publish it in the devmap.]\n    next[Record blockers and clears weekly so stack-coherence patterns can demonstrate that the pilot stayed within its declared scope.]"}], :conclusion "  A six-month evaluation is feasible because the project is deliberately\n  scoped to test whether mid-level reasoning cues can be extracted and\n  validated, not to build new AI systems or long-term infrastructure.", :directives {:audience "EPSRC reviewers", :factor "Keen investigation (dhammavicaya)", :references ["repository-transition/invariants" "stack-coherence/stack-blocker-detection" "devmap-coherence/next-steps-to-done"], :sigils ["👭/六" "📐/工"], :title "Feasibility — Six-Month Pilot Discipline", :tone "plain-analytic"}, :id "ai4ci/pilot-feasibility", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["repository-transition/invariants" "stack-coherence/stack-blocker-detection" "devmap-coherence/next-steps-to-done"], :scores {}, :sigils ["👭/六" "📐/工"], :source-path "library/ai4ci/pilot-feasibility.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Feasibility — Six-Month Pilot Discipline", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Recognise that the project studies two complementary sources of reasoning\n  information in mathematical texts: the exploratory process signals that hint\n  at how a proof was found, and the expository signals intentionally placed in\n  published work to help readers reconstruct the logic."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are clarifying the two evidence sources the pilot will annotate and compare."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    Human understanding of mathematics relies on both the implicit heuristics\n    behind a proof (\"try this\", \"by analogy\") and the explicit narrative cues\n    authors include in published solutions (\"note that\", \"it is natural to\n    consider\")."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Process cues are often partial or suppressed, while expository cues are\n    fragmentary and uneven across texts; neither source alone provides a stable\n    representation of the reasoning structure that learners reconstruct."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Analyse both kinds of cues: use baseline entity and relation extraction to\n    anchor formal steps, and annotate how each informal phrase—whether exploratory\n    or expository—links to specific objects, steps, or subgoals in the proof."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Process cues reveal the reasoning that originally generated the proof, while\n    expository cues reveal how experts communicate that reasoning to others; the\n    combination provides the richest available evidence for reconstructing\n    mid-level structure without requiring access to the original scratch work."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Distinguish and label exploratory vs. expository cues in the annotated dataset.]\n    next[Test with seminar participants how each type supports understanding.]\n    next[Report which signals form reliable scaffolds for mathematical explanation.]"}], :conclusion "  Recognise that the project studies two complementary sources of reasoning\n  information in mathematical texts: the exploratory process signals that hint\n  at how a proof was found, and the expository signals intentionally placed in\n  published work to help readers reconstruct the logic.", :directives {:audience "EPSRC reviewers", :sigils ["🍂/才" "➰/了"], :title "Process and Exposition as Complementary Evidence", :tone "plain-analytic"}, :id "ai4ci/process-vs-exposition", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🍂/才" "➰/了"], :source-path "library/ai4ci/process-vs-exposition.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Process and Exposition as Complementary Evidence", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  The project’s core contribution is to test, in a tightly scoped pilot,\n  whether the informal, mid-level reasoning moves inside mathematical\n  solutions can be made explicit, validated, and shown to help different\n  cohorts understand proofs."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are summarizing the pilot’s core claim and the minimum evidence needed to validate it."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    AI systems currently train on polished proofs and expositions that omit\n    much of the exploratory reasoning and heuristic guidance that human\n    mathematicians rely on when solving graduate-level problems."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    The remaining informal layers live in the textual wraparound of worked\n    solutions and in oral explanations; extracting their structure requires a\n    disciplined workflow, not just more model capacity—AI proposals, human\n    validation, and collective evaluation must be combined so that evidence\n    about the reasoning layer is explicit."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Deliver a focused pilot that (a) selects a small set of graduate-level\n    worked problems; (b) uses existing tools to propose links between\n    informal phrases and formal steps; (c) has annotators accept, edit, or\n    reject each proposal; and (d) tests the resulting dual-layer proofs in\n    seminars with undergraduates, postgraduates, and professionals to see\n    which cues genuinely support understanding."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Once we know whether a stable mid-level reasoning layer can be captured\n    from exposition—and where it breaks down—we can state clearly what kind\n    of process-level data will be needed for future AI that aims to train\n    humans, and what cannot be obtained from textual traces alone."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Assemble a cleared mini-corpus of graduate-level exam solutions with both symbolic steps and expository text, coordinating with data stewards for release notes.]\n    next[Specify the annotation protocol (including AI proposal settings and review rubric) and register it in the evidence ledger before piloting.]\n    next[Design the seminar prompts and coding scheme so clarity/confusion signals can be fed back into future iterations.]"}], :conclusion "  The project’s core contribution is to test, in a tightly scoped pilot,\n  whether the informal, mid-level reasoning moves inside mathematical\n  solutions can be made explicit, validated, and shown to help different\n  cohorts understand proofs.", :directives {:audience "EPSRC reviewers", :factor "Keen investigation (dhammavicaya)", :references ["hdm/outline-first-detail-later" "hdm/human-machine-dialogue" "devmap-coherence/prototype-alignment-bridge" "stack-coherence/evidence-ledger" "stack-coherence/ready-blocked-triage" "stack-coherence/stack-blocker-detection" "devmap-coherence/next-steps-to-done"], :sigils ["👭/才" "➰/了"], :title "AI4CI — Mid-Level Reasoning Pilot", :tone "plain-analytic"}, :id "ai4ci/project-summary-core", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["hdm/outline-first-detail-later" "hdm/human-machine-dialogue" "devmap-coherence/prototype-alignment-bridge" "stack-coherence/evidence-ledger" "stack-coherence/ready-blocked-triage" "stack-coherence/stack-blocker-detection" "devmap-coherence/next-steps-to-done"], :scores {}, :sigils ["👭/才" "➰/了"], :source-path "library/ai4ci/project-summary-core.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "AI4CI — Mid-Level Reasoning Pilot", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Recognise that the project treats proof patterns as a hypothesised mid-level\n  hierarchy, analogous to the hierarchy of mathematical concepts, and uses a\n  small, controlled study to see whether local fragments of this hierarchy can\n  be made visible."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are framing proof patterns as a mid-level hierarchy the pilot can sample in a controlled way."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    Mathematicians rely on recurring tactics—\"reduce to a simpler case\", \"apply\n    a standard theorem\", \"argue by symmetry\"—that function like proof patterns:\n    reusable structures sitting between informal heuristics and fully formal\n    derivations."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    This implicit hierarchy of proof patterns has never been systematically\n    mapped, and a six-month pilot cannot construct it in full. Existing texts\n    only show traces of these patterns, mixed with both exploratory and\n    expository language."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Use a gain-of-function style approach: focus on a small, curated set of\n    worked proofs, amplify the strongest informal and expository cues, and see\n    whether any stable, recognisable pattern fragments can be annotated\n    consistently across examples."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    If such fragments can be identified and linked to specific objects and\n    steps, they provide the first empirical foothold on an underlying proof-\n    pattern hierarchy, even if only at a local scale. If they cannot, this\n    reveals real limits on what present methods can recover."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Treat recurring tactic descriptors that survive annotation as candidate proof-pattern atoms.]\n    next[Report how often they appear, where they fail, and what cues were discarded.]\n    next[Use findings to guide future, larger-scale work on proof-pattern structure.]"}], :conclusion "  Recognise that the project treats proof patterns as a hypothesised mid-level\n  hierarchy, analogous to the hierarchy of mathematical concepts, and uses a\n  small, controlled study to see whether local fragments of this hierarchy can\n  be made visible.", :directives {:audience "EPSRC reviewers", :sigils ["🔺/今" "🌏/二"], :title "Proof Patterns as a Hypothesised Mid-Level Hierarchy", :tone "plain-analytic"}, :id "ai4ci/proof-pattern-hierarchy", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🔺/今" "🌏/二"], :source-path "library/ai4ci/proof-pattern-hierarchy.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Proof Patterns as a Hypothesised Mid-Level Hierarchy", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Understand that the project addresses a gap untouched by current mathematical\n  AI systems: not solving problems, but exposing the reasoning cues—both\n  exploratory and expository—that humans rely on to understand mathematical work."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are distinguishing AI4CI from current SOTA by focusing on explanatory cues rather than solution accuracy."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    State-of-the-art models such as ChatGPT or AlphaGeometry can often produce\n    plausible solutions to textbook or competition-style problems, showing strong\n    pattern-matching and symbolic fluency on polished inputs."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    These systems do not reliably check their own work, cannot explain their\n    reasoning at the level needed for teaching, and fail to produce stable links\n    between informal phrases and the specific steps or objects they reference.\n    Their outputs also lack insight into how humans reconstruct proofs from\n    published exposition."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Treat problem-solving capability as separate from explanatory capability, and\n    focus the project on analysing how existing proofs communicate reasoning:\n    what exploratory traces remain, what expository cues guide the reader, and\n    whether these signals can be structurally annotated."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Explanation and understanding depend on reconstructing intermediate\n    motivations and relationships, not on producing correct final answers.\n    Current SOTA models offer limited guidance here, leaving a gap that this\n    pilot is uniquely positioned to explore."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Use AI only as a draft annotator for linking informal cues to symbolic steps.]\n    next[Contrast human vs. model judgement on explanation quality.]\n    next[Report where explanatory capability breaks down despite strong problem-solving.]"}], :conclusion "  Understand that the project addresses a gap untouched by current mathematical\n  AI systems: not solving problems, but exposing the reasoning cues—both\n  exploratory and expository—that humans rely on to understand mathematical work.", :directives {:audience "EPSRC reviewers", :sigils ["🎋/未" "👯/上"], :title "Distinguishing This Project from Current Mathematical AI SOTA", :tone "plain-analytic"}, :id "ai4ci/sota-distinction", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🎋/未" "👯/上"], :source-path "library/ai4ci/sota-distinction.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Distinguishing This Project from Current Mathematical AI SOTA", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Existing math-aware NLP tools can anchor the extraction of reasoning cues\n  by identifying objects and relations, but they cannot determine which\n  informal phrases convey tactic or motivation without human judgement."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are grounding the pipeline in existing NER/RE outputs while clarifying what still requires human review."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    Baseline tools can already parse mathematical text, tag entities and\n    relations, and suggest step boundaries that point to where explanations\n    might attach."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    These tools only indicate where symbols occur and how they relate; they\n    do not by themselves distinguish between phrases that encode genuine\n    reasoning moves and those that merely narrate, summarise, or smooth the\n    exposition."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Treat NER/RE outputs and step boundaries as anchors, cluster recurring\n    informal phrases around them, and subject resulting “reasoning cue”\n    hypotheses to human review rather than accepting model guesses as\n    structure."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    This isolates the real research question—whether human-recognisable\n    reasoning structure can be recovered from exposition—from the underlying\n    engineering, avoiding overclaim about what current NLP can deliver on its\n    own."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Prototype the anchor-plus-cue method on a small proof sample and record success/failure for each hypothesised reasoning cue.]\n    next[Document how rejected cues were generated and reviewed so future work can refine both the NLP components and the human protocol without changing the underlying infrastructure.]"}], :conclusion "  Existing math-aware NLP tools can anchor the extraction of reasoning cues\n  by identifying objects and relations, but they cannot determine which\n  informal phrases convey tactic or motivation without human judgement.", :directives {:audience "EPSRC reviewers", :factor "Keen investigation (dhammavicaya)", :references ["docs/protocol.md" "src/f2/router.clj" "library/ai4ci/project-summary-core"], :sigils ["👴/了" "💢/申"], :title "Technical Grounding — From NER/RE to Informal Structure", :tone "plain-analytic"}, :id "ai4ci/technical-grounding", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["docs/protocol.md" "src/f2/router.clj" "library/ai4ci/project-summary-core"], :scores {}, :sigils ["👴/了" "💢/申"], :source-path "library/ai4ci/technical-grounding.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Technical Grounding — From NER/RE to Informal Structure", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Future AI can and should be built that can train humans in cognitively\n  intense skills, which requires an explicit, structured account of the\n  mid-level reasoning cues humans already use to understand complex work."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are framing the long-term AI4CI vision around explicit reasoning cues that support human learning."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    Contemporary AI systems can solve many formal problems in domains like\n    mathematics, but they typically learn from polished outcomes rather than\n    from the reasoning processes that human learners depend on."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    These processes do not appear as full interaction logs; they survive\n    mainly in the expository layer as informal hints, motivations, and\n    tactic-describing phrases. We do not yet know whether these traces can\n    be systematically identified, linked to precise steps, and represented\n    as a stable structure that generalises across problems and cohorts."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Treat expository traces as candidates for an underlying reasoning layer\n    and design a principled way to surface, examine, and test them, combining\n    AI proposals, human validation, and collective evaluation."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Only by making this tacit reasoning layer explicit can we both deepen our\n    scientific understanding of how humans communicate complex thinking and\n    provide the process-level structures that future AI will need in order to\n    help people build expertise rather than merely reproduce correct answers."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Develop and test a constrained workflow in mathematics that extracts, validates, and evaluates mid-level reasoning cues, and use the results to state clearly whether a recoverable “reasoning layer” exists that AI could train on.]"}], :conclusion "  Future AI can and should be built that can train humans in cognitively\n  intense skills, which requires an explicit, structured account of the\n  mid-level reasoning cues humans already use to understand complex work.", :directives {:audience "EPSRC reviewers", :sigils ["🏃/支" "🌞/功"], :title "AI4CI — Human-Centred Future of AI", :tone "plain-analytic"}, :id "ai4ci/umbrella", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🏃/支" "🌞/功"], :source-path "library/ai4ci/umbrella.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "AI4CI — Human-Centred Future of AI", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "In a self-modifying inference loop, gate each action CLASS with a per-class admissibility predicate (`can-propose?` / `action-admissible?`) checked BEFORE ranking. That predicate — not an ad-hoc loop guard — is the cycle-stopper that keeps self-modification recursion-safe."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "An AIF agent that can propose actions which modify its own action space, inventory, or goal (self-reference): extend the typed-edge inventory, deform the goal-structure, pursue a capability not on its pre-registered ascent."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The agent's action space includes self-modifying moves — actions that re-author the agent rather than act within its fixed niche."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Without a principled gate, self-modifying actions compose into runaway recursion: the agent expands its own powers, which expands what it can propose, unboundedly (\"ants starting parliaments\"). The reflex fix — bolting per-situation loop guards onto the ranker — is fragile, un-auditable, and multiplies with every new action class."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Define a per-class admissibility predicate and apply it BEFORE expected-free-energy ranking. Un-registered `:pursue`, goal-extending `:decompose`, and unagreed operator-exit advances are **inadmissible** — refused (or NAG'd), never scored. Only admissible classes reach the ranker. The per-class predicate IS the recursion-safety; no separate runaway guard is needed, because the unsafe classes never enter the policy at all."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    \"Water doesn't flow uphill; ants don't start parliaments.\" The EFE goal is bound to the pre-registered ascent; off-goal and self-extending moves are BHK arrows that do not compose into that ascent (task-as-arrow), and the inherited I4 exogeneity (the fast loop cannot force the glacial — structural-tension-as-observation) is what makes the binding hold. Gating at the **pursuit** boundary, not the discovery boundary, is the right cut: discovering an off-ascent capability is fine (it becomes a NAG), pursuing one needs consent. One structural property replaces a scatter of per-action guards."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Order matters: admissibility runs BEFORE ranking; an inadmissible action must never appear in `rank-actions`/`rank-star-map-actions` output.\n    - Pairs with pattern-warranted-choice-point: an admissibility refusal NAGs with the admissibility warrant (which class, why inadmissible, what registration would make it admissible).\n    - Pairs with niche-construction: admissibility is the boundary the endogenous-extension (aif2) direction is permitted to move — and exactly where it must be re-mechanised if it does.\n    - evidence: inadmissible-refusal count by class; any admissibility breach (an inadmissible action that got ranked) is a bug, not a tradeoff.\n    - evidence-shape: {:admissible/refusals {:keyword :int}, :admissibility/breaches :int, :registration-events :int}"} {:name "DOES-NOT-APPLY", :name-key "does-not-apply", :slug "does-not-apply", :text "    Inference loops with a FIXED action space and no self-modifying classes (there is nothing for admissibility to gate — ordinary EFE ranking suffices). Discovery/observation (never gate discovery; gate only pursuit)."} {:name "INSTANCES", :name-key "instances", :slug "instances", :text "    - M-capability-star-map INV-G: the `:cap/pentagon` pursuit is machine-refuted; un-registered `:pursue` + goal-extending `:decompose` are refused before ranking (`futon3c/.../logic/capability_star_map_invariants.clj`; `futon2/.../aif/efe.clj action-admissible?` → `rank-star-map-actions`). \"Water doesn't flow uphill\" enforced in code, not just argued."}], :conclusion "In a self-modifying inference loop, gate each action CLASS with a per-class admissibility predicate (`can-propose?` / `action-admissible?`) checked BEFORE ranking. That predicate — not an ad-hoc loop guard — is the cycle-stopper that keeps self-modification recursion-safe.", :directives {:audience "designers of self-modifying inference loops, AIF engine authors, guardrail authors", :keywords ["admissibility" "can-propose" "recursion-safety" "self-modification" "pre-registered-ascent" "INV-G" "pursuit-boundary"], :references ["aif/niche-construction" "futon-theory/task-as-arrow" "futon-theory/structural-tension-as-observation" "orchestration/pattern-warranted-choice-point"], :sigils ["🛂/↻"], :style "pattern", :title "Admissibility as Recursion-Safety — Gate the Action CLASS Before You Rank", :tone "declarative"}, :id "aif/admissibility", :keywords ["admissibility" "can-propose" "recursion-safety" "self-modification" "pre-registered-ascent" "INV-G" "pursuit-boundary"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["aif/niche-construction" "futon-theory/task-as-arrow" "futon-theory/structural-tension-as-observation" "orchestration/pattern-warranted-choice-point"], :scores {}, :sigils ["🛂/↻"], :source-path "library/aif/admissibility.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Admissibility as Recursion-Safety — Gate the Action CLASS Before You Rank", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Compose the pragmatic (risk) EFE term as bias² (squared deviation of predicted mean from preference prior) + variance (predictive uncertainty contribution). Both terms in the same units; both pulling the agent toward predictable closeness to preference."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "The pragmatic value term in Expected Free Energy is supposed to penalise candidates whose predicted outcomes are far from the agent's preferences. A naïve implementation reads only the predicted mean — but AIF is Bayesian, so the *uncertainty* about whether the predicted mean is right also matters."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    You want the risk term to penalise both:\n      - **bias**: predicted state is far from preferred state (the candidate is unlikely to land near preference)\n      - **variance**: high predictive uncertainty about where the state will land (even if the predicted mean is at preference, the agent isn't confident it'll get there)\n    A candidate with low bias and low variance is preferred over a candidate with low bias and high variance, all else equal."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    A risk term that uses only `(mean - prior)²` ignores predictive uncertainty. Two candidates with identical means but different variances rank equal under bias-only risk — which is wrong: the high-variance candidate carries higher operational risk of missing preference even though it has the same expected outcome."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Compute the risk term per institution (or per state-instance) as:\n      `risk[i] = (mean-D[i] - prior-D)² + (mean-A[i] - prior-A)² + var-D[i] + var-A[i]`\n    Both the squared-deviation (bias²) and the predictive variance contribute additively to the per-instance risk. Average over instances in the band for the band-level risk-term. Configurable priors per opts."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Under a Gaussian observation likelihood, the expected squared deviation from preference is `bias² + variance` (the variance-bias decomposition). Including both terms makes the risk match the canonical pragmatic value in AIF. Including only bias is a common implementation shortcut that produces a non-Bayesian agent."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Confirm your forward model returns predicted variance, not just predicted mean. If only mean is available, you can only compute the bias half — and the agent is not properly belief-aware in its risk scoring.\n    - Read predicted (mean, variance) per state-instance from the forward-model output; compose the per-instance risk as `Σ ((mean_k - prior_k)² + var_k)` over coordinates k.\n    - Average over instances in the relevant scope (band, cohort, agent's responsibility area). Comparable across candidates if the scope is fixed.\n    - Document the priors: which coordinate has which preference value, why, where it lives in config. Priors should be configurable per opts, not hardcoded.\n    - DONE (UKRN v3 HO-05, 2026-05-14): risk-term-v3 implemented at notebooks/ukrn_v3_efe.clj:42-58. Uses both (D, A) coordinates; reads predicted variance from HO-04's forward model; PRIOR-D = PRIOR-A = 0.85 with opts override. Test risk-term-v3-has-prior-and-variance-properties verifies the variance contribution is additive. Closes the materially-incomplete-policy-score finding from codex-6's review of the UKRN scoping note. Evidence: handoffs/v3_runner/validation_HO-05.md.\n    - Practice: ensure the prior is reachable. A prior of (1.0, 1.0) on a coordinate that asymptotes below 1.0 produces a permanent positive bias contribution that nothing can drive to zero — that's interpretable but should be deliberate. Calibrate the prior to a reachable preference, not an aspirational ceiling, unless you specifically want the agent to be perpetually striving."}], :conclusion "Compose the pragmatic (risk) EFE term as bias² (squared deviation of predicted mean from preference prior) + variance (predictive uncertainty contribution). Both terms in the same units; both pulling the agent toward predictable closeness to preference.", :directives {:audience "fulab agents, aif implementers, designers of EFE term implementations", :sigils ["🎯/双"], :style "pattern", :title "Risk Term as Squared Deviation Plus Predictive Variance", :tone "technical"}, :id "aif/belief-aware-risk-term", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🎯/双"], :source-path "library/aif/belief-aware-risk-term.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Risk Term as Squared Deviation Plus Predictive Variance", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Maintain a compact belief map of operational hypotheses that is updated each step."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "The agent needs an internal state about what it is doing and why, beyond the raw trace."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    You want decisions to depend on tractable hypotheses (progress, blockers, pattern fit, uncertainty) rather than raw conversation history."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    If the belief state is just the conversation, the agent cannot cleanly update, compare, or falsify its own assumptions."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Represent (mu) as a belief map of operational hypotheses (for example: :goal, :subgoals, :current-hypothesis, :blockers, :pattern-fit, :expected-next-observation, :uncertainty), updated at each step using observation and outcomes."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    A compact belief state makes predict -> observe -> update implementable and testable."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Define :aif/belief-state schema in tatami_schema.clj with keys [:goal :subgoals :current-hypothesis :blockers :pattern-fit :expected-next-observation :uncertainty].\n    - Extend aif_bridge/aif-step->event to validate mu against the schema before emitting :aif/tick events.\n    - Add (mu-diff prev-mu next-mu) helper and log diffs as :aif/belief-delta events in pattern_competence.clj.\n    - DONE (UKRN v3 HO-01, 2026-05-14): per-institution BeliefState schema with mean+variance per coordinate, named handle (not inlined), centralised initialisation via initial-belief-state. Evidence: notebooks/ukrn_v2_contracts.clj:22-30; notebooks/ukrn_v2_substrate.clj:286-298. Validation: handoffs/v3_runner/validation_HO-01.md.\n    - DONE (UKRN v3 HO-01): trajectory-CSV emission of belief means and variances per tick, enabling visible belief evolution in the output stream. Evidence: notebooks/ukrn_v2_runner.clj:110-140.\n    - Practice refinement: prefer a *named schema handle* (e.g. BeliefState) over inlining belief shape inside agent/instance schemas — keeps the schema stable across update-rule changes and reusable by predictive-forward-model code. Surfaced 2026-05-14 in HO-01 review.\n    - Practice refinement: centralise initialisation in a single helper (e.g. initial-belief-state) rather than duplicating tick-0 values across constructors. Surfaced 2026-05-14 in HO-01 review."}], :conclusion "Maintain a compact belief map of operational hypotheses that is updated each step.", :directives {:audience "fulab agents, aif implementers", :sigils ["〰️/双"], :style "pattern", :title "Belief State as Operational Hypotheses", :tone "technical"}, :id "aif/belief-state-operational-hypotheses", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["〰️/双"], :source-path "library/aif/belief-state-operational-hypotheses.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Belief State as Operational Hypotheses", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Treat action selection as choosing from a bounded candidate set of pattern IDs."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Action selection in fulab often means selecting (and committing to) a pattern to apply next."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    You want pattern choice to be legible and comparable, and you want to avoid collapse into one habitual pattern."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    If the action space is any pattern at any time, evaluation becomes unstructured and selection becomes arbitrary or dominated by recency."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Define (a) as a constructed candidate set of pattern IDs produced by retrieval plus gating rules, with bounded size and explicit reasons for inclusion and exclusion."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Constraining the action space makes downstream scoring meaningful rather than performative."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Add :aif/candidate-entry and :aif/candidate-chosen event types to tatami_schema.clj.\n    - In learn_or_act/expand-hints, emit :aif/candidate-entry for each pattern in :search/candidates.\n    - In learn_or_act/decide success path, emit :aif/candidate-chosen with pattern-id and runner stats.\n    - Create aif_coverage.clj with (coverage-report session-id) that computes entered-vs-chosen ratios per pattern."}], :conclusion "Treat action selection as choosing from a bounded candidate set of pattern IDs.", :directives {:audience "fulab agents, aif implementers", :sigils ["📥/力"], :style "pattern", :title "Candidate Pattern Action Space", :tone "technical"}, :id "aif/candidate-pattern-action-space", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["📥/力"], :source-path "library/aif/candidate-pattern-action-space.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Candidate Pattern Action Space", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Compose predictive-noise variance from named, domain-meaningful contributing sources, not from a single opaque σ². Each source maps to a structural feature of the candidate action."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "The Gaussian noise wrapper around a deterministic forward-model kernel needs to assign variance to its predictions. A single hardcoded σ² is operationally inflexible and analytically opaque — there's no way to ask *why* this candidate has high predictive uncertainty."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    You want predictive noise to be tunable (each source has a calibration constant), auditable (per-candidate variance decomposition emitted in the trace), and operationally meaningful (the agent's uncertainty about a candidate reflects identifiable structural features of that candidate)."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    A single σ² hardcoded into the noise wrap conflates multiple sources of uncertainty into one knob. Tuning becomes guesswork (which knob should I turn?); diagnostics become impossible (was the high G ambiguity from missing capabilities, off-target facet emphasis, or mode-boundary proximity?)."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Decompose the predictive noise σ² into named additive contributions, each driven by a structural feature of the candidate:\n      - Coverage uncertainty: σ² += k_coverage × (1 − fraction-of-capability-portfolio-online). Less of the agent's capability stack is firing → more uncertainty about how the candidate's effects propagate.\n      - Specificity uncertainty: σ² += k_specificity × (1 − facet-emphasis-coverage). The candidate emphasises a narrow vs broad facet of the observation space → off-facet effects less predictable when the emphasis is narrow.\n      - Boundary uncertainty: σ² += k_boundary × 𝟙[predicted-state near classification-mode-boundary]. Near a regime-change threshold, small noise produces large mode-class differences → predictive uncertainty about *what cell the agent ends up in* is high even when uncertainty about (state-coordinates) is low.\n    Each k_* is a configurable constant. Initial values are calibrated against intuitive cases; validation harness can refine via sensitivity sweeps."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Named contributions give every variance increment a domain-meaningful story. Auditing a high-G candidate becomes \"this candidate's variance is dominated by coverage uncertainty, because most of the capability portfolio is offline\"; tuning becomes \"the coverage term is over-weighted, drop k_coverage from 0.01 to 0.005\". A single opaque σ² admits neither."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Identify the 2–4 structural features of your candidate action space that should contribute to predictive uncertainty.\n    - Define a configuration map of k_* constants; thread through the forward model's opts argument.\n    - Emit the variance decomposition (per-source contributions) in the predictive trace alongside the total variance. The decomposition is what diagnostic UIs and validation harnesses consume.\n    - Calibrate: pick initial k_* values that produce intuitive orderings on a handful of test cases. Sweep in validation if outputs aren't behaving.\n    - DONE (UKRN v3 HO-04, 2026-05-14): three-source decomposition implemented in notebooks/ukrn_v3_forward.clj prediction-noise-variance (lines 91-120). Sources: capability portfolio fraction, facet emphasis coverage, mode-threshold proximity. Constants: DEFAULT-K-CAP=0.01, DEFAULT-K-FACET=0.005, DEFAULT-K-THRESHOLD=0.02, DEFAULT-THRESHOLD-EPSILON=0.05. Smoke output shows the threshold-proximity term operationally meaningful: SYN2 with predicted D=0.519 (within 0.05 of threshold 0.5) gets variance 0.029, producing a bimodal mode-probability distribution (50.8% absorbed + 42.4% mismatch) that correctly reflects regime-change ambiguity. Evidence: handoffs/v3_runner/validation_HO-04.md.\n    - Practice: emit the decomposition keys alongside the total variance in the trace record. Consumers (validation harness, UI panels, EFE term computations) can read whichever component they need."}], :conclusion "Compose predictive-noise variance from named, domain-meaningful contributing sources, not from a single opaque σ². Each source maps to a structural feature of the candidate action.", :directives {:audience "fulab agents, aif implementers, designers of predictive forward models", :sigils ["🎨/由"], :style "pattern", :title "Decomposed Prediction Noise as Named Variance Contributions", :tone "technical"}, :id "aif/decomposed-prediction-noise", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🎨/由"], :source-path "library/aif/decomposed-prediction-noise.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Decomposed Prediction Noise as Named Variance Contributions", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Make evidence weighting explicit by logging a precision registry per observation channel."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "The agent must treat some inputs as more reliable than others (tests vs narrative claims, trace anchors vs inferred intent)."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    You want the agent to discount low-trust evidence without discarding it, and to become stricter when outcomes are volatile."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Without an explicit precision layer, evidence weighting becomes implicit prompt rhetoric and cannot be audited."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Represent (Pi_o) as a per-channel precision registry keyed to observation features (and/or evidence sources), and apply it wherever prediction error, scoring, or gating occurs."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Making precision explicit turns trust into a tunable control surface rather than an undocumented bias."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Define :aif/precision-registry schema in tatami_schema.clj with channel keys [:tests :typecheck :static-analysis :tool-output :user-constraints :model-inference].\n    - Add default-precision-registry to pattern_competence.clj with initial weights (e.g., :tests 0.9, :model-inference 0.5).\n    - Emit :aif/precision-adjusted events when check-pur or check-psr modifies channel weights based on anchor resolution failures.\n    - Expose (precision-for channel session) helper for G-term computation in aif_bridge.clj."}], :conclusion "Make evidence weighting explicit by logging a precision registry per observation channel.", :directives {:audience "fulab agents, aif implementers", :sigils ["🗿/由"], :style "pattern", :title "Evidence Precision Registry", :tone "technical"}, :id "aif/evidence-precision-registry", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🗿/由"], :source-path "library/aif/evidence-precision-registry.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Evidence Precision Registry", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Compute expected free energy as named terms with persisted breakdowns, not a single opaque scalar."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "The agent needs a single scalar (G) per candidate pattern, derived from separable considerations."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    You want pattern selection to balance delivery risk, ambiguity, information value, and cost in a way you can tune and audit."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    A single opaque score encourages post-hoc rationalization and makes tuning indistinguishable from storytelling."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Compute G(a) as a weighted sum of named terms (risk, ambiguity, information gain, constraint violation, cost, coordination pressure), and store the term breakdown for each candidate."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    A decomposed G makes disagreements concrete (which term dominated) and enables targeted adjustment."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Define :aif/g-breakdown schema in tatami_schema.clj with term keys [:risk :ambiguity :info-gain :constraint-violation :cost :coordination-pressure].\n    - Extend aif_bridge/extract-evidence to decompose G into named terms instead of scalar g-mean.\n    - DONE: Add :aif/g-terms key to psr-template in pattern_competence.clj. Evidence: src/futon3/fulab/pattern_competence.clj\n    - Store term breakdowns in PSR :forecast section.\n    - Add (flip-analysis psr) that computes which term delta would have changed the chosen pattern.\n    - DONE (UKRN v3 HO-05, 2026-05-14): four-term scorecard scaffold at notebooks/ukrn_v3_efe.clj score-candidate-belief-aware (lines 102-135). Terms: risk-term-v3, ambiguity-term-v3, pattern-efe (placeholder until HO-06), priority-alignment. Smoke output shows {risk 0.167, ambiguity −4.32, pattern-efe −0.1, priority-alignment 0.030, G −4.28}. Term breakdown emitted as a map, not just a scalar. See companion patterns aif/belief-aware-risk-term and aif/predictive-entropy-as-ambiguity for the term-form specifications. Evidence: handoffs/v3_runner/validation_HO-05.md.\n    - Practice refinement (HO-05): when terms have very different natural scales (e.g. linear-space risk ~0.1 vs log-space ambiguity ~−4), the λ weights in `G = λ_p · risk + λ_e · ambiguity + ...` must be calibrated to balance magnitudes. Surface the scale-asymmetry as a calibration concern, not as a bug."}], :conclusion "Compute expected free energy as named terms with persisted breakdowns, not a single opaque scalar.", :directives {:audience "fulab agents, aif implementers", :sigils ["🐜/专"], :style "pattern", :title "Expected Free Energy as Multi-Term Scorecard", :tone "technical"}, :id "aif/expected-free-energy-scorecard", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🐜/专"], :source-path "library/aif/expected-free-energy-scorecard.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Expected Free Energy as Multi-Term Scorecard", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "When an EFE term admits multiple theoretically defensible formulations, build them all as alternative implementations and let the validation harness select between them empirically. Do not pre-commit to one variant on a priori grounds."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Some EFE terms (notably pattern-EFE-style terms, info-gain composites, multi-source ambiguity terms) have multiple plausible operationalisations. Each variant is defensible in theory; differences may be small in some operational regimes and large in others. Picking one a priori risks choosing wrong; picking by argument risks rationalising rather than measuring."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    You want the agent's term implementation to be the one that *demonstrably* discriminates candidates well, recovers known operational outcomes well, and produces traceable comparisons. You want the choice between variants to be a finding, not a stance."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    A common failure mode is to implement one variant, get plausible outputs, and ship — never knowing whether a different variant would have produced sharper discrimination or better operational fidelity. Another failure mode is to argue for one variant in committee, ship, and discover months later that an unselected variant would have been substantively different."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Build the competing variants as alternative implementations:\n      - Pure functions with matching signatures: `(variant-A candidate state opts)`, `(variant-B candidate state opts)`, etc.\n      - Selectable via opt: e.g. `:term-variant :a` / `:b` / `:c` in the EFE scorer.\n      - Default variant is whichever is closest to the prior baseline (e.g., the v2 pre-rewrite term form) — preserving backward compat under regression testing.\n    Then run the validation harness against each variant under matched configurations. Named validation properties (recovery-rate, headline-outcome, discrimination-score, computational-cost) constitute the comparison metrics. Pick the winner empirically."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Building both is cheap relative to the operational risk of picking wrong. Building both also forces the architectural seam (dispatch on opt; matching signatures) which is itself a valuable clean-up. And the *delta* between variants — measured per-run, per-config — is a finding worth reporting even after one variant wins."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Identify the variants. Name them explicitly (R-1, R-2, R-3 in the literature-style; or by characterising principle, e.g. capability-gated vs probabilistic-compound).\n    - Implement each as a pure function with the same signature shape. Add a dispatcher at the EFE scorer (single `:term-variant` opt).\n    - Define comparison metrics in the validation harness:\n      - Recovery-rate against known classifications.\n      - Headline-outcome stability (does the variant change a paper's quantitative claims?).\n      - Candidate discrimination (G-spread across top-K; higher spread = more discriminating).\n      - Trajectory-class behaviour (do simulated trajectories show qualitative patterns correctly under each variant?).\n      - Computational cost (per-EFE-evaluation latency).\n    - Run the harness; pick the winner on the merits.\n    - **Term-set parity**: when comparing variants of one term within a larger EFE that has *other* terms (or other patterns the term aggregates over), preserve the larger term set across variants. If variant-A applies to 5 patterns and variant-B applies to 3 of those 5, the comparison is confounded by term-set drift, not just by variant semantics. Either both variants cover the full term set (extending the smaller variant to handle the missing terms gracefully, e.g. as a no-(D, A)-constraint case) or neither does.\n    - DONE (UKRN v3 HO-06, 2026-05-14): R-1 (capability-gated) and R-3 (compound capability-gate ∩ (D, A)-region) of pattern-EFE implemented at notebooks/ukrn_v3_efe.clj:117-127, 129-151. Dispatched via `:pattern-efe-variant` opt. Smoke comparison on a single high-band candidate: R-1 G = −4.28, R-3 G = −4.28 — meaningful 5% difference in the pattern-EFE component, small G-level difference. Term-set parity preserved: codex extended Joe's 5-pattern mapping to all 8 v2 patterns to keep comparison clean. HO-11 will run the empirical comparison across full headline + lesion configs. Evidence: handoffs/v3_runner/validation_HO-06.md.\n    - Practice: small per-tick differences in a single EFE term can compound across many ticks into large differences in trajectory outcomes — or they can wash out. Empirical comparison settles this; intuition doesn't."}], :conclusion "When an EFE term admits multiple theoretically defensible formulations, build them all as alternative implementations and let the validation harness select between them empirically. Do not pre-commit to one variant on a priori grounds.", :directives {:audience "fulab agents, aif implementers, AIF designers facing under-determined term choices", :sigils ["⚖️/由"], :style "pattern", :title "Experimental Comparison of EFE Term Variants", :tone "technical"}, :id "aif/experimental-comparison-of-EFE-variants", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["⚖️/由"], :source-path "library/aif/experimental-comparison-of-EFE-variants.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Experimental Comparison of EFE Term Variants", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Compute and emit variational free energy F = 0.5 × mean(Π · ε²) per tick as a canonical scalar that summarises agent surprise."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "AIF computes two distinct quantities. **G** (Expected Free Energy) is per-candidate-action, drives policy. **F** (Variational Free Energy) is per-tick, summarises how surprised the agent currently is by its observations. They are different objects; conflating them is a common documentation hazard."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    You want a single, comparable-across-time scalar that says \"how badly is the agent's current belief explaining what it just observed?\" — useful for validation (F should decrease on average as the agent learns), diagnostics (F-spikes flag surprise events worth investigating), and operational triggers (F-above-threshold can signal hyperparameter adjustment)."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Without an explicit F-per-tick, the agent's belief evolution becomes opaque — there's no scalar to plot, no acceptance criterion to test, no operational alarm to trigger. And G-per-candidate is not a substitute: G is about the future; F is about the present."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Implement F as a pure function (mu, observation, precision-opts) → non-negative scalar, applying the formula F = 0.5 × mean(Π_k · ε_k²) over all observation channels k. Emit it as part of the per-tick trace alongside μ and ε. When μ matches observation exactly, F should be zero by construction."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    F is the quantity AIF agents are minimising (variationally); making it observable per tick gives a clean validation target (V3: F decreases on average) and a clean operational signal (sudden F-jumps merit attention). Keeping F distinct from G in vocabulary, file location, and trace schema prevents the cross-stack confusion where one is mistaken for the other."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Implement free-energy as a pure function in the same namespace as the belief update (canonical pairing: same module owns both rules). Pure function, takes precision options as inputs.\n    - Trace F per tick alongside μ and ε; include in CSV/evidence emissions.\n    - Acceptance: F = 0 when belief matches observation; F ≥ 0 always; F decreases over a run on average. The third property is V3 in the AIF completeness validation harness.\n    - DONE (UKRN v3 HO-02, 2026-05-14): free-energy implemented as pure function in notebooks/ukrn_v3_belief.clj:42-51. Hand-computed test cases pass. Available for the V3 validation property in the planned validation harness.\n    - Practice: when documenting an AIF surface, refer to F as \"free energy\" or \"variational free energy\" and G as \"expected free energy\" or \"EFE-per-candidate\". Cross-stack vocabulary discipline reduces handoff ambiguity."}], :conclusion "Compute and emit variational free energy F = 0.5 × mean(Π · ε²) per tick as a canonical scalar that summarises agent surprise.", :directives {:audience "fulab agents, aif implementers", :sigils ["🪔/双"], :style "pattern", :title "Variational Free Energy as a Per-Tick Scalar", :tone "technical"}, :id "aif/free-energy-as-tick-scalar", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🪔/双"], :source-path "library/aif/free-energy-as-tick-scalar.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Variational Free Energy as a Per-Tick Scalar", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "When multiple agents operating over shared state can independently choose actions that share consumable resources, resolve conflicts at multiple budget hierarchy levels: aggregate setup-budget conservation across all agents per tick; per-instance forward projection of each action's downstream resource consumption to detect instances that would predictively exhaust."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "A multi-agent (or multi-band, or hierarchical) AIF setup gives each agent or sub-agent local autonomy: each scores candidate actions and selects via softmax. But the candidates often consume shared resources — setup budget for capability activation, engagement-budget for per-instance participation, attention budget across the agent's responsibility area. A naive *union* of independent selections will violate resource constraints whenever multiple agents simultaneously prefer expensive candidates."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    You want collective action selection that respects budget constraints at every hierarchy level. You want the agent (a) to never recommend allocations that exceed available aggregate budget per tick, and (b) to factor in *downstream* consequences of each candidate on per-instance budgets so the agent doesn't recommend \"great-looking now\" candidates that cause instances to exhaust their budgets in the next K ticks."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    A naive union-of-selections leaves the budget invariant unenforced — over-subscription happens silently. A naive single-coordinator approach throws away the value of the local-agent factoring, requiring all candidates to compete in one big softmax. Neither is right."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Implement conflict resolution at *each* hierarchy level explicitly:\n    - **Aggregate level**: a coordinator function `resolve-conflicts` that takes per-agent selections, applies them in priority order (e.g. importance band, deadline order, severity rank), debiting shared budget per applied action. Actions that exceed remaining budget are dropped to a no-op baseline or downgraded; trace records which were dropped and why.\n    - **Per-instance level**: a `feasibility-penalty` EFE subterm. For each candidate, project per-instance resource consumption forward over K ticks using a cheap approximation (constant status; only the budget decays). Count instances predicted to fall below their minimum-viable budget threshold. The penalty scales linearly with that count, multiplied by a tunable λ weight.\n    Both layers produce trace fields. The trace fields land in the per-tick record (R8) so the agent's reasoning about resource constraints is auditable."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Conservation at the aggregate level is a *hard* constraint (you literally can't spend more than the budget; violating it makes the simulation incoherent). Forward-projecting per-instance consumption is a *soft* constraint (the agent might be willing to risk some instances exhausting if the candidate's other terms are sufficiently good). Hard constraints belong in the action selector; soft constraints belong in the EFE term. Implementing both gives the agent the right kind of attention to budget at every level."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Identify the budget hierarchy in your domain: aggregate (per-tick setup or attention budget shared across agents) and per-instance (resource each instance consumes to participate, with a threshold below which the instance disengages).\n    - Implement `resolve-conflicts`: priority-ordered application; per-agent fallback to no-op baseline; trace records.\n    - Implement `feasibility-penalty`: K-tick forward projection; cheap approximation by default (constant non-budget status; only budget decays). Avoid full kernel-replay over K ticks unless the cheap approximation gives wrong answers.\n    - Calibrate the aggregate budget against the actual action-cost distribution: pick a value that admits one expensive action or two cheap actions per tick; tighter than \"everything fits\", looser than \"always blocks\".\n    - Calibrate λ_feasibility: should produce a penalty large enough to deter clearly-truncating candidates but not so large that no candidate ever passes it.\n    - DONE (UKRN v3 HO-07, 2026-05-14): `resolve-band-conflicts` at notebooks/ukrn_v3_kernel.clj:30-79 implements aggregate setup-budget conservation in band priority order (high → middle → thin). `budget-feasibility-penalty` at notebooks/ukrn_v3_efe.clj:205-211 implements per-institution K=4 tick projection; λ=0.5 per predicted truncation. Smoke output: a candidate against population with 6 high-band institutions at budget=20 produces feasibility-penalty=3.0 (6 at-risk × 0.5), shifting G from −4.28 to −1.28 (+70% deterrent). Closes V9 (setup-budget conservation) and V11 (per-institution budget feasibility). Evidence: handoffs/v3_runner/validation_HO-07.md.\n    - Practice: duplicate same-tick proposals (two agents proposing the same action) should typically pay the aggregate cost once, not twice — debit on first-occurrence-of-action-id, not per-agent. Mirrors set-semantics of resource activation.\n    - Practice: K-tick forward projection complexity should match operational stakes. Full kernel-replay K times per candidate is expensive; the cheap approximation (status held constant; budget decays linearly) is usually sufficient because the agent's decision is *should I avoid candidates that drain budgets* not *what exactly will happen K ticks from now*. The approximation is fit for purpose."}], :conclusion "When multiple agents operating over shared state can independently choose actions that share consumable resources, resolve conflicts at multiple budget hierarchy levels: aggregate setup-budget conservation across all agents per tick; per-instance forward projection of each action's downstream resource consumption to detect instances that would predictively exhaust.", :directives {:audience "fulab agents, aif implementers, multi-agent-AIF designers, simulation-of-collective-action designers", :sigils ["🏛️/双"], :style "pattern", :title "Hierarchical Budget-Aware Action Selection", :tone "technical"}, :id "aif/hierarchical-budget-aware-action-selection", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🏛️/双"], :source-path "library/aif/hierarchical-budget-aware-action-selection.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Hierarchical Budget-Aware Action Selection", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "To measure an action's effect on a system that drifts and settles over time, capture the BASELINE tight — right before the action, not before the preparatory work — and read the EFFECT settled — after the state stabilizes (≥2 consecutive reads agree within ε) — and EXCLUDE any pair where drift or transience confounds the window. A before/after number is only as good as the window it brackets."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You measure `effect = after − before` around an action, in a system whose state\n    propagates asynchronously (scans, caches, eventual consistency, a live field other agents touch)."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      You record a predicted/realised (or before/after) pair to score an action's effect on such a\n      system."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      Two window failures inflate or fabricate the number, and both look clean:\n      (a) STALE BASELINE — you captured \"before\" *early* (e.g. before minting the artifact the action\n      commits), and the system drifted in between; the measured Δ is mostly that drift, not the action.\n      (b) TRANSIENT READBACK — you read \"after\" at the first post-action sample, catching an in-flight\n      spike that decays to baseline; the measured Δ is a scan-timing artifact."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "      Bracket the window tightly. BEFORE: capture the baseline immediately prior to the field-visible\n      action — do the preparatory work first, *then* baseline, *then* commit. AFTER: read at the\n      settled state — require ≥2 consecutive reads agreeing within ε; tag `:settled` vs `:transient`\n      and exclude transient. And if the system drifted independently inside the window, DROP the pair\n      (untagged / proposal-mode) rather than attribute the drift to the action."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      A measurement's validity lives in its window, not its arithmetic. The durable effect is what\n      persists between a tight baseline and a settled readback; everything else — drift, spikes — is\n      the window leaking. Excluding a confounded pair keeps the dataset honest; recording it launders\n      noise as signal. (The two failures, caught live: a 0.277 \"move\" that was a transient spike\n      (retracted on settled re-read); and a 0.277 \"move\" that was field drift during artifact-minting\n      (excluded, proposal-mode) — same magnitude, both fake, both caught by tightening the window.)"} {:name "how-to-apply", :name-key "how-to-apply", :slug "how-to-apply", :text "      Order operations: prepare → baseline → act → settle → read → (exclude if confounded). Never\n      baseline before the slow part; never read before the fast part settles; never attribute a\n      window's drift to the action inside it. Distilled from the FutonZero G-SIM calibration arc\n      (WM-pilot, 2026-06-11). Sibling of `[[aif/no-self-certification]]`."}], :conclusion "To measure an action's effect on a system that drifts and settles over time, capture the BASELINE tight — right before the action, not before the preparatory work — and read the EFFECT settled — after the state stabilizes (≥2 consecutive reads agree within ε) — and EXCLUDE any pair where drift or transience confounds the window. A before/after number is only as good as the window it brackets.", :directives {:audience "anyone measuring an action's effect on a drifting, settling system", :keywords ["measurement" "baseline" "settled read" "transient spike" "stale begin" "drift" "before-after" "eventual consistency" "prediction error" "confound"], :sigils ["⊢⊣/≈"], :style "pattern", :title "Measurement-Window Hygiene (Tight Baseline, Settled Readback, Drop the Confounded)", :tone "practical"}, :id "aif/measurement-window-hygiene", :keywords ["measurement" "baseline" "settled read" "transient spike" "stale begin" "drift" "before-after" "eventual consistency" "prediction error" "confound"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["⊢⊣/≈"], :source-path "library/aif/measurement-window-hygiene.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Measurement-Window Hygiene (Tight Baseline, Settled Readback, Drop the Confounded)", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "An inference agent whose generative model depends on an externally-authored substrate cannot, *as currently architected*, construct its own niche; the agents operating its peripherals do, and the agent's false-floor readings are how those agents know what to construct. (Scope matters: this is a property of the current niche-authoring arrangement, not a theorem about active inference — see HOWEVER-endogenous below.)"} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "An active-inference agent (e.g. the futon War Machine) reads a substrate of typed vertices and edges, computes T / ∇T / ΔT or analogues, and ranks actions. Its inference is bounded by the typed-edge inventory of the substrate it can see. The substrate is itself authored by other agents and operators."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    You are designing an AIF agent whose generative model depends on a structured substrate (hypergraph, knowledge graph, typed event log) that is authored by parties other than the agent itself."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    The agent cannot extend its own substrate's typed-edge inventory — it has operations on values within the niche but not on the meta-level structure of the niche. When action classes surface that the inventory doesn't cover, the agent reads ΔT = 0 (false floor), not error. It looks confident-and-wrong; it is in fact correctly-uninformed, and the substrate is incomplete."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Treat false-floor readings as **niche-construction signals**, not bugs. Each false-floor names a typed-edge family the substrate is missing relative to the action classes agents actually take. The operators-and-agents who use the inference agent do the niche construction: they add the missing typed-edge families, ingest the missing vertex types, derive the missing relations. On the next tick the agent sees a richer niche and ranks more accurately."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Niche construction (Lewontin 1983; Odling-Smee / Laland / Feldman 2003; Friston in active-inference terms) is the loop by which an organism modifies its environment so its inference becomes tractable. The futon agent is in the same loop; only the niche is symbolic (a typed graph) rather than physical. Confident false-floors masquerading as signal is the worst outcome — the agent makes apparently-warranted-but-actually-uninformed choices. Visible false-floors that operators can read as \"edges missing here\" is the right outcome — the agent's blindness becomes the input to its own niche's improvement."} {:name "HOWEVER-endogenous (2026-05-31, claude-1 + Joe, emacs-repl)", :name-key "however-endogenous (2026-05-31, claude-1 + joe, emacs-repl)", :slug "however-endogenous-2026-05-31-claude-1-joe-emacs-repl", :text "    The \"cannot\" above is scoped to the current architecture, where the substrate is authored by parties other than the agent. It is NOT a theorem about active inference. Two strands show the boundary can be redrawn so niche-construction becomes endogenous: (a) hierarchical AIF learns high-level priors that re-weight uncertainty-tolerance — Kiverstein & Miller (2023) on playfulness: a learned high-level prior expands the phenotypic bound on tolerated surprise, sending the agent to the competence edge where new structure is found; (b) the variational free-energy principle has been applied to niche construction directly (Constant / Ramstead / Veissière / Friston, \"A variational approach to niche construction\"), redrawing the Markov blanket to include the constructed niche so inventory-edits become variational moves over an extended boundary rather than an external handoff. The futon realisation is the **aif2** direction: a boundary-tier set of criteria (mechanising AIF+ I1 boundary-integrity + I3 timescale-separation) over whether and how the WM may re-author its own typed-edge support — distinct from the inference-tier R1-R12. The false-floor / zero-anamnesis lull, under a learned playfulness prior, reads not as \"hand off\" but as \"forage at the edge\" — the niche-construction trigger from the inside. See M-aif2."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Surface false-floor readings as such — don't silently zero-out a signal; report \"ΔT = 0 (no incident edges of supported family)\" so absence is legible to operators.\n    - When new action classes are added to a ranking surface, audit which typed-edge families would feed ΔT (or analogue) for that class, and which are present-vs-missing in the substrate.\n    - For the futon stack as of 2026-05-27: the WM's ΔT over `code/v05/related-mission` works for sorry-actions but gives ΔT = 0 false-floor for anchor-reframe / file-edit / excursion-step actions. Construction backlog: (a) feed `code/v05/mission-cross-ref` (already in substrate from M-weird-modernism Task 8 single-parser consolidation) into the Laplacian; (b) derive `code/v05/file → mission` from `:mission/code-paths`; (c) ingest E-prefix excursion docs as a typed vertex family (currently not ingested).\n    - Connection to aliveness synthesis (M-action-cost-modelling §3.8): anamnesis = un-discharged tension drives niche construction; niche construction discharges anamnesis into mana. The three terms form a single loop, with niche construction as the operative mechanism by which agent-substrate aliveness shifts.\n    - Sibling-pattern dependency: when authoring missions that ship new action classes, the mission's INSTANTIATE phase should explicitly enumerate the niche-construction-audit per class (which edges feed the Laplacian, which are absent). Treat per-class niche-construction state as first-class mission metadata, not implicit.\n    - aif2 / endogenous niche-construction (2026-05-31): treat the action-class inventory itself as hidden state to be inferred (an extension of R12, or candidate R13), and mechanise the AIF+ I1 (boundary integrity) + I3 (timescale) invariants for the case where the boundary is *permitted to move*. The meta-model is `futon2.aif.*` modelled as its own AIF+ `.aif.edn` lifeform. See M-aif2 (IDENTIFY)."}], :conclusion "An inference agent whose generative model depends on an externally-authored substrate cannot, *as currently architected*, construct its own niche; the agents operating its peripherals do, and the agent's false-floor readings are how those agents know what to construct. (Scope matters: this is a property of the current niche-authoring arrangement, not a theorem about active inference — see HOWEVER-endogenous below.)", :directives {:audience "operators of fulab / futon-stack agents, mission authors, designers of read/write substrates for an active-inference engine", :keywords ["niche-construction" "bootstrap" "typed-edge-inventory" "false-floor" "substrate" "generative-model" "anamnesis" "mana" "agency" "action-class" "ΔT"], :references ["aif/expected-free-energy-scorecard" "aif/structured-observation-vector" "futon-theory/structural-tension-as-observation"], :sigils ["🐜/巢"], :style "pattern", :title "Niche Construction as the Bootstrap Loop of an Inference Agent", :tone "declarative"}, :id "aif/niche-construction", :keywords ["niche-construction" "bootstrap" "typed-edge-inventory" "false-floor" "substrate" "generative-model" "anamnesis" "mana" "agency" "action-class" "ΔT"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["aif/expected-free-energy-scorecard" "aif/structured-observation-vector" "futon-theory/structural-tension-as-observation"], :scores {}, :sigils ["🐜/巢"], :source-path "library/aif/niche-construction.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Niche Construction as the Bootstrap Loop of an Inference Agent", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "A measurement / verdict apparatus must never be moveable by evidence it manufactured itself. Enforce this STRUCTURALLY — with tags the apparatus cannot fake (independence, measured-vs-fallback, settled-vs-transient) and a verdict that counts only those tags — not by vigilance."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are building an instrument that issues a verdict — \"calibrated\", \"passed\",\n    \"admissible\", \"rewardable\" — from evidence that the same system also produces, stages, or\n    scores. The verdict will look earned long before it is."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      An instrument issues a verdict from evidence it also produces or stages (a calibrator over\n      its own predictions, a gate over its own outputs, a reward channel over its own scores)."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      On first contact with reality, every layer tries to certify ITSELF, and each attempt looks\n      rigorous: the forward model scores its own constants (`prior == value`); the evidence reader\n      eats its own staging files; the realised channel copies predicted on success (a censored\n      0.0); the calibration clears on degenerate volume. Vigilance cannot catch these — an\n      instrument's *first* catches are about itself, and no human watches every path."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "      Require that a verdict may be moved ONLY by evidence the verdict-maker did not manufacture.\n      Tag every evidence record at birth with the properties the apparatus cannot fake —\n      `:independent?` (was there an out-of-band witness?), source (`:measured` vs\n      `:target-absent-fallback`), settledness (`:settled` vs `:transient`) — and have the verdict\n      count ONLY the records whose tags clear. Strictness default: **untagged never counts**."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      Circularity is invisible from inside the loop precisely because every internal reading is\n      consistent with every other — that is what `prior == value` means. The only thing that breaks\n      it is a referent the maker had no hand in: an external witness, a settled re-read of a world\n      it does not control, a tag it cannot forge. Structure survives where vigilance fails, and it\n      converts \"be careful\" (unauditable) into \"the tag is absent, so it does not count\" (checkable)."} {:name "canonical-instance", :name-key "canonical-instance", :slug "canonical-instance", :text "      The FutonZero G-SIM calibration arc (WM-pilot, 2026-06-11): four distinct self-certification\n      attempts surfaced live — censored fallback, transient spike, `prior == value`, and\n      operator-gate-as-fruit — each caught only because an independence/source/settled tag existed\n      to exclude it, and each then encoded as a permanent structural guard. See\n      `[[M-the-perfect-crime]]` (the forensic framing: the crime relocates to a scarcer witness;\n      this pattern is the structural half of that ladder) and the reward-red-team fixture\n      (the executable suite of the shapes)."} {:name "how-to-apply", :name-key "how-to-apply", :slug "how-to-apply", :text "      Before trusting any \"it passed\", ask: did this verdict move on evidence the system made?\n      If yes, find the un-manufactured referent (external witness / settled read / out-of-band\n      tag) and gate the verdict on it. If there is none, the verdict is decoration. Minted from the\n      arc that learned it, WM-pilot 2026-06-11 (arc-2 cycle 13)."}], :conclusion "A measurement / verdict apparatus must never be moveable by evidence it manufactured itself. Enforce this STRUCTURALLY — with tags the apparatus cannot fake (independence, measured-vs-fallback, settled-vs-transient) and a verdict that counts only those tags — not by vigilance.", :directives {:audience "anyone building a measurement, calibration, gate, certifier, or reward channel", :keywords ["self-certification" "circular evidence" "prior-equals-value" "independence tag" "censored observation" "calibration" "verdict" "structural enforcement" "untagged-never-counts"], :sigils ["⊘/⟲"], :style "pattern", :title "No Self-Certification (A Verdict Moves Only on Evidence Its Maker Did Not Manufacture)", :tone "foundational"}, :id "aif/no-self-certification", :keywords ["self-certification" "circular evidence" "prior-equals-value" "independence tag" "censored observation" "calibration" "verdict" "structural enforcement" "untagged-never-counts"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["⊘/⟲"], :source-path "library/aif/no-self-certification.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "No Self-Certification (A Verdict Moves Only on Evidence Its Maker Did Not Manufacture)", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Maintain a commitment temperature that modulates how strongly the policy commits to low-G choices."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Even with identical scores, the agent sometimes should explore alternatives and other times should commit."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    You want exploration versus exploitation to depend on operational pressure (deadlines, repeated failures, user impatience, instability), not on randomness alone."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Without an explicit (tau), the agent either thrashes or tunnels with no controllable dial."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Maintain (tau) as a commitment temperature that modulates softmax over (-G/tau), coupled to diagnostic signals (recent regressions, uncertainty in (mu), contradiction rate, time pressure, repeated test failures)."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    A single commitment dial allows systematic control of stochasticity without changing the scoring model."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Add :aif/tau-adjustment event type to tatami_schema.clj with keys [:prev-tau :next-tau :trigger :diagnostic-signals].\n    - Implement tau-coupling rules in aif_bridge.clj: increase tau on :test-failure or :uncertainty > 0.7; decrease on :tests-passing streak.\n    - Persist tau history in lab/aif/tau-cache.edn keyed by pattern-id; load on session start.\n    - Add (policy-entropy session) metric to aif_coverage.clj; correlate with outcome stability in session summaries.\n    - DONE (UKRN v3 HO-05, 2026-05-14): abstain? rule implemented at notebooks/ukrn_v3_efe.clj:75-92. Computes G-spread across top-K candidates, divides by τ, abstains if the resulting discriminability is ≤ floor (default 0.5, configurable). Edge case: < 2 candidates returns true (degenerate). Test abstain-fires-only-under-low-discriminability covers well-separated, weakly-separated, threshold, and just-over-threshold regimes. Closes R6 abstain criterion for UKRN v3-runner. Evidence: handoffs/v3_runner/validation_HO-05.md.\n    - Practice (HO-05): abstain semantics are part of R6 in the AIF completeness criteria. An implementation that has softmax without abstain is operationally incomplete — it always picks something, even when statistically indistinguishable candidates would warrant inaction. Make abstain explicit; make the threshold configurable."}], :conclusion "Maintain a commitment temperature that modulates how strongly the policy commits to low-G choices.", :directives {:audience "fulab agents, aif implementers", :sigils ["🍲/由"], :style "pattern", :title "Policy Precision as Commitment Temperature", :tone "technical"}, :id "aif/policy-precision-commitment-temperature", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🍲/由"], :source-path "library/aif/policy-precision-commitment-temperature.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Policy Precision as Commitment Temperature", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Update beliefs via predictive-coding (μ ← μ + α·Π·ε) and evolve variance with a sensor-variance floor so uncertainty stays meaningful."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "The agent receives an observation each tick and must integrate it into its belief state. The integration rule determines both mean trajectory and uncertainty trajectory."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    You want belief updates to be principled (Bayesian-shaped under Gaussian approximation), tunable (single learning rate; per-channel precision), and operationally robust (variance doesn't collapse to zero; doesn't blow up)."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Without an explicit update rule the agent either slams beliefs to each observation (over-trusts) or never moves them (over-anchors). Variance with no sensor-variance floor decays toward zero under any informative-observation history, killing the predictive-entropy term in EFE for later ticks."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Implement two coupled updates per tick:\n      - Mean: μ ← μ + α · Π · ε, where ε = observation − μ, Π is per-channel precision, α is learning rate.\n      - Variance: var ← (1−α)·var + α·(observation − new-μ)² + σ²_sensor, where σ²_sensor is the sensor's intrinsic variance floor.\n    The residual against the *new* mean (not the old mean) is what informs the variance update — this matches the moment-matched EWMA approximation of Bayesian update under Gaussian likelihood."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    The mean rule realises predictive coding (Friston et al.); the variance rule keeps belief uncertainty in a meaningful regime where ambiguity terms in EFE stay informative. σ²_sensor as a *configurable* parameter supports noiseless-mode sanity checks and sensitivity sweeps without recompilation."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Implement update-mu as a pure function: (mu, observed-state, opts) → BeliefState. Take α, Π, σ²_sensor from opts with named defaults.\n    - Use observation − new-mean (post-update residual) in the variance term, not observation − old-mean. The shorthand can confuse handoff specs; cite the acceptance-criterion hand-calculation as source of truth.\n    - Floor variance at 0 explicitly via (max 0 (...)); under σ²_sensor > 0 the floor is rarely tested in practice but the explicit floor protects against pathological inputs.\n    - DONE (UKRN v3 HO-02, 2026-05-14): update-mu and free-energy implemented at notebooks/ukrn_v3_belief.clj:1-51. α=0.4, Π=1.0, σ²_sensor=0.02 defaults configurable via thresholds map. Verified across 8 hand-computed test cases. Evidence: handoffs/v3_runner/validation_HO-02.md.\n    - Validation: variance should shrink under low-prediction-error observations (test A4: var goes from 0.04 to 0.024 under perfect prediction and σ²=0); grow under surprise (test A3: var goes from 0.04 to 0.080 under ε=0.5 and σ²=0.02). Both tests should be in the validation harness for any new AIF implementation following this pattern."}], :conclusion "Update beliefs via predictive-coding (μ ← μ + α·Π·ε) and evolve variance with a sensor-variance floor so uncertainty stays meaningful.", :directives {:audience "fulab agents, aif implementers", :sigils ["🌀/由"], :style "pattern", :title "Predictive-Coding Belief Update with Variance Evolution", :tone "technical"}, :id "aif/predictive-coding-belief-update", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🌀/由"], :source-path "library/aif/predictive-coding-belief-update.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Predictive-Coding Belief Update with Variance Evolution", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Compute the epistemic (ambiguity) EFE term as the entropy of the predictive observation distribution under each candidate. For a diagonal-covariance Gaussian, this simplifies to `0.5 × log(σ²_D × σ²_A)` (dropping additive constants that don't affect candidate ranking)."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "The epistemic value term in Expected Free Energy is supposed to favour information-gaining actions — candidates that produce *less ambiguous* observations, so the agent can refine its beliefs efficiently. A naïve implementation uses heuristic proxies (capability-portfolio breadth, count of available action branches, action-class diversity). These approximate but don't actually compute the AIF quantity."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    You want the ambiguity term to be the *true predictive entropy* of the observation distribution under each candidate. Candidates that produce sharper predictions (lower predictive variance) get lower ambiguity; candidates that produce diffuse predictions (high predictive variance, near-threshold predictions, etc.) get higher ambiguity. The agent prefers ambiguity-reducing candidates because they're information-gaining."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Substituting a heuristic for the actual entropy makes the agent's \"epistemic\" preferences correlated-with-but-not-equal-to information theory. The agent may prefer actions that have many sub-options but produce nothing informative; or avoid actions that look unambiguous in heuristic terms but produce truly precise observations. Heuristic-based epistemic terms are common but not AIF-canonical."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    For a candidate action producing predicted observation distribution `o ∼ N(μ, Σ)`, the differential entropy of `o` is:\n      `H[o] = 0.5 × log((2πe)^k × det(Σ))`\n    where k is the dimension of the observation vector. For diagonal-covariance (independent channels), `det(Σ) = Π_k σ²_k`. For comparing across candidates, the additive constants (`0.5 × k × log(2πe)`) drop out, leaving:\n      `ambiguity-comparable(candidate) = 0.5 × log(Π_k σ²_k)`\n    In two dimensions (D, A): `ambiguity-comparable = 0.5 × log(σ²_D × σ²_A)`."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Predictive entropy is the AIF-canonical epistemic quantity. The diagonal-Gaussian simplification is tractable and matches the diagonal-covariance assumption that downstream code (mode-quadrant integration; visualisation) already uses. Dropping constants preserves comparability across candidates while avoiding spurious absolute-entropy precision — a value of `−4.3` in dropped-constants form is comparable to `−4.4` for a different candidate but doesn't claim to be \"−4.3 bits\"."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Verify your forward model returns predicted variance per observation channel. If not, the entropy isn't computable.\n    - Floor the variance per channel (`max(variance-epsilon, var_k)`) before passing to log, to avoid `log(0)` under near-deterministic candidates. variance-epsilon = 1e-12 or similar.\n    - Decide whether to keep or drop the Gaussian constants. Drop them for comparable-across-candidates output; keep them for absolute-entropy interpretation. Document the choice in the function docstring.\n    - DONE (UKRN v3 HO-05, 2026-05-14): ambiguity-term-v3 implemented at notebooks/ukrn_v3_efe.clj:60-73. Diagonal-Gaussian factorisation; constants dropped; variance-epsilon floor. Smoke output shows value ≈ −4.3 for a representative high-band candidate. Test ambiguity-term-v3-is-monotonic-and-finite verifies monotonicity and finite behaviour under near-zero variance. Evidence: handoffs/v3_runner/validation_HO-05.md.\n    - Practice: when comparing this term against pragmatic and other EFE terms in the final G composition, note the scale difference — the entropy form is in log-space (typically yielding values in the range [-10, 0] for variance in [1e-4, 1]), while pragmatic risk² is in linear coordinate-square space (typically [0, 2] for unit-bounded coordinates). The λ_e weight in `G = λ_p · risk + λ_e · ambiguity + ...` must be calibrated to balance the magnitudes."}], :conclusion "Compute the epistemic (ambiguity) EFE term as the entropy of the predictive observation distribution under each candidate. For a diagonal-covariance Gaussian, this simplifies to `0.5 × log(σ²_D × σ²_A)` (dropping additive constants that don't affect candidate ranking).", :directives {:audience "fulab agents, aif implementers, designers of EFE term implementations", :sigils ["🌫️/由"], :style "pattern", :title "Ambiguity Term as Predictive Entropy of the Observation Distribution", :tone "technical"}, :id "aif/predictive-entropy-as-ambiguity", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🌫️/由"], :source-path "library/aif/predictive-entropy-as-ambiguity.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Ambiguity Term as Predictive Entropy of the Observation Distribution", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Extract the deterministic dynamics core into a pure shared kernel; have both the live step and the predictive forward model call into it. One implementation, two callers."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "An AIF agent that scores candidate actions via Expected Free Energy needs a predictive forward model — a function that takes a candidate action and returns the predicted next-state distribution. The naive implementation maintains a separate \"shadow\" dynamics for the predictive call, which inevitably drifts from the live dynamics as the code evolves."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    You want the predictive forward model's outputs to be principled (the agent's beliefs about candidate actions match what the live dynamics would actually do under those actions), maintainable (one set of dynamics rules to update), and verifiable (a single regression-test surface that catches accidental divergence)."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Two implementations of the same dynamics is two implementations to maintain, two places for bugs to hide, two surfaces where the forward model's predictions can disagree with what the live step actually does. The cost of maintaining bit-equivalent shadows grows quadratically with the dynamics complexity."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Factor the live step into (a) a *pure deterministic kernel* taking explicit (state, action, seed, opts) and returning a next state, and (b) a *thin orchestration wrapper* that threads defaults and calls the kernel. The wrapper preserves the live API; the kernel becomes callable by:\n      - The wrapper, in live operation.\n      - The predictive forward model, for candidate scoring — typically via a second arity that admits supplied candidate actions and skips the live action-selection step.\n    The predictive forward model then becomes a thin probabilistic wrapper around the same kernel: call kernel with candidate, add Gaussian noise (or other principled uncertainty representation), produce next-state distribution."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    One implementation of the deterministic dynamics is the only place where the rules live. The kernel is what the live step does *and* what the forward model predicts. Any update to one is automatically reflected in the other. The bit-equivalence test (live-path output unchanged under refactor) is the cheapest catchnet for accidental drift."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Audit the live step for impurities: I/O calls, atom mutations, time-of-day reads, side-effect emissions. The kernel must be pure; impurities belong in the wrapper.\n    - Define the kernel signature with explicit (state, action, seed, opts) — no implicit context, no defaults inside the kernel itself.\n    - Add a second arity for the forward-model seam: `(kernel state ext seed opts candidate-actions)` — supplied actions skip live action selection.\n    - Document the RNG-consumption divergence: when candidate actions are supplied, the kernel doesn't consume action-selection RNG. The forward model's Gaussian noise wrap is the source of predictive uncertainty, not kernel RNG draws. This must be in the kernel docstring.\n    - Capture a pre-refactor regression baseline. Commit it (e.g. as a CSV fixture). Automated test compares post-refactor output to baseline byte-for-byte.\n    - DONE (UKRN v3 HO-03, 2026-05-14): step-kernel extracted to notebooks/ukrn_v3_kernel.clj:159-193; engagement-step reduced to 8-line wrapper at notebooks/ukrn_v2_engagement.clj:51-58; bit-equivalence regression fixture at test/fixtures/ho03_v11_headline_trajectory_baseline.csv. Evidence: handoffs/v3_runner/validation_HO-03.md.\n    - DONE (UKRN v3 HO-04, 2026-05-14): predict-engagement-step at notebooks/ukrn_v3_forward.clj:122-149 wraps step-kernel 5-arity with Gaussian noise propagation; smoke output shows threshold-proximity behaviour discriminates candidates correctly. Evidence: handoffs/v3_runner/validation_HO-04.md.\n    - Practice: keep the kernel and the forward model in separate namespaces. The kernel is the deterministic shared core; the forward model is the probabilistic wrapper. Separation makes the call surface for downstream EFE consumers clear."}], :conclusion "Extract the deterministic dynamics core into a pure shared kernel; have both the live step and the predictive forward model call into it. One implementation, two callers.", :directives {:audience "fulab agents, aif implementers, simulation-based-AIF designers", :sigils ["⚙️/由"], :style "pattern", :title "Shared Kernel for the Predictive Forward Model", :tone "technical"}, :id "aif/shared-kernel-predictive-forward-model", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["⚙️/由"], :source-path "library/aif/shared-kernel-predictive-forward-model.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Shared Kernel for the Predictive Forward Model", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Update beliefs only for instances the agent is actually observing this tick. Paused, truncated, dropped, or otherwise out-of-loop instances retain their prior belief unchanged."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "When an AIF agent operates over multiple instances (institutions, sessions, processes, conversation threads), each instance has an engagement status that determines whether the agent receives meaningful observations from it. The naive update-everything-every-tick policy is operationally wrong when some instances are not contributing fresh information."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    You want the belief evolution to faithfully reflect what the agent actually saw — not what it would have seen under unconditional observation. You want paused or truncated instances to retain their last-known belief as a marker for \"the agent stopped observing here at this prior\", rather than drifting under noise or stale dynamics."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    A blanket \"update every instance every tick\" policy will:\n      - Mistake rollback dynamics (e.g. paused-status state rollback) for genuine observations, eroding the agent's stored belief about non-observed instances.\n      - Cause truncated instances' beliefs to drift toward their frozen-but-decaying state, producing post-truncation noise in trace records.\n      - Make it hard to read trace records, because every instance moves every tick regardless of whether it should."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Gate the belief update on instance status, distinguishing *engagement status* (does the agent act on the instance this tick) from *observation availability* (does the agent see the instance's state this tick):\n      - For status :active — agent is engaging *and* observing. Apply update-mu using the post-step state.\n      - For status :paused — agent is *not engaging* but *is still observing* (the dynamics still computes the instance's (D, A) state; only the engagement-side effects are rolled back). Apply update-mu using the post-step (D, A) as observation.\n      - For status :truncated — the agent's relationship with the instance has ended; no further observations are meaningful for the simulation's purpose. Retain pre-step :mu unchanged.\n    The trace record emits :mu for all instances every tick. The agent's belief about a :paused instance evolves under observation noise (variance grows by sensor-variance per tick) and tracks the post-step (D, A) coordinates; only a :truncated instance shows a frozen belief."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Belief is a record of what the agent learned from observation. Updating it when no observation was made introduces a fiction; preserving it when no observation was made preserves the operational meaning. Status-gating makes the gating decision explicit and auditable."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Identify the canonical status states in your domain (e.g. :active / :paused / :truncated for engagement-style agents; :running / :blocked / :complete for session-style agents). Document which are observation-bearing.\n    - Implement the gate as a case-dispatch in the per-tick post-processing step, *not* inside update-mu itself. update-mu stays a pure function over (μ, observation, opts); the gate decides whether to call it.\n    - Test: run a population with an instance in non-active status; verify the instance's belief is unchanged across the tick. Run another with the same instance returning to active; verify belief updates resume.\n    - DONE (UKRN v3 HO-02, 2026-05-14): status-gated update first implemented in notebooks/ukrn_v2_engagement.clj. Active gets update-mu; paused and truncated retain pre-step :mu. Test paused-and-truncated-institutions-keep-their-beliefs verified that initial implementation. Evidence: handoffs/v3_runner/validation_HO-02.md.\n    - REFINED (UKRN v3 HO-08b, 2026-05-15): the original \"only :active updates beliefs\" semantic was over-conservative. Under the v3 integration where most institutions go :paused early (abstain cascade until capabilities come online), the over-conservative gate caused beliefs to never evolve over the run — surfaced as four cascading test failures (belief-movement; noiseless-variance-decay; both lesion-contrasts). The corrected semantic distinguishes engagement (the agent's action) from observation (the agent's perception): :active and :paused both update; only :truncated freezes. Implementation in notebooks/ukrn_v3_kernel.clj postprocess-institution. Test renamed to truncated-institutions-keep-beliefs-paused-institutions-update. Evidence: handoffs/v3_runner/validation_HO-08b.md.\n    - Practice: the gate is a *post-step* decision, applied after the dynamics step has run. The dynamics may still update other state (NPT factors, capabilities) for non-active instances via rollback logic; that's about action, not perception. The agent's belief tracks what the agent observes, which can continue under :paused even when engagement is paused.\n    - Lesson: when defining \"the agent isn't observing this instance\" for a status-gated update, ask explicitly whether the post-step state still carries the instance's coordinates. If yes (e.g., dynamics computes (D, A) for paused instances and the simulation reports them), then *observation is still happening* and beliefs should update. Reserving the freeze for genuinely-no-more-observations cases (truncated, dropped, deleted) keeps belief evolution coherent across the run."}], :conclusion "Update beliefs only for instances the agent is actually observing this tick. Paused, truncated, dropped, or otherwise out-of-loop instances retain their prior belief unchanged.", :directives {:audience "fulab agents, aif implementers, hierarchical-AIF designers", :sigils ["🚪/双"], :style "pattern", :title "Status-Gated Belief Update for Multi-Instance Agents", :tone "technical"}, :id "aif/status-gated-belief-update", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🚪/双"], :source-path "library/aif/status-gated-belief-update.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Status-Gated Belief Update for Multi-Instance Agents", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Treat each step as a typed observation vector so scoring and learning can be compared across time."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "A pattern-guided agent must decide based on tool output, trace events, and user feedback."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    You want the agent's observation to be comparable across steps so scoring and learning do not collapse into prose interpretation."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Tool output and user feedback arrive as heterogeneous artifacts (json, logs, diffs, snippets, natural language) that are hard to compare or weight consistently."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Maintain an explicit (o) as a typed, normalized feature map derived from session state (for example: test status, compile status, diff size, failing spec count, user-stated constraints, time since last anchor, contradiction flags)."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    A stable observation vector is the prerequisite for meaningful precision control and policy scoring."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Define :aif/observation-vector schema in tatami_schema.clj with keys [:test-status :compile-status :diff-size :failing-spec-count :user-constraints :time-since-anchor :contradiction-flags].\n    - Add (extract-observation-vector session-state) to aif_bridge.clj that normalizes tool output into the typed map.\n    - Include observation vector in :aif/tick events; require it for PSR context/anchors validation.\n    - Add (observation-delta o1 o2) and log deltas alongside policy changes in learn_or_act/decide."}], :conclusion "Treat each step as a typed observation vector so scoring and learning can be compared across time.", :directives {:audience "fulab agents, aif implementers", :sigils ["📎/了"], :style "pattern", :title "Structured Observation Vector", :tone "technical"}, :id "aif/structured-observation-vector", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["📎/了"], :source-path "library/aif/structured-observation-vector.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Structured Observation Vector", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Require each score term to declare the observation channels and precision settings it consumed."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "A pattern-guided agent evaluates candidates with a multi-term expected free energy score built from heterogeneous evidence."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    You want tuning decisions and failures to be diagnosable at the level of which evidence actually drove each score term."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Without explicit traceability, terms like risk or ambiguity silently absorb whatever signals are available, making post-hoc explanations indistinguishable from rationalization."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Require every G term contribution to declare the observation keys it consumed, the precision channels applied, and the intermediate values used to compute that term, and persist this mapping alongside the score."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Explicit term-to-channel linkage prevents semantic drift in scoring terms and makes policy behavior mechanically inspectable."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - DONE: Validate PSR :aif/g-terms provenance keys (term-id, observation-keys, precision-channels, intermediate-values, final-contribution) in check-psr. Evidence: src/futon3/hx/logic.clj\n    - DONE: Add (dominant-channels psr) analysis that flags when a single channel accounts for >60% of a term's value. Evidence: src/futon3/hx/logic.clj\n    - DONE: Add term provenance + dominant channel summaries to fulab pattern reports. Evidence: src/futon3/fulab/pattern_competence.clj, dev/lab-pattern-report.clj\n    - DONE: Emit :aif summaries in MUSN turn start/select/use responses and log them in musn_stream. Evidence: src/futon3/musn/service.clj, dev/musn_stream.clj, src/futon3/musn/schema.clj\n    - BLOCKED: Define :aif/term-provenance schema in tatami_schema.clj with keys [:term-id :observation-keys :precision-channels :intermediate-values :final-contribution]. blocked-by[tatami-schema]\n    - BLOCKED: Include term provenance in fulab-pattern-check CLI output for top-3 candidates. blocked-by[term-provenance-schema, fulab-pattern-check]"}], :conclusion "Require each score term to declare the observation channels and precision settings it consumed.", :directives {:audience "fulab agents, aif implementers", :sigils ["🏃/功"], :style "pattern", :title "Term-to-Channel Traceability", :tone "technical"}, :id "aif/term-to-channel-traceability", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🏃/功"], :source-path "library/aif/term-to-channel-traceability.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Term-to-Channel Traceability", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "When you calibrate a model whose own outputs compose the field it is scored against, split the calibration into TWO layers and never let one stand in for the other. L1 — dynamics/consistency (does the field respond to measured state?): cheap, accrues every cycle, and is NEVER value evidence. L2 — outcome-vs-prediction (did real functioning result?): scarce, witnessed, gated, and is the ONLY value clearance."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are calibrating a forward/value model against a live field, and the field's value\n    function is computed from the same effects the model predicts with (`prior == value` risk)."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      Predicted-vs-realised pairs are your evidence that the model is \"calibrated\"."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      On such a field the comparison is largely self-referential: it tests dynamics-consistency\n      (did the world move as the model's state-response said?), NOT whether the model's VALUE is\n      right — because the realised IS the model's own output. A model can pass this layer forever\n      while being worthless at predicting real outcomes. Worse, fixing the obvious circularity\n      (making predict-effects state-sensitive) RELOCATES it rather than removing it: state-blindness\n      breaks, but the error *magnitude* stays model-priced."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "      Name and keep both layers explicitly. **L1 = G-vs-G dynamics**: cheap, every cycle, labelled\n      never-value-evidence; it earns its keep as a consistency/sanity layer. **L2 =\n      outcome-vs-prediction** against WITNESSED outcomes the model did not produce — closure-folds,\n      three-witness certificates, attested real functioning — scarce, gated behind the reward\n      discipline. Only L2 clears value. Forbid L1's cheap pass from being reported as L2 clearance."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      The only thing that escapes `prior == value` is a referent the model had no hand in. L1's\n      referent (the field) is the model's own output, so L1 can only ever measure internal\n      consistency. L2's referent (a witnessed outcome) is external, so it is the only layer that can\n      be WRONG about value — which is exactly why it is the gate, not an enhancement."} {:name "canonical-instance", :name-key "canonical-instance", :slug "canonical-instance", :text "      FutonZero G-SIM (WM-pilot, 2026-06-11): L1 dynamics live — the state-sensitive model is 2.55×\n      better than the constant counterfactual at predicting no-move — while L2 stays gated behind\n      pudding-G1's binding of the arrow-witness to witnessed affect/outcome. The split was forced by\n      discovering the calibration was self-certifying (`[[aif/no-self-certification]]`)."}], :conclusion "When you calibrate a model whose own outputs compose the field it is scored against, split the calibration into TWO layers and never let one stand in for the other. L1 — dynamics/consistency (does the field respond to measured state?): cheap, accrues every cycle, and is NEVER value evidence. L2 — outcome-vs-prediction (did real functioning result?): scarce, witnessed, gated, and is the ONLY value clearance.", :directives {:audience "anyone calibrating a value/forward model against a field the model helps compose", :keywords ["calibration" "prior-equals-value" "dynamics" "value" "witnessed outcome" "three-witness" "self-reference" "G-vs-G" "reward gating"], :sigils ["L1/L2"], :style "pattern", :title "Two-Layer Calibration (Dynamics-Consistency vs Witnessed-Outcome)", :tone "architectural"}, :id "aif/two-layer-calibration", :keywords ["calibration" "prior-equals-value" "dynamics" "value" "witnessed outcome" "three-witness" "self-reference" "G-vs-G" "reward gating"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["L1/L2"], :source-path "library/aif/two-layer-calibration.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Two-Layer Calibration (Dynamics-Consistency vs Witnessed-Outcome)", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Treat the baseline cyber-ant as a narratable instance of the Futon2 AIF loop:\n  its observation ABI, predictive-cycle trace, hunger dynamics, and policy harness\n  are all frozen so Futon5 can lift the behaviour into an operator."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are turning the Futon2 AIF loop into a portable pattern asset for Futon5."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    You want a generated agent to behave exactly like today’s handcrafted AIF\n    ants, including mode hysteresis, hunger/tau coupling, and softmax tilts."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    The implementation currently lives as code and tests inside Futon2; none of\n    those behaviours are catalogued as pattern data Futon5 can compile."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Canonically describe the loop from `g-observe` → `perceive/perceive` →\n    `affect/update-tau` → `policy/choose-action`, referencing the single-tick\n    trace (`doc/trace/single_tick.*`) and policy harness goldens so Futon5 has\n    deterministic source material."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Once the narrative and fixtures exist as pattern assets, Futon5 can attach a\n    `:cyber/baseline` operator that emits an AIF config identical to Futon2’s\n    defaults, making cyber-ants “pattern flashed” twins of the current species."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Bind this description to a Futon5 operator signature that exports the\n         `:aif` config delta plus textual explanation for cyber-ant flashing.]"}], :conclusion "  Treat the baseline cyber-ant as a narratable instance of the Futon2 AIF loop:\n  its observation ABI, predictive-cycle trace, hunger dynamics, and policy harness\n  are all frozen so Futon5 can lift the behaviour into an operator.", :directives {:audience "futon5 operator-smiths, futon2 simulation agents, future-you", :references ["futon2/doc/stack-baseline.md" "futon2/doc/trace/single_tick.edn" "futon2/test/resources/goldens/policy_harness.edn"], :sigils ["🗿/三" "🌝/四"], :style "pattern", :title "Baseline Cyber-AIF Ant", :tone "analytical"}, :id "ants/baseline-cyber-ant", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["futon2/doc/stack-baseline.md" "futon2/doc/trace/single_tick.edn" "futon2/test/resources/goldens/policy_harness.edn"], :scores {}, :sigils ["🗿/三" "🌝/四"], :source-path "library/ants/baseline-cyber-ant.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Baseline Cyber-AIF Ant", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  When an AIF agent carries significant cargo yet keeps returning without\n  depositing, clamp tau, penalise reuse of :return, and prioritise deposit\n  completion. The pattern generalises to any delivery/return loop."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are preventing cargo return thrash in cyber-ant policy."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The agent has cargo ≥ 0.05, repeatedly chooses :return while `:dep` = 0,\n    or stalls near the nest."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Without explicit clamps the agent “thrashes”: it walks home, refuses to\n    deposit, and never re-enters outbound mode, starving the colony or the task."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Detect cargo-stall streaks, apply negative G adjustments to :return until a\n    deposit event fires, slightly boost :hold when resting on the nest, and cap\n    tau around 0.65 so the agent exploits the completion action. Emit telemetry\n    for stalled runs so analyzers can intervene."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    The same logic applies to graph walkers (deliver package, reward commit) or\n    any pattern that alternates gather/deliver phases."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Bind this pattern to Futon5’s uplift so cyber-ants (and future courier\n         agents) inherit the stall detector and tau clamp.]"}], :conclusion "  When an AIF agent carries significant cargo yet keeps returning without\n  depositing, clamp tau, penalise reuse of :return, and prioritise deposit\n  completion. The pattern generalises to any delivery/return loop.", :directives {:audience "futon5 uplift authors, futon2 logisticians, futon3 librarians", :references ["futon2/src/ants/aif/policy.clj" "futon2/doc/trace/single_tick.edn" "futon2/test/resources/goldens/policy_harness.edn"], :sigils ["📐/归"], :style "pattern", :title "Cargo Return Discipline Pattern", :tone "investigative"}, :id "ants/cargo-return-discipline", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["futon2/src/ants/aif/policy.clj" "futon2/doc/trace/single_tick.edn" "futon2/test/resources/goldens/policy_harness.edn"], :scores {}, :sigils ["📐/归"], :source-path "library/ants/cargo-return-discipline.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Cargo Return Discipline Pattern", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Hunger drives precision: cyber-ants honour Futon2’s goldens so that `tick-hunger`\n  + `update-hunger` + `hunger->tau` feed into `modulate-precisions` and\n  `update-tau`, including reserve pressure and depot/risk contributions."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are exporting hunger-to-tau coupling so Futon5 can flash faithful agents."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The affect layer reports hunger trends (`dhdt`), ingest shortfalls, and\n    colony reserves matching the six golden scenarios (normal, starvation,\n    overstimulation, heavy-cargo, high-risk, high-ingest)."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Without explicit coupling rules the Futon5 operator cannot guarantee that a\n    flashed agent reproduces the tau annealing Futon2 already verifies."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Reference the hunger/tau goldens and declare the response curve: hungrier\n    ants lower tau, home safety lifts tau, deficit reserves subtract from tau,\n    and clamp logic cuts exploitation during high-risk cargo returns."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Futon5 needs those declarative mappings to export a faithful cyber-ant\n    affect config; otherwise the new agents would drift away from the proven\n    hunger dynamics."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Expose this coupling as metadata in the cyber-ant operator so Futon2\n         can attach the generated config per species.]"}], :conclusion "  Hunger drives precision: cyber-ants honour Futon2’s goldens so that `tick-hunger`\n  + `update-hunger` + `hunger->tau` feed into `modulate-precisions` and\n  `update-tau`, including reserve pressure and depot/risk contributions.", :directives {:audience "futon5 operator authors, futon2 affect engineers", :keywords ["hunger" "precision" "tau" "affect" "urgency" "survival" "exploit" "explore" "need" "drive"], :references ["futon2/test/resources/goldens/hunger_tau.edn" "futon2/src/ants/aif/affect.clj" "futon2/src/ants/aif/core.clj"], :sigils ["💫/六" "👴/万"], :style "pattern", :title "Hunger ↔ Precision Coupling Pattern", :tone "formal-analytic"}, :id "ants/hunger-precision-coupling", :keywords ["hunger" "precision" "tau" "affect" "urgency" "survival" "exploit" "explore" "need" "drive"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["futon2/test/resources/goldens/hunger_tau.edn" "futon2/src/ants/aif/affect.clj" "futon2/src/ants/aif/core.clj"], :scores {}, :sigils ["💫/六" "👴/万"], :source-path "library/ants/hunger-precision-coupling.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Hunger ↔ Precision Coupling Pattern", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Regulate pheromone laying by coupling trail gradients to hunger, novelty, and\n  reserve pressure so trails serve as communicative signals rather than noise."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are tuning pheromone output so trails carry useful signal."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The local pheromone signal is weak, novelty high, or reserves deficit, while\n    the agent is outbound or maintaining a corridor."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Without adaptive scaling, ants either flood the board with trails (entropy)\n    or fail to leave breadcrumbs when scouts discover food deserts."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Bias :pheromone G downward when novelty is high and cargo low, but penalise\n    it near the nest or during reserve crises; anneal tau upward to encourage\n    exploration pulses. Use the Futon2 analyzer’s `:pher` metrics to adjust the\n    laying rate."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    The same principle applies to graph walkers emitting hints or proofs: you\n    want signaling to adapt to novelty and resource pressure."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Expose the trail-tuner knobs via Futon5 so cyber-ants (and future graph\n         agents) can publish adjustable signal intensity.]"}], :conclusion "  Regulate pheromone laying by coupling trail gradients to hunger, novelty, and\n  reserve pressure so trails serve as communicative signals rather than noise.", :directives {:audience "futon5 improvisers, futon2 trail engineers, futon3 librarians", :references ["futon2/src/ants/aif/policy.clj" "futon2/doc/trace/single_tick.edn"], :sigils ["➰/去"], :style "pattern", :title "Adaptive Trail Tuner Pattern", :tone "investigative"}, :id "ants/pheromone-trail-tuner", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["futon2/src/ants/aif/policy.clj" "futon2/doc/trace/single_tick.edn"], :scores {}, :sigils ["➰/去"], :source-path "library/ants/pheromone-trail-tuner.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Adaptive Trail Tuner Pattern", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Cyber-ants inherit the “white space” doctrine: when food + pheromones slump\n  below the epsilon bands and novelty is high, anneal tau upward, penalise\n  foraging, and gently bias toward pheromone laying or scouting returns."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are declaring the white-space heuristic so cyber-ants can mirror Futon2 behavior."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    Observations satisfy the Futon2 `white?` detector — food trace, pheromone\n    trace, and recent gather are all low, and the ant is off-nest."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Without this guard the agents burn ticks circling barren cells, starving the\n    colony even when hunger pressure says “exploit elsewhere.”"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Encode the heuristics currently embedded in `policy/choose-action` as a\n    pattern operator: raise G-adjustments for :forage, lower them for :return and\n    :pheromone, and stretch tau when the scout sits on empty nest tiles."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Futon5 needs the declarative rule (“white space implies scout stance”) so it\n    can flash cyber-ants or future agents with the same homing instinct."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Link this pattern to the cyber-ant operator so Futon5 emits the\n         matching `:white-space` action deltas and tau clamps.]"}], :conclusion "  Cyber-ants inherit the “white space” doctrine: when food + pheromones slump\n  below the epsilon bands and novelty is high, anneal tau upward, penalise\n  foraging, and gently bias toward pheromone laying or scouting returns.", :directives {:audience "futon5 improvisers, futon2 telemetry readers", :keywords ["explore" "scout" "search" "discovery" "sparse" "empty" "novelty" "unknown" "frontier" "navigate"], :references ["futon2/doc/trace/single_tick.edn" "futon2/src/ants/aif/policy.clj" "futon2/test/resources/goldens/policy_harness.edn"], :sigils ["🌹/刊" "👎/二"], :style "pattern", :title "White-Space Scout Pattern", :tone "investigative"}, :id "ants/white-space-scout", :keywords ["explore" "scout" "search" "discovery" "sparse" "empty" "novelty" "unknown" "frontier" "navigate"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["futon2/doc/trace/single_tick.edn" "futon2/src/ants/aif/policy.clj" "futon2/test/resources/goldens/policy_harness.edn"], :scores {}, :sigils ["🌹/刊" "👎/二"], :source-path "library/ants/white-space-scout.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "White-Space Scout Pattern", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Action selection should be constrained by available budget or\n  license, not by priority alone. A proposed action is *gated* —\n  permitted to run — only when the operator's session-level mana\n  balance can sustain its expected cost. Block-closure at hinges\n  awards mana; allostatic-load drains it; the gate is the live\n  intersection of \"what does this action cost\" and \"what does the\n  session have to spend.\" Without the gate, priority sorting alone\n  recommends everything that is locally desirable, regardless of\n  whether the substrate can sustain its execution. With the gate,\n  the system selects within a budget the operator's metabolic state\n  can actually afford."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "    The candidate invariant `mana-gated-work` was named in the\n    futon5a stack-stereolithography queue (`run-064`,\n    `F-budgeted-action-selection`) with the summary: \"Action\n    selection should be constrained by available budget or license,\n    not by priority alone. Some proposed work should be selectable\n    only when enough budget is available, but this remains weakly\n    implemented.\" The mission `M-bounded-in-flight-state` formalises\n    the *balance dynamic* (`structure/mana-allostasis`) the gate\n    operates on; this pattern names the gate itself.\n\n    Production precedent: `futon5/scripts/nonstarter_mana.clj`'s\n    `sospeso` command computes `(1-p)*C` to pool as pure *dana*\n    (Buddhist generosity) when an action of cost C is committed at\n    confidence p. The pool IS the session-level budget; the\n    confidence-weighted cost IS the gating signal."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The system is selecting which proposed action to run from a\n    queue (the priority queue, the mission queue, the agent\n    backpack, etc.), and the actions vary in cost."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Pure priority-sorted selection has no metabolic awareness:\n      - High-priority but high-cost actions can be selected even\n        when the session's mana balance is depleted, accelerating\n        substrate corruption.\n      - Low-priority but low-cost actions that would maintain\n        balance never get a turn because higher-priority items\n        always exist.\n      - The operator's felt fatigue has no path into the action-\n        selection loop."} {:name "ANTI-PATTERNS", :name-key "anti-patterns", :slug "anti-patterns", :text "      - **Priority-only selection** (no gate): the default broken\n        state this pattern names.\n      - **Hard-cap-only selection** (no priority): everything below\n        a threshold runs in arbitrary order; misses important work.\n      - **Per-agent budgets** (precursor): see\n        `p4ng/agent-as-mana-unit-was-the-wrong-unit`.\n      - **Counterfeit gate-pass**: action declares its cost as\n        lower than it actually is to slip through. Mitigated by\n        cost-confidence weighting (sospeso) and post-hoc\n        reconciliation against actual block-closure cost."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Gate the selector: a candidate action runs if and only if the\n    session's current mana balance can afford its expected cost.\n    Define cost in terms of the channels of `mana-allostasis`:\n    working-tree pressure increment, mission-proliferation increment,\n    session-sprawl increment, untriaged-evidence increment. Define\n    *afford* in terms of the session's running balance + a small\n    safety margin. Reject (with `:reason :insufficient-budget`)\n    rather than execute when the gate fails.\n\n    Selection becomes a two-step process:\n      1. Priority sort the candidate set.\n      2. Walk the sorted list; for each candidate, ask the gate\n         \"can the session afford this?\" Take the first that\n         passes; defer the rest with explicit reason."} {:name "DIAGNOSTIC", :name-key "diagnostic", :slug "diagnostic", :text "      - Selector consistently rejects high-priority items: budget\n        is depleted at session level. Operator should close\n        blocks (any drain channel) before continuing.\n      - Selector consistently accepts everything: gate may not be\n        wired, or budget thresholds may be too generous. Recheck\n        cost-quantum estimates.\n      - Selector accepts one action then immediately rejects the\n        next at apparently equal cost: stochastic gate behaviour\n        points at miscalibrated confidence in the cost estimator."} {:name "ON-DANA-AS-COST", :name-key "on-dana-as-cost", :slug "on-dana-as-cost", :text "      The `nonstarter_mana.clj` precursor's *sospeso → dana* pattern\n      is operationally sound. An action committed at confidence p\n      with cost C *gives* `(1-p)*C` to the pool — confidence-\n      discounted cost is the right cost-quantum because low-confidence\n      actions burn budget proportionally more even when they\n      succeed. Dana (generosity) is the right metaphor: an action's\n      cost is given to the future operator who will close the\n      eventual block. The session that takes on cost is the session\n      that owes the closure."} {:name "ON-UNIT-CORRECTION", :name-key "on-unit-correction", :slug "on-unit-correction", :text "      Action-selection budgets attach to sessions, not to agents.\n      See `p4ng/agent-as-mana-unit-was-the-wrong-unit` for the\n      precursor reasoning. An agent has no metabolism beyond its\n      turn; the session is the temporal frame in which budgets\n      integrate. A multi-agent session pools its mana economy at\n      the session level, not partitioned per-agent."} {:name "REFERENCES", :name-key "references", :slug "references", :text "      - structure/mana-allostasis — the balance dynamic the gate consults.\n      - structure/block-as-futonic-revolution — the unit whose closure replenishes the budget.\n      - invariant-coherence/drain-channel-shape — the per-channel cost estimators feed the gate.\n      - p4ng/agent-as-mana-unit-was-the-wrong-unit — the precursor unit-error this pattern corrects.\n      - futon5a/holes/stack-stereolithography-runs/run-064-budgeted-action-selection--mana-gated-work.md — the candidate invariant in the queue this pattern formalises.\n      - futon5/scripts/nonstarter_mana.clj — the production-code precursor."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Priority captures what matters in the abstract; budget captures\n    what the system can actually sustain right now. Both readings\n    are needed. A priority-only selector is a recipe for substrate\n    corruption (the operator runs out of capacity to close blocks\n    faster than load accumulates). A budget-only selector is a\n    recipe for stagnation (high-priority work never gets selected\n    because something cheaper always squeezes in). The gate\n    composes them."}], :conclusion "  Action selection should be constrained by available budget or\n  license, not by priority alone. A proposed action is *gated* —\n  permitted to run — only when the operator's session-level mana\n  balance can sustain its expected cost. Block-closure at hinges\n  awards mana; allostatic-load drains it; the gate is the live\n  intersection of \"what does this action cost\" and \"what does the\n  session have to spend.\" Without the gate, priority sorting alone\n  recommends everything that is locally desirable, regardless of\n  whether the substrate can sustain its execution. With the gate,\n  the system selects within a budget the operator's metabolic state\n  can actually afford.", :directives {:audience "action selectors, agent dispatch implementers, mission-control operators", :factor "Wise-restraint (saṁyama) — selecting only what budget allows", :keywords ["mana" "budget" "license" "action-selection" "gating" "dana" "sospeso"], :references ["structure/mana-allostasis" "structure/block-as-futonic-revolution" "invariant-coherence/drain-channel-shape" "p4ng/agent-as-mana-unit-was-the-wrong-unit"], :sigils ["💰/可"], :style "pattern", :title "Mana-Gated Work: action selection bounded by available budget", :tone "analytic-foundational"}, :id "budgeted-action-selection/mana-gated-work", :keywords ["mana" "budget" "license" "action-selection" "gating" "dana" "sospeso"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["structure/mana-allostasis" "structure/block-as-futonic-revolution" "invariant-coherence/drain-channel-shape" "p4ng/agent-as-mana-unit-was-the-wrong-unit"], :scores {}, :sigils ["💰/可"], :source-path "library/budgeted-action-selection/mana-gated-work.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Mana-Gated Work: action selection bounded by available budget", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "When several Missions depend on one shared knowledge structure that no single Mission can own, run them through a **Campaign** — a Temporary Autonomous Institution one tier above Mission that charters a joint goal, delivers and verifies the shared standard, and then **dissolves**, leaving the constituent Missions as the durable residue."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Some work decomposes into a cluster of Missions that all build towards the *same* standard (a metric, schema, contract, substrate). The cluster has coordination needs — ordering, a shared deliverable, a joint completion criterion — that sit above any one Mission."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    You have multi-Mission work whose members share one dependency, and the joint goal is real but owned by no single Mission."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Mission-shape mis-fits this two ways (see [[campaign-coherence/shared-standard-has-no-single-owner]]): a Mission that grows to hold the cluster loses Mission discipline; a tangle of Missions with no organising frame lets each look successful while the joint goal slips. And a *standing* institution over-fits: it persists past its purpose, accreting coordination overhead with no end."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Charter a **Temporary Autonomous Institution** with the 7-phase Campaign spine: CHARTER (joint goal, often from a War Bulletin) → CONSTITUTION (survey dependents + governance) → ESCROW (register held dependencies) → STANDARD-ARGUE → STANDARD-VERIFY (the shared standard, fit for all) → RUN/DELIVER (keystone ships it) → DISSOLUTION. Constituent Missions run their **own** lifecycles; the Campaign coordinates, never re-runs their phases. Built to end: when the joint completion criterion fires, the Campaign dissolves and the Missions remain."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Temporariness is the point. The cluster needs an institution *for a while* — to own the shared standard's cross-mission adequacy and the escrow that holds dependent work — but a permanent one would outlive its goal and become overhead. Autonomy (self-governance for the duration) + temporariness (dissolution on joint-criterion) is exactly what a bounded cross-mission effort wants. The War Room persists and narrates; the Campaign ends and delivers."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Distinguish keystone Mission (builds the standard) from paired/dependent Missions (consumers) in CHARTER membership.\n    - Make the joint completion criterion genuinely *cross-mission* (\"standard verified AND ≥1 dependent requirement released-and-consumed\"), never \"all members done.\"\n    - At DISSOLUTION, hand any unsatisfied escrow entries back as ordinary dependencies / follow-on missions — named, never dropped.\n    - First instance: `Campaign-substrate-completion` (keystone M-substrate-metric; paired M-aif2 + M-differentiable-code)."}], :conclusion "When several Missions depend on one shared knowledge structure that no single Mission can own, run them through a **Campaign** — a Temporary Autonomous Institution one tier above Mission that charters a joint goal, delivers and verifies the shared standard, and then **dissolves**, leaving the constituent Missions as the durable residue.", :directives {:audience "futonic campaign authors, mission coordinators, anyone with multi-mission work sharing one dependency", :keywords ["campaign" "temporary-autonomous-institution" "mission-cluster" "shared-standard" "charter" "dissolution" "tier-above-mission" "TAZ"], :references ["campaign-coherence/shared-standard-has-no-single-owner" "campaign-coherence/cross-mission-escrow" "structure/what-problem-is-this-actually-solving"], :sigils ["⛺/會"], :style "pattern", :title "The Campaign Is a Temporary Autonomous Institution", :tone "declarative"}, :id "campaign-coherence/campaign-as-temporary-institution", :keywords ["campaign" "temporary-autonomous-institution" "mission-cluster" "shared-standard" "charter" "dissolution" "tier-above-mission" "TAZ"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["campaign-coherence/shared-standard-has-no-single-owner" "campaign-coherence/cross-mission-escrow" "structure/what-problem-is-this-actually-solving"], :scores {}, :sigils ["⛺/會" "(CHARTER→CONSTITUTION→ESCROW→STANDARD-ARGUE→STANDARD-VERIFY→RUN/DELIVER→DISSOLUTION)\""], :source-path "library/campaign-coherence/campaign-as-temporary-institution.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "The Campaign Is a Temporary Autonomous Institution", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Register each cross-Mission dependency on a shared standard as a **held requirement** that releases in two steps — `held → contract-released` (on STANDARD-VERIFY) `→ satisfied` (on delivery) — so dependent work is *named but not built on sand*, and the requirement neither blocks the cluster nor silently disappears."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "In a Campaign, paired (dependent) Missions need things from the shared standard the keystone Mission delivers. They must not build the dependent slices before the standard exists — but those requirements must not be forgotten either."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    A Mission has work that depends on a shared standard another Mission (or the Campaign) is still establishing."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Two naive options both fail. Build the dependent slice now → you build on sand (the standard may change or mis-fit). Drop the requirement until \"later\" → it goes silent and the dependent Mission quietly diverges or the joint goal slips. And a single release point (\"wait until the standard ships\") blocks the dependent Mission longer than necessary — it could be *designing to the spec* well before the live delivery exists."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Put every dependency in an **escrow ledger** entry: `{:from-mission, :on <standard|keystone>, :requirement, :status}`. Mark the dependent slice `:held` (named, not built). Release in **two steps**: STANDARD-VERIFY freezes the contract → flip to `:contract-released` (the Mission may now build *to the verified spec*, unblocked but not on sand); RUN/DELIVER ships the live standard → flip to `:satisfied` (the Mission consumes it). At DISSOLUTION, any entry not `:satisfied` becomes an ordinary dependency / follow-on mission — named, never dropped."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    The two-step release separates *contract certainty* from *delivery*: a verified contract is enough to build against (decoupling the dependent Mission's progress from the keystone's delivery latency), while live delivery is what completes consumption. Naming the requirement in a ledger makes \"we're holding off building this\" a first-class, auditable state rather than an implicit gap. This is HEAD-as-escrow (a mission HEAD that crystallises intent without committing to schema) lifted to the cross-mission case: the requirement is escrowed *across* the Campaign's membership."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - One ledger entry per dependency; status is one of `:held | :contract-released | :satisfied`.\n    - Name what triggers each release: STANDARD-VERIFY → contract-released; RUN/DELIVER → satisfied.\n    - Treat `:held` count + oldest-held age as Campaign-health telemetry (a long-held requirement is a stall signal).\n    - First ledger: Campaign-substrate-completion — `M-aif2` holds \"curvature as tension-signal\", `M-differentiable-code` holds \"continuous embedding for code-graph gradients\", both `:on M-substrate-metric`."}], :conclusion "Register each cross-Mission dependency on a shared standard as a **held requirement** that releases in two steps — `held → contract-released` (on STANDARD-VERIFY) `→ satisfied` (on delivery) — so dependent work is *named but not built on sand*, and the requirement neither blocks the cluster nor silently disappears.", :directives {:audience "futonic campaign authors, mission coordinators managing dependencies on a shared standard", :keywords ["escrow" "held-requirement" "cross-mission-dependency" "two-step-release" "contract-released" "HEAD-as-escrow" "build-to-verified-spec"], :references ["campaign-coherence/campaign-as-temporary-institution" "campaign-coherence/shared-standard-has-no-single-owner" "futon-theory/mission-dependency" "futon-theory/mission-interface-signature"], :sigils ["🔒/託"], :style "pattern", :title "Cross-Mission Escrow — Hold Dependent Work in Two Release Steps", :tone "declarative"}, :id "campaign-coherence/cross-mission-escrow", :keywords ["escrow" "held-requirement" "cross-mission-dependency" "two-step-release" "contract-released" "HEAD-as-escrow" "build-to-verified-spec"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["campaign-coherence/campaign-as-temporary-institution" "campaign-coherence/shared-standard-has-no-single-owner" "futon-theory/mission-dependency" "futon-theory/mission-interface-signature"], :scores {}, :sigils ["🔒/託"], :source-path "library/campaign-coherence/cross-mission-escrow.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Cross-Mission Escrow — Hold Dependent Work in Two Release Steps", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "A shared standard that several Missions must build towards needs an ARGUE + VERIFY of its **cross-mission adequacy** that no constituent Mission can own; that ownerless adequacy-check is precisely why Campaign-shape exists, and it is the Campaign's load-bearing phase (STANDARD-VERIFY)."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Several Missions depend on one shared knowledge structure (a metric, schema, contract, substrate). Each Mission knows what *it* needs from the standard; none can see whether the standard fits *all* of them."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    A standard must be the same across multiple Missions, and \"is this standard adequate for everyone who depends on it?\" is a question with an answer."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    No single Mission can answer it. Absorb the standard into one Mission and it gets built to that Mission's needs — the others inherit a mis-fit and each *looks* successful while the joint goal silently slips (proxy-problem substitution). Leave it implicit and each Mission builds its own version — it **forks** into per-Mission shadows that don't compose."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Make the standard's **cross-mission adequacy** a first-class deliverable owned by a Campaign. STANDARD-ARGUE argues *why this standard, fit for all consumers*; STANDARD-VERIFY verifies it **as a design, before any Mission builds to it** (logic-model-before-code at Campaign tier: conforming witness ⇒ 0 violations; one adversarial trace per consumer-requirement ⇒ caught). A requirement the standard can't meet is a Campaign blocker, not a Mission's problem. On VERIFY-pass the contract freezes — *that* is \"the shared standard to build towards.\""} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    The adequacy check is structurally ownerless: it ranges over requirements that live in *different* Missions, so it can only be performed one tier up. This is the `structure/what-problem-is-this-actually-solving` answer to \"why not just a Mission?\" — without the Campaign, the shared standard is either captured (mis-fit for the others) or forked (no shared standard at all). Verifying it before anyone commits is what stops the Missions building toward a moving or mis-fit target."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - In STANDARD-ARGUE, show per-consumer that the standard satisfies each escrowed requirement; surface any it can't (revise the standard or re-scope the member).\n    - In STANDARD-VERIFY, plant one adversarial trace per consumer-requirement; the coverage contract is per-requirement, not sampled.\n    - Keep this distinct from the keystone Mission's *own* VERIFY: the Campaign verifies \"fit for all consumers\" (the contract); the Mission verifies \"this build is sound\" (the implementation).\n    - Worked failure to cite: war-bulletin-10 WR-21's three-way fork of the thin scalar field — the evidence that a single Mission could not have owned the substrate-2 metric."}], :conclusion "A shared standard that several Missions must build towards needs an ARGUE + VERIFY of its **cross-mission adequacy** that no constituent Mission can own; that ownerless adequacy-check is precisely why Campaign-shape exists, and it is the Campaign's load-bearing phase (STANDARD-VERIFY).", :directives {:audience "futonic campaign authors, mission coordinators, anyone tempted to absorb a shared dependency into one mission", :keywords ["shared-standard" "cross-mission-adequacy" "campaign-grounding" "proxy-problem" "fork" "standard-verify" "no-single-owner"], :references ["campaign-coherence/campaign-as-temporary-institution" "campaign-coherence/cross-mission-escrow" "structure/what-problem-is-this-actually-solving" "mission-coherence/logic-model-before-code"], :sigils ["📐/共"], :style "pattern", :title "A Shared Standard Has No Single-Mission Owner — That's Why the Campaign Exists", :tone "declarative"}, :id "campaign-coherence/shared-standard-has-no-single-owner", :keywords ["shared-standard" "cross-mission-adequacy" "campaign-grounding" "proxy-problem" "fork" "standard-verify" "no-single-owner"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["campaign-coherence/campaign-as-temporary-institution" "campaign-coherence/cross-mission-escrow" "structure/what-problem-is-this-actually-solving" "mission-coherence/logic-model-before-code"], :scores {}, :sigils ["📐/共"], :source-path "library/campaign-coherence/shared-standard-has-no-single-owner.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "A Shared Standard Has No Single-Mission Owner — That's Why the Campaign Exists", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  A career-mode tension that does NOT resolve by picking a side. The mode\n  with the most autonomy and the highest upside — **C** (consulting /\n  \"free solo\") — is also the mode with **unbounded downside**: a single\n  failure can be terminal (\"sudden death\"). Both naive resolutions fail.\n  Pure-C free-solos an un-roped route and bets the operator on never\n  falling. Pure-**A** (industry) caps the downside but surrenders the\n  autonomy and optionality that were *the actual objective* — the rope\n  becomes a cage.\n\n  The resolved form is **the rope**: hold a **basket** (B academic + C\n  consulting) in which **B backstops C's tail**, and treat any A-commitment\n  as a **bounded experiment that preserves optionality** — sample the arm,\n  observe, retain the option, do not dilute. The Anthropic Fellowship's\n  4-month-bounded-with-retention shape is exactly this: a Thompson-sampling\n  move on A-mode that keeps the rest of the basket live. *Before you free-solo\n  El Capitan, climb it roped a bunch of times.*"} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "The same operator-thinking-with-metaphor surfaces independently\n    across the corpus, which is what makes it a pattern and not an\n    essay-local mood:\n    - **Anthropic Fellows Q7 / §6** — the B+C basket; B as \"the rope that\n      prevents C-mode failures from leading to sudden death\"; the Fellowship\n      as temp-A retaining optionality; \"applying ≠ taking\"; not\n      equity-dilutive.\n    - **Glasgow Q6** — \"Before you climb El Capitan without a rope, you\n      should do it with a rope a bunch of times\" (the academic role as the\n      rope for learning the route).\n    - **COGITO §6** — the free-solo-vs-funding-pursuit contradiction; the\n      \"job as customer\" reframe.\n    The interest-mining reduce flagged free-solo/rope as a cross-EOI\n    *merge candidate* (shared backbone). This pattern IS that shared\n    identity: a constellation node that points HERE is a peer in the\n    topology without claiming to be an interest-star."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "The tension is a genuine conflict of forces; the pattern holds\n    them, it does not dissolve them (see substructure)."} {:name "FORCES", :name-key "forces", :slug "forces", :text "      - **Autonomy / upside** pulls toward C (the work Joe actually wants to\n        do is C-shaped: research-entrepreneur, missing-customer, Hyperreal).\n      - **Tail-risk / ruin** pulls toward A's floor (C-mode failures\n        compound; one can be terminal).\n      - **The goal is the optionality itself** — so a resolution that\n        eliminates optionality (pure-A) has defeated its own purpose, even\n        though it \"wins\" on downside.\n      - **A bounded experiment is not a commitment** — applying ≠ taking;\n        a retention-option is information-with-the-arm-kept, not a trap."} {:name "ANTI-PATTERNS", :name-key "anti-patterns", :slug "anti-patterns", :text "      - **Free-soloing without a rope.** Choosing C with no B backstop:\n        one failure is terminal. This is the failure the rope exists to\n        prevent.\n      - **Abandoning C for A's safety.** Caps the downside but surrenders\n        the autonomy that was the objective. The rope becomes the cage; the\n        operator is safe and not doing the work they wanted.\n      - **Reading \"rope\" as \"never solo\".** The rope is for *learning the\n        route*, not for never climbing it. A permanent rope is a never-realised C.\n      - **Collapsing a bounded experiment into a commitment.** Treating the\n        Fellowship (or any temp-A) as terminal — reading the fine print as a\n        trap, or taking it as if permanent — discards the\n        info-gain-with-retained-option that made the arm worth sampling.\n      - **Modelling the modes as statically fungible.** Reputation, skill,\n        and customer-relationships accumulate in one mode and translate\n        (with friction) into another; a resolution that ignores the\n        exchange-matrix M_{ij} mis-prices every basket."}], :conclusion "  A career-mode tension that does NOT resolve by picking a side. The mode\n  with the most autonomy and the highest upside — **C** (consulting /\n  \"free solo\") — is also the mode with **unbounded downside**: a single\n  failure can be terminal (\"sudden death\"). Both naive resolutions fail.\n  Pure-C free-solos an un-roped route and bets the operator on never\n  falling. Pure-**A** (industry) caps the downside but surrenders the\n  autonomy and optionality that were *the actual objective* — the rope\n  becomes a cage.\n\n  The resolved form is **the rope**: hold a **basket** (B academic + C\n  consulting) in which **B backstops C's tail**, and treat any A-commitment\n  as a **bounded experiment that preserves optionality** — sample the arm,\n  observe, retain the option, do not dilute. The Anthropic Fellowship's\n  4-month-bounded-with-retention shape is exactly this: a Thompson-sampling\n  move on A-mode that keeps the rest of the basket live. *Before you free-solo\n  El Capitan, climb it roped a bunch of times.*", :directives {:audience "an operator choosing among career modes (industry / academic / consulting), or any agent modelling a livelihood trajectory where the highest-autonomy option also carries the highest ruin-risk; the interest-network constellation, which points at this pattern instead of minting a \"Tension:\" interest-star", :factor "Equanimity (upekkhā)", :keywords ["career" "free-solo" "rope" "optionality" "basket" "tail-risk" "bounded-experiment" "A-B-C-modes" "thompson-sampling" "sudden-death"], :references ["futon-theory/all-or-nothing" "futon-theory/wyrd"], :sigils ["🧗/繩"], :title "Free Solo and the Rope (the autonomy/tail-risk career tension)", :tone "structural-generative"}, :id "career-coherence/free-solo-vs-rope", :keywords ["career" "free-solo" "rope" "optionality" "basket" "tail-risk" "bounded-experiment" "A-B-C-modes" "thompson-sampling" "sudden-death"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["futon-theory/all-or-nothing" "futon-theory/wyrd"], :scores {}, :sigils ["🧗/繩"], :source-path "library/career-coherence/free-solo-vs-rope.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Free Solo and the Rope (the autonomy/tail-risk career tension)", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Keep MUSN behaviour legible by pruning experiments, deprecated flows, and\n  orphaned namespaces before they are mistaken for supported features."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are aligning the codebase with the devmap so only supported paths remain visible."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    Repository-transition diagnostics already insist on tracking code paths to\n    explicit obligations."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    RID-era handlers and paused experiments still live in the tree with no\n    devmap references, confusing refactors and reviewers."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Schedule periodic dead-code sweeps: identify unreferenced namespaces,\n    obsolete protocol handlers, and one-off experiments; delete or archive them;\n    and ensure devmaps point only at active code paths."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Dhammavicaya requires that “what the system does” and “what the devmap\n    claims” match; dead code collapses that link."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Run `clojure -M:test` with namespace coverage and flag modules unused outside tests.]\n    next[Archive RID protocol handlers once MUSN adapters replace them, citing commits in the evidence ledger.]"}], :conclusion "  Keep MUSN behaviour legible by pruning experiments, deprecated flows, and\n  orphaned namespaces before they are mistaken for supported features.", :directives {:audience "maintainers, transport stewards", :factor "Keen investigation (dhammavicaya)", :keywords ["unused" "remove" "cleanup" "delete" "unreferenced" "stale" "orphan" "prune" "deprecate"], :references ["repository-transition/diagnostics" "repository-transition/strawman" "stack-coherence/staleness-scan" "stack-coherence/evidence-ledger"], :sigils ["🌆/本" "🌹/门"], :title "Dead Code Hygiene", :tone "analytic-neutral"}, :id "code-coherence/dead-code-hygiene", :keywords ["unused" "remove" "cleanup" "delete" "unreferenced" "stale" "orphan" "prune" "deprecate"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["repository-transition/diagnostics" "repository-transition/strawman" "stack-coherence/staleness-scan" "stack-coherence/evidence-ledger"], :scores {}, :sigils ["🌆/本" "🌹/门"], :source-path "library/code-coherence/dead-code-hygiene.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Dead Code Hygiene", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "When prose claims \"X subsumes Y\" / \"Y becomes free given X\" / \"Y falls out of X\", REQUIRE that BOTH X and the subsumed Y are actually live (implemented + verified), or BOTH are explicitly flagged deferred. A chained-claim verb standing between two absent things papers over a DOUBLE absence — and reads as rigorous while doing it."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "A mission or design doc asserts a derivation chain — \"subsumes\", \"becomes free\",\n    \"falls out of\", \"for free\", \"as a (free) bonus\", \"layer-0 byproduct\" — between a subsumer X\n    and a subsumed Y. The sentence is well-cited, internally consistent, structured. It looks done."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      A doc asserts a subsumption / derivation between X and Y using a chained-claim verb, and a\n      reader (or the author) is about to treat the relationship as established."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      The verb's rhetorical force makes the claim read as rigorous even when NEITHER X nor Y is\n      live — high-definition prose substituting for the reality (the perfect-crime failure mode,\n      `[[M-the-perfect-crime]]`). Checking only one side is not enough: the crime is specifically\n      \"X subsumes Y with neither X nor Y actually live.\" A one-sided check passes the half that\n      happens to exist and waves the absent half through."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "      Gate the claim with a TWO-SIDED live-check. Is the subsumer X implemented and verified? Is\n      the subsumed Y implemented and verified? If BOTH live → the claim is checkable; proceed and\n      cite the evidence. If EITHER is absent → do one of: (a) make it live, or (b) FLAG it deferred\n      explicitly (\"X will subsume Y once both land; neither is live yet\"). Never let the verb stand\n      unqualified between two absences."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      A subsumption claim is a promise that one live thing absorbs another live thing's work. With\n      both absent, the promise has no referents — it is the high-definition map of a territory that\n      does not exist. This is the sharper sibling of we-do-discipline (`[[feedback_we_do_discipline]]`):\n      we-do guards aspirational capability claims in general; this guards the specific, seductive\n      shape where a *relation verb* between two absences inherits the credibility of a verified\n      derivation. The forensic target is the authoring practice, not the code."} {:name "canonical-instance", :name-key "canonical-instance", :slug "canonical-instance", :text "      The corpus-wide \"Tornhill's hotspots become free\" / \"subsumes code-maat\" claims audited in\n      `[[M-the-perfect-crime]]` — chained-claim verbs asserting subsumption where neither the\n      Tornhill instrument nor its downstream consumer was live (84 raw hits / 74 candidates across\n      38 files). The verb made each read as a settled derivation; the two-sided live-check is what\n      separates the real ones from the crime."} {:name "how-to-apply", :name-key "how-to-apply", :slug "how-to-apply", :text "      In review, grep changed docs for the verb-set above; for each hit, name X and Y and ask \"is\n      each live?\" Promote confirmed-both-absent hits to the perfect-crime register (sorrys-on-\n      authoring, distinct from `sorrys.edn`). Graduated from `[[feedback_subsumption_claim_discipline]]`\n      (memory) to library-grade on 2026-06-11 per M-the-perfect-crime's carried-forward item."}], :conclusion "When prose claims \"X subsumes Y\" / \"Y becomes free given X\" / \"Y falls out of X\", REQUIRE that BOTH X and the subsumed Y are actually live (implemented + verified), or BOTH are explicitly flagged deferred. A chained-claim verb standing between two absent things papers over a DOUBLE absence — and reads as rigorous while doing it.", :directives {:audience "mission authors, reviewers, anyone writing \"X subsumes Y\" in a design doc", :keywords ["subsumption" "chained claim" "becomes-free" "falls-out-of" "double absence" "live-check" "perfect crime" "authoring discipline" "we-do-discipline"], :sigils ["⊇/∅"], :style "pattern", :title "Subsumption-Claim Discipline (Both Sides Live, or Both Flagged)", :tone "corrective"}, :id "code-coherence/subsumption-claim-discipline", :keywords ["subsumption" "chained claim" "becomes-free" "falls-out-of" "double absence" "live-check" "perfect crime" "authoring discipline" "we-do-discipline"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["⊇/∅"], :source-path "library/code-coherence/subsumption-claim-discipline.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Subsumption-Claim Discipline (Both Sides Live, or Both Flagged)", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "When a choice has a FINDABLE warrant — a standing contract, a documented preference, the method, or prior coaching — name the warrant (a proto-PSR) and proceed. Reserve operator escalations for GENUINELY undetermined forks (competing legitimate values, irreversible, operator-life-decision). Escalating a determined fork burns a round-trip and abdicates a judgement you are equipped to make."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You face a fork — which target, which approach, which option — and feel the pull to\n    ask the operator rather than decide."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      You are about to escalate a choice to the operator (or, autonomous, to hold and wait)."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      Most forks are *determined*: the warrant exists and is findable — the standing contract\n      already prefers X; the doc names the next step; the method derives it; you were coached on it.\n      Escalating a determined fork is over-asking: it costs an operator round-trip and signals you\n      cannot make a judgement you can. (Over-asking is as much a discipline failure as over-reaching.)"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "      Run the test: *is the warrant findable?* If YES → name it explicitly (a proto-PSR: \"choosing\n      X because the contract/doc/method/coaching says so\") and proceed. If NO — genuinely\n      undetermined: competing legitimate values, irreversible, or an operator-life-decision → escalate\n      it; or, in an autonomous run, take the safe default (skip/hold) and QUEUE the question for\n      review rather than block."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      The operator's attention is the scarce resource; spend it only where the choice is genuinely\n      theirs. This is the inverse of trusting the method early (`[[feedback_trust_the_method_early]]`):\n      don't *force* a direction prematurely, but don't *ask* when it is already determined. The\n      warranted-work layer (`[[M-chipwitz-corps]]`) is the institutional form of this rule — supply\n      the warrant and proceed."} {:name "canonical-instance", :name-key "canonical-instance", :slug "canonical-instance", :text "      The WM-pilot cycle-5 A/B fork (full vs partial discharge) was put to the operator as a\n      question, but the standing contract already preferred the measured-pair path — it should have\n      been proto-PSR'd and flown. The over-ask became the ChipWitz lesson; the retrospective that\n      first flattered it as \"correct escalation\" was itself caught by the review split. Distilled\n      WM-pilot 2026-06-11."}], :conclusion "When a choice has a FINDABLE warrant — a standing contract, a documented preference, the method, or prior coaching — name the warrant (a proto-PSR) and proceed. Reserve operator escalations for GENUINELY undetermined forks (competing legitimate values, irreversible, operator-life-decision). Escalating a determined fork burns a round-trip and abdicates a judgement you are equipped to make.", :directives {:audience "agents deciding whether to escalate a choice to the operator", :keywords ["fork" "warrant" "proto-PSR" "over-asking" "operator attention" "escalation" "safe-default" "autonomy" "ChipWitz"], :sigils ["⑂/⊢"], :style "pattern", :title "Determined Forks Get a Warrant, Not a Question", :tone "corrective"}, :id "collaboration-coherence/determined-fork-proto-psr", :keywords ["fork" "warrant" "proto-PSR" "over-asking" "operator attention" "escalation" "safe-default" "autonomy" "ChipWitz"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["⑂/⊢"], :source-path "library/collaboration-coherence/determined-fork-proto-psr.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Determined Forks Get a Warrant, Not a Question", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Collaborative work needs deliberate, recurring coupling to its surrounding field — adjacent projects, external readers, prior art — or it accretes its own private vocabulary, repeats solved problems, and rejects useful inputs as foreign.  The pattern is to schedule and structure the coupling, not to hope it happens; the surface form to detect (\"Not Invented Here,\" fenced field, blinkered horse) is named in Guattari's transversality framing as the absence of a coefficient that has to be set."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Distributed peer-learning projects and small teams default to inward attention.  In-group vocabulary tightens; in-group references stack; the project becomes legible to itself faster than it becomes legible to anyone else.  Without counter-pressure, the same dynamics that produce focus produce isolation.  Adjacent projects solve overlapping problems independently and never compose.  External readers find no entry path.  The \"transversality coefficient\" — the rate of permitted exposure — has to be set deliberately or it sets itself to zero."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The pattern operates on the axis: focus ↔ exposure.\n    Irreducible: a project cannot simultaneously be at maximum inward attention and maximum outward porosity.  Each unit of coupling work costs focus; each unit of unbroken focus costs coupling."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Real costs: deep focus is what lets a small group make progress on hard problems.  Sustained focus without exposure is what makes that progress invisible to (and unusable by) anyone outside it.  Both effects are continuous; the question is whether the coupling rate is named.\n    Active dynamic: the pull recurs whenever a deadline tightens, when a project encounters its first internal vocabulary that wasn't there before, and when a newcomer arrives and finds the entry-cost prohibitive."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "      - Not-invented-here build: the group writes a custom version of a tool / framework / vocabulary that already exists, without recording why the existing artefact was rejected.\n      - Vocabulary drift: in-group terms calcify with no equivalent in adjacent fields; the project becomes harder to explain over time, not easier.\n      - Newcomer rejection: arrivals consistently bounce off; the group attributes it to the newcomer rather than reading it as exposure-surface failure.\n      - Cross-project blindness: another project solves the same problem in parallel and the group doesn't notice for months or years.\n      - Defensive overfocus: criticism from outside is reflexively rejected as \"they don't understand the context\"; the rejection is the symptom, not the response."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Operate an explicit move on the named axis (see substructure below); steward the pattern against the declared discipline."} {:name "COMPOSITIONS", :name-key "compositions", :slug "compositions", :text "      (1) Scheduled exposure: name a recurring slot — monthly external review, periodic prior-art audit, regular cross-project show-and-tell — where the project deliberately steps outside its own frame.  Cadence matters more than depth.\n      (2) Adjacent-project mapping: maintain a list of projects working on overlapping problems, with current state and one-line interface.  The list is a wrapper artefact; the maintenance is the coupling.\n      (3) NIH-detection: when the group reaches for a custom build, ask explicitly what existing artefact was considered and rejected.  Make reuse the default branch; force \"make\" to justify itself.\n      (4) Newcomer-as-probe: treat each newcomer's stuck moment as evidence that the coupling rate is too low — the entry path measures the project's exposure surface.\n      (5) Friendly external readers: maintain at least one named external reader who isn't a contributor but knows the project well enough to flag in-group drift."} {:name "CHECK", :name-key "check", :slug "check", :text "      Engaged when: the project's vocabulary, references, and design choices are recognisably composed with its surrounding field, and at least one structured exposure surface is current.  Failing when: project vocabulary has diverged from any neighbouring usage, contributors cannot name the closest adjacent project, or the project's own reuse-vs-make decisions never include explicit reuse-search."}], :conclusion "Collaborative work needs deliberate, recurring coupling to its surrounding field — adjacent projects, external readers, prior art — or it accretes its own private vocabulary, repeats solved problems, and rejects useful inputs as foreign.  The pattern is to schedule and structure the coupling, not to hope it happens; the surface form to detect (\"Not Invented Here,\" fenced field, blinkered horse) is named in Guattari's transversality framing as the absence of a coefficient that has to be set.", :directives {:audience "peer-learning facilitators, project conveners, distributed-team leads", :keywords ["isolation" "not-invented-here" "transversality" "exposure" "cross-pollination" "blinkers"], :references ["collaboration-coherence/navel-gazing" "peeragogy/wrapper" "peeragogy/newcomer"], :style "pattern", :title "Stay Coupled to the Surrounding Field", :tone "practical"}, :id "collaboration-coherence/isolation", :keywords ["isolation" "not-invented-here" "transversality" "exposure" "cross-pollination" "blinkers"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["collaboration-coherence/navel-gazing" "peeragogy/wrapper" "peeragogy/newcomer"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/collaboration-coherence/isolation.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Stay Coupled to the Surrounding Field", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Problem-solving in collaborative work always takes time, attention, and friction; expecting tools, platforms, or processes to come pre-built (or to \"just work\" once installed) is the failure mode that strips a project of its own learning.  The pattern is to name the magical assumption explicitly, estimate its friction cost, and budget for the work the project will actually have to do — preserving the friction-as-learning property the project depends on."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Voluntary peer-learning projects routinely reach for the next tool / framework / process under the implicit hope that adoption will dissolve the underlying problem.  Sometimes adoption helps; often it just relocates the problem (now we have the same coordination issue, plus a new tool to learn).  The deeper failure is that \"magical thinking\" robs the project of the *process* — the working-through that constitutes much of what a peer-learning project is meant to produce.  If the tooling were truly that good, the handbook wouldn't need to exist."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The pattern operates on the axis: faith in tooling ↔ attention to process cost.\n    Irreducible: a project cannot simultaneously be working on the underlying problem and waiting for tooling to make the underlying problem disappear.  At any moment one or the other is happening."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Real costs: tooling really does matter — a good substrate halves the work.  But the marginal value of tooling is bounded above by the project's actual problem; past that bound, tooling investment is displacement.  Both effects are continuous; the question is whether the project knows which side of the bound it is on.\n    Active dynamic: the pull recurs whenever the project hits a frustration that has a \"if only we had X\" response, whenever a new tool appears in the discourse, and whenever a contributor proposes adoption rather than configuration of an existing tool."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "      - Pre-built fantasy: \"the ideal platform would come with X already solved\" — said without engagement with whether such a platform exists or could exist.\n      - Adoption cascade: a new tool is adopted, brings a problem, which is solved by adopting another tool, and so on; each round defers rather than discharges the underlying issue.\n      - Friction-blindness: contributors propose adoption without naming the onboarding or maintenance cost; the cost is paid silently by whoever ends up doing it.\n      - Process-stripping: a frustration that was teaching the project something gets dissolved by tooling, and the project later realises the learning is gone with it.\n      - Stress-driven habit shift: under pressure the project falls back on already-trained tools rather than reaching for the most appropriate one (echoing the Science Magazine quote in the source — habit displaces goal-directed choice under stress)."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Operate an explicit move on the named axis (see substructure below); steward the pattern against the declared discipline."} {:name "COMPOSITIONS", :name-key "compositions", :slug "compositions", :text "      (1) Name the magical assumption: when the next tool / process / framework is proposed, write down what specifically is expected to become easier, by how much, and on what timescale.  The act of writing usually changes the proposal.\n      (2) Friction budget: maintain an explicit estimate of the project's available attention.  New adoptions get a friction line item — onboarding, configuration, ongoing maintenance, integration with existing tooling.  When the budget overruns, something gets dropped.\n      (3) Adopt-after-use: try to solve the underlying problem with existing tooling first.  If the existing tooling visibly fails, the diagnostic information is what justifies adoption.  Without that diagnostic, adoption is speculation.\n      (4) Preserve process learning: when a frustration is dissolved by tooling, ask what the project was *learning* from that frustration.  If the answer is something useful, recover it deliberately (e.g. document the now-tooling-handled problem so contributors who need to understand it still can).\n      (5) Magical-thinking audit: periodically review which assumptions about tooling have actually paid off and which have not.  Pattern revision based on evidence beats the next adoption proposal."} {:name "CHECK", :name-key "check", :slug "check", :text "      Engaged when: every tooling adoption decision has a recorded prior expectation and a recorded post-hoc audit.  Failing when: tools accumulate without commensurate progress on the underlying problem, or the project repeatedly proposes the same kind of tooling solution with no acknowledgement of why prior versions didn't deliver."}], :conclusion "Problem-solving in collaborative work always takes time, attention, and friction; expecting tools, platforms, or processes to come pre-built (or to \"just work\" once installed) is the failure mode that strips a project of its own learning.  The pattern is to name the magical assumption explicitly, estimate its friction cost, and budget for the work the project will actually have to do — preserving the friction-as-learning property the project depends on.", :directives {:audience "peer-learning facilitators, project conveners, tool-builders", :keywords ["magical-thinking" "friction-budget" "process-cost" "ideal-platform" "premature-tooling"], :references ["peeragogy/use-or-make" "collaboration-coherence/stasis" "collaboration-coherence/navel-gazing"], :style "pattern", :title "Expect Process Cost; Name the Friction", :tone "practical"}, :id "collaboration-coherence/magical-thinking", :keywords ["magical-thinking" "friction-budget" "process-cost" "ideal-platform" "premature-tooling"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["peeragogy/use-or-make" "collaboration-coherence/stasis" "collaboration-coherence/navel-gazing"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/collaboration-coherence/magical-thinking.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Expect Process Cost; Name the Friction", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Voluntary collaborative learning produces a continuous gradient of participation — from full completion through partial engagement to silent observation — and that gradient is structural, not pathological.  The pattern is to design for the gradient: separate metrics per participation tier, low-cost partial-participation paths, low-cost re-entry, and explicit acceptance that \"lurker\" and \"non-completer\" are valid project states rather than failures to be eliminated."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Online peer-learning groups (MOOCs the canonical case, but the dynamic is general) attract orders of magnitude more lurkers than completers.  Stanford's MOOC AI class started with 100,000+ and finished with 12%; that ratio is structural to voluntary, low-entry-cost, asynchronous learning.  The failure mode named \"messy with lurkers\" is not the participation gradient itself but the *misreading* of it as broken — applying completion-rate metrics borrowed from compulsory education, designing as if the gradient should be flat, or treating lurkers as a defect to be filtered.  The mess is real; the misreading is the antipattern."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The pattern operates on the axis: completion-rate metrics ↔ participation-gradient design.\n    Irreducible: a single metric cannot simultaneously honour the value of completion and the value of partial participation — they answer different questions.  The choice is which question the project's design optimises."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Real costs: completion is a real outcome and metric.  Designing only for completion truncates the project's reach to the small minority that completes.  Designing for the gradient accepts attrition as a feature and instruments accordingly.\n    Active dynamic: the pull recurs whenever the project reports its statistics, whenever onboarding is redesigned, and whenever a funder asks about completion rates."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "      - Completion-only metric: project reports its success as completion percentage, treating attrition as universal failure.\n      - Hidden gradient: partial participation has no recognised value, so partial participants leave invisibly and the project is surprised by the resulting headcount.\n      - High-cost re-entry: someone who lurked for a month finds the cost of joining mid-stream prohibitive; the lurking phase that should produce participants instead produces departures.\n      - Lurker-shaming: project communications treat lurkers as a problem to be solved rather than a tier to be designed for.\n      - Cohort-only thinking: the project assumes a fixed cohort, then reads the gradient (which is what voluntary participation actually is) as cohort failure."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Operate an explicit move on the named axis (see substructure below); steward the pattern against the declared discipline."} {:name "COMPOSITIONS", :name-key "compositions", :slug "compositions", :text "      (1) Multi-tier metrics: separately track completion, partial completion (named milestones), active engagement (visible contribution), and reach (lurker count, content access).  Each tier has its own success criterion.\n      (2) Low-cost partial paths: explicit value at each gradient level — a lurker who never contributes still gets something; a partial-participant who quits at week three has done something useful; the completer is the highest tier but not the only tier.\n      (3) Low-cost re-entry: attrition is reversible.  Someone who dropped at week three can rejoin at week eight without restarting.  The infrastructure for re-entry (tagged checkpoints, durable artefacts, async-friendly content) is the design move.\n      (4) Acknowledge dissipation: name the participation gradient explicitly in the project's self-description.  This converts attrition from embarrassment to design feature.\n      (5) Pair with carrying-capacity: the same participants who lurk in one phase may contribute in another; the same group's carrying capacity is bounded; treating lurkers as inactive contributors-in-waiting respects both."} {:name "CHECK", :name-key "check", :slug "check", :text "      Engaged when: project reports separately track lurkers, partial participants, and completers; design decisions visibly accommodate all three.  Failing when: a single completion-rate number is the headline metric, or onboarding makes partial participation unavailable."}], :conclusion "Voluntary collaborative learning produces a continuous gradient of participation — from full completion through partial engagement to silent observation — and that gradient is structural, not pathological.  The pattern is to design for the gradient: separate metrics per participation tier, low-cost partial-participation paths, low-cost re-entry, and explicit acceptance that \"lurker\" and \"non-completer\" are valid project states rather than failures to be eliminated.", :directives {:audience "peer-learning facilitators, MOOC organisers, online-community hosts", :keywords ["lurking" "partial-participation" "completion-rate" "mooc-attrition" "participation-floor"], :references ["peeragogy/newcomer" "peeragogy/carrying-capacity" "collaboration-coherence/misunderstanding-power-laws"], :style "pattern", :title "Design for the Participation Gradient", :tone "practical"}, :id "collaboration-coherence/messy-with-lurkers", :keywords ["lurking" "partial-participation" "completion-rate" "mooc-attrition" "participation-floor"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["peeragogy/newcomer" "peeragogy/carrying-capacity" "collaboration-coherence/misunderstanding-power-laws"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/collaboration-coherence/messy-with-lurkers.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Design for the Participation Gradient", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Participation in voluntary online collaboration follows a power law (90/9/1, Zipf, long-tail) as a structural property of networks, not as a defect of any particular community.  The pattern is to acknowledge this explicitly, design with the power-law shape in mind, and resist provisionist responses (\"if we just change the system, participation will be equal\") that mistake an emergent network property for a fixable bug.  Within the power-law shape, real moves still exist (lower-the-floor, raise-the-ceiling, rotate-who-is-in-the-1%) — they just aren't moves toward flatness."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Asynchronous online groups invariably show steeply unequal participation: a few power contributors, a layer of occasional ones, a long tail of lurkers.  The same shape governs city sizes, word frequency, energy use, and most network phenomena.  Communities repeatedly attempt to flatten it (\"wouldn't it be great if everyone participated equally?\") and repeatedly fail in the same way.  The antipattern is not the power law itself but the misreading: treating its persistence as evidence the community has chosen the wrong system, the wrong tools, or the wrong norms.  The persistence is structural; the system, tools, and norms have other failure modes worth fixing, but the power-law shape isn't one of them."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The pattern operates on the axis: equality aspiration ↔ structural reality.\n    Irreducible: a community cannot simultaneously be voluntary, low-entry-cost, asynchronous AND have flat participation distribution.  The first three conditions produce the fourth (long tail) by themselves."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Real costs: equality of voice is a legitimate aspiration; the project's claim to be peer-driven depends on it.  But power-law dynamics are an emergent property of the network, not a policy choice.  Both forces operate continuously; flattening one (per design) does not eliminate the other (per network structure).\n    Active dynamic: the pull recurs whenever participation statistics are reported, whenever a moderator considers a new \"engagement intervention,\" and whenever a community accepts a proposal that promises to \"fix\" engagement asymmetry."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "      - Provisionist proposal: \"if we change the system / tooling / norms / onboarding, participation will be more equal\" — without engagement with why prior interventions of this shape failed.\n      - Equality-as-failure-state: any deviation from flat participation is read as evidence of dysfunction, prompting interventions that don't change the distribution.\n      - Power-law denial: project reports omit the long tail entirely, focusing on the 1% / 9% as if they were the whole community.\n      - Symbolic-violence blindness: the project treats whose framings dominate as a neutral fact, missing that the power-law shape produces asymmetric influence over shared meaning.\n      - Conflation of voice and frequency: a quiet participant raises a concern; the response is \"well, you haven't been very engaged\" — substituting frequency-of-prior-contribution for legitimacy-of-current-contribution."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Operate an explicit move on the named axis (see substructure below); steward the pattern against the declared discipline."} {:name "COMPOSITIONS", :name-key "compositions", :slug "compositions", :text "      (1) Acknowledge structurally: name the power-law shape in the project's self-description.  Move from \"we hope everyone participates equally\" to \"we expect 90/9/1 and design for it.\"\n      (2) Lower the floor: make low-cost participation valuable on its own terms (see collaboration-coherence/messy-with-lurkers).  Power-law-aware design extracts more value from the long tail rather than trying to shorten it.\n      (3) Raise the ceiling: make high-engagement participation more rewarding without requiring it.  The 1% is real; supporting them is legitimate.  The error is conflating \"support the 1%\" with \"make everyone the 1%.\"\n      (4) Rotate the 1%: deliberately pass the most-active role across participants over time so no single person gets stuck at the top of the distribution (which is a carrying-capacity issue, not a participation-equality one).\n      (5) Distinguish equality-of-voice from equality-of-frequency: equal voice is about whose contributions are taken seriously when they occur, not about contribution counts.  The former is achievable; the latter is structurally unattainable in voluntary networks.\n      (6) Symbolic-violence check: per the source's Sapir-Whorf / Bourdieu reference, the most-frequent participants shape the language and norms of the group.  Power-law-aware moderation includes deliberate counter-pressure on whose framings dominate."} {:name "CHECK", :name-key "check", :slug "check", :text "      Engaged when: the project's design and metrics treat the long tail as a feature; engagement asymmetry is accepted at the structural level while specific failure modes (silenced voices, single-person bottlenecks) are addressed individually.  Failing when: project leadership pursues \"equal participation\" as the goal, or the failure of equality-engineering interventions is treated as a community pathology rather than as evidence the goal was misframed."}], :conclusion "Participation in voluntary online collaboration follows a power law (90/9/1, Zipf, long-tail) as a structural property of networks, not as a defect of any particular community.  The pattern is to acknowledge this explicitly, design with the power-law shape in mind, and resist provisionist responses (\"if we just change the system, participation will be equal\") that mistake an emergent network property for a fixable bug.  Within the power-law shape, real moves still exist (lower-the-floor, raise-the-ceiling, rotate-who-is-in-the-1%) — they just aren't moves toward flatness.", :directives {:audience "peer-learning facilitators, online-community hosts, distributed-project leads", :keywords ["power-law" "90-9-1" "long-tail" "equality-fantasy" "zipf" "structural-asymmetry"], :references ["peeragogy/carrying-capacity" "peeragogy/moderation" "collaboration-coherence/messy-with-lurkers"], :style "pattern", :title "Respect the Long Tail; Design Against the Bargain You Strike With It", :tone "reflective"}, :id "collaboration-coherence/misunderstanding-power-laws", :keywords ["power-law" "90-9-1" "long-tail" "equality-fantasy" "zipf" "structural-asymmetry"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["peeragogy/carrying-capacity" "peeragogy/moderation" "collaboration-coherence/messy-with-lurkers"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/collaboration-coherence/misunderstanding-power-laws.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Respect the Long Tail; Design Against the Bargain You Strike With It", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Reflection and feedback have value only when there is a mechanism for acting on them; the failure mode is feedback channels that absorb critique without consequence — the Bateson double-bind in which the receiver invites criticism while making any effective criticism unwelcome.  The pattern is to close every feedback loop with a named action surface: where critiques go, who decides, what gets changed, what doesn't, and why.  Criticism without an action surface is noise; the *art of paragogical praxis* is to make something useful out of what would otherwise be noise."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Peer-learning projects, like most reflective communities, accumulate feedback channels (forums, retrospectives, action reviews, comment threads, evaluation surveys).  The failure mode is not too much reflection — it is reflection without a connected action surface.  Contributors offer critique; the critique is heard; the project does not change in response.  Over time, contributors stop bothering, or the most useful critics leave.  The Bateson double-bind makes this concrete: \"go ahead, criticise me — but all effective criticism will be unwelcome.\"  When the implicit reception of criticism is hostile or null, the explicit invitation is destructive."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The pattern operates on the axis: reflection ↔ action.\n    Irreducible: a feedback channel cannot simultaneously be a free-flowing reflective space AND a tightly bounded action queue.  The project must decide which channel is which, and how each transitions to the other."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Real costs: reflection without action is hollow; action without reflection is reactive.  Both pure modes fail; the question is how the project structures the interface between them.\n    Active dynamic: the pull recurs at every retrospective, every survey, every \"tell us how we're doing\" prompt — and especially when feedback arrives that the project would prefer not to act on."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "      - Open invitation, closed reception: \"we welcome criticism!\" followed by reflexive defensiveness when criticism actually arrives.  The double-bind in its purest form.\n      - Reflection without closure: retrospectives become weekly lament with no commitments; over time they stop being attended.\n      - Anonymous absorption: feedback arrives via survey or comment box, gets summarised, and the summary disappears.  No one can say what changed.\n      - Critic-as-problem: when criticism is uncomfortable, the focus shifts to \"how the critique was delivered\" rather than what it said.  The deflection is the symptom.\n      - Echo-chamber retrospectives: the same group reviews its own work without external eyes; critique that would be useful is structurally absent.  See also collaboration-coherence/isolation."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Operate an explicit move on the named axis (see substructure below); steward the pattern against the declared discipline."} {:name "COMPOSITIONS", :name-key "compositions", :slug "compositions", :text "      (1) Action-surface naming: every feedback channel has a recipient (named role or person), a decision cadence (when proposals get adjudicated), and an output convention (how the project records what changed and why).  No channel exists without these three.\n      (2) Triage discipline: incoming feedback is sorted into act / defer / decline / out-of-scope, with the rationale recorded.  Decline is a legitimate outcome; absence-of-decision is not.\n      (3) Time-boxed reflection: retrospectives have a closing action — at least one named change to be tried before the next retrospective.  Otherwise, the retrospective converts to lament.\n      (4) Visible track record: maintain a public log of \"changes made in response to feedback\" so contributors can see whether their critique landed.  This is the wrapper pattern applied to the feedback loop.\n      (5) Name the double-bind: when the project notices itself implicitly punishing useful criticism (defensiveness, no-action, reframing the critic as the problem) — name the dynamic explicitly.  The double-bind dissolves when both parties can see it."} {:name "CHECK", :name-key "check", :slug "check", :text "      Engaged when: contributors can point to recent changes the project made in response to recent feedback, and contributors who offered feedback know whether it landed or not.  Failing when: critique flows in but nothing flows out; or contributors quietly stop offering useful feedback because the previous round was absorbed without effect."}], :conclusion "Reflection and feedback have value only when there is a mechanism for acting on them; the failure mode is feedback channels that absorb critique without consequence — the Bateson double-bind in which the receiver invites criticism while making any effective criticism unwelcome.  The pattern is to close every feedback loop with a named action surface: where critiques go, who decides, what gets changed, what doesn't, and why.  Criticism without an action surface is noise; the *art of paragogical praxis* is to make something useful out of what would otherwise be noise.", :directives {:audience "peer-learning facilitators, project conveners, design-review participants", :keywords ["feedback" "double-bind" "criticism" "reflection" "action" "bateson" "paragogical-praxis"], :references ["collaboration-coherence/magical-thinking" "peeragogy/wrapper" "peeragogy/discerning-a-pattern"], :style "pattern", :title "Close the Feedback Loop with an Action; Avoid the Bateson Double-Bind", :tone "practical"}, :id "collaboration-coherence/navel-gazing", :keywords ["feedback" "double-bind" "criticism" "reflection" "action" "bateson" "paragogical-praxis"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["collaboration-coherence/magical-thinking" "peeragogy/wrapper" "peeragogy/discerning-a-pattern"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/collaboration-coherence/navel-gazing.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Close the Feedback Loop with an Action; Avoid the Bateson Double-Bind", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Apparent stasis in a multi-dimensional project is almost always a single dimension having stopped while others remain available; the failure mode is identifying with the stuck dimension and concluding the whole project is stuck.  The pattern is to identify which dimensions are actually moving, work deliberately on those, and accept the stuck dimension as out-of-reach for now (rather than spending energy on the dimension where progress is most expensive).  This is \"find the moving dimension,\" not \"force the stuck one.\""} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Voluntary, distributed projects routinely encounter dimensions on which progress slows to zero — a maintainer who can't find time, a tool that won't ship, a funder who won't decide, a contributor who has stepped back.  When a project's identity has crystallised around the stuck dimension (e.g. \"we're the project that's waiting for X\"), the felt experience is total stasis even though most of the project is still movable.  Living systems, the source notes, are never really in stasis; what is happening is that one or more dimensions of life are moving very slowly.  The grilled-cheese-sandwich metaphor catches it: even a balanced diet has individual foods that aren't on the menu today."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The pattern operates on the axis: pursuing the stuck dimension ↔ rotating to a moving one.\n    Irreducible: a project cannot simultaneously be patient with a stuck dimension and treat its stuckness as the project's defining state.  Patience requires the project to keep moving on other dimensions."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Real costs: the stuck dimension might be load-bearing — abandoning it forfeits something the project needs.  Pursuing it might be the only path forward.  Equally, pursuing it past the point of diminishing returns starves dimensions where progress is cheaper.  Both forces operate continuously.\n    Active dynamic: the pull recurs whenever a contributor looks at the project and sees the stuck-thing first; when frustration converts to identity (\"we are the people waiting for X\"); when fresh energy shows up and gets routed to the stuck dimension instead of a moving one."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "      - Stuck-dimension identification: the project's self-description leads with what it's waiting for (\"we're stuck on X\") rather than what it's doing now.\n      - Misallocated energy: fresh contributor energy is repeatedly routed to the stuck dimension where it bounces; the moving dimensions have no one assigned to them.\n      - Felt-total-stasis: the perceived stuckness is total even though external observers can name multiple moving dimensions.  The discrepancy is itself diagnostic.\n      - Carrying-capacity exhaustion masquerading as stasis: a single contributor's bandwidth limit reads to the project as the project being stuck.  See peeragogy/carrying-capacity for the disambiguation.\n      - Premature pivot: under stasis felt as total, the project pivots — abandoning the moving dimensions along with the stuck one.  The opposite failure mode of stuck-dimension identification, but produced by the same misreading."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Operate an explicit move on the named axis (see substructure below); steward the pattern against the declared discipline."} {:name "COMPOSITIONS", :name-key "compositions", :slug "compositions", :text "      (1) Dimension inventory: enumerate the project's current dimensions (technical, social, content, infrastructure, external relations) and rate each for current movement.  The act of enumeration usually surfaces moving dimensions the contributor had stopped seeing.\n      (2) Deliberate rotation: choose one moving dimension to focus on for a defined period; deliberately don't pursue the stuck one.  The constraint is the discipline.\n      (3) Stuck-dimension monitor: maintain a low-cost watch on the stuck dimension (per-quarter check-in, named monitor) so the project notices when it becomes movable again, without letting it dominate attention while it remains stuck.\n      (4) Identity decoupling: state explicitly that the project is not defined by its stuck dimension.  This is harder than it sounds — the stuck dimension is often the most-discussed thing precisely because it isn't moving.\n      (5) Carrying-capacity respect: stasis on one dimension is often a carrying-capacity symptom on a contributor (they're maxed out on something else, including life).  Pair with peeragogy/carrying-capacity rather than treating stasis as project failure."} {:name "CHECK", :name-key "check", :slug "check", :text "      Engaged when: the project can name at least one dimension it's currently making progress on, and the stuck dimension is monitored without dominating attention.  Failing when: every internal conversation returns to the stuck dimension, or the project's identity has merged with what it's waiting for."}], :conclusion "Apparent stasis in a multi-dimensional project is almost always a single dimension having stopped while others remain available; the failure mode is identifying with the stuck dimension and concluding the whole project is stuck.  The pattern is to identify which dimensions are actually moving, work deliberately on those, and accept the stuck dimension as out-of-reach for now (rather than spending energy on the dimension where progress is most expensive).  This is \"find the moving dimension,\" not \"force the stuck one.\"", :directives {:audience "peer-learning facilitators, project leads, contributors who feel stuck", :keywords ["stasis" "stuck" "dimension" "balanced-progress" "carrying-capacity" "perceived-stuck"], :references ["peeragogy/carrying-capacity" "collaboration-coherence/magical-thinking" "peeragogy/roadmap"], :style "pattern", :title "Find the Moving Dimension; Don't Confuse a Stuck Axis with a Stuck Project", :tone "reflective"}, :id "collaboration-coherence/stasis", :keywords ["stasis" "stuck" "dimension" "balanced-progress" "carrying-capacity" "perceived-stuck"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["peeragogy/carrying-capacity" "collaboration-coherence/magical-thinking" "peeragogy/roadmap"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/collaboration-coherence/stasis.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Find the Moving Dimension; Don't Confuse a Stuck Axis with a Stuck Project", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Weak ties and strong ties serve different functions; both are useful, neither is \"better.\"  The failure mode is self-deception about which kind of tie a relationship actually is — calling a weak-tie audience \"peers,\" calling a strong-tie team \"the network,\" or asking weak-tie services from strong-tie relationships and vice-versa.  The pattern is to name the actual ties honestly, match asks to tie type, and convert ties deliberately when the project needs the conversion."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Peer-learning projects often blur the distinction.  Participants share a forum, watch each other's video calls, read each other's posts — and conclude they are \"peers\" in a strong sense.  In practice, most such ties are weak: useful for diverse information, weak for instrumental support.  Strong ties are reciprocal, demand-bearing, sustained by repeated context-rich interaction.  Weak ties bring more diverse knowledge but cannot be asked for the favours strong ties can.  The handbook's source note catches it: \"I'm failing to be a good peeragogue... should be responsible not only for your own learning but for your peers' learning\"; if no one is taking responsibility for anyone else's learning, the relationship is a weak-tie audience, not a peer-tie team.  Self-deception about which kind of tie is in play converts to disappointment when the wrong kind of ask is made."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The pattern operates on the axis: aspirational tie-strength ↔ actual tie-strength.\n    Irreducible: a tie cannot simultaneously be cheaply joinable (audience-shaped) and reciprocally demand-bearing (team-shaped).  Each tie is one or the other at any moment, and the conversion between them is deliberate work."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Real costs: aspirations to peer-like reciprocity are part of what brings a community together.  Naming actual tie-strength can feel like puncturing the aspiration.  But asks calibrated to aspiration rather than reality reliably fail and corrode trust.\n    Active dynamic: the pull recurs whenever someone introduces \"the community\" or \"the network\" without distinguishing layers, whenever a request flies through a forum that is structured as audience but treated as team, and whenever a member feels betrayed because the implicit ask wasn't met."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "      - Peer-illusion: the project calls everyone a \"peer\" while actual ties are mostly audience-shaped; instrumental asks fail and the project reads the failure as participation pathology.\n      - Co-consumer claim: members report being \"co-learners\" when the actual relationship is co-watching the same content (a 4th-wall audience, in the source's framing).\n      - Forum-as-team: an audience forum is treated as a team channel; significant decisions are posted to it and the random uptake is read as community endorsement.\n      - Strong-tie ghosting: someone asks a strong-tie favour through a weak-tie surface, gets no response, and concludes the community has failed them — when the actual issue is the wrong surface.\n      - Conversion-by-osmosis fantasy: the project assumes weak ties will become strong through proximity alone, without the deliberate context-rich interaction that conversion actually requires."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Operate an explicit move on the named axis (see substructure below); steward the pattern against the declared discipline."} {:name "COMPOSITIONS", :name-key "compositions", :slug "compositions", :text "      (1) Honest naming: in the project's self-description, distinguish layers: audience (read-only, weak ties), participants (occasional, weak-to-medium), contributors (engaged, medium-to-strong), team (committed, strong, reciprocal).  Use the names consistently.\n      (2) Match ask to tie: information and weak-signal asks (recommendations, tool suggestions, perspective) belong on weak-tie surfaces; instrumental asks (review my draft, debug with me, cover my workload) belong on strong-tie surfaces.  Posting strong-tie asks to weak-tie audiences gets random uptake at best.\n      (3) Deliberate conversion: weak ties become strong through *repeated context-rich interaction* — pair work, named co-facilitation, sustained back-and-forth.  Project design that makes conversion easy (named pairings, mentorship pairs, working groups) extends the strong-tie layer over time.\n      (4) Don't grandfather aspiration: when the project's self-description outpaces the actual tie strength, revise the self-description rather than scolding the participants.  The community is the data; the labels should match.\n      (5) Pair with carrying-capacity: strong ties cost more per relationship than weak ties; respecting the carrying capacity of strong-tie holders is part of honest tie-management."} {:name "CHECK", :name-key "check", :slug "check", :text "      Engaged when: project self-description distinguishes audience / participants / team layers; asks are routed by tie-type; named transitions exist between layers.  Failing when: \"peers\" is the only label, asks are made indiscriminately, or members feel betrayed because the implicit relationship was stronger than the actual one."}], :conclusion "Weak ties and strong ties serve different functions; both are useful, neither is \"better.\"  The failure mode is self-deception about which kind of tie a relationship actually is — calling a weak-tie audience \"peers,\" calling a strong-tie team \"the network,\" or asking weak-tie services from strong-tie relationships and vice-versa.  The pattern is to name the actual ties honestly, match asks to tie type, and convert ties deliberately when the project needs the conversion.", :directives {:audience "peer-learning facilitators, project conveners, network conveners", :keywords ["weak-ties" "strong-ties" "tie-strength" "granovetter" "peer-illusion" "audience-vs-team"], :references ["peeragogy/carrying-capacity" "peeragogy/newcomer" "collaboration-coherence/messy-with-lurkers"], :style "pattern", :title "Be Honest About Tie Strength; Match Asks to Tie Type", :tone "reflective"}, :id "collaboration-coherence/weak-tie-conflation", :keywords ["weak-ties" "strong-ties" "tie-strength" "granovetter" "peer-illusion" "audience-vs-team"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["peeragogy/carrying-capacity" "peeragogy/newcomer" "collaboration-coherence/messy-with-lurkers"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/collaboration-coherence/weak-tie-conflation.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Be Honest About Tie Strength; Match Asks to Tie Type", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  follow the devmap contribution protocol\n    1. Clock in on one futon + prototype before writing code: update the clause’s `+ next[...]` with `doing[...] owner[...] evidence[...]` (or `blocked-by[...]`), and ensure the futon-level `@state/@next` reflect reality.\n    2. Build the evidence: run the futon3 helper stack from this repo (`clojure -M:sigils`, `scripts/check_devmap_next.clj` once it lands, Tatami trails) so sigils, next-tags, and proof trails stay coherent.\n    3. Clock out: replace the clause’s `+ next[...]` with `done[...] evidence[...]` (or `start` for untouched designs), note any new blockers, regenerate `holes/artifacts/devmaps.pdf`, and mention the futon/prototype in the commit or work log."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are touching devmaps or prototypes and need the devmap state to stay auditable."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    contributors touch devmaps, prototypes, or interface tooling"} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    without an explicit “clock-in” ritual the `@state` tags and `+ next[...]` hooks drift, leaving future runs unable to tell what moved or who touched a clause"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    follow the devmap contribution protocol\n      1. Clock in on one futon + prototype before writing code: update the clause’s `+ next[...]` with `doing[...] owner[...] evidence[...]` (or `blocked-by[...]`), and ensure the futon-level `@state/@next` reflect reality.\n      2. Build the evidence: run the futon3 helper stack from this repo (`clojure -M:sigils`, `scripts/check_devmap_next.clj` once it lands, Tatami trails) so sigils, next-tags, and proof trails stay coherent.\n      3. Clock out: replace the clause’s `+ next[...]` with `done[...] evidence[...]` (or `start` for untouched designs), note any new blockers, regenerate `holes/artifacts/devmaps.pdf`, and mention the futon/prototype in the commit or work log."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    keeping `next[...]` hooks and futon-level state current is how humans and FUTON3’s coherence checks agree on what changed and what to do next."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Pick one active devmap clause and walk it through the clock-in and clock-out ritual to validate the protocol.]"}], :conclusion "  follow the devmap contribution protocol\n    1. Clock in on one futon + prototype before writing code: update the clause’s `+ next[...]` with `doing[...] owner[...] evidence[...]` (or `blocked-by[...]`), and ensure the futon-level `@state/@next` reflect reality.\n    2. Build the evidence: run the futon3 helper stack from this repo (`clojure -M:sigils`, `scripts/check_devmap_next.clj` once it lands, Tatami trails) so sigils, next-tags, and proof trails stay coherent.\n    3. Clock out: replace the clause’s `+ next[...]` with `done[...] evidence[...]` (or `start` for untouched designs), note any new blockers, regenerate `holes/artifacts/devmaps.pdf`, and mention the futon/prototype in the commit or work log.", :directives {:factor "Keen investigation (dhammavicaya)", :keywords ["contribute" "clock-in" "clock-out" "ownership" "handoff" "protocol" "workflow" "ritual" "checklist"], :sigils ["🌆/工"], :title "Devmap Contribution Protocol"}, :id "contributing/devmap-contribution-protocol", :keywords ["contribute" "clock-in" "clock-out" "ownership" "handoff" "protocol" "workflow" "ritual" "checklist"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🌆/工"], :source-path "library/contributing/devmap-contribution-protocol.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Devmap Contribution Protocol", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  follow this protocol for every scan session\n    1. Declare scope (futons/patterns) and timestamp in the log (Tatami trail, repo note, or AGENTS entry).\n    2. Record the stack-coherence patterns applied (e.g. `stack-coherence/readme-devmap-sync`, `stack-coherence/evidence-ledger`, `stack-coherence/stack-blocker-detection`) and capture raw findings alongside file references.\n    3. Note the diffs or fixes made during the scan (commits, README updates, new patterns) and whether they resolved the flagged obligations.\n    4. Append a short `next-scan` note: outstanding issues to revisit plus the evidence path (log file, commit hash, Tatami trail)."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are running a stack-coherence scan and need a durable log that future reviewers can trust."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    we run stack-coherence scans (patterns, devmap reviews, sigil checks) to keep the futons honest"} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    without a repeatable logging protocol we cannot compare progress between scans or prove that obligations moved forward"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    follow this protocol for every scan session\n      1. Declare scope (futons/patterns) and timestamp in the log (Tatami trail, repo note, or AGENTS entry).\n      2. Record the stack-coherence patterns applied (e.g. `stack-coherence/readme-devmap-sync`, `stack-coherence/evidence-ledger`, `stack-coherence/stack-blocker-detection`) and capture raw findings alongside file references.\n      3. Note the diffs or fixes made during the scan (commits, README updates, new patterns) and whether they resolved the flagged obligations.\n      4. Append a short `next-scan` note: outstanding issues to revisit plus the evidence path (log file, commit hash, Tatami trail)."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    dhammavicaya demands observable progress; consistent scan logs let us compare runs, tighten feedback loops, and hand future contributors the exact history of what was checked and why."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Log the next scan using this protocol in a futon3 trail or repo note so the audit history starts cleanly.]"}], :conclusion "  follow this protocol for every scan session\n    1. Declare scope (futons/patterns) and timestamp in the log (Tatami trail, repo note, or AGENTS entry).\n    2. Record the stack-coherence patterns applied (e.g. `stack-coherence/readme-devmap-sync`, `stack-coherence/evidence-ledger`, `stack-coherence/stack-blocker-detection`) and capture raw findings alongside file references.\n    3. Note the diffs or fixes made during the scan (commits, README updates, new patterns) and whether they resolved the flagged obligations.\n    4. Append a short `next-scan` note: outstanding issues to revisit plus the evidence path (log file, commit hash, Tatami trail).", :directives {:factor "Keen investigation (dhammavicaya)", :sigils ["👘/本"], :title "Stack Coherence Scan Logging Protocol"}, :id "contributing/stack-scan-logging-protocol", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["👘/本"], :source-path "library/contributing/stack-scan-logging-protocol.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Stack Coherence Scan Logging Protocol", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  When building agential coding infrastructure, measure token consumption and execution time as proxies for energy usage"} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are building systems where AI agents perform significant computational work (code generation, analysis, search)."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    Agential coding can increase productivity and enable new capabilities"} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Current energy usage patterns for AI inference are non-trivial; without measurement, efficiency claims are unfounded"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Instrument all agent operations to measure: tokens consumed (input + output), wall-clock time, retries/failures. Log these metrics per mission/session. Compare agent-assisted work against human-only baseline where possible."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    (1) Planetary: Energy efficiency matters. If agential coding is less efficient than human coding, that's a real cost.\n    (2) Economic: People will pay for proven efficiency gains. Unmeasured efficiency is a claim, not a fact.\n    (3) Optimization: You can only improve what you measure. Metrics reveal where agents waste tokens on unproductive loops."} {:name "EVIDENCE", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "      evidence[futon3a/P4 (mission queue) includes pass criterion for token/time measurement per mission]\n      evidence[futon3a sidecar logs include session duration and turn counts (proxy for token use)]"} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Add token counters to all fucodex/fuclaude sessions (input tokens, output tokens, total)]\n    next[Log mission-level metrics: tokens/mission, time/mission, success rate]\n    next[Baseline: measure human time for equivalent tasks (where feasible)]\n    next[After 10+ missions: analyze whether night-shift automation is more efficient than equivalent human work]\n    next[If efficiency gains are proven: document them; if not: identify waste and optimize]"}], :conclusion "  When building agential coding infrastructure, measure token consumption and execution time as proxies for energy usage", :directives {:factor "Tranquility (passaddhi) — avoid running hot, measure actual consumption", :sigils ["🔥/功" "✌️/计"], :title "Agential Efficiency Measurement"}, :id "control/agential-efficiency-measurement", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🔥/功" "✌️/计"], :source-path "library/control/agential-efficiency-measurement.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Agential Efficiency Measurement", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  When agents write significant code in open-source contexts, make learning loops explicit via tool-use logging (PSR/PUR, sidecar audit trails)"} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are building infrastructure for agent-assisted or agent-authored open-source software."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    Agential programming is real — agents write, review, and maintain significant portions of codebases"} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    You remain committed to open-source principles: transparency, auditability, collaborative improvement"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Develop explicit open-source infrastructure for agents: tool-use logging (sidecar), pattern selection records (PSR), pattern use records (PUR), confidence scoring, evidence chains"} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Human OSS relies on tacit learning loops (code review norms, testing practices, maintainer judgment acquired through socialization). Agents don't learn through socialization — their learning loops must be explicit, machine-checkable, and auditable. This makes certain economic and coordination patterns visible that were previously implicit."} {:name "EVIDENCE", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "      evidence[futon3a sidecar implements PSR/PUR logging for pattern-guided agent work]\n      evidence[musn_chat_supervisor.clj aggregates pattern traces for review]\n      evidence[p4ng paper (Patterns for a New Generation) describes PSR/PUR as audit mechanism]"} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Document how sidecar logging enables new coordination patterns beyond traditional OSS]\n    next[Identify which tacit human OSS practices need explicit agent infrastructure]\n    next[Consider: does this infrastructure frighten traditional OSS communities? If so, how to frame it gently?]\n    next[Explore post-OSS economic models that emerge when learning loops are explicit]"}], :conclusion "  When agents write significant code in open-source contexts, make learning loops explicit via tool-use logging (PSR/PUR, sidecar audit trails)", :directives {:factor "Joy (pīti) — effective action that leaves traceable evidence", :sigils ["📎/本" "📁/历"], :title "Agential Open Source Infrastructure"}, :id "control/agential-oss-infrastructure", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["📎/本" "📁/历"], :source-path "library/control/agential-oss-infrastructure.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Agential Open Source Infrastructure", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  When using design patterns as agent governance, teach agents to maintain pattern quality by promoting evidence and updating core reasoning"} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are using flexiarg patterns as a control layer for agential coding infrastructure."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    Patterns guide agent behavior through IF/HOWEVER/THEN/BECAUSE reasoning and accumulate evidence in EVIDENCE/NEXT-STEPS sections"} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Agents don't automatically know to maintain patterns — they may treat patterns as write-only logs, appending evidence without updating core reasoning or promoting next-steps to evidence when completed"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Explicitly teach agents pattern hygiene: (1) move completed NEXT-STEPS to EVIDENCE when done, (2) update core IF/HOWEVER/THEN/BECAUSE if evidence refutes or refines the reasoning, (3) prune obsolete next-steps, (4) mark patterns for promotion (:stub→:greenfield→:active→:settled) when criteria met"} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Patterns can increase in quality through use and become living documents that reflect learned experience, rather than degrading into unmaintained dumping grounds. This keeps the library curated and trustworthy."} {:name "EVIDENCE", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "      evidence[2026-01-22: During futon3a.devmap review, Joe spotted that P3 (Meme Layer) had dependency direction wrong and P2 (Compass) was missing prior art from futon2/fulab. These were fixed by updating EVIDENCE section and refining BECAUSE clause — pattern improved through review]\n      evidence[library/control patterns themselves model this: effort-estimation, agential-oss-infrastructure, agential-efficiency-measurement were created when meta-patterns became clear during devmap work]"} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Add pattern hygiene instructions to fucodex/fuclaude system prompts]\n    next[When agent completes a next-step, prompt it to move that step to evidence]\n    next[In morning review protocol (futon3a/P5), include pattern hygiene check: any next-steps that should be promoted?]\n    next[In library evidence feedback (futon3a/P6), flag patterns with stale next-steps (not updated in 4+ weeks) for hygiene review]\n    next[Test: run 5 sessions with explicit hygiene prompts, measure whether patterns improve vs degrade]"}], :conclusion "  When using design patterns as agent governance, teach agents to maintain pattern quality by promoting evidence and updating core reasoning", :directives {:factor "Energy (viriya) — sustained effort to maintain what matters", :sigils ["🌊/水" "📁/久"], :title "Agential Pattern Hygiene"}, :id "control/agential-pattern-hygiene", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🌊/水" "📁/久"], :source-path "library/control/agential-pattern-hygiene.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Agential Pattern Hygiene", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  When estimating work for devmap prototypes, track both token consumption (AI work) and human time (decision/review), with confidence levels"} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are planning development work that involves both human decision-making and AI execution (via Codex/Claude)."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    Traditional effort estimates use person-hours, but AI-assisted work splits effort between human (design, review, decisions) and tokens (implementation, search, analysis)"} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Tracking only time misses token costs; tracking only tokens misses human bottlenecks; neither captures uncertainty"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Use effort schema: {:tokens {:context Nk :implementation Nk :review Nk} :hours {:joe N :codex N} :confidence :high|:medium|:low}"} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    This format is machine-readable, separates concerns (reading vs building vs checking), makes token budgets explicit, and flags uncertainty via confidence"} {:name "EVIDENCE", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "      evidence[Introduced in futon3a.devmap (2026-01-22) for prototype effort tracking]"} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Apply effort-estimation schema to all futon3a prototypes]\n    next[After 5+ prototypes have estimates and actuals, compare predicted vs actual]\n    next[Update schema if token/hours split doesn't match reality]\n    next[Add :elapsed field once we have actuals to compare against estimates]"}], :conclusion "  When estimating work for devmap prototypes, track both token consumption (AI work) and human time (decision/review), with confidence levels", :directives {:factor "Keen investigation (dhammavicaya) — measure what matters", :sigils ["⌛️/计" "👉/计"], :title "Effort Estimation with Token & Human Time"}, :id "control/effort-estimation", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["⌛️/计" "👉/计"], :source-path "library/control/effort-estimation.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Effort Estimation with Token & Human Time", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  A six-gate pipeline over typed evidence records is the minimal structure that turns messy activity into organized knowledge with auditable records."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "    Futon3 already has working pieces (transport, check DSL, trails, workday intake, pattern retrieval bridges), but they are not composed into a single boundary that enforces pattern use and durable evidence."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    you want FUTON3 to reliably produce records that other futons can use to learn and act"} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    when pattern selection, execution, validation, and persistence are scattered, the system cannot enforce invariants like \"no work runs without PSR\" or \"no success acknowledged before durability\""} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    compose the existing implementations through a gate pipeline:\n    - G5 defines TaskSpec and binds intent to mission + success criteria\n    - G4 authorizes an agent and records an Assignment\n    - G3 requires PSR (or gap declaration) and records candidates\n    - G2 runs bounded execution and registers an Artifact\n    - G1 produces PUR by evaluating criteria against artifacts\n    - G0 gates success on durability and requires PAR to close the loop"} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    this structure is checkable (gate ordering, typed rejections, evidence shapes) and creates a proof-path that can be replayed, audited, and fed into glacial library evolution"} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Define a machine-checkable exotype diagram and concrete instantiation, and stop-the-line when concrete ports/edges drift from the exotype.]\n    next[Wire futon3b gate modules to futon3 transport surfaces (WS/HTTP) while preserving the \"first failing gate wins\" invariant.]"}], :conclusion "  A six-gate pipeline over typed evidence records is the minimal structure that turns messy activity into organized knowledge with auditable records.", :directives {:audience "futon maintainers, coordination reviewers", :keywords ["coordination" "gate" "proof-path" "typed-graph" "evidence"], :references ["coordination/task-shape-validation" "coordination/mandatory-psr" "coordination/mandatory-pur" "coordination/session-durability-check" "futon-theory/theory-as-exotype"], :title "Coordination Argument (Gate Pipeline)", :tone "formal-analytic"}, :id "coordination/ARGUMENT", :keywords ["coordination" "gate" "proof-path" "typed-graph" "evidence"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["coordination/task-shape-validation" "coordination/mandatory-psr" "coordination/mandatory-pur" "coordination/session-durability-check" "futon-theory/theory-as-exotype"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/coordination/ARGUMENT.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Coordination Argument (Gate Pipeline)", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  If you want validation and durability to be meaningful, require execution to emit a registered artifact record with stable identifiers and provenance."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "\"Something happened\" is not enough; we need an artifact we can point to."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    you want later gates to validate outcomes and persist evidence"} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    without an artifact record, later gates cannot refer to the product of execution"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    make artifact registration an explicit G2 output, and treat missing artifacts as a gate failure"} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    artifact identity is the join-key that makes PSR→PUR→PAR compositional"}], :conclusion "  If you want validation and durability to be meaningful, require execution to emit a registered artifact record with stable identifiers and provenance.", :directives {:audience "coordination maintainers, futon3b builders", :keywords ["artifact" "registration" "graph" "provenance"], :references ["futon-theory/proof-path" "futon-theory/stop-the-line"], :title "Artifact Registration (G2)", :tone "formal-analytic"}, :id "coordination/artifact-registration", :keywords ["artifact" "registration" "graph" "provenance"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["futon-theory/proof-path" "futon-theory/stop-the-line"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/coordination/artifact-registration.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Artifact Registration (G2)", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  If you want replayable proof-paths, record an explicit assignment evidence record that binds agent-id to task-id and treat conflicting assignments as a gate rejection."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Coordination needs a durable \"who is responsible\" record."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    you want artifacts and validation outcomes to be attributable"} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    without a recorded assignment, later evidence cannot be traced to accountable actors"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    emit an Assignment record at G4 and reject when a task is already assigned to another agent (exclusive binding)"} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    attribution is a core coordination invariant; ambiguity becomes debt"}], :conclusion "  If you want replayable proof-paths, record an explicit assignment evidence record that binds agent-id to task-id and treat conflicting assignments as a gate rejection.", :directives {:audience "coordination maintainers, futon3b builders", :keywords ["assignment" "exclusive" "conflict" "concurrency"], :references ["futon-theory/proof-path" "futon-theory/stop-the-line"], :title "Assignment Binding (G4)", :tone "formal-analytic"}, :id "coordination/assignment-binding", :keywords ["assignment" "exclusive" "conflict" "concurrency"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["futon-theory/proof-path" "futon-theory/stop-the-line"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/coordination/assignment-binding.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Assignment Binding (G4)", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  If you want safe and predictable coordination, execute tasks under explicit budgets (time, tokens, side effects) and reject when budgets are exceeded."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Coordination must treat execution as a resource-consuming action with a defined budget."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    you want an execution gate that cannot run away or wedge the system"} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    unbounded execution produces unpredictable latency and failure modes that break multi-agent systems"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    enforce a budget at G2 and short-circuit with a typed rejection when exceeded"} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    budgets create selection pressure and prevent hidden coordination costs"}], :conclusion "  If you want safe and predictable coordination, execute tasks under explicit budgets (time, tokens, side effects) and reject when budgets are exceeded.", :directives {:audience "coordination maintainers, futon3b builders", :keywords ["budget" "execution" "bounded" "timeout" "sandbox"], :references ["futon-theory/error-hierarchy" "futon-theory/stop-the-line"], :title "Bounded Execution (G2)", :tone "formal-analytic"}, :id "coordination/bounded-execution", :keywords ["budget" "execution" "bounded" "timeout" "sandbox"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["futon-theory/error-hierarchy" "futon-theory/stop-the-line"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/coordination/bounded-execution.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Bounded Execution (G2)", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  If you want reliable coordination, gate task execution on an explicit capability match between the task requirements and the registered agent."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "\"Agent can do it\" must be checked, not assumed."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    you want to route tasks to heterogeneous agents (humans, Codex, Claude, services)"} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    without authorization, tasks can be executed by agents that lack tools, permissions, or responsibility"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    require the agent to be present in the registry and validate capability match at G4"} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    otherwise failures are silent and attribution is unclear (who was supposed to do what?)"}], :conclusion "  If you want reliable coordination, gate task execution on an explicit capability match between the task requirements and the registered agent.", :directives {:audience "coordination maintainers, futon3b builders", :keywords ["auth" "capability" "registry" "assignment" "reject"], :references ["futon-theory/error-hierarchy" "futon-theory/stop-the-line"], :title "Capability Gate (G4)", :tone "formal-analytic"}, :id "coordination/capability-gate", :keywords ["auth" "capability" "registry" "assignment" "reject"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["futon-theory/error-hierarchy" "futon-theory/stop-the-line"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/coordination/capability-gate.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Capability Gate (G4)", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  If you want robustness against self-serving validation, define a cross-validation path where another agent (or a deterministic harness) can re-run the validation on the same evidence."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Validation should be replayable by someone other than the executor."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    you want to reduce confabulation and improve trust in PUR outcomes"} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    a single agent validating its own work can silently lower standards"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    require validation evidence to be sufficient for replay, and define a cross-validation protocol that can be invoked from the PUR chain"} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    coordination quality depends on independent verification of claimed progress"}], :conclusion "  If you want robustness against self-serving validation, define a cross-validation path where another agent (or a deterministic harness) can re-run the validation on the same evidence.", :directives {:audience "coordination maintainers, futon3b builders", :keywords ["cross-validation" "review" "check" "replay"], :references ["futon-theory/coordination-protocol" "futon-theory/rapid-debugging"], :title "Cross Validation Protocol (G1)", :tone "formal-analytic"}, :id "coordination/cross-validation-protocol", :keywords ["cross-validation" "review" "check" "replay"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["futon-theory/coordination-protocol" "futon-theory/rapid-debugging"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/coordination/cross-validation-protocol.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Cross Validation Protocol (G1)", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  If you want progress claims to be comparable across sessions, bind each task to an explicit mission reference and success criteria before authorization or execution."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Intent strings are not enough; the system needs mission-scoped obligations."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    you want the same \"kind of work\" to be evaluated consistently over time"} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    free-form intent without mission binding makes evidence drift and prevents traceability to devmaps"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    require `:task/mission-ref` and `:task/success-criteria` and verify mission is active at G5"} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    mission binding is the only stable anchor that makes later PSR/PUR/PAR meaningful"}], :conclusion "  If you want progress claims to be comparable across sessions, bind each task to an explicit mission reference and success criteria before authorization or execution.", :directives {:audience "coordination maintainers, futon3b builders", :keywords ["mission" "intent" "binding" "scope" "success-criteria"], :references ["futon-theory/agent-contract" "futon-theory/stop-the-line"], :title "Intent to Mission Binding (G5)", :tone "formal-analytic"}, :id "coordination/intent-to-mission-binding", :keywords ["mission" "intent" "binding" "scope" "success-criteria"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["futon-theory/agent-contract" "futon-theory/stop-the-line"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/coordination/intent-to-mission-binding.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Intent to Mission Binding (G5)", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  If you want execution to be pattern-governed rather than post-hoc rationalized, require a Pattern Selection Record (PSR) before execution, or a typed gap declaration."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Pattern use must be chosen before acting, not retroactively claimed."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    you want work to be guided by shared patterns and constraints"} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    letting execution proceed without PSR makes validation ambiguous and invites confabulated explanations"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    require PSR (pattern selected) or Gap PSR (pattern missing) at G3 before G2 can run"} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    this makes the pipeline auditable: you can compare chosen pattern vs outcome later"}], :conclusion "  If you want execution to be pattern-governed rather than post-hoc rationalized, require a Pattern Selection Record (PSR) before execution, or a typed gap declaration.", :directives {:audience "coordination maintainers, futon3b builders", :keywords ["psr" "pattern" "selection" "gap" "obligation"], :references ["futon-theory/proof-path" "futon-theory/stop-the-line"], :title "Mandatory PSR (G3)", :tone "formal-analytic"}, :id "coordination/mandatory-psr", :keywords ["psr" "pattern" "selection" "gap" "obligation"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["futon-theory/proof-path" "futon-theory/stop-the-line"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/coordination/mandatory-psr.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Mandatory PSR (G3)", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  If you want the system to produce organized knowledge, require a Pattern Use Record (PUR) that evaluates success criteria against produced artifacts."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Pattern selection only matters if we evaluate whether it worked."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    you want coordination outputs to be evidence-driven"} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    without PUR, you cannot distinguish successful pattern use from failure, drift, or luck"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    require G1 to emit a PUR for the PSR, and reject when criteria are not met"} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    this creates a stable learning signal for the library evolution loop"}], :conclusion "  If you want the system to produce organized knowledge, require a Pattern Use Record (PUR) that evaluates success criteria against produced artifacts.", :directives {:audience "coordination maintainers, futon3b builders", :keywords ["pur" "validation" "criteria" "evaluation" "outcome"], :references ["futon-theory/proof-path" "futon-theory/stop-the-line"], :title "Mandatory PUR (G1)", :tone "formal-analytic"}, :id "coordination/mandatory-pur", :keywords ["pur" "validation" "criteria" "evaluation" "outcome"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["futon-theory/proof-path" "futon-theory/stop-the-line"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/coordination/mandatory-pur.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Mandatory PUR (G1)", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  If you want the library evolution loop to have usable signals, require a PAR (post-action review) before closing a coordinated session and treat its absence as a gate rejection."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Without PAR, the system accumulates execution without learning."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    you want the system to improve patterns and coordination over time"} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    evidence without retrospective interpretation does not produce tensions or canonicalization candidates"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    require a PAR record at G0, minimally capturing what worked, what did not, and suggested improvements"} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    PAR is the bridge between Level 0 execution and Level 1 library evolution"}], :conclusion "  If you want the library evolution loop to have usable signals, require a PAR (post-action review) before closing a coordinated session and treat its absence as a gate rejection.", :directives {:audience "coordination maintainers, futon3b builders", :keywords ["par" "retrospective" "obligation" "evidence"], :references ["futon-theory/proof-path" "futon-theory/stop-the-line"], :title "PAR as Obligation (G0)", :tone "formal-analytic"}, :id "coordination/par-as-obligation", :keywords ["par" "retrospective" "obligation" "evidence"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["futon-theory/proof-path" "futon-theory/stop-the-line"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/coordination/par-as-obligation.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "PAR as Obligation (G0)", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  If you want pattern selection to be reviewable and improvable, log the candidate search process (query, candidates, scores) as part of PSR evidence."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "A PSR should explain not just the winner but the considered alternatives."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    you want pattern retrieval quality to improve over time"} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    without candidate logs, retrieval is a black box and failures cannot be debugged"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    record `:psr/candidates` and a short rationale in the PSR output of G3"} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    the search protocol is part of the evidence, and it feeds the glacial library evolution loop later"}], :conclusion "  If you want pattern selection to be reviewable and improvable, log the candidate search process (query, candidates, scores) as part of PSR evidence.", :directives {:audience "coordination maintainers, futon3b builders", :keywords ["search" "candidates" "retrieval" "compass" "notions"], :references ["futon-theory/minimum-viable-events" "futon-theory/proof-path"], :title "Pattern Search Protocol (G3)", :tone "formal-analytic"}, :id "coordination/pattern-search-protocol", :keywords ["search" "candidates" "retrieval" "compass" "notions"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["futon-theory/minimum-viable-events" "futon-theory/proof-path"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/coordination/pattern-search-protocol.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Pattern Search Protocol (G3)", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  If you want coordinated work to compound, gate successful responses on durable persistence of the full proof-path and its evidence records."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "If evidence is not durable, coordination is theater."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    you want an audit trail that can be replayed after the session ends"} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    acknowledging success before persistence creates silent loss modes (accepted-but-not-stored)"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    verify durability at G0 and reject success when persistence fails"} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    a proof-path is only real if it can be reconstructed later"}], :conclusion "  If you want coordinated work to compound, gate successful responses on durable persistence of the full proof-path and its evidence records.", :directives {:audience "coordination maintainers, futon3b builders", :keywords ["durability" "persistence" "proof-path" "receipt"], :references ["futon-theory/durability-first" "futon-theory/proof-path" "futon-theory/error-hierarchy"], :title "Session Durability Check (G0)", :tone "formal-analytic"}, :id "coordination/session-durability-check", :keywords ["durability" "persistence" "proof-path" "receipt"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["futon-theory/durability-first" "futon-theory/proof-path" "futon-theory/error-hierarchy"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/coordination/session-durability-check.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Session Durability Check (G0)", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  If you want coordination to be auditable and repeatable, gate execution on a minimal well-typed task specification and reject malformed tasks with gate attribution."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "A coordination pipeline must not accept \"do stuff\" inputs; it must accept a task with mission binding and success criteria."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    you want multiple agents and tools to execute work safely"} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    accepting underspecified tasks makes later evidence unverifiable and failures ambiguous"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    validate the task shape at the boundary (G5) and reject with structured errors carrying `:gate/id` and `:error/key`"} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    early rejection is cheaper than running execution/validation on nonsense and later arguing about what was meant"} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Define TaskSpec shape and enforce it for all inputs crossing the gate pipeline boundary.]"}], :conclusion "  If you want coordination to be auditable and repeatable, gate execution on a minimal well-typed task specification and reject malformed tasks with gate attribution.", :directives {:audience "coordination maintainers, futon3b builders", :keywords ["gate" "validation" "task" "schema" "reject" "boundary"], :references ["futon-theory/error-hierarchy" "futon-theory/stop-the-line"], :title "Task Shape Validation (G5)", :tone "formal-analytic"}, :id "coordination/task-shape-validation", :keywords ["gate" "validation" "task" "schema" "reject" "boundary"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["futon-theory/error-hierarchy" "futon-theory/stop-the-line"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/coordination/task-shape-validation.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Task Shape Validation (G5)", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Carrying One's Own Question"} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are helping a researcher move from mentor-defined problems to a self-owned line of inquiry."} {:name "if", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "A researcher has gained methodological competence but still relies on supervisors to define the next inquiry step."} {:name "however", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "Independence emerges when a doctoral candidate can articulate, justify, and refine a question they claim as their own line of inquiry."} {:name "then", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Support them in naming that question explicitly—write a brief rationale for why it matters, revisit and document how it evolves, and surface it inside presentations, draft sections, and supervisory dialogue so ownership is unmistakable."} {:name "because", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "Independence is not the absence of supervision; it is the presence of a clearly owned line of inquiry. Making ownership visible strengthens confidence, clarifies expectations, and aligns supervisory teams around the researcher’s judgement."} {:name "next-steps", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "+   next[Ask them to draft a one-page \"own question\" memo and review it together.]\n+   next[Add the question to their research log and revisit it at each check-in.]"}], :conclusion "Carrying One's Own Question", :directives {:audience "CORPS mentors, doctoral supervisors, research stewards", :sigils ["💗/心"], :style "design-pattern", :title "Carrying One's Own Question (Apprentice → Independent Investigator)", :tone "analytic"}, :id "corps/carrying-ones-own-question", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["💗/心"], :source-path "library/corps/carrying-ones-own-question.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Carrying One's Own Question (Apprentice → Independent Investigator)", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "The CORPS Five Arrows clarify the developmental transitions that support effective participation in research environments. [🚴/五]"} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Doctoral programmes provide discipline-specific training and supervision, yet the shifts that enable researchers to act with confidence, collaboration, and responsibility often remain implicit."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "Doctoral programmes aim to produce confident, collaborative, responsible researchers."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "The transitions that build those capacities are rarely made explicit or scaffolded."} {:name "then", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Provide a structured developmental scaffold that enables researchers to practise these transitions through small adjustments to their workflows and reflective documentation, without altering existing supervisory arrangements or disciplinary expectations."} {:name "optional-extension", :name-key "optional-extension", :slug "optional-extension", :text "Programmes such as talk4real may build on these transitions by linking individual development to collective inquiry routines, though such integration is not required for CORPS."} {:name "because", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "The five transitions—student to colleague; apprentice to independent investigator; individual contributor to collaborative team member; private learner to public practitioner; project-focused worker to systems-aware researcher—already occur within doctoral pathways, but without a shared framework they are difficult to recognise, support, or evaluate. Making these transitions explicit helps align supervisory expectations with cohort development and with the institutional research culture that doctoral programmes aim to foster."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "next[Draft a one-page summary of the five arrows that supervisors can review with candidates.]"}], :conclusion "The CORPS Five Arrows clarify the developmental transitions that support effective participation in research environments. [🚴/五]", :directives {:audience "research funders, doctoral-training consortia, university R&I leads", :sigils ["🚴/五"], :style "Clear, concrete funding-case prose", :title "Relationship to existing doctoral training", :tone "formal-analytic"}, :id "corps/five-arrows-fit", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🚴/五"], :source-path "library/corps/five-arrows.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Relationship to existing doctoral training", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Letting the Trace Teach"} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are helping a researcher move private learning into a public-facing identity."} {:name "if", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "Doctoral researchers accumulate tacit knowledge but keep it inside informal notes, calls, or unshared materials."} {:name "however", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "Without public artefacts their emerging expertise stays invisible to collaborators, employers, and interdisciplinary partners."} {:name "then", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Encourage the release of small, well-scoped artefacts—annotated examples, brief reflections, walkthroughs, explanatory notes—into venues where relevant peers already circulate."} {:name "because", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "Public-facing traces signal identity and invite collaboration; they provide low-risk entry points for others to understand, support, or extend the researcher’s work while helping the researcher build confidence as a contributor."} {:name "next-steps", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "+   next[Select one trace-worthy insight from the last month and publish it in a shared venue.]\n+   next[Invite one collaborator to respond or extend the artefact.]"}], :conclusion "Letting the Trace Teach", :directives {:audience "CORPS mentors, doctoral supervisors, research stewards", :sigils ["📤/文"], :style "design-pattern", :title "Letting the Trace Teach (Private Learning → Public-Facing Identity)", :tone "analytic"}, :id "corps/letting-the-trace-teach", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["📤/文"], :source-path "library/corps/letting-the-trace-teach.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Letting the Trace Teach (Private Learning → Public-Facing Identity)", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Seeing the Surrounding Conditions"} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are helping a researcher situate a project inside institutional systems and dependencies."} {:name "if", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "A doctoral project sits inside wider institutional systems—data governance, shared infrastructure, ethics boards, administrative rhythms—but the researcher only sees the immediate task."} {:name "however", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "Responsible decisions about methods, data, and collaboration require awareness of upstream dependencies and downstream effects."} {:name "then", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Guide the researcher through mapping dependencies, meeting governance partners, and reflecting on how their actions touch technical, administrative, and academic stakeholders so the surrounding system stays in view."} {:name "because", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "Systems-level awareness improves decision quality, anticipates bottlenecks, and prepares researchers for future roles with broader responsibility by teaching how individual work affects the collective environment."} {:name "next-steps", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "  next[Use this pattern to guide the CORPS sketch expansion toward system-level dependencies.]"}], :conclusion "Seeing the Surrounding Conditions", :directives {:audience "CORPS mentors, doctoral supervisors, research stewards", :sigils ["🌏/田"], :style "design-pattern", :title "Seeing the Surrounding Conditions (Project → System)", :tone "analytic"}, :id "corps/seeing-the-surrounding-conditions", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🌏/田"], :source-path "library/corps/seeing-the-surrounding-conditions.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Seeing the Surrounding Conditions (Project → System)", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Standing in the Circle"} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are helping a doctoral researcher shift from student to colleague inside a research team."} {:name "if", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "Doctoral researchers are expected to participate in research teams, represent their work, and contribute to shared decisions, yet their reasoning and trade-offs remain invisible to peers."} {:name "however", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "When a researcher is always positioned as “learner receiving instruction,” collaborators miss the moment when they are ready to act as colleagues."} {:name "then", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Provide structured opportunities for them to narrate decisions in a peer-facing format—decision notes, reflective summaries, short presentations of provisional conclusions—so their judgement is legible to the team rather than hidden inside supervisory conversations."} {:name "because", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "Explicit reasoning creates recognition: supervisors and peers can respond at the appropriate level, collaboration becomes more mature, and the researcher internalises professional discourse norms that move them from student to colleague."} {:name "next-steps", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "+   next[Ask them to produce a decision note after the next team meeting.]\n+   next[Schedule a short peer-facing update where they present one provisional conclusion.]"}], :conclusion "Standing in the Circle", :directives {:audience "CORPS mentors, doctoral supervisors, research stewards", :sigils ["👥/仁"], :style "design-pattern", :title "Standing in the Circle (Student → Colleague)", :tone "analytic"}, :id "corps/standing-in-the-circle", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["👥/仁"], :source-path "library/corps/standing-in-the-circle.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Standing in the Circle (Student → Colleague)", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Working Where Others Can See"} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are shifting an individual workflow into a collaborative team setting where visibility matters."} {:name "if", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "A project depends on mutual visibility of tasks, decisions, and intermediate outputs, yet the researcher’s workflow remains private or idiosyncratic."} {:name "however", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "Teams cannot coordinate or intervene early when work stays on personal drives or in opaque notebooks."} {:name "then", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Move key elements of the workflow into shared spaces—open notes, reproducible scripts, transparent task boards—and annotate decision points so colleagues can interpret and respond without extra meetings."} {:name "because", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "Collaboration relies on visible work surfaces. When intermediate steps are reviewable, teams reduce duplication, coordinate effectively, and offer support before issues harden into blockers."} {:name "next-steps", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "+   next[Choose one shared workspace (notes, repo, or task board) and migrate the next deliverable there.]\n+   next[Add a brief decision log so teammates can respond asynchronously.]"}], :conclusion "Working Where Others Can See", :directives {:audience "CORPS mentors, doctoral supervisors, research stewards", :sigils ["👀/示"], :style "design-pattern", :title "Working Where Others Can See (Individual Contributor → Collaborative Team Member)", :tone "analytic"}, :id "corps/working-where-others-can-see", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["👀/示"], :source-path "library/corps/working-where-others-can-see.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Working Where Others Can See (Individual Contributor → Collaborative Team Member)", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "An outward EoI can be cold on the recipient's side yet warm on yours when a Mission is its unlock-precondition. Model the (Mission → EoI) relation as a first-class edge, gate the EoI on the mission producing a showable artefact, and the asymmetry — cold-for-them, warm-for-you — becomes precisely what makes the eventual contact non-arbitrary instead of a fan note or a bare ask."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "A strawman names an outward contact whose honest hook is a *convergence* between your work and theirs (not \"I used your theory\" but \"a system built for other reasons landed on your structure\"). For a peer this is sendable now; for a senior or cold recipient the convergence isn't yet legible — there is no artefact that makes it visible in thirty seconds. The temptation is to send on enthusiasm or to defer indefinitely; both waste the lead."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    You hold a strawman for an outward EoI where (a) the hook is a real convergence with the recipient's work, and (b) the recipient is cold or senior enough that the convergence must be *shown*, not asserted — and (c) some mission of yours, run for its own reasons, would produce exactly the artefact that makes the convergence legible."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Sending while the convergence is invisible burns a one-shot first impression and reads as fan-mail or an ask; indefinitely deferring loses the lead and lets the territory move. Treating \"cold\" as a single property hides the real structure: the contact is cold on *their* side (they don't know you) but can be made warm on *yours* (you have something worth showing) — and the bridge between the two is a specific mission's deliverable."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Record the EoI as DEFERRED with an explicit gate that names the mission and its done-condition — the (Mission → EoI) edge as a first-class object. The mission is the warming precondition: when it yields the showable artefact, the contact becomes warm-on-your-side (legible convergence, concrete thing to show) even though it stays cold on theirs, and *that* asymmetry is the EoI's justification. The strawman carries the hook and the predicted tensions; the edge carries the WHEN. Do not collapse the gate into a vague \"later.\""} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    The quality of a cold outward contact is set by what you can make legible at first glance, not by your enthusiasm. Naming the unlocking mission converts an open-ended \"someday\" into a checkable trigger, keeps the lead alive without spending it prematurely, and makes the eventual EoI self-justifying (\"I built X for other reasons; here it is; it converges with your Y\"). It also turns a connector-node mission into a generator of well-timed EoIs that point through it at downstream nodes, rather than terminating in a verdict."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - In the strawman frontmatter, set `status: DEFERRED` and a `gate:` field naming the unlocking mission + its done-condition (the showable artefact).\n    - Record the (Mission → EoI) edge where the interest-network / buckets can see it, so the EoI surfaces automatically when the mission's artefact lands — don't rely on remembering.\n    - Keep the strawman's hook and predicted-tensions current; the gate changes WHEN, not WHAT.\n    - Distinguish the two failure modes the gate prevents: premature-send (cold on both sides, nothing to show) and lead-decay (deferred with no trigger, so it never fires).\n    - Honest scope: the mission warms the contact on YOUR side only. The recipient is still cold; the EoI's discipline (concision, one striking hook, no overclaim) still applies in full."}], :conclusion "An outward EoI can be cold on the recipient's side yet warm on yours when a Mission is its unlock-precondition. Model the (Mission → EoI) relation as a first-class edge, gate the EoI on the mission producing a showable artefact, and the asymmetry — cold-for-them, warm-for-you — becomes precisely what makes the eventual contact non-arbitrary instead of a fan note or a bare ask.", :directives {:audience "futonic mission authors, EoI / strawman writers, anyone gating outreach on a deliverable", :keywords ["eoi" "outward-correspondence" "cold-contact" "warm-on-my-side" "mission-as-precondition" "deferred-eoi" "artefact-gate" "credibility-asymmetry" "interest-network-connector" "mission-eoi-edge"], :references ["eoi-engine/tension-cv-shape" "eoi-engine/tension-foreclosure" "mission-coherence/logic-model-before-code"], :sigils ["✉/鍵"], :style "pattern", :title "A Mission Can Warm an Outward EoI That Is Cold on the Recipient's Side", :tone "declarative"}, :id "correspondence-coherence/mission-unlocks-eoi", :keywords ["eoi" "outward-correspondence" "cold-contact" "warm-on-my-side" "mission-as-precondition" "deferred-eoi" "artefact-gate" "credibility-asymmetry" "interest-network-connector" "mission-eoi-edge"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["eoi-engine/tension-cv-shape" "eoi-engine/tension-foreclosure" "mission-coherence/logic-model-before-code"], :scores {}, :sigils ["✉/鍵"], :source-path "library/correspondence-coherence/mission-unlocks-eoi.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "A Mission Can Warm an Outward EoI That Is Cold on the Recipient's Side", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Reframe finished grant-style capability demonstrations as consulting pitches for recipients with immediate needs, so the same craft stops producing only long review cycles and starts producing revenue opportunities."} {:name "CONTEXT", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "     An academic practitioner writes grant proposals that demonstrate capability, methodology, and relevance to a funder's priorities. The same skill - framing what you can do in terms of what the recipient needs - is exactly what consulting proposals require."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "     The practitioner has experience writing grants (UKRN, EPSRC, etc.) and has demonstrable capabilities (Bayesian modelling, pattern languages, knowledge management, AI integration)."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "     Grant proposals go to institutions with long review cycles and uncertain outcomes. Consulting pitches go to organisations with immediate needs and shorter cycles. The same skill applied to the wrong recipient produces months of waiting instead of weeks of revenue."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "     For each capability demonstration (working paper, tool, methodology), ask: \"who would pay for this as a service rather than fund it as research?\" Reframe the grant narrative as a consulting proposal. The Bayesian logic model becomes \"evaluation consulting.\" The pattern language becomes \"structured methodology.\" The knowledge graph becomes \"enterprise knowledge management.\""} {:name "NOTE", :name-key "note", :slug "note", :text "       The inverse also applies: a successful consulting engagement generates evidence that strengthens future grant proposals. The pattern is bidirectional, not a one-way conversion."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "     The fabrication \"I need a grant to do this work\" dissolves when you realise the work is already done and the question is who values it. Grants fund potential; consulting pays for demonstrated capability. The practitioner who has built the stack already has the demonstration."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Review each completed grant-style artifact and write one concrete consulting version aimed at a recipient with a present-tense operational need.]"}], :conclusion "Reframe finished grant-style capability demonstrations as consulting pitches for recipients with immediate needs, so the same craft stops producing only long review cycles and starts producing revenue opportunities.", :directives {:title "This Grant Proposal Could Have Been a Consulting Pitch"}, :id "depositing/grant-to-pitch", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/depositing/grant-to-pitch.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "This Grant Proposal Could Have Been a Consulting Pitch", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Combine a recurring scan, a live conversation, and a fixed frame so pipeline-building becomes a repeatable practice that turns fantasy about getting paid for passion into concrete assessment."} {:name "CONTEXT", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "     A practitioner has capabilities but no pipeline. The gap between \"what I can do\" and \"who will pay for it\" feels like an abyss. Fantasy fabrication fills the gap: \"wouldn't it be nice to get paid for what I love doing.\""} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "     The practitioner has a systematic scan (probes), an agent to talk with (structured conversation), and a frame schema (what to record)."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "     Scanning alone produces data, not direction. Talking alone produces narrative, not structure. Recording alone produces archives, not momentum."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "     Combine all three into a daily hour: scan the landscape (5 min automated), talk through each item as a braindump (45 min), structure the output as a frame (10 min). The frame is the artifact. The conversation is the adapter. The scan is the stimulus."} {:name "NOTE", :name-key "note", :slug "note", :text "       This is intentional samskara - the deliberate fabrication of mental formations that serve the practitioner. The \"unhelpful fabrication\" is the dream of being paid for passion. The \"helpful fabrication\" is the daily discipline of scanning, assessing, and recording. Both are fabricated; one produces evidence, the other produces fantasy."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "     Fantasy dissolves when confronted with specifics. \"Wouldn't it be nice\" becomes \"Logseq has $50K on OpenCollective and 913 open tickets - do I want to close tickets or pitch a roadmap?\" The pattern replaces vague aspiration with concrete assessment, repeated daily until the pipeline either materialises or the approach is revised."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Run the daily scan-talk-frame hour until each probe turns into either a concrete pitch target or an explicit rejection of the current approach.]"}], :conclusion "Combine a recurring scan, a live conversation, and a fixed frame so pipeline-building becomes a repeatable practice that turns fantasy about getting paid for passion into concrete assessment.", :directives {:title "Scan -> Talk -> Frame"}, :id "depositing/scan-talk-frame", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/depositing/scan-talk-frame.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Scan -> Talk -> Frame", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Treat merged upstream patches as consulting signal: a public change to a codebase the client already cares about says more than self-promotion ever can."} {:name "CONTEXT", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "     A practitioner wants to signal expertise to potential clients or employers but dislikes self-promotion. Blog posts, talks, and social media feel performative."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "     The practitioner uses open-source dependencies deeply enough to find bugs, missing features, or design improvements."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "     Sending one patch to one project is invisible. Writing blog posts about capabilities feels hollow without evidence."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "     Systematically identify patch opportunities in the upstream dependency network. Prioritise patches to projects with commercial ecosystems (XTDB, Datahike, Logseq) or active communities (Reagent, Malli). Each merged patch is a permanent, verifiable signal that says \"I understand your codebase well enough to improve it.\""} {:name "NOTE", :name-key "note", :slug "note", :text "       The Drawbridge CIDER adapter is the prototype instance: a real gap (no existing adapter), deep expertise (daily use of Drawbridge in the stack), and high visibility (every CIDER user would benefit). Estimated effort: 2-3 days. Signal value: permanent."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "     A merged PR is the strongest possible consulting signal. It is not self-promotion - it is demonstrated competence, publicly verifiable, attached to a real codebase the client cares about. The practitioner's dependency graph IS their professional network; patches are introductions."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Maintain a ranked patch target list drawn from the dependency graph and treat each merged upstream contribution as both depositing and lead-generation evidence.]"}], :conclusion "Treat merged upstream patches as consulting signal: a public change to a codebase the client already cares about says more than self-promotion ever can.", :directives {:title "Upstream Patches as Consulting Signal"}, :id "depositing/upstream-patch-signal", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/depositing/upstream-patch-signal.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Upstream Patches as Consulting Signal", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  declare a baseline freeze window, capture canonical transcripts/configs/seed graphs, and wire audit hooks (`check!`, graph exports, viriya deltas) so every new change proves it still matches the frozen reference"} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You should implement a baseline freeze to maintain integrity by capturing key configurations and setting up audit hooks."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    a futon layer claims to offer a \"known-good\" transport or memory baseline that downstream futons rely on"} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    baselines tend to rot—golden transcripts drift, flags diverge, and the story of \"what already works\" fractures across READMEs"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    declare a baseline freeze window, capture canonical transcripts/configs/seed graphs, and wire audit hooks (`check!`, graph exports, viriya deltas) so every new change proves it still matches the frozen reference"} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    dhammavicaya is about investigating what *already* exists; without explicit freezes and audit hooks, every promise of determinism becomes oral history"} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Name a freeze window, then snapshot the canonical transcripts/configs/seed graphs into a fixed location.]\n    next[Add a `check!` or export hook that compares current state to the freeze baseline on each release.]"}], :conclusion "  declare a baseline freeze window, capture canonical transcripts/configs/seed graphs, and wire audit hooks (`check!`, graph exports, viriya deltas) so every new change proves it still matches the frozen reference", :directives {:audience "futon stewards, devmap editors", :factor "Keen investigation (dhammavicaya)", :keywords ["freeze" "baseline" "snapshot" "audit" "hook" "verify" "check" "golden" "reference" "canonical" "regression"], :references ["devmap-coherence/ifr-state-convergence" "devmap-coherence/prototype-maturity-lifecycle"], :sigils ["🌝/四" "🔃/叉"], :title "Baseline Freeze & Audit Hooks", :tone "analytic-neutral"}, :id "devmap-coherence/baseline-freeze", :keywords ["freeze" "baseline" "snapshot" "audit" "hook" "verify" "check" "golden" "reference" "canonical" "regression"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["devmap-coherence/ifr-state-convergence" "devmap-coherence/prototype-maturity-lifecycle"], :scores {}, :sigils ["🌝/四" "🔃/叉"], :source-path "library/devmap-coherence/baseline-freeze.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Baseline Freeze & Audit Hooks", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  scrub each clause so it references only the layer’s own mechanisms,\n  blockers, exports, and guarantees; move projections into the appropriate futon\n  or retire them until evidence exists."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "To ensure your devmaps accurately describe a layer's mechanisms, avoid referencing unrelated projections that dilute clarity."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    devmaps are supposed to describe the guarantees and mechanisms intrinsic to a\n    layer."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    entries often drift into upstream/downstream speculation (agents,\n    policies, future futons), diluting responsibility boundaries."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    scrub each clause so it references only the layer’s own mechanisms,\n    blockers, exports, and guarantees; move projections into the appropriate futon\n    or retire them until evidence exists."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    sharp scope makes layers composable and keeps stack blockers\n    observable."} {:name "EVIDENCE", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "      evidence[library/devmap-coherence/prototype-alignment-bridge.flexiarg:1]"} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Apply this check to FUTON1–4 devmaps; annotate clauses that mention external futons without bridges.]"}], :conclusion "  scrub each clause so it references only the layer’s own mechanisms,\n  blockers, exports, and guarantees; move projections into the appropriate futon\n  or retire them until evidence exists.", :directives {:audience "futon stewards, devmap editors", :factor "Keen investigation (dhammavicaya)", :keywords ["scope" "boundary" "responsibility" "layer" "separation" "focus" "scrub" "clean" "organize" "clarify"], :references ["devmap-coherence/prototype-alignment-bridge" "stack-coherence/stack-blocker-detection" "stack-coherence/evidence-ledger"], :sigils ["📐/工" "📁/本"], :title "Devmap Scope Discipline", :tone "analytic-neutral"}, :id "devmap-coherence/devmap-scope-discipline", :keywords ["scope" "boundary" "responsibility" "layer" "separation" "focus" "scrub" "clean" "organize" "clarify"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["devmap-coherence/prototype-alignment-bridge" "stack-coherence/stack-blocker-detection" "stack-coherence/evidence-ledger"], :scores {}, :sigils ["📐/工" "📁/本"], :source-path "library/devmap-coherence/devmap-scope-discipline.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Devmap Scope Discipline", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  verify that vitality signals (git activity, recording cadence, tai-chi prompts) flow into FUTON3 telemetry, that epistemic rhythm reviews happen on schedule, and that the mindful ground for wondrous apprehension is observable rather than assumed"} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "FUTON0 provides the embodied/experiential base through which all other futons perceive. Its signals must be traceable."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    FUTON0 claims to supply dependable vitality indicators and epistemic rhythm without added lifelogging friction"} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    vitality signals scatter across cron jobs, notes, and implicit habits; without explicit telemetry hooks, the mindful ground becomes oral history"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    verify that vitality signals (git activity, recording cadence, tai-chi prompts) flow into FUTON3 telemetry, that epistemic rhythm reviews happen on schedule, and that the mindful ground for wondrous apprehension is observable rather than assumed"} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    sati is the quality of presence that makes all other factors possible; FUTON0 must prove its signals are actually reaching the stack, not just claimed"} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Add a vitality telemetry check to the FUTON3 morning review that confirms F0 signals arrived.]\n    next[Log any missed rhythm reviews (weekly/monthly) as drift markers for F0 health.]"}], :conclusion "  verify that vitality signals (git activity, recording cadence, tai-chi prompts) flow into FUTON3 telemetry, that epistemic rhythm reviews happen on schedule, and that the mindful ground for wondrous apprehension is observable rather than assumed", :directives {:factor "Mindfulness (sati)", :keywords