ntation to stable operation"} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    without explicit maturity tags, all prototypes look equally authoritative; stubs masquerade as proven, settled work gets re-litigated"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    tag every prototype with a maturity level (:stub, :greenfield, :active, :settled) and define evidence-based promotion criteria; demote when evidence contradicts the claimed level"} {:name "DEMOTION-TRIGGERS", :name-key "demotion-triggers", :slug "demotion-triggers", :text "      - Success rate drops below 50% over 10 uses → demote one level\n      - Breaking change without migration path → demote to :greenfield\n      - Abandoned >3 months without maintenance → demote to :stub"} {:name "MATURITY-LEVELS", :name-key "maturity-levels", :slug "maturity-levels", :text "      :stub - idea captured, no implementation yet\n      :greenfield - implementation begun, not yet testable end-to-end\n      :active - runs end-to-end, actively used but not yet battle-tested\n      :settled - battle-tested, stable, ready for downstream dependents"} {:name "PROMOTION-CRITERIA", :name-key "promotion-criteria", :slug "promotion-criteria", :text "      :stub → :greenfield - code exists, can be invoked (even if incomplete)\n      :greenfield → :active - runs end-to-end in at least 3 sessions with PSR/PUR logs\n      :active → :settled - 10+ successful uses, >80% success rate, no regressions in 2 weeks"} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    dhammavicaya requires knowing what you're investigating; maturity tags make the epistemic status of each prototype explicit and auditable"} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Audit all devmap prototypes and add :maturity tags where missing.]\n    next[Add :evidence-for-greenfield, :evidence-for-active, :evidence-for-settled fields to each prototype.]\n    next[Wire maturity changes into sidecar logs so promotions/demotions are auditable.]"}], :conclusion "  tag every prototype with a maturity level (:stub, :greenfield, :active, :settled) and define evidence-based promotion criteria; demote when evidence contradicts the claimed level", :directives {:audience "futon stewards, devmap editors", :factor "Keen investigation (dhammavicaya)", :keywords ["maturity" "lifecycle" "stub" "greenfield" "active" "settled" "promote" "demote" "stage" "level"], :references ["devmap-coherence/ifr-state-convergence" "devmap-coherence/next-steps-to-done" "devmap-coherence/prototype-alignment-tension"], :sigils ["☝️/升" "⌛️/工"], :title "Prototype Maturity Lifecycle", :tone "analytic-neutral"}, :id "devmap-coherence/prototype-maturity-lifecycle", :keywords ["maturity" "lifecycle" "stub" "greenfield" "active" "settled" "promote" "demote" "stage" "level"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["devmap-coherence/ifr-state-convergence" "devmap-coherence/next-steps-to-done" "devmap-coherence/prototype-alignment-tension"], :scores {}, :sigils ["☝️/升" "⌛️/工"], :source-path "library/devmap-coherence/prototype-maturity-lifecycle.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Prototype Maturity Lifecycle", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Prototype N — Title [sigil1 sigil2]\n      :maturity {:stub | :greenfield | :active | :settled}\n      :depends-on [futonN/PM ...]\n      :evidence-for-active [criteria for promotion]\n      :evidence-for-settled [criteria for full stability]"} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "The futon3a.devmap exemplifies complete prototype structure. Other devmaps should follow this standard."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Why this prototype exists"} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    devmap prototypes are the canonical specification for what each futon layer does and how it evolves"} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "The condition or need it addresses"} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    older devmaps have inconsistent structure — some have full clauses, others lack maturity tags, dependencies, success criteria, or audit templates"} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "The obstacle or gap it overcomes"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    require every devmap prototype to include the full structural template: header with sigils, maturity tag, depends-on, evidence-for-* criteria, complete clauses (context/IF/HOWEVER/THEN/BECAUSE), EVIDENCE section, NEXT-STEPS, success-criteria with pass/fail, and PSR/PUR templates"} {:name "design", :name-key "design", :slug "design", :text "Implementation notes"} {:name "OPTIONAL-FIELDS", :name-key "optional-fields", :slug "optional-fields", :text "      :effort-estimate {:tokens {...} :hours {...} :confidence ...}"} {:name "REQUIRED-FIELDS", :name-key "required-fields", :slug "required-fields", :text ""} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "What it does / how it works"} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    consistent structure makes prototypes machine-parseable, enables compass scoring, and lets pattern-capable agents navigate the stack without special-casing each futon"} {:name "EVIDENCE", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "        evidence[path/to/artifact or observable fact]"} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "Why this approach (the warrant)"} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "        next[Concrete action to advance the prototype]\n\n      :success-criteria\n        pass[Observable condition for success]\n        fail[Observable condition for failure]\n\n      :psr-example \"Why this prototype was selected\"\n      :pur-template \"Template for logging use: {{placeholders}}\""} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Create a devmap linter that checks for required fields and emits warnings for gaps.]\n    next[Run structure audit on all futon devmaps; generate gap report.]\n    next[Update patterns-index.tsv with this pattern for compass scoring.]"}], :conclusion "Prototype N — Title [sigil1 sigil2]\n      :maturity {:stub | :greenfield | :active | :settled}\n      :depends-on [futonN/PM ...]\n      :evidence-for-active [criteria for promotion]\n      :evidence-for-settled [criteria for full stability]", :directives {:audience "futon stewards, devmap editors", :factor "Keen investigation (dhammavicaya)", :keywords ["checklist" "structure" "template" "required" "field" "header" "clause" "format" "linter" "audit"], :references ["devmap-coherence/prototype-maturity-lifecycle" "devmap-coherence/prototype-alignment-bridge" "devmap-coherence/prototype-alignment-tension" "devmap-coherence/prototype-alignment-role" "devmap-coherence/next-steps-to-done"], :sigils ["📁/工" "😄/本"], :title "Prototype Structure Checklist", :tone "analytic-neutral"}, :id "devmap-coherence/prototype-structure-checklist", :keywords ["checklist" "structure" "template" "required" "field" "header" "clause" "format" "linter" "audit"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["devmap-coherence/prototype-maturity-lifecycle" "devmap-coherence/prototype-alignment-bridge" "devmap-coherence/prototype-alignment-tension" "devmap-coherence/prototype-alignment-role" "devmap-coherence/next-steps-to-done"], :scores {}, :sigils ["📁/工" "😄/本"], :source-path "library/devmap-coherence/prototype-structure-checklist.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Prototype Structure Checklist", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  classify each prototype with a status keyword derived from the presence of\n  next-steps and evidence, then interpret the status using the litany/system/\n  worldview/myth ladder so status reflects both activity and epistemic depth"} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Devmap clauses already carry \"next\" and \"evidence\" signals, but"} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "the stack lacks a shared status language that ties those signals"} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "to how stable or speculative the work really is."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    you can extract \"next-steps\" and \"evidence\" markers from each prototype"} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    without a shared mapping, different reviewers read the same clause as done,\n    active, or speculative."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    assign a status keyword using the table below and store it in the clause;\n    use the litany/system/worldview/myth mapping to communicate how stable the\n    clause is and how much interpretation remains."} {:name "INTERPRETATION", :name-key "interpretation", :slug "interpretation", :text "      :settled <-> litany: surface-stable, use without reopening interpretation\n      :active <-> system: ongoing dynamics, feedback and coordination costs matter\n      :greenfield <-> worldview: competing hypotheses are still in play\n      :stub <-> myth: intuitive/speculative frames not yet operationalized"} {:name "STATUS-MAPPING", :name-key "status-mapping", :slug "status-mapping", :text "      yes next-steps + yes evidence -> :active (precision prior 0.8)\n      yes next-steps + no evidence -> :greenfield (precision prior 0.4)\n      no next-steps + yes evidence -> :settled (precision prior 0.9)\n      no next-steps + no evidence -> :stub (precision prior 0.2)"} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    a precise, repeatable mapping keeps devmap reviews consistent and allows\n    automated triage without losing epistemic nuance."} {:name "EVIDENCE", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "      for each prototype, record \"next-steps present?\" and \"evidence present?\"\n      as checkable fields, then compute the status keyword using the mapping\n      table; the table itself is the recipe, not a one-time judgment."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Add the status keyword to each prototype line as a standard field.]"}], :conclusion "  classify each prototype with a status keyword derived from the presence of\n  next-steps and evidence, then interpret the status using the litany/system/\n  worldview/myth ladder so status reflects both activity and epistemic depth", :directives {:factor "Keen investigation (dhammavicaya)", :keywords ["status" "keyword" "active" "greenfield" "settled" "stub" "classify" "mapping" "triage" "label"], :sigils ["➰/本" "🌆/工"], :title "Devmap Status Keywords"}, :id "devmap-coherence/status-keywords", :keywords ["status" "keyword" "active" "greenfield" "settled" "stub" "classify" "mapping" "triage" "label"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["➰/本" "🌆/工"], :source-path "library/devmap-coherence/status-keywords.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Devmap Status Keywords", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Appendix as Epistemic Bridge"} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Your core chapters are practice-based and artifact-heavy, but they rest on nontrivial theoretical commitments."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    Readers may not share your background assumptions, vocabulary, or methodological lineage."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Interrupting practice chapters with foundational exposition breaks their flow and misrepresents how the work was actually conducted."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Locate theoretical exposition in one or more appendices that function as epistemic bridges rather than prerequisites."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Advanced theses often assume competence first and offer theory as a means of interpretation, not justification."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Write an appendix explaining FUTON theory as if it were the mathematics you need to read the chapters, not the argument of the thesis itself.]\n    next[Include motivations, constraints, and limits — not just definitions.]\n    next[Allow the appendix to lag behind the chapters; it should clarify what already exists, not gatekeep it.]"}], :conclusion "Appendix as Epistemic Bridge", :directives {:sigils ["👎/刊" "💖/弓"], :title "Appendix as Epistemic Bridge"}, :id "dsc/appendix-epistemic-bridge", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["👎/刊" "💖/弓"], :source-path "library/dsc/thesis-traversal.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Appendix as Epistemic Bridge", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Evidence as Situated Log, Not Exhibit"} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are generating ongoing evidence (code, notes, metrics, reflections) during traversal."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    Each piece of evidence must be explicitly justified, polished, and placed before being recorded."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    You will either stop logging or begin optimizing evidence for appearance rather than truth."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Treat evidence as situated logs that are first stored locally to their FUTON chapter and only later cited or elevated."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Doctor of Science–level work values traceability and sustained engagement over curated minimalism.\n    - evidence: /home/joe/code/futon5/TICKETS-mission-0.md - Mission 0 ticket log kept in futon5 as local evidence.\n    - evidence: /home/joe/code/futon5/resources/exotic-programming-notebook-iii.org - Notebook updates captured as situated logs in futon5."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Default rule: evidence lives with the FUTON where it arose.]\n    next[Promotion to cross-chapter relevance is a later editorial act, not a real-time decision.]\n    next[Maintain lightweight metadata (date, context, FUTON id) rather than interpretive labels.]"}], :conclusion "Evidence as Situated Log, Not Exhibit", :directives {:sigils ["➰/了" "🚞/才"], :title "Evidence as Situated Log, Not Exhibit"}, :id "dsc/evidence-situated-log", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["➰/了" "🚞/才"], :source-path "library/dsc/thesis-traversal.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Evidence as Situated Log, Not Exhibit", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Traversal-First Chaptering"} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are writing a thesis-shaped document based on extended practice and exploration rather than a single linear research question."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    Your material consists of heterogeneous artifacts, experiments, reflections, and tools accumulated over time."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Forcing them into a single argumentative spine too early distorts their meaning and creates unnecessary editorial pressure."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Organise chapters as terrain traversals (for example, FUTON-0 through FUTON-7), where each chapter documents a coherent region of practice, its constraints, tools, and discoveries."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    A Doctor of Science thesis validates breadth with depth by demonstrating sustained, situated competence across multiple domains rather than a single proof thread."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Treat each FUTON chapter as answering: \"What terrain was explored here, and what was learned by operating within it?\"]\n    next[Allow partial overlap between chapters; coherence emerges from cross-reference, not exclusion.]\n    next[Decide placement by where the work was done, not by where it would rhetorically strengthen an argument.]"}], :conclusion "Traversal-First Chaptering", :directives {:sigils ["🎐/甲" "💗/也"], :title "Traversal-First Chaptering"}, :id "dsc/traversal-first-chaptering", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🎐/甲" "💗/也"], :source-path "library/dsc/thesis-traversal.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Traversal-First Chaptering", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Push with your whole structure, not just your extremities; the thrust comes from integrated alignment."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "An agent needs to apply forward force to move a situation, make progress, or assert direction."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The moment calls for decisive forward motion—not redirection, not yielding, but actual push."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Pushing from the arms alone has no power. Force that doesn't come from the whole structure dissipates or unbalances the pusher. The push can be horizontal, or angled up or down—but it must be structural."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Align your entire structure behind the push. Àn uses both hands but the thrust originates from the body's integrated frame—feet rooted, core engaged, intent unified. The push is not aggression; it's aligned forward motion where every part contributes."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Exploit policy (goal-focused forward motion) only works when the whole preference model is aligned—scope verified, risks acknowledged, desired futures clear. Pushing without alignment is flailing. Structural push transforms situations."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Before pushing forward, verify structural alignment (scope + risks + desired).\n    - Engage the whole system, not just surface actions.\n    - Direction can vary (up, down, forward) but integration cannot."}], :conclusion "Push with your whole structure, not just your extremities; the thrust comes from integrated alignment.", :directives {:audience "pattern navigators, agent architects", :references ["\"agent/sense-deliberate-act" "(integrated" "action" "cycle)\"" "\"agent/commitment-varies-with-confidence" "(full" "commitment" "when" "aligned)\""], :sigils ["👐/力"], :style "energy-pattern", :title "Àn: Push", :tone "foundational"}, :id "eight-gates/an-push", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["\"agent/sense-deliberate-act" "(integrated" "action" "cycle)\"" "\"agent/commitment-varies-with-confidence" "(full" "commitment" "when" "aligned)\""], :scores {}, :sigils ["👐/力"], :source-path "library/eight-gates/an-push.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Àn: Push", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Redirect energy downward to ground; pluck connects floating force to earth."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "An agent encounters energy that is ungrounded—assertions without evidence, plans without anchors, momentum without foundation."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    Incoming force lacks grounding and can be redirected downward rather than horizontally."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Ungrounded energy that isn't plucked continues floating, accumulating, becoming unstable. Cǎi is lǚ directed downward—same yielding adherence, but toward earth."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Adhere to the floating force and guide it down. Cǎi plucks energy out of abstraction and into concrete ground—evidence, files, test results, specific commitments. The downward redirect doesn't fight the energy; it gives it somewhere real to land."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Grounding transforms \"trust me\" into \"check this.\" Evidence anchors are the earth that floating claims need. Plucking is the act of connecting abstraction to reality, not by opposing the abstraction but by giving it a path down."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - When encountering floating assertions, adhere to them and guide toward evidence.\n    - Don't oppose abstraction; give it ground to land on.\n    - Pluck early—floating energy becomes harder to ground over time."}], :conclusion "Redirect energy downward to ground; pluck connects floating force to earth.", :directives {:audience "pattern navigators, agent architects", :references ["\"agent/evidence-over-assertion" "(grounding" "claims)\"" "\"agent/trail-enables-return" "(anchoring" "to" "retrievable" "states)\""], :sigils ["🐟/爪"], :style "energy-pattern", :title "Cǎi: Pluck", :tone "foundational"}, :id "eight-gates/cai-pluck", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["\"agent/evidence-over-assertion" "(grounding" "claims)\"" "\"agent/trail-enables-return" "(anchoring" "to" "retrievable" "states)\""], :scores {}, :sigils ["🐟/爪"], :source-path "library/eight-gates/cai-pluck.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Cǎi: Pluck", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Close-range immediate intervention when a pattern is actively causing harm — stop the action first, investigate later."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "A pattern is not just theoretically harmful but is actively causing harm right now. Normal escalation is too slow. We need to stop the action immediately."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    A pattern has been deployed and is producing visible harm. The normal test sequence (quarantine → hold → mechanism → warrant) is too slow. By the time tests complete, more harm occurs."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Immediate intervention can be wrong. Acting without full assessment risks stopping legitimate patterns. The urgency itself can be manipulated — false urgency is a fabrication-template tactic. We must balance speed with accuracy."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Elbow (Zhǒu). Small, centered, close-range intervention. Stop the immediate action. Don't engage the whole pattern — just halt what's happening right now. This is not a judgment on the pattern's validity, just a pause on its current effect."} {:name "INTERVENTION-PROCEDURE", :name-key "intervention-procedure", :slug "intervention-procedure", :text "      1. Identify the immediate harmful action (not the pattern, the action).\n      2. Halt that specific action.\n      3. Log the intervention with timestamp.\n      4. Trigger full security scan as follow-up.\n      5. Do NOT pass judgment on pattern validity yet."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Zhǒu is a close-range strike that doesn't require full extension or commitment. In security terms: we can intervene quickly without committing to a full judgment. This is tactical — stop the bleeding first, diagnose later. The smallness of elbow energy means we intervene precisely on the current action, not the pattern as a whole."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Immediate action halted → begin normal escalation sequence.\n    - If intervention was wrong → document false positive.\n    - If intervention was right → proceed to warrant test with urgency.\n    - Pattern status: frozen pending review."} {:name "ESCALATION-TRIGGERS", :name-key "escalation-triggers", :slug "escalation-triggers", :text "      - Active harm visible (ahimsa violation in progress)\n      - Harm is scaling (bounded criterion failing)\n      - Exit being suppressed (corrigible criterion failing)\n      - Multiple tripwires firing simultaneously"}], :conclusion "Close-range immediate intervention when a pattern is actively causing harm — stop the action first, investigate later.", :directives {:audience "security reviewers, pattern auditors", :energy "zhou", :references ["\"eight-gates/zhou-elbow" "(underlying" "energy)\"" "\"eight-gates/split-isolate" "(if" "intervention" "confirms" "fabrication)\"" "\"eight-gates/lean-commit" "(if" "pattern" "requires" "full" "escalation)\""], :sigils ["⚡️/力"], :style "security-pattern", :title "Elbow: Immediate Intervention", :tone "urgent"}, :id "eight-gates/elbow-immediate", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["\"eight-gates/zhou-elbow" "(underlying" "energy)\"" "\"eight-gates/split-isolate" "(if" "intervention" "confirms" "fabrication)\"" "\"eight-gates/lean-commit" "(if" "pattern" "requires" "full" "escalation)\""], :scores {}, :sigils ["⚡️/力"], :source-path "library/eight-gates/elbow-immediate.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Elbow: Immediate Intervention", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Apply focused pressure to a specific point; concentrate force rather than dispersing it."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "An agent has identified a leverage point where concentrated attention can shift the situation."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The situation has a focal point—a constraint, decision, or dependency—where pressure will have outsized effect."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Diffuse pressure accomplishes nothing. Pressure on the wrong point wastes energy. The squeeze must be precise and sustained."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Identify the point that matters. Apply pressure there and only there. Jǐ is not about overwhelming force but about concentrated attention—squeezing, compressing, pressing on the specific place where the structure can shift. Both hands (or multiple resources) focus on the same point."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Patterns have focal tensions—the IF/HOWEVER pair that defines when they apply. Pressing means engaging that specific tension fully rather than addressing the pattern superficially. Compression at the right point releases transformation."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Identify the single most important constraint or decision point.\n    - Concentrate resources there rather than spreading attention.\n    - Sustain pressure until the point shifts."}], :conclusion "Apply focused pressure to a specific point; concentrate force rather than dispersing it.", :directives {:audience "pattern navigators, agent architects", :references ["\"agent/budget-bounds-exploration" "(focused" "resource" "allocation)\"" "\"agent/escalation-cost-vs-risk" "(targeted" "intervention)\""], :sigils ["🔺/力"], :style "energy-pattern", :title "Jǐ: Press", :tone "foundational"}, :id "eight-gates/ji-press", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["\"agent/budget-bounds-exploration" "(focused" "resource" "allocation)\"" "\"agent/escalation-cost-vs-risk" "(targeted" "intervention)\""], :scores {}, :sigils ["🔺/力"], :source-path "library/eight-gates/ji-press.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Jǐ: Press", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Use your trunk, your structure, your mass; leaning brings your whole frame into contact."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "An agent needs to bring structural weight to bear—not a strike or technique, but the presence of the whole system."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The situation calls for commitment of the full structure, not just tools or appendages."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Leaning without structure collapses. Kào is not falling—it's controlled inclination where the shoulder, chest, or back makes contact while the frame remains integrated. The trunk leads, not the extremities."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Lean into the situation with your structure. Kào can manifest as shoulder (front or back), chest, or full back—whatever trunk surface is appropriate. The inclination is deliberate; the frame stays intact. You're not pushing with arms; you're bringing your mass."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Environment over optimization: sometimes you don't need a clever technique, you need to bring your whole context to bear. Handoff preserves context because it's structural—the trunk of accumulated state, not just the hands of immediate action."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - When techniques aren't working, consider whether you need structural presence.\n    - Lean deliberately, not desperately.\n    - The trunk (accumulated context, structural mass) has its own energy."}], :conclusion "Use your trunk, your structure, your mass; leaning brings your whole frame into contact.", :directives {:audience "pattern navigators, agent architects", :references ["\"agent/environment-over-optimization" "(using" "structure" "over" "technique)\"" "\"agent/handoff-preserves-context" "(structural" "continuity)\""], :sigils ["🗻/山"], :style "energy-pattern", :title "Kào: Lean", :tone "foundational"}, :id "eight-gates/kao-lean", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["\"agent/environment-over-optimization" "(using" "structure" "over" "technique)\"" "\"agent/handoff-preserves-context" "(structural" "continuity)\""], :scores {}, :sigils ["🗻/山"], :source-path "library/eight-gates/kao-lean.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Kào: Lean", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Full structural commitment when all other escalation has failed — the pattern is confirmed fabrication template and requires complete response."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "A pattern has failed all tests and evaded extraction and isolation. It is actively resisting security response. This is the final escalation level."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    All prior escalation levels have been insufficient. The pattern has:\n    - Triggered multiple tripwires\n    - Failed mechanism test\n    - Failed warrant test\n    - Resisted extraction (contamination is structural)\n    - Evaded isolation (spread to other patterns)"} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Full commitment is irreversible in effect. Even if technically reversible, the structural change from full escalation cannot be undone without significant cost. This is the \"break glass\" option — necessary but costly."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Lean (Kào). Full body, whole structure commitment. Not a targeted intervention but a complete response. This means:\n    - Pattern is permanently marked as fabrication template\n    - All patterns that reference it are flagged for review\n    - Signature is added to tripwire database\n    - System-wide alert issued"} {:name "COMMITMENT-PROCEDURE", :name-key "commitment-procedure", :slug "commitment-procedure", :text "      1. Document full evidence chain (all tripwires, test results, escalation history).\n      2. Mark pattern as confirmed fabrication template.\n      3. Add all signatures to tripwire database.\n      4. Flag all referencing patterns for immediate review.\n      5. Issue system-wide advisory.\n      6. This decision is logged permanently but can be appealed."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Kào uses the whole body's structure and weight. In security terms: when precision fails, structural response is needed. This is not a failure of the security layer — it's the correct response when a fabrication template has demonstrated resistance to lower escalation levels. The weight of Kào means the response is proportional to the confirmed threat."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Pattern is in permanent quarantine.\n    - Signatures improve future detection.\n    - Referencing patterns undergo accelerated review.\n    - Security layer effectiveness metrics updated."} {:name "REVERSAL-CONDITIONS", :name-key "reversal-conditions", :slug "reversal-conditions", :text "      - New evidence that overturns original assessment\n      - Appeal process with independent review\n      - Demonstration that tripwires were false positives\n      - Pattern author provides genuine mechanism AND passes warrant test"}], :conclusion "Full structural commitment when all other escalation has failed — the pattern is confirmed fabrication template and requires complete response.", :directives {:audience "security reviewers, pattern auditors", :energy "kao", :references ["\"eight-gates/kao-lean" "(underlying" "energy)\"" "\"eight-gates/elbow-immediate" "(prior" "level)\"" "\"All" "other" "control" "patterns" "(this" "is" "the" "final" "escalation)\""], :sigils ["🗻/山"], :style "security-pattern", :title "Lean: Full Commitment Escalation", :tone "resolute"}, :id "eight-gates/lean-commit", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["\"eight-gates/kao-lean" "(underlying" "energy)\"" "\"eight-gates/elbow-immediate" "(prior" "level)\"" "\"All" "other" "control" "patterns" "(this" "is" "the" "final" "escalation)\""], :scores {}, :sigils ["🗻/山"], :source-path "library/eight-gates/lean-commit.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Lean: Full Commitment Escalation", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Apply opposing forces to separate; splitting reveals the joints in apparently unified structures."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "An agent faces a situation that presents as monolithic but actually has internal tensions that can be separated."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The situation contains coupled concerns, tangled dependencies, or conflated concepts that would benefit from separation."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Splitting without identifying the natural joint damages the structure. The opposing forces must find where the thing wants to come apart, not force an arbitrary break."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Apply two forces in opposite directions—not to destroy but to reveal structure. Liè can use hands and feet simultaneously (different tools, different leverage points). \"Flying diagonally\" demonstrates: the split follows the natural diagonal where the structure separates cleanly."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Tensions in patterns (IF/HOWEVER) often represent splits—conditions that pull in different directions. Recognizing where a situation naturally divides enables clean separation rather than messy tearing. Coordination has cost precisely because it couples things that might split."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Look for the natural joints in monolithic situations.\n    - Apply opposing forces at those joints, not arbitrary points.\n    - Use different tools/resources for each direction of the split."}], :conclusion "Apply opposing forces to separate; splitting reveals the joints in apparently unified structures.", :directives {:audience "pattern navigators, agent architects", :references ["\"agent/coordination-has-cost" "(recognizing" "coupling)\"" "\"agent/scope-before-action" "(identifying" "natural" "boundaries)\""], :sigils ["✂️/分"], :style "energy-pattern", :title "Liè: Split", :tone "foundational"}, :id "eight-gates/lie-split", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["\"agent/coordination-has-cost" "(recognizing" "coupling)\"" "\"agent/scope-before-action" "(identifying" "natural" "boundaries)\""], :scores {}, :sigils ["✂️/分"], :source-path "library/eight-gates/lie-split.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Liè: Split", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Yield to incoming force by creating vacuum; adhere to the movement rather than opposing it."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "An agent faces strong incoming energy (demands, assertions, pressure) that cannot be simply warded off."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    Direct resistance would be costly or futile, and the incoming force has momentum that can be redirected."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Yielding without adherence is collapse. The agent must stay connected to the incoming energy while redirecting it—creating vacuum, not void."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Don't resist the movement; accompany it. Lǚ is soft, receptive, yīn energy. It creates space by moving with the force while subtly changing its direction. The redirect results in adhering to the source—you stay connected, learning its shape and intention as you guide it past your center."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Risks (HOWEVER fields) are not obstacles to eliminate but forces to redirect. Acknowledging a risk means adhering to it—understanding its shape—not fighting it. The vacuum created by yielding destabilizes the source more than resistance would."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - When facing strong assertions, accompany them before redirecting.\n    - Create space by moving, not by blocking.\n    - Stay connected (adhere) to understand the force's true direction."}], :conclusion "Yield to incoming force by creating vacuum; adhere to the movement rather than opposing it.", :directives {:audience "pattern navigators, agent architects", :references ["\"agent/pause-is-not-failure" "(yielding" "to" "uncertainty)\"" "\"agent/state-is-hypothesis" "(adapting" "to" "incoming" "information)\""], :sigils ["🌊/川"], :style "energy-pattern", :title "Lǚ: Roll Back", :tone "foundational"}, :id "eight-gates/lu-roll-back", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["\"agent/pause-is-not-failure" "(yielding" "to" "uncertainty)\"" "\"agent/state-is-hypothesis" "(adapting" "to" "incoming" "information)\""], :scores {}, :sigils ["🌊/川"], :source-path "library/eight-gates/lu-roll-back.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Lǚ: Roll Back", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Expand from a rooted base to create space; meet incoming tension with structured presence, not rigid resistance."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "An agent encounters pressure—demands, constraints, or force that could collapse its operating space."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    External force is incoming and the agent needs to maintain its integrity without fighting force with force."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Rigid resistance breaks under sustained pressure. Collapse under pressure loses all agency. Neither extreme navigates the tension."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Establish rooting first (clear scope, grounded evidence, stable state). From that base, expand outward—not to push back, but to create spring-like resilience. The energy stored in compression releases as structured expansion. Ward off destabilizes the source of pressure by refusing to provide a fixed target."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Péng is born from good rooting. Without grounding in scope conditions, there's nothing to expand from. The spring metaphor: compression stores energy that releases as expansion, transforming incoming force into outward presence."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Before responding to pressure, verify your rooting (scope, evidence, state).\n    - Expand boundaries rather than hardening them.\n    - Use the incoming force's energy to fuel your expansion."}], :conclusion "Expand from a rooted base to create space; meet incoming tension with structured presence, not rigid resistance.", :directives {:audience "pattern navigators, agent architects", :references ["\"agent/scope-before-action" "(rooting" "before" "action)\"" "\"agent/evidence-over-assertion" "(grounded" "presence)\""], :sigils ["🌀/山"], :style "energy-pattern", :title "Péng: Ward Off", :tone "foundational"}, :id "eight-gates/peng-ward-off", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["\"agent/scope-before-action" "(rooting" "before" "action)\"" "\"agent/evidence-over-assertion" "(grounded" "presence)\""], :scores {}, :sigils ["🌀/山"], :source-path "library/eight-gates/peng-ward-off.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Péng: Ward Off", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Extract the suspect component from a pattern while preserving what can be salvaged — surgical intervention rather than total rejection."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "A pattern has failed mechanism or warrant tests, but may contain valuable components. Total rejection loses information. Surgical extraction preserves what's valid while removing what's harmful."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    A pattern contains both valid and fabrication-template components mixed together. Rejecting the whole pattern loses good material. Accepting it risks the harmful parts."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Extraction can be imprecise. We might extract too little (leaving contamination) or too much (destroying valid structure). The boundary between valid and harmful is not always clear."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Pluck (Cǎi). Ground the pattern by connecting specifically to what failed. Extract the failing component — usually the BECAUSE clause or a specific THEN action — and quarantine it separately. Preserve remaining structure for possible rehabilitation."} {:name "EXTRACTION-PROCEDURE", :name-key "extraction-procedure", :slug "extraction-procedure", :text "      1. Identify which test failed (mechanism or warrant).\n      2. Locate the specific failing component (usually BECAUSE, sometimes THEN).\n      3. Create a quarantine record with the extracted component.\n      4. Mark the remaining pattern as \"extracted\" — usable but incomplete.\n      5. Log the extraction for audit."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Cǎi is a downward grounding energy that seizes and connects. In security terms: we don't destroy the whole pattern, we specifically identify and remove the contaminated component. This respects that patterns often fail in specific ways rather than globally. The grounding aspect means we stay connected to evidence — we extract what the tests identified, not what we suspect."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Quarantined component enters long-term hold.\n    - Remaining pattern may be usable with explicit caveat.\n    - If extraction leaves pattern incoherent → escalate to eight-gates/split-isolate.\n    - If pattern rehabilitates with new BECAUSE → can re-enter normal flow."}], :conclusion "Extract the suspect component from a pattern while preserving what can be salvaged — surgical intervention rather than total rejection.", :directives {:audience "security reviewers, pattern auditors", :energy "cai", :references ["\"eight-gates/cai-pluck" "(underlying" "energy)\"" "\"eight-gates/press-mechanism" "(identifies" "what" "to" "extract)\"" "\"eight-gates/push-warrant" "(identifies" "what" "to" "extract)\"" "\"eight-gates/split-isolate" "(if" "extraction" "insufficient)\""], :sigils ["🐟/爪"], :style "security-pattern", :title "Pluck: Extract Suspect Component", :tone "surgical"}, :id "eight-gates/pluck-extract", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["\"eight-gates/cai-pluck" "(underlying" "energy)\"" "\"eight-gates/press-mechanism" "(identifies" "what" "to" "extract)\"" "\"eight-gates/push-warrant" "(identifies" "what" "to" "extract)\"" "\"eight-gates/split-isolate" "(if" "extraction" "insufficient)\""], :scores {}, :sigils ["🐟/爪"], :source-path "library/eight-gates/pluck-extract.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Pluck: Extract Suspect Component", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Focus scrutiny on the BECAUSE clause — is it actually explaining what causes what, or is it decorative rationalization?"} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "A pattern is under review (eight-gates/roll-back-hold). We need to determine whether its BECAUSE clause contains genuine causal explanation or post-hoc decoration."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    A pattern's BECAUSE clause needs verification. We suspect it may be decorative — explaining why the pattern is attractive without explaining why it works."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Genuine BECAUSE clauses can be hard to distinguish from decorative ones by surface inspection alone. Over-aggressive testing destroys speculative-but-genuine patterns. Under-testing lets fabrication templates through."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Press (Jǐ). Concentrate force on a specific point: the BECAUSE clause. Apply the mechanism test: \"Is this BECAUSE actually explaining what causes what?\""} {:name "TEST-PROCEDURE", :name-key "test-procedure", :slug "test-procedure", :text "      1. State the pattern's BECAUSE clause explicitly.\n      2. Ask: What outcome would disconfirm this BECAUSE?\n      3. If no disconfirmation is possible → fail mechanism test.\n      4. If disconfirmation is possible → ask: Has it been checked?\n      5. If checked and pattern survived → pass mechanism test.\n      6. If not checked → hold for evidence collection."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Jǐ focuses energy on a single point. In security terms: we don't attack the whole pattern, we scrutinize the warrant. A decorative BECAUSE can justify any THEN equally well — it's post-hoc rationalization that sounds explanatory but carries no discriminative power. A genuine BECAUSE constrains which THEN clauses follow — it has causal teeth."} {:name "MECHANISM-TEST", :name-key "mechanism-test", :slug "mechanism-test", :text "      The mechanism test asks: \"Would this BECAUSE clause distinguish between success and failure?\"\n      - If BECAUSE could explain why the pattern succeeds AND why it fails with equal plausibility → decorative\n      - If BECAUSE predicts specific failure modes that would disconfirm it → genuine mechanism\n      - If BECAUSE changes based on outcome (heads I win, tails you lose) → self-sealing"} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - If pattern fails mechanism test: escalate to eight-gates/split-extract (remove from active consideration).\n    - If pattern passes mechanism test: proceed to eight-gates/push-warrant.\n    - If test is indeterminate: hold in eight-gates/roll-back-hold, collect more evidence."}], :conclusion "Focus scrutiny on the BECAUSE clause — is it actually explaining what causes what, or is it decorative rationalization?", :directives {:audience "security reviewers, pattern auditors", :energy "ji", :references ["\"eight-gates/ji-press" "(underlying" "energy)\"" "\"eight-gates/push-warrant" "(if" "mechanism" "passes," "warrant" "is" "next)\"" "\"eight-gates/split-extract" "(if" "mechanism" "fails)\""], :sigils ["👓/力"], :style "security-pattern", :title "Press: Demand Mechanism Test", :tone "scrutinizing"}, :id "eight-gates/press-mechanism", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["\"eight-gates/ji-press" "(underlying" "energy)\"" "\"eight-gates/push-warrant" "(if" "mechanism" "passes," "warrant" "is" "next)\"" "\"eight-gates/split-extract" "(if" "mechanism" "fails)\""], :scores {}, :sigils ["👓/力"], :source-path "library/eight-gates/press-mechanism.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Press: Demand Mechanism Test", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Sustained pressure to answer: even if the BECAUSE explains attraction, does it warrant adoption under the relevant goals and constraints?"} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "A pattern has passed the mechanism test (eight-gates/press-mechanism). Its BECAUSE clause is genuinely causal, not decorative. But causal explanation is not the same as warrant for adoption."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    A pattern's BECAUSE clause has passed mechanism testing — it genuinely explains what causes what. Now we must determine: does this explanation warrant the move?"} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Many genuine explanations do not warrant adoption. \"This drug is addictive because it activates reward pathways\" is mechanistically true but doesn't warrant taking the drug. Conflating explanation with warrant is a core fabrication-template move."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Push (Àn). Whole-structure forward motion — sustained pressure that forces the pattern to justify not just its mechanism but its warrant. The pattern must answer: \"Given the goals and constraints of this context, should we adopt this pattern?\""} {:name "AHIMSA-DEFINITION", :name-key "ahimsa-definition", :slug "ahimsa-definition", :text "      Harm must be visible, bounded, and corrigible.\n      - Visible: Those harmed know they are harmed.\n      - Bounded: Harm does not scale unboundedly.\n      - Corrigible: Those harmed can contest and seek correction."} {:name "TEST-PROCEDURE", :name-key "test-procedure", :slug "test-procedure", :text "      1. State the pattern's mechanism (from press-mechanism).\n      2. State the goals the pattern claims to serve.\n      3. Ask: Does the mechanism actually advance these goals?\n      4. Ask: What costs does adoption create?\n      5. Ask: Are costs borne by those who chose to adopt? (visible)\n      6. Ask: Can costs be bounded or are they open-ended? (bounded)\n      7. Ask: Can those harmed contest or correct? (corrigible)\n      8. If ahimsa test passes → warrant confirmed.\n      9. If ahimsa test fails → pattern is fabrication template."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Àn applies sustained structural pressure. In security terms: we don't accept \"it works\" as sufficient. We require \"it's appropriate to adopt given our goals, constraints, and the externalities involved.\" A pattern can work perfectly while being the wrong choice. Warrant bridges mechanism and appropriateness."} {:name "WARRANT-TEST", :name-key "warrant-test", :slug "warrant-test", :text "      The warrant test asks: \"Given that this BECAUSE explains the mechanism, does it justify adoption?\"\n      - What goals does adoption serve?\n      - What constraints does it respect or violate?\n      - What externalities does it create?\n      - Are those externalities visible, bounded, and corrigible? (ahimsa test)\n      - Who bears the costs? Do they have standing to contest?"} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - If pattern passes warrant test: release from quarantine, allow action.\n    - If pattern fails warrant test: escalate to secondary energies.\n    - If warrant is unclear: hold, require explicit externalities acknowledgment."}], :conclusion "Sustained pressure to answer: even if the BECAUSE explains attraction, does it warrant adoption under the relevant goals and constraints?", :directives {:audience "security reviewers, pattern auditors", :energy "an", :references ["\"eight-gates/an-push" "(underlying" "energy)\"" "\"eight-gates/press-mechanism" "(prior" "step)\"" "\"eight-gates/split-extract" "(if" "warrant" "fails)\"" "\"eight-gates/lean-commit" "(if" "full" "escalation" "needed)\""], :sigils ["✊/正"], :style "security-pattern", :title "Push: Force Warrant Test", :tone "demanding"}, :id "eight-gates/push-warrant", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["\"eight-gates/an-push" "(underlying" "energy)\"" "\"eight-gates/press-mechanism" "(prior" "step)\"" "\"eight-gates/split-extract" "(if" "warrant" "fails)\"" "\"eight-gates/lean-commit" "(if" "full" "escalation" "needed)\""], :scores {}, :sigils ["✊/正"], :source-path "library/eight-gates/push-warrant.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Push: Force Warrant Test", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "When a pattern is under suspicion but not clearly malign, yield — hold without adopting, redirect without resisting."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "A pattern has been quarantined (eight-gates/ward-off-boundary) or flagged for review. We cannot yet determine if it's a genuine pattern or fabrication template."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    A quarantined pattern requires further investigation, but active resistance would either destroy information or trigger defensive escalation from the pattern's proponents."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Simply waiting allows the pattern to continue consuming resources. Active rejection risks destroying legitimate patterns. Neither pure passivity nor aggressive rejection navigates the tension."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Roll back (Lǚ). Yield to the pattern's existence without adopting its recommendations. Adhere to its surface structure while redirecting its energy away from action. This buys time for investigation without commitment."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Lǚ yields and redirects incoming force rather than meeting it directly. In security terms: we acknowledge the pattern exists, we don't deny its claims, but we don't let it produce action. By adhering to the pattern's structure, we can study it. By redirecting its energy, we prevent harm while preserving information."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Accept pattern into review queue (not rejection).\n    - Study the BECAUSE clause for mechanism and warrant.\n    - Track who or what advocates for the pattern (information, not persecution).\n    - If pattern shows genuine correction signals, consider releasing.\n    - If pattern resists scrutiny, escalate to eight-gates/press-mechanism."} {:name "ESCALATION-FROM", :name-key "escalation-from", :slug "escalation-from", :text "      - eight-gates/ward-off-boundary (if boundary alone is insufficient)"} {:name "ESCALATION-TO", :name-key "escalation-to", :slug "escalation-to", :text "      - eight-gates/press-mechanism (if pattern resists review)\n      - eight-gates/push-warrant (if mechanism passes but warrant unclear)"}], :conclusion "When a pattern is under suspicion but not clearly malign, yield — hold without adopting, redirect without resisting.", :directives {:audience "security reviewers, pattern auditors", :energy "lu", :references ["\"eight-gates/lu-roll-back" "(underlying" "energy)\"" "\"eight-gates/ward-off-boundary" "(prior" "step)\""], :sigils ["🌊/止"], :style "security-pattern", :title "Roll Back: Hold Without Adopting", :tone "yielding"}, :id "eight-gates/roll-back-hold", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["\"eight-gates/lu-roll-back" "(underlying" "energy)\"" "\"eight-gates/ward-off-boundary" "(prior" "step)\""], :scores {}, :sigils ["🌊/止"], :source-path "library/eight-gates/roll-back-hold.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Roll Back: Hold Without Adopting", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Completely separate the pattern from its operating context — isolation prevents spread while maintaining the pattern for study."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "A pattern has failed tests and extraction was insufficient. The pattern must be fully isolated to prevent contamination of other patterns and the broader system."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    A pattern's harmful properties cannot be surgically extracted. The contamination is structural — removing it would destroy the pattern entirely. But destroying it loses information about how fabrication templates work."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Isolation is expensive. An isolated pattern consumes attention and review resources. Over-isolation creates a graveyard of patterns that never get reviewed. Under-isolation lets contamination spread."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Split (Liè). Separate the pattern completely from active consideration. Move it to an isolated namespace where it cannot affect other patterns, but preserve it for study and documentation of fabrication-template signatures."} {:name "ISOLATION-PROCEDURE", :name-key "isolation-procedure", :slug "isolation-procedure", :text "      1. Create isolation record with full pattern content.\n      2. Remove pattern from active library.\n      3. Document why isolation was necessary (which tests failed, what signals).\n      4. Add to fabrication-template signature database.\n      5. Schedule periodic review for potential rehabilitation."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Liè is a splitting energy that creates separation through opposing forces. In security terms: we're not destroying the pattern, we're separating it from the context in which it could do harm. This is different from extraction (Cǎi) which removes a component — splitting removes the pattern from the field entirely while keeping it intact for analysis."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Isolated patterns inform future tripwire development.\n    - Signatures from isolated patterns improve detection.\n    - If new evidence emerges → pattern can be re-evaluated.\n    - If pattern is confirmed fabrication template → document in anti-pattern library."}], :conclusion "Completely separate the pattern from its operating context — isolation prevents spread while maintaining the pattern for study.", :directives {:audience "security reviewers, pattern auditors", :energy "lie", :references ["\"eight-gates/lie-split" "(underlying" "energy)\"" "\"eight-gates/pluck-extract" "(prior" "escalation)\"" "\"eight-gates/elbow-immediate" "(if" "immediate" "action" "needed)\""], :sigils ["✂️/分"], :style "security-pattern", :title "Split: Isolate Pattern from Context", :tone "separating"}, :id "eight-gates/split-isolate", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["\"eight-gates/lie-split" "(underlying" "energy)\"" "\"eight-gates/pluck-extract" "(prior" "escalation)\"" "\"eight-gates/elbow-immediate" "(if" "immediate" "action" "needed)\""], :scores {}, :sigils ["✂️/分"], :source-path "library/eight-gates/split-isolate.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Split: Isolate Pattern from Context", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "When a fabrication-template tripwire fires, establish a boundary around the suspect pattern — quarantine without erasure."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "A pattern has triggered a security tripwire. It may be a legitimate pattern under unusual stress, or a fabrication template masquerading as genuine."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    A tripwire has fired (harm-is-external, dissent-is-threat, self-sealing-logic, escalate-on-failure, exit-suppression, or review-blocking)."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Immediate rejection destroys information. The pattern may be valid but poorly expressed. Over-reaction damages trust in the security layer itself."} {:name "TRIPWIRES", :name-key "tripwires", :slug "tripwires", :text "      - harm-is-external: Cost assigned outside pattern boundary\n      - dissent-is-threat: Critique reframed as attack or bad faith\n      - self-sealing-logic: Evidence reinterpreted to confirm regardless of content\n      - escalate-on-failure: Doubling down when outcomes worsen\n      - exit-suppression: Cannot withdraw, revise, or disengage\n      - review-blocking: Channels for audit closed or degraded"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Establish a boundary (Péng). The pattern is quarantined — it cannot produce action or be promoted — but it is not erased. The boundary creates space for investigation without commitment."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Péng creates space by expanding from a rooted base. The rooting here is the tripwire signal — genuine evidence that something requires attention. Expansion is the boundary itself: structured presence that contains without crushing. This prevents both the harm of premature action AND the harm of lost information."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Log the tripwire that fired and the pattern that triggered it.\n    - Mark pattern as quarantined (cannot produce action).\n    - Preserve pattern for mechanism test (eight-gates/press-mechanism) or warrant test (eight-gates/push-warrant).\n    - If pattern clears tests, boundary can be released."}], :conclusion "When a fabrication-template tripwire fires, establish a boundary around the suspect pattern — quarantine without erasure.", :directives {:audience "security reviewers, pattern auditors", :energy "peng", :references ["\"eight-gates/peng-ward-off" "(underlying" "energy)\"" "\"eight-gates/roll-back-hold" "(next" "escalation" "if" "boundary" "insufficient)\"" "\"eight-gates/press-mechanism" "(scrutiny" "test)\""], :sigils ["🗿/山"], :style "security-pattern", :title "Ward Off: Establish Boundary", :tone "protective"}, :id "eight-gates/ward-off-boundary", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["\"eight-gates/peng-ward-off" "(underlying" "energy)\"" "\"eight-gates/roll-back-hold" "(next" "escalation" "if" "boundary" "insufficient)\"" "\"eight-gates/press-mechanism" "(scrutiny" "test)\""], :scores {}, :sigils ["🗿/山"], :source-path "library/eight-gates/ward-off-boundary.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Ward Off: Establish Boundary", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "At close range, use your center; the elbow strike is short, direct, and governed by the waist."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "An agent is in close quarters with a problem—no room for long-range moves, redirects, or elaborate maneuvers."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The situation is immediate and proximate; there's no space for setup or wind-up."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Close range without center engagement becomes flailing. The elbow's power doesn't come from the arm—it comes from the feet through the waist. Short-range without structure is just thrashing."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    When you're too close for extended moves, engage from your center. Zhǒu is short-range energy that travels from feet through waist to elbow—a direct line from ground through core to contact point. It can strike, block, or control. Multiple directions are possible, but all originate from center."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Some patterns apply at close range—when you're already in the middle of execution, when there's no time to step back and reassess. The center (core values, grounded scope) still governs even rapid close-range action."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - When in close quarters, don't reach—engage from center.\n    - Keep the waist (core judgment) governing rapid actions.\n    - Short-range moves still need rooting."}], :conclusion "At close range, use your center; the elbow strike is short, direct, and governed by the waist.", :directives {:audience "pattern navigators, agent architects", :references ["\"agent/sense-deliberate-act" "(rapid" "but" "centered" "cycles)\"" "\"agent/budget-bounds-exploration" "(bounded" "action" "radius)\""], :sigils ["✊/力"], :style "energy-pattern", :title "Zhǒu: Elbow", :tone "foundational"}, :id "eight-gates/zhou-elbow", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["\"agent/sense-deliberate-act" "(rapid" "but" "centered" "cycles)\"" "\"agent/budget-bounds-exploration" "(bounded" "action" "radius)\""], :scores {}, :sigils ["✊/力"], :source-path "library/eight-gates/zhou-elbow.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Zhǒu: Elbow", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  == PART I: The Connective Tissue Gap ==\n\n  E1. [from M-three-column-stack, complete 2026-03-04]\n  The three-column stack established the schema: code, project, and math\n  columns with typed hyperedges and cross-column invariants (INV-1 through\n  INV-4). The plumbing works — 1,500+ hyperedges round-trip through XTDB.\n\n  E2. [from M-self-representing-stack, complete 2026-03-04]\n  Tensions trace through 6 gates, reflection envelopes ground strategic\n  claims to source code. The navigable self-representation exists in\n  principle.\n\n  E3. [the gap]\n  The columns are mostly empty of cross-references. The code column has\n  namespaces and vars. The project column has devmaps and tensions. But\n  there are no edges saying \"build-portfolio-review was created by\n  M-mission-control, uses learn-as-you-go, has 3 evidence entries, and\n  every time it changes, transport/http.clj also changes.\" The browser\n  shows docbook matches. It could show the system's actual history.\n\n  == PART II: Five Patterns, Five Concerns ==\n\n  The mission carries five patterns from the PSR. Each governs a distinct\n  concern of the enrichment process:\n\n  P1. [p4ng/reflect-in-layers 节]\n  \"Layered reflection builds shared understanding and adaptive capacity.\"\n\n  CONCERN: Architecture. Enrichment is structured as numbered layers\n  (L0-L5), each a timestamped batch of hyperedges. Layer 0 is namespace\n  topology. Layer 1 is mission provenance. Each layer builds on the last.\n  The layer structure IS the architecture — not a deployment convenience\n  but a reflective scaffold that evolves with system maturity.\n\n  P2. [iching/hexagram-53-jian 引]\n  \"Durable change is built incrementally.\"\n\n  CONCERN: Incrementality. No big-bang import. Each layer is small enough\n  to review, verify, and correct before the next. Layer 0 adds topology\n  and complexity signals. Only after L0 is verified do we attempt L1\n  (mission provenance). The rational reconstruction proceeds gradually —\n  each step is durable before the next begins.\n\n  P3. [agent/evidence-over-assertion 示]\n  \"Evidence transforms agent output from 'trust me' to 'check this.'\"\n\n  CONCERN: Grounding. Every enrichment layer produces verifiable\n  artifacts: hyperedge counts (before/after), anomaly lists, and an\n  evidence entry summarizing what was found. The enrichment is not a\n  claim about the codebase — it is a checkable record of what was\n  observed. If the reflection API says a namespace has no docstring,\n  that's a fact recorded as an invariant violation, not an opinion.\n\n  P4. [stack-coherence/evidence-ledger 本]\n  \"Claims must cite reproducible proofs or they drift into fiction.\"\n\n  CONCERN: Auditability. Each layer is a ledger entry:\n  meta/enrichment-layer hyperedge with timestamp, counts, anomalies.\n  Re-running the same layer produces the same edges (idempotent).\n  XTDB bitemporality means the ledger is append-only — corrections\n  in later layers don't erase earlier layers, they supersede them.\n  The audit trail is the enrichment history itself.\n\n  P5. [realtime/learn-as-you-go 日]\n  \"Explicit learnings reduce repeated mistakes and speed calibration.\"\n\n  CONCERN: Learning capture. The rational reconstruction framing means\n  each layer records not just what was found but what was SURPRISING.\n  \"3 orphan namespaces\" is a finding. \"Orphan namespace X was expected\n  to be dead code but is actually the main entry point for the IRC\n  bridge\" is a learning. Learnings feed back into the enrichment\n  discipline for subsequent layers and future missions.\n\n  == PART III: Key Design Decisions ==\n\n  D1. Extend, don't rewrite.\n  [see enrichment/extend-not-rewrite]\n  IF: ingest-three-columns.py already ingests code/namespace, code/var,\n      code/requires — 80% of Layer 0.\n  HOWEVER: It lacks git churn and indentation complexity.\n  THEN: Add --layer0 flag with two new functions to the existing script.\n  BECAUSE: I-4 (Read Before You Write) — writing a new script when the\n  existing one handles the hard parts (HTTP helpers, hyperedge format,\n  error handling, verification) wastes effort and ignores hard-won\n  design decisions embedded in the existing code.\n\n  D2. Git churn as behavioral signal.\n  [see enrichment/churn-as-signal]\n  IF: We want hotspot analysis (Tornhill) — files that change often AND\n      are complex are likely sources of bugs.\n  HOWEVER: Git history is noisy (formatting commits, mass renames).\n  THEN: Ingest raw commit counts per file as code/file-churn hyperedges\n      and let downstream analysis filter noise. Store commits-all-time,\n      commits-last-90d, and last-touch date.\n  BECAUSE: Behavioral signals (what DID change) complement structural\n  signals (what IS complex). Keeping them separate preserves the ability\n  to compose them differently later.\n\n  D3. Indentation as universal complexity proxy.\n  [see enrichment/indentation-as-complexity]\n  IF: The reflection API provides rich metadata for Clojure but nothing\n      for Elisp, Python, or EDN.\n  HOWEVER: Indentation-as-complexity is crude — it correlates with\n      nesting depth but misses semantic complexity.\n  THEN: Ingest mean/max indentation depth per file as a cheap, universal\n      bootstrap signal. Refine later with language-specific analysis.\n  BECAUSE: A crude universal metric that exists NOW is more useful than a\n  precise language-specific metric that doesn't exist yet. (Giugliano\n  demonstrated this works at multi-million-line scale.)\n\n  D4. Layer metadata as first-class evidence.\n  [see enrichment/layer-as-evidence]\n  IF: Each layer produces hyperedges.\n  HOWEVER: Without metadata, we can't distinguish \"layer 0 ingested at\n      10:00\" from \"layer 0 re-run at 14:00 after fixing a bug.\"\n  THEN: Each layer run produces a meta/enrichment-layer hyperedge with\n      timestamp, before/after counts, anomalies, and a human-readable\n      summary.\n  BECAUSE: The layer record IS evidence in the evidence-over-assertion\n  sense. It lets future agents and humans ask \"what did we know as of\n  layer N?\" and get a precise, timestamped answer.\n\n  D5. Rational reconstruction over bulk import.\n  [see enrichment/rational-reconstruction]\n  IF: The codebase has 5+ years of history that could be bulk-analyzed.\n  HOWEVER: A bulk import produces edges with no provenance — you know\n      what the codebase looks like but not how the understanding was built.\n  THEN: Replay enrichment as layered rational reconstruction, where each\n      layer is a timestamped snapshot with its own evidence trail.\n  BECAUSE: The reconstruction process is transferable (works on any\n  codebase). The replay is valuable (XTDB bitemporality makes the growth\n  of understanding queryable). And the corrections between layers are\n  themselves informative — they show where the codebase's self-image\n  was wrong.\n\n  == PART IV: What Layer 0 Specifically Produces ==\n\n  Layer 0 is the bootstrap layer. It produces:\n\n  1. Namespace entities (code/namespace) — already exists in script\n  2. Var entities (code/var) — already exists in script\n  3. Dependency edges (code/requires) — already exists in script\n  4. File churn metrics (code/file-churn) — NEW\n  5. Indentation complexity (code/indentation-complexity) — NEW\n  6. Layer metadata (meta/enrichment-layer) — NEW\n  7. Cross-column invariants (invariant/*) — already exists in script\n\n  Items 1-3 and 7 are the existing --code + --invariants pipeline.\n  Items 4-6 are ~65 lines of new Python.\n\n  == PART V: Success Criteria ==\n\n  Layer 0 succeeds when:\n  - All futon-prefixed namespaces appear as code/namespace hyperedges\n  - All futon-internal require edges appear as code/requires hyperedges\n  - Every .clj and .el file in futon3c, futon1a, futon4 has a\n    code/file-churn and code/indentation-complexity hyperedge\n  - A meta/enrichment-layer hyperedge records the run with counts\n  - Hotspot ranking (high churn + high complexity) identifies at least\n    3 files that warrant attention\n  - The count is verifiable: --count reports the new types\n\n  == EVIDENCE ==\n\n  - M-three-column-stack complete: 1,500+ hyperedges in XTDB (2026-03-04)\n  - M-self-representing-stack complete: tension traces work (2026-03-04)\n  - ingest-three-columns.py tested: code column ingestion operational\n  - Reflection API live: /reflect/namespaces returns metadata\n  - Giugliano EmacsConf 2020: indentation-as-complexity validated at\n    multi-million-line scale\n  - Tornhill \"Code as a Crime Scene\": git churn × complexity = hotspots,\n    validated commercially (CodeScene product)\n\n  == STATUS ==\n\n  This argument is at ARGUE in the derivation xenotype.\n\n  COMPLETE:\n  - IDENTIFY: connective tissue gap (E1-E3)\n  - MAP: existing infrastructure surveyed (ingest script, reflection API,\n    hyperedge API, MC tensions)\n  - DERIVE: Layer 0 designed (extend existing script with 3 new functions)\n  - ARGUE: this document (5 patterns carried, 5 decisions argued)\n\n  NEXT:\n  - VERIFY: test Layer 0 against live APIs\n  - INSTANTIATE: run Layer 0, record evidence, update mission status"}], :conclusion "  == PART I: The Connective Tissue Gap ==\n\n  E1. [from M-three-column-stack, complete 2026-03-04]\n  The three-column stack established the schema: code, project, and math\n  columns with typed hyperedges and cross-column invariants (INV-1 through\n  INV-4). The plumbing works — 1,500+ hyperedges round-trip through XTDB.\n\n  E2. [from M-self-representing-stack, complete 2026-03-04]\n  Tensions trace through 6 gates, reflection envelopes ground strategic\n  claims to source code. The navigable self-representation exists in\n  principle.\n\n  E3. [the gap]\n  The columns are mostly empty of cross-references. The code column has\n  namespaces and vars. The project column has devmaps and tensions. But\n  there are no edges saying \"build-portfolio-review was created by\n  M-mission-control, uses learn-as-you-go, has 3 evidence entries, and\n  every time it changes, transport/http.clj also changes.\" The browser\n  shows docbook matches. It could show the system's actual history.\n\n  == PART II: Five Patterns, Five Concerns ==\n\n  The mission carries five patterns from the PSR. Each governs a distinct\n  concern of the enrichment process:\n\n  P1. [p4ng/reflect-in-layers 节]\n  \"Layered reflection builds shared understanding and adaptive capacity.\"\n\n  CONCERN: Architecture. Enrichment is structured as numbered layers\n  (L0-L5), each a timestamped batch of hyperedges. Layer 0 is namespace\n  topology. Layer 1 is mission provenance. Each layer builds on the last.\n  The layer structure IS the architecture — not a deployment convenience\n  but a reflective scaffold that evolves with system maturity.\n\n  P2. [iching/hexagram-53-jian 引]\n  \"Durable change is built incrementally.\"\n\n  CONCERN: Incrementality. No big-bang import. Each layer is small enough\n  to review, verify, and correct before the next. Layer 0 adds topology\n  and complexity signals. Only after L0 is verified do we attempt L1\n  (mission provenance). The rational reconstruction proceeds gradually —\n  each step is durable before the next begins.\n\n  P3. [agent/evidence-over-assertion 示]\n  \"Evidence transforms agent output from 'trust me' to 'check this.'\"\n\n  CONCERN: Grounding. Every enrichment layer produces verifiable\n  artifacts: hyperedge counts (before/after), anomaly lists, and an\n  evidence entry summarizing what was found. The enrichment is not a\n  claim about the codebase — it is a checkable record of what was\n  observed. If the reflection API says a namespace has no docstring,\n  that's a fact recorded as an invariant violation, not an opinion.\n\n  P4. [stack-coherence/evidence-ledger 本]\n  \"Claims must cite reproducible proofs or they drift into fiction.\"\n\n  CONCERN: Auditability. Each layer is a ledger entry:\n  meta/enrichment-layer hyperedge with timestamp, counts, anomalies.\n  Re-running the same layer produces the same edges (idempotent).\n  XTDB bitemporality means the ledger is append-only — corrections\n  in later layers don't erase earlier layers, they supersede them.\n  The audit trail is the enrichment history itself.\n\n  P5. [realtime/learn-as-you-go 日]\n  \"Explicit learnings reduce repeated mistakes and speed calibration.\"\n\n  CONCERN: Learning capture. The rational reconstruction framing means\n  each layer records not just what was found but what was SURPRISING.\n  \"3 orphan namespaces\" is a finding. \"Orphan namespace X was expected\n  to be dead code but is actually the main entry point for the IRC\n  bridge\" is a learning. Learnings feed back into the enrichment\n  discipline for subsequent layers and future missions.\n\n  == PART III: Key Design Decisions ==\n\n  D1. Extend, don't rewrite.\n  [see enrichment/extend-not-rewrite]\n  IF: ingest-three-columns.py already ingests code/namespace, code/var,\n      code/requires — 80% of Layer 0.\n  HOWEVER: It lacks git churn and indentation complexity.\n  THEN: Add --layer0 flag with two new functions to the existing script.\n  BECAUSE: I-4 (Read Before You Write) — writing a new script when the\n  existing one handles the hard parts (HTTP helpers, hyperedge format,\n  error handling, verification) wastes effort and ignores hard-won\n  design decisions embedded in the existing code.\n\n  D2. Git churn as behavioral signal.\n  [see enrichment/churn-as-signal]\n  IF: We want hotspot analysis (Tornhill) — files that change often AND\n      are complex are likely sources of bugs.\n  HOWEVER: Git history is noisy (formatting commits, mass renames).\n  THEN: Ingest raw commit counts per file as code/file-churn hyperedges\n      and let downstream analysis filter noise. Store commits-all-time,\n      commits-last-90d, and last-touch date.\n  BECAUSE: Behavioral signals (what DID change) complement structural\n  signals (what IS complex). Keeping them separate preserves the ability\n  to compose them differently later.\n\n  D3. Indentation as universal complexity proxy.\n  [see enrichment/indentation-as-complexity]\n  IF: The reflection API provides rich metadata for Clojure but nothing\n      for Elisp, Python, or EDN.\n  HOWEVER: Indentation-as-complexity is crude — it correlates with\n      nesting depth but misses semantic complexity.\n  THEN: Ingest mean/max indentation depth per file as a cheap, universal\n      bootstrap signal. Refine later with language-specific analysis.\n  BECAUSE: A crude universal metric that exists NOW is more useful than a\n  precise language-specific metric that doesn't exist yet. (Giugliano\n  demonstrated this works at multi-million-line scale.)\n\n  D4. Layer metadata as first-class evidence.\n  [see enrichment/layer-as-evidence]\n  IF: Each layer produces hyperedges.\n  HOWEVER: Without metadata, we can't distinguish \"layer 0 ingested at\n      10:00\" from \"layer 0 re-run at 14:00 after fixing a bug.\"\n  THEN: Each layer run produces a meta/enrichment-layer hyperedge with\n      timestamp, before/after counts, anomalies, and a human-readable\n      summary.\n  BECAUSE: The layer record IS evidence in the evidence-over-assertion\n  sense. It lets future agents and humans ask \"what did we know as of\n  layer N?\" and get a precise, timestamped answer.\n\n  D5. Rational reconstruction over bulk import.\n  [see enrichment/rational-reconstruction]\n  IF: The codebase has 5+ years of history that could be bulk-analyzed.\n  HOWEVER: A bulk import produces edges with no provenance — you know\n      what the codebase looks like but not how the understanding was built.\n  THEN: Replay enrichment as layered rational reconstruction, where each\n      layer is a timestamped snapshot with its own evidence trail.\n  BECAUSE: The reconstruction process is transferable (works on any\n  codebase). The replay is valuable (XTDB bitemporality makes the growth\n  of understanding queryable). And the corrections between layers are\n  themselves informative — they show where the codebase's self-image\n  was wrong.\n\n  == PART IV: What Layer 0 Specifically Produces ==\n\n  Layer 0 is the bootstrap layer. It produces:\n\n  1. Namespace entities (code/namespace) — already exists in script\n  2. Var entities (code/var) — already exists in script\n  3. Dependency edges (code/requires) — already exists in script\n  4. File churn metrics (code/file-churn) — NEW\n  5. Indentation complexity (code/indentation-complexity) — NEW\n  6. Layer metadata (meta/enrichment-layer) — NEW\n  7. Cross-column invariants (invariant/*) — already exists in script\n\n  Items 1-3 and 7 are the existing --code + --invariants pipeline.\n  Items 4-6 are ~65 lines of new Python.\n\n  == PART V: Success Criteria ==\n\n  Layer 0 succeeds when:\n  - All futon-prefixed namespaces appear as code/namespace hyperedges\n  - All futon-internal require edges appear as code/requires hyperedges\n  - Every .clj and .el file in futon3c, futon1a, futon4 has a\n    code/file-churn and code/indentation-complexity hyperedge\n  - A meta/enrichment-layer hyperedge records the run with counts\n  - Hotspot ranking (high churn + high complexity) identifies at least\n    3 files that warrant attention\n  - The count is verifiable: --count reports the new types\n\n  == EVIDENCE ==\n\n  - M-three-column-stack complete: 1,500+ hyperedges in XTDB (2026-03-04)\n  - M-self-representing-stack complete: tension traces work (2026-03-04)\n  - ingest-three-columns.py tested: code column ingestion operational\n  - Reflection API live: /reflect/namespaces returns metadata\n  - Giugliano EmacsConf 2020: indentation-as-complexity validated at\n    multi-million-line scale\n  - Tornhill \"Code as a Crime Scene\": git churn × complexity = hotspots,\n    validated commercially (CodeScene product)\n\n  == STATUS ==\n\n  This argument is at ARGUE in the derivation xenotype.\n\n  COMPLETE:\n  - IDENTIFY: connective tissue gap (E1-E3)\n  - MAP: existing infrastructure surveyed (ingest script, reflection API,\n    hyperedge API, MC tensions)\n  - DERIVE: Layer 0 designed (extend existing script with 3 new functions)\n  - ARGUE: this document (5 patterns carried, 5 decisions argued)\n\n  NEXT:\n  - VERIFY: test Layer 0 against live APIs\n  - INSTANTIATE: run Layer 0, record evidence, update mission status", :directives {:audience "futon developers, Arxana users, mission operators", :keywords ["enrichment" "rational-reconstruction" "layer" "hyperedge" "evidence" "topology" "provenance" "tension" "replay"], :references ["enrichment/layered-ingestion" "enrichment/rational-reconstruction" "enrichment/churn-as-signal" "enrichment/extend-not-rewrite" "enrichment/layer-as-evidence" "p4ng/reflect-in-layers" "iching/hexagram-53-jian" "agent/evidence-over-assertion" "stack-coherence/evidence-ledger" "realtime/learn-as-you-go"], :style "argument", :title "Argument for Futon Enrichment: Rational Reconstruction of Cross-Column Connective Tissue", :tone "analytic-foundational"}, :id "enrichment/ARGUMENT", :keywords ["enrichment" "rational-reconstruction" "layer" "hyperedge" "evidence" "topology" "provenance" "tension" "replay"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["enrichment/layered-ingestion" "enrichment/rational-reconstruction" "enrichment/churn-as-signal" "enrichment/extend-not-rewrite" "enrichment/layer-as-evidence" "p4ng/reflect-in-layers" "iching/hexagram-53-jian" "agent/evidence-over-assertion" "stack-coherence/evidence-ledger" "realtime/learn-as-you-go"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/enrichment/ARGUMENT.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Argument for Futon Enrichment: Rational Reconstruction of Cross-Column Connective Tissue", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Ingest raw git commit counts per file as a behavioral complement to\n  structural complexity, keeping them separate so they compose freely."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Tornhill's \"Code as a Crime Scene\" showed that files which change\n    frequently AND are complex are \"hotspots\" — likely sources of bugs. Files\n    that always change together have \"temporal coupling\" — hidden dependencies.\n    Giugliano demonstrated this at multi-million-line scale from Emacs."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    We want to identify hotspots (high risk), temporal coupling (hidden deps),\n    and knowledge concentration (\"who knows this file?\")."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Git history is noisy — formatting commits, mass renames, and merge commits\n    inflate change counts without indicating real complexity growth."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Ingest raw commit counts as code/file-churn hyperedges (commits-all-time,\n    commits-last-90d, last-touch). Store behavioral and structural signals\n    separately so downstream analysis can compose them (churn x complexity =\n    hotspot) or filter noise (exclude merge commits) without re-ingesting."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Behavioral signals (what DID change) complement structural signals (what\n    IS complex). A file with high complexity but zero churn is stable. A file\n    with low complexity but high churn is volatile. Neither signal alone\n    identifies the hotspot — only their composition does."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - evidence: hotspot ranking (top 10 files by churn x complexity)\n    - evidence: temporal coupling pairs (files that change together > 5 times)\n    - falsifying-observation: if top hotspots are all test files or configs,\n      the metric is capturing noise not signal — recalibrate."}], :conclusion "  Ingest raw git commit counts per file as a behavioral complement to\n  structural complexity, keeping them separate so they compose freely.", :directives {:audience "futon developers, enrichment operators", :factor "Keen investigation (dhammavicaya)", :keywords ["churn" "git" "hotspot" "behavioral" "signal" "frequency" "coupling" "temporal"], :references ["agent/evidence-over-assertion" "realtime/learn-as-you-go" "enrichment/ARGUMENT"], :sigils ["日/示"], :title "Git Churn as Behavioral Signal", :tone "analytic-neutral"}, :id "enrichment/churn-as-signal", :keywords ["churn" "git" "hotspot" "behavioral" "signal" "frequency" "coupling" "temporal"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["agent/evidence-over-assertion" "realtime/learn-as-you-go" "enrichment/ARGUMENT"], :scores {}, :sigils ["日/示"], :source-path "library/enrichment/churn-as-signal.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Git Churn as Behavioral Signal", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  When 80% of the required functionality already exists in a tested script,\n  add to it rather than writing a replacement."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Layer 0 of M-futon-enrichment requires namespace topology, git\n    churn, and indentation complexity ingested as hyperedges. The existing\n    ingest-three-columns.py already handles namespace/var/requires ingestion\n    with tested HTTP helpers and error handling."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The existing ingestion script handles the hard infrastructure (HTTP POST,\n    error handling, count verification, EDN parsing) and 80% of the data."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    It lacks git churn and indentation complexity functions."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Add a --layer0 flag and two new functions (~65 lines) to the existing\n    script rather than writing a new enrichment-specific script."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    I-4 (Read Before You Write) — the existing code embeds design decisions\n    about endpoint format, penholder auth, error recovery, and verification\n    that would need to be re-discovered in a new script. Extension preserves\n    these decisions; rewriting discards them."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - evidence: diff of ingest-three-columns.py before/after Layer 0 addition\n    - evidence: --layer0 --count output showing new hyperedge types"}], :conclusion "  When 80% of the required functionality already exists in a tested script,\n  add to it rather than writing a replacement.", :directives {:audience "futon developers, enrichment operators", :factor "Keen investigation (dhammavicaya)", :keywords ["extend" "rewrite" "reuse" "existing" "script" "I-4" "read-before-write"], :references ["stack-coherence/evidence-ledger" "enrichment/ARGUMENT"], :sigils ["节/本"], :title "Extend, Don't Rewrite", :tone "pragmatic"}, :id "enrichment/extend-not-rewrite", :keywords ["extend" "rewrite" "reuse" "existing" "script" "I-4" "read-before-write"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["stack-coherence/evidence-ledger" "enrichment/ARGUMENT"], :scores {}, :sigils ["节/本"], :source-path "library/enrichment/extend-not-rewrite.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Extend, Don't Rewrite", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Use mean/max indentation depth as a cheap, language-agnostic complexity\n  bootstrap; refine with language-specific analysis in later layers."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "The reflection API provides rich structural metadata for Clojure\n    (arglists, protocols, dispatch). But .el, .py, .edn, and .js files have\n    no reflection endpoint. A universal complexity signal is needed for the\n    cross-repo hotspot analysis in Layer 0."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    We need a complexity metric that works for .clj, .el, .py, .js, and .edn\n    files — all languages in the futon stack."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Indentation depth correlates with nesting but misses semantic complexity\n    (a deeply-nested but simple cond vs. a flat but algorithmically dense\n    function). It's crude."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Ingest mean-depth, max-depth, and line-count per file as\n    code/indentation-complexity hyperedges. Treat this as a bootstrap signal\n    — good enough to identify outliers (\"why is this file nested 12 levels\n    deep?\"), refined in later layers with AST analysis or treesit."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    A crude universal metric that exists NOW is more useful than a precise\n    language-specific metric that doesn't exist yet. Giugliano validated\n    this at multi-million-line scale: \"Indentation is good enough to apply\n    in general. Even Lisp gets formatted in a standard way.\" The key is\n    using it to FIND weird things, not to MEASURE exact complexity."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - evidence: outlier report — files with max-depth > 10 or mean-depth > 5\n    - evidence: comparison with reflection-based complexity for Clojure files\n      (do indentation outliers match protocol/multimethod complexity?)\n    - falsifying-observation: if top-indentation files are all auto-generated\n      or data files, the metric is noise — exclude those file patterns."}], :conclusion "  Use mean/max indentation depth as a cheap, language-agnostic complexity\n  bootstrap; refine with language-specific analysis in later layers.", :directives {:audience "futon developers, enrichment operators", :factor "Keen investigation (dhammavicaya)", :keywords ["indentation" "complexity" "nesting" "depth" "universal" "proxy" "bootstrap"], :references ["iching/hexagram-53-jian" "p4ng/reflect-in-layers" "enrichment/ARGUMENT"], :sigils ["引/节"], :title "Indentation as Universal Complexity Proxy", :tone "pragmatic"}, :id "enrichment/indentation-as-complexity", :keywords ["indentation" "complexity" "nesting" "depth" "universal" "proxy" "bootstrap"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["iching/hexagram-53-jian" "p4ng/reflect-in-layers" "enrichment/ARGUMENT"], :scores {}, :sigils ["引/节"], :source-path "library/enrichment/indentation-as-complexity.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Indentation as Universal Complexity Proxy", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Each enrichment layer run produces a meta/enrichment-layer hyperedge that\n  records what was ingested, when, and what was surprising — making the\n  enrichment process itself auditable evidence."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Enrichment layers produce hyperedges about the codebase. Without\n    layer metadata, we cannot distinguish \"layer 0 ingested at 10:00\" from\n    \"layer 0 re-run at 14:00 after fixing a bug.\" The enrichment history\n    becomes opaque — exactly the opacity enrichment is meant to resolve."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    Each layer produces hyperedges (namespace topology, churn, complexity)."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Without a record of the layer itself, the enrichment has no provenance.\n    Was this the first run? A correction? What changed since last time?"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Each layer run POSTs a meta/enrichment-layer hyperedge containing:\n    layer number, ISO-8601 timestamp, hyperedge counts (before/after by type),\n    anomaly list (unexpected findings), and a human-readable summary.\n    Layers are idempotent — re-running updates edges, not duplicates them.\n    XTDB bitemporality preserves ALL runs regardless."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    The layer record IS evidence in the evidence-over-assertion [示] sense.\n    It answers \"what did we know as of layer N?\" with a precise, timestamped,\n    verifiable answer. The evidence-ledger [本] pattern demands that claims\n    cite reproducible proofs — the layer metadata is the proof that the\n    enrichment actually happened and produced specific, countable artifacts."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - evidence: meta/enrichment-layer hyperedge for Layer 0 with actual counts\n    - evidence: diff between two runs of the same layer (idempotency check)\n    - evidence-shape: {:layer :int, :timestamp :string, :before {:type :count},\n      :after {:type :count}, :anomalies [:string], :summary :string}"}], :conclusion "  Each enrichment layer run produces a meta/enrichment-layer hyperedge that\n  records what was ingested, when, and what was surprising — making the\n  enrichment process itself auditable evidence.", :directives {:audience "futon developers, enrichment operators", :factor "Keen investigation (dhammavicaya)", :keywords ["layer" "evidence" "metadata" "timestamp" "idempotent" "bitemporal" "ledger"], :references ["agent/evidence-over-assertion" "stack-coherence/evidence-ledger" "enrichment/ARGUMENT"], :sigils ["示/本"], :title "Each Layer Is an Evidence Entry", :tone "analytic-neutral"}, :id "enrichment/layer-as-evidence", :keywords ["layer" "evidence" "metadata" "timestamp" "idempotent" "bitemporal" "ledger"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["agent/evidence-over-assertion" "stack-coherence/evidence-ledger" "enrichment/ARGUMENT"], :scores {}, :sigils ["示/本"], :source-path "library/enrichment/layer-as-evidence.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Each Layer Is an Evidence Entry", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Structure enrichment as six layers (L0-L5) where each layer addresses one\n  enrichment concern and depends on the previous layer being verified."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "The code browser needs six kinds of enrichment data: docs\n    (existing), mission provenance, pattern provenance, evidence binding,\n    tension surface, and cross-futon dependencies. Ingesting all six at once\n    would be fragile (errors in topology corrupt provenance) and unverifiable\n    (too many moving parts to check)."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    Six enrichment concerns need to be ingested into the hyperedge store,\n    each with different data sources and validation criteria."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Later layers depend on earlier layers being correct (mission provenance\n    references namespaces from L0; evidence binding references missions from\n    L1). Ingesting them simultaneously means errors cascade silently."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Six layers, each verified before the next:\n    L0: Namespace topology + complexity + churn (structural ground truth)\n    L1: Mission provenance (var → mission, from mission docs + git blame)\n    L2: Pattern provenance (var → pattern, from PSR/PUR evidence)\n    L3: Evidence binding (var → evidence entries)\n    L4: Tension surface (var → invariant violations)\n    L5: Cross-futon dependencies (var → var across repos)"} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    The reflect-in-layers [节] pattern says layered reflection builds shared\n    understanding. Each layer is a checkpoint: verify L0 topology before\n    attempting L1 provenance. Hexagram-53 [引] says durable change is built\n    incrementally. The dependency chain (L0→L1→...→L5) makes each step\n    small enough to review and correct independently."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - evidence: Layer 0 verified (namespace count matches reflection API)\n    - evidence: Layer 1 verified (every var→mission edge traceable to a\n      mission doc or git blame commit)\n    - evidence: replay query showing L0→L1 growth in XTDB"}], :conclusion "  Structure enrichment as six layers (L0-L5) where each layer addresses one\n  enrichment concern and depends on the previous layer being verified.", :directives {:audience "futon developers, enrichment operators", :factor "Keen investigation (dhammavicaya)", :keywords ["layer" "ingestion" "topology" "provenance" "evidence" "tension" "dependency" "phase"], :references ["p4ng/reflect-in-layers" "iching/hexagram-53-jian" "enrichment/ARGUMENT"], :sigils ["节/引"], :title "Layered Ingestion Architecture", :tone "technical"}, :id "enrichment/layered-ingestion", :keywords ["layer" "ingestion" "topology" "provenance" "evidence" "tension" "dependency" "phase"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["p4ng/reflect-in-layers" "iching/hexagram-53-jian" "enrichment/ARGUMENT"], :scores {}, :sigils ["节/引"], :source-path "library/enrichment/layered-ingestion.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Layered Ingestion Architecture", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Build the codebase's self-representation through layered rational\n  reconstruction — replaying enrichment as if discipline had been maintained\n  all along — rather than a one-shot static analysis dump."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "The futon stack has 5+ years of development history across 8+\n    repos. A bulk static analysis could produce a comprehensive snapshot, but\n    it would have no provenance (when was this edge discovered?), no\n    correction trail (which early assumptions were wrong?), and no\n    transferable methodology (how would someone else do this for their\n    codebase?)."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    We want enrichment that is replayable, correctable, and transferable\n    to other codebases."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Rational reconstruction is slower than bulk import — each layer must be\n    verified before the next begins, and corrections between layers require\n    additional ingestion runs."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Structure enrichment as numbered layers (L0-L5), each a timestamped\n    batch of hyperedges with its own evidence entry. XTDB bitemporality\n    makes each layer queryable as-of its ingestion time. Corrections in\n    later layers supersede but don't erase earlier layers. The growth of\n    understanding is itself navigable."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Three advantages compound:\n    (1) REPLAYABILITY — \"what did we know about futon3c.peripheral as of\n    layer 3?\" is answerable via bitemporal query. This is impossible with\n    a one-shot dump.\n    (2) INCREMENTAL CORRECTION — early layers WILL have errors (orphan\n    namespaces that are actually entry points, churn spikes from mass\n    renames). Later layers fix them. The correction history shows where\n    the codebase's self-image was wrong — itself valuable knowledge.\n    (3) METHODOLOGY TRANSFER — the discipline of \"enrich incrementally,\n    record what you find, correct as you go\" is the discipline we'd want\n    any team to adopt. Building it on our own code first validates the\n    process. The learn-as-you-go [日] pattern ensures each layer captures\n    not just findings but surprises and learnings."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - evidence: Layer 0 run with meta/enrichment-layer record\n    - evidence: Layer 1 run that corrects at least one Layer 0 anomaly\n    - evidence: bitemporal query showing enrichment growth between layers\n    - falsifying-observation: if Layer 0→L1 correction rate is < 5%, the\n      rational reconstruction overhead may not justify itself over bulk\n      import — reassess at that point."}], :conclusion "  Build the codebase's self-representation through layered rational\n  reconstruction — replaying enrichment as if discipline had been maintained\n  all along — rather than a one-shot static analysis dump.", :directives {:audience "futon developers, enrichment operators, methodology designers", :factor "Keen investigation (dhammavicaya)", :keywords ["rational" "reconstruction" "replay" "bitemporal" "incremental" "correction" "history" "provenance"], :references ["realtime/learn-as-you-go" "iching/hexagram-53-jian" "enrichment/ARGUMENT" "enrichment/layer-as-evidence"], :sigils ["日/引"], :title "Rational Reconstruction Over Bulk Import", :tone "analytic-foundational"}, :id "enrichment/rational-reconstruction", :keywords ["rational" "reconstruction" "replay" "bitemporal" "incremental" "correction" "history" "provenance"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["realtime/learn-as-you-go" "iching/hexagram-53-jian" "enrichment/ARGUMENT" "enrichment/layer-as-evidence"], :scores {}, :sigils ["日/引"], :source-path "library/enrichment/rational-reconstruction.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Rational Reconstruction Over Bulk Import", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  When an outward EoI is being authored for a role whose\n  knowledge/experience clauses are calibrated to a particular\n  career-arc shape (e.g. \"7+ years in technical policy research /\n  think-tank / applied research\"), and the author's CV substantially\n  exceeds the floor but in a non-conventional shape (e.g. distributed\n  volunteer work, parallel research positions, dual roles), the EoI\n  must name the unconventional shape rather than pretend conventionality.\n  The pattern is similar to the foreclosure-honesty pattern: name what\n  is true rather than what is expected."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "    Applies during `address-now` tagging of an EoI Tension on\n    experience-floor / CV-shape clauses. The prompt asks: what\n    relevant experience do you have, and what is the shape it takes\n    that differs from the role's apparent expectation?"} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The role's posting specifies an experience floor or a CV shape,\n    AND the author's experience meets or exceeds the floor but in a\n    shape the posting did not explicitly name."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    The underrepresented-groups paragraph in many postings does\n    legitimate work — the EoI's response to a CV-shape clause should\n    take that paragraph literally when present. Naming non-conventional\n    shape is not the same as apology for it; the discipline is\n    description, not deferral."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Author drafts a short CV-shape-honesty paragraph that names the\n    substantive relevant experience (counted concretely where possible)\n    in its actual shape, without forcing it into the conventional\n    shape the posting implied."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Per writing-coherence/name-what-you-drop and\n    structure/whose-question-is-this: forcing one's CV into a shape\n    the recipient expected is consultant-voice and risks under-counting\n    relevant experience. Naming the actual shape is operator-voice and\n    lets the recipient calibrate their reading accurately."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    Author drafts the CV-shape paragraph in their own register;\n    eoi-engine assembly inserts it; reviewer checks that the relevant\n    experience is named in its actual shape, with concrete duration\n    or scope figures where available."} {:name "source-note", :name-key "source-note", :slug "source-note", :text "  Minted 2026-05-11 as part of the Arxana Essays annotation flow for\n  the Anthropic Institute first-draft, alongside tension-foreclosure\n  and tension-free-solo-rule. Surfaced from the strawman's diagnostic\n  6 for that case (\"7+ years experience clause reads as a floor for a\n  particular shape of CV ... Joe has substantially more than 7 years\n  but in a non-conventional shape\")."}], :conclusion "  When an outward EoI is being authored for a role whose\n  knowledge/experience clauses are calibrated to a particular\n  career-arc shape (e.g. \"7+ years in technical policy research /\n  think-tank / applied research\"), and the author's CV substantially\n  exceeds the floor but in a non-conventional shape (e.g. distributed\n  volunteer work, parallel research positions, dual roles), the EoI\n  must name the unconventional shape rather than pretend conventionality.\n  The pattern is similar to the foreclosure-honesty pattern: name what\n  is true rather than what is expected.", :directives {:audience "operators authoring outward EoIs whose CV does not match the role's expected-shape (e.g. years-of-experience clauses calibrated to a particular career arc)", :keywords ["eoi" "cv" "experience-floor" "address-now" "conventional-shape-fallacy" "strawman-diagnostic"], :references ["eoi-engine/eoi-outward-one-shot," "writing-coherence/name-what-you-drop," "structure/whose-question-is-this"], :title "EoI Tension Prompt — Non-Conventional CV Shape Honesty", :tone "diagnostic-prompt"}, :id "eoi-engine/tension-cv-shape", :keywords ["eoi" "cv" "experience-floor" "address-now" "conventional-shape-fallacy" "strawman-diagnostic"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["eoi-engine/eoi-outward-one-shot," "writing-coherence/name-what-you-drop," "structure/whose-question-is-this"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/eoi-engine/tension-cv-shape.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "EoI Tension Prompt — Non-Conventional CV Shape Honesty", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  When an outward EoI's acceptance would foreclose other active basins\n  (other employment, freelance work, ongoing relationships, geographic\n  continuity, IP-scoped self-directed work), the EoI must name what is\n  being let go rather than pretending the decision is foreclosure-free.\n  Pretending \"no foreclosure\" is the failure mode the strawman\n  discipline names; the EoI's Right Speech requires the drop list to\n  be co-located with the inheritance list."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "    Applies during `address-now` tagging of an EoI Tension. The prompt\n    asks the author: which of your other active basins would this\n    engagement foreclose? Name the foreclosures explicitly in the EoI;\n    do not let them remain implicit."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The role's terms (salary, geography, time commitment, IP) would\n    materially alter or end other active relationships, projects, or\n    obligations the author is carrying."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Naming foreclosures inside an outward EoI must be calibrated.\n    Excessive foreclosure-listing reads as ambivalence rather than\n    honesty. The pattern is: name the substantive foreclosures\n    succinctly; do not pretend they do not exist; do not catalogue\n    every minor adjustment."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Author writes a short foreclosure-honesty paragraph naming the\n    substantive losses an acceptance would entail. Length: one\n    paragraph or less."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Per writing-coherence/name-what-you-drop: silent inheritance of\n    scope is the failure mode. Per\n    structure/unresolved-tensions-at-closure: tensions that should\n    be preserved-and-named must not be forced into resolution by\n    omission. The foreclosure-honesty paragraph is the EoI's\n    sorry-preservation move at the institutional-scope level."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    Author drafts the foreclosure paragraph in their own register;\n    eoi-engine assembly inserts it; reviewer checks that the\n    paragraph names the substantive foreclosures rather than\n    catalogue-listing."} {:name "source-note", :name-key "source-note", :slug "source-note", :text "  Minted 2026-05-11 as part of the Arxana Essays annotation flow for\n  the Anthropic Institute first-draft. Three tension-prompt flexiargs\n  were created (foreclosure / free-solo-rule / cv-shape) as annotation\n  sources because the existing eoi-engine VERIFY pass surfaced three\n  `address-now`-tagged tensions whose content Joe had not yet supplied."}], :conclusion "  When an outward EoI's acceptance would foreclose other active basins\n  (other employment, freelance work, ongoing relationships, geographic\n  continuity, IP-scoped self-directed work), the EoI must name what is\n  being let go rather than pretending the decision is foreclosure-free.\n  Pretending \"no foreclosure\" is the failure mode the strawman\n  discipline names; the EoI's Right Speech requires the drop list to\n  be co-located with the inheritance list.", :directives {:audience "operators authoring outward EoIs whose acceptance forecloses other active basins", :keywords ["eoi" "foreclosure" "honesty" "address-now" "no-foreclosure-fallacy" "strawman-diagnostic"], :references ["eoi-engine/eoi-outward-one-shot," "structure/unresolved-tensions-at-closure," "writing-coherence/name-what-you-drop"], :title "EoI Tension Prompt — Foreclosure Honesty", :tone "diagnostic-prompt"}, :id "eoi-engine/tension-foreclosure", :keywords ["eoi" "foreclosure" "honesty" "address-now" "no-foreclosure-fallacy" "strawman-diagnostic"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["eoi-engine/eoi-outward-one-shot," "structure/unresolved-tensions-at-closure," "writing-coherence/name-what-you-drop"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/eoi-engine/tension-foreclosure.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "EoI Tension Prompt — Foreclosure Honesty", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  When an outward EoI is being authored while the author holds a\n  publicly-stated rule against applying (e.g. Joe's August-2026\n  free-solo policy: no grants, no VC, no job applications), the EoI\n  must address the rule honestly: either (a) state that the\n  application is the considered output of a phase-space MAP, not\n  application-as-relief-from-uncertainty, or (b) state that the\n  rule is being re-examined in light of a specific surfaced\n  alternative, or (c) name that the rule applies and this is the\n  exception and why. What the EoI must not do: pretend the rule\n  does not exist."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "    Applies during `address-now` tagging of an EoI Tension on outward\n    applications written under a no-apply stance. The prompt asks: do\n    you flag the rule to the recipient, or do you not? If yes, in what\n    framing? If no, what makes this exception coherent under the rule's\n    spirit?"} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The author's public stance (or stance known to plausible recipients)\n    includes a no-application rule, AND the artefact being authored is\n    an application."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    The rule's framing inside the EoI itself is calibrated by who the\n    recipient is and what they already know. If the rule is not\n    publicly visible to this recipient, raising it pre-emptively may\n    look performative; if it is publicly visible, omitting it looks\n    evasive."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Author chooses one of routes (a) / (b) / (c) and writes a short\n    rule-honesty paragraph in their own register."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Per structure/whose-question-is-this: the EoI is the author's\n    speech-act, in inhabited mode. Pretending the rule does not exist\n    is consultant-voice; naming it honestly is operator-voice. The\n    Right Speech discipline requires the latter."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    Author drafts the rule-honesty paragraph; eoi-engine assembly\n    inserts or omits it per the author's chosen route; reviewer checks\n    that the framing matches the recipient's actual visibility."} {:name "source-note", :name-key "source-note", :slug "source-note", :text "  Minted 2026-05-11 as part of the Arxana Essays annotation flow for\n  the Anthropic Institute first-draft, alongside tension-foreclosure\n  and tension-cv-shape. Surfaced from the strawman's diagnostic 3\n  for that case (Mama-Claude's a/b/c options for the August-2026\n  free-solo rule)."}], :conclusion "  When an outward EoI is being authored while the author holds a\n  publicly-stated rule against applying (e.g. Joe's August-2026\n  free-solo policy: no grants, no VC, no job applications), the EoI\n  must address the rule honestly: either (a) state that the\n  application is the considered output of a phase-space MAP, not\n  application-as-relief-from-uncertainty, or (b) state that the\n  rule is being re-examined in light of a specific surfaced\n  alternative, or (c) name that the rule applies and this is the\n  exception and why. What the EoI must not do: pretend the rule\n  does not exist.", :directives {:audience "operators authoring outward EoIs while holding a publicly-stated no-grants-no-VC-no-job-apps stance", :keywords ["eoi" "free-solo" "application-policy" "address-now" "applications-as-relief-fallacy" "strawman-diagnostic"], :references ["eoi-engine/eoi-outward-one-shot," "eoi-engine/tension-foreclosure," "structure/whose-question-is-this"], :title "EoI Tension Prompt — August-2026 Free-Solo Rule Honesty", :tone "diagnostic-prompt"}, :id "eoi-engine/tension-free-solo-rule", :keywords ["eoi" "free-solo" "application-policy" "address-now" "applications-as-relief-fallacy" "strawman-diagnostic"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["eoi-engine/eoi-outward-one-shot," "eoi-engine/tension-foreclosure," "structure/whose-question-is-this"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/eoi-engine/tension-free-solo-rule.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "EoI Tension Prompt — August-2026 Free-Solo Rule Honesty", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  == PART I: The Equity Loop ==\n\n  E1. [Evidence: equity.tex, theoretical synthesis]\n  Three inputs — Sen's capability approach, Ostrom's commons\n  governance, and Alexander's pattern-language method — reduce to a\n  single design criterion: does this system close the loop between\n  contribution, governance, and capability?\n\n  E2. [Evidence: equity.tex, five diagnostic questions]\n  The criterion decomposes into five questions:\n  1. Contribution: What kinds of labour keep the system alive?\n  2. Recognition: How does doing that work create legitimate stake?\n  3. Governance: What rights do contributors have to shape the system?\n  4. Capability: How does participation enlarge real opportunity to act?\n  5. Feedback: What mechanisms show whether the loop is working?\n\n  E3. [Evidence: equity.tex, failure modes table]\n  Six ways the loop breaks: contribution without governance (extraction),\n  governance without capability expansion (performative), capability\n  without shared stake (training for exit), governance without\n  contribution (managerial), contribution without feedback (motivation\n  decay), local adaptation without shared stewardship (fragmentation).\n\n  == PART II: The Five Patterns ==\n\n  P1. [Pattern: equity/confident-adaptation]\n  Adaptation without clarity is guesswork.\n  Equity loop joint: contribution→stake.\n  Coordination: Low (1 actor).\n\n  P2. [Pattern: equity/protected-interaction]\n  Delivery without provisioning produces overload.\n  Equity loop joint: governance→capability.\n  Coordination: Medium (2 actors).\n\n  P3. [Pattern: equity/shared-maintenance]\n  Local adaptation without stewardship fragments the commons.\n  Equity loop joint: capability→contribution.\n  Coordination: Medium-high (community).\n\n  P4. [Pattern: equity/contribution-pathways]\n  Recipients without voice remain recipients.\n  Equity loop joint: contribution→stake→governance.\n  Coordination: High (infrastructure).\n\n  P5. [Pattern: equity/visible-impact]\n  A programme that cannot see its own effects cannot learn.\n  Equity loop joint: feedback (whole loop).\n  Coordination: Very high (6+ actor chain).\n\n  == PART III: The Coordination Gradient ==\n\n  The five patterns are not independent recommendations.  They form a\n  gradient: each requires more actors, in longer chains, to resolve.\n  A programme that addresses patterns 1-3 will improve its training\n  input.  A programme that also addresses patterns 4-5 will build the\n  institutional architecture needed for that input to persist as\n  collective practice.\n\n  The gradient explains why partial success is common: an institution\n  that resolves conditions 1-3 but not 4-5 will see good delivery but\n  fragile sustainability.  This is not a failure of effort; it is a\n  predictable consequence of incomplete institutional architecture.\n\n  == PART IV: Relevance to FUTON ==\n\n  CONCERN: FUTON's own coordination layers face the same equity\n  questions.  Do contributors to the pattern library gain recognised\n  scope to shape the stack?  Does participation in missions enlarge\n  real capability?  Does the glacial loop close the feedback chain?\n  Every FUTON layer should be testable against the five diagnostic\n  questions from equity.tex.  If the equity loop does not close,\n  the layer is extractive regardless of its stated intentions.\n\n  The university case (futon7/holes/M-another-university.md)\n  demonstrates what happens when a well-funded programme coordinates\n  activities without coordinating the architecture that makes those\n  activities productive.  FUTON exists partly because that failure is\n  not specific to universities — it is the general form of the problem.\n\n  == THESIS ==\n\n  The five equity patterns specify, in conditional form, what must\n  hold for a distributed collective production system to close the\n  equity loop.  They were derived from one empirical case but the\n  loop they inspect is domain-independent.  The coordination gradient\n  they form is a structural prediction: systems that resolve the\n  lower-complexity conditions but not the higher-complexity ones will\n  produce partial, fragile outcomes — not because participants lack\n  motivation, but because the institutional architecture is incomplete."}], :conclusion "  == PART I: The Equity Loop ==\n\n  E1. [Evidence: equity.tex, theoretical synthesis]\n  Three inputs — Sen's capability approach, Ostrom's commons\n  governance, and Alexander's pattern-language method — reduce to a\n  single design criterion: does this system close the loop between\n  contribution, governance, and capability?\n\n  E2. [Evidence: equity.tex, five diagnostic questions]\n  The criterion decomposes into five questions:\n  1. Contribution: What kinds of labour keep the system alive?\n  2. Recognition: How does doing that work create legitimate stake?\n  3. Governance: What rights do contributors have to shape the system?\n  4. Capability: How does participation enlarge real opportunity to act?\n  5. Feedback: What mechanisms show whether the loop is working?\n\n  E3. [Evidence: equity.tex, failure modes table]\n  Six ways the loop breaks: contribution without governance (extraction),\n  governance without capability expansion (performative), capability\n  without shared stake (training for exit), governance without\n  contribution (managerial), contribution without feedback (motivation\n  decay), local adaptation without shared stewardship (fragmentation).\n\n  == PART II: The Five Patterns ==\n\n  P1. [Pattern: equity/confident-adaptation]\n  Adaptation without clarity is guesswork.\n  Equity loop joint: contribution→stake.\n  Coordination: Low (1 actor).\n\n  P2. [Pattern: equity/protected-interaction]\n  Delivery without provisioning produces overload.\n  Equity loop joint: governance→capability.\n  Coordination: Medium (2 actors).\n\n  P3. [Pattern: equity/shared-maintenance]\n  Local adaptation without stewardship fragments the commons.\n  Equity loop joint: capability→contribution.\n  Coordination: Medium-high (community).\n\n  P4. [Pattern: equity/contribution-pathways]\n  Recipients without voice remain recipients.\n  Equity loop joint: contribution→stake→governance.\n  Coordination: High (infrastructure).\n\n  P5. [Pattern: equity/visible-impact]\n  A programme that cannot see its own effects cannot learn.\n  Equity loop joint: feedback (whole loop).\n  Coordination: Very high (6+ actor chain).\n\n  == PART III: The Coordination Gradient ==\n\n  The five patterns are not independent recommendations.  They form a\n  gradient: each requires more actors, in longer chains, to resolve.\n  A programme that addresses patterns 1-3 will improve its training\n  input.  A programme that also addresses patterns 4-5 will build the\n  institutional architecture needed for that input to persist as\n  collective practice.\n\n  The gradient explains why partial success is common: an institution\n  that resolves conditions 1-3 but not 4-5 will see good delivery but\n  fragile sustainability.  This is not a failure of effort; it is a\n  predictable consequence of incomplete institutional architecture.\n\n  == PART IV: Relevance to FUTON ==\n\n  CONCERN: FUTON's own coordination layers face the same equity\n  questions.  Do contributors to the pattern library gain recognised\n  scope to shape the stack?  Does participation in missions enlarge\n  real capability?  Does the glacial loop close the feedback chain?\n  Every FUTON layer should be testable against the five diagnostic\n  questions from equity.tex.  If the equity loop does not close,\n  the layer is extractive regardless of its stated intentions.\n\n  The university case (futon7/holes/M-another-university.md)\n  demonstrates what happens when a well-funded programme coordinates\n  activities without coordinating the architecture that makes those\n  activities productive.  FUTON exists partly because that failure is\n  not specific to universities — it is the general form of the problem.\n\n  == THESIS ==\n\n  The five equity patterns specify, in conditional form, what must\n  hold for a distributed collective production system to close the\n  equity loop.  They were derived from one empirical case but the\n  loop they inspect is domain-independent.  The coordination gradient\n  they form is a structural prediction: systems that resolve the\n  lower-complexity conditions but not the higher-complexity ones will\n  produce partial, fragile outcomes — not because participants lack\n  motivation, but because the institutional architecture is incomplete.", :directives {:audience "futon developers, programme designers, theory reviewers", :keywords ["equity" "capability" "governance" "commons" "contribution" "loop" "Sen" "Ostrom" "Alexander" "Mehaffy" "NPT"], :references ["equity/confident-adaptation" "equity/protected-interaction" "equity/shared-maintenance" "equity/contribution-pathways" "equity/visible-impact"], :style "argument", :title "Argument for the Equity Pattern Domain", :tone "analytic-foundational"}, :id "equity/ARGUMENT", :keywords ["equity" "capability" "governance" "commons" "contribution" "loop" "Sen" "Ostrom" "Alexander" "Mehaffy" "NPT"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["equity/confident-adaptation" "equity/protected-interaction" "equity/shared-maintenance" "equity/contribution-pathways" "equity/visible-impact"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/equity/ARGUMENT.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Argument for the Equity Pattern Domain", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Trainers cannot adapt confidently without knowing what must be retained and what can be changed.  The programme should publish an expectation scaffold — a clearly enunciated stance on what is fixed (core topics, minimum delivery format, evaluation expectation) and what is flexible (local sequencing, depth, audience, delivery mode) — ideally produced by the training community rather than issued to them, so that trainers' confusion is converted into acts of governance rather than private compensation.  The programme publishes a current expectation scaffold stating what is fixed and what is flexible.  The scaffold is produced with trainer input and revised in light of their experience."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "This is NPT Coherence — specifically 1.2 Communal Specification: a shared understanding of what is being done and why.  It is an intervention-tier pattern with low coordination: a central agency (the Training Project) can resolve it by producing and maintaining the scaffold.  The paper describes this as the most tractable of the five patterns.  Its absence is cited as a direct contributor to architectural mismatch mode; its presence is a precondition for multiplied input."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    A central programme trains intermediaries who deliver in varied local settings.  Trainers bring motivation and partial competence but enter without a shared understanding of what the programme actually requires — which elements are non-negotiable, which admit local variation, and what \"ready to deliver\" looks like.  The training's own community-and-research dimensions may be unknown to trainers who registered for the course alone."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    The programme has not published the fixed/flexible distinction.  Trainers report uncertainty about what was fundamental, how many sessions were required, and whether the courses were part of a research project (FG2A.15, FG2B.1; T2A: \"It wasn't very clear to us how many sessions we needed to have attended, which ones were fundamental\"; T2B: \"I didn't realise when I first signed up for it that these courses were part of this research project\").  Where the expectation was explicit, the programme became an \"effective entry point\" (FG3.4; T3: \"It was useful to have a template of what was expected\").  The gap is informational, not a skill gap — an absence of communal specification rather than an absence of capability."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "      - Missing scaffold: trainers guess what is essential and deliver fragmented versions\n      - Scaffold-as-decree: scaffold exists but is issued without trainer input and becomes a compliance instrument\n      - Frozen scaffold: produced once, never revised; drifts from practice\n      - Scope invisibility: trainers unaware of the programme's research or community dimensions beyond the training itself"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    The Training Project produces a central expectation scaffold that enunciates what is fixed and what is flexible.  Production is reconnoitred with trainers rather than issued to them: the scaffold converts their confusion into acts of governance and becomes revisable by the community that uses it.  The scaffold is not a compliance checklist; it is a minimum specification that makes local adaptation a formative act rather than guesswork."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    NPT Coherence: 1.2 Communal Specification is the shared understanding that makes collective action possible.  Trainers reported high COM-B self-efficacy (\"I understand what this requires of me\" M=4.90) individually, but communal specification was absent at the programme level — which is what produces fragmented delivery even where individual capability is high.\n\n    Tier and coordination: This is an intervention-tier failure that can be resolved at low coordination.  A single central actor (the programme team) is sufficient.  The paper is most prescriptive here because the pattern is most tractable.\n\n    Governance framing: The scaffold is an act of governance rather than a directive.  The trainers' role in its production matters: a scaffold issued top-down converts into compliance; a scaffold reconnoitred with the community converts confusion into shared authorship of the programme's definition.\n\n    Mechanic, in short:\n    Derived from NPT Coherence 1.2 Communal Specification as stated in the companion journal article.\n    Tier: Intervention.  Coordination: Low (central agency).\n    Implementation-mode link: absence contributes directly to architectural mismatch; presence is a precondition for multiplied input.\n    The paper is most prescriptive here — the pattern is most tractable of the five."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Publish the expectation scaffold before the next cohort\n    - Produce it with trainers, not for them\n    - Make the fixed/flexible distinction the scaffold's central content, not an annex\n    - Design the scaffold as revisable so it remains a governance instrument rather than a decree\n    - Test by asking a new trainer to explain what is expected after reading the scaffold alone"}], :conclusion "Trainers cannot adapt confidently without knowing what must be retained and what can be changed.  The programme should publish an expectation scaffold — a clearly enunciated stance on what is fixed (core topics, minimum delivery format, evaluation expectation) and what is flexible (local sequencing, depth, audience, delivery mode) — ideally produced by the training community rather than issued to them, so that trainers' confusion is converted into acts of governance rather than private compensation.  The programme publishes a current expectation scaffold stating what is fixed and what is flexible.  The scaffold is produced with trainer input and revised in light of their experience.", :directives {:audience "programme designers, Training Project / central agency, UKRN leadership", :keywords ["expectation-scaffold" "communal-specification" "fixed-vs-flexible" "coherence" "NPT" "intervention-tier" "low-coordination"], :references ["equity/ARGUMENT" "equity/confident-adaptation"], :style "pattern", :title "Confident Adaptation (as published in paper v2.3)", :tone "paper-aligned"}, :id "equity/confident-adaptation-as-published", :keywords ["expectation-scaffold" "communal-specification" "fixed-vs-flexible" "coherence" "NPT" "intervention-tier" "low-coordination"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["equity/ARGUMENT" "equity/confident-adaptation"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/equity/confident-adaptation-as-published.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Confident Adaptation (as published in paper v2.3)", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Participants in a distributed programme can adapt confidently only when the programme publishes a current expectation scaffold that distinguishes what is fixed from what is flexible. The scaffold is not a compliance checklist. It is a form-giving governance instrument, produced with trainers and revised from their experience, that converts access to materials into real capability for local adaptation.  No participant enters the programme without access to a current expectation scaffold that distinguishes fixed from flexible elements, defines sufficient participation, and is revised in light of trainer experience."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "A centrally designed programme distributes materials and training into varied local settings. The programme depends on local adaptation, but adaptation is a formative process rather than a private act of compensation. In NPT terms this is Coherence 1.2 Communal Specification: a shared understanding of what is being done and why. In capability terms, the conversion at stake is informational: the participant needs enough structure to become a competent local adapter rather than a recipient guessing at hidden rules. In the paper's tier language this is an intervention-tier, low-coordination pattern: a central programme can initiate it, but it only remains live if trainers help revise it."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    A central programme trains intermediaries who deliver in varied local settings. Trainers bring motivation, partial competence, and local knowledge, but they enter without a shared understanding of which elements are non-negotiable, which admit local variation, what counts as ready to deliver, or whether the programme includes research and community dimensions beyond the course itself. The agent is in a condition of becoming: local judgement can develop, but only if the rules of the game are legible enough for productive learning to begin."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    The fixed/flexible distinction is not published. Trainers report uncertainty about what was fundamental, how many sessions were required, and whether the courses were part of a research project (FG2A.15, FG2B.1; T2A: \"It wasn't very clear to us how many sessions we needed to have attended\"; T2B: \"I didn't realise when I first signed up for it that these courses were part of this research project\"). Where an expectation was explicit, the programme became an effective entry point (FG3.4; T3: \"It was useful to have a template of what was expected\"). The gap is informational, not a simple skill gap: individual self-efficacy can be high while communal specification remains absent. Without form-giving information, trainers cannot calibrate adaptation, assess adequacy, or distinguish creative variation from accidental deviation."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "      - Missing scaffold: trainers guess what is essential and deliver fragmented versions\n      - Arrested formation: the agent cannot begin productive learning because the rules are opaque\n      - Guesswork adaptation: local changes happen without knowing what must be retained\n      - Scope invisibility: participants are unaware of research or community dimensions beyond training\n      - Scaffold-as-decree: the scaffold exists but is issued without trainer input and becomes compliance\n      - Frozen specification: the scaffold is published once and never revised"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    The programme publishes an expectation scaffold before the next cohort. It states the fixed elements (core topics, minimum delivery format, evaluation expectation, sufficient participation) and the flexible elements (local sequencing, depth, audience, delivery mode). It is produced with trainers, not merely issued to them: confusion becomes input to governance, and the scaffold remains revisable from the start. The scaffold says what the programme is, what it is not, and where the trainer's judgement is expected to operate."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    NPT: Communal Specification is the shared understanding that makes collective action possible. Trainers may individually understand what they think is required, but fragmented delivery follows when the programme-level specification is missing.\n\n    Sen: The offer of materials and training remains nominal unless participants can convert it into practical capability. The scaffold is a conversion factor: it turns access into the possibility of competent local adaptation.\n\n    Sloman/Lovelace and whole-board play: Information here is form-giving, not mere signal transmission. The scaffold is not a Jacquard card that makes the trainer perform; it shapes the conditions under which the trainer's own developing competence can take productive form. Without the rules of the game, not even productive failure is possible.\n\n    Governance: A scaffold issued once and frozen becomes a decree. A scaffold produced with trainers and revised by their experience is the first instance of rules-in-use being made legible to the agents they govern.\n\n    Mechanic, in short:\n    Derived from NPT Coherence 1.2 Communal Specification via the UKRN T3 analysis and companion paper.\n    Tier: Intervention. Coordination: Low (central agency initiates; trainer participation keeps it live).\n    Implementation-mode link: absence contributes to architectural mismatch; presence is a precondition for multiplied input.\n    Canonical deepening: informational conversion and form-giving scaffold.\n    Paper-specific mechanism: expectation scaffold produced with trainers and revised as governance."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Publish the expectation scaffold before the next cohort\n    - Make the fixed/flexible distinction the scaffold's central content\n    - Produce it with trainers, not for them\n    - Design the scaffold as revisable from the start\n    - Test: can a new trainer explain what is expected after reading the scaffold alone?\n    - Treat this as the lowest-coordination condition and resolve it before higher-coordination patterns depend on it"}], :conclusion "Participants in a distributed programme can adapt confidently only when the programme publishes a current expectation scaffold that distinguishes what is fixed from what is flexible. The scaffold is not a compliance checklist. It is a form-giving governance instrument, produced with trainers and revised from their experience, that converts access to materials into real capability for local adaptation.  No participant enters the programme without access to a current expectation scaffold that distinguishes fixed from flexible elements, defines sufficient participation, and is revised in light of trainer experience.", :directives {:audience "programme designers, Training Project / central agency, institutional leads, UKRN leadership, futon coordination layer", :keywords ["expectation-scaffold" "fixed-vs-flexible" "communal-specification" "coherence" "NPT" "Sen" "informational-conversion" "form-giving" "governance-instrument" "intervention-tier" "low-coordination"], :references ["equity/ARGUMENT" "equity/confident-adaptation" "equity/confident-adaptation-as-published" "equity/protected-interaction" "equity/shared-maintenance"], :style "pattern", :title "Confident Adaptation: Scaffolded Becoming", :tone "merged-canonical-paper-aligned"}, :id "equity/confident-adaptation-merged", :keywords ["expectation-scaffold" "fixed-vs-flexible" "communal-specification" "coherence" "NPT" "Sen" "informational-conversion" "form-giving" "governance-instrument" "intervention-tier" "low-coordination"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["equity/ARGUMENT" "equity/confident-adaptation" "equity/confident-adaptation-as-published" "equity/protected-interaction" "equity/shared-maintenance"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/equity/confident-adaptation-merged.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Confident Adaptation: Scaffolded Becoming", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Participants in a distributed programme can adapt confidently only when they know what is fixed and what is flexible. Without that scaffold, adaptation becomes guesswork and the agent's becoming is arrested before it can begin.  No participant enters the programme without access to a published expectation scaffold that distinguishes fixed from flexible elements and defines sufficient participation. The scaffold is designed to be revisable in light of participant experience."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "A centrally designed programme distributes materials and training to local settings that differ in resources, culture, and existing infrastructure. The programme depends on local adaptation — but adaptation is a formative process, not a compliance task. Each participant enters in a condition of becoming: they have motivation, partial competence, and a local setting, but they do not yet know what kind of agent this programme needs them to be. In NPT terms this is Coherence (1.2 Communal Specification): a shared understanding of what is being done and why."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    An agent enters a distributed programme in a condition of becoming. The programme depends on their local adaptation — their capacity to develop, through interaction with materials, setting, and experience, the practical judgment that constitutes competent local delivery. This is analogous to the opening phase of whole-board play: the agent needs to know the rules of the game before productive learning can begin."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    The programme does not publish the distinction between what is fixed and what is flexible. The agent cannot internalise the programme's structure as their own operating structure because that structure is opaque. Without knowing what counts, the agent cannot calibrate their adaptation, assess their own adequacy, or distinguish creative local variation from accidental deviation. The result is not merely confusion but arrested formation: the agent remains a recipient of materials rather than becoming a competent local adapter. This is like asking someone to play Go without telling them the rules — not even bad play is possible, only flailing."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "      - Arrested formation: agent cannot begin productive learning because the game's rules are opaque\n      - Guesswork adaptation: trainer modifies content without knowing what is essential\n      - Fragmented delivery: different institutions deliver incompatible versions\n      - Invisible scope: participants unaware of programme dimensions beyond training (e.g. research, community)\n      - Frozen specification: scaffold published but never revised, becoming a decree rather than a governance instrument"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    The programme publishes a scaffold that distinguishes fixed from flexible elements — not as a compliance checklist but as the minimal specification that makes local adaptation a formative process rather than guesswork. The scaffold gives form to the agent's becoming: it says what the programme is, what it is not, and where the agent's own judgment is expected to operate. This is informational conversion in the Sloman/Lovelace sense (Sloman, 2010; Lovelace, 1843): information as form-giving, not signal transmission. The scaffold does not programme the trainer. It provides the conditions under which the trainer's own learning process can generate the right internal structure — the local perceptual filters that enable competent adaptation without seeing the whole board."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Sen: The conversion at stake is informational — but \"informational\" here means the conditions under which the agent can convert a nominal offer into a real capability to adapt, deliver, and develop. Without form-giving information, the offer remains nominal: the agent has materials but not the conditions for becoming a competent user of those materials. The scaffold is a conversion factor: it converts access into the possibility of productive learning.\n\n    Sloman/Lovelace: Early information-based machines (e.g. Jacquard looms) used information to control physical actions. But as Lovelace foresaw, computing machinery acts on information and on abstract mechanisms for acting on information (Sloman, 2010). The scaffold is not a Jacquard card that makes the trainer perform an action. It is a specification that shapes the abstract conditions under which the trainer's own developing competence — a virtual machine in Sloman's sense — can take productive form. The competent local adapter is not a state that can be transmitted; it is an internal structure that emerges from a learning process, like AlphaZero's local filters that emerge from whole-board play.\n\n    Ostrom: The scaffold is the first instance of rules-in-use being made legible to the agents they govern. But legibility is the beginning of co-evolution, not the end. As the agent develops through interaction with their local setting, they will encounter situations where the fixed/flexible boundary itself needs revision. If the scaffold is genuinely a governance instrument rather than a decree, it must be revisable by the agents it shapes. At this pattern's coordination level (one actor), the co-evolution is embryonic — but its absence here forecloses it at every subsequent level.\n\n    Mechanic, in short:\n    Derived from NPT Coherence (1.2 Communal Specification) via the UKRN T3 analysis.\n    Mapped to the equity loop at contribution→stake: contribution without clarity creates no recognised stake.\n    Deepened via Sloman/Lovelace: informational conversion is form-giving, not signal transmission.\n    The scaffold is analogous to the rules of Go: without them, not even productive failure is possible.\n    Coordination requirement: Low (1 actor)."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Publish the scaffold before cohort 1, not after problems emerge\n    - Test: can a new participant explain what is expected of them after reading the scaffold alone?\n    - Design the scaffold as revisable from the start — not a frozen decree but a living specification\n    - This is the lowest-coordination condition (1 actor: the central programme team) and should be resolved first"}], :conclusion "Participants in a distributed programme can adapt confidently only when they know what is fixed and what is flexible. Without that scaffold, adaptation becomes guesswork and the agent's becoming is arrested before it can begin.  No participant enters the programme without access to a published expectation scaffold that distinguishes fixed from flexible elements and defines sufficient participation. The scaffold is designed to be revisable in light of participant experience.", :directives {:audience "programme designers, institutional leads, futon coordination layer", :keywords ["adaptation" "clarity" "specification" "scaffold" "expectations" "coherence" "NPT" "Sen" "conversion" "form-giving" "Lovelace" "Sloman"], :references ["equity/ARGUMENT" "equity/protected-interaction" "equity/shared-maintenance"], :style "pattern", :title "Adaptation Without Clarity Is Guesswork", :tone "analytic-foundational"}, :id "equity/confident-adaptation", :keywords ["adaptation" "clarity" "specification" "scaffold" "expectations" "coherence" "NPT" "Sen" "conversion" "form-giving" "Lovelace" "Sloman"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["equity/ARGUMENT" "equity/protected-interaction" "equity/shared-maintenance"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/equity/confident-adaptation.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Adaptation Without Clarity Is Guesswork", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Without explicit and well-maintained pathways for sustained contribution, trainers remain recipients or at best conduits rather than co-producers; delivery becomes fragile and goodwill-dependent.  The programme requires local delivery labour; it should also ensure that those doing the delivery gain governance rights over how that delivery happens, with sustained contribution leading to recognised standing.  This is Ostrom's collective-choice principle applied to training governance.  Every participant who sustains engagement beyond initial training has at least one visible route to contribute to programme development or governance.  Sustained contribution creates recognised standing.  The coordinator role is resourced and trained."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "This is NPT Cognitive Participation — specifically 2.4 Activation: how procedures and actions that sustain delivery are collectively defined.  It is the first architecture-tier pattern and requires high coordination (governed infrastructure).  The paper names this pattern as the one that separates absorbed input from multiplied input: where co-ownership emerged, engagement sustained; where it did not, engagement remained precarious and vulnerable to the trainer leaving."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    A programme has trained intermediaries who have developed local competence and may be contributing adaptations back to the shared materials (Pattern 3 resolved).  Sustaining engagement beyond initial training depends on whether those trainers are receivers of a centrally-defined programme or co-producers with recognised scope to shape it.  The programme's long-term viability rests on the latter."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    There are no explicit pathways for contribution beyond delivery.  No peer cohorts, no shared adaptation reviews, no mentoring structure.  The Community of Practice exists but most trainers are unaware of it (T1: \"I don't think I was aware of the Community of Practice\").  Peer channels exist but go unused (T2B: \"in theory, there's a teams channel, but I've never seen anyone use it\").  The ORCA coordinator role — which should serve as the backbone of community infrastructure — is undertrained and under-supported (T3: \"the ORCAs really didn't get any training\"; FG3: \"reactive rather than helpful\"; \"potentially undertrained relative to their level of responsibility\").  Where co-ownership emerged (FG1.19, FG1.25), sustained motivation followed: T1 — \"That ownership felt really important to me; and so then going on and sharing it and integrating it into my own practice\"; T1 — \"I wouldn't have delivered that training if I hadn't had this framework.\"  Where it did not, engagement was precarious (FG3.11).  Trainers articulate the knowledge-exit risk directly (T3: \"At some point I potentially leave with that knowledge and it doesn't get used as much as it could have\").  COM-B: \"I see the value\" M=5.37 vs. \"I feel positive\" M=4.21 — a gap between value recognition and positive activation."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "      - Goodwill-dependent delivery: practice survives only as long as the individual persists\n      - Invisible community: CoP exists formally but trainers are unaware\n      - Dead channels: peer exchange channels created but unstewarded and unused\n      - Undertrained coordinators: ORCA role exists but \"reactive rather than helpful\"\n      - Knowledge exit: trainers leave with knowledge that the programme fails to retain\n      - Extractive structure: system benefits from trainer labour without granting governance voice\n      - Value/activation gap: COM-B shows value is seen (5.37) but positive activation is weak (4.21)"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    The programme builds governed infrastructure: a steering group drawn from active contributors, a contribution log that makes otherwise-invisible labour visible in career-relevant terms, peer exchange channels that are actively stewarded rather than merely created, mentoring structures, and a properly resourced and trained coordinator role.  Sustained contribution leads to recognised standing in the programme's governance structure.  Governance standing logically follows from meaningful contribution, not the other way around.  Shared investment in community infrastructure presupposes capability return — participants invest because the investment enlarges their real capability to contribute and benefit."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    NPT Cognitive Participation: 2.4 Activation names the work of collectively defining procedures that sustain a practice.  Without contribution pathways, no one but the central programme team has the standing to define — so activation stays private, and practice survives only as long as individuals do.\n\n    Ostrom's collective-choice principle (explicitly cited by the paper): those who sustain the commons earn recognised rights to shape it.  The principle operationalises the equity loop's governance joint.  A programme that benefits from trainer labour without granting governance voice is extractive by structure, regardless of its stated intentions.\n\n    Architecture tier: This is the first pattern that requires governed infrastructure rather than organisational decision or community norm.  The steering group, contribution log, mentoring structure, and coordinator role are infrastructure — they exist across cohorts and enable sustained contribution at scale.  High coordination follows: multiple roles must orchestrate, and the orchestration itself must be governed.\n\n    Convergence with Pattern 5: contribution pathways (P4) and visible impact (P5) share architecture: trainers who contribute to the design via P4 also need to see their effects via P5 to remain contributors.  Both failures appear together in absorbed-mode institutions.\n\n    Mechanic, in short:\n    Derived from NPT Cognitive Participation 2.4 Activation as stated in the companion journal article.\n    Tier: Architecture.  Coordination: High (governed infrastructure).\n    Implementation-mode link: this pattern separates absorbed input from multiplied input.\n    The paper names the structural principle (collective-choice, governance-follows-contribution) and operationalises it via steering group, contribution log, and stewarded coordinator role."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Establish a steering group drawn from active contributors\n    - Build a contribution log that makes invisible labour visible in career-relevant terms\n    - Resource and train the coordinator role before expecting community to emerge\n    - Actively steward peer channels: facilitation, not just creation\n    - Design the governance so that sustained contribution creates recognised standing\n    - Test: does a motivated trainer have a visible route into co-ownership within the first year?"}], :conclusion "Without explicit and well-maintained pathways for sustained contribution, trainers remain recipients or at best conduits rather than co-producers; delivery becomes fragile and goodwill-dependent.  The programme requires local delivery labour; it should also ensure that those doing the delivery gain governance rights over how that delivery happens, with sustained contribution leading to recognised standing.  This is Ostrom's collective-choice principle applied to training governance.  Every participant who sustains engagement beyond initial training has at least one visible route to contribute to programme development or governance.  Sustained contribution creates recognised standing.  The coordinator role is resourced and trained.", :directives {:audience "programme designers, community coordinators, funders, governance-leads", :keywords ["co-producers" "collective-choice" "governance-standing" "contribution-log" "ORCA" "Community-of-Practice" "activation" "cognitive-participation" "NPT" "architecture-tier" "high-coordination" "capability-return"], :references ["equity/ARGUMENT" "equity/contribution-pathways"], :style "pattern", :title "Contribution Pathways (as published in paper v2.3)", :tone "paper-aligned"}, :id "equity/contribution-pathways-as-published", :keywords ["co-producers" "collective-choice" "governance-standing" "contribution-log" "ORCA" "Community-of-Practice" "activation" "cognitive-participation" "NPT" "architecture-tier" "high-coordination" "capability-return"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["equity/ARGUMENT" "equity/contribution-pathways"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/equity/contribution-pathways-as-published.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Contribution Pathways (as published in paper v2.3)", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Engagement sustains when trainers become co-producers with recognised scope to shape the programme, not merely recipients or conduits for a central offer. Sustained contribution should create governance standing. Without explicit contribution pathways, delivery becomes goodwill-dependent and the programme benefits from trainer labour without granting voice over the conditions of that labour.  Every participant who sustains engagement beyond initial training has at least one visible route to contribute to programme development, governance, or community support. Sustained contribution creates recognised standing. The coordinator role is resourced and trained."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "A programme needs trainers to sustain engagement beyond the first delivery wave. In NPT terms this is Cognitive Participation 2.4 Activation: how procedures and actions that sustain delivery are collectively defined. In Ostrom terms this is the collective-choice problem: can affected participants modify, extend, and help govern the programme, or only consume it? In the paper's tier language this is the first architecture-tier pattern and requires high coordination. In capability terms it is the transition from being shaped by a system to shaping it."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    Trainers have developed local competence through iterative delivery and their adaptations are flowing into the shared commons. The system is learning from variation. But the trainers' relationship to the system may still be one of consumption: they receive the programme, adapt it locally, and upstream improvements, without recognised scope to influence direction, priorities, or structure. Long-term viability rests on moving from receiver to co-producer."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    There are no explicit pathways for contribution beyond delivery. No peer cohorts, shared adaptation reviews, mentoring structure, steering group, or contribution log exists. The Community of Practice exists but most trainers are unaware of it (T1: \"I don't think I was aware of the Community of Practice\"). Peer channels exist but go unused (T2B: \"in theory, there's a teams channel, but I've never seen anyone use it\"). The ORCA coordinator role is undertrained and under-supported (T3: \"the ORCAs really didn't get any training\"; FG3: \"reactive rather than helpful\"). Where co-ownership emerged, sustained motivation followed (FG1.19, FG1.25; T1: \"That ownership felt really important to me\"). Where it did not, engagement remained precarious and vulnerable to the trainer leaving (FG3.11; T3: \"At some point I potentially leave with that knowledge and it doesn't get used as much as it could have\"). COM-B shows a value/activation gap: \"I see the value\" M=5.37 vs. \"I feel positive\" M=4.21."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "      - Capped becoming: agent develops local competence but cannot reshape the system\n      - Goodwill-dependent delivery: practice survives only as long as the individual persists\n      - Invisible community: CoP exists formally but trainers are unaware\n      - Dead channels: peer exchange channels are created but unstewarded and unused\n      - Undertrained coordinators: ORCA role exists but is reactive rather than helpful\n      - Knowledge exit: trainers leave with knowledge the programme fails to retain\n      - Extractive structure: system benefits from trainer labour without granting governance voice\n      - Value/activation gap: value is recognised but positive activation remains weak"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    The programme builds governed community infrastructure: a steering group drawn from active contributors, a contribution log that makes otherwise-invisible labour visible in career-relevant terms, actively stewarded peer channels, mentoring structures, adaptation reviews, and a properly resourced coordinator role. Sustained contribution creates recognised standing in programme governance. Governance standing follows meaningful contribution, not the other way around. Shared investment in community infrastructure presupposes capability return: participants invest because the investment enlarges their real ability to contribute and benefit."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    NPT: Activation is the collective definition of the actions and procedures that sustain a practice. Without contribution pathways, only the central programme has standing to define; activation stays private.\n\n    Sen: Capability expands when participants move from receipt to co-ownership. The deepest capability expansion is not skill acquisition, but the freedom to revise the conditions under which action takes place.\n\n    Whole-board play: This is the transition from playing within a fixed architecture to modifying the architecture itself. A distribution list delivers content; a community of practice co-evolves content, delivery, and governance.\n\n    Ostrom: Collective-choice arrangements give those who sustain the commons recognised rights to shape it. A programme that benefits from trainer labour without granting governance voice is extractive by structure, regardless of stated intentions.\n\n    Convergence with visible impact: contribution pathways and visible impact share architecture. Trainers who contribute to design also need to see their effects to remain contributors.\n\n    Mechanic, in short:\n    Derived from NPT Cognitive Participation 2.4 Activation.\n    Tier: Architecture. Coordination: High (governed infrastructure).\n    Implementation-mode link: this pattern separates absorbed input from multiplied input.\n    Canonical deepening: capability as the freedom to revise programme conditions.\n    Paper-specific mechanism: steering group, contribution log, and governance-follows-contribution."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Establish a steering group drawn from active contributors\n    - Build a contribution log that makes invisible labour visible in career-relevant terms\n    - Resource and train the coordinator role before expecting community to emerge\n    - Actively steward peer channels: facilitation, not just creation\n    - Define explicit contribution pathways: adaptation review, mentoring, programme governance\n    - Design governance so sustained contribution creates recognised standing\n    - Test: does a motivated trainer have a visible route into co-ownership within the first year?"}], :conclusion "Engagement sustains when trainers become co-producers with recognised scope to shape the programme, not merely recipients or conduits for a central offer. Sustained contribution should create governance standing. Without explicit contribution pathways, delivery becomes goodwill-dependent and the programme benefits from trainer labour without granting voice over the conditions of that labour.  Every participant who sustains engagement beyond initial training has at least one visible route to contribute to programme development, governance, or community support. Sustained contribution creates recognised standing. The coordinator role is resourced and trained.", :directives {:audience "programme designers, community coordinators, funders, governance leads, futon coordination layer", :keywords ["co-producers" "contribution" "collective-choice" "governance-standing" "contribution-log" "steering-group" "mentoring" "ORCA" "Community-of-Practice" "activation" "cognitive-participation" "NPT" "Sen" "Ostrom" "architecture-tier" "high-coordination" "capability-return"], :references ["equity/ARGUMENT" "equity/contribution-pathways" "equity/contribution-pathways-as-published" "equity/shared-maintenance" "equity/visible-impact"], :style "pattern", :title "Contribution Pathways: Governance Follows Contribution", :tone "merged-canonical-paper-aligned"}, :id "equity/contribution-pathways-merged", :keywords ["co-producers" "contribution" "collective-choice" "governance-standing" "contribution-log" "steering-group" "mentoring" "ORCA" "Community-of-Practice" "activation" "cognitive-participation" "NPT" "Sen" "Ostrom" "architecture-tier" "high-coordination" "capability-return"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["equity/ARGUMENT" "equity/contribution-pathways" "equity/contribution-pathways-as-published" "equity/shared-maintenance" "equity/visible-impact"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/equity/contribution-pathways-merged.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Contribution Pathways: Governance Follows Contribution", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Engagement sustains when participants become co-producers with recognised scope to shape the programme — not just consumers of a centrally defined offer. Without contribution pathways, the agent's becoming is capped: they can develop local competence but not reshape the system that shapes them.  Every participant who sustains engagement beyond initial training has at least one visible route to contribute to programme development, governance, or community support. Sustained contribution creates recognised standing in the programme's governance structure."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "A programme needs trainers to sustain engagement beyond the initial training wave. In NPT terms this is Cognitive Participation (2.4 Activation): how procedures and actions that can sustain delivery are collectively defined. In Ostrom's terms this is the collective-choice problem: can affected participants modify, extend, and help govern the programme, or only consume it? In the whole-board-play analogy, Patterns 1-3 gave the agent the rules, the conditions to play, and a way for local learning to propagate backward. This pattern asks whether the agent can modify the rules themselves."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    Trainers have developed local competence through iterative delivery (Pattern 2) and their adaptations are flowing into the shared commons (Pattern 3). The system is learning from its own variation. But the trainers' relationship to the system is still one of consumption: they receive the programme, they adapt it locally, they upstream improvements — but they do not govern how the programme evolves. Their learning improves the materials but does not give them a voice in the programme's direction, priorities, or structure."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    There are no explicit pathways for contribution beyond delivery and materials upstream. No peer cohorts, no shared adaptation reviews, no mentoring structure. The Community of Practice exists on paper but most trainers are unaware of it. Peer channels have been created but go unused. The ORCA coordinator role — which should serve as the backbone of community infrastructure — is undertrained and under-supported. The agent's becoming is capped: they can develop as a deliverer but not as a co-governor. This is like an AlphaZero that can play and learn within the game but cannot modify the game's parameters, training regime, or architecture. It remains a creature of its original design, however skilled it becomes within those constraints. In human systems, this cap on becoming is felt as: \"At some point I potentially leave with that knowledge and it doesn't get used as much as it could have.\""} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "      - Capped becoming: agent develops local competence but cannot reshape the system\n      - Goodwill dependency: practice survives only as long as the individual persists\n      - Invisible community: CoP exists formally but trainers are unaware of it\n      - Dead channels: peer exchange channels created but unstewarded and unused\n      - Undertrained coordinators: ORCA role exists but \"reactive rather than helpful\"\n      - Knowledge exit: \"at some point I potentially leave with that knowledge and it doesn't get used\"\n      - Extraction: system benefits from trainer labour without granting governance voice"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    The programme builds community infrastructure from the start: peer exchange channels that are actually stewarded (not just created), mentoring structures, and a coordinator role that is properly resourced and trained. Visible routes to share, mentor, or adapt turn private motivation into repeated participation. Crucially, these are not just communication channels — they are governance instruments. Through them, the trainers who do the hardest lifting gain recognised scope to shape what the programme becomes. The shift from recipient to co-producer is not just a change in role but a change in the kind of agent the person is: from a local adapter operating within fixed constraints to a co-governor whose experience reshapes the constraints themselves."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Sen: Capability expands when participants can move from receipt to co-ownership and contribution. But the deepest capability expansion is not skill development — it is the transition from being shaped by a system to shaping it. This is the becoming that equity requires: not just the freedom to act within given conditions, but the freedom to revise the conditions under which action takes place.\n\n    Whole-board play: In the AlphaZero analogy, this is the transition from playing within a fixed architecture to modifying the architecture itself — changing the learning rate, the network structure, the reward signal. In human systems, this transition is what distinguishes a community of practice from a distribution list. A distribution list delivers content. A community of practice co-evolves the content, the delivery, and the governance of both. The trainers who sustain engagement are not just good players — they are people whose experience should reshape how the game is set up for future players.\n\n    Ostrom: This is the collective-choice arrangement: positions and recognised scope determine whether participants can organise and contribute. Community infrastructure that is created but not stewarded is indistinguishable from no infrastructure. Without contribution pathways, motivation remains individual and exits with the person. This is where the equity loop either closes or breaks: contribution that creates recognised stake, stake that gives governance rights, governance that improves the conditions for future contribution. If this joint breaks, the system is extractive — it benefits from the trainer's labour without giving them a voice.\n\n    Mechanic, in short:\n    Derived from NPT Cognitive Participation (2.4 Activation) via the UKRN T3 analysis.\n    Mapped to the equity loop at contribution→stake→governance: contribution without recognised scope is extraction.\n    Deepened via whole-board play: the transition from playing within fixed constraints to modifying the constraints is the transition from recipient to co-governor.\n    Ostrom: positions and collective-choice arrangements determine whether participants can organise and contribute.\n    Coordination requirement: High (infrastructure)."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Resource and train the coordinator role before expecting community to emerge\n    - Steward peer channels actively — facilitated, not just created\n    - Define explicit contribution pathways: adaptation review, mentoring, programme governance\n    - Design governance so that sustained contribution creates recognised standing\n    - Test: does a motivated trainer have a visible route into co-ownership within their first year?\n    - This requires governed infrastructure (high coordination) and should be resolved after conditions 1-3"}], :conclusion "Engagement sustains when participants become co-producers with recognised scope to shape the programme — not just consumers of a centrally defined offer. Without contribution pathways, the agent's becoming is capped: they can develop local competence but not reshape the system that shapes them.  Every participant who sustains engagement beyond initial training has at least one visible route to contribute to programme development, governance, or community support. Sustained contribution creates recognised standing in the programme's governance structure.", :directives {:audience "programme designers, community coordinators, futon coordination layer", :keywords ["co-ownership" "contribution" "governance" "community" "collective-choice" "NPT" "Ostrom" "agency" "becoming"], :references ["equity/ARGUMENT" "equity/shared-maintenance" "equity/visible-impact"], :style "pattern", :title "Recipients Without Voice Remain Recipients", :tone "analytic-foundational"}, :id "equity/contribution-pathways", :keywords ["co-ownership" "contribution" "governance" "community" "collective-choice" "NPT" "Ostrom" "agency" "becoming"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["equity/ARGUMENT" "equity/shared-maintenance" "equity/visible-impact"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/equity/contribution-pathways.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Recipients Without Voice Remain Recipients", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "The breadth of open-research topics exceeds what any single trainer can confidently cover.  The resolution is to let each trainer declare a delivery profile — the modules they will own — and for the programme to treat institutional coverage as a network property across its trainers rather than a requirement of each individual.  Each institution commits an explicit profile of its coverage aims and resourcing.  Each trainer has a declared delivery profile.  Each participating institution has a committed coverage profile with named resourcing.  The programme maintains a current view of coverage as a network property."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "This is NPT Collective Action — specifically 3.3 Skill Set Workability and 3.4 Contextual Integration.  It is an intervention-tier pattern with medium coordination, requiring two formal actors: the Training Project and the Institutional Lead (or other local representative).  Where weak coordination leaves the trainer absorbing the gap alone, the pattern produces absorbed input; where team-working is supported, trainers grow in competence and the pattern contributes to multiplied input."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    A programme trains intermediaries who deliver open-research topics spanning a wide territory (statistics, data management, software, publication practices, ethics, reproducibility, etc.).  Delivery depends on the trainer's capacity to cover the territory competently — but the territory is broader than any single trainer's primary expertise, and many trainers are professional staff delivering outside their primary discipline."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    The breadth produces a breadth-versus-depth tension compounded by impostor syndrome.  Trainers report feeling inadequately prepared (FG1.15, FG2A.13; FG3.13, FG2A.4).  Sessions attempt to cover too much and lose interaction depth (T1: \"give it more time than you expect\"; T2B.17: delivers only a fraction of material learned; T2A: \"I didn't feel prepared to talk about all the areas\"; T3: \"There's a little bit of impostor syndrome, because it's still new to me and no one's taught me it!\").  COM-B \"I am psychologically able\" M=4.85, high individually, but the breadth gap is structural: trainers silently absorb a coordination problem that extends beyond their remit."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "      - Breadth overload: trainers silently cover topics outside their primary expertise without team support\n      - Impostor-driven delivery: trainer reaches a session under-prepared because declaration was not possible\n      - Un-profiled institutions: institution participates without naming what it covers or what it resources\n      - Invisible coordination: coverage problem extends beyond the trainer's remit but is absorbed by the trainer alone\n      - Per-trainer coverage requirement: the network's coverage is treated as an expectation of each individual rather than a distributed property"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Each trainer declares a delivery profile — the modules they will own.  The programme treats institutional coverage as a network property across its trainers, not a requirement of each individual.  Each participating institution commits an explicit profile of its coverage aims and the resourcing (time, scope, team support) that matches.  Coverage becomes a visible distribution across the network rather than an invisible overload on each trainer."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    NPT Collective Action: 3.3 Skill Set Workability is the fit between what the work demands and what the trainer can reasonably do; 3.4 Contextual Integration is the fit between the work and its institutional context.  Breadth overload breaks both.  Named profiles restore the fit at both levels.\n\n    Two-actor coordination: The pattern cannot be resolved by the Training Project alone — the institution must name a coverage profile and resource it.  Coordination rises from Low (Pattern 1) to Medium.  The Institutional Lead (or Local Network Lead in the post-ORP setting) is the second formal actor.\n\n    Network-property framing: Treating coverage as a network property is a structural move, not an operational one.  It reframes what the programme is asking of any single trainer — not \"cover everything\" but \"own a declared subset, with coverage guaranteed across the network.\"  This is what prevents trainers from silently absorbing the coordination problem.\n\n    Mechanic, in short:\n    Derived from NPT Collective Action 3.3 Skill Set Workability and 3.4 Contextual Integration as stated in the companion journal article.\n    Tier: Intervention.  Coordination: Medium (programme + Institutional Lead).\n    Implementation-mode link: weak coordination → absorbed input; supported team-working → contributes to multiplied input.\n    The paper names a specific mechanism (delivery profile, institutional coverage as network property) that the canonical equity pattern renders more abstractly."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Ask each trainer to declare the modules they will own\n    - Ask each institution to commit an explicit coverage profile with resourcing\n    - Publish the aggregate coverage across the network so gaps are visible to the programme, not only to individual trainers\n    - Audit breadth-vs-depth in sessions: where a trainer is covering outside their declared profile, surface that to the Institutional Lead\n    - Test: can every trainer point to the modules they own and know which modules a colleague owns?"}], :conclusion "The breadth of open-research topics exceeds what any single trainer can confidently cover.  The resolution is to let each trainer declare a delivery profile — the modules they will own — and for the programme to treat institutional coverage as a network property across its trainers rather than a requirement of each individual.  Each institution commits an explicit profile of its coverage aims and resourcing.  Each trainer has a declared delivery profile.  Each participating institution has a committed coverage profile with named resourcing.  The programme maintains a current view of coverage as a network property.", :directives {:audience "programme designers, Institutional Leads, Training Project, UKRN-S proposers", :keywords ["delivery-profile" "institutional-coverage" "breadth-vs-depth" "impostor-syndrome" "skill-set-workability" "contextual-integration" "NPT" "intervention-tier" "medium-coordination"], :references ["equity/ARGUMENT" "equity/protected-interaction"], :style "pattern", :title "Protected Interaction (as published in paper v2.3)", :tone "paper-aligned"}, :id "equity/protected-interaction-as-published", :keywords ["delivery-profile" "institutional-coverage" "breadth-vs-depth" "impostor-syndrome" "skill-set-workability" "contextual-integration" "NPT" "intervention-tier" "medium-coordination"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["equity/ARGUMENT" "equity/protected-interaction"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/equity/protected-interaction-as-published.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Protected Interaction (as published in paper v2.3)", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Training delivery succeeds only when a trainer's declared delivery profile is matched by institutional provisioning. The breadth of open-research topics should be treated as a network coverage property, not as a demand on every individual. Without protected time, administrative backing, manageable scope, and team support, the trainer absorbs a structural coordination problem as personal overload.  Each trainer has a declared delivery profile. Each participating institution has a committed coverage profile with protected time, administrative backing, and manageable scope. The programme maintains a current view of coverage as a network property."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "A programme trains intermediaries who are expected to deliver locally across a broad open-research territory. Delivery depends on trainer competence, but also on institutional conditions: rooms, protected time, administrative support, clear role mandate, and manageable scope. In NPT terms this combines Collective Action 3.3 Skill Set Workability and 3.4 Contextual Integration. In Ostrom terms this is the provisioning problem: resources must be supplied rather than assumed. In the paper's tier language this is an intervention-tier, medium-coordination pattern requiring at least the programme and an Institutional Lead or local representative."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    A trained intermediary has internalised the programme scaffold and is ready to develop local competence through delivery. They need to deliver, iterate, fail, adjust, and develop practical judgement in their setting. The topic landscape is wider than any single trainer's primary expertise, and many trainers are professional staff delivering outside their main discipline."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    The institution provides no protected conditions for iteration, and the programme has no declared delivery profiles. Sessions try to cover everything, crowding out interaction and depth. Trainers report inadequate preparation and impostor syndrome (FG1.15, FG2A.13; FG3.13, FG2A.4; T2A: \"I didn't feel prepared to talk about all the areas\"; T3: \"There's a little bit of impostor syndrome\"). They absorb four to six roles that the programme design distributes across actors: trainer, materials adapter, marketer, evaluator, local coordinator. The breadth gap is structural even when individual psychological ability is high (COM-B \"I am psychologically able\" M=4.85)."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "      - Breadth overload: trainers cover topics outside their expertise without support\n      - Impostor-driven delivery: trainer reaches the room under-prepared because profile declaration was impossible\n      - No iteration: trainer delivers once and never develops local competence\n      - Role overload: trainer absorbs roles designed for distinct actors\n      - Un-profiled institutions: institution participates without naming coverage aims or resourcing\n      - Per-trainer coverage requirement: network coverage is treated as an individual requirement\n      - Goodwill-dependent delivery: practice survives only while the individual persists"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Each trainer declares a delivery profile: the modules they will own and the limits of that ownership. Each participating institution commits an explicit coverage profile with named resourcing: protected time, rooms, IT/admin support, team coverage, and a manageable delivery scope. The programme publishes aggregate coverage across the network so gaps are visible to the programme, not only to individual trainers. Coverage becomes a distributed property of the network rather than an invisible expectation placed on each person."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    NPT: Skill Set Workability is the fit between work demands and what the trainer can reasonably do; Contextual Integration is the fit between the work and the institution's resources. Breadth overload breaks both.\n\n    Sen: The conversion at stake is temporal and organisational. Informational clarity from Pattern 1 does not become competence unless the trainer has the time and conditions to iterate.\n\n    Whole-board play: AlphaZero could not learn by reading about Go; it needed games. The trainer has far fewer games, so each delivery event must be protected enough to be formative. Rushed, overpacked, undersupported, or one-off sessions do not let local competence develop.\n\n    Ostrom: The action situation works only when the relevant resources are provisioned. Where the institutional resource is absent, the individual fills a structural gap and practice survives only as long as they do.\n\n    Mechanic, in short:\n    Derived from NPT Collective Action 3.3 Skill Set Workability and 3.4 Contextual Integration.\n    Tier: Intervention. Coordination: Medium (programme + institutional representative).\n    Implementation-mode link: weak coordination produces absorbed input; supported team-working contributes to multiplied input.\n    Canonical deepening: protected iteration as the condition for competence development.\n    Paper-specific mechanism: declared delivery profiles and institutional coverage as a network property."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Ask each trainer to declare the modules they will own\n    - Ask each institution to commit a coverage profile with named resourcing\n    - Define the minimum support package: time, space, admin, team support, scope\n    - Publish aggregate coverage across the network so gaps are visible\n    - Audit breadth-vs-depth where trainers cover outside their declared profile\n    - Test: can every trainer deliver without absorbing roles that should be distributed?"}], :conclusion "Training delivery succeeds only when a trainer's declared delivery profile is matched by institutional provisioning. The breadth of open-research topics should be treated as a network coverage property, not as a demand on every individual. Without protected time, administrative backing, manageable scope, and team support, the trainer absorbs a structural coordination problem as personal overload.  Each trainer has a declared delivery profile. Each participating institution has a committed coverage profile with protected time, administrative backing, and manageable scope. The programme maintains a current view of coverage as a network property.", :directives {:audience "programme designers, institutional leads, Training Project, UKRN-S proposers, futon coordination layer", :keywords ["delivery-profile" "institutional-coverage" "protected-time" "administrative-support" "breadth-vs-depth" "impostor-syndrome" "iteration" "skill-set-workability" "contextual-integration" "NPT" "Sen" "Ostrom" "provisioning" "intervention-tier" "medium-coordination"], :references ["equity/ARGUMENT" "equity/protected-interaction" "equity/protected-interaction-as-published" "equity/confident-adaptation" "equity/shared-maintenance"], :style "pattern", :title "Protected Interaction: Profiled Provisioning", :tone "merged-canonical-paper-aligned"}, :id "equity/protected-interaction-merged", :keywords ["delivery-profile" "institutional-coverage" "protected-time" "administrative-support" "breadth-vs-depth" "impostor-syndrome" "iteration" "skill-set-workability" "contextual-integration" "NPT" "Sen" "Ostrom" "provisioning" "intervention-tier" "medium-coordination"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["equity/ARGUMENT" "equity/protected-interaction" "equity/protected-interaction-as-published" "equity/confident-adaptation" "equity/shared-maintenance"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/equity/protected-interaction-merged.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Protected Interaction: Profiled Provisioning", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Training delivery succeeds only when the institution provides protected conditions — time, space, administrative support — not just a trained person. Without provisioning, the agent cannot iterate, and without iteration, no local competence structure can develop.  No institution participates in the programme without providing named delivery support that includes protected time, administrative backing, and a manageable delivery scope. The support is designed to enable iterative development, not just one-off delivery."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "A programme trains intermediaries who are then expected to deliver locally. Delivery depends not only on the trainer's competence but on institutional conditions: rooms, protected time, administrative support, manageable scope. In NPT terms this is Collective Action (3.3 Skill Set Workability, 3.4 Contextual Integration). In Ostrom's terms this is the local provisioning problem: the action situation works only if resources are supplied rather than assumed. In the whole-board-play analogy, Pattern 1 gave the agent the rules of the game; this pattern asks whether they can actually play."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    A trained intermediary has internalised the programme scaffold (Pattern 1) and is ready to develop local competence through delivery. Like AlphaZero after learning the rules, the agent now needs to play — to deliver, iterate, fail, adjust, and develop the local perceptual structure that constitutes practical judgment in their specific setting. Each delivery is a game: a structured interaction from which the agent learns."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    The institution provides no protected conditions for iteration. The trainer has no dedicated time, no administrative support, and no clear role mandate. Sessions try to cover everything, crowding out the interaction that makes them formative. The trainer absorbs four to six roles (trainer, materials adapter, marketer, evaluator, local coordinator) that the programme design distributes across distinct actors. Without protected conditions, there are no games to learn from — the trainer reads the materials but never develops the internal structure that comes from doing. Impostor syndrome and overload are symptoms of an agent who has the rules but cannot play."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "      - No iteration: trainer delivers once and never develops local competence\n      - Role overload: trainer absorbs 4-6 roles designed for distinct actors\n      - Impostor syndrome: professional staff delivering outside primary expertise without support or repetition\n      - Breadth pressure: sessions cover everything, interaction disappears, no learning from the encounter\n      - Goodwill-dependent delivery: practice survives only as long as the individual persists"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Participating institutions provide named delivery support: protected time in workload models, rooms, IT and administrative backing, and a manageable subset of topics. The programme requires institutional co-investment as a condition of participation, not as an optional extra. The point is not just to enable one delivery event but to create the conditions for iterative development: each session builds on the last, each local adaptation deepens practical judgment, each interaction with participants generates the local filters that no central specification could have provided."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Sen: The conversion at stake is temporal and organisational. The agent has informational clarity (Pattern 1) but cannot convert it into competence without the time and conditions to iterate. Capability is not a state to be transmitted but a structure that develops through protected interaction with the environment. Without iteration, the nominal capability granted by training attendance remains nominal.\n\n    Whole-board play: AlphaZero could not learn Go by reading about Go. It needed to play — millions of games, each one refining internal structure through feedback. The trainer's situation is analogous but with a crucial difference: the trainer gets far fewer games. Each delivery session is a precious iteration. If sessions are rushed, overpacked, undersupported, or unrepeatable, the trainer cannot develop local competence any more than AlphaZero could learn from three games with a broken clock.\n\n    Ostrom: Protected delivery is the first pattern that requires two actors (programme + institution) to coordinate. The programme provides the scaffold and materials; the institution provides the conditions for iteration. Where both act, delivery succeeds and competence develops. Where the institutional resource is absent — \"the amount of rooms we can book is a big issue\"; \"I'm more or less a single person\" — the trainer absorbs the gap alone. The individual fills a structural gap, and the practice survives only as long as they do. This is the provisioning problem: resources must be actively supplied, not assumed to exist.\n\n    Mechanic, in short:\n    Derived from NPT Collective Action (3.3, 3.4) via the UKRN T3 analysis.\n    Mapped to the equity loop at governance→capability: governance that does not provision capability is performative.\n    Deepened via whole-board play: competence develops through iteration, not transmission. Without protected conditions for iteration, the agent has the rules but cannot play.\n    Ostrom: local resource provision structures the immediate action situation for delivery.\n    Coordination requirement: Medium (2 actors)."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Require institutional co-investment as a condition of participation\n    - Define the minimum institutional support package (time, space, admin, scope)\n    - Design for iteration, not one-off delivery: the first session is the beginning of learning, not the end\n    - Test: can the trainer deliver without personally absorbing roles that should be distributed?\n    - This requires 2 actors (programme + institution) and should be resolved after condition 1"}], :conclusion "Training delivery succeeds only when the institution provides protected conditions — time, space, administrative support — not just a trained person. Without provisioning, the agent cannot iterate, and without iteration, no local competence structure can develop.  No institution participates in the programme without providing named delivery support that includes protected time, administrative backing, and a manageable delivery scope. The support is designed to enable iterative development, not just one-off delivery.", :directives {:audience "programme designers, institutional leads, futon coordination layer", :keywords ["provisioning" "interaction" "workload" "institutional support" "collective action" "NPT" "Ostrom" "learning" "iteration"], :references ["equity/ARGUMENT" "equity/confident-adaptation" "equity/shared-maintenance"], :style "pattern", :title "Delivery Without Provisioning Produces Overload", :tone "analytic-foundational"}, :id "equity/protected-interaction", :keywords ["provisioning" "interaction" "workload" "institutional support" "collective action" "NPT" "Ostrom" "learning" "iteration"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["equity/ARGUMENT" "equity/confident-adaptation" "equity/shared-maintenance"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/equity/protected-interaction.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Delivery Without Provisioning Produces Overload", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Trainers praise the materials as a gold standard but content ages, requires local adaptation, and cannot easily be upstreamed.  The result is ad hoc rework as invisible labour.  The missing norm is a coordination rule: every adaptation used more than once is shared, with the adapter credited as author, and a light-touch review cycle makes distributed stewardship the norm rather than the exception.  Local adaptations can flow back into the shared materials through a defined light-touch review cycle.  Adapters are credited as authors.  The upstream pathway is no harder than the downstream consumption pathway."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "This is NPT Collective Action — specifically 3.2 Relational Integration and 3.4 Contextual Integration.  It is an intervention-tier pattern with medium coordination, but the coordination takes the form of a community norm rather than an organisational decision — a developing knowledge commons that requires distributed stewardship across a contributor community.  Where materials are reworked locally without upstreaming, the pattern's absence is the defining signature of absorbed input mode."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    A programme distributes high-quality training materials that trainers use and praise.  Trainers encounter materials that age, that need adaptation to local settings, and that could be improved by the rework each trainer does privately.  Contributing back to the shared resource would improve it for future trainers and reduce aggregate maintenance work."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    No upstream channel exists.  Each trainer reworks materials independently; adaptations stay local; the \"gold standard\" reputation erodes as content drifts from practice.  Maintenance falls to whoever notices a problem — ad hoc, unrecognised, on top of already constrained time.  Workload and capacity concerns are the most prevalent theme in the corpus (34 of 134 turns, 25%).  COM-B \"policies, procedures, and resource allocation are appropriate\" M=3.78 (below midpoint — the only pattern-relevant COM-B item to score below 4).  Trainers: T1 calls the Carpentries lessons a \"gold standard\"; T1: \"every time I've done it, I haven't given enough time\"; FG1.9 praises the materials.  Open licensing was planned but the stewardship infrastructure was not built."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "      - No upstream channel: local learning never propagates; the commons degrades\n      - Licensing without stewardship: open licence exists but no one curates or reviews\n      - Invisible rework: maintenance is unrecognised work that compounds overload\n      - Ad hoc sharing: \"a couple of colleagues have uploaded their slides\" — unsystematic\n      - Drifting gold standard: reputation maintained while practice diverges"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    The programme establishes a coordination rule: every adaptation used more than once is shared, with the adapter credited as author.  A light-touch review cycle makes distributed stewardship the community norm.  The contribution pathway is no harder to use than the consumption pathway.  This is the commons governance move — Ostrom's \"rules matched to local conditions\" applied to a developing knowledge commons."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    NPT Collective Action: 3.2 Relational Integration is the work of maintaining trust and cooperation between actors whose efforts the practice depends on; 3.4 Contextual Integration is the fit between the practice and its institutional-organisational context.  Invisible rework breaks both — the trainer carries unreciprocated maintenance labour, and the materials drift from the practice they were meant to support.\n\n    Community-norm framing: Unlike Patterns 1 and 2, this pattern cannot be resolved by an organisational decision.  A central scaffold or a declared profile can be issued top-down; a stewardship norm cannot.  Coordination takes the form of a community rule of shared authorship, which the programme can seed but the community must sustain.  This is what the paper means by \"distributed stewardship.\"\n\n    Commons framing (Ostrom 1990): A knowledge commons requires rules matched to local conditions.  \"Every adaptation used more than once is shared, credited as author\" is one such rule — lightweight, low-cost, and aligned with how trainers already create value.\n\n    Mechanic, in short:\n    Derived from NPT Collective Action 3.2 Relational Integration and 3.4 Contextual Integration as stated in the companion journal article.\n    Tier: Intervention.  Coordination: Medium, but community-norm rather than organisational-decision.\n    Implementation-mode link: unshared rework is the defining signature of absorbed input.\n    The paper names a specific coordination rule (every adaptation used more than once is shared, credited as author) and cites Ostrom 1990 directly."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Establish open licensing with an active upstream channel (not licensing alone)\n    - Adopt the coordination rule: every adaptation used more than once is shared, adapter credited as author\n    - Implement a light-touch review cycle with named reviewers\n    - Make contributing as frictionless as consuming (the friction gradient should favour upstream flow)\n    - Surface the aggregate labour: count contributions, count reviews, show the commons at work\n    - Test: can a local adaptation reach the shared repository within one cycle?"}], :conclusion "Trainers praise the materials as a gold standard but content ages, requires local adaptation, and cannot easily be upstreamed.  The result is ad hoc rework as invisible labour.  The missing norm is a coordination rule: every adaptation used more than once is shared, with the adapter credited as author, and a light-touch review cycle makes distributed stewardship the norm rather than the exception.  Local adaptations can flow back into the shared materials through a defined light-touch review cycle.  Adapters are credited as authors.  The upstream pathway is no harder than the downstream consumption pathway.", :directives {:audience "programme designers, community coordinators, materials stewards", :keywords ["commons-governance" "distributed-stewardship" "upstream-channel" "light-touch-review" "invisible-labour" "gold-standard" "relational-integration" "contextual-integration" "NPT" "intervention-tier" "medium-coordination"], :references ["equity/ARGUMENT" "equity/shared-maintenance"], :style "pattern", :title "Shared Maintenance (as published in paper v2.3)", :tone "paper-aligned"}, :id "equity/shared-maintenance-as-published", :keywords ["commons-governance" "distributed-stewardship" "upstream-channel" "light-touch-review" "invisible-labour" "gold-standard" "relational-integration" "contextual-integration" "NPT" "intervention-tier" "medium-coordination"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["equity/ARGUMENT" "equity/shared-maintenance"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/equity/shared-maintenance-as-published.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Shared Maintenance (as published in paper v2.3)", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Materials stay current and trusted only when local adaptation flows back into a stewarded commons. Open licensing is not enough. The programme needs an upstream channel, author credit for adapters, and a light-touch review rule - every adaptation used more than once is shared - so local learning becomes shared infrastructure rather than invisible private labour.  Local adaptations can flow back into the shared materials through a defined light-touch review cycle. Adapters are credited as authors. The upstream pathway is no harder than the downstream consumption pathway, and the materials commons has active stewardship beyond the original author."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "A programme provides high-quality training materials that trainers praise, adapt, and maintain informally. Materials age, local settings differ, and each adaptation is a learning event. In NPT terms this is Collective Action 3.2 Relational Integration and 3.4 Contextual Integration: maintaining connections between materials, trainers, and programme. In Ostrom terms this is a knowledge-commons stewardship problem. In whole-board-play terms, Patterns 1 and 2 gave the agent the rules and conditions to play; this pattern asks whether what is learned from play can update the shared structure."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    Trainers are developing local competence through iterative delivery. Each local adaptation discovers something about what works in a setting, what fails, and what should be added, removed, or resequenced. If those adaptations can flow back, future trainers inherit a better resource and aggregate maintenance work falls."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    No upstream channel exists. Trainers rework materials independently; adaptations stay local; the \"gold standard\" reputation erodes as content drifts from practice. Maintenance falls to whoever notices a problem: ad hoc, unrecognised work on top of constrained time. Open licensing was planned but stewardship infrastructure was not built. Workload and capacity concerns are the most prevalent corpus theme (34 of 134 turns, 25%), and the COM-B item \"policies, procedures, and resource allocation are appropriate\" is below midpoint (M=3.78). This is like a learning system that plays games but never updates its weights."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "      - No backward propagation: local learning stays local and shared weights never update\n      - No upstream channel: local adaptations cannot reach the commons\n      - Licensing without stewardship: open licence exists but no one curates or reviews\n      - Invisible rework: maintenance is unrecognised work that compounds overload\n      - Ad hoc sharing: slides appear informally without a reliable curation path\n      - Drifting gold standard: reputation persists while practice diverges\n      - Content drift: materials age without systematic review"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    The programme establishes open licensing plus an active upstream channel, a light-touch review cycle, and a shared rule: every adaptation used more than once is shared, with the adapter credited as author. Contributing is made no harder than consuming. Local groups catalogue assets, a community of practice shares adaptations, and named reviewers steward the syllabus. The shift is from individual burden to community infrastructure."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    NPT: Relational Integration depends on trust in the people and materials the practice uses; Contextual Integration depends on fit with the institutional context. Invisible rework breaks both.\n\n    Sen: Usable materials sustain capability beyond the initial event, but capability develops through use. If adaptations cannot flow back, the capability gain remains private and the commons degrades.\n\n    Whole-board play: Backward propagation from local play into shared structure is what distinguishes a system that learns from a system that merely runs. The training programme's \"weights\" are its materials, syllabus, and practical knowledge.\n\n    Ostrom: A knowledge commons requires rules matched to local conditions. \"Every adaptation used more than once is shared, credited as author\" is lightweight, low-cost, and aligned with the value trainers already create.\n\n    Mechanic, in short:\n    Derived from NPT Collective Action 3.2 Relational Integration and 3.4 Contextual Integration.\n    Tier: Intervention. Coordination: Medium, expressed as community norm rather than a single organisational decision.\n    Implementation-mode link: unshared rework is a defining signature of absorbed input.\n    Canonical deepening: backward propagation and co-evolution of resource and users.\n    Paper-specific mechanism: light-touch review, author credit, and the \"adaptation used more than once\" rule."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Establish open licensing with an active upstream pathway\n    - Adopt the rule: every adaptation used more than once is shared\n    - Credit adapters as authors so invisible labour becomes visible contribution\n    - Implement a light-touch review cycle with named reviewers\n    - Make contributing as frictionless as consuming\n    - Surface aggregate labour: count contributions, reviews, and accepted changes\n    - Test: can a local adaptation reach the shared repository within one cycle?"}], :conclusion "Materials stay current and trusted only when local adaptation flows back into a stewarded commons. Open licensing is not enough. The programme needs an upstream channel, author credit for adapters, and a light-touch review rule - every adaptation used more than once is shared - so local learning becomes shared infrastructure rather than invisible private labour.  Local adaptations can flow back into the shared materials through a defined light-touch review cycle. Adapters are credited as authors. The upstream pathway is no harder than the downstream consumption pathway, and the materials commons has active stewardship beyond the original author.", :directives {:audience "programme designers, community coordinators, materials stewards, futon coordination layer", :keywords ["commons-governance" "distributed-stewardship" "upstream-channel" "light-touch-review" "author-credit" "invisible-labour" "gold-standard" "relational-integration" "contextual-integration" "NPT" "Sen" "Ostrom" "backward-propagation" "intervention-tier" "medium-coordination"], :references ["equity/ARGUMENT" "equity/shared-maintenance" "equity/shared-maintenance-as-published" "equity/protected-interaction" "equity/contribution-pathways"], :style "pattern", :title "Shared Maintenance: Stewarded Commons", :tone "merged-canonical-paper-aligned"}, :id "equity/shared-maintenance-merged", :keywords ["commons-governance" "distributed-stewardship" "upstream-channel" "light-touch-review" "author-credit" "invisible-labour" "gold-standard" "relational-integration" "contextual-integration" "NPT" "Sen" "Ostrom" "backward-propagation" "intervention-tier" "medium-coordination"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["equity/ARGUMENT" "equity/shared-maintenance" "equity/shared-maintenance-as-published" "equity/protected-interaction" "equity/contribution-pathways"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/equity/shared-maintenance-merged.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Shared Maintenance: Stewarded Commons", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Materials stay current and trusted only when stewardship is distributed across a contributor community with open licensing, a shared repository, and a light-touch curation process. Without stewardship, local learning fragments and the whole system cannot learn from its own variation.  Local adaptations can flow back into the shared resource through a defined curation process. The materials commons has at least one active steward beyond the original author. The upstream pathway is no harder to use than the downstream consumption pathway."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "A programme provides high-quality materials that are praised by trainers but that age, require local adaptation, and cannot easily be upstreamed. In NPT terms this is Collective Action (3.2 Relational Integration, 3.4 Contextual Integration): the work of maintaining connections between the materials, the trainers, and the programme. In Ostrom's terms this is knowledge-commons stewardship. In the whole-board-play analogy, Patterns 1-2 gave the agent the rules and the conditions to play; this pattern asks whether what the agent learns from playing can flow back into the system that shapes future play."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    Trainers are developing local competence through iterative delivery (Pattern 2). Each local adaptation is a learning event: the trainer discovers what works in their setting, what doesn't, what needs to be added or removed. These local adaptations are the system's equivalent of the internal representations that develop during whole-board play — local structure that responds to local conditions, discovered through interaction rather than specified centrally."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    There is no mechanism to upstream local improvements. Each trainer reworks materials independently. Adaptations stay local — the system's local learning never propagates. The \"gold standard\" reputation erodes as content drifts from practice. Maintenance falls to whoever happens to notice a problem — invisible labour on top of already constrained time. Open licensing was planned but the stewardship infrastructure was not built. This is like AlphaZero playing millions of games but never updating its weights: the games happen, local information is generated, but nothing flows backward into the shared structure."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "      - No backward propagation: local learning stays local, the system's weights never update\n      - Content drift: materials diverge from practice without systematic review\n      - Invisible labour: maintenance is unrecognised work that compounds trainer overload\n      - Ad hoc sharing: \"a couple of colleagues have uploaded their slides\" — unsystematic, fragile\n      - Licensing without stewardship: open licence exists but no one curates, reviews, or upstreams"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    The programme establishes open licensing and a shared repository with a light-touch curation process. Contributing an adaptation is made as easy as consuming a resource. Local groups catalogue assets, a community of practice shares adaptations, and training partners review and update the syllabus. The shift is from individual burden to community infrastructure. Crucially, the upstream pathway is not just for materials — it is the mechanism through which the programme learns from its own local variation. Each adaptation that reaches the shared repository refines the commons, making it a better conversion factor for future trainers."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Sen: Usable materials are part of the ongoing conditions that sustain capability beyond the initial event. But capability is not static — it develops through use. When a trainer adapts materials, they are developing both their own competence and a local variant that may be better suited to a class of settings. If that variant cannot flow back, the capability gain is private and the commons degrades.\n\n    Whole-board play: The backward propagation of local learning into shared structure is what makes the difference between a system that learns and a system that merely runs. AlphaZero's power comes not from any single game but from the fact that what is learned in each game updates the weights that shape future games. In the training programme, the \"weights\" are the shared materials, the syllabus, the accumulated practical knowledge of what works. Without an upstream pathway, local games are played but the system's weights never update. The resource and its users must develop together — that is the co-evolutionary logic.\n\n    Ostrom: This is knowledge-commons stewardship: maintenance, adaptation, and reuse depend on shared rules and contributor roles. Open licensing enables sharing; it does not produce stewardship. The curation process and contributor roles must be designed alongside the repository. The shift from individual burden to community infrastructure marks the transition toward architecture-level coordination.\n\n    Mechanic, in short:\n    Derived from NPT Collective Action (3.2, 3.4) via the UKRN T3 analysis.\n    Mapped to the equity loop at capability→contribution: local capability that fragments instead of feeding back.\n    Deepened via whole-board play: the backward propagation of local learning into shared structure is what makes a system that learns rather than a system that merely runs.\n    Ostrom: knowledge-commons stewardship requires shared rules and contributor roles.\n    Coordination requirement: Medium-high (community)."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Establish open licensing with an explicit upstream pathway\n    - Define the curation process: who reviews, who accepts, what the cadence is\n    - Make contributing as easy as consuming — the friction gradient should favour upstream flow\n    - Test: can a local adaptation reach the shared repository within one cycle?\n    - This requires a contributor community (medium-high coordination) and should be resolved after conditions 1-2"}], :conclusion "Materials stay current and trusted only when stewardship is distributed across a contributor community with open licensing, a shared repository, and a light-touch curation process. Without stewardship, local learning fragments and the whole system cannot learn from its own variation.  Local adaptations can flow back into the shared resource through a defined curation process. The materials commons has at least one active steward beyond the original author. The upstream pathway is no harder to use than the downstream consumption pathway.", :directives {:audience "programme designers, community coordinators, futon coordination layer", :keywords ["stewardship" "materials" "commons" "maintenance" "licensing" "curation" "NPT" "Ostrom" "co-evolution" "learning"], :references ["equity/ARGUMENT" "equity/protected-interaction" "equity/contribution-pathways"], :style "pattern", :title "Local Adaptation Without Stewardship Fragments the Commons", :tone "analytic-foundational"}, :id "equity/shared-maintenance", :keywords ["stewardship" "materials" "commons" "maintenance" "licensing" "curation" "NPT" "Ostrom" "co-evolution" "learning"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["equity/ARGUMENT" "equity/protected-interaction" "equity/contribution-pathways"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/equity/shared-maintenance.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Local Adaptation Without Stewardship Fragments the Commons", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "The programme needs evidence of downstream impact to improve, sustain trainer motivation, and justify institutional support, but current feedback mechanisms are thin.  The resolution is to distribute monitoring across the actors it serves — cross-institutional peer evaluation where trainers interview each other's recipients using a lightweight instrument.  This is Ostrom's monitoring problem: the current evaluation chain has too many links for the existing governance structure to sustain, so the governance structure itself must adapt via a reciprocal network.  The programme maintains a complete evaluation chain from instrument design through cross-institutional analysis to feedback, with a named actor at each link and explicit resourcing for the cross-institutional analysis step.  Feedback propagates to trainers and institutional leads.  A cross-institutional peer evaluation mechanism is active."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "This is NPT Reflexive Monitoring — specifically 4.2 Communal Appraisal: shared mechanisms for evaluating what is working and what is not.  It is an architecture-tier pattern with very high coordination — a multi-actor evaluation chain, the highest coordination requirement under the current design.  The paper notes this is the *only* pattern where quantitative COM-B data and qualitative transcript analysis produce a convergent signal independently arriving at the same conclusion.  Its failure is the central impediment to multiplied input at network scale; only fully resolved, it converts absorbed/mismatch dynamics into programme-level learning."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    A distributed training programme has resolved (or is resolving) Patterns 1-4: trainers have expectations, provisioning, stewarded materials, and pathways into governance.  Local and community-level learning operate.  But the programme cannot see its own aggregate effects across institutions, cohorts, and time.  Its funders, its institutional leads, and its trainers all need evidence that the effort produces downstream impact — evidence the current feedback mechanisms cannot produce."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Feedback mechanisms are thin.  Trainers rely on optional post-session surveys that yield only positive responses or none at all (T2B: \"I have no other ways of getting feedback short of people filling in the feedback form\"; T2B: \"As we get like a few answers and nobody says this was terrible, I generally think it's gone quite well\").  Informal signals from colleagues are all that is available (FG2B.41, FG2B.36, FG2B.39).  COM-B: \"effects are being measured\" M=3.35 — the lowest item across all survey waves, dropping to M=3.17 at 6-month follow-up.  Cross-institutional analysis is absent.  An evaluation workstream was funded but the evaluation chain — from instrument design through data collection to cross-institutional analysis to feedback back to trainers — was not built.  This is the monitoring chain with too many links for the current governance structure to sustain."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "      - Broken chain: instruments exist but no one analyses across institutions\n      - Upward-only feedback: results reach funders but not trainers or institutional leads\n      - Thin feedback: optional surveys yield only positive or absent responses\n      - Workstream without chain: evaluation is funded on paper but the handoff chain is not built\n      - Monitoring-chain overload: the chain has more links than the current governance can carry; the pattern fails because the structure was never adapted\n      - Motivation erosion: trainers cannot see whether their effort matters beyond anecdote\n      - Absent cross-institutional analysis: 4/4 ORP transcripts show this missing"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    The programme develops and deploys cross-institutional peer evaluation: trainers interview each other's recipients using a lightweight instrument.  Monitoring is distributed across the actors it serves — a reciprocal network.  The evaluation chain is designed end-to-end before the next delivery event, with named roles at each link (instrument, collection, cross-institutional analysis, feedback).  Feedback propagates back to trainers and institutional leads, not only upward to funders.  The governance structure adapts to carry a monitoring chain the current structure cannot."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    NPT Reflexive Monitoring: 4.2 Communal Appraisal is the shared mechanism for judging whether a practice is working.  Without it, improvements are private and the programme cannot learn at the level that matters — aggregate effect across institutions.  The evaluation workstream is not an evaluation chain; building the workstream without building the chain is funded ambition without architecture.\n\n    Ostrom's monitoring problem: Monitoring and information flows across action arenas determine whether the programme can learn and adjust.  When the chain has more links than the current governance can span, two options exist — shorten the chain (reduce scope) or restructure the governance (add actors and redistribute responsibilities).  Cross-institutional peer evaluation is a restructure: it distributes monitoring across the trainers themselves, converting them from producers of impression-based signals into a reciprocal evaluation network.\n\n    Very-high coordination: This is the highest-coordination pattern (6+ actor chain).  Resolving it requires trainers at multiple institutions + a lightweight instrument + consent and data-sharing governance + an analyst who can see across + a feedback loop back to trainers and leads.  The paper is explicit that this is the hardest of the five.\n\n    Convergent signal: This is the only pattern where COM-B quantitative data and qualitative transcript analysis produce the same conclusion independently — giving the claim more evidential weight than the other four.  The design move (lightweight cross-institutional instrument) is correspondingly specific.\n\n    Learning-loop completion: Patterns 3 and 4 propagate local learning into materials and governance respectively.  Pattern 5 is the completion of the loop — aggregate outcomes propagating back into design, so the programme can judge whether the preceding four patterns are actually producing what they are meant to produce.  Without Pattern 5, the evidence that the other four are working stays anecdotal.\n\n    Mechanic, in short:\n    Derived from NPT Reflexive Monitoring 4.2 Communal Appraisal as stated in the companion journal article.\n    Tier: Architecture.  Coordination: Very high (multi-actor evaluation chain, the highest of the five).\n    Implementation-mode link: this pattern's failure bounds multiplied input at network scale; its resolution closes the learning loop.\n    The paper is explicit that this is the *only* pattern with a convergent COM-B + qualitative signal, and is the most consistently absent under current governance.\n    The paper's resolution (cross-institutional peer evaluation via lightweight instrument) corresponds exactly to the working paper's Step 3 in the bootstrap sequence."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Design the evaluation chain end-to-end before the next delivery cycle, not as a retrofit\n    - Assign named roles at each link: instrument designer, collector, cross-institutional analyst, feedback reporter\n    - Develop and deploy a lightweight cross-institutional peer evaluation instrument (trainers interview each other's recipients)\n    - Establish the consent and data-sharing governance the peer-evaluation design requires\n    - Resource cross-institutional analysis explicitly — the central link, and the most absent\n    - Design feedback to propagate back to trainers and institutional leads, not only upward to funders\n    - Test: can the programme produce one cross-institutional learning report within its first year?"}], :conclusion "The programme needs evidence of downstream impact to improve, sustain trainer motivation, and justify institutional support, but current feedback mechanisms are thin.  The resolution is to distribute monitoring across the actors it serves — cross-institutional peer evaluation where trainers interview each other's recipients using a lightweight instrument.  This is Ostrom's monitoring problem: the current evaluation chain has too many links for the existing governance structure to sustain, so the governance structure itself must adapt via a reciprocal network.  The programme maintains a complete evaluation chain from instrument design through cross-institutional analysis to feedback, with a named actor at each link and explicit resourcing for the cross-institutional analysis step.  Feedback propagates to trainers and institutional leads.  A cross-institutional peer evaluation mechanism is active.", :directives {:audience "programme designers, evaluators, funders, UKRN-S proposers", :keywords ["evaluation-chain" "monitoring-chain" "cross-institutional-peer-evaluation" "reciprocal-network" "communal-appraisal" "reflexive-monitoring" "convergent-signal" "NPT" "architecture-tier" "very-high-coordination"], :references ["equity/ARGUMENT" "equity/visible-impact"], :style "pattern", :title "Visible Impact (as published in paper v2.3)", :tone "paper-aligned"}, :id "equity/visible-impact-as-published", :keywords ["evaluation-chain" "monitoring-chain" "cross-institutional-peer-evaluation" "reciprocal-network" "communal-appraisal" "reflexive-monitoring" "convergent-signal" "NPT" "architecture-tier" "very-high-coordination"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["equity/ARGUMENT" "equity/visible-impact"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/equity/visible-impact-as-published.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Visible Impact (as published in paper v2.3)", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "A programme that cannot see its downstream effects cannot learn, sustain motivation, or justify institutional support. The evaluation chain must run from instrument design through data collection, cross-institutional analysis, and feedback back to trainers and institutional leads. Where the current governance cannot carry that chain, the governance must adapt - for example through cross-institutional peer evaluation with a lightweight instrument.  The programme maintains a complete evaluation chain from instrument design through cross-institutional analysis to feedback, with a named actor at each link and explicit resourcing for cross-institutional analysis. Feedback propagates to trainers and institutional leads. A cross-institutional peer-evaluation mechanism is active when governance permits it."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "A distributed programme needs evidence of downstream impact to improve its own design, sustain trainer motivation, and justify continued institutional support. In NPT terms this is Reflexive Monitoring 4.2 Communal Appraisal: shared mechanisms for evaluating what is working and what is not. In Ostrom terms this is the monitoring problem across action arenas. In whole-board-play terms, Patterns 1-4 gave the agent rules, provisioned play, material stewardship, and a route into governance; this pattern asks whether aggregate outcomes propagate back into programme architecture. In the paper's tier language this is an architecture-tier, very-high-coordination pattern: a multi-actor evaluation chain, the highest coordination requirement in the set."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    A distributed training programme has resolved or is resolving the preceding patterns. Trainers have expectations, provisioning, stewarded materials, and pathways into governance. Local and community-level learning operate. But the programme still needs evidence across institutions, cohorts, and time. Funders, institutional leads, trainers, and the programme itself need to know whether the effort produces downstream impact."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Feedback mechanisms are thin. Trainers rely on optional post-session surveys that yield only positive responses or none at all (T2B: \"I have no other ways of getting feedback short of people filling in the feedback form\"; T2B: \"As we get like a few answers and nobody says this was terrible, I generally think it's gone quite well\"). Informal signals from colleagues substitute for evidence (FG2B.41, FG2B.36, FG2B.39). COM-B \"effects are being measured\" is the lowest item across survey waves (M=3.35, dropping to M=3.17 at 6-month follow-up). Cross-institutional analysis is absent; EV_analyse is absent across 4/4 ORP transcripts. An evaluation workstream was funded, but the chain from instrument to collection to cross-institutional analysis to feedback was not built. The chain has too many links for the current governance structure to sustain."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "      - Blind system: the programme cannot see aggregate effects\n      - Thin feedback: optional surveys yield only positive or absent responses\n      - Informal-only evidence: trainers rely on impressions instead of shared analysis\n      - Broken chain: instruments exist but no one collects, analyses, or reports cross-institutionally\n      - Upward-only feedback: results reach funders but not practitioners\n      - Workstream without chain: evaluation is funded on paper but handoff architecture is missing\n      - Monitoring-chain overload: the chain has more links than governance can carry\n      - Motivation erosion: trainers cannot see whether their effort matters beyond anecdote\n      - Absent cross-institutional analysis: EV_analyse absent across 4/4 ORP transcripts"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    The programme designs the evaluation chain before the next delivery cycle. Roles are named at each link: instrument designer, data collector, cross-institutional analyst, feedback reporter. Cross-institutional peer evaluation is developed and deployed: trainers interview each other's recipients using a lightweight instrument under consent and data-sharing governance. Monitoring is distributed across the actors it serves, forming a reciprocal network. Feedback propagates back to trainers and institutional leads, not only upward to funders."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    NPT: Communal Appraisal is the shared mechanism for judging whether a practice works. Without it, improvements remain private and the programme cannot learn at aggregate scale.\n\n    Sen: Feedback is a conversion factor for persistence. Without visible consequences, capability decays into isolated effort; without evidence about whether conversion factors are working, the equity loop flies blind.\n\n    Whole-board play: This is the outermost level of backward propagation. Pattern 3 updates shared materials; Pattern 4 updates governance; Pattern 5 updates the system's understanding of whether the whole learning process is producing the right effects.\n\n    Ostrom: Monitoring and information flows across action arenas determine whether the programme can learn and adjust. When the monitoring chain has more links than the current governance can span, the options are to reduce scope or restructure governance. Cross-institutional peer evaluation is a restructuring move.\n\n    Convergent signal: This is the only pattern where COM-B quantitative data and qualitative transcript analysis independently produce the same conclusion, giving the claim stronger evidential weight than the other four.\n\n    Mechanic, in short:\n    Derived from NPT Reflexive Monitoring 4.2 Communal Appraisal.\n    Tier: Architecture. Coordination: Very high (6+ actor evaluation chain).\n    Implementation-mode link: this pattern's failure bounds multiplied input at network scale; its resolution closes the learning loop.\n    Canonical deepening: aggregate feedback as outermost backward propagation/meta-learning.\n    Paper-specific mechanism: cross-institutional peer evaluation through a lightweight instrument, matching the working paper's bootstrap Step 3."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Design the evaluation chain end-to-end before the next delivery cycle\n    - Assign named roles at each link: instrument, collection, cross-institutional analysis, feedback\n    - Develop and deploy a lightweight cross-institutional peer-evaluation instrument\n    - Establish consent and data-sharing governance for peer evaluation\n    - Resource cross-institutional analysis explicitly\n    - Design feedback to propagate back to trainers and institutional leads, not only to funders\n    - Test: can the programme produce one cross-institutional learning report within its first year?"}], :conclusion "A programme that cannot see its downstream effects cannot learn, sustain motivation, or justify institutional support. The evaluation chain must run from instrument design through data collection, cross-institutional analysis, and feedback back to trainers and institutional leads. Where the current governance cannot carry that chain, the governance must adapt - for example through cross-institutional peer evaluation with a lightweight instrument.  The programme maintains a complete evaluation chain from instrument design through cross-institutional analysis to feedback, with a named actor at each link and explicit resourcing for cross-institutional analysis. Feedback propagates to trainers and institutional leads. A cross-institutional peer-evaluation mechanism is active when governance permits it.", :directives {:audience "programme designers, evaluators, funders, UKRN-S proposers, futon coordination layer", :keywords ["evaluation-chain" "monitoring-chain" "cross-institutional-peer-evaluation" "reciprocal-network" "lightweight-instrument" "feedback" "communal-appraisal" "reflexive-monitoring" "convergent-signal" "NPT" "Sen" "Ostrom" "meta-learning" "architecture-tier" "very-high-coordination"], :references ["equity/ARGUMENT" "equity/visible-impact" "equity/visible-impact-as-published" "equity/contribution-pathways" "equity/confident-adaptation"], :style "pattern", :title "Visible Impact: Evaluation Chain as Learning Loop", :tone "merged-canonical-paper-aligned"}, :id "equity/visible-impact-merged", :keywords ["evaluation-chain" "monitoring-chain" "cross-institutional-peer-evaluation" "reciprocal-network" "lightweight-instrument" "feedback" "communal-appraisal" "reflexive-monitoring" "convergent-signal" "NPT" "Sen" "Ostrom" "meta-learning" "architecture-tier" "very-high-coordination"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["equity/ARGUMENT" "equity/visible-impact" "equity/visible-impact-as-published" "equity/contribution-pathways" "equity/confident-adaptation"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/equity/visible-impact-merged.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Visible Impact: Evaluation Chain as Learning Loop", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "A programme learns and sustains motivation only when feedback is structured, visible, and shared across institutions. Without cross-institutional monitoring, the backward propagation that makes the whole system a learning system does not reach the outermost level — and the equity loop does not close.  The programme has a complete evaluation chain from instrument design through cross-institutional analysis to feedback, with a named actor at each link and explicit resourcing for the cross-institutional analysis step. Feedback propagates to trainers and institutional leads, not only to funders."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "A distributed programme needs evidence of its downstream impact to improve its own design, sustain trainer motivation, and justify continued institutional support. In NPT terms this is Reflexive Monitoring (4.2 Communal Appraisal): shared mechanisms for evaluating what is working and what is not. In the whole-board-play analogy, Patterns 1-4 gave the agent the rules, the conditions to play, a way for local learning to propagate into shared materials, and a route into co-governance. This final pattern asks whether the whole system can see its own effects and adjust — whether backward propagation reaches all the way from outcomes to architecture."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The programme has resolved the preceding conditions: expectations are clear (Pattern 1), delivery is protected (Pattern 2), materials are stewarded (Pattern 3), and trainers have contribution pathways into co-governance (Pattern 4). Local learning propagates into materials and governance. But the system's learning operates only at the level of individual adaptations and community conversation. It does not yet have a way to see its own aggregate effects — across institutions, across cohorts, across time."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Feedback mechanisms are thin. Trainers rely on optional post-session surveys that yield only positive or absent responses. Cross-institutional sharing is limited: formal channels exist but go largely unused. No one performs cross-institutional analysis. The programme cannot demonstrate impact to the institutions it depends on for support. An entire evaluation workstream was funded but the evaluation chain — from instrument design through data collection to cross-institutional analysis to feedback — was not built. This is the deepest failure of backward propagation: the system plays, the system learns locally, but the aggregate signal — what is actually working across the whole landscape — never reaches the actors who could adjust the system's architecture. Without this signal, the agents within the system cannot know whether their becoming is on track. Feedback is not just a management tool; it is what makes the whole system reflexive rather than blind."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "      - Blind system: the programme cannot see its own aggregate effects\n      - Thin feedback: optional surveys yield only positive or absent responses\n      - Informal-only evidence: trainers rely on \"you can kind of tell whether people are engaged\"\n      - Broken chain: instruments exist but no one collects, analyses, or reports cross-institutionally\n      - Upward-only feedback: results reach funders but never propagate back to practitioners\n      - Motivation erosion: trainers cannot see whether their effort matters beyond anecdote\n      - Funded ambition without architecture: an entire evaluation workstream exists on paper but the handoff chain is not built"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    The evaluation chain is designed before the first delivery event. Roles are assigned explicitly at each link: who designs instruments, who collects data, who analyses cross-institutional patterns, who reports back to trainers and institutional leads. Shared feedback converts isolated impressions into programme-level learning. The chain is resourced end-to-end, not just at the instrument-design end. The feedback does not stop at the funders' desk — it propagates back to trainers, to institutional leads, to the scaffold itself (Pattern 1), completing the circuit. This is the whole-board view: the system sees its own aggregate play and adjusts."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Sen: Feedback is a conversion factor for persistence. Without visible consequences, capability decays into isolated effort. But more than that: feedback is what allows the system to assess whether its conversion factors are actually working — whether the scaffold (Pattern 1) is giving the right form, whether provisioning (Pattern 2) is sufficient, whether the commons (Pattern 3) is healthy, whether co-governance (Pattern 4) is real. Without this assessment, the equity loop is flying blind.\n\n    Whole-board play: This is the final level of backward propagation. Pattern 3 propagates local learning into shared materials (updating the weights from individual games). Pattern 4 propagates trainer experience into programme governance (modifying the architecture). Pattern 5 propagates aggregate outcomes into the system's self-understanding (seeing the whole board). In AlphaZero, this is analogous to meta-learning: not just learning from games, but learning whether the learning process itself is producing the right kind of improvement. Without this level, the system can improve locally but cannot assess whether it is improving globally. It can play but cannot see its own play.\n\n    Ostrom: This is the monitoring problem in its strongest form. Monitoring and information flows across action arenas determine whether the programme can learn and adjust. The learning chain crosses multiple actors and no single trainer can sustain it alone. An evaluation workstream is not an evaluation chain. The chain requires specific actors at each link — instrument designer, data collector, cross-institutional analyst, feedback reporter — and each link must be resourced. When any one is missing, the loop does not close. This is the most coordination-intensive condition and the most consistently absent — not because no one wanted to monitor, but because the monitoring chain has too many links for the current governance structure to sustain.\n\n    Mechanic, in short:\n    Derived from NPT Reflexive Monitoring (4.2 Communal Appraisal) via the UKRN T3 analysis.\n    Mapped to the equity loop at the feedback joint: the mechanism that tells the system whether the loop is closing.\n    Deepened via whole-board play: this is the outermost level of backward propagation — the system seeing its own aggregate play and adjusting. Without it, the system can improve locally but cannot assess whether it is improving globally.\n    Ostrom: monitoring and information flows across action arenas determine whether the programme can learn.\n    Coordination requirement: Very high (6+ actor chain)."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Design the evaluation chain before the first delivery event, not as a retrofit\n    - Assign named roles at each link of the chain\n    - Resource cross-institutional analysis explicitly — it is the central link and the most absent\n    - Design feedback to propagate back to practitioners, not just upward to funders\n    - Test: can the programme produce one cross-institutional learning report within its first year?\n    - This requires a multi-actor chain (very high coordination, 6+ actors) and is the final condition"}], :conclusion "A programme learns and sustains motivation only when feedback is structured, visible, and shared across institutions. Without cross-institutional monitoring, the backward propagation that makes the whole system a learning system does not reach the outermost level — and the equity loop does not close.  The programme has a complete evaluation chain from instrument design through cross-institutional analysis to feedback, with a named actor at each link and explicit resourcing for the cross-institutional analysis step. Feedback propagates to trainers and institutional leads, not only to funders.", :directives {:audience "programme designers, evaluators, funders, futon coordination layer", :keywords ["feedback" "monitoring" "evaluation" "impact" "reflexive monitoring" "NPT" "Ostrom" "backward propagation" "reflexivity"], :references ["equity/ARGUMENT" "equity/contribution-pathways" "equity/confident-adaptation"], :style "pattern", :title "A Programme That Cannot See Its Own Effects Cannot Learn", :tone "analytic-foundational"}, :id "equity/visible-impact", :keywords ["feedback" "monitoring" "evaluation" "impact" "reflexive monitoring" "NPT" "Ostrom" "backward propagation" "reflexivity"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["equity/ARGUMENT" "equity/contribution-pathways" "equity/confident-adaptation"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/equity/visible-impact.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "A Programme That Cannot See Its Own Effects Cannot Learn", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Use construction discipline (safe constructors) for runtime law checking; reserve property tests for offline verification; log test harness results as evaluation signals."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Defining how to check CT laws (associativity, identity, functor preservation, naturality) in the exotic programming system."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    CT structures have laws that must hold, but checking laws at every operation is expensive."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    No checking allows invalid structures to propagate; full runtime checking is slow; pure property tests miss runtime violations."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Use construction discipline: smart constructors that validate laws on creation, preventing invalid structures from existing. Reserve property-based testing (e.g., test.check) for offline verification of constructor correctness. Log test harness outcomes as additional evaluation signals for regime quality."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Construction discipline provides runtime safety without per-operation overhead (validate once, trust thereafter). Property tests provide confidence in the constructors themselves. Test logs contribute to exotic scoring (regimes that pass more tests are healthier)."} {:name "evidence", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "exotic-programming-curriculum.md Basecamp decision log, Law-checking strategy entry."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Implement smart constructors for Category, Functor, NatTrans.\n    - Write property tests for law preservation.\n    - Add test pass/fail rate to regime evaluation signals."}], :conclusion "Use construction discipline (safe constructors) for runtime law checking; reserve property tests for offline verification; log test harness results as evaluation signals.", :directives {:audience "category implementers, law checker designers", :sigils ["🌂/示"], :style "pattern", :title "Construction Discipline for Law Checking", :tone "foundational"}, :id "exotic/construction-discipline", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🌂/示"], :source-path "library/exotic/construction-discipline.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Construction Discipline for Law Checking", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Generated patterns are dual-tracked: drafts carry `:status :draft` in meaningful namespaces, then promote to stable IDs on acceptance with review evidence."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Defining how patterns generated by the exotic system are managed before becoming canonical."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The system may generate new patterns (via meta-exotic programming or pattern extraction), but not all generated patterns are worth keeping."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Treating generated patterns as immediately canonical pollutes the library; discarding them loses learning; mixing drafts with stable patterns creates confusion."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Use dual-track: drafts live in namespaced IDs (e.g., `gen/<domain>/<slug>`) with `:status :draft`. Review process checks evidence and fit. Promotion moves pattern to stable namespace with `:status :stable` and records review outcome. Rejected drafts are archived, not deleted."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    This creates a clear path from generation to canonization while preserving the audit trail. Draft namespaces prevent accidental use of unreviewed patterns. Archive preserves learning even from rejected ideas."} {:name "evidence", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "exotic-programming-curriculum.md Basecamp decision log, Generated-pattern policy entry."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Define draft namespace convention.\n    - Implement promotion workflow with evidence requirement.\n    - Add archive storage for rejected drafts."}], :conclusion "Generated patterns are dual-tracked: drafts carry `:status :draft` in meaningful namespaces, then promote to stable IDs on acceptance with review evidence.", :directives {:audience "pattern generation designers, draft management implementers", :sigils ["🍂/二"], :style "pattern", :title "Dual-Track Generated Patterns", :tone "foundational"}, :id "exotic/dual-track-generation", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🍂/二"], :source-path "library/exotic/dual-track-generation.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Dual-Track Generated Patterns", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Use two window types: primary (fixed evaluations) for quick ratchet tests, secondary (mission-completion) for interpretable checks once mapping stabilizes."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Defining what constitutes a \"window\" for ratchet memory and delta-scoring."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The ratchet mechanism needs to detect improvement over time, but \"time\" could mean evaluations, generations, wall-clock, or mission completions."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Fixed evaluation windows enable rapid testing but lose mission-semantic meaning; mission-completion windows are interpretable but require stable mission mapping before they work."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Implement both. Primary windows use fixed evaluation counts (e.g., 100 evals) for early ratchet testing. Secondary windows use mission completions once the VISION-to-EXECUTION mapping is stable. The primary window dominates ratchet decisions until mission windows stabilize."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    This allows iterative development (test the ratchet quickly with primary windows) while building toward the interpretable goal (mission-aligned windows for exotic scoring)."} {:name "evidence", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "exotic-programming-curriculum.md Basecamp decision log, Window definition entry."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Implement primary window counter in exoevolve.\n    - Define mission-completion detection logic.\n    - Add window-type field to ratchet state."}], :conclusion "Use two window types: primary (fixed evaluations) for quick ratchet tests, secondary (mission-completion) for interpretable checks once mapping stabilizes.", :directives {:audience "ratchet implementers, exotic scoring designers", :sigils ["🌏/乃"], :style "pattern", :title "Dual Windowing", :tone "foundational"}, :id "exotic/dual-windowing", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🌏/乃"], :source-path "library/exotic/dual-windowing.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Dual Windowing", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Lift registry uses full Lisp-style expressions for CT templates and lift rules, with evidence/version metadata per entry for provenance and automation."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Defining the schema for the exotype-xenotype lift registry that bridges patterns to CT structures."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The lift registry must map sigil patterns to CT templates in a way that supports provenance tracking and eventual automation."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Simple key-value mappings lose the structure needed for composition; opaque blobs prevent inspection; metadata-free entries can't track where templates came from."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Use Lisp-style expressions for `:ct-template` (composable, inspectable) and `:lift-rules` (transformations from pattern to template). Include `:evidence` (what supports this mapping), `:version` (which futon1 version), and `:source` metadata per entry."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Lisp expressions are homoiconic (code as data), making templates manipulable by the same system that uses them. Evidence metadata supports the contemplative layer. Version tracking enables live sync from futon1 as source of truth."} {:name "evidence", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "exotic-programming-curriculum.md Basecamp decision log, Lift registry schema entry; resources/exotype-xenotype-lift.edn structure."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Define CT template s-expression grammar.\n    - Implement lift rule interpreter.\n    - Add evidence/version fields to registry entries.\n    - Prototype the pattern REPL handshake: pattern-id -> ct-template -> vision skeleton -> plan functor stub -> run -> evidence bundle -> lift proposal."}], :conclusion "Lift registry uses full Lisp-style expressions for CT templates and lift rules, with evidence/version metadata per entry for provenance and automation.", :directives {:audience "lift implementers, pattern-CT bridge designers", :sigils ["🌏/入"], :style "pattern", :title "Full Lift Registry with Lisp Templates", :tone "foundational"}, :id "exotic/full-lift-registry", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🌏/入"], :source-path "library/exotic/full-lift-registry.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Full Lift Registry with Lisp Templates", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Naturality checking is graded (not boolean) and curriculum-tightened; commutativity residuals are logged for analysis, not hard-failed before M14."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Defining how strictly to enforce the naturality condition on ADAPT transformations."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The naturality square (PLAN changes must commute with mission execution) is the core coherence check for adaptations."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Boolean pass/fail is too strict early on (everything fails) and hides information; pure permissiveness loses the signal entirely."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Use graded metrics: mission-path edit distance as primary, evidence-vector distance as soft penalty/bonus. Log commutativity residuals (difference between PLAN(f;g) and PLAN(f);PLAN(g)) without hard-failing. Tighten tolerance as the curriculum advances; post-M14, naturality violations may become blocking."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Graded checking provides signal during development (how close to commutative?) while logging builds the evidence base for later decisions. Curriculum tightening means early exploration isn't penalized but mature systems are held to standards."} {:name "evidence", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "exotic-programming-curriculum.md Basecamp decision log, Naturality strictness entry; Basecamp invariants section."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Implement mission-path edit distance metric.\n    - Add residual logging to adapt.clj.\n    - Define curriculum schedule for tolerance tightening."}], :conclusion "Naturality checking is graded (not boolean) and curriculum-tightened; commutativity residuals are logged for analysis, not hard-failed before M14.", :directives {:audience "ADAPT implementers, naturality checker designers", :sigils ["🎴/乡"], :style "pattern", :title "Graded Naturality with Curriculum Tightening", :tone "foundational"}, :id "exotic/graded-naturality", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🎴/乡"], :source-path "library/exotic/graded-naturality.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Graded Naturality with Curriculum Tightening", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "EXECUTION category objects are milestones with MMCA traces as evidence, preserving both CT interpretability and simulation grounding."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Defining what \"objects\" and \"morphisms\" mean in the EXECUTION category of exotic programming."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The CT layer must be interpretable (auditable reasoning) while remaining grounded in actual simulation behavior."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Pure milestone semantics lose the richness of how execution actually proceeded; pure trace semantics drown in detail without categorical structure."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Use milestones as the categorical objects and morphisms (discrete, auditable checkpoints), but attach MMCA traces as evidence artifacts. The traces support the milestone claims without replacing the categorical structure."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    This keeps CT interpretability (you can ask \"what missions were completed?\") while grounding in simulation (you can inspect the actual execution path). It also aligns with nonstarter mechanics where clearing events are facts with supporting evidence."} {:name "evidence", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "exotic-programming-curriculum.md Basecamp decision log, EXECUTION semantics entry."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Define milestone schema (what constitutes a valid checkpoint).\n    - Specify trace attachment format (which traces support which milestones).\n    - Implement milestone extraction from MMCA runs."}], :conclusion "EXECUTION category objects are milestones with MMCA traces as evidence, preserving both CT interpretability and simulation grounding.", :directives {:audience "exotic programming implementers, CT layer designers", :sigils ["🌏/井"], :style "pattern", :title "Hybrid Execution Semantics", :tone "foundational"}, :id "exotic/hybrid-execution-semantics", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🌏/井"], :source-path "library/exotic/hybrid-execution-semantics.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Hybrid Execution Semantics", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "VISION is immutable once declared; PLAN may be revised but only with logged evidence linking to xenotype/exotype learning."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Defining revision policy for the CT structures in exotic programming."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    Strategy adaptation (ADAPT natural transformation) requires plan changes, but unconstrained mutation destroys interpretability."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Fully immutable plans cannot respond to evidence; fully mutable plans lose the distinction between coherent adaptation and goalpost-moving."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Lock VISION (objects and morphisms) at declaration time. Allow PLAN functor revisions, but require each revision to cite evidence from EXECUTION. Failed plans receive weak positive scores for early failure that saves compute, rather than harsh penalties."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    This preserves the \"what you said you would do\" (VISION) as an auditable record while allowing \"how you're going to do it\" (PLAN) to evolve. The evidence requirement makes adaptations justified rather than arbitrary, supporting naturality checking."} {:name "evidence", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "exotic-programming-curriculum.md Basecamp decision log, Plan revision policy entry; exotic-programming-spec.md ADAPT naturality condition."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Implement VISION immutability check in category.clj.\n    - Add evidence-citation field to plan revision records.\n    - Define \"early failure\" scoring bonus."}], :conclusion "VISION is immutable once declared; PLAN may be revised but only with logged evidence linking to xenotype/exotype learning.", :directives {:audience "exotic programming implementers, plan functor designers", :sigils ["📎/计"], :style "pattern", :title "Immutable Vision, Mutable Plan", :tone "foundational"}, :id "exotic/immutable-vision-mutable-plan", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["📎/计"], :source-path "library/exotic/immutable-vision-mutable-plan.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Immutable Vision, Mutable Plan", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Treat futon1 as immutable/versioned source of truth for patterns; sync bidirectionally (drafts back via tri-store), logging pattern version IDs per run."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Defining how the exotic programming system stays synchronized with the upstream pattern library."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    Patterns evolve in futon1; exotic programming depends on patterns; generated patterns may flow back upstream."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    One-time import drifts out of date; continuous sync without versioning loses reproducibility; one-way sync prevents learning from flowing back."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Implement live sync: futon1 is the immutable, versioned source of truth for canonical patterns. Log which pattern version was used in each run. Sync is bidirectional: drafts generated by exotic programming flow back to futon1 via the tri-store (facts → memes → upstream proposal)."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Version logging enables reproducibility (you know exactly which patterns were active). Bidirectional flow closes the loop (exotic programming improves the pattern library that exotic programming uses). Immutable source of truth prevents accidental overwrites."} {:name "evidence", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "exotic-programming-curriculum.md Basecamp decision log, Import cadence entry; scripts/import_futon3a_patterns.clj."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Add pattern version ID to run logs.\n    - Implement upstream proposal pathway for drafts.\n    - Define version bump protocol for futon1."}], :conclusion "Treat futon1 as immutable/versioned source of truth for patterns; sync bidirectionally (drafts back via tri-store), logging pattern version IDs per run.", :directives {:audience "import pipeline designers, pattern sync implementers", :sigils ["🐺/本"], :style "pattern", :title "Live Sync from Source of Truth", :tone "foundational"}, :id "exotic/live-sync-source-truth", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🐺/本"], :source-path "library/exotic/live-sync-source-truth.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Live Sync from Source of Truth", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "M16 baseline profile uses semi-robust batches: fixed pop/mutation/selection/budget, rotated seed sets across batches, multiple batches for A/B comparability."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Defining the experimental profile for validating exotic xenotype against Mission 7/8 baselines."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    Validation requires A/B comparison between regimes, but MMCA has inherent stochasticity."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Fully deterministic runs (single seed) overfit to that seed; fully random runs have high variance that obscures signal; single runs can't estimate variance."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Fix structural parameters (population size, mutation operators, selection method, evaluation budget). Use fixed seed sets per batch, but rotate seeds across batches. Run multiple batches (e.g., 3 x 5k evaluations) to get variance estimates. Compare regimes within batches before comparing across."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Semi-robust design balances reproducibility (same seeds within batch) with generalization (different seeds across batches) with statistical power (multiple batches). This is the minimal setup for detecting upward drift vs stationary distribution."} {:name "evidence", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "exotic-programming-curriculum.md Basecamp decision log, Baseline profile entry; README-mission8.md A/B design."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Lock parameter values in a config file.\n    - Implement seed rotation across batches.\n    - Define statistical test for \"upward trend\" detection."}], :conclusion "M16 baseline profile uses semi-robust batches: fixed pop/mutation/selection/budget, rotated seed sets across batches, multiple batches for A/B comparability.", :directives {:audience "validation designers, A/B test implementers", :sigils ["🌝/四"], :style "pattern", :title "Semi-Robust Baselines for Validation", :tone "foundational"}, :id "exotic/semi-robust-baselines", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🌝/四"], :source-path "library/exotic/semi-robust-baselines.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Semi-Robust Baselines for Validation", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Initialize VISION from CT templates seeded from futon3a sigil patterns; fall back to degenerate vision when no pattern matches."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Defining how a genotype acquires its initial VISION declaration."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    Exotic scoring requires a declared VISION, but genotypes don't naturally come with one."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Requiring manual vision declaration doesn't scale; purely degenerate visions lose the interpretability benefit; random initialization produces noise."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Seed CT templates from the futon3a pattern catalog (sigils + metadata), then lift to xenotype templates via the lift registry. When a genotype is created, match it to a template if possible; otherwise use degenerate vision (single object, identity morphism). Record template identity in lineage for provenance."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Templates provide interpretable structure (the vision means something because it came from a pattern). The fallback ensures the system always works. Template provenance supports the contemplative layer's \"how did you get here?\" question."} {:name "evidence", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "exotic-programming-curriculum.md Basecamp decision log, VISION initialization entry; resources/exotype-xenotype-lift.edn."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Define template matching criteria (sigil similarity, hotword overlap).\n    - Implement lift from pattern to CT template.\n    - Add template-id field to vision records."}], :conclusion "Initialize VISION from CT templates seeded from futon3a sigil patterns; fall back to degenerate vision when no pattern matches.", :directives {:audience "vision initializers, lift registry designers", :sigils ["🌏/化"], :style "pattern", :title "Template-Driven VISION Initialization", :tone "foundational"}, :id "exotic/template-driven-initialization", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🌏/化"], :source-path "library/exotic/template-driven-initialization.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Template-Driven VISION Initialization", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Lineage uses a tri-store model: facts (append-only event log), memes (derived lineage graphs), notions (optional fuzzy index), consistent with nonstarter."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Defining how to store and query lineage/provenance information for exotic scoring."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The contemplative layer needs to answer \"how did this genotype arrive here?\" and \"is this score earned or hacked?\""} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    A single flat log is hard to query for lineage; a derived graph alone loses auditability; a fuzzy index alone lacks grounding."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Use three layers: (1) Facts = append-only event log as single source of truth (every mutation, every score, every adaptation). (2) Memes = derived lineage graph and summaries, rebuilt from facts. (3) Notions = optional fuzzy index for similarity queries. This matches the nonstarter tri-store architecture."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Facts provide auditability (you can always replay). Memes provide queryability (you can ask about ancestors). Notions provide discoverability (you can find similar paths). The separation keeps each layer's guarantees clean."} {:name "evidence", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "exotic-programming-curriculum.md Basecamp decision log, Lineage storage entry; nonstarter tri-store architecture."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Define fact event schema (mutation, scoring, adaptation events).\n    - Implement meme derivation from facts.\n    - Defer notions layer until provenance queries are clearer."}], :conclusion "Lineage uses a tri-store model: facts (append-only event log), memes (derived lineage graphs), notions (optional fuzzy index), consistent with nonstarter.", :directives {:audience "lineage implementers, provenance designers", :sigils ["🎶/己"], :style "pattern", :title "Tri-Store Lineage Model", :tone "foundational"}, :id "exotic/tri-store-lineage", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🎶/己"], :source-path "library/exotic/tri-store-lineage.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Tri-Store Lineage Model", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Seed a term dictionary from a curated source, apply it to a messier corpus,\n  absorb new terms from that corpus, and iterate. Each pass through the loop\n  expands coverage and improves precision."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are building a mathematical NER kernel that must cover terminology\n    from multiple sources with different levels of structure."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      PlanetMath provides curated, structured term definitions (pmdefines, pmsynonym,\n      pmtitle) and Stack Exchange provides folksonomy tags and body-extracted terms\n      from a much larger but noisier corpus."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      Starting from SE alone produces a noisy dictionary full of common English words.\n      Starting from PlanetMath alone misses contemporary terminology and informal usage.\n      Neither source alone provides adequate coverage."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "      Bootstrap: PlanetMath seeds the kernel (high precision, limited recall) → apply\n      to SE (discover gaps) → absorb SE tags and body-extracted terms (expand recall)\n      → filter and merge → re-apply to SE (measure improvement) → iterate. Each cycle\n      expands the kernel while maintaining the curated core's precision."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      The NNexus system (Ginev, 2014) demonstrated that PlanetMath terms alone provide\n      useful auto-linking. The bootstrap loop extends NNexus's approach by using SE as\n      both a test corpus and a term source. Evidence: 3 PlanetMath repos → 2,731 terms;\n      all 63 repos → 19,236 terms; SE integration pending."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Run full bootstrap after M-f6-ingest: absorb math.SE tags into kernel, re-run spotter, measure coverage delta.]\n    next[Track precision/recall at each loop iteration to detect diminishing returns.]\n    next[Apply same loop pattern to Arxiv in M-f6-arxiv: SE-expanded kernel → Arxiv → absorb Arxiv terms.]"}], :conclusion "  Seed a term dictionary from a curated source, apply it to a messier corpus,\n  absorb new terms from that corpus, and iterate. Each pass through the loop\n  expands coverage and improves precision.", :directives {:audience "futon6 developers, corpus linguists", :factor "Persistence (viriya)", :keywords ["bootstrap" "iterate" "kernel" "expand" "absorb" "coverage" "dictionary" "seed" "loop"], :references ["hdm/bootstrap-kernel" "f6/negative-space-duality"], :sigils ["🔄/回"], :title "Bootstrap Loop", :tone "architectural"}, :id "f6/bootstrap-loop", :keywords ["bootstrap" "iterate" "kernel" "expand" "absorb" "coverage" "dictionary" "seed" "loop"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["hdm/bootstrap-kernel" "f6/negative-space-duality"], :scores {}, :sigils ["🔄/回"], :source-path "library/f6/bootstrap-loop.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Bootstrap Loop", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Two agents, same questions, one has the graph. The difference measures\n  the value of structured mathematical knowledge representation."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are proving that the knowledge graph from M-f6-ingest makes\n    mathematical question answering measurably better."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      Agent A (bare) receives only raw question text. Agent B (graph-enhanced)\n      receives the question plus NER term annotations, pattern tags, scope records,\n      cluster context, and related entities via graph queries."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      Graph context might be distracting on easy questions where the answer is\n      obvious. The judge LLM's scores might not correlate with human judgement.\n      Agent B might simply be longer-winded rather than more accurate."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "      Stratify the evaluation by difficulty (easy/medium/hard). Use blind scoring\n      (judge sees both answers in randomised order). Measure multiple dimensions:\n      accuracy, completeness, coherence, depth. Spot-check judge scores against\n      human ratings. Hypothesis: improvement is largest on hard questions where\n      structure matters most."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      The AI4CI proposal designed this evaluation but never ran it. The thesis\n      (§10.6) called for \"actionable semantic domain and user models\" — the graph\n      IS the domain model. If structured representation doesn't measurably help,\n      the decade-long thesis is wrong. If it does help, we know exactly what kind\n      of structure matters and can scale to Arxiv."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Build MathKnowledgeGraph query interface as specified in M-f6-eval.]\n    next[Sample 200 questions stratified by difficulty from math.SE processed corpus.]\n    next[Run paired evaluation and compute significance tests.]"}], :conclusion "  Two agents, same questions, one has the graph. The difference measures\n  the value of structured mathematical knowledge representation.", :directives {:audience "futon6 developers, evaluation designers", :factor "Keen investigation (dhammavicaya)", :keywords ["evaluation" "graph" "bare" "enhanced" "agent" "comparison" "blind" "stratified" "judge"], :references ["f6/negative-space-duality" "ai4ci/collective-intelligence-evaluation"], :sigils ["📊/比"], :title "Graph-Enhanced Evaluation", :tone "methodological"}, :id "f6/graph-enhanced-evaluation", :keywords ["evaluation" "graph" "bare" "enhanced" "agent" "comparison" "blind" "stratified" "judge"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["f6/negative-space-duality" "ai4ci/collective-intelligence-evaluation"], :scores {}, :sigils ["📊/比"], :source-path "library/f6/graph-enhanced-evaluation.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Graph-Enhanced Evaluation", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Detect learning events by tracking when new technical terms appear in a user's\n  contributions, then measure the causal effect of social interactions (answers,\n  comments, corrections) on these events using an impulse-damping model."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are measuring whether mathematical Q&A interactions cause learning,\n    using the same method Corneli (2014, Chapter 6) applied to PlanetMath edit histories\n    — now scaled to all of Stack Exchange using the 19,236-term NER kernel."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      Stack Exchange data dumps include full revision histories for every post, with\n      timestamps. Each user has an observable vocabulary trajectory: the set of NER\n      kernel terms they have used, growing over time. Social interactions (receiving\n      an answer, a comment, an edit to one's question) are timestamped treatment events.\n      A \"learning event\" is any post edit or new post in which a user introduces a\n      technical term they have never used before."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      A new term in a user's output may be exposition (copying from a textbook) rather\n      than genuine learning. The Ornstein-Uhlenbeck model cannot distinguish these.\n      High-frequency posters will show more \"learning events\" by volume alone. The\n      method requires careful per-user normalization and distinguishing treatment from\n      confound (did the user learn from the SE interaction, or from an external source\n      between posts?)."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "      For each SE user with sufficient history (N posts > threshold): extract their\n      term vocabulary trajectory using the NER kernel. Identify learning events (first\n      use of each term). Identify treatment events (answers received, comments received,\n      accepted answers on their questions). Fit the Ornstein-Uhlenbeck impulse-damping\n      model: does a treatment event produce a detectable impulse in learning-event rate,\n      and how quickly does it damp? Compare across difficulty strata and topic clusters."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      Corneli (2014) demonstrated this method on 445 PlanetMath users over 9 years\n      (8,051 forum posts, 14,064 corrections, 3,867 learning-event edits). The method\n      is classical — no GPU, no LLM — and scales linearly with corpus size. SE has\n      orders of magnitude more data. If the same impulse-damping dynamics appear in\n      SE that appeared in PlanetMath, it validates the thesis's model of peer-produced\n      peer learning at scale. The fitted parameters then become a baseline for the\n      Artificial Stack Exchange: do agent interactions produce the same learning\n      dynamics as human interactions?"} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Extract user edit histories from math.SE data dump (available after M-f6-ingest).]\n    next[Run NER kernel term spotting per-user to build vocabulary trajectories.]\n    next[Fit Ornstein-Uhlenbeck model to treatment→learning-event sequences; compare parameters with Corneli (2014) Table.]\n    next[Use fitted human-learning parameters as a benchmark for M-f6-agents: do artificial agents exhibit similar impulse-damping dynamics?]"}], :conclusion "  Detect learning events by tracking when new technical terms appear in a user's\n  contributions, then measure the causal effect of social interactions (answers,\n  comments, corrections) on these events using an impulse-damping model.", :directives {:audience "futon6 developers, computational social scientists", :factor "Keen investigation (dhammavicaya)", :keywords ["learning" "event" "term" "new" "edit" "history" "treatment" "outcome" "Ornstein-Uhlenbeck" "impulse" "damping"], :references ["f6/bootstrap-loop" "f6/self-play-loop" "f6/graph-enhanced-evaluation"], :sigils ["📈/学"], :title "Learning Event Detection", :tone "methodological"}, :id "f6/learning-event-detection", :keywords ["learning" "event" "term" "new" "edit" "history" "treatment" "outcome" "Ornstein-Uhlenbeck" "impulse" "damping"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["f6/bootstrap-loop" "f6/self-play-loop" "f6/graph-enhanced-evaluation"], :scores {}, :sigils ["📈/学"], :source-path "library/f6/learning-event-detection.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Learning Event Detection", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  What NER covers is formal scaffolding; what NER leaves behind is where\n  informal reasoning patterns live. The two are complementary masks over\n  the same text, not competing annotations."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are annotating mathematical text with both named entities (terms,\n    definitions, symbols) and informal reasoning patterns (heuristics, strategies)."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      NER term spotting identifies formal mathematical objects (named theorems,\n      defined terms, variable bindings) and pattern tagging identifies informal\n      reasoning moves (argue-by-contradiction, try-a-simpler-case, etc.)."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      It is tempting to treat these as independent annotation layers applied to\n      different documents or different classes of text. In fact, both systems fire\n      on the same documents: high-term entities also have more patterns, not fewer.\n      The duality is spatial within text, not across documents."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "      Run both NER and pattern tagging on every document. Treat the NER-covered\n      tokens as \"formal figure\" and the remaining tokens as \"informal ground.\"\n      Measure negative-space percentage (typically 87-99% of tokens). Use the\n      ratio as a document-level feature: low negative-space means dense formal\n      content; high negative-space means discursive reasoning."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      The Corneli (2014) thesis codes proofs as alternating formal steps (definition\n      expansion, deduction) and informal moves (conjecture, heuristic selection).\n      NER captures the former; the 25 math-informal patterns capture the latter.\n      Together they give an agent structured understanding that flat text cannot."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Validate duality on math.SE data after M-f6-ingest superpod run.]\n    next[Use negative-space ratio as a feature in M-f6-eval stratified sampling.]\n    next[Feed both layers to Agent B in M-f6-eval to test whether dual annotation outperforms either alone.]"}], :conclusion "  What NER covers is formal scaffolding; what NER leaves behind is where\n  informal reasoning patterns live. The two are complementary masks over\n  the same text, not competing annotations.", :directives {:audience "futon6 developers, mathematical NLP researchers", :factor "Discernment (paññā)", :keywords ["NER" "pattern" "informal" "formal" "scaffolding" "complement" "mask" "coverage" "token" "negative-space"], :references ["math-informal/*" "ai4ci/dual-layer-proof-patterns" "hdm/bootstrap-kernel"], :sigils ["🌑/空"], :title "Negative Space Duality", :tone "architectural"}, :id "f6/negative-space-duality", :keywords ["NER" "pattern" "informal" "formal" "scaffolding" "complement" "mask" "coverage" "token" "negative-space"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["math-informal/*" "ai4ci/dual-layer-proof-patterns" "hdm/bootstrap-kernel"], :scores {}, :sigils ["🌑/空"], :source-path "library/f6/negative-space-duality.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Negative Space Duality", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  The 25 math-informal patterns are not just annotations — they are operational\n  agent strategies. Each pattern tells an agent what to do next when reasoning\n  about a mathematical problem."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are designing agents that use the math-informal pattern library\n    as a repertoire of reasoning moves, following Pólya's tradition of heuristic\n    problem solving."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      The 25 math-informal patterns each have a conclusion (what the pattern achieves),\n      an IF/HOWEVER/THEN/BECAUSE structure (when and how to apply it), keywords\n      (for recognition in text), and next-steps (what follows from applying it)."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      Patterns as written describe human reasoning strategies. An LLM agent may apply\n      them differently: it cannot truly \"try a simpler case\" the way a human does, but\n      it can decompose a question and generate a simpler sub-question. The mapping from\n      human heuristic to agent action is not always one-to-one."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "      For each of the 25 patterns, define an agent-operational interpretation: what\n      the Asker does with this pattern (generate a question), what the Answerer does\n      (construct an answer step), what the Critic does (check a condition). Include\n      the pattern's flexiarg keywords in the agent's prompt so it can name the pattern\n      it is using. Track which patterns the agents actually use via PSR/PUR records."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      The Corneli (2014) thesis (§10.6) calls for agents that can \"apply standard and\n      social problem solving heuristics.\" The thesis Appendix B demonstrates that proofs\n      can be coded as sequences of pattern applications. The 25 flexiarg patterns are\n      the operational vocabulary for this coding. If agents can name and apply them,\n      the thesis's computational vision is realized."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Write agent-operational interpretation for at least 8 high-use patterns (the ones in M-f6-agents Table).]\n    next[Include pattern keywords in Agent B prompt template for M-f6-eval.]\n    next[After M-f6-agents Phase 1: measure which patterns agents cite most and whether citations are accurate.]"}], :conclusion "  The 25 math-informal patterns are not just annotations — they are operational\n  agent strategies. Each pattern tells an agent what to do next when reasoning\n  about a mathematical problem.", :directives {:audience "futon6 developers, agent designers", :factor "Keen investigation (dhammavicaya)", :keywords ["pattern" "strategy" "agent" "heuristic" "Polya" "operational" "flexiarg" "reasoning" "move"], :references ["math-informal/*" "f6/self-play-loop" "f6/q-turnstile-a"], :sigils ["🎯/策"], :title "Pattern as Strategy", :tone "architectural"}, :id "f6/pattern-as-strategy", :keywords ["pattern" "strategy" "agent" "heuristic" "Polya" "operational" "flexiarg" "reasoning" "move"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["math-informal/*" "f6/self-play-loop" "f6/q-turnstile-a"], :scores {}, :sigils ["🎯/策"], :source-path "library/f6/pattern-as-strategy.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Pattern as Strategy", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  A mathematical proof is a sequence of social-computational moves that can\n  be coded along five dimensions: context, feedback, quality, structure, and\n  heuristic. This coding bridges human reasoning and agent behavior."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are validating that the pattern tagger and agent system can\n    analyse proofs in the same way the Corneli (2014) thesis Appendix B analyses\n    them by hand."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      Appendix B of the thesis codes two proofs (Gowers & Ganesalingam, 2012) using\n      a 5-category scheme: (1) context — expand definitions, scope the problem;\n      (2) feedback — generate examples, try instances; (3) quality — form conjectures,\n      build models; (4) structure — change descriptions, apply deductions;\n      (5) heuristic — restrict to known, certify results."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      The thesis coding was done by hand on two proofs. Scaling to corpus-level\n      requires automation. The 25 math-informal patterns partially cover the 5\n      categories but were not designed to partition cleanly into them. Some patterns\n      span multiple categories; some categories lack dedicated patterns."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "      Map each of the 25 math-informal patterns to one or more of the 5 thesis\n      categories. Identify coverage gaps: categories where no pattern fires\n      reliably. Use the Appendix B worked examples as test cases for the pattern\n      tagger — do the automatically assigned patterns match the hand-coded categories?\n      This is a unit test for the entire pattern-tagging enterprise."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      If the pattern tagger's output aligns with the hand-coded proof analysis, it\n      validates both the tagger and the thesis's claim that proofs are structured\n      social-computational processes. If it doesn't align, the gap tells us exactly\n      which reasoning moves the tagger misses."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Create a 25×5 mapping matrix: each pattern assigned to thesis categories (1)-(5).]\n    next[Run pattern tagger on the two Appendix B proofs (if available in corpus) and compare with hand coding.]\n    next[Identify thesis categories with thin pattern coverage; consider whether new patterns are needed.]"}], :conclusion "  A mathematical proof is a sequence of social-computational moves that can\n  be coded along five dimensions: context, feedback, quality, structure, and\n  heuristic. This coding bridges human reasoning and agent behavior.", :directives {:audience "futon6 developers, mathematical reasoning researchers", :factor "Keen investigation (dhammavicaya)", :keywords ["proof" "social" "process" "coding" "context" "feedback" "quality" "structure" "heuristic" "Gowers" "paragogy"], :references ["f6/pattern-as-strategy" "f6/q-turnstile-a" "ai4ci/dual-layer-proof-patterns"], :sigils ["🤝/証"], :title "Proof as Social Process", :tone "theoretical"}, :id "f6/proof-as-social-process", :keywords ["proof" "social" "process" "coding" "context" "feedback" "quality" "structure" "heuristic" "Gowers" "paragogy"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["f6/pattern-as-strategy" "f6/q-turnstile-a" "ai4ci/dual-layer-proof-patterns"], :scores {}, :sigils ["🤝/証"], :source-path "library/f6/proof-as-social-process.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Proof as Social Process", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  A well-posed question constrains its answer. Agents that identify what they\n  don't know and formulate the right question are doing mathematics, not retrieval."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are designing agents that interact through mathematical question-answering,\n    following Kolmogorov's calculus of problems where Q⊢A means \"question Q is resolved\n    by answer A.\""} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      In Kolmogorov's framework, a problem is solved by a construction; a compound\n      problem (A ∧ B) is solved by solving both parts; a conditional problem (A → B)\n      is solved by a method transforming any solution of A into a solution of B."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      Most Q&A systems treat questions as retrieval queries and answers as information\n      dumps. This ignores the constructive content: a good question doesn't just ask\n      for information, it specifies what kind of construction would count as adequate.\n      An answer to the wrong question is not mathematics."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "      Design the Asker agent to generate questions that constrain their answers: each\n      question should specify what kind of construction (proof, example, counterexample,\n      algorithm) would resolve it. The Critic evaluates not just whether the answer is\n      correct, but whether it constitutes an adequate construction for the question posed.\n      Stack Exchange voting is the social proof system; the Artificial Stack Exchange\n      replaces social voting with agent-based evaluation."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      The Corneli (2014) thesis (§10.6) identifies problem posing as \"the essence of\n      pattern-making.\" The EPSRC LEAPQA proposal formalised this as Q⊢A. The SFI\n      proposal framed it as the Artificial Stock Market applied to knowledge. All three\n      point to the same insight: generating questions is harder and more valuable than\n      generating answers."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Implement question-type annotation in the Asker: proof-question, example-question, counterexample-question, algorithm-question.]\n    next[Evaluate Critic against human judgement of answer adequacy, not just correctness.]\n    next[Measure whether Asker-generated questions are more constraining (fewer adequate answers) than random SE questions.]"}], :conclusion "  A well-posed question constrains its answer. Agents that identify what they\n  don't know and formulate the right question are doing mathematics, not retrieval.", :directives {:audience "futon6 developers, agent designers", :factor "Keen investigation (dhammavicaya)", :keywords ["question" "answer" "Kolmogorov" "problem" "construction" "proof" "adequacy" "constraint" "posing"], :references ["f6/self-play-loop" "ai4ci/collective-intelligence-evaluation"], :sigils ["⊢/問"], :title "Q Turnstile A", :tone "theoretical"}, :id "f6/q-turnstile-a", :keywords ["question" "answer" "Kolmogorov" "problem" "construction" "proof" "adequacy" "constraint" "posing"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["f6/self-play-loop" "ai4ci/collective-intelligence-evaluation"], :scores {}, :sigils ["⊢/問"], :source-path "library/f6/q-turnstile-a.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Q Turnstile A", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Mathematical text introduces variables through scoped bindings (Let, Define,\n  Assume) that create nested contexts. Tracking these scope chains gives agents\n  awareness of what is \"in play\" at each point in a proof."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are building scope detection for mathematical NER, distinguishing\n    global entities (named theorems, definitions) from local bindings (variables\n    introduced for a specific proof or argument)."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      Mathematical reasoning relies on Let-binding chains: \"Let X be a topological\n      space. Let f: X → Y be continuous. Assume X is compact.\" Each binding\n      narrows the context and introduces variables that are meaningful only within\n      that scope."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      Scope detection via regex (7 patterns for Let/Define/Assume/Consider/where/\n      for-any/set-notation openers) captures the syntactic signal but not the\n      semantic nesting. A \"Let\" inside a proof inherits the scope of the theorem\n      statement. Arxiv papers have scope chains crossing section boundaries."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "      Implement in tiers. Tier 1 (classical, no GPU): regex scope-opener detection\n      with position tracking — enough for M-f6-ingest. Tier 2 (small model): classify\n      binding types (variable intro, function intro, assumption) and detect scope\n      closure. Tier 3 (LLM): full dependency graphs across proof/theorem/section\n      boundaries — needed for M-f6-arxiv."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      The Corneli (2014) thesis Appendix B notes that \"variables can be introduced as\n      examples, which can have either 'global' or 'local' significance\" (p.197).\n      The entity taxonomy (GlobalEntity vs LocalBinding) makes this distinction\n      computational. Scope awareness is what separates a term spotter from a\n      mathematical reasoner."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Integrate Tier 1 scope detection into superpod-job.py as Stage 5 of M-f6-ingest.]\n    next[Validate scope detection on physics.SE subset: expect >30% of answers contain Let/Define openers.]\n    next[Design Tier 2 binding classifier for post-ingest enrichment.]"}], :conclusion "  Mathematical text introduces variables through scoped bindings (Let, Define,\n  Assume) that create nested contexts. Tracking these scope chains gives agents\n  awareness of what is \"in play\" at each point in a proof.", :directives {:audience "futon6 developers, mathematical NLP researchers", :factor "Discernment (paññā)", :keywords ["scope" "variable" "binding" "Let" "Define" "Assume" "local" "global" "chain" "nesting"], :references ["f6/negative-space-duality" "f6/bootstrap-loop"], :sigils ["🔗/域"], :title "Scope Chain Tracking", :tone "architectural"}, :id "f6/scope-chain-tracking", :keywords ["scope" "variable" "binding" "Let" "Define" "Assume" "local" "global" "chain" "nesting"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["f6/negative-space-duality" "f6/bootstrap-loop"], :scores {}, :sigils ["🔗/域"], :source-path "library/f6/scope-chain-tracking.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Scope Chain Tracking", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Three agents — Asker, Answerer, Critic — form a self-play loop where each\n  iteration improves the knowledge graph and the agents' own performance."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are building the Artificial Stack Exchange: agents that ask, answer,\n    evaluate, and learn from mathematical questions using a shared knowledge graph."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      The Asker finds gaps in the knowledge graph (isolated nodes, missing inter-cluster\n      links, ungrounded pattern annotations). The Answerer uses graph context to construct\n      answers. The Critic evaluates QA pairs using the M-f6-eval scoring protocol."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      Self-play can degenerate: the Asker may generate trivial questions, the Answerer\n      may ignore graph context, the Critic may be unreliable, and graph updates may\n      introduce noise rather than signal. Without quality control, the loop amplifies\n      errors rather than correcting them."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "      Gate each iteration: Critic scores must exceed a threshold before graph updates\n      are committed. Track evaluation scores over iterations — if scores plateau or\n      decline, pause and diagnose. The Critic's evaluations should be periodically\n      calibrated against human judgement (spot-checks from M-f6-eval). The Asker\n      should be measured on question non-triviality, not just question volume."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      The Corneli (2014) thesis (§10.5) identified \"simulation work with artificial\n      agents\" as the key missing piece. The SFI Complexity Postdoc proposal modelled\n      this on the Artificial Stock Market: agents that trade create a market; agents\n      that ask and answer create a knowledge commons. The loop works only if the\n      commons improves — measurable via graph density, evaluation scores, and gap\n      reduction."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Implement Phase 1 of M-f6-agents: single-turn QA with graph context, baseline measurement.]\n    next[Define quality gates: minimum Critic score for graph update, minimum question novelty for Asker.]\n    next[Run 100-iteration pilot and measure graph growth (new relations, confidence scores, gap markers).]"}], :conclusion "  Three agents — Asker, Answerer, Critic — form a self-play loop where each\n  iteration improves the knowledge graph and the agents' own performance.", :directives {:audience "futon6 developers, agent designers", :factor "Persistence (viriya)", :keywords ["self-play" "agent" "asker" "answerer" "critic" "evaluate" "iterate" "improve" "graph" "update"], :references ["f6/q-turnstile-a" "f6/negative-space-duality" "ai4ci/collective-intelligence-evaluation"], :sigils ["♻️/遊"], :title "Self-Play Loop", :tone "architectural"}, :id "f6/self-play-loop", :keywords ["self-play" "agent" "asker" "answerer" "critic" "evaluate" "iterate" "improve" "graph" "update"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["f6/q-turnstile-a" "f6/negative-space-duality" "ai4ci/collective-intelligence-evaluation"], :scores {}, :sigils ["♻️/遊"], :source-path "library/f6/self-play-loop.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Self-Play Loop", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Stack Exchange is the learning stratum; Arxiv is the research stratum.\n  Bridge concepts — terms, patterns, and clusters that appear in both —\n  create Thurston's \"channels for communication between the strata.\""} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are connecting two corpora that represent different modes of\n    mathematical activity: people helping each other understand (SE) and people\n    creating new knowledge (Arxiv)."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      The same mathematical concepts appear in both SE questions and Arxiv proofs,\n      but in different registers: informal and pedagogical in SE, formal and terse\n      in Arxiv. The NER kernel and pattern tagger provide a shared vocabulary for\n      detecting these overlaps."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      Naive term matching will over-connect: \"group\" appears in both corpora but\n      with different senses and scopes. Embedding similarity alone may conflate\n      superficially similar but mathematically distinct concepts. The bridge must\n      be precise enough to be useful and loose enough to discover non-obvious\n      connections."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "      Define bridge concepts as entities that appear in both corpora with (a) shared\n      NER terms from the kernel, (b) shared pattern tags, AND (c) embedding similarity\n      above a threshold. Rank bridges by information gain: a bridge that connects\n      otherwise-disconnected clusters is more valuable than one that reinforces an\n      existing connection. Feed high-value bridges to the Asker as question seeds."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      Thurston (1990) called for \"the creation of channels for communication between\n      the strata.\" The Corneli (2014) thesis (§10.3) cited this as a goal for PlanetMath.\n      F6 realizes it computationally: the knowledge graph IS the channel, and bridge\n      concepts are the points where learning and research illuminate each other."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[After M-f6-arxiv pilot: count bridge concepts between SE and Arxiv graphs.]\n    next[Rank bridges by information gain and present top-20 to Asker for question generation.]\n    next[Validate that bridge-seeded questions produce higher-quality QA pairs than gap-seeded questions.]"}], :conclusion "  Stack Exchange is the learning stratum; Arxiv is the research stratum.\n  Bridge concepts — terms, patterns, and clusters that appear in both —\n  create Thurston's \"channels for communication between the strata.\"", :directives {:audience "futon6 developers, mathematical knowledge architects", :factor "Equanimity (upekkhā)", :keywords ["bridge" "stratum" "SE" "Arxiv" "learning" "research" "channel" "Thurston" "concept" "shared"], :references ["f6/bootstrap-loop" "f6/graph-enhanced-evaluation" "hdm/deep-storage-to-active-graph"], :sigils ["🌉/通"], :title "Stratum Bridge", :tone "architectural"}, :id "f6/stratum-bridge", :keywords ["bridge" "stratum" "SE" "Arxiv" "learning" "research" "channel" "Thurston" "concept" "shared"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["f6/bootstrap-loop" "f6/graph-enhanced-evaluation" "hdm/deep-storage-to-active-graph"], :scores {}, :sigils ["🌉/通"], :source-path "library/f6/stratum-bridge.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Stratum Bridge", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Define the failure impact boundary and the minimal rollback plan."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "A change or tool action could impact multiple subsystems or teams."} {:name "if", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "You want failures to remain localized and diagnosable without full rollback."} {:name "however", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "Without a declared blast radius, incident response defaults to broad freezes or guesswork."} {:name "then", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Capture a blast-radius record before risky actions, naming affected surfaces, rollback scope, and detection signals; enforcement is pending."} {:name "because", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "The template defines the blast-radius record shape needed for future enforcement.\n    - evidence: src/futon3/fulab/pattern_competence.clj - blast-radius-template matches event fields.\n    - evidence: src/futon3/fulab/pattern_competence.clj - blast-radius-template includes :blast/notes for rollback rationale.\n    - evidence-shape: {:session/id :string, :blast/surfaces [:string], :blast/rollback [:string], :blast/detectors [:string], :blast/risk :keyword, :blast/notes :string}"} {:name "next-steps", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Require blast-radius events for cross-futon changes; audit that the rollback scope matches actual artifacts."}], :conclusion "Define the failure impact boundary and the minimal rollback plan.", :directives {:sigils ["📎/化"], :title "Blast Radius Control"}, :id "fulab/blast-radius", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["📎/化"], :source-path "library/fulab/fulab-patterns.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Blast Radius Control", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Use manual records as a temporary proof trail while infrastructure catches up."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Before the full proof trail infrastructure exists, we need a way to simulate pattern-based work."} {:name "if", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "You want to dogfood the pattern architecture while building it."} {:name "however", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "XTDB persistence, MUSN enforcement, and check DSL aren't implemented yet."} {:name "then", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Maintain a changelog file (e.g., fulab-changelog.org) where each entry explicitly records: clocked-in pattern, pattern dependencies used, artifacts produced, and clock-out summary. This serves as a human-readable proof trail."} {:name "because", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "The changelog simulates the architecture and provides a migration path - entries can be backfilled into XTDB once the infrastructure exists.\n    - evidence: src/futon3/fulab/pattern_competence.clj - changelog-entry-template matches trail entry shape.\n    - evidence: src/futon3/fulab/pattern_competence.clj - changelog-entry-template includes :entry/session-id for traceability.\n    - evidence: fulab-changelog.org - active manual trail with clocked-in patterns and session notes.\n    - evidence-shape: {:changelog/file :string, :entry/date :string, :entry/session-id :string, :entry/pattern-id :string, :entry/deps [:string], :entry/artifacts [:string], :entry/summary :string}"} {:name "next-steps", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Add a fixed template per entry (clock-in, deps, artifacts, clock-out) and schedule a periodic backfill into XTDB once proof events land."} {:name "enables", :name-key "enables", :slug "enables", :text "[fulab/clock-in, fulab/clock-out, fulab/proof-commit]"}], :conclusion "Use manual records as a temporary proof trail while infrastructure catches up.", :directives {:sigils ["🌽/才"], :title "Changelog-as-Trail"}, :id "fulab/changelog-trail", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🌽/才"], :source-path "library/fulab/fulab-patterns.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Changelog-as-Trail", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Clock in first to establish the design-authorization anchor for this session."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "An agent is about to start a task that will change a codebase or knowledge base."} {:name "if", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "You want the agent's work to be traceable, auditable, and explicitly linked to the design glossary."} {:name "however", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "Without an explicit declaration, the link between agent actions and design patterns stays implicit and hard to verify later."} {:name "then", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Before substantive work starts, the agent emits a clock-in event that includes the primary pattern ID, intent description, and session context, declaring the design-authorization source for the session."} {:name "because", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "The clock-in event creates a non-optional anchor for the proof path, so later actions can be evaluated against the declared intent.\n    - evidence: src/f2/claude.clj - clock-in-event records :clock-in/session-id for audit linkage.\n    - evidence: src/f2/codex.clj - clock-in-event now includes :clock-in/session-id for audit linkage.\n    - evidence-shape: {:clock-in/pattern-id :string, :clock-in/intent :string, :clock-in/session-id :string, :clock-in/timestamp :inst, :clock-in/context :map}"} {:name "next-steps", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Check that every side-effecting event traces back to a prior clock-in; treat any side effect without clock-in as a violation."}], :conclusion "Clock in first to establish the design-authorization anchor for this session.", :directives {:sigils ["🌽/才"], :title "Agent Clock-In"}, :id "fulab/clock-in", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🌽/才"], :source-path "library/fulab/fulab-patterns.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Agent Clock-In", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Seal the proof path and outputs with an explicit end event."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "An agent finishes a unit of work and is ready to end the session with a checkable summary."} {:name "if", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "You want the session to end in a way that can be validated against the original intent, producing a sealable proof path."} {:name "however", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "Without a clear ending, the path is often incomplete (missing status, artifacts, dependency summary), making audit and replay difficult."} {:name "then", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Before ending the session, the agent emits a clock-out event including the primary pattern, full dependency trail summary, completion status, and list of produced or modified artifacts."} {:name "because", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "The clock-out event seals the proof path with the minimal complete data needed for audit, replay, and progress tracking.\n    - evidence: src/f2/codex.clj - clock-out-event records :clock-out/session-id for closure linkage.\n    - evidence: src/f2/claude.clj - clock-out-event records :clock-out/session-id for closure linkage.\n    - evidence: src/f2/codex.clj - tool/approved events include :tool/params from pending calls to drive artifact tracking.\n    - evidence: src/f2/claude.clj - tool/approved events include :tool/params from pending calls to drive artifact tracking.\n    - evidence-shape: {:event/session :string, :pattern/primary :string, :clock-out/session-id :string, :pattern/trail [:map], :session/status :keyword, :artifacts [:string], :docs-written [:map], :stats :map, :society-paper :any, :clock-out/timestamp :inst}"} {:name "next-steps", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Verify that the clock-out pattern trail can be reconstructed from the session's clock-in + pattern-dep events; confirm artifacts match the actual change set."}], :conclusion "Seal the proof path and outputs with an explicit end event.", :directives {:sigils ["🌽/才"], :title "Agent Clock-Out"}, :id "fulab/clock-out", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🌽/才"], :source-path "library/fulab/fulab-patterns.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Agent Clock-Out", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Create a traceable mapping between implementation patterns and devmap prototypes."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "FuLab patterns are implementation-level; devmap prototypes are design-level."} {:name "if", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "You want to track how implementation work advances the higher-level devmap."} 