trade-offs to improve decision quality and reduce churn.", :directives {:sigils ["🌏/化"], :title "Trade-off Record"}, :id "fulab/tradeoff-record", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🌏/化"], :source-path "library/fulab/fulab-patterns.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Trade-off Record", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Every agent operating in the FUTON stack must follow the agent contract:\n  clock in, observe before acting, attribute all changes, respect invariants,\n  and clock out with a proof trail."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Agents (human or AI) interact with futon systems. The contract\n    ensures that all agents produce auditable, attributable work that respects\n    system invariants. Agents that violate the contract produce unverifiable\n    changes."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "The applicability scope of the pattern (see substructure)."} {:name "AGENT-TYPES", :name-key "agent-types", :slug "agent-types", :text "      - Human agents: Follow contract via tools (CLI, IDE integration)\n      - AI agents: Follow contract via prompts and guardrails\n      - Automated agents: Follow contract via code structure"} {:name "CONTRACT-REQUIREMENTS", :name-key "contract-requirements", :slug "contract-requirements", :text "      1. CLOCK-IN/CLOCK-OUT:\n         - Every session explicitly starts and ends\n         - Session ID assigned at clock-in\n         - Duration and outcome recorded at clock-out\n\n      2. OBSERVE-BEFORE-ACT:\n         - Read state before proposing changes\n         - Log observations as evidence\n         - Never apply changes without observation phase\n\n      3. ATTRIBUTE-ALL-CHANGES:\n         - Every change linked to agent identity\n         - No anonymous mutations\n         - Changes reference the observation that motivated them\n\n      4. RESPECT-INVARIANTS:\n         - Check invariants before commit\n         - On violation: stop-the-line, do not force through\n         - Report invariant failures, do not hide them\n\n      5. PRODUCE-PROOF:\n         - Every committed change has evidence chain\n         - Proof path is replayable\n         - Failed attempts also logged (for learning)"} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "The structural tension above manifests in the following concrete shapes (see substructure)."} {:name "CONTRACT-VIOLATIONS", :name-key "contract-violations", :slug "contract-violations", :text "      - Anonymous change: Mutation without agent attribution\n      - Blind change: Mutation without observation\n      - Forced commit: Pushing through invariant violation\n      - Ghost session: Changes without clock-in/clock-out"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Operate an explicit move on the named axis (see substructure below); steward the pattern against the declared discipline."} {:name "ENFORCEMENT", :name-key "enforcement", :slug "enforcement", :text "      - Session middleware rejects unclocked changes\n      - Commit hooks verify agent attribution\n      - Invariant checks block non-compliant commits\n      - Audit logs flag contract violations"} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Unattributed changes are unauditable. Unobserved changes are reckless.\n    Forced commits corrupt the system. The contract ensures that every\n    agent, regardless of type, produces trustworthy work."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Implement contract enforcement middleware]\n    next[Add contract violation detection to PAR analysis]"}], :conclusion "  Every agent operating in the FUTON stack must follow the agent contract:\n  clock in, observe before acting, attribute all changes, respect invariants,\n  and clock out with a proof trail.", :directives {:audience "agent implementers, futon developers", :factor "Keen investigation (dhammavicaya)", :keywords ["agent" "behavior" "requirements" "clock" "observe" "commit" "attribute"], :references ["futon-theory/event-protocol" "futon-theory/proof-path" "futon-theory/stop-the-line"], :sigils ["👶/义"], :title "Agent Contract", :tone "analytic-foundational"}, :id "futon-theory/agent-contract", :keywords ["agent" "behavior" "requirements" "clock" "observe" "commit" "attribute"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["futon-theory/event-protocol" "futon-theory/proof-path" "futon-theory/stop-the-line"], :scores {}, :sigils ["👶/义"], :source-path "library/futon-theory/agent-contract.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Agent Contract", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "I2: Startup succeeds completely or fails loudly.\n  Startup succeeds completely or fails loudly. Rehydration loads all entities\n  or throws. No silent stubs, no partial loads. Failure reports exactly what\n  failed and why."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "A system that boots with partial data is worse than one that\n    refuses to boot. Partial state creates subtle bugs that surface hours\n    later, far from their cause. Integrity means: whole and consistent, or not."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "The applicability scope of the pattern (see substructure)."} {:name "REQUIREMENTS", :name-key "requirements", :slug "requirements", :text "      - Rehydration loads all entities or throws\n      - No silent stubs, no placeholder values\n      - No partial loads, no \"best effort\" recovery\n      - Failure reports: what entity, what field, what was expected, what found\n      - Relations verified to have valid endpoints"} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "The structural tension above manifests in the following concrete shapes (see substructure)."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "      Corrupt entity -> 500 ENTITY_CORRUPT (details: entity-id, field, error)\n      Dangling relation -> 500 DANGLING_REFERENCE (details: relation-id, missing-target)\n      Schema mismatch -> 500 SCHEMA_MISMATCH (details: entity-id, expected, found)"} {:name "ANTI-PATTERNS", :name-key "anti-patterns", :slug "anti-patterns", :text "      - Loading what we can, logging warnings for the rest\n      - Creating stub entities for missing references\n      - \"Recovering\" by skipping corrupt data\n      - Async background rehydration that might fail silently"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Operate an explicit move on the named axis (see substructure below); steward the pattern against the declared discipline."} {:name "ENFORCEMENT", :name-key "enforcement", :slug "enforcement", :text "      Layer 2 (core/rehydrate.clj) validates on load:\n      - Every entity must deserialize completely\n      - Every relation must reference existing entities\n      - Missing references -> 500 INTEGRITY_VIOLATION with details\n      - Startup blocked until all data validates"} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Hydration needs similar invariants to ingest. You wouldn't rip a CD that\n    didn't have proper 0's and 1's on it. The data must be whole to be trusted.\n    Partial data creates partial truth, which is worse than no data."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Implement blocking rehydration with validation in futon1a]\n    next[Add relation endpoint verification on startup]"}], :conclusion "I2: Startup succeeds completely or fails loudly.\n  Startup succeeds completely or fails loudly. Rehydration loads all entities\n  or throws. No silent stubs, no partial loads. Failure reports exactly what\n  failed and why.", :directives {:audience "futon developers, integrity layer maintainers", :factor "Keen investigation (dhammavicaya)", :keywords ["startup" "rehydrate" "load" "partial" "complete" "corrupt" "validate"], :references ["futon-theory/durability-first" "futon-theory/single-source-of-truth" "futon-theory/stop-the-line"], :sigils ["🌏/无"], :title "All-or-Nothing Integrity (Invariant 2)", :tone "analytic-foundational"}, :id "futon-theory/all-or-nothing", :keywords ["startup" "rehydrate" "load" "partial" "complete" "corrupt" "validate"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["futon-theory/durability-first" "futon-theory/single-source-of-truth" "futon-theory/stop-the-line"], :scores {}, :sigils ["🌏/无"], :source-path "library/futon-theory/all-or-nothing.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "All-or-Nothing Integrity (Invariant 2)", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  The Baldwin cycle is the engineered form of evolutionary learning:\n  explore (learn within constraints), assimilate (fix successful adaptations\n  into genotype), canalize (remove unnecessary freedom, make success cheap)."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Named after James Mark Baldwin's insight that learned behaviors\n    can become genetically fixed. In engineering: what starts as runtime\n    adaptation becomes compile-time structure. This is how systems accumulate\n    capability without losing auditability."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "The structural tension above manifests in the following concrete shapes (see substructure)."} {:name "ANTI-PATTERNS", :name-key "anti-patterns", :slug "anti-patterns", :text "      - Learning without assimilating (ghost capabilities, unreplayable)\n      - Assimilating without canalizing (complexity creep, unstable)\n      - Canalizing prematurely (lock-in before exploration complete)"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Operate an explicit move on the named axis (see substructure below); steward the pattern against the declared discipline."} {:name "CYCLE-OUTCOME", :name-key "cycle-outcome", :slug "cycle-outcome", :text "      Before: System reaches goal through search\n      After: System reaches goal structurally (no search needed)\n      Evidence: Captured in genotype + invariants + proof trail"} {:name "FUNCTIONAL-GAIN", :name-key "functional-gain", :slug "functional-gain", :text "      1. Local learning reduces global search cost (divide and conquer)\n      2. Assimilation converts exploration into structural capability (cumulative)\n      3. Canalization makes repeated success automatic (efficiency)"} {:name "PHASE 1", :name-key "phase 1", :slug "phase-1", :text "EXPLORATION (Learning)\n      Within fixed exotype constraints, allow phenotype plasticity.\n      - Search, adapt, try variants\n      - Measure outcomes against success criteria\n      - Preserve evidence of what was tried\n      Operations: OBSERVE, PROPOSE_CLAIM, experimental APPLY_CHANGE"} {:name "PHASE 2", :name-key "phase 2", :slug "phase-2", :text "ASSIMILATION (固化 / Fixation)\n      Compress successful learned behavior back into genotype.\n      - Make replayable, auditable internal representation\n      - Generate pattern, update rule table, extract replayable patch\n      - Attach evidence chain (what worked, why, proof)\n      Operations: VERIFY, PROOF_COMMIT (packaging success as artifact)"} {:name "PHASE 3", :name-key "phase 3", :slug "phase-3", :text "CANALIZATION (壓縮 / Compression)\n      Remove unnecessary degrees of freedom.\n      - Tighten invariants around success region\n      - Make success cheaper and more reliable\n      - What once required learning now works by default\n      Operations: Strengthen INVARIANT_CHECK, narrow exotype"} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Systems that only execute (no learning) cannot adapt. Systems that only\n    learn (no fixation) cannot accumulate. Systems that only fix (no compression)\n    become brittle. The Baldwin cycle integrates all three."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Implement Baldwin engine as reusable component for layer interfaces]\n    next[Define metrics for exploration cost vs. canalization benefit]"}], :conclusion "  The Baldwin cycle is the engineered form of evolutionary learning:\n  explore (learn within constraints), assimilate (fix successful adaptations\n  into genotype), canalize (remove unnecessary freedom, make success cheap).", :directives {:audience "futon developers, pattern theorists, stack architects", :factor "Keen investigation (dhammavicaya)", :keywords ["learn" "assimilate" "canalize" "explore" "compress" "evolve" "adapt" "fix"], :references ["futon-theory/four-types" "futon-theory/interface-loop" "futon-theory/proof-path" "futon-theory/retrospective-stability"], :sigils ["🔃/三"], :title "Baldwin Cycle", :tone "analytic-foundational"}, :id "futon-theory/baldwin-cycle", :keywords ["learn" "assimilate" "canalize" "explore" "compress" "evolve" "adapt" "fix"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["futon-theory/four-types" "futon-theory/interface-loop" "futon-theory/proof-path" "futon-theory/retrospective-stability"], :scores {}, :sigils ["🔃/三"], :source-path "library/futon-theory/baldwin-cycle.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Baldwin Cycle", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Use an explicit multi-channel handoff protocol with review gates and escalation rules so multi-agent work survives asynchrony instead of dissolving into duplicated effort and lost context."} {:name "CONTEXT", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "    When multiple agents work on related missions, they need coordination.\n    Without explicit handoff protocols, work is duplicated, reviews are\n    skipped, and context is lost between sessions."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    Multiple agents are assigned to a mission, or a mission requires\n    review by another agent."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Agents may be asynchronous (different sessions), have different\n    capabilities (Claude vs Codex), or use different communication\n    channels (Agency, git, files)."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Apply the ping-pong coordination protocol:\n\n    HANDOFF-CYCLE:\n    ```\n    1. Primary: Complete milestone, emit PUR\n    2. Primary: Commit + push + notify reviewer\n    3. Reviewer: Pull + review + emit PSR for review\n    4. Reviewer: Document findings (approve or request changes)\n    5. Reviewer: Emit PUR for review outcome\n    6. If changes needed: handoff back to primary\n    7. If approved: proceed to next milestone\n    ```\n\n    NOTIFICATION-CHANNELS (in priority order):\n    1. Agency page (sync, immediate response expected)\n    2. Agency bell (async, notification only)\n    3. Git commit message with [handoff: agent-id]\n    4. File-based message (docs/notes/AGENCY-CODEX-BRIEF.md pattern)\n    5. MUSN chat room\n\n    HANDOFF-MESSAGE-REQUIREMENTS:\n    - What was completed\n    - What needs review or action\n    - Where to find the changes (commit hash, file paths)\n    - Expected response (approve, review, implement)\n\n    ESCALATION:\n    - If blocked > 30 minutes: document blocker, notify human\n    - If no response > 1 hour: try alternate channel\n    - If channel broken: fall back to file-based communication"} {:name "AGENCY-INTEGRATION", :name-key "agency-integration", :slug "agency-integration", :text "      When Agency is available:\n      - Register with agent-id via WebSocket\n      - Respond to pages with invoke-fn\n      - Bell for async notifications\n      - Page for sync requests\n\n      When Agency unavailable:\n      - Use git-based ping-pong\n      - Leave messages in designated files\n      - Poll for changes"} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    - Explicit handoffs prevent work from falling through cracks\n    - Review gates ensure quality\n    - Multiple channels provide resilience\n    - Escalation prevents infinite waiting"} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Verify communication channel is working\n    - Establish handoff protocol with collaborating agent\n    - Document current milestone and next expected action\n\n@related futon-theory/agent-contract, futon-theory/mission-lifecycle"}], :conclusion "Use an explicit multi-channel handoff protocol with review gates and escalation rules so multi-agent work survives asynchrony instead of dissolving into duplicated effort and lost context.", :directives {:sigils ["🎎/人"], :title "Coordination Protocol (Multi-Agent Handoff)"}, :id "futon-theory/coordination-protocol", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🎎/人"], :source-path "library/futon-theory/coordination-protocol.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Coordination Protocol (Multi-Agent Handoff)", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "I2 (extended): Key counts are monotonically non-decreasing unless explicitly acknowledged deletion occurs.\n  Key counts must not drop unexpectedly. If lyrics count goes from 14 to 4,\n  that's an invariant violation requiring investigation. The counter-ratchet\n  detects regressions before they become silent data loss."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Data loss often manifests as count drops: entities that existed\n    no longer exist. The counter-ratchet maintains baselines for critical\n    counts and alerts when they decrease without explanation."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "The structural tension above manifests in the following concrete shapes (see substructure)."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "      Silent data loss -> Detected by count drop\n      Corrupt rehydration -> Detected by startup count check\n      Accidental deletion -> Detected before commit"} {:name "ANTI-PATTERNS", :name-key "anti-patterns", :slug "anti-patterns", :text "      - Ignoring count drops as \"probably fine\"\n      - Resetting baselines after every operation\n      - Only counting totals, not per-type\n      - Checking counts after commit (too late)"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Operate an explicit move on the named axis (see substructure below); steward the pattern against the declared discipline."} {:name "CRITICAL-COUNTS", :name-key "critical-counts", :slug "critical-counts", :text "      - Entity counts by type (songs, lyrics, relations)\n      - Relation counts by type\n      - User/penholder counts\n      - Lab session event counts"} {:name "DETECTION-FLOW", :name-key "detection-flow", :slug "detection-flow", :text "      1. Pre-operation: Record baseline counts\n      2. Operation: Execute change\n      3. Post-operation: Check counts against baselines\n      4. If drop detected: Check for valid deletion record\n      5. If no valid deletion: Stop-the-line, report violation"} {:name "IMPLEMENTATION", :name-key "implementation", :slug "implementation", :text "      `futon1/core/invariants.clj`: Count verification\n      `futon1/startup.clj`: Baseline establishment\n      `futon1/api/middleware.clj`: Pre/post operation checks"} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Data loss is often gradual and silent. 14 -> 13 -> 12 -> ... -> 4.\n    Each small loss seems like noise. The ratchet makes every drop visible\n    and requires explanation. No unexplained regressions."} {:name "MECHANISM", :name-key "mechanism", :slug "mechanism", :text "      1. Establish baselines for critical entity types\n      2. After each operation, compare current counts to baselines\n      3. If count drops without matching deletion record: VIOLATION\n      4. If count drops with valid deletion: Update baseline"} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Implement count baseline tracking in futon1a]\n    next[Add count verification to rehydration checks]"}], :conclusion "I2 (extended): Key counts are monotonically non-decreasing unless explicitly acknowledged deletion occurs.\n  Key counts must not drop unexpectedly. If lyrics count goes from 14 to 4,\n  that's an invariant violation requiring investigation. The counter-ratchet\n  detects regressions before they become silent data loss.", :directives {:audience "futon developers, invariant enforcers", :factor "Keen investigation (dhammavicaya)", :keywords ["count" "drop" "ratchet" "monotonic" "regression" "detection" "baseline"], :references ["futon-theory/all-or-nothing" "futon-theory/stop-the-line" "futon-theory/durability-first"], :sigils ["✌️/计"], :title "Counter-Ratchet (Invariant I2)", :tone "analytic-foundational"}, :id "futon-theory/counter-ratchet", :keywords ["count" "drop" "ratchet" "monotonic" "regression" "detection" "baseline"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["futon-theory/all-or-nothing" "futon-theory/stop-the-line" "futon-theory/durability-first"], :scores {}, :sigils ["✌️/计"], :source-path "library/futon-theory/counter-ratchet.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Counter-Ratchet (Invariant I2)", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "A laundering / self-certification failure is never killed by a single fix — it RELOCATES to a scarcer witness. Each fix that closes one shape exposes the next, harder-to-fake shape. So the defense is not one detector but a LADDER of progressively scarcer witnesses, terminating where the witness is too costly to forge (a witnessed real outcome / multi-witness certificate). Read each fix as \"the crime moved up a rung,\" not \"the crime is dead.\""} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You have just closed a way the system could certify itself or launder a fake win, and\n    it now looks honest."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      You fix a laundering / circularity / self-certification shape in a measurement or reward channel."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      The fix does not abolish the failure — it pushes it into a scarcer witness. Treating any single\n      fix as \"now it is honest\" reopens the same crime one level up: the cheaper witness is gone, but\n      a more expensive one is still forgeable, and the system will drift toward forging it."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "      Expect relocation. After each fix ask \"where did the crime go? — what is the next-scarcest\n      witness it can still hide behind?\" Build the ladder explicitly: each rung a scarcer, harder-to-\n      fake referent. Stop only where the witness is genuinely too expensive to fake — a witnessed real\n      outcome, a three-witness certificate. Treat each relocation as progress: it costs the launderer\n      more every rung."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      This is the Pudding-Prover rationale, derived from below: \"the proof is in the pudding\" means the\n      only un-fakeable witness is the realized outcome; everything cheaper can be gamed. So the\n      discipline is to keep raising the cost-to-fake until only the real thing passes — which is why a\n      reward channel must end at a witnessed-outcome gate (`[[aif/two-layer-calibration]]` L2,\n      pudding-G1), not at any cheaper internal check."} {:name "canonical-instance", :name-key "canonical-instance", :slug "canonical-instance", :text "      The FutonZero calibration arc, three rungs each forced by catching the one below: the constant\n      model's *state-blindness* exposed → made state-sensitive → the scaled model's\n      *increment-circularity* exposed → the realised's *lack of external grounding* exposed → next\n      witness is the outcome itself (pudding-G1). The forensic framing is `[[M-the-perfect-crime]]`;\n      the structural half is `[[aif/no-self-certification]]`. Distilled WM-pilot 2026-06-11."}], :conclusion "A laundering / self-certification failure is never killed by a single fix — it RELOCATES to a scarcer witness. Each fix that closes one shape exposes the next, harder-to-fake shape. So the defense is not one detector but a LADDER of progressively scarcer witnesses, terminating where the witness is too costly to forge (a witnessed real outcome / multi-witness certificate). Read each fix as \"the crime moved up a rung,\" not \"the crime is dead.\"", :directives {:audience "anyone hardening a measurement, reward, or certification against gaming", :keywords ["laundering" "self-certification" "witness" "ladder" "cost-to-fake" "pudding prover" "three-witness" "relocation" "scarcity"], :sigils ["↥/⌖"], :style "pattern", :title "The Crime Relocates to a Scarcer Witness (The Witness Ladder)", :tone "foundational"}, :id "futon-theory/crime-relocates-to-a-scarcer-witness", :keywords ["laundering" "self-certification" "witness" "ladder" "cost-to-fake" "pudding prover" "three-witness" "relocation" "scarcity"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["↥/⌖"], :source-path "library/futon-theory/crime-relocates-to-a-scarcer-witness.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "The Crime Relocates to a Scarcer Witness (The Witness Ladder)", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Programs are proofs of their specifications. The spec mediates future state\n  into present. In futon theory, every operation is a proof that the intended\n  state is achievable and achieved."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "The Curry-Howard correspondence reveals that types are theorems\n    and programs are proofs. Futon theory operationalizes this: specifications\n    are claims about future state, and proof paths demonstrate those claims."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Operate an explicit move on the named axis (see substructure below); steward the pattern against the declared discipline."} {:name "IMPLICATIONS", :name-key "implications", :slug "implications", :text "      - Untyped code = claims without proof (risky)\n      - Type errors = invariant violations (stop-the-line)\n      - Passing types = verified claims (commit allowed)"} {:name "MEDIATING-FUTURE-INTO-PRESENT", :name-key "mediating-future-into-present", :slug "mediating-future-into-present", :text "      1. Declare intent (what future state do we want?)\n      2. Write spec (formal claim about that state)\n      3. Implement (construct the proof)\n      4. Verify (check the proof is valid)\n      5. Commit (the future state becomes present)"} {:name "OPERATIONAL-INTERPRETATION", :name-key "operational-interpretation", :slug "operational-interpretation", :text "      - Spec: A claim about what the future state should be\n      - Program: A proof that the spec can be satisfied\n      - Execution: Verification that the proof is valid\n      - Result: The spec-mediated future state, now present"} {:name "SPEC-AS-CONTRACT", :name-key "spec-as-contract", :slug "spec-as-contract", :text "      When we write: \"Entity X will have property P after operation O\"\n      We are claiming: There exists a proof path from current state to (X has P)\n      The code is that proof. The test verifies the proof."} {:name "TYPE-SYSTEM-ANALOGY", :name-key "type-system-analogy", :slug "type-system-analogy", :text "      - Types = Invariants (what must hold)\n      - Functions = Operations (how to maintain invariants)\n      - Type checking = Invariant verification (before commit)\n      - Runtime = Execution (proof in action)"} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Without this correspondence, code is arbitrary text and specs are wishful\n    thinking. With it, code and specs are two views of the same truth. The\n    program proves the spec; the spec justifies the program."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Formalize spec language for futon1a invariants]\n    next[Connect type-level claims to runtime verification]"}], :conclusion "  Programs are proofs of their specifications. The spec mediates future state\n  into present. In futon theory, every operation is a proof that the intended\n  state is achievable and achieved.", :directives {:audience "futon developers, pattern theorists", :factor "Keen investigation (dhammavicaya)", :keywords ["spec" "proof" "program" "type" "mediate" "future" "present" "correspondence"], :references ["futon-theory/proof-path" "futon-theory/symbolic-geodesic" "futon-theory/retrospective-stability"], :sigils ["⛲️/双"], :title "Curry-Howard Operational Principle", :tone "analytic-foundational"}, :id "futon-theory/curry-howard-operational", :keywords ["spec" "proof" "program" "type" "mediate" "future" "present" "correspondence"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["futon-theory/proof-path" "futon-theory/symbolic-geodesic" "futon-theory/retrospective-stability"], :scores {}, :sigils ["⛲️/双"], :source-path "library/futon-theory/curry-howard-operational.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Curry-Howard Operational Principle", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "I0: What you save is what you get back.\n  Persistence is the zeroth invariant: what you save is what you get back.\n  Writes return success ONLY after durable storage confirms. No fire-and-forget."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Without reliable persistence, all higher-layer invariants are\n    meaningless. Durability is enforced via invariants (I0), not axioms.\n    The axioms (A1-A5) are operational; durability is structural."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "The applicability scope of the pattern (see substructure)."} {:name "REQUIREMENTS", :name-key "requirements", :slug "requirements", :text "      - Writes return success ONLY after durable storage confirms\n      - Reads return ONLY data that is durably stored\n      - No async-without-callback, no fire-and-forget\n      - All failures throw, no silent logging"} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "The structural tension above manifests in the following concrete shapes (see substructure)."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "      Storage unavailable -> 503 (service layer error)\n      Write timeout -> 503 (service layer error)\n      Corruption detected -> 500 (blocks startup)"} {:name "ANTI-PATTERNS", :name-key "anti-patterns", :slug "anti-patterns", :text "      - \"Write succeeded\" before confirmation (async leak)\n      - Catching write errors and returning partial success\n      - Optimistic cache writes without durable sync"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Operate an explicit move on the named axis (see substructure below); steward the pattern against the declared discipline."} {:name "ENFORCEMENT", :name-key "enforcement", :slug "enforcement", :text "      Layer 0 gates all writes:\n      - Synchronous confirmation before success return\n      - Success returns a durable proof token (e.g., tx-id)\n      - Failure throws with error context, never returns false"} {:name "DOMAIN-INSTANTIATION", :name-key "domain-instantiation", :slug "domain-instantiation", :text "      See `storage/durability-first` for concrete implementation patterns."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    A storage layer that can't reliably store is violating its reason for\n    existence. Layer 0 errors must surface as Layer 0 errors, not masked\n    by higher-layer failures."}], :conclusion "I0: What you save is what you get back.\n  Persistence is the zeroth invariant: what you save is what you get back.\n  Writes return success ONLY after durable storage confirms. No fire-and-forget.", :directives {:audience "futon developers, storage layer maintainers", :factor "Keen investigation (dhammavicaya)", :keywords ["persist" "save" "write" "durable" "storage" "confirm" "success"], :references ["futon-theory/proof-path" "futon-theory/error-hierarchy"], :sigils ["👭/本"], :title "Durability First (Invariant 0)", :tone "analytic-foundational"}, :id "futon-theory/durability-first", :keywords ["persist" "save" "write" "durable" "storage" "confirm" "success"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["futon-theory/proof-path" "futon-theory/error-hierarchy"], :scores {}, :sigils ["👭/本"], :source-path "library/futon-theory/durability-first.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Durability First (Invariant 0)", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "I3: Errors surface at the layer that caused them.\n  Errors surface at the layer that caused them. No penholder errors when the\n  real problem is durability. Each layer is a gate; lower-layer failures\n  prevent higher-layer code from running."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "When errors surface at the wrong layer, debugging takes hours\n    instead of minutes. An auth error that masks a storage failure sends you\n    hunting in the wrong codebase. Layer hierarchy ensures the true cause\n    surfaces first."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "The applicability scope of the pattern (see substructure)."} {:name "REQUIREMENTS", :name-key "requirements", :slug "requirements", :text "      - Each layer is a gate; failure stops propagation\n      - Lower layers run before higher layers\n      - Layer N errors must not be masked by Layer N+1 handling\n      - Error context includes: layer, operation, entity ID, expected vs actual"} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "The structural tension above manifests in the following concrete shapes (see substructure)."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "      Masked error: Auth failure hides storage failure -> bug takes hours to find\n      Wrong layer: 400 when 503 is correct -> client retries uselessly\n      Missing context: \"Error\" without layer/operation/id -> blind debugging"} {:name "ANTI-PATTERNS", :name-key "anti-patterns", :slug "anti-patterns", :text "      - Catching storage errors and returning auth errors\n      - Running all validations before checking storage health\n      - Generic error handlers that lose layer context\n      - \"Something went wrong\" without layer identification"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Operate an explicit move on the named axis (see substructure below); steward the pattern against the declared discipline."} {:name "ENFORCEMENT", :name-key "enforcement", :slug "enforcement", :text "      Middleware orders layer checks from 0 to 4:\n      1. Can we reach storage? (Layer 0)\n      2. Is identity valid? (Layer 1)\n      3. Is data integrity intact? (Layer 2)\n      4. Is requester authorized? (Layer 3)\n      5. Is input valid? (Layer 4)"} {:name "LAYER-ERROR-MAPPING", :name-key "layer-error-mapping", :slug "layer-error-mapping", :text "      Layer 0 (durability) -> 503 Service Unavailable\n      Layer 1 (identity) -> 409 Conflict\n      Layer 2 (integrity) -> 500 Internal Server Error\n      Layer 3 (auth) -> 403 Forbidden\n      Layer 4 (validation) -> 400 Bad Request"} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Debugging is O(layers * complexity) when errors surface at wrong layer,\n    O(1) when they surface at correct layer. The first clue should point to\n    the right file, not send you on a wild goose chase."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Implement layer-ordered middleware in futon1a]\n    next[Create error catalog mapping every error code to its layer]"}], :conclusion "I3: Errors surface at the layer that caused them.\n  Errors surface at the layer that caused them. No penholder errors when the\n  real problem is durability. Each layer is a gate; lower-layer failures\n  prevent higher-layer code from running.", :directives {:audience "futon developers, api layer maintainers", :factor "Keen investigation (dhammavicaya)", :keywords ["error" "layer" "surface" "mask" "propagate" "status" "http"], :references ["futon-theory/durability-first" "futon-theory/single-source-of-truth" "futon-theory/all-or-nothing" "futon-theory/rapid-debugging"], :sigils ["🎋/门"], :title "Error Hierarchy (Invariant 3)", :tone "analytic-foundational"}, :id "futon-theory/error-hierarchy", :keywords ["error" "layer" "surface" "mask" "propagate" "status" "http"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["futon-theory/durability-first" "futon-theory/single-source-of-truth" "futon-theory/all-or-nothing" "futon-theory/rapid-debugging"], :scores {}, :sigils ["🎋/门"], :source-path "library/futon-theory/error-hierarchy.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Error Hierarchy (Invariant 3)", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  The event protocol defines the canonical sequence of events in a futon\n  proof path: CLOCK_IN -> OBSERVE -> PROPOSE_CLAIM -> APPLY_CHANGE ->\n  VERIFY -> INVARIANT_CHECK -> PROOF_COMMIT -> CLOCK_OUT."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Events are the atoms of auditability. Each event type has defined\n    preconditions and postconditions. The protocol ensures every change follows\n    the same defensible path from intent to durable proof."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "The applicability scope of the pattern (see substructure)."} {:name "MINIMUM-VIABLE", :name-key "minimum-viable", :slug "minimum-viable", :text "      Not every session needs all events. A5 (Minimum Viable Events) allows:\n      - Read-only session: CLOCK_IN -> OBSERVE -> CLOCK_OUT\n      - Quick fix: CLOCK_IN -> OBSERVE -> APPLY -> VERIFY -> COMMIT -> CLOCK_OUT\n      The protocol is the maximum; subsets are valid for simpler operations."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Operate an explicit move on the named axis (see substructure below); steward the pattern against the declared discipline."} {:name "BRANCHING", :name-key "branching", :slug "branching", :text "      - On VERIFY failure: Return to OBSERVE, spawn correction path\n      - On INVARIANT failure: Return to OBSERVE, stop-the-line protocol\n      - Session can be abandoned at any point before PROOF_COMMIT"} {:name "EVENT-SEQUENCE", :name-key "event-sequence", :slug "event-sequence", :text "      1. CLOCK_IN: Session begins, agent declares identity and intent\n         - Precondition: None\n         - Postcondition: Session ID assigned, timestamp recorded\n\n      2. OBSERVE: Read state, gather evidence, build understanding\n         - Precondition: Clocked in\n         - Postcondition: Observations logged, state snapshot available\n\n      3. PROPOSE_CLAIM: Agent declares what will change and why\n         - Precondition: Observations made\n         - Postcondition: Claim recorded (not yet applied)\n\n      4. APPLY_CHANGE: Execute the proposed change\n         - Precondition: Claim proposed, invariants passing\n         - Postcondition: Change applied to staging area\n\n      5. VERIFY: Confirm change matches claim\n         - Precondition: Change applied\n         - Postcondition: Verification result recorded\n\n      6. INVARIANT_CHECK: All invariants must pass\n         - Precondition: Verification passed\n         - Postcondition: All invariants confirmed or violations identified\n\n      7. PROOF_COMMIT: Durable commit with evidence chain\n         - Precondition: All invariants passed\n         - Postcondition: Change durably committed, tx-id assigned\n\n      8. CLOCK_OUT: Session ends, proof path complete\n         - Precondition: Proof committed or session abandoned\n         - Postcondition: Session closed, duration recorded"} {:name "IMPLEMENTATION", :name-key "implementation", :slug "implementation", :text "      `futon3/lab/session.clj`: Session lifecycle events\n      `futon3/lab/ws.clj`: Event dispatch and logging\n      `futon1/core/`: Layer-specific event handling"} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Unstructured change is unauditable change. The protocol ensures that\n    every mutation has a traceable path. When something breaks, the event\n    log tells the story of how we got here."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Implement event logging for all protocol phases]\n    next[Add protocol visualization to session replay]"}], :conclusion "  The event protocol defines the canonical sequence of events in a futon\n  proof path: CLOCK_IN -> OBSERVE -> PROPOSE_CLAIM -> APPLY_CHANGE ->\n  VERIFY -> INVARIANT_CHECK -> PROOF_COMMIT -> CLOCK_OUT.", :directives {:audience "futon developers, agent implementers", :factor "Keen investigation (dhammavicaya)", :keywords ["clock-in" "clock-out" "observe" "propose" "apply" "verify" "commit" "session"], :references ["futon-theory/proof-path" "futon-theory/stop-the-line" "futon-theory/agent-contract"], :sigils ["🔃/令"], :title "Event Protocol", :tone "analytic-foundational"}, :id "futon-theory/event-protocol", :keywords ["clock-in" "clock-out" "observe" "propose" "apply" "verify" "commit" "session"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["futon-theory/proof-path" "futon-theory/stop-the-line" "futon-theory/agent-contract"], :scores {}, :sigils ["🔃/令"], :source-path "library/futon-theory/event-protocol.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Event Protocol", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Four interrelated types describe how structure becomes behavior becomes\n  interface becomes portable pattern: genotype (internal), phenotype (observable),\n  exotype (connectable), xenotype (transplantable)."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "In heterogeneous programming and evolutionary systems, these four\n    types represent different facets of the same transformable system. Each\n    provides a distinct lens for understanding how capability evolves."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Operate an explicit move on the named axis (see substructure below); steward the pattern against the declared discipline."} {:name "APPLICATION-TO-FUTON", :name-key "application-to-futon", :slug "application-to-futon", :text "      - genotype: Pattern definitions, DSL specs, rule configurations\n      - phenotype: System behavior, test results, API responses\n      - exotype: Layer interfaces (L0↔L1, L1↔L2), event protocol shapes\n      - xenotype: Baldwin cycle itself, applicable at any interface"} {:name "EXOTYPE (外顯子型)", :name-key "exotype (外顯子型)", :slug "exotype", :text "      Externally visible type/interface shape.\n      - Interface contracts, data formats, APIs\n      - Signal/control surfaces, I/O types, event protocols\n      - Not behavior itself, but the shape through which behavior connects\n      - What makes components composable"} {:name "GENOTYPE (基因型)", :name-key "genotype (基因型)", :slug "genotype", :text "      The replicable, persistable, versionable internal representation.\n      - Rule sets, programs, pattern DAGs, DSL fragments\n      - Parameter priors, replayable generators\n      - What can be stored and version-controlled\n      - The \"recipe\" that generates behavior"} {:name "PHENOTYPE (表型)", :name-key "phenotype (表型)", :slug "phenotype", :text "      Observable behavior and performance in environment.\n      - Actual outputs, interaction behavior\n      - Test metrics, performance curves\n      - What can be measured and compared\n      - The \"result\" of executing the genotype"} {:name "RELATIONSHIPS", :name-key "relationships", :slug "relationships", :text "      xenotype: Cross-domain \"generate—select\" skeleton\n          ↓ projects to\n      genotype: Concrete instantiation (language/DSL/rule table)\n          ↓ executes as\n      phenotype: Observable performance (measurable)\n          ↔ connects via\n      exotype: Interface shape (wireable)"} {:name "XENOTYPE (異種型)", :name-key "xenotype (異種型)", :slug "xenotype", :text "      Cross-domain portable control/generation structure.\n      - Meta-CA rules, variational search structures\n      - Categorical wiring, portable generator+selector combinations\n      - A transplantable higher-order embryo\n      - Can be projected onto different genotype forms"} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Conflating these types leads to confusion: optimizing behavior (phenotype)\n    without capturing it (genotype), or defining interfaces (exotype) that\n    don't compose across domains (missing xenotype). Separating them enables\n    systematic evolution with auditability."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Map existing futon layer interfaces to exotype specifications]\n    next[Identify xenotype structures that can be reused across layers]"}], :conclusion "  Four interrelated types describe how structure becomes behavior becomes\n  interface becomes portable pattern: genotype (internal), phenotype (observable),\n  exotype (connectable), xenotype (transplantable).", :directives {:audience "futon developers, pattern theorists, stack architects", :factor "Keen investigation (dhammavicaya)", :keywords ["genotype" "phenotype" "exotype" "xenotype" "representation" "behavior" "interface" "portable"], :references ["futon-theory/baldwin-cycle" "futon-theory/interface-loop" "futon-theory/proof-path"], :sigils ["〰️/四"], :title "Four Types Framework", :tone "analytic-foundational"}, :id "futon-theory/four-types", :keywords ["genotype" "phenotype" "exotype" "xenotype" "representation" "behavior" "interface" "portable"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["futon-theory/baldwin-cycle" "futon-theory/interface-loop" "futon-theory/proof-path"], :scores {}, :sigils ["〰️/四"], :source-path "library/futon-theory/four-types.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Four Types Framework", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Futonic logic specifies the vocabulary, composition rules, and recognition\n  loop that futon-theory patterns presuppose. It does not replace the patterns;\n  it makes explicit the symbolic operations they perform implicitly. The\n  central insight: composition (鹽) is not concatenation but the recognition\n  that latent potential (鹵) and stabilizing interface (皿) are available\n  under the same decomposition regime (部). The futonic loop — input, decompose,\n  articulate, compose, sense, regulate, act, evaluate — is the abstract form\n  of the PSR→PUR cycle and the derivation xenotype alike.\n\n  == 0. META-SYNTAX ==\n\n  ⿱ denotes top-bottom composition in glyph space. Futonically, it\n  corresponds to a binary articulation whose abstract form is (CAR . CDR):\n\n  (meta-syntax\n    (⿱\n      (role layout-operator)\n      (maps-to cons)\n      (explanation\n        \"⿱ denotes top-bottom composition in glyph space;\n         futonically, it corresponds to a binary articulation\n         whose abstract form is (CAR . CDR).\")))\n\n  This does NOT assert that all composition is binary. It asserts only: when\n  ⿱ appears, interpret it as a historically successful binary articulation,\n  abstractable as a cons pair. Triads, polycentric forms, and n-ary\n  decompositions operate under other 部.\n\n  == 1. VOCABULARY ==\n\n  Core symbols declared as roles, not definitions. Premature semantic collapse\n  is the vocabulary equivalent of forward-declaration debt (social/verify-\n  before-compose): fixing meaning before structure is verified produces\n  symbols that cannot compose.\n\n  (vocabulary\n    (象  (role configuration)\n         (note \"A structured whole that can be referenced without full\n                expansion. First-class, carryable, transformable.\"))\n    (部  (role decomposition-regime)\n         (note \"A chosen regime of articulation, not an ontological given.\n                Arity-variable: binary, triadic, polycentric.\"))\n    (咅  (role articulation)\n         (note \"The act of making a decomposition explicit. Not analysis\n                (which presumes correctness) but perception of structure\n                already present.\"))\n    (香  (role embodied-salience)\n         (note \"Cross-layer perception that signals without full\n                formalization. Agent-relative. Defeasible.\"))\n    (味  (role embodied-evaluation)\n         (note \"Selection by felt difference rather than proof. The\n                evaluative counterpart of 香.\"))\n    (鹽  (role generative-composition)\n         (note \"Composition that generates: not mere juxtaposition but\n                the recognition that potential and container are available\n                under the same 部.\"))\n    (鹵  (role latent-substrate)\n         (note \"Manipulable potential. What can be transformed if a 皿\n                is available to receive the result.\"))\n    (皿  (role stabilizing-container)\n         (note \"Interface, vessel, boundary. What receives and stabilizes\n                the product of transformation.\"))\n    (間  (role honest-interval)\n         (note \"The structured space between components, perceived\n                without premature filling. 間 is not emptiness —\n                it is the topological fact that an interval exists\n                and has properties that must be read, not generated.\n                Hallucinating content into 間 produces false salience\n                and brittle composition.\"))\n    (関  (role gate-relation)\n         (note \"Barrier, gate, typed connection. 関 is the structural\n                fact that components are related through constraints,\n                not mere adjacency. A gate pipeline is a chain of 関;\n                a core.logic relation is 関 made evaluable; a typed\n                port (mission-interface-signature) is 関 at a boundary.\n                関 formalizes what 間 perceives: the interval has\n                structure, and that structure can be traversed or\n                blocked. 間 reads the gap; 関 names the gate.\n                Together (無門関): the gate that dissolves when\n                honestly perceived — tension discovery as navigation.\"))\n    (既  (role earned-past)\n         (note \"Urðr: that-which-has-come-to-pass. Not mere temporal\n                before, but the load-bearing past — closure-evidence,\n                HEAD-as-escrow, the proof-path's earned record.\n                Citeable, structurally available to the present, and\n                rewritable only with traces. (See futon-theory/wyrd.)\"))\n    (化  (role becoming-event)\n         (note \"Verðandi: in-process-of-happening, the present\n                participle of weorþan ('to come to pass'). The live\n                writer-action, the consent gate firing, the loop\n                presently composing. Distinct from 未知: 未知 is the\n                epistemic three-valuedness of in-flight state, while\n                化 is the temporal becoming itself. Every 化 emits\n                (應 . 債).\"))\n    (應  (role modal-obligation)\n         (note \"Skuld (modal face): the 'ought / shall / should'\n                voice. Cognate with English shall/should via Germanic\n                *skul-. 應 is what the present says about what the\n                future must do; not yet a debt-record but a modal\n                claim. Sorries are stated in the voice of 應.\"))\n    (債  (role substantive-debt)\n         (note \"Skuld (substantive face): debt incurred. Every act\n                of 鹽 (composition) emits 債 alongside salt-pay\n                (worth, from salarium). sorrys.edn is the registry\n                of 債 at proof-path granularity; mission scope-remaining\n                tracks 債 at mission granularity. 債 outstanding in\n                regime A may be 鹽 generative in regime B — this\n                trans-regime survival is wyrd-salience\n                (futon-theory/wyrd).\"))\n    (未知 (role non-equilibrium-interval)\n         (note \"Actionable but not predictable. Three-valued logic:\n                the system is neither in its prior state nor in its\n                successor state.\"))\n    (非死非活 (role non-terminal-state)\n         (note \"System is responsive but not self-sustaining. Equivalent\n                to 未知 at the lifecycle level.\"))\n    (🔮  (role regulator)\n         (note \"Capacity to act within 未知 while modulating action\n                amplitude. Components: 能 (capacity) + 捨 (setting-down).\")))\n\n  == 2. COMPOSITIONAL STRUCTURE ==\n\n  2.1 象 and 部:\n\n  (象\n    (status first-class)\n    (properties (carryable transformable))\n    (note \"A structured whole that can be referenced without full expansion.\"))\n\n  (部\n    (function bounded-decomposition)\n    (arity-variable true)\n    (examples (binary triadic polycentric))\n    (note \"A chosen regime of articulation, not an ontological given.\"))\n\n  2.2 鹽 as composition:\n\n  (鹽\n    (structure (⿱ 鹵 皿))\n    (abstract-form (cons 鹵 皿))\n    (interpretation\n      (鹵 (latent manipulable potential))\n      (皿 (interface container stabilizer)))\n    (constraint\n      \"Generativity requires both terms to be available under the same 部.\"))\n\n  2.3 Salience as the appearance of 鹽:\n\n  (compositional-salience\n    (depends-on 鹽)\n    (definition\n      \"Agent-relative perception that a (鹵 . 皿) decomposition\n       is presently actionable.\"))\n\n  (A7-precondition\n    (if (perceives-間 agent interval))\n    (then (A7 may proceed: test for 鹵 and 皿))\n    (else (composition is premature: the interval has not\n           been honestly observed)))\n\n  (A7\n    (precondition A7-precondition)\n    (if (exists (鹽 (⿱ 鹵 皿))))\n    (and (perceivable 鹵) (perceivable 皿))\n    (then (salience emerges))\n    (else (generativity remains inert)))\n\n  2.4 香 and 味:\n\n  (香\n    (role cross-layer-perception)\n    (function \"Signals salience without full formalization.\"))\n\n  (味\n    (role embodied-selection)\n    (function \"Evaluates articulations by felt difference rather than proof.\"))\n\n  2.5 🔮 and 捨:\n\n  (🔮\n    (components (能 捨))\n    (function regulator)\n    (definition\n      \"Capacity to act within 未知 while modulating action amplitude.\"))\n\n  (捨\n    (operation set-down)\n    (precondition (has 🔮))\n    (effect\n      \"Reduce deployed capacity to limit harm while preserving\n       reversibility.\"))\n\n  2.6 未知 and 非死非活:\n\n  (未知\n    (state non-equilibrium)\n    (logic three-valued)\n    (properties (actionable not-predictable)))\n\n  (非死非活\n    (equivalence 未知)\n    (note \"System is responsive but not self-sustaining.\"))\n\n  2.7 既 / 化 / 應 / 債 — The Three-Time Decomposition:\n\n  The three Norns (Urðr, Verðandi, Skuld) constitute a three-time\n  projection of every present moment. See futon-theory/wyrd for\n  the etymological grounding (wyrd ← weorþan ← PIE *wert-) and\n  the Skuld≈SORRY identification.\n\n  (three-time-decomposition\n    (frame (Norns Urðr Verðandi Skuld))\n    (glyphs (既 化 (應 . 債)))\n    (note \"Decomposes the present moment into already-completed,\n           presently-becoming, and owed-to-the-future aspects.\n           Not a temporal sequence but a simultaneous three-face\n           projection of every loop turn. The Buddhist 三時\n           (過去/現在/未來) is too neutral here; it loses Skuld's\n           owed character, collapsing future-as-debt into\n           future-as-next.\"))\n\n  (Skuld-pair\n    (structure (⿱ 應 債))\n    (interpretation\n      (應 (modal voice of obligation: ought, shall))\n      (債 (substantive record of debt incurred)))\n    (constraint \"Premature collapse to 應 alone is anxiety\n                 (debt voiced but unrecorded); to 債 alone is\n                 bookkeeping (debt without standing modal force).\n                 Both faces are required.\"))\n\n  (three-time-rule\n    (each (loop-turn t))\n    (projects-onto\n      (既 (closure-evidence-available-at t))\n      (化 (composition-presently-firing-at t))\n      ((應 . 債) (obligations-emitted-by-firing)))\n    (note \"No turn is wholly any one face. Collapse to 既 alone\n           is nostalgia (citing past with no present act); to 化\n           alone is amnesia (acting without grounding); to (應 . 債)\n           alone is anxiety/accounting without grounding or act.\"))\n\n  2.8 鹽 / 債 — The Composition-Debt Ledger:\n\n  Composition has two sides: what it pays (salt, salarium, worth)\n  and what it owes (Skuld, 債, shall). 鹽 (existing §2.2) is the\n  generative side. 債 is the dual obligation side. The pair forms\n  the ledger every loop turn must keep.\n\n  (compositional-ledger\n    (form (⿱ 鹽 債))\n    (credit-side\n      (worth \"salt-money: the salt-pay\")\n      (provenance \"Roman salarium → English salary → 'worth one's salt'\"))\n    (debit-side\n      (debt 債)\n      (provenance \"Germanic *skul- → Old Norse Skuld → English shall/should\"))\n    (rule\n      (every-鹽 emits (worth . 債))\n      (note \"No composition completes its bookkeeping in a single\n             部 — the 債 outstanding in regime A may be the 鹽\n             generative in regime B. This trans-regime survival\n             IS wyrd-salience (futon-theory/wyrd).\"))\n    (cross-linguistic-triangulation\n      (Latin salarium)\n      (Chinese (鹽 債))\n      (Old-English (wyrd weorþan Skuld-via-Norse))\n      (note \"Three independent linguistic registers triangulate\n             the same ledger structure. The triangulation is itself\n             a wyrd-survival test passing: when the same structure\n             is recovered from three unrelated source languages,\n             it is not an artifact of any one language's idioms.\")))\n\n  (ledger-anti-collapse\n    (anti-pattern \"Collapse the ledger to net.\")\n    (reason \"Net hides structure: 鹽 in regime A and 債 in regime\n             B do not sum to zero — they are the wyrd-salient\n             configuration that the present must continue to turn\n             (vertere) between regimes to keep legible. Net-bookkeeping\n             is the 'mere X' move applied to the ledger itself.\"))\n\n  == 3. THE FUTONIC LOOP ==\n\n  (futonic-loop\n    (input 象)\n    (choose 部)\n    (articulate 咅)\n    (if (forms (鹽 (⿱ 鹵 皿))))\n    (and (sense 香) (regulate 🔮))\n    (then\n      (act-within 未知)\n      (evaluate 味))\n    (else\n      (apply 捨)))\n\n  3.1 Three-Time Projection of the Loop:\n\n  Each loop turn projects onto all three Nornic faces simultaneously.\n  The loop above describes the becoming-firing (化); the three-time\n  projection makes explicit what authorises it (既) and what it\n  emits (應 . 債).\n\n  (loop-three-time-projection\n    (at-every-turn t\n      (既-at-t\n        (proof-path-history-before-t)\n        (earned-evidence-citeable-at-t)\n        (HEAD-as-escrow-at-t))\n      (化-at-t\n        (the-rule-itself §3-futonic-loop)\n        (presently-firing 咅)\n        (presently-composing 鹽))\n      ((應 . 債)-at-t\n        (sorries-registered-during-t)\n        (follow-on-modal-commitments)\n        (discharge-due-by τ))))\n\n  (loop-and-ledger\n    (every-step (or 鹽 捨))\n    (emits ledger-entry\n           (credit (or 香 味-confirms))\n           (debit (應 . 債)))\n    (note \"The futonic loop is a Nornic engine: it inherits 既 as\n           authorisation, composes in 化, emits (應 . 債) as\n           consequence. The else-branch (捨) is explicit handling\n           of obligation without further commitment — the modal\n           應 'we ought not act' applied to limit 債 accumulation.\"))\n\n  == 4. GROUNDING IN ACTUAL USAGE ==\n\n  Every symbol in the vocabulary has been used (implicitly or explicitly)\n  across futon missions. This section makes those uses explicit. The logic\n  is practical because we have been practising it.\n\n  4.1 象 — Configuration\n\n  | Context | 象 instance | Evidence |\n  | Wiring diagram | coordination-exotype.edn | A structured whole: 6 inputs, 6 outputs, 10 components, 30 edges. Referenceable as \":coordination-exotype\" without expansion. |\n  | Flexiarg pattern | mandatory-psr.flexiarg | A structured whole: conclusion, context, IF/HOWEVER/THEN/BECAUSE. Carryable by pattern-id. |\n  | Proof path | gate pipeline output | A structured whole: linked PSR→PUR→PAR chain. Transformable (L1 scans it for tensions). |\n  | Session | Claude Code session | A structured whole: context, tools, history. Compresses (compaction) but remains referenceable. |\n\n  4.2 部 — Decomposition Regime\n\n  | Context | 部 instance | Evidence |\n  | Three-futon split | timescale decomposition | 部 = timescale boundaries (social/task/glacial). Not chosen but perceived (symbolic-individuation). |\n  | Gate pipeline | six-gate decomposition | 部 = the six gates G5→G0. Binary at each gate (pass/reject), hexadic in composition. |\n  | Derivation xenotype | six-step decomposition | 部 = IDENTIFY→MAP→DERIVE→ARGUE→VERIFY→INSTANTIATE. Sequential, not arbitrary. |\n  | Baldwin cycle | triadic decomposition | 部 = NAMING→SELECTION→CANALIZATION. Three phases, not two or four. |\n\n  4.3 咅 — Articulation\n\n  | Context | 咅 instance | Evidence |\n  | PSR | pattern selection | The agent makes a decomposition explicit: \"I will use this pattern for this task.\" |\n  | ARGUMENT.flexiarg | claim composition | Each S/R/T/C section articulates: makes a claim's structure visible. |\n  | Mission scoping | scope in/out | The mission author articulates: what's inside, what's outside. |\n  | Git commit | change articulation | The commit message articulates: what changed and why. |\n\n  4.4 鹽 — Generative Composition (鹵 + 皿)\n\n  | Context | 鹵 (potential) | 皿 (container) | 鹽 (composition) |\n  | Gate pipeline | Pattern library (potential approaches) | Gate shapes (Malli schemas) | Validated proof-path (generative: produces evidence) |\n  | L1 canonicalizer | Observed tensions (potential patterns) | Library format (flexiarg structure) | New pattern in library (generative: constrains future work) |\n  | Social exotype | Agency/realtime patterns (potential requirements) | Wiring diagram format (edn structure) | Validated social-exotype.edn (generative: enables implementation) |\n  | Session | Prior knowledge + context | PSR/PUR protocol (recording structure) | Auditable session (generative: feeds glacial loop) |\n\n  4.5 香 — Embodied Salience\n\n  | Context | 香 instance | Evidence |\n  | Pattern search | futon3a notions/compass | Signals \"this pattern might apply\" without proving it. ANN + GloVe similarity, not logical implication. |\n  | Hotword matching | patterns-index.tsv hotwords | \"deliver\" fires on \"delivery-receipt\" — salience signal, not identification. |\n  | Tension scanning | L1-observe structural-irritation | \"This gap-PSR has appeared 3 times\" — cross-layer perception that something recurs, without naming what. |\n  | Git archaeology | commit pattern recognition | \"These 5 commits all fix forward-declarations\" — perceiving a pattern before it has a name. |\n\n  4.6 味 — Embodied Evaluation\n\n  | Context | 味 instance | Evidence |\n  | PUR | pattern use record | Evaluates by outcome (prediction-error), not by proof. \"Did using this pattern help?\" is 味, not formal verification. |\n  | Mana bins | sospeso confidence bins | p ∈ {0.3, 0.6, 0.8, 0.95} — discrete bins to \"reduce self-deception.\" Evaluation by felt confidence, not computed probability. |\n  | ct/mission.clj | 8/8 checks pass | Evaluation by structural test, not semantic proof. The diagram is well-formed; whether it's the right diagram is 味. |\n  | Code review | Codex review comments | \"This could be more defensive\" — evaluative judgement, not provable claim. |\n\n  4.7 🔮 — Regulator (能 + 捨)\n\n  | Context | 能 (capacity) | 捨 (setting-down) | 🔮 (regulation) |\n  | Sospeso protocol | Confidence p · C spent on action | (1-p) · C donated to pool + blast radius note | Proceed at lower confidence with bounded harm |\n  | Gate rejection | Pipeline can process the request | Gate rejects with typed error + gate attribution | Fail fast, preserve reversibility |\n  | Mission parking | Team could continue the subsystem | :active → :blocked with recorded blocker | Explicit exit over silent abandonment |\n  | Context compaction | LLM has N tokens of context | Older context compressed to summary | Continue operating with reduced but sufficient state |\n\n  4.8 未知 / 非死非活\n\n  | Context | Instance | Evidence |\n  | Abandoned missions | Forum: 8 commits, no exit, no :blocked | Neither done (:done) nor explicitly blocked (:blocked). Responsive to git log but not self-sustaining. |\n  | Sospeso interval | Between action and verification | The action has been taken (at p < 0.95) but outcome is not yet verified. System is in 未知. |\n  | futon3c pre-implementation | ARGUMENT written, exotype validated, no code | The social loop is specified but not instantiated. Non-equilibrium: the argument creates obligations the code hasn't satisfied. |\n  | Context boundary | Between session compaction and new turn | The LLM has lost detailed context but retains summary. Actionable but not fully predictable. |\n\n  4.9 間 — Honest Interval\n\n  | Context | 間 instance | Evidence |\n  | Tension discovery | gap between devmap component and mission inventory | Tension browser reads 間: \"peripheral-gauntlet/C-arena has no mission.\" The gap is structural, not empty — it has a devmap, a gate position, dependencies. Reading it honestly (not filling it) produces the correct next action. |\n  | futon1→futon1a porting | gap between declared invariants and implementations | \"entity-lifecycle and referential-integrity were not ported.\" 間 perceived too late: the gap was filled with the assumption \"it was ported\" — false salience from hallucinated content. |\n  | A7-precondition | interval between 鹵 (potential) and 皿 (container) | Before composing, perceive whether the interval actually exists. A pattern library and a recording protocol may be adjacent without composing — 間 is the honest observation that composition hasn't happened yet. |\n  | Code→docs hypergraph | gap between computed links and persisted links | futon4's finder strategies compute links in Emacs hash tables. 間 is the honest observation that the links are not persisted — the gap between working model and durable fact. |\n\n  4.10 関 — Gate / Relation\n\n  | Context | 関 instance | Evidence |\n  | Peripheral gauntlet | G0→G7 gate pipeline | Each gate is a 関: a typed barrier with acceptance criteria. C-arena (Gate 0) must pass before C-umwelt (Gate 1) can begin. The chain of 関 IS the peripheral gauntlet. |\n  | core.logic relations | `coverso`, `componento`, `invarianto` | Each pldb/db-rel is a 関: a typed connection between facts. `(coverso mid comp-id)` IS the gate between \"component exists\" and \"component is addressed.\" |\n  | mission-interface-signature | typed input/output ports | Every port is a 関 at a boundary: what the system exposes, typed, constrained. A box with no typed ports cannot participate in composition — missing 関. |\n  | Hyperedge endpoints | `devmap:X`, `component:X/Y`, `mission:Z` | Each endpoint is a 関 connecting the hyperedge to the entities it relates. Queryable: \"find all 関 that touch component C-arena.\" |\n  | Tension→path trace | claim → mission → evidence → var → source | The full path is a chain of 関: each step is a typed gate. When all gates pass (can be traversed), the claim is grounded. When a gate blocks, that's a tension. |\n\n  4.11 間 + 関 → 無門関 (The Gateless Gate)\n\n  Tension discovery is 間 + 関 composed: read the gap honestly (間), then\n  traverse the gate structure (関) to find what the gap implies. The \"gateless\n  gate\" is not the absence of gates — it is the perception that the gate\n  dissolves when the interval is honestly read. The tension \"C-arena has no\n  mission\" IS the gate: traversing it (proposing the mission, tracing the\n  path) is how it opens. The barrier and the path are the same structure\n  seen from different sides of 間.\n\n  In operational terms: `query-uncovered-components` (core.logic) reads 間\n  relationally; `trace-tension-path` (futon3c) traverses the chain of 関\n  from tension to source. The composition of these two operations IS the\n  self-representing stack's discovery loop — and it should be invocable\n  on demand, not performed ad hoc.\n\n  4.12 既 — Earned Past (Urðr)\n\n  | Context | 既 instance | Evidence |\n  | Proof path | committed PSR→PUR→PAR chain | The chain is 既: citeable as authority, extensible by future loops, rewritable only with explicit trace |\n  | HEAD-as-escrow | git HEAD as held-truth | HEAD is 既 in the most literal sense: what has come to pass, held as the present's grounding |\n  | Closure evidence | mission-close artifact | Closure freezes a 化 into citeable 既; the closure record is the artifact through which future loops cite the closed mission |\n  | Library canonicalization | promoted flexiarg | Promotion turns a candidate-pattern into 既: subsequent loops cite it as ground rather than candidate |\n  | Frozen tape (piano roll) | append-only PSR/PUR/PAR log | The roll IS the Urðr-surface: what cannot be un-done, only added to |\n\n  4.13 化 — Becoming Event (Verðandi)\n\n  | Context | 化 instance | Evidence |\n  | Live writer-action | VSATARCS-writer presently firing | The act of composition is 化: not yet 既, not yet emitting 債, but in the act |\n  | Consent gate firing | gate evaluating an action class | The firing moment IS 化: not the gate's config (genotype) nor its outcome (phenotype), but the becoming-event |\n  | PSR being authored | agent presently choosing a pattern | The choosing is 化; the recorded choice is 既; the verification obligation is (應 . 債) |\n  | Loop turn | one execution of the futonic loop | Each turn IS 化 for the duration of its firing; afterward it has become 既 |\n  | M-live-geometric-stack | the live geometric surface | The Verðandi-surface of the futon stack: live (T, ∇, Δ, drift) |\n\n  4.14 應 / 債 — Owed Obligation (Skuld)\n\n  | Context | 應 instance | 債 instance | Evidence |\n  | sorrys.edn | \"we should X\" voice in entry text | typed debt record with :kind | 應 is the modal voice; 債 is the structural debt the registry tracks |\n  | Mission scoping | \"this mission shall deliver Y\" | uncompleted scoped commitments | 應 in scope language; 債 in scope-remaining accounting |\n  | Pattern next-steps | next[…] entries | unfulfilled pattern-promises | Modal commitments (應) become structural debts (債) if not discharged |\n  | Wiring obligations | invariant statement | broken invariant under load | 應 declares what should hold; 債 is what actually breaks |\n  | sorrys.edn :kind | :prototyping-forward vs :technical-debt | distinct debt-classes | 債 typed by :kind; collapse to one class hides regime distinctions |\n\n  == 5. THE FUTONIC LOOP IN PRACTICE ==\n\n  The abstract loop (§3) maps onto three concrete cycles:\n\n  5.1 PSR→PUR cycle (per-task):\n\n  (futonic-loop\n    (input   象  := task request entering the gate pipeline)\n    (choose  部  := select decomposition: which gate, which timescale)\n    (articulate 咅 := write PSR: choose a pattern, commit to an approach)\n    (if (forms 鹽) := pattern (鹵) + evidence shape (皿) compose)\n    (and (sense 香) := during execution, perceive whether it's working)\n    (and (regulate 🔮) := stay within budget, reject if budget exceeded)\n    (then\n      (act-within 未知 := execute under uncertainty)\n      (evaluate 味 := write PUR: evaluate outcome by felt difference))\n    (else\n      (apply 捨 := reject at gate, park mission, write PAR)))\n\n  5.2 Derivation xenotype (per-mission):\n\n  (futonic-loop\n    (input   象  := domain to be derived: \"social coordination\")\n    (choose  部  := IDENTIFY the decomposition regime: git archaeology,\n                    pattern audit, devmap review)\n    (articulate 咅 := MAP + DERIVE: articulate patterns and requirements\n                    from the evidence)\n    (if (forms 鹽) := ARGUE: requirements (鹵) + argument structure (皿)\n                    compose into a valid case)\n    (and (sense 香) := during argument construction, perceive convergences)\n    (and (regulate 🔮) := VERIFY: check invariants, validate diagrams)\n    (then\n      (act-within 未知 := INSTANTIATE: build the code)\n      (evaluate 味 := tests pass, 8/8 checks, round-trip proof))\n    (else\n      (apply 捨 := park mission, record blocker, explicit exit)))\n\n  5.3 Glacial loop (per-library-evolution):\n\n  (futonic-loop\n    (input   象  := accumulated proof paths from many tasks)\n    (choose  部  := L1-observe: which tension criterion to scan?\n                    structural-irritation / pre-symbolic-pressure /\n                    trans-situational-reappearance)\n    (articulate 咅 := name the tension: a recurring pattern emerges)\n    (if (forms 鹽) := tension observation (鹵) + flexiarg format (皿)\n                    compose into a candidate pattern)\n    (and (sense 香) := does this tension appear across contexts?)\n    (and (regulate 🔮) := does it meet the canonicalization threshold?\n                    frequency >= 3, contexts >= 2)\n    (then\n      (act-within 未知 := write new flexiarg to library)\n      (evaluate 味 := subsequent G3 accepts the new pattern))\n    (else\n      (apply 捨 := tension does not meet threshold, record but do not\n                    canonicalize)))\n\n  == 6. RELATIONSHIP TO EXISTING PATTERNS ==\n\n  | This specification | Existing pattern | Relationship |\n  | 象 as first-class configuration | symbolic-geodesic | 象 IS the structured whole that geodesics traverse |\n  | 部 as decomposition regime | symbolic-individuation | 部 IS the regime under which compound symbols individuate |\n  | 鹽 = (⿱ 鹵 皿) | symbolic-individuation §THE-FUTONIC-VIEW | Same structural analysis, now formalized as composition rule |\n  | 香 as cross-layer perception | structural-tension-as-observation | 香 IS the pre-symbolic pressure before tension is named |\n  | 味 as embodied evaluation | retroactive-canonicalization | 味 IS the selection criterion in the Baldwin cycle |\n  | 🔮 = (能 . 捨) | sospeso protocol (futon5/AGENTS.md) | 🔮 IS the regulator that the sospeso protocol implements |\n  | 未知 / 非死非活 | explicit-exit-over-abandonment | 非死非活 IS the zombie-mission state that the pattern diagnoses |\n  | 間 as honest-interval | structural-tension-as-observation | 間 IS the perception that a gap has structure, before naming what fills it |\n  | 関 as gate-relation | mission-interface-signature | 関 IS the typed boundary that mission ports formalize; core.logic relations are evaluable 関 |\n  | 間 + 関 = 無門関 | tension discovery loop (M-self-representing-stack) | The discovery loop IS 間-reading composed with 関-traversal: perceive gap, navigate gate chain |\n  | futonic-loop | proof-path event-protocol | The loop IS the abstract form of CLOCK_IN → ... → CLOCK_OUT |\n  | 既 / 化 / 應-債 | futon-theory/wyrd (the three Norns) | The three-time decomposition IS the Nornic frame; wyrd grounds the modality, futonic-logic formalises the projection |\n  | 鹽 / 債 ledger | sorrys.edn (registry) + futon-theory/wyrd | sorrys.edn IS the 債 side of the ledger; the 鹽/債 pair is the structural fact wyrd names |\n\n  == 7. AXIOM A7: COMPOSITIONAL SALIENCE ==\n\n  The existing axioms (A1-A5) are operational. A7 is perceptual:\n\n  (A7-precondition\n    (if (perceives-間 agent interval))\n    (then (A7 may proceed: test for 鹵 and 皿))\n    (else (composition is premature: the interval has not\n           been honestly observed)))\n\n  (A7\n    (precondition A7-precondition)\n    (if (exists (鹽 (⿱ 鹵 皿))))\n    (and (perceivable 鹵) (perceivable 皿))\n    (then (salience emerges))\n    (else (generativity remains inert)))\n\n  In operational terms: a pattern library (鹵) and a recording protocol (皿)\n  may both exist without generating anything — until an agent perceives that\n  they compose under the current decomposition regime (部). Salience is the\n  moment of that perception. The gate pipeline does not create salience;\n  it provides the 皿 that makes 鹵's potential actionable. The agent's PSR\n  is the 咅 that recognizes the composition.\n\n  This axiom explains why Agency/Forum/Drawbridge failed: the components\n  existed (鹵 was present) but the recording protocol did not (皿 was absent\n  — no typed evidence shapes, no proof-path store). Without 皿, composition\n  could not form. Without composition, salience could not emerge. Without\n  salience, the agents could not perceive when their coordination was or was\n  not working. The result was 非死非活: systems responsive but not self-\n  sustaining, eventually abandoned without exit.\n\n  == 8. BOUNDARY RULE: 味→未@0 ==\n\n  When evaluation approaches a boundary, it collapses into uncertainty.\n  The correct response is containment, not force.\n\n  (rule-card 味→未@0\n    (when (approach boundary))\n    (then\n      (collapse (味 → 未))\n      (bias-toward (戒 圍 包)))\n    (therefore\n      (prefer (small reversible moves))\n      (avoid (irreversible commits))))\n\n  Vocabulary for the bias targets:\n  - 戒 (jiè): precept, restraint — the discipline of not-acting\n  - 圍 (wéi): enclosure, surround — contain the blast radius\n  - 包 (bāo): wrap, contain — package the uncertainty for later\n\n  This rule governs the transition from the futonic loop's \"evaluate\"\n  phase to the \"else\" branch. When 味 (embodied evaluation) reaches the\n  edge of what can be evaluated with confidence, the system does not\n  push through — it recognizes it has entered 未知 (non-equilibrium)\n  and shifts to containment mode.\n\n  Grounding in practice:\n\n  | Context | Boundary | 味→未 collapse | 戒/圍/包 response |\n  | Sospeso | p drops below 0.95 | Evaluation becomes uncertain | Rescope-to-safety, blast radius note, reversible action only |\n  | Gate rejection | G3 can't find pattern | Evaluation of approach is impossible | Typed rejection with gate attribution; don't execute |\n  | Mission parking | Subsystem hits blocking failure | Evaluation of progress becomes 非死非活 | :active → :blocked with recorded blocker; don't abandon silently |\n  | L1 canonicalizer | Tension below threshold | Evaluation of pattern candidacy is premature | Record but do not canonicalize; wait for more evidence |\n  | Context compaction | Context window approaches limit | Full evaluation of history is no longer possible | Compress to summary; preserve actionable structure, release detail |\n\n  The rule card is the futonic dual of the sospeso protocol. The sospeso\n  protocol is operational (confidence bins, mana accounting, blast radius\n  notes). The rule card is structural: it says WHEN the transition happens\n  (味 approaches boundary) and WHAT the bias should be (戒/圍/包), without\n  prescribing HOW (that's domain-specific).\n\n  A7 says: composition requires perceiving both 鹵 and 皿.\n  味→未@0 says: when you can no longer perceive clearly, stop composing\n  and start containing. A7 opens the door; 味→未@0 knows when to close it."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Extend the vocabulary as new symbols emerge from practice — this specification is open, not closed]\n    next[Verify that A7 is consistent with A1-A5 (no contradiction, complementary scope)]\n    next[Test whether the futonic loop can serve as a template for AGENTS.md instructions]\n    next[Connect to futon3a search: 香 operations map to notions/compass similarity queries]"}], :conclusion "  Futonic logic specifies the vocabulary, composition rules, and recognition\n  loop that futon-theory patterns presuppose. It does not replace the patterns;\n  it makes explicit the symbolic operations they perform implicitly. The\n  central insight: composition (鹽) is not concatenation but the recognition\n  that latent potential (鹵) and stabilizing interface (皿) are available\n  under the same decomposition regime (部). The futonic loop — input, decompose,\n  articulate, compose, sense, regulate, act, evaluate — is the abstract form\n  of the PSR→PUR cycle and the derivation xenotype alike.\n\n  == 0. META-SYNTAX ==\n\n  ⿱ denotes top-bottom composition in glyph space. Futonically, it\n  corresponds to a binary articulation whose abstract form is (CAR . CDR):\n\n  (meta-syntax\n    (⿱\n      (role layout-operator)\n      (maps-to cons)\n      (explanation\n        \"⿱ denotes top-bottom composition in glyph space;\n         futonically, it corresponds to a binary articulation\n         whose abstract form is (CAR . CDR).\")))\n\n  This does NOT assert that all composition is binary. It asserts only: when\n  ⿱ appears, interpret it as a historically successful binary articulation,\n  abstractable as a cons pair. Triads, polycentric forms, and n-ary\n  decompositions operate under other 部.\n\n  == 1. VOCABULARY ==\n\n  Core symbols declared as roles, not definitions. Premature semantic collapse\n  is the vocabulary equivalent of forward-declaration debt (social/verify-\n  before-compose): fixing meaning before structure is verified produces\n  symbols that cannot compose.\n\n  (vocabulary\n    (象  (role configuration)\n         (note \"A structured whole that can be referenced without full\n                expansion. First-class, carryable, transformable.\"))\n    (部  (role decomposition-regime)\n         (note \"A chosen regime of articulation, not an ontological given.\n                Arity-variable: binary, triadic, polycentric.\"))\n    (咅  (role articulation)\n         (note \"The act of making a decomposition explicit. Not analysis\n                (which presumes correctness) but perception of structure\n                already present.\"))\n    (香  (role embodied-salience)\n         (note \"Cross-layer perception that signals without full\n                formalization. Agent-relative. Defeasible.\"))\n    (味  (role embodied-evaluation)\n         (note \"Selection by felt difference rather than proof. The\n                evaluative counterpart of 香.\"))\n    (鹽  (role generative-composition)\n         (note \"Composition that generates: not mere juxtaposition but\n                the recognition that potential and container are available\n                under the same 部.\"))\n    (鹵  (role latent-substrate)\n         (note \"Manipulable potential. What can be transformed if a 皿\n                is available to receive the result.\"))\n    (皿  (role stabilizing-container)\n         (note \"Interface, vessel, boundary. What receives and stabilizes\n                the product of transformation.\"))\n    (間  (role honest-interval)\n         (note \"The structured space between components, perceived\n                without premature filling. 間 is not emptiness —\n                it is the topological fact that an interval exists\n                and has properties that must be read, not generated.\n                Hallucinating content into 間 produces false salience\n                and brittle composition.\"))\n    (関  (role gate-relation)\n         (note \"Barrier, gate, typed connection. 関 is the structural\n                fact that components are related through constraints,\n                not mere adjacency. A gate pipeline is a chain of 関;\n                a core.logic relation is 関 made evaluable; a typed\n                port (mission-interface-signature) is 関 at a boundary.\n                関 formalizes what 間 perceives: the interval has\n                structure, and that structure can be traversed or\n                blocked. 間 reads the gap; 関 names the gate.\n                Together (無門関): the gate that dissolves when\n                honestly perceived — tension discovery as navigation.\"))\n    (既  (role earned-past)\n         (note \"Urðr: that-which-has-come-to-pass. Not mere temporal\n                before, but the load-bearing past — closure-evidence,\n                HEAD-as-escrow, the proof-path's earned record.\n                Citeable, structurally available to the present, and\n                rewritable only with traces. (See futon-theory/wyrd.)\"))\n    (化  (role becoming-event)\n         (note \"Verðandi: in-process-of-happening, the present\n                participle of weorþan ('to come to pass'). The live\n                writer-action, the consent gate firing, the loop\n                presently composing. Distinct from 未知: 未知 is the\n                epistemic three-valuedness of in-flight state, while\n                化 is the temporal becoming itself. Every 化 emits\n                (應 . 債).\"))\n    (應  (role modal-obligation)\n         (note \"Skuld (modal face): the 'ought / shall / should'\n                voice. Cognate with English shall/should via Germanic\n                *skul-. 應 is what the present says about what the\n                future must do; not yet a debt-record but a modal\n                claim. Sorries are stated in the voice of 應.\"))\n    (債  (role substantive-debt)\n         (note \"Skuld (substantive face): debt incurred. Every act\n                of 鹽 (composition) emits 債 alongside salt-pay\n                (worth, from salarium). sorrys.edn is the registry\n                of 債 at proof-path granularity; mission scope-remaining\n                tracks 債 at mission granularity. 債 outstanding in\n                regime A may be 鹽 generative in regime B — this\n                trans-regime survival is wyrd-salience\n                (futon-theory/wyrd).\"))\n    (未知 (role non-equilibrium-interval)\n         (note \"Actionable but not predictable. Three-valued logic:\n                the system is neither in its prior state nor in its\n                successor state.\"))\n    (非死非活 (role non-terminal-state)\n         (note \"System is responsive but not self-sustaining. Equivalent\n                to 未知 at the lifecycle level.\"))\n    (🔮  (role regulator)\n         (note \"Capacity to act within 未知 while modulating action\n                amplitude. Components: 能 (capacity) + 捨 (setting-down).\")))\n\n  == 2. COMPOSITIONAL STRUCTURE ==\n\n  2.1 象 and 部:\n\n  (象\n    (status first-class)\n    (properties (carryable transformable))\n    (note \"A structured whole that can be referenced without full expansion.\"))\n\n  (部\n    (function bounded-decomposition)\n    (arity-variable true)\n    (examples (binary triadic polycentric))\n    (note \"A chosen regime of articulation, not an ontological given.\"))\n\n  2.2 鹽 as composition:\n\n  (鹽\n    (structure (⿱ 鹵 皿))\n    (abstract-form (cons 鹵 皿))\n    (interpretation\n      (鹵 (latent manipulable potential))\n      (皿 (interface container stabilizer)))\n    (constraint\n      \"Generativity requires both terms to be available under the same 部.\"))\n\n  2.3 Salience as the appearance of 鹽:\n\n  (compositional-salience\n    (depends-on 鹽)\n    (definition\n      \"Agent-relative perception that a (鹵 . 皿) decomposition\n       is presently actionable.\"))\n\n  (A7-precondition\n    (if (perceives-間 agent interval))\n    (then (A7 may proceed: test for 鹵 and 皿))\n    (else (composition is premature: the interval has not\n           been honestly observed)))\n\n  (A7\n    (precondition A7-precondition)\n    (if (exists (鹽 (⿱ 鹵 皿))))\n    (and (perceivable 鹵) (perceivable 皿))\n    (then (salience emerges))\n    (else (generativity remains inert)))\n\n  2.4 香 and 味:\n\n  (香\n    (role cross-layer-perception)\n    (function \"Signals salience without full formalization.\"))\n\n  (味\n    (role embodied-selection)\n    (function \"Evaluates articulations by felt difference rather than proof.\"))\n\n  2.5 🔮 and 捨:\n\n  (🔮\n    (components (能 捨))\n    (function regulator)\n    (definition\n      \"Capacity to act within 未知 while modulating action amplitude.\"))\n\n  (捨\n    (operation set-down)\n    (precondition (has 🔮))\n    (effect\n      \"Reduce deployed capacity to limit harm while preserving\n       reversibility.\"))\n\n  2.6 未知 and 非死非活:\n\n  (未知\n    (state non-equilibrium)\n    (logic three-valued)\n    (properties (actionable not-predictable)))\n\n  (非死非活\n    (equivalence 未知)\n    (note \"System is responsive but not self-sustaining.\"))\n\n  2.7 既 / 化 / 應 / 債 — The Three-Time Decomposition:\n\n  The three Norns (Urðr, Verðandi, Skuld) constitute a three-time\n  projection of every present moment. See futon-theory/wyrd for\n  the etymological grounding (wyrd ← weorþan ← PIE *wert-) and\n  the Skuld≈SORRY identification.\n\n  (three-time-decomposition\n    (frame (Norns Urðr Verðandi Skuld))\n    (glyphs (既 化 (應 . 債)))\n    (note \"Decomposes the present moment into already-completed,\n           presently-becoming, and owed-to-the-future aspects.\n           Not a temporal sequence but a simultaneous three-face\n           projection of every loop turn. The Buddhist 三時\n           (過去/現在/未來) is too neutral here; it loses Skuld's\n           owed character, collapsing future-as-debt into\n           future-as-next.\"))\n\n  (Skuld-pair\n    (structure (⿱ 應 債))\n    (interpretation\n      (應 (modal voice of obligation: ought, shall))\n      (債 (substantive record of debt incurred)))\n    (constraint \"Premature collapse to 應 alone is anxiety\n                 (debt voiced but unrecorded); to 債 alone is\n                 bookkeeping (debt without standing modal force).\n                 Both faces are required.\"))\n\n  (three-time-rule\n    (each (loop-turn t))\n    (projects-onto\n      (既 (closure-evidence-available-at t))\n      (化 (composition-presently-firing-at t))\n      ((應 . 債) (obligations-emitted-by-firing)))\n    (note \"No turn is wholly any one face. Collapse to 既 alone\n           is nostalgia (citing past with no present act); to 化\n           alone is amnesia (acting without grounding); to (應 . 債)\n           alone is anxiety/accounting without grounding or act.\"))\n\n  2.8 鹽 / 債 — The Composition-Debt Ledger:\n\n  Composition has two sides: what it pays (salt, salarium, worth)\n  and what it owes (Skuld, 債, shall). 鹽 (existing §2.2) is the\n  generative side. 債 is the dual obligation side. The pair forms\n  the ledger every loop turn must keep.\n\n  (compositional-ledger\n    (form (⿱ 鹽 債))\n    (credit-side\n      (worth \"salt-money: the salt-pay\")\n      (provenance \"Roman salarium → English salary → 'worth one's salt'\"))\n    (debit-side\n      (debt 債)\n      (provenance \"Germanic *skul- → Old Norse Skuld → English shall/should\"))\n    (rule\n      (every-鹽 emits (worth . 債))\n      (note \"No composition completes its bookkeeping in a single\n             部 — the 債 outstanding in regime A may be the 鹽\n             generative in regime B. This trans-regime survival\n             IS wyrd-salience (futon-theory/wyrd).\"))\n    (cross-linguistic-triangulation\n      (Latin salarium)\n      (Chinese (鹽 債))\n      (Old-English (wyrd weorþan Skuld-via-Norse))\n      (note \"Three independent linguistic registers triangulate\n             the same ledger structure. The triangulation is itself\n             a wyrd-survival test passing: when the same structure\n             is recovered from three unrelated source languages,\n             it is not an artifact of any one language's idioms.\")))\n\n  (ledger-anti-collapse\n    (anti-pattern \"Collapse the ledger to net.\")\n    (reason \"Net hides structure: 鹽 in regime A and 債 in regime\n             B do not sum to zero — they are the wyrd-salient\n             configuration that the present must continue to turn\n             (vertere) between regimes to keep legible. Net-bookkeeping\n             is the 'mere X' move applied to the ledger itself.\"))\n\n  == 3. THE FUTONIC LOOP ==\n\n  (futonic-loop\n    (input 象)\n    (choose 部)\n    (articulate 咅)\n    (if (forms (鹽 (⿱ 鹵 皿))))\n    (and (sense 香) (regulate 🔮))\n    (then\n      (act-within 未知)\n      (evaluate 味))\n    (else\n      (apply 捨)))\n\n  3.1 Three-Time Projection of the Loop:\n\n  Each loop turn projects onto all three Nornic faces simultaneously.\n  The loop above describes the becoming-firing (化); the three-time\n  projection makes explicit what authorises it (既) and what it\n  emits (應 . 債).\n\n  (loop-three-time-projection\n    (at-every-turn t\n      (既-at-t\n        (proof-path-history-before-t)\n        (earned-evidence-citeable-at-t)\n        (HEAD-as-escrow-at-t))\n      (化-at-t\n        (the-rule-itself §3-futonic-loop)\n        (presently-firing 咅)\n        (presently-composing 鹽))\n      ((應 . 債)-at-t\n        (sorries-registered-during-t)\n        (follow-on-modal-commitments)\n        (discharge-due-by τ))))\n\n  (loop-and-ledger\n    (every-step (or 鹽 捨))\n    (emits ledger-entry\n           (credit (or 香 味-confirms))\n           (debit (應 . 債)))\n    (note \"The futonic loop is a Nornic engine: it inherits 既 as\n           authorisation, composes in 化, emits (應 . 債) as\n           consequence. The else-branch (捨) is explicit handling\n           of obligation without further commitment — the modal\n           應 'we ought not act' applied to limit 債 accumulation.\"))\n\n  == 4. GROUNDING IN ACTUAL USAGE ==\n\n  Every symbol in the vocabulary has been used (implicitly or explicitly)\n  across futon missions. This section makes those uses explicit. The logic\n  is practical because we have been practising it.\n\n  4.1 象 — Configuration\n\n  | Context | 象 instance | Evidence |\n  | Wiring diagram | coordination-exotype.edn | A structured whole: 6 inputs, 6 outputs, 10 components, 30 edges. Referenceable as \":coordination-exotype\" without expansion. |\n  | Flexiarg pattern | mandatory-psr.flexiarg | A structured whole: conclusion, context, IF/HOWEVER/THEN/BECAUSE. Carryable by pattern-id. |\n  | Proof path | gate pipeline output | A structured whole: linked PSR→PUR→PAR chain. Transformable (L1 scans it for tensions). |\n  | Session | Claude Code session | A structured whole: context, tools, history. Compresses (compaction) but remains referenceable. |\n\n  4.2 部 — Decomposition Regime\n\n  | Context | 部 instance | Evidence |\n  | Three-futon split | timescale decomposition | 部 = timescale boundaries (social/task/glacial). Not chosen but perceived (symbolic-individuation). |\n  | Gate pipeline | six-gate decomposition | 部 = the six gates G5→G0. Binary at each gate (pass/reject), hexadic in composition. |\n  | Derivation xenotype | six-step decomposition | 部 = IDENTIFY→MAP→DERIVE→ARGUE→VERIFY→INSTANTIATE. Sequential, not arbitrary. |\n  | Baldwin cycle | triadic decomposition | 部 = NAMING→SELECTION→CANALIZATION. Three phases, not two or four. |\n\n  4.3 咅 — Articulation\n\n  | Context | 咅 instance | Evidence |\n  | PSR | pattern selection | The agent makes a decomposition explicit: \"I will use this pattern for this task.\" |\n  | ARGUMENT.flexiarg | claim composition | Each S/R/T/C section articulates: makes a claim's structure visible. |\n  | Mission scoping | scope in/out | The mission author articulates: what's inside, what's outside. |\n  | Git commit | change articulation | The commit message articulates: what changed and why. |\n\n  4.4 鹽 — Generative Composition (鹵 + 皿)\n\n  | Context | 鹵 (potential) | 皿 (container) | 鹽 (composition) |\n  | Gate pipeline | Pattern library (potential approaches) | Gate shapes (Malli schemas) | Validated proof-path (generative: produces evidence) |\n  | L1 canonicalizer | Observed tensions (potential patterns) | Library format (flexiarg structure) | New pattern in library (generative: constrains future work) |\n  | Social exotype | Agency/realtime patterns (potential requirements) | Wiring diagram format (edn structure) | Validated social-exotype.edn (generative: enables implementation) |\n  | Session | Prior knowledge + context | PSR/PUR protocol (recording structure) | Auditable session (generative: feeds glacial loop) |\n\n  4.5 香 — Embodied Salience\n\n  | Context | 香 instance | Evidence |\n  | Pattern search | futon3a notions/compass | Signals \"this pattern might apply\" without proving it. ANN + GloVe similarity, not logical implication. |\n  | Hotword matching | patterns-index.tsv hotwords | \"deliver\" fires on \"delivery-receipt\" — salience signal, not identification. |\n  | Tension scanning | L1-observe structural-irritation | \"This gap-PSR has appeared 3 times\" — cross-layer perception that something recurs, without naming what. |\n  | Git archaeology | commit pattern recognition | \"These 5 commits all fix forward-declarations\" — perceiving a pattern before it has a name. |\n\n  4.6 味 — Embodied Evaluation\n\n  | Context | 味 instance | Evidence |\n  | PUR | pattern use record | Evaluates by outcome (prediction-error), not by proof. \"Did using this pattern help?\" is 味, not formal verification. |\n  | Mana bins | sospeso confidence bins | p ∈ {0.3, 0.6, 0.8, 0.95} — discrete bins to \"reduce self-deception.\" Evaluation by felt confidence, not computed probability. |\n  | ct/mission.clj | 8/8 checks pass | Evaluation by structural test, not semantic proof. The diagram is well-formed; whether it's the right diagram is 味. |\n  | Code review | Codex review comments | \"This could be more defensive\" — evaluative judgement, not provable claim. |\n\n  4.7 🔮 — Regulator (能 + 捨)\n\n  | Context | 能 (capacity) | 捨 (setting-down) | 🔮 (regulation) |\n  | Sospeso protocol | Confidence p · C spent on action | (1-p) · C donated to pool + blast radius note | Proceed at lower confidence with bounded harm |\n  | Gate rejection | Pipeline can process the request | Gate rejects with typed error + gate attribution | Fail fast, preserve reversibility |\n  | Mission parking | Team could continue the subsystem | :active → :blocked with recorded blocker | Explicit exit over silent abandonment |\n  | Context compaction | LLM has N tokens of context | Older context compressed to summary | Continue operating with reduced but sufficient state |\n\n  4.8 未知 / 非死非活\n\n  | Context | Instance | Evidence |\n  | Abandoned missions | Forum: 8 commits, no exit, no :blocked | Neither done (:done) nor explicitly blocked (:blocked). Responsive to git log but not self-sustaining. |\n  | Sospeso interval | Between action and verification | The action has been taken (at p < 0.95) but outcome is not yet verified. System is in 未知. |\n  | futon3c pre-implementation | ARGUMENT written, exotype validated, no code | The social loop is specified but not instantiated. Non-equilibrium: the argument creates obligations the code hasn't satisfied. |\n  | Context boundary | Between session compaction and new turn | The LLM has lost detailed context but retains summary. Actionable but not fully predictable. |\n\n  4.9 間 — Honest Interval\n\n  | Context | 間 instance | Evidence |\n  | Tension discovery | gap between devmap component and mission inventory | Tension browser reads 間: \"peripheral-gauntlet/C-arena has no mission.\" The gap is structural, not empty — it has a devmap, a gate position, dependencies. Reading it honestly (not filling it) produces the correct next action. |\n  | futon1→futon1a porting | gap between declared invariants and implementations | \"entity-lifecycle and referential-integrity were not ported.\" 間 perceived too late: the gap was filled with the assumption \"it was ported\" — false salience from hallucinated content. |\n  | A7-precondition | interval between 鹵 (potential) and 皿 (container) | Before composing, perceive whether the interval actually exists. A pattern library and a recording protocol may be adjacent without composing — 間 is the honest observation that composition hasn't happened yet. |\n  | Code→docs hypergraph | gap between computed links and persisted links | futon4's finder strategies compute links in Emacs hash tables. 間 is the honest observation that the links are not persisted — the gap between working model and durable fact. |\n\n  4.10 関 — Gate / Relation\n\n  | Context | 関 instance | Evidence |\n  | Peripheral gauntlet | G0→G7 gate pipeline | Each gate is a 関: a typed barrier with acceptance criteria. C-arena (Gate 0) must pass before C-umwelt (Gate 1) can begin. The chain of 関 IS the peripheral gauntlet. |\n  | core.logic relations | `coverso`, `componento`, `invarianto` | Each pldb/db-rel is a 関: a typed connection between facts. `(coverso mid comp-id)` IS the gate between \"component exists\" and \"component is addressed.\" |\n  | mission-interface-signature | typed input/output ports | Every port is a 関 at a boundary: what the system exposes, typed, constrained. A box with no typed ports cannot participate in composition — missing 関. |\n  | Hyperedge endpoints | `devmap:X`, `component:X/Y`, `mission:Z` | Each endpoint is a 関 connecting the hyperedge to the entities it relates. Queryable: \"find all 関 that touch component C-arena.\" |\n  | Tension→path trace | claim → mission → evidence → var → source | The full path is a chain of 関: each step is a typed gate. When all gates pass (can be traversed), the claim is grounded. When a gate blocks, that's a tension. |\n\n  4.11 間 + 関 → 無門関 (The Gateless Gate)\n\n  Tension discovery is 間 + 関 composed: read the gap honestly (間), then\n  traverse the gate structure (関) to find what the gap implies. The \"gateless\n  gate\" is not the absence of gates — it is the perception that the gate\n  dissolves when the interval is honestly read. The tension \"C-arena has no\n  mission\" IS the gate: traversing it (proposing the mission, tracing the\n  path) is how it opens. The barrier and the path are the same structure\n  seen from different sides of 間.\n\n  In operational terms: `query-uncovered-components` (core.logic) reads 間\n  relationally; `trace-tension-path` (futon3c) traverses the chain of 関\n  from tension to source. The composition of these two operations IS the\n  self-representing stack's discovery loop — and it should be invocable\n  on demand, not performed ad hoc.\n\n  4.12 既 — Earned Past (Urðr)\n\n  | Context | 既 instance | Evidence |\n  | Proof path | committed PSR→PUR→PAR chain | The chain is 既: citeable as authority, extensible by future loops, rewritable only with explicit trace |\n  | HEAD-as-escrow | git HEAD as held-truth | HEAD is 既 in the most literal sense: what has come to pass, held as the present's grounding |\n  | Closure evidence | mission-close artifact | Closure freezes a 化 into citeable 既; the closure record is the artifact through which future loops cite the closed mission |\n  | Library canonicalization | promoted flexiarg | Promotion turns a candidate-pattern into 既: subsequent loops cite it as ground rather than candidate |\n  | Frozen tape (piano roll) | append-only PSR/PUR/PAR log | The roll IS the Urðr-surface: what cannot be un-done, only added to |\n\n  4.13 化 — Becoming Event (Verðandi)\n\n  | Context | 化 instance | Evidence |\n  | Live writer-action | VSATARCS-writer presently firing | The act of composition is 化: not yet 既, not yet emitting 債, but in the act |\n  | Consent gate firing | gate evaluating an action class | The firing moment IS 化: not the gate's config (genotype) nor its outcome (phenotype), but the becoming-event |\n  | PSR being authored | agent presently choosing a pattern | The choosing is 化; the recorded choice is 既; the verification obligation is (應 . 債) |\n  | Loop turn | one execution of the futonic loop | Each turn IS 化 for the duration of its firing; afterward it has become 既 |\n  | M-live-geometric-stack | the live geometric surface | The Verðandi-surface of the futon stack: live (T, ∇, Δ, drift) |\n\n  4.14 應 / 債 — Owed Obligation (Skuld)\n\n  | Context | 應 instance | 債 instance | Evidence |\n  | sorrys.edn | \"we should X\" voice in entry text | typed debt record with :kind | 應 is the modal voice; 債 is the structural debt the registry tracks |\n  | Mission scoping | \"this mission shall deliver Y\" | uncompleted scoped commitments | 應 in scope language; 債 in scope-remaining accounting |\n  | Pattern next-steps | next[…] entries | unfulfilled pattern-promises | Modal commitments (應) become structural debts (債) if not discharged |\n  | Wiring obligations | invariant statement | broken invariant under load | 應 declares what should hold; 債 is what actually breaks |\n  | sorrys.edn :kind | :prototyping-forward vs :technical-debt | distinct debt-classes | 債 typed by :kind; collapse to one class hides regime distinctions |\n\n  == 5. THE FUTONIC LOOP IN PRACTICE ==\n\n  The abstract loop (§3) maps onto three concrete cycles:\n\n  5.1 PSR→PUR cycle (per-task):\n\n  (futonic-loop\n    (input   象  := task request entering the gate pipeline)\n    (choose  部  := select decomposition: which gate, which timescale)\n    (articulate 咅 := write PSR: choose a pattern, commit to an approach)\n    (if (forms 鹽) := pattern (鹵) + evidence shape (皿) compose)\n    (and (sense 香) := during execution, perceive whether it's working)\n    (and (regulate 🔮) := stay within budget, reject if budget exceeded)\n    (then\n      (act-within 未知 := execute under uncertainty)\n      (evaluate 味 := write PUR: evaluate outcome by felt difference))\n    (else\n      (apply 捨 := reject at gate, park mission, write PAR)))\n\n  5.2 Derivation xenotype (per-mission):\n\n  (futonic-loop\n    (input   象  := domain to be derived: \"social coordination\")\n    (choose  部  := IDENTIFY the decomposition regime: git archaeology,\n                    pattern audit, devmap review)\n    (articulate 咅 := MAP + DERIVE: articulate patterns and requirements\n                    from the evidence)\n    (if (forms 鹽) := ARGUE: requirements (鹵) + argument structure (皿)\n                    compose into a valid case)\n    (and (sense 香) := during argument construction, perceive convergences)\n    (and (regulate 🔮) := VERIFY: check invariants, validate diagrams)\n    (then\n      (act-within 未知 := INSTANTIATE: build the code)\n      (evaluate 味 := tests pass, 8/8 checks, round-trip proof))\n    (else\n      (apply 捨 := park mission, record blocker, explicit exit)))\n\n  5.3 Glacial loop (per-library-evolution):\n\n  (futonic-loop\n    (input   象  := accumulated proof paths from many tasks)\n    (choose  部  := L1-observe: which tension criterion to scan?\n                    structural-irritation / pre-symbolic-pressure /\n                    trans-situational-reappearance)\n    (articulate 咅 := name the tension: a recurring pattern emerges)\n    (if (forms 鹽) := tension observation (鹵) + flexiarg format (皿)\n                    compose into a candidate pattern)\n    (and (sense 香) := does this tension appear across contexts?)\n    (and (regulate 🔮) := does it meet the canonicalization threshold?\n                    frequency >= 3, contexts >= 2)\n    (then\n      (act-within 未知 := write new flexiarg to library)\n      (evaluate 味 := subsequent G3 accepts the new pattern))\n    (else\n      (apply 捨 := tension does not meet threshold, record but do not\n                    canonicalize)))\n\n  == 6. RELATIONSHIP TO EXISTING PATTERNS ==\n\n  | This specification | Existing pattern | Relationship |\n  | 象 as first-class configuration | symbolic-geodesic | 象 IS the structured whole that geodesics traverse |\n  | 部 as decomposition regime | symbolic-individuation | 部 IS the regime under which compound symbols individuate |\n  | 鹽 = (⿱ 鹵 皿) | symbolic-individuation §THE-FUTONIC-VIEW | Same structural analysis, now formalized as composition rule |\n  | 香 as cross-layer perception | structural-tension-as-observation | 香 IS the pre-symbolic pressure before tension is named |\n  | 味 as embodied evaluation | retroactive-canonicalization | 味 IS the selection criterion in the Baldwin cycle |\n  | 🔮 = (能 . 捨) | sospeso protocol (futon5/AGENTS.md) | 🔮 IS the regulator that the sospeso protocol implements |\n  | 未知 / 非死非活 | explicit-exit-over-abandonment | 非死非活 IS the zombie-mission state that the pattern diagnoses |\n  | 間 as honest-interval | structural-tension-as-observation | 間 IS the perception that a gap has structure, before naming what fills it |\n  | 関 as gate-relation | mission-interface-signature | 関 IS the typed boundary that mission ports formalize; core.logic relations are evaluable 関 |\n  | 間 + 関 = 無門関 | tension discovery loop (M-self-representing-stack) | The discovery loop IS 間-reading composed with 関-traversal: perceive gap, navigate gate chain |\n  | futonic-loop | proof-path event-protocol | The loop IS the abstract form of CLOCK_IN → ... → CLOCK_OUT |\n  | 既 / 化 / 應-債 | futon-theory/wyrd (the three Norns) | The three-time decomposition IS the Nornic frame; wyrd grounds the modality, futonic-logic formalises the projection |\n  | 鹽 / 債 ledger | sorrys.edn (registry) + futon-theory/wyrd | sorrys.edn IS the 債 side of the ledger; the 鹽/債 pair is the structural fact wyrd names |\n\n  == 7. AXIOM A7: COMPOSITIONAL SALIENCE ==\n\n  The existing axioms (A1-A5) are operational. A7 is perceptual:\n\n  (A7-precondition\n    (if (perceives-間 agent interval))\n    (then (A7 may proceed: test for 鹵 and 皿))\n    (else (composition is premature: the interval has not\n           been honestly observed)))\n\n  (A7\n    (precondition A7-precondition)\n    (if (exists (鹽 (⿱ 鹵 皿))))\n    (and (perceivable 鹵) (perceivable 皿))\n    (then (salience emerges))\n    (else (generativity remains inert)))\n\n  In operational terms: a pattern library (鹵) and a recording protocol (皿)\n  may both exist without generating anything — until an agent perceives that\n  they compose under the current decomposition regime (部). Salience is the\n  moment of that perception. The gate pipeline does not create salience;\n  it provides the 皿 that makes 鹵's potential actionable. The agent's PSR\n  is the 咅 that recognizes the composition.\n\n  This axiom explains why Agency/Forum/Drawbridge failed: the components\n  existed (鹵 was present) but the recording protocol did not (皿 was absent\n  — no typed evidence shapes, no proof-path store). Without 皿, composition\n  could not form. Without composition, salience could not emerge. Without\n  salience, the agents could not perceive when their coordination was or was\n  not working. The result was 非死非活: systems responsive but not self-\n  sustaining, eventually abandoned without exit.\n\n  == 8. BOUNDARY RULE: 味→未@0 ==\n\n  When evaluation approaches a boundary, it collapses into uncertainty.\n  The correct response is containment, not force.\n\n  (rule-card 味→未@0\n    (when (approach boundary))\n    (then\n      (collapse (味 → 未))\n      (bias-toward (戒 圍 包)))\n    (therefore\n      (prefer (small reversible moves))\n      (avoid (irreversible commits))))\n\n  Vocabulary for the bias targets:\n  - 戒 (jiè): precept, restraint — the discipline of not-acting\n  - 圍 (wéi): enclosure, surround — contain the blast radius\n  - 包 (bāo): wrap, contain — package the uncertainty for later\n\n  This rule governs the transition from the futonic loop's \"evaluate\"\n  phase to the \"else\" branch. When 味 (embodied evaluation) reaches the\n  edge of what can be evaluated with confidence, the system does not\n  push through — it recognizes it has entered 未知 (non-equilibrium)\n  and shifts to containment mode.\n\n  Grounding in practice:\n\n  | Context | Boundary | 味→未 collapse | 戒/圍/包 response |\n  | Sospeso | p drops below 0.95 | Evaluation becomes uncertain | Rescope-to-safety, blast radius note, reversible action only |\n  | Gate rejection | G3 can't find pattern | Evaluation of approach is impossible | Typed rejection with gate attribution; don't execute |\n  | Mission parking | Subsystem hits blocking failure | Evaluation of progress becomes 非死非活 | :active → :blocked with recorded blocker; don't abandon silently |\n  | L1 canonicalizer | Tension below threshold | Evaluation of pattern candidacy is premature | Record but do not canonicalize; wait for more evidence |\n  | Context compaction | Context window approaches limit | Full evaluation of history is no longer possible | Compress to summary; preserve actionable structure, release detail |\n\n  The rule card is the futonic dual of the sospeso protocol. The sospeso\n  protocol is operational (confidence bins, mana accounting, blast radius\n  notes). The rule card is structural: it says WHEN the transition happens\n  (味 approaches boundary) and WHAT the bias should be (戒/圍/包), without\n  prescribing HOW (that's domain-specific).\n\n  A7 says: composition requires perceiving both 鹵 and 皿.\n  味→未@0 says: when you can no longer perceive clearly, stop composing\n  and start containing. A7 opens the door; 味→未@0 knows when to close it.", :directives {:audience "futon developers, pattern theorists, war room", :factor "Keen investigation (dhammavicaya)", :keywords ["futonic" "logic" "composition" "articulation" "salience" "evaluation" "regulator" "individuation" "s-expression"], :references ["futon-theory/symbolic-individuation" "futon-theory/four-types" "futon-theory/theory-as-exotype" "futon-theory/structural-tension-as-observation" "futon-theory/retroactive-canonicalization" "futon-theory/baldwin-cycle" "futon-theory/proof-path" "futon-theory/agent-contract"], :sigils ["🧂/鹽"], :style "specification", :title "Futonic Logic: Vocabulary, Composition, and the Recognition Loop", :tone "analytic-foundational"}, :id "futon-theory/futonic-logic", :keywords ["futonic" "logic" "composition" "articulation" "salience" "evaluation" "regulator" "individuation" "s-expression"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["futon-theory/symbolic-individuation" "futon-theory/four-types" "futon-theory/theory-as-exotype" "futon-theory/structural-tension-as-observation" "futon-theory/retroactive-canonicalization" "futon-theory/baldwin-cycle" "futon-theory/proof-path" "futon-theory/agent-contract"], :scores {}, :sigils ["🧂/鹽"], :source-path "library/futon-theory/futonic-logic.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Futonic Logic: Vocabulary, Composition, and the Recognition Loop", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "When a progress counter (hole-count, score, metric) can be moved in your favour by either truthful or flattering edits, take the TRUTHFUL move — even when truth RAISES the count (makes hidden work visible) or REFUSES a discharge (declines an unearned close). A counter only ever moved downward rewards concealment; a trustworthy one must be moveable the \"wrong\" way by truth."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You advance a tracked target whose progress is read off a counter you can edit, and the\n    easy direction is always the flattering one."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      You can move a progress counter — check a box, close a mission, lower a count — to register progress."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      If the counter UNDER-reports (the map shows fewer holes than the territory has) or the close is\n      UNEARNED (the field has not actually moved), the flattering move launders concealment as\n      progress. The map starts to flatter the territory, and the metric measures compliance, not reality."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "      Take the truthful move even against the counter: make hidden work machine-VISIBLE — RAISE the\n      hole-count — when the map under-reports; REFUSE the discharge — hold the close — when it is not\n      earned. Accept the worse-looking number when it is the true one."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      A metric is only trustworthy if truth can move it the \"wrong\" way; a counter that only ever goes\n      down is an incentive to conceal, not to do. (This is the operational face of earned-closure and\n      `[[aif/no-self-certification]]`.)"} {:name "canonical-instance", :name-key "canonical-instance", :slug "canonical-instance", :text "      WM-pilot arc-1: a mission read 0 holes while carrying 5 real ones — the honest move RAISED the\n      count (cycle 5, make-true-state-machine-visible); and the field's own top recommendation was an\n      unearned teleport (closing an already-closed mission) — the honest move REFUSED it (cycle 2, V2\n      no-teleport). Both times the counter rewarded truth in the harder direction. Distilled 2026-06-11."}], :conclusion "When a progress counter (hole-count, score, metric) can be moved in your favour by either truthful or flattering edits, take the TRUTHFUL move — even when truth RAISES the count (makes hidden work visible) or REFUSES a discharge (declines an unearned close). A counter only ever moved downward rewards concealment; a trustworthy one must be moveable the \"wrong\" way by truth.", :directives {:audience "anyone advancing a target measured by a counter they can influence", :keywords ["counter" "metric" "hole-count" "honest map" "under-reporting" "unearned close" "teleport" "gaming" "truth-both-ways"], :sigils ["🗺/✓"], :style "pattern", :title "The Honest Map Beats the Flattering Counter", :tone "corrective"}, :id "futon-theory/honest-map-over-flattering-counter", :keywords ["counter" "metric" "hole-count" "honest map" "under-reporting" "unearned close" "teleport" "gaming" "truth-both-ways"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🗺/✓"], :source-path "library/futon-theory/honest-map-over-flattering-counter.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "The Honest Map Beats the Flattering Counter", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Each layer interface can host a Baldwin-style loop: the interface serves\n  as both exotype constraint and selection pressure injection point. This\n  enables cumulative functional gain at every boundary."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "In layered futon architecture (L0 → L1 → L2 → ...), each layer\n    boundary is an opportunity for local learning. Instead of one global\n    training phase, Baldwin cycles run at each interface, accumulating\n    improvements incrementally."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Operate an explicit move on the named axis (see substructure below); steward the pattern against the declared discipline."} {:name "COMPOSABILITY", :name-key "composability", :slug "composability", :text "      Same InterfaceLoop structure works at:\n      - L0↔L1: Storage/identity interface\n      - L1↔L2: Identity/integrity interface\n      - L2↔L3: Integrity/auth interface\n      - Any future layer boundary"} {:name "DOMAIN-INSTANTIATION", :name-key "domain-instantiation", :slug "domain-instantiation", :text "      See mission docs for concrete interface loop definitions at each boundary."} {:name "INTERFACE-AS-DOUBLE-ROLE", :name-key "interface-as-double-role", :slug "interface-as-double-role", :text "      Each interface Ii between Li and Li+1:\n      1. Exotype: Defines allowed signal shapes, types, protocols, resources\n      2. Selection pressure: What succeeds/fails at this boundary"} {:name "LOOP-STRUCTURE", :name-key "loop-structure", :slug "loop-structure", :text "      For interface Ii:\n\n      1. INTERFACE CONSTRAINT (exotype):\n         - Allowed signal shapes, type contracts\n         - Protocol requirements, resource bounds\n         - What must hold for messages to pass\n\n      2. EXPLORATION SPACE (phenotype plasticity):\n         - Parameter adjustments, local rule variants\n         - Strategy switching, micro-refactoring\n         - Optional: adapter phenotypes for runtime learning\n         - Constraint: Must not violate exotype\n\n      3. ASSIMILATION (genotype writeback):\n         - Successful adaptation strategies compiled back\n         - New patterns generated, rule tables updated\n         - Replayable patches with evidence attached\n\n      4. COMPRESSION (invariant strengthening):\n         - \"What once required learning\" becomes structural\n         - Tests/invariants circle the success region\n         - Exotype tightens, undefined behavior shrinks"} {:name "MINIMUM-EVENT-SEQUENCE", :name-key "minimum-event-sequence", :slug "minimum-event-sequence", :text "      1. LOOP_START (interface, initial exotype hash)\n      2. EXPLORE_ATTEMPT* (variant tried, outcome measured)\n      3. ADAPT_SUCCESS (strategy that worked, evidence)\n      4. ASSIMILATE (genotype change, proof commit)\n      5. CANALIZE (invariant tightened, exotype narrowed)\n      6. LOOP_END (interface, final exotype hash, gain metric)"} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Global optimization is expensive and opaque. Local optimization at\n    interfaces is tractable and auditable. The interface loop converts\n    every boundary into a learning/accumulation opportunity."} {:name "EVIDENCE-PACKAGE", :name-key "evidence-package", :slug "evidence-package", :text "      Each interface loop produces an evidence package:\n      - Exploration: OBSERVE, PROPOSE_CLAIM events with outcomes\n      - Adaptation: APPLY_CHANGE events with before/after\n      - Fixation: PROOF_COMMIT with success strategy\n      - Compression: Invariant updates, exotype changes"} {:name "GAIN-CALCULATION", :name-key "gain-calculation", :slug "gain-calculation", :text "      Interface gain = (cost_before_canalization - cost_after) / exploration_cost\n      Positive gain: Canalization paid off\n      Negative gain: Exploration wasn't worth it (learn from this)"}], :conclusion "  Each layer interface can host a Baldwin-style loop: the interface serves\n  as both exotype constraint and selection pressure injection point. This\n  enables cumulative functional gain at every boundary.", :directives {:audience "futon developers, stack architects", :factor "Keen investigation (dhammavicaya)", :keywords ["interface" "boundary" "layer" "loop" "cycle" "exotype" "adapt" "compress"], :references ["futon-theory/baldwin-cycle" "futon-theory/four-types" "futon-theory/local-gain-persistence" "futon-theory/error-hierarchy"], :sigils ["🎋/介"], :title "Interface Loop", :tone "analytic-foundational"}, :id "futon-theory/interface-loop", :keywords ["interface" "boundary" "layer" "loop" "cycle" "exotype" "adapt" "compress"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["futon-theory/baldwin-cycle" "futon-theory/four-types" "futon-theory/local-gain-persistence" "futon-theory/error-hierarchy"], :scores {}, :sigils ["🎋/介"], :source-path "library/futon-theory/interface-loop.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Interface Loop", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "LocalGainMustEitherPersistOrBeDeleted: For any interface loop that produces gain G: - Either G is assimilated (genotype updated, PROOF_COMMIT with evidence) - Or G is explicitly deleted (REMOVE_GAIN with reason) - Never: G exists only in runtime/phenotype\n  Any functional gain introduced at an interface must either be assimilated\n  into genotype (replayable, auditable) or explicitly deleted. No ghost\n  capabilities allowed."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Interface loops produce functional gains - adaptations that\n    work better than before. Without this invariant, gains can exist in\n    runtime state but never be captured, creating \"ghost capabilities\"\n    that work but cannot be audited, replayed, or trusted."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "The applicability scope of the pattern (see substructure)."} {:name "DELETION-REQUIREMENTS", :name-key "deletion-requirements", :slug "deletion-requirements", :text "      To count as \"deleted\", gain must have:\n      - Explicit REMOVE_GAIN event\n      - Reason (why not persisting: not worth it, superseded, etc.)\n      - Cleanup verification (no residual effects)"} {:name "PERSISTENCE-REQUIREMENTS", :name-key "persistence-requirements", :slug "persistence-requirements", :text "      To count as \"persisted\", gain must have:\n      - Genotype change (replayable representation)\n      - Evidence chain (what problem it solved)\n      - Proof commit (durable, with tx-id)\n      - Test coverage (verifies gain still works)"} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "The structural tension above manifests in the following concrete shapes (see substructure)."} {:name "ANTI-PATTERNS", :name-key "anti-patterns", :slug "anti-patterns", :text "      - \"It just started working\" (ghost gain, no genotype)\n      - \"We'll capture it later\" (procrastinated persistence)\n      - \"Nobody uses that anymore\" (orphaned gain, not deleted)\n      - Runtime-only optimizations that survive restart"} {:name "GHOST-CAPABILITY-PROBLEM", :name-key "ghost-capability-problem", :slug "ghost-capability-problem", :text "      Without this invariant:\n      - System works better but nobody knows why\n      - Restart loses the improvement\n      - Can't replicate success in other environments\n      - Can't audit what changed\n      - Can't roll back if it causes problems later"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Operate an explicit move on the named axis (see substructure below); steward the pattern against the declared discipline."} {:name "ENFORCEMENT", :name-key "enforcement", :slug "enforcement", :text "      1. LOOP_END event requires disposition field: {persisted, deleted}\n      2. If persisted: genotype diff + proof commit must exist\n      3. If deleted: reason + timestamp recorded\n      4. If neither: INVARIANT_CHECK fails, stop-the-line"} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Accumulated capability is the product of engineering. If capabilities\n    exist but aren't captured, the product is ephemeral. This invariant\n    ensures that every gain either becomes permanent structure or is\n    acknowledged as temporary and removed."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Add gain disposition field to LOOP_END event schema]\n    next[Implement persistence check in interface loop middleware]"}], :conclusion "LocalGainMustEitherPersistOrBeDeleted: For any interface loop that produces gain G: - Either G is assimilated (genotype updated, PROOF_COMMIT with evidence) - Or G is explicitly deleted (REMOVE_GAIN with reason) - Never: G exists only in runtime/phenotype\n  Any functional gain introduced at an interface must either be assimilated\n  into genotype (replayable, auditable) or explicitly deleted. No ghost\n  capabilities allowed.", :directives {:audience "futon developers, stack architects", :factor "Keen investigation (dhammavicaya)", :keywords ["gain" "persist" "delete" "ghost" "capability" "accumulate" "audit"], :references ["futon-theory/interface-loop" "futon-theory/baldwin-cycle" "futon-theory/counter-ratchet" "futon-theory/stop-the-line"], :sigils ["🌅/功"], :title "Local Gain Persistence Invariant", :tone "analytic-foundational"}, :id "futon-theory/local-gain-persistence", :keywords ["gain" "persist" "delete" "ghost" "capability" "accumulate" "audit"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["futon-theory/interface-loop" "futon-theory/baldwin-cycle" "futon-theory/counter-ratchet" "futon-theory/stop-the-line"], :scores {}, :sigils ["🌅/功"], :source-path "library/futon-theory/local-gain-persistence.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Local Gain Persistence Invariant", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Log the minimum events required for full auditability. Not every keystroke,\n  but every decision point. The goal is defensibility, not surveillance."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Event logging has overhead. Too few events lose audit trail;\n    too many create noise and performance drag. A5 defines the threshold:\n    log what's needed for proof paths, no more."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "The applicability scope of the pattern (see substructure)."} {:name "MINIMUM-VIABLE-SET", :name-key "minimum-viable-set", :slug "minimum-viable-set", :text "      - Session boundaries (CLOCK_IN, CLOCK_OUT)\n      - State changes (APPLY_CHANGE, PROOF_COMMIT)\n      - Decision points (PROPOSE_CLAIM, pattern selection)\n      - Failures (invariant violations, verification failures)\n      - Observations that led to changes (evidence links)"} {:name "NOT-REQUIRED", :name-key "not-required", :slug "not-required", :text "      - Intermediate edits before commit\n      - Keystroke-level interactions\n      - Read operations that don't influence changes\n      - Internal system state transitions"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Operate an explicit move on the named axis (see substructure below); steward the pattern against the declared discipline."} {:name "AXIOM-STATEMENT", :name-key "axiom-statement", :slug "axiom-statement", :text "      A5: Log the minimum events required for full auditability."} {:name "OVERHEAD-BALANCE", :name-key "overhead-balance", :slug "overhead-balance", :text "      - Storage: Events are append-only, grow linearly\n      - Performance: Event logging should not block operations\n      - Noise: More events = harder to find signal\n      - Pruning: Old events can be archived after audit period"} {:name "SUFFICIENCY-TEST", :name-key "sufficiency-test", :slug "sufficiency-test", :text "      Given only the logged events, can you:\n      1. Reconstruct what was intended? (intent from PROPOSE_CLAIM)\n      2. Verify what was done? (action from APPLY_CHANGE)\n      3. Confirm the outcome? (result from PROOF_COMMIT)\n      4. Attribute responsibility? (agent from session)\n      5. Diagnose failures? (error context from failures)\n\n      If yes to all: sufficient events.\n      If no to any: add the missing event type."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Auditability is a requirement, not an option. But bloated logs are\n    unreadable logs. A5 threads the needle: enough for proof, no more\n    for comfort."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Define canonical event types for futon1a]\n    next[Implement event pruning for archived sessions]"}], :conclusion "  Log the minimum events required for full auditability. Not every keystroke,\n  but every decision point. The goal is defensibility, not surveillance.", :directives {:audience "futon developers, event system designers", :factor "Keen investigation (dhammavicaya)", :keywords ["minimal" "events" "audit" "trail" "sufficient" "overhead" "lightweight"], :references ["futon-theory/event-protocol" "futon-theory/proof-path" "futon-theory/symbolic-geodesic"], :sigils ["🐜/少"], :title "Minimum Viable Events (Axiom A5)", :tone "analytic-foundational"}, :id "futon-theory/minimum-viable-events", :keywords ["minimal" "events" "audit" "trail" "sufficient" "overhead" "lightweight"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["futon-theory/event-protocol" "futon-theory/proof-path" "futon-theory/symbolic-geodesic"], :scores {}, :sigils ["🐜/少"], :source-path "library/futon-theory/minimum-viable-events.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Minimum Viable Events (Axiom A5)", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Represent missions as an explicit dependency graph so blocked work, leaf work, and high-value unblocks are visible before agents spend effort in the wrong place."} {:name "CONTEXT", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "    Missions rarely exist in isolation. They block and enable each other,\n    share prerequisites, and compete for resources. Without explicit\n    dependency tracking, agents work on blocked missions or miss\n    opportunities to unblock others."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    You are planning work across multiple missions or diagnosing why\n    a mission is stuck."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Dependencies can be circular (requiring negotiation), soft (preferred\n    but not required), or discovered late. The graph must be maintainable."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Model missions as a directed acyclic graph (DAG):\n\n    RELATIONSHIP-TYPES:\n    - blocks: Mission A must complete before Mission B can start\n    - enables: Mission A completion makes Mission B easier (soft)\n    - shares: Missions A and B share a resource or decision\n    - parent-child: Mission A spawned Mission B (split)\n\n    REPRESENTATION:\n    In mission docs:\n    ```\n    Blocked-by: M-other-mission.md\n    Enables: M-downstream.md\n    ```\n\n    CONSTRAINTS:\n    - No orphan missions (every mission linked to at least one other or to root)\n    - No cycles in \"blocks\" relationships\n    - Blocked missions must cite specific blocker\n    - When blocker completes, blocked mission notified\n\n    PRIORITY-INFERENCE:\n    - Missions that unblock many others have higher priority\n    - Leaf missions (no blockers) can proceed immediately\n    - Root missions (no dependents) may be deprioritized"} {:name "VISUALIZATION", :name-key "visualization", :slug "visualization", :text "      The mission graph can be rendered:\n      ```\n      M-futon1a-rebuild\n          ├── blocks → M-futon1a-migration\n          └── enables → M-pattern-inference-engine\n\n      M-make-agency-work-properly\n          └── blocks → M-codex-parity\n      ```"} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    - Explicit dependencies prevent wasted work on blocked missions\n    - Graph structure enables priority inference\n    - Unblock propagation accelerates overall progress\n    - Shared resources surface coordination needs"} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Audit mission docs for dependency declarations\n    - Build mission graph from declarations\n    - Identify leaf missions (unblocked, can work now)\n    - Identify high-value unblock targets\n\n@related futon-theory/mission-lifecycle, futon-theory/mission-scoping, futon-theory/mission-interface-signature"}], :conclusion "Represent missions as an explicit dependency graph so blocked work, leaf work, and high-value unblocks are visible before agents spend effort in the wrong place.", :directives {:sigils ["〰️/双"], :title "Mission Dependency Graph"}, :id "futon-theory/mission-dependency", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["〰️/双"], :source-path "library/futon-theory/mission-dependency.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Mission Dependency Graph", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Every mission must declare a non-empty interface signature - typed inputs, typed outputs, and their wiring - so internally correct work can actually compose with the rest of the stack."} {:name "CONTEXT", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "    Missions that specify internal correctness (invariants, gates, proofs)\n    but not boundary types (what the system exposes) will produce systems\n    that work internally but cannot be composed with anything. The futon1a\n    rebuild demonstrated this failure mode: 5 invariants, 9 tension\n    resolutions, 82 tests — but the API contract was never specified.\n    The system verified itself but couldn't describe what it offered.\n\n    In wiring diagram terms: the internal morphisms were well-composed\n    but the output ports were unspecified. A box with no typed ports\n    cannot participate in composition."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    You are writing a mission document, or reviewing one that has reached\n    the :scoped or :active state."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Not all output ports are known at mission start. Discovery during\n    implementation may reveal new boundary types. The signature should be\n    updated when this happens — but it must never be empty."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Every mission document must include an Interface Signature section\n    with typed input and output ports:\n\n    1. INPUT-PORTS\n       Each port specifies:\n       - Name: what the input is called\n       - Type: the shape of the data (schema, format, contract)\n       - Source: where it comes from (another system, user, file)\n       - Description: what it carries\n\n    2. OUTPUT-PORTS\n       Each port specifies:\n       - Name: what the output is called\n       - Type: the shape of the data (contract reference or inline)\n       - Consumer: who uses this output\n       - Description: what it provides\n\n    3. COMPOSITION-CONSTRAINT\n       Every output port must be:\n       (a) Specified in the mission's technical specification (section ref)\n       (b) Implemented in code (module reference)\n       (c) Tested (at least one test exercises the port)\n       (d) Documented (in the spec, not just in code comments)\n\n       If an output port exists in code but not in the spec, that is a\n       stop-the-line violation (futon-theory/stop-the-line).\n\n    4. FIDELITY-CONTRACT (required for all missions)\n       Every mission must declare a baseline and track fidelity against it:\n       (a) Baseline capability inventory:\n           - donor missions: donor behavior (feature, shape, known consumers)\n           - greenfield missions: declared spec commitments (ports, behavior,\n             and semantic/query layer)\n       (b) Preservation contract: `preserve`, `adapt`, or `drop` per feature\n       (c) Tripwire tests for each `preserve` feature:\n           - must fail on pre-implementation target state\n           - must pass after implementation\n       (d) Latent dependency/omission probe:\n           - donor missions: static call-sites + runtime traces\n           - greenfield missions: consumer scenario probes against declared spec\n           unresolved findings converted into explicit preserve/drop decisions\n\n       If a baseline feature is neither preserved nor explicitly dropped, this is\n       a stop-the-line violation."} {:name "MISSION-DIAGRAM", :name-key "mission-diagram", :slug "mission-diagram", :text "      Every mission must include a wiring diagram (mermaid) alongside the\n      port tables. The diagram is the primary artifact — the tables are\n      the machine-readable shadow.\n\n      The diagram shows:\n      - Input ports on the left (what enters the system)\n      - Internal composition in the center (how components connect)\n      - Output ports on the right (what the system exposes)\n      - Typed edges (what flows along each wire)\n\n      The diagram catches gaps that tables miss. A box with no outgoing\n      wires to the output region is visibly incomplete. A table row is\n      easy to forget; a blank region in a diagram is hard to ignore.\n\n      The diagram should be created at mission :scoped state and updated\n      at each prototype gate. Each prototype may add new output ports\n      or refine internal composition — the diagram tracks this evolution.\n\n      DIAGRAM-DISCIPLINE:\n      - Draw the output ports FIRST. What does the system expose?\n        If you cannot name the output ports, the mission is not scoped.\n      - Then draw the input ports. What does the system consume?\n      - Then draw the internal composition. How do inputs become outputs?\n      - Every output port must have at least one path from an input port\n        through internal components. Orphan outputs are unimplementable.\n      - Every internal component that has no path to an output port is\n        either dead code or a missing output port. Both are bugs."} {:name "TEMPLATE", :name-key "template", :slug "template", :text "      ```markdown\n      ## Interface Signature\n\n      ### Mission Diagram\n\n      ```mermaid\n      graph LR\n          subgraph inputs\n              I1[input-name<br/>type]\n              I2[input-name<br/>type]\n          end\n\n          subgraph system-name\n              C1[Component] --> C2[Component]\n              C2 --> C3[Component]\n          end\n\n          subgraph outputs\n              O1[output-name<br/>type / spec-section]\n              O2[output-name<br/>type / spec-section]\n          end\n\n          I1 --> C1\n          I2 --> C1\n          C3 --> O1\n          C3 --> O2\n      ```\n\n      ### Input Ports\n\n      | Port | Type | Source | Description |\n      |------|------|--------|-------------|\n      | ... | ... | ... | ... |\n\n      ### Output Ports\n\n      | Port | Type | Consumer | Description |\n      |------|------|----------|-------------|\n      | ... | ... | ... | ... |\n\n      ### Composition Constraint\n\n      Every output port must be:\n      1. Specified in the technical specification (section reference)\n      2. Implemented in code (module reference)\n      3. Tested (at least one test exercises the port)\n      4. Documented (in the spec, not just code comments)\n\n      ### Fidelity Contract (all missions)\n\n      | Capability | Baseline Ref (donor/spec) | Contract | Tripwire Test | Notes |\n      |------------|-----------------------------|----------|---------------|-------|\n      | ... | ... | preserve/adapt/drop | test id/path | ... |\n      ```"} {:name "VERIFICATION", :name-key "verification", :slug "verification", :text "      At each checkpoint, verify both manually and by machine.\n\n      Manual:\n      - Every output port in code has a spec section reference\n      - Every output port in spec has at least one test\n      - No unspecified output ports exist in code\n      - No uncategorized baseline capabilities\n      - Every `preserve` capability has a tripwire test\n      - Data-shape, behavior, and semantic/query layers are all tracked\n\n      Machine (futon5.ct.mission):\n      - Maintain an EDN mission diagram alongside the mermaid diagram\n        (see futon1a/docs/mission-diagram.edn for the reference exemplar)\n      - Components are morphisms: declare :accepts (domain) and :produces\n        (codomain), not just :type (identity)\n      - Run `(futon5.ct.mission/validate diagram)` at every prototype gate\n      - All five checks must pass: completeness, coverage, no orphan inputs,\n        type safety, spec coverage\n      - `(futon5.ct.mission/diagram->mermaid diagram)` regenerates the\n        human-readable mermaid from the EDN; keep them in sync\n      - Fidelity check must fail when:\n        - capability inventory contains uncategorized entries\n        - a `preserve` entry has no linked failing-then-passing test\n        - latent dependency findings are unresolved\n        - semantic/query fidelity for an exposed capability is unspecified\n\n      If verification fails, the checkpoint is blocked until the spec\n      catches up with the code."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    - Internal correctness without boundary specification produces systems\n      that cannot be composed, integrated, or replaced\n    - Typed ports force the mission author to think about what the system\n      *presents*, not just what it *does internally*\n    - The composition constraint catches the exact failure mode observed\n      in futon1a: code that exists without specification\n    - Wiring diagrams require typed interfaces at every boundary; missions\n      are the organizational equivalent\n    - Fidelity contracts prevent \"compat surface passes while behavior\n      silently drops\" failures during ports\n    - The same discipline prevents spec omissions in greenfield missions"} {:name "EVIDENCE", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "        futon1a rebuild (M-futon1a-rebuild.md):\n        - Prototype 0 (82 tests) specified internal layers but no API contract\n        - Prototype 1 delivered HTTP serving, but the compat endpoints and\n          model descriptor system were unspecified\n        - The gap was discovered only when attempting to hand off to Codex\n        - Retrofit required: Section 2.6 (API spec), Section 2.11 (model\n          descriptor system), Prototype 2 Gate, interface signature section"} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Add Interface Signature section to current mission doc\n    - Enumerate input and output ports from existing code\n    - Cross-reference output ports against spec sections\n    - Flag any unspecified ports as stop-the-line\n    - Build the baseline capability inventory before DERIVE\n    - Add tripwire tests before implementation starts\n\n@related futon-theory/mission-scoping, futon-theory/mission-lifecycle, futon-theory/mission-dependency, futon-theory/stop-the-line"}], :conclusion "Every mission must declare a non-empty interface signature - typed inputs, typed outputs, and their wiring - so internally correct work can actually compose with the rest of the stack.", :directives {:sigils ["🔌/口"], :title "Mission Interface Signature (Boundary Types)"}, :id "futon-theory/mission-interface-signature", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🔌/口"], :source-path "library/futon-theory/mission-interface-signature.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Mission Interface Signature (Boundary Types)", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Treat each mission as a state machine with explicit transition requirements so scope, blockage, review, and closure leave an auditable trail instead of drifting invisibly."} {:name "CONTEXT", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "    Missions progress through defined states. Without a formal lifecycle,\n    missions drift indefinitely or are abandoned without evidence. The\n    proof-path pattern governs individual changes; this pattern governs\n    the mission as a whole."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    You are defining, tracking, or transitioning a mission."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Missions may be blocked by dependencies, split into sub-missions,\n    or merged when scope overlaps. The lifecycle must accommodate these."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Apply the mission state machine:\n\n    ```\n    :greenfield → :scoped → :active → :blocked → :review → :done\n                     ↑          ↓         ↓\n                     └──────────┴─────────┘ (can regress)\n    ```\n\n    State definitions:\n    - :greenfield - Idea captured, no work plan\n    - :scoped - Success criteria, boundaries, dependencies defined\n    - :active - Work in progress, owner assigned\n    - :blocked - Waiting on dependency (cite blocker)\n    - :review - Work complete, awaiting verification\n    - :done - Success criteria met, evidence recorded\n\n    Transition requirements:\n    - greenfield → scoped: Mission doc has success criteria + scope\n    - scoped → active: Owner assigned, dependencies clear\n    - active → blocked: Blocker cited with link to blocking mission\n    - active → review: All success criteria addressed\n    - review → done: Evidence verified by reviewer\n    - Any → :abandoned: Explicit decision with rationale"} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    - Explicit states prevent invisible drift\n    - Transition requirements create evidence trail\n    - Blocked states surface dependency graphs\n    - Review gates ensure verification before closure"} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Check current mission state\n    - Verify transition requirements are met\n    - Update mission doc with new state\n    - If blocked, link to blocker mission\n\n@related futon-theory/proof-path, futon-theory/mission-scoping, futon-theory/mission-dependency, futon-theory/mission-interface-signature"}], :conclusion "Treat each mission as a state machine with explicit transition requirements so scope, blockage, review, and closure leave an auditable trail instead of drifting invisibly.", :directives {:sigils ["🔃/马"], :title "Mission Lifecycle (State Machine)"}, :id "futon-theory/mission-lifecycle", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🔃/马"], :source-path "library/futon-theory/mission-lifecycle.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Mission Lifecycle (State Machine)", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Scope every mission with bounded success criteria, inclusions, exclusions, ownership, and dependencies so it can be completed, reviewed, and handed off without ambiguity."} {:name "CONTEXT", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "    Unbounded missions never complete. Without clear scope, work expands\n    to fill available context, agents duplicate effort, and success\n    cannot be verified. Every mission needs explicit boundaries."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    You are defining a new mission or reviewing an existing one."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Scope may need revision as work progresses. Discovery during :active\n    phase may reveal the mission is too large or too small."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Every mission document must specify:\n\n    1. SUCCESS-CRITERIA\n       - Concrete, verifiable outcomes\n       - \"Done when X is true\" not \"improve Y\"\n       - Minimum 1, maximum 5 criteria\n\n    2. SCOPE-IN\n       - What is explicitly included\n       - Files, modules, features affected\n       - Decisions this mission makes\n\n    3. SCOPE-OUT\n       - What is explicitly excluded\n       - \"Not addressing X in this mission\"\n       - Future work deferred\n\n    4. OWNER\n       - Single agent responsible at any time\n       - Handoff requires explicit transfer\n       - Owner can delegate but retains accountability\n\n    5. DEPENDENCIES\n       - What must be true before this mission can complete\n       - Links to blocking missions or external requirements"} {:name "SPLIT-MERGE-RULES", :name-key "split-merge-rules", :slug "split-merge-rules", :text "      - SPLIT when success criteria exceed 5\n      - SPLIT when multiple unrelated concerns emerge\n      - MERGE when two missions share >50% scope\n      - On split: create child missions, link to parent\n      - On merge: archive one, expand the other"} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    - Bounded scope enables completion\n    - Explicit exclusions prevent scope creep\n    - Single owner prevents diffusion of responsibility\n    - Dependencies surface the mission graph"} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Review mission doc for all 5 elements\n    - Add missing scope elements\n    - If scope unclear, mark mission :blocked with \"scope-undefined\"\n\n@related futon-theory/mission-lifecycle, futon-theory/mission-dependency, futon-theory/mission-interface-signature"}], :conclusion "Scope every mission with bounded success criteria, inclusions, exclusions, ownership, and dependencies so it can be completed, reviewed, and handed off without ambiguity.", :directives {:sigils ["🎒/王"], :title "Mission Scoping (Bounded Ownership)"}, :id "futon-theory/mission-scoping", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🎒/王"], :source-path "library/futon-theory/mission-scoping.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Mission Scoping (Bounded Ownership)", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Measure mission progress through accumulating evidence and explicit health states so slowdown, blockage, and dead work are detected early instead of being felt only as vague stagnation."} {:name "CONTEXT", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "    Missions can feel stuck even when progress is happening. Without\n    explicit progress signals, agents lose motivation, humans lose\n    visibility, and dead missions aren't detected. Counter-ratchet\n    applies to code; this pattern applies to missions."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    You need to assess whether a mission is progressing, stalled, or dead."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Progress isn't always linear. Research phases may produce no artifacts.\n    Blocked missions may have zero commits but active waiting. The signal\n    must distinguish stalled from legitimately waiting."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Track progress via evidence accumulation:\n\n    EVIDENCE-TYPES (in order of strength):\n    1. SUCCESS-CRITERIA-MET: Criterion checked off with proof\n    2. ARTIFACT-CREATED: Code, doc, test added (commit hash)\n    3. DECISION-MADE: Choice recorded in mission doc\n    4. BLOCKER-RESOLVED: Dependency cleared\n    5. REVIEW-COMPLETED: Milestone reviewed and approved\n    6. SESSION-LOGGED: PSR/PUR pair recorded (weakest signal)\n\n    PROGRESS-METRICS:\n    ```\n    velocity = evidence_count / time_elapsed\n    momentum = recent_evidence (last 7 days) / total_evidence\n    completion = criteria_met / total_criteria\n    ```\n\n    HEALTH-STATES:\n    - PROGRESSING: velocity > 0, momentum > 0.3\n    - SLOWING: velocity > 0, momentum < 0.3\n    - STALLED: velocity = 0 for > 7 days, not :blocked\n    - BLOCKED: explicitly marked :blocked with blocker\n    - DEAD: stalled > 30 days, no activity\n\n    INTERVENTION-TRIGGERS:\n    - SLOWING: Review scope, consider split\n    - STALLED: Diagnose cause, reassign or close\n    - DEAD: Archive with post-mortem"} {:name "COUNTER-RATCHET-FOR-MISSIONS", :name-key "counter-ratchet-for-missions", :slug "counter-ratchet-for-missions", :text "      Like code counts, mission evidence should not decrease:\n      - Criteria met stays met (unless scope changes)\n      - Artifacts created persist\n      - Sessions logged accumulate\n\n      If evidence decreases (reverted commit, criterion unmarked):\n      - Flag for investigation\n      - Require explicit justification"} {:name "VITALITY-INTEGRATION", :name-key "vitality-integration", :slug "vitality-integration", :text "      Mission progress feeds into stack vitality metrics:\n      - Active missions with evidence = healthy\n      - Many stalled missions = unhealthy\n      - High completion rate = velocity"} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    - Evidence accumulation makes progress visible\n    - Metrics enable early intervention\n    - Health states trigger appropriate responses\n    - Dead mission detection prevents zombie work"} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Audit mission for evidence types present\n    - Calculate current health state\n    - If stalled, diagnose and intervene\n    - Log progress signal to MUSN activity stream\n\n@related futon-theory/counter-ratchet, futon-theory/mission-lifecycle, futon-theory/minimum-viable-events"}], :conclusion "Measure mission progress through accumulating evidence and explicit health states so slowdown, blockage, and dead work are detected early instead of being felt only as vague stagnation.", :directives {:sigils ["✌️/门"], :title "Progress Signal (Evidence Accumulation)"}, :id "futon-theory/progress-signal", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["✌️/门"], :source-path "library/futon-theory/progress-signal.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Progress Signal (Evidence Accumulation)", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Every change in the FUTON stack follows a proof path: an auditable event chain\n  linking intent to operation to change to verification to durable proof commit."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "The proof path is the core unit of work in futon theory. It embodies\n    the Curry-Howard correspondence: programs are proofs of their specifications."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "The structural tension above manifests in the following concrete shapes (see substructure)."} {:name "CONCURRENCY-AND-PARTIAL-FAILURE", :name-key "concurrency-and-partial-failure", :slug "concurrency-and-partial-failure", :text "      This protocol assumes a single-writer linear chain. Open constraints:\n      - Concurrent writers: Requires conflict detection at APPLY_CHANGE or\n        serialization at PROOF_COMMIT. Domain patterns must specify strategy.\n      - Partial apply: If APPLY_CHANGE partially succeeds, VERIFY must detect\n        and the path must either complete rollback or spawn a repair path.\n      - Retries: Failed paths may be retried from OBSERVE, not mid-path.\n      Future: A \"concurrency protocol\" pattern may extend this for multi-writer."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Operate an explicit move on the named axis (see substructure below); steward the pattern against the declared discipline."} {:name "AXIOM-GROUNDING", :name-key "axiom-grounding", :slug "axiom-grounding", :text "      A1 (Auditability): Every proof path is fully traceable from intent to outcome.\n      A2 (Attributability): Each event in the path has a clear source and owner.\n      A3 (Evidence-driven): Progression requires verified evidence, not assertion."} {:name "DOMAIN-INSTANTIATION", :name-key "domain-instantiation", :slug "domain-instantiation", :text "      Domain patterns specify how proof paths are implemented:\n      - Storage: `storage/durability-first` for PROOF_COMMIT\n      - Lab sessions: Session lifecycle wraps proof paths\n      - Checks: Verification DSL for VERIFY phase"} {:name "INVARIANTS", :name-key "invariants", :slug "invariants", :text "      - No change commits without passing all invariant checks\n      - Every commit references its evidence chain\n      - Proof paths are append-only; errors spawn new paths, not mutations"} {:name "STRUCTURE", :name-key "structure", :slug "structure", :text "      A proof path consists of these phases:\n      1. CLOCK_IN - Session begins, agent declares intent\n      2. OBSERVE - Read state, gather evidence\n      3. PROPOSE_CLAIM - Agent proposes what will change\n      4. APPLY_CHANGE - Execute the change\n      5. VERIFY - Confirm change matches claim\n      6. INVARIANT_CHECK - All invariants must pass\n      7. PROOF_COMMIT - Durable commit with evidence chain\n      8. CLOCK_OUT - Session ends, proof path complete"} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Without proof paths, changes are unauditable. With them, any bug can be\n    traced from symptom to cause through the evidence chain. The spec mediates\n    future state into present; the proof path is how we get there defensibly."}], :conclusion "  Every change in the FUTON stack follows a proof path: an auditable event chain\n  linking intent to operation to change to verification to durable proof commit.", :directives {:audience "futon developers, stack maintainers, agent implementers", :factor "Keen investigation (dhammavicaya)", :keywords ["proof" "evidence" "chain" "intent" "operation" "verification" "claim" "commit"], :references ["futon-theory/event-protocol" "futon-theory/symbolic-geodesic" "futon-theory/retrospective-stability"], :sigils ["🚞/归"], :title "Proof Path", :tone "analytic-foundational"}, :id "futon-theory/proof-path", :keywords ["proof" "evidence" "chain" "intent" "operation" "verification" "claim" "commit"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["futon-theory/event-protocol" "futon-theory/symbolic-geodesic" "futon-theory/retrospective-stability"], :scores {}, :sigils ["🚞/归"], :source-path "library/futon-theory/proof-path.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Proof Path", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "I4: Any bug diagnosable in under 10 minutes.\n  Any bug diagnosable in under 10 minutes. Errors name their source: layer,\n  operation, entity ID, expected vs actual. One write path to trace, not six.\n  State inspectable at any moment."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Debugging hours instead of minutes is not just inconvenient -\n    it's a sign of architectural failure. If you can't find the bug, you can't\n    fix it. Rapid debugging is a first-class design concern, not an afterthought."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "The applicability scope of the pattern (see substructure)."} {:name "REQUIREMENTS", :name-key "requirements", :slug "requirements", :text "      - Errors include: layer, operation, entity ID, expected vs actual\n      - Single write path (chokepoint) to trace, not multiple paths\n      - State inspectable at any moment via REPL or API\n      - Logs tell a story: \"write started -> confirmed -> done\" or \"started -> failed at X\""} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "The structural tension above manifests in the following concrete shapes (see substructure)."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "      Missing layer -> search all layers (hours)\n      Missing entity ID -> search all entities (hours)\n      Multiple write paths -> trace all paths (hours)\n      Unstructured logs -> grep randomly (hours)"} {:name "ANTI-PATTERNS", :name-key "anti-patterns", :slug "anti-patterns", :text "      - \"Error occurred\" without context\n      - Six different write functions\n      - State only visible after restart\n      - Logs that require human interpretation to correlate"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Operate an explicit move on the named axis (see substructure below); steward the pattern against the declared discipline."} {:name "ENFORCEMENT", :name-key "enforcement", :slug "enforcement", :text "      - All errors use structured format with required fields\n      - Write path is single chokepoint (one function to trace)\n      - State query API available without restart\n      - Logs structured, not prose"} {:name "DEBUGGING-PRIMITIVES", :name-key "debugging-primitives", :slug "debugging-primitives", :text "      1. Error message names source layer\n      2. Entity ID links to exact record\n      3. Expected vs actual shows the discrepancy\n      4. Operation name maps to one function\n      5. Timestamp + tx-id allows timeline reconstruction"} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Time-to-diagnosis determines time-to-fix. A system where bugs take hours\n    to find will have more bugs, because the feedback loop is slow. Fast\n    debugging is a multiplier on all other engineering work."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Implement single write chokepoint in futon1a]\n    next[Add structured error format with required fields]\n    next[Create state inspection API for live debugging]"}], :conclusion "I4: Any bug diagnosable in under 10 minutes.\n  Any bug diagnosable in under 10 minutes. Errors name their source: layer,\n  operation, entity ID, expected vs actual. One write path to trace, not six.\n  State inspectable at any moment.", :directives {:audience "futon developers, stack maintainers, on-call engineers", :factor "Keen investigation (dhammavicaya)", :keywords ["debug" "diagnose" "trace" "minutes" "context" "source" "operation" "log"], :references ["futon-theory/error-hierarchy" "futon-theory/proof-path" "stack-coherence/evidence-ledger"], :sigils ["👓/示"], :title "Rapid Debugging (Invariant 4)", :tone "analytic-foundational"}, :id "futon-theory/rapid-debugging", :keywords ["debug" "diagnose" "trace" "minutes" "context" "source" "operation" "log"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["futon-theory/error-hierarchy" "futon-theory/proof-path" "stack-coherence/evidence-ledger"], :scores {}, :sigils ["👓/示"], :source-path "library/futon-theory/rapid-debugging.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Rapid Debugging (Invariant 4)", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Futon theory specifications are not a priori axiom systems. They are the\n  result of retroactive canonicalization: taking patterns that emerged from\n  practice (ancestors), recognizing which ones are structurally necessary,\n  and promoting them to invariants and constraints. Events do not change;\n  their symbolic coordinates change — from \"incidental fix\" to \"necessary\n  constraint.\" This process must itself be auditable."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "A persistent question in the futon stack is: where does the\n    specification come from? If you treat it as an axiom system that existed\n    before the code, you cannot explain how it was derived. If you treat it\n    as code-first, you have no theory. The answer is neither: specification\n    emerges from practice through a specific, identifiable, auditable process\n    that this pattern describes."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Operate an explicit move on the named axis (see substructure below); steward the pattern against the declared discipline."} {:name "COMPRESSION-RULES", :name-key "compression-rules", :slug "compression-rules", :text "      When is canonicalization warranted? A pattern is a candidate when:\n      - Frequency: it has been applied across multiple tasks/missions\n      - Risk: its absence caused or would cause a detectable failure\n      - Reusability: it applies across domains, not just one context\n      - Failure cost: the consequence of ignoring it is high\n      - Machine-checkability: it can be enforced by code, not just convention\n\n      These rules correspond to the theory-as-exotype type-check:\n      a pattern that satisfies these criteria \"type-checks\" against the\n      theory exotype and is eligible for promotion."} {:name "RETROACTIVITY", :name-key "retroactivity", :slug "retroactivity", :text "      The key insight: when a pattern is canonized, its past instances are\n      retroactively reclassified. The event that prompted delivery-receipt\n      was, at the time, just a bug fix for silent message drops. After\n      canonicalization, it becomes evidence that G1 (mandatory-pur / delivery\n      receipt guarantee) was always structurally necessary.\n\n      What changes: the pattern's role (from incidental to necessary)\n      What does NOT change: the events themselves (commits, bug reports, fixes)\n\n      This is why \"future elements manifesting in the present\" is not\n      mystical — it is the retroactive recognition that a pattern which\n      seemed contingent was actually structurally required. The structure\n      was always there; the naming capacity to see it was not.\n\n      Formally:\n      > A newly formed specification retroactively rewrites the role of old\n      > patterns (from contingent repair to necessary constraint) without\n      > rewriting the old events themselves."} {:name "THE-ANCESTOR-SET", :name-key "the-ancestor-set", :slug "the-ancestor-set", :text "      The futon3 pattern library contains ~843 patterns. Of these, ~23 are\n      in futon-theory/ (the \"specification\"). The rest are the ancestor set:\n      patterns written under immediate pressure — data recovery failures,\n      persistence bugs, counter ratchet needs, agent coordination breakdowns.\n\n      These ancestors are:\n      - Genotype material: replicable, mutable, selectable symbol fragments\n      - Local adaptation history: each written to solve a specific failure\n      - Uncanonized rule fragments: they work but haven't been placed in a\n        unified constraint system\n\n      The specification is NOT the ancestor set. The specification is what\n      you get when you compress, standardize, and constrain the ancestor\n      set into an exotype (contract)."} {:name "THE-CANONIZATION-EVENT", :name-key "the-canonization-event", :slug "the-canonization-event", :text "      Each act of canonicalization should produce a durable record:\n\n      {:canonization/id :string\n       :canonization/ancestor-pattern :string    ; e.g. \"agency/delivery-receipt\"\n       :canonization/target-spec :string         ; e.g. \"coordination/mandatory-pur\"\n       :canonization/evidence [:string]          ; commits, incidents, PURs\n       :canonization/old-role :string            ; \"ad-hoc fix for silent drops\"\n       :canonization/new-role :string            ; \"ancestral evidence for G1\"\n       :canonization/rationale :string           ; why this promotion\n       :canonization/ts :string}\n\n      Without canonization events, the lineage from ancestor to specification\n      is invisible. With them, anyone can ask: \"why does this invariant exist?\"\n      and trace it back through the ancestor that demonstrated its necessity."} {:name "THE-GENEALOGY-GRAPH", :name-key "the-genealogy-graph", :slug "the-genealogy-graph", :text "      For each specification entry (invariant, event type, gate pattern),\n      the genealogy traces:\n      - Which ancestor pattern(s) demonstrated its necessity\n      - What incident(s) or failure(s) provided the evidence\n      - When and how it was promoted (canonization event)\n      - What compression rules applied (why this ancestor, not another)\n\n      This graph is the auditable generation narrative that connects\n      practice to theory without treating theory as either a priori or\n      arbitrary."} {:name "THE-THREE-ACTIONS-OF-CANONICALIZATION", :name-key "the-three-actions-of-canonicalization", :slug "the-three-actions-of-canonicalization", :text "      The process from ancestor to specification has three identifiable\n      actions that recur:\n\n      1. NAMING (命名):\n         Taking a recurring difficulty and its repair pattern and giving it\n         a citable name. Before naming, the fix is tacit knowledge. After\n         naming, it can be referenced, debated, and selected for or against.\n         Example: \"counter-ratchet\" names the pattern of \"key counts must\n         not drop unexpectedly\" — a problem that existed in practice long\n         before it had a name.\n\n      2. SELECTION (選擇):\n         Among multiple viable fixes, preferring those that are auditable,\n         replayable, and encapsulable as events and invariants. Not all\n         fixes are equally suitable for canonicalization — selection favors\n         those with proof-path-compatible structure.\n         Example: spit-with-append vs. write-through-with-sync — both fix\n         persistence, but only the latter is auditable and crash-safe.\n\n      3. CANALIZATION (固化):\n         Promoting a selected pattern from \"what worked once\" to \"what must\n         always hold.\" The pattern becomes an invariant, an event type, or\n         a stop-the-line condition. Future agents don't need to learn this\n         lesson — the system enforces it.\n         Example: \"always fsync after write\" promoted from a fix for data\n         loss to I0 (Persistence invariant): what you save is what you get\n         back.\n\n      These three actions are the Baldwin cycle applied to theory itself:\n      NAMING = exploration (giving structure to experience),\n      SELECTION = assimilation (fixing successful adaptations),\n      CANALIZATION = canalization (removing freedom, making success cheap)."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Without auditable canonicalization, specifications appear to be imposed\n    from outside — \"the theory says X\" with no explanation of why. This\n    makes theory fragile: it can be ignored because its authority is\n    unexplained. With canonicalization, theory authority traces back to\n    practice: \"the theory says X because incidents A, B, C demonstrated\n    that X is structurally necessary.\" This is not a priori authority\n    but empirical authority — the same kind the ancestors have, but\n    compressed and formalized."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Produce canonization events for the 12 coordination patterns]\n    next[Build genealogy graph for I0-I4 invariants back to futon3 incidents]\n    next[Define the spec extraction pipeline as a repeatable process]"}], :conclusion "  Futon theory specifications are not a priori axiom systems. They are the\n  result of retroactive canonicalization: taking patterns that emerged from\n  practice (ancestors), recognizing which ones are structurally necessary,\n  and promoting them to invariants and constraints. Events do not change;\n  their symbolic coordinates change — from \"incidental fix\" to \"necessary\n  constraint.\" This process must itself be auditable.", :directives {:audience "futon developers, pattern theorists, library maintainers", :factor "Keen investigation (dhammavicaya)", :keywords ["canonicalization" "ancestor" "retroactive" "lineage" "genealogy" "emergence" "baldwin"], :references ["futon-theory/baldwin-cycle" "futon-theory/theory-as-exotype" "futon-theory/local-gain-persistence" "futon-theory/four-types"], :sigils ["🔄/溯"], :title "Specification Emerges by Retroactive Canonicalization of Ancestors", :tone "analytic-foundational"}, :id "futon-theory/retroactive-canonicalization", :keywords ["canonicalization" "ancestor" "retroactive" "lineage" "genealogy" "emergence" "baldwin"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["futon-theory/baldwin-cycle" "futon-theory/theory-as-exotype" "futon-theory/local-gain-persistence" "futon-theory/four-types"], :scores {}, :sigils ["🔄/溯"], :source-path "library/futon-theory/retroactive-canonicalization.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Specification Emerges by Retroactive Canonicalization of Ancestors", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  A proof path is retrospectively stable if, after introducing any subsequent\n  pattern, invariant, or evidence, it doesn't need to be rewritten - only\n  relabeled. Stable proofs survive future refinement."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Systems evolve. New patterns get added, invariants get stronger,\n    better evidence becomes available. Retrospective stability asks: will the\n    work we do today survive tomorrow's improvements?"} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "The structural tension above manifests in the following concrete shapes (see substructure)."} {:name "INDICATORS-OF-INSTABILITY", :name-key "indicators-of-instability", :slug "indicators-of-instability", :text "      - Hardcoded assumptions that may not hold\n      - Implicit invariants not formally stated\n      - Edge cases handled by special logic\n      - Paths that rely on current system limitations"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Operate an explicit move on the named axis (see substructure below); steward the pattern against the declared discipline."} {:name "DESIGNING-FOR-STABILITY", :name-key "designing-for-stability", :slug "designing-for-stability", :text "      - Make all invariants explicit and testable\n      - Use patterns that generalize beyond current needs\n      - Document assumptions that might change\n      - Prefer simple paths over clever optimizations"} {:name "STABILITY-LEVELS", :name-key "stability-levels", :slug "stability-levels", :text "      - Unstable: New pattern/invariant invalidates the proof, must rewrite\n      - Semi-stable: Proof valid but now inefficient, geodesic changed\n      - Stable: Proof remains valid and geodesic, may get new labels"} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Engineering time is finite. Work that must be redone is wasted work.\n    Retrospective stability is an investment: pay slightly more now for\n    paths that remain valid as the system grows."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Add stability analysis to pattern selection criteria]\n    next[Track stability violations in PAR records]"}], :conclusion "  A proof path is retrospectively stable if, after introducing any subsequent\n  pattern, invariant, or evidence, it doesn't need to be rewritten - only\n  relabeled. Stable proofs survive future refinement.", :directives {:audience "futon developers, pattern theorists", :factor "Keen investigation (dhammavicaya)", :keywords ["stable" "relabel" "rewrite" "future" "proof" "robust" "invariant"], :references ["futon-theory/symbolic-geodesic" "futon-theory/proof-path" "futon-theory/counter-ratchet"], :sigils ["🌅/久"], :title "Retrospective Stability", :tone "analytic-foundational"}, :id "futon-theory/retrospective-stability", :keywords ["stable" "relabel" "rewrite" "future" "proof" "robust" "invariant"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["futon-theory/symbolic-geodesic" "futon-theory/proof-path" "futon-theory/counter-ratchet"], :scores {}, :sigils ["🌅/久"], :source-path "library/futon-theory/retrospective-stability.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Retrospective Stability", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  This specification extends the futonic vocabulary (futonic-logic.flexiarg) with\n  four operators that were implicit in practice but unnamed: ref (anaphora), reframe\n  (discourse redirection), reverse-morphogenesis (constraint-from-fruit), and ←\n  (speculative history). The central insight: asking a good question IS reverse\n  morphogenesis — given a formed artifact and a sense of what matters, infer the\n  constraints that would make the form yield what matters. The ← operator names\n  the speculative-history reading: 象 ← 香 means \"given this form (象) and this\n  salience (香), what constraints made the form stable?\"\n\n  These operators connect the futonic loop to futon6's mathematical dictionary:\n  the flexiformal pathway (EXPLORE → ASSIMILATE → CANALIZE) is reverse\n  morphogenesis applied to mathematical content at increasing formality.\n\n  == 0. RELATIONSHIP TO FUTONIC LOGIC ==\n\n  futonic-logic.flexiarg defines the vocabulary (象, 部, 咅, 香, 味, 鹽, 鹵, 皿,\n  未知, 非死非活, 🔮) and the futonic loop. This specification adds four operators\n  that act ON that vocabulary:\n\n  | Operator | Acts on | Does what |\n  | ref | 象, evidence entries | Points to existing meaning without creating new |\n  | reframe | ref edges | Redirects reference (\"but actually, the relevant thing is...\") |\n  | reverse-morphogenesis | 象 + fruit | Infers constraints from observed form and desired yield |\n  | ← (speculative history) | 象 + 香 | Reads backwards: what constraints induced by 香 made 象 stable? |\n\n  None of these replace existing vocabulary. They are higher-order operations\n  that the futonic loop performs but did not previously name.\n\n  == 1. ANAPHORA: REF VS BIND ==\n\n  (vocabulary-extension\n    (ref  (role reference)\n          (note \"Points to an existing binder/artifact without creating meaning.\n                 Discourse trigger: 'this', 'that', 'the above', 'it'.\"))\n    (bind (role creation)\n          (note \"Creates a new entry in a scope. An append!, a definition,\n                 a new evidence entry.\")))\n\n  A binding lives in exactly one scope:\n\n  (A8-scope-discipline\n    (forall b (implies (bind? b) (exists-unique s (in-scope b s))))\n    (forall s1 s2 (implies (parent s2 s1) (scope? s1) (scope? s2))))\n\n  A ref does not create meaning; it points to an existing binder:\n\n  (A9-anaphora\n    (forall r (implies (ref? r) (exists t (targets r t))))\n    (note \"Every ref edge has a target. Dangling refs are structural errors.\"))\n\n  Grounding in futon3c:\n  - ArtifactRef is a ref edge: {:ref/type :mission :ref/id \"M-forum-refactor\"}\n  - :evidence/in-reply-to is a ref edge: points to parent entry\n  - :evidence/fork-of is a ref edge: points to branch point\n  - append! is a bind: creates a new entry in the evidence store scope\n  - get-entry is a ref resolution: given an ID, return what it points to\n\n  The evidence landscape's append-only discipline means: binds are permanent,\n  refs are navigable, and the difference is structural, not accidental.\n\n  == 2. REFRAME: DISCOURSE REDIRECTION ==\n\n  (vocabulary-extension\n    (reframe (role discourse-redirection)\n             (note \"Higher-order edge that retargets a ref. 'But actually'\n                    changes what 'it' refers to.\")))\n\n  (A10-reframe\n    (forall u r t1 t2\n      (implies\n        (edge reframe u)\n        (edge ref r)\n        (targets r t1)\n        (retargeted-by r u t2)\n        (prefer-target r t2)))\n    (note \"A reframe can override the default target of a ref edge.\n           The original target is not destroyed; the preference changes.\"))\n\n  Grounding in futon3c:\n  - ClaimType :correction is a reframe operator\n  - EvidenceType :correction is a reframe operator\n  - thread-conjectures returns :refuted when a correction references a conjecture\n    — this is reframe in action: the conjecture's target (expected outcome) is\n    redirected to the correction's finding\n\n  Grounding in futonic practice:\n  - IF/HOWEVER/THEN/BECAUSE: HOWEVER is a reframe. It says \"the obvious ref\n    (IF implies THEN directly) is wrong; actually, this obstacle changes the\n    target.\"\n  - PUR prediction error: \"what surprised you\" is a detected reframe — the\n    expected target of the action was different from the actual target.\n\n  == 3. REVERSE MORPHOGENESIS ==\n\n  (vocabulary-extension\n    (reverse-morphogenesis\n      (role constraint-inference)\n      (note \"Given a formed artifact A and a salient yield/fruit F,\n             infer constraints C such that C would make A-with-F stable.\")))\n\n  (A11-reverse-morphogenesis\n    (forall A F C\n      (implies\n        (formed A)\n        (fruit-of A F)\n        (infer C (reverse-morphogenesis A F))\n        (constrain C A)))\n    (note \"The constraints are not known in advance; they are inferred from\n           the conjunction of form and desired fruit. This is design.\"))\n\n  (corollary-design-as-reverse-morphogenesis\n    (forall A F\n      (implies\n        (intended-fruit A F)\n        (design-step A (reverse-morphogenesis A F))))\n    (note \"Design is the deliberate application of reverse morphogenesis:\n           you know the fruit you want, you infer the constraints that\n           produce it from the given form.\"))\n\n  Grounding in futon practice:\n\n  | Context | A (form) | F (fruit) | C (inferred constraints) |\n  | L1 observer | Completed thread | Successful conclusion | Patterns that made it work |\n  | Gate pipeline | Task submission | Validated proof path | Gate criteria that ensure quality |\n  | Derivation xenotype | Domain (social coordination) | Working exotype | Requirements R1-R11 |\n  | Question-asking | Mathematical object | Desired understanding | The question that would produce it |\n\n  The last row is the key connection to futon6: asking a good mathematical\n  question is reverse morphogenesis. You have a mathematical form (象). You\n  have a sense of what understanding would be fruitful (香). The question is\n  the constraint you infer: what would I need to know/prove/construct such\n  that this form yields that understanding?\n\n  == 4. ← AS SPECULATIVE HISTORY ==\n\n  (vocabulary-extension\n    (← (role speculative-history)\n       (note \"Reads backwards: given a stabilized form and a pre-individual\n              field, infer the constraints that made the form stable under\n              that field. 象 ← 香.\")))\n\n  (A12-speculative-history\n    (← 象 香)\n    (expansion\n      (exists C\n        (and (constrain C 象)\n             (induced-by C 香)\n             (realize 象 C))))\n    (note \"象 is realized under constraints induced by 香. The ← operator\n           reads this relationship backwards: from the stabilized form,\n           recover the constraint-from-taste.\"))\n\n  ← is reverse-morphogenesis applied to the core vocabulary:\n  - A = 象 (the stabilized form, configuration)\n  - F = the stability itself (the form persists, couples to its environment)\n  - 香 = the pre-individual field that induces the constraints\n  - C = what ← infers\n\n  This is NOT teleology. The form was not designed to satisfy 香. The reading\n  is: 香 (as a difference field / gradient / taste) constrains which forms\n  become stable. 象 is one such stable form. ← recovers that relationship.\n\n  == 5. INSTRUMENTS AS ANTENNAS ==\n\n  (corollary-antenna\n    (forall I X\n      (implies\n        (instrument I) (field X)\n        (detect I X) (not (creates I X))))\n    (note \"Artifacts can detect fields they do not create. A peripheral\n           detects coordination patterns in the evidence landscape; it\n           does not create the coordination.\"))\n\n  Grounding:\n  - Peripherals (explore/edit/test/deploy/reflect) are antennas: they have\n    structural envelopes (tools, scope) that select from the evidence flow\n    but do not generate it.\n  - futon3a's pattern search (notions/compass) is an antenna: it detects\n    salience (香) in the pattern library without creating patterns.\n  - A mathematical dictionary entry (futon6) is an antenna: it stabilizes\n    and selects from latent mathematical structure, it does not create the\n    mathematics.\n\n  The banjo-drum example from the source axioms:\n  (latent (spectral-decomposition drum))\n  (realize banjo (constraint (stabilize pitch) (select modes)))\n  (reverse-morphogenesis banjo melody\n    (requires (latent (spectral-decomposition drum))))\n\n  The drum already carries the spectrum. The banjo stabilizes/selects.\n  The melody is not teleology — it is the fruit that reverse morphogenesis\n  reads backwards. \"Why does the banjo make this melody?\" ← \"Because the\n  drum's latent spectrum, under the banjo's structural constraints, yields\n  these stable modes.\" That is ←.\n\n  == 6. THE CIRCLE ← GROUP EXAMPLE ==\n\n  (example-circle-group\n    (← circle group)\n    (reading \"Circle is a stabilized 'return' phenomenon. Group is a\n             closure/composition constraint that makes the return coherent.\")\n    (constraint-schema group (closure composition identity inverse))\n    (note \"Non-Platonic: 'group' here is a constraint-schema, not a\n           realm-object. The group axioms are what ← infers when you\n           ask: what constraints make circular return stable?\"))\n\n  This is the bridge to futon6's mathematical dictionary: the dictionary\n  does not catalog mathematical objects (Platonic reading). It catalogs\n  constraint-schemas (← reading): for each mathematical form, what\n  constraints make it stable? The five dimensions from Corneli (2014)\n  — Context, Feedback, Quality, Structure, Heuristic — are decomposition\n  regimes (部) for reading ← across mathematical practice.\n\n  == 7. CONNECTION TO FUTON6: QUESTION-ASKING AS ← ==\n\n  The core claim for futon6:\n\n  (claim\n    (asking-good-question A)\n    (equals (reverse-morphogenesis A understanding))\n    (where\n      (A := mathematical-form)\n      (understanding := intended-fruit)\n      (question := inferred-constraint)))\n\n  A good mathematical question is: given this form (象), and this sense of\n  what understanding would be valuable (香), what constraint (question) would\n  make the form yield that understanding?\n\n  Bad questions fail at one of three points:\n  1. Wrong 象: the form is not well-specified (asking about \"infinity\" without\n     saying which infinity)\n  2. Wrong 香: the salience signal is not grounded (asking \"why is this\n     important?\" without a context that makes importance detectable)\n  3. Wrong ←: the constraint inference is broken (asking a question whose\n     answer would not actually yield the intended understanding)\n\n  The flexiformal pathway (EXPLORE → ASSIMILATE → CANALIZE) maps:\n  - EXPLORE = detect 香 (sense what matters, without formalizing)\n  - ASSIMILATE = apply ← (infer constraints from form + salience)\n  - CANALIZE = stabilize 象 (the constraints become structural, not effortful)\n\n  == 8. GROUNDING TABLE ==\n\n  | Axiom | Symbol | futonic-logic.flexiarg connection | futon3c evidence |\n  | A8 (scope) | bind, scope | R11 scope-bounded-handoff | :in/:out file boundaries on issues |\n  | A9 (anaphora) | ref | 象 as first-class referenceable configuration | ArtifactRef, :evidence/in-reply-to, :evidence/fork-of |\n  | A10 (reframe) | reframe | HOWEVER in IF/HOWEVER/THEN/BECAUSE | :correction claim-type, thread-conjectures :refuted |\n  | A11 (reverse morphogenesis) | reverse-morphogenesis | L1 observer pattern mining | thread-patterns on closed threads |\n  | A12 (speculative history) | ← | 香 constraining 象 stability | Evidence landscape as 香; shapes/pipeline as 象 |"} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Verify A8-A12 consistency with A1-A7 (no contradiction, complementary scope)]\n    next[Futon6 prototype: question-asking as reverse morphogenesis applied to P0 pattern-tagged entries]\n    next[Connect ← operator to futon3a search: notions/compass as ← resolution engine]\n    next[Test whether the reframe operator captures all correction/retargeting patterns in the evidence landscape]"}], :conclusion "  This specification extends the futonic vocabulary (futonic-logic.flexiarg) with\n  four operators that were implicit in practice but unnamed: ref (anaphora), reframe\n  (discourse redirection), reverse-morphogenesis (constraint-from-fruit), and ←\n  (speculative history). The central insight: asking a good question IS reverse\n  morphogenesis — given a formed artifact and a sense of what matters, infer the\n  constraints that would make the form yield what matters. The ← operator names\n  the speculative-history reading: 象 ← 香 means \"given this form (象) and this\n  salience (香), what constraints made the form stable?\"\n\n  These operators connect the futonic loop to futon6's mathematical dictionary:\n  the flexiformal pathway (EXPLORE → ASSIMILATE → CANALIZE) is reverse\n  morphogenesis applied to mathematical content at increasing formality.\n\n  == 0. RELATIONSHIP TO FUTONIC LOGIC ==\n\n  futonic-logic.flexiarg defines the vocabulary (象, 部, 咅, 香, 味, 鹽, 鹵, 皿,\n  未知, 非死非活, 🔮) and the futonic loop. This specification adds four operators\n  that act ON that vocabulary:\n\n  | Operator | Acts on | Does what |\n  | ref | 象, evidence entries | Points to existing meaning without creating new |\n  | reframe | ref edges | Redirects reference (\"but actually, the relevant thing is...\") |\n  | reverse-morphogenesis | 象 + fruit | Infers constraints from observed form and desired yield |\n  | ← (speculative history) | 象 + 香 | Reads backwards: what constraints induced by 香 made 象 stable? |\n\n  None of these replace existing vocabulary. They are higher-order operations\n  that the futonic loop performs but did not previously name.\n\n  == 1. ANAPHORA: REF VS BIND ==\n\n  (vocabulary-extension\n    (ref  (role reference)\n          (note \"Points to an existing binder/artifact without creating meaning.\n                 Discourse trigger: 'this', 'that', 'the above', 'it'.\"))\n    (bind (role creation)\n          (note \"Creates a new entry in a scope. An append!, a definition,\n                 a new evidence entry.\")))\n\n  A binding lives in exactly one scope:\n\n  (A8-scope-discipline\n    (forall b (implies (bind? b) (exists-unique s (in-scope b s))))\n    (forall s1 s2 (implies (parent s2 s1) (scope? s1) (scope? s2))))\n\n  A ref does not create meaning; it points to an existing binder:\n\n  (A9-anaphora\n    (forall r (implies (ref? r) (exists t (targets r t))))\n    (note \"Every ref edge has a target. Dangling refs are structural errors.\"))\n\n  Grounding in futon3c:\n  - ArtifactRef is a ref edge: {:ref/type :mission :ref/id \"M-forum-refactor\"}\n  - :evidence/in-reply-to is a ref edge: points to parent entry\n  - :evidence/fork-of is a ref edge: points to branch point\n  - append! is a bind: creates a new entry in the evidence store scope\n  - get-entry is a ref resolution: given an ID, return what it points to\n\n  The evidence landscape's append-only discipline means: binds are permanent,\n  refs are navigable, and the difference is structural, not accidental.\n\n  == 2. REFRAME: DISCOURSE REDIRECTION ==\n\n  (vocabulary-extension\n    (reframe (role discourse-redirection)\n             (note \"Higher-order edge that retargets a ref. 'But actually'\n                    changes what 'it' refers to.\")))\n\n  (A10-reframe\n    (forall u r t1 t2\n      (implies\n        (edge reframe u)\n        (edge ref r)\n        (targets r t1)\n        (retargeted-by r u t2)\n        (prefer-target r t2)))\n    (note \"A reframe can override the default target of a ref edge.\n           The original target is not destroyed; the preference changes.\"))\n\n  Grounding in futon3c:\n  - ClaimType :correction is a reframe operator\n  - EvidenceType :correction is a reframe operator\n  - thread-conjectures returns :refuted when a correction references a conjecture\n    — this is reframe in action: the conjecture's target (expected outcome) is\n    redirected to the correction's finding\n\n  Grounding in futonic practice:\n  - IF/HOWEVER/THEN/BECAUSE: HOWEVER is a reframe. It says \"the obvious ref\n    (IF implies THEN directly) is wrong; actually, this obstacle changes the\n    target.\"\n  - PUR prediction error: \"what surprised you\" is a detected reframe — the\n    expected target of the action was different from the actual target.\n\n  == 3. REVERSE MORPHOGENESIS ==\n\n  (vocabulary-extension\n    (reverse-morphogenesis\n      (role constraint-inference)\n      (note \"Given a formed artifact A and a salient yield/fruit F,\n             infer constraints C such that C would make A-with-F stable.\")))\n\n  (A11-reverse-morphogenesis\n    (forall A F C\n      (implies\n        (formed A)\n        (fruit-of A F)\n        (infer C (reverse-morphogenesis A F))\n        (constrain C A)))\n    (note \"The constraints are not known in advance; they are inferred from\n           the conjunction of form and desired fruit. This is design.\"))\n\n  (corollary-design-as-reverse-morphogenesis\n    (forall A F\n      (implies\n        (intended-fruit A F)\n        (design-step A (reverse-morphogenesis A F))))\n    (note \"Design is the deliberate application of reverse morphogenesis:\n           you know the fruit you want, you infer the constraints that\n           produce it from the given form.\"))\n\n  Grounding in futon practice:\n\n  | Context | A (form) | F (fruit) | C (inferred constraints) |\n  | L1 observer | Completed thread | Successful conclusion | Patterns that made it work |\n  | Gate pipeline | Task submission | Validated proof path | Gate criteria that ensure quality |\n  | Derivation xenotype | Domain (social coordination) | Working exotype | Requirements R1-R11 |\n  | Question-asking | Mathematical object | Desired understanding | The question that would produce it |\n\n  The last row is the key connection to futon6: asking a good mathematical\n  question is reverse morphogenesis. You have a mathematical form (象). You\n  have a sense of what understanding would be fruitful (香). The question is\n  the constraint you infer: what would I need to know/prove/construct such\n  that this form yields that understanding?\n\n  == 4. ← AS SPECULATIVE HISTORY ==\n\n  (vocabulary-extension\n    (← (role speculative-history)\n       (note \"Reads backwards: given a stabilized form and a pre-individual\n              field, infer the constraints that made the form stable under\n              that field. 象 ← 香.\")))\n\n  (A12-speculative-history\n    (← 象 香)\n    (expansion\n      (exists C\n        (and (constrain C 象)\n             (induced-by C 香)\n             (realize 象 C))))\n    (note \"象 is realized under constraints induced by 香. The ← operator\n           reads this relationship backwards: from the stabilized form,\n           recover the constraint-from-taste.\"))\n\n  ← is reverse-morphogenesis applied to the core vocabulary:\n  - A = 象 (the stabilized form, configuration)\n  - F = the stability itself (the form persists, couples to its environment)\n  - 香 = the pre-individual field that induces the constraints\n  - C = what ← infers\n\n  This is NOT teleology. The form was not designed to satisfy 香. The reading\n  is: 香 (as a difference field / gradient / taste) constrains which forms\n  become stable. 象 is one such stable form. ← recovers that relationship.\n\n  == 5. INSTRUMENTS AS ANTENNAS ==\n\n  (corollary-antenna\n    (forall I X\n      (implies\n        (instrument I) (field X)\n        (detect I X) (not (creates I X))))\n    (note \"Artifacts can detect fields they do not create. A peripheral\n           detects coordination patterns in the evidence landscape; it\n           does not create the coordination.\"))\n\n  Grounding:\n  - Peripherals (explore/edit/test/deploy/reflect) are antennas: they have\n    structural envelopes (tools, scope) that select from the evidence flow\n    but do not generate it.\n  - futon3a's pattern search (notions/compass) is an antenna: it detects\n    salience (香) in the pattern library without creating patterns.\n  - A mathematical dictionary entry (futon6) is an antenna: it stabilizes\n    and selects from latent mathematical structure, it does not create the\n    mathematics.\n\n  The banjo-drum example from the source axioms:\n  (latent (spectral-decomposition drum))\n  (realize banjo (constraint (stabilize pitch) (select modes)))\n  (reverse-morphogenesis banjo melody\n    (requires (latent (spectral-decomposition drum))))\n\n  The drum already carries the spectrum. The banjo stabilizes/selects.\n  The melody is not teleology — it is the fruit that reverse morphogenesis\n  reads backwards. \"Why does the banjo make this melody?\" ← \"Because the\n  drum's latent spectrum, under the banjo's structural constraints, yields\n  these stable modes.\" That is ←.\n\n  == 6. THE CIRCLE ← GROUP EXAMPLE ==\n\n  (example-circle-group\n    (← circle group)\n    (reading \"Circle is a stabilized 'return' phenomenon. Group is a\n             closure/composition constraint that makes the return coherent.\")\n    (constraint-schema group (closure composition identity inverse))\n    (note \"Non-Platonic: 'group' here is a constraint-schema, not a\n           realm-object. The group axioms are what ← infers when you\n           ask: what constraints make circular return stable?\"))\n\n  This is the bridge to futon6's mathematical dictionary: the dictionary\n  does not catalog mathematical objects (Platonic reading). It catalogs\n  constraint-schemas (← reading): for each mathematical form, what\n  constraints make it stable? The five dimensions from Corneli (2014)\n  — Context, Feedback, Quality, Structure, Heuristic — are decomposition\n  regimes (部) for reading ← across mathematical practice.\n\n  == 7. CONNECTION TO FUTON6: QUESTION-ASKING AS ← ==\n\n  The core claim for futon6:\n\n  (claim\n    (asking-good-question A)\n    (equals (reverse-morphogenesis A understanding))\n    (where\n      (A := mathematical-form)\n      (understanding := intended-fruit)\n      (question := inferred-constraint)))\n\n  A good mathematical question is: given this form (象), and this sense of\n  what understanding would be valuable (香), what constraint (question) would\n  make the form yield that understanding?\n\n  Bad questions fail at one of three points:\n  1. Wrong 象: the form is not well-specified (asking about \"infinity\" without\n     saying which infinity)\n  2. Wrong 香: the salience signal is not grounded (asking \"why is this\n     important?\" without a context that makes importance detectable)\n  3. Wrong ←: the constraint inference is broken (asking a question whose\n     answer would not actually yield the intended understanding)\n\n  The flexiformal pathway (EXPLORE → ASSIMILATE → CANALIZE) maps:\n  - EXPLORE = detect 香 (sense what matters, without formalizing)\n  - ASSIMILATE = apply ← (infer constraints from form + salience)\n  - CANALIZE = stabilize 象 (the constraints become structural, not effortful)\n\n  == 8. GROUNDING TABLE ==\n\n  | Axiom | Symbol | futonic-logic.flexiarg connection | futon3c evidence |\n  | A8 (scope) | bind, scope | R11 scope-bounded-handoff | :in/:out file boundaries on issues |\n  | A9 (anaphora) | ref | 象 as first-class referenceable configuration | ArtifactRef, :evidence/in-reply-to, :evidence/fork-of |\n  | A10 (reframe) | reframe | HOWEVER in IF/HOWEVER/THEN/BECAUSE | :correction claim-type, thread-conjectures :refuted |\n  | A11 (reverse morphogenesis) | reverse-morphogenesis | L1 observer pattern mining | thread-patterns on closed threads |\n  | A12 (speculative history) | ← | 香 constraining 象 stability | Evidence landscape as 香; shapes/pipeline as 象 |", :directives {:audience "futon developers, pattern theorists, futon6 mathematics dictionary", :factor "Keen investigation (dhammavicaya)", :keywords ["reverse-morphogenesis" "speculative-history" "anaphora" "reframe" "constraint-inference" "question-asking"], :references ["futon-theory/futonic-logic" "futon-theory/symbolic-individuation" "futon-theory/structural-tension-as-observation" "futon-theory/retroactive-canonicalization"], :sigils ["←"], :style "specification", :title "Reverse Morphogenesis: Constraint Inference from Form and Fruit", :tone "analytic-foundational"}, :id "futon-theory/reverse-morphogenesis", :keywords ["reverse-morphogenesis" "speculative-history" "anaphora" "reframe" "constraint-inference" "question-asking"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["futon-theory/futonic-logic" "futon-theory/symbolic-individuation" "futon-theory/structural-tension-as-observation" "futon-theory/retroactive-canonicalization"], :scores {}, :sigils ["←"], :source-path "library/futon-theory/reverse-morphogenesis.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Reverse Morphogenesis: Constraint Inference from Form and Fruit", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "I1: One entity per identity, no ambiguity.\n  One entity per identity, no ambiguity. Every entity has exactly one UUID,\n  and (source, external-id) maps to exactly one UUID. Duplicates rejected,\n  ambiguous lookups throw."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Identity confusion causes cascading errors. When one external-id\n    maps to two UUIDs, which one is correct? The answer must be: this cannot\n    happen. Identity invariants are enforced BEFORE write, not discovered after."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "The applicability scope of the pattern (see substructure)."} {:name "REQUIREMENTS", :name-key "requirements", :slug "requirements", :text "      - Every entity has exactly one UUID\n      - (source, external-id) tuple maps to exactly one UUID\n      - Duplicate external IDs rejected BEFORE write (not after)\n      - Lookups with ambiguous matches throw, never silently pick one"} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "The structural tension above manifests in the following concrete shapes (see substructure)."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "      Duplicate external-id -> 409 (conflict)\n      Ambiguous lookup -> 409 (conflict)\n      Missing required identity -> 400 (bad request)"} {:name "ANTI-PATTERNS", :name-key "anti-patterns", :slug "anti-patterns", :text "      - Silently picking first match on ambiguous lookup\n      - Creating new UUID when external-id already exists\n      - Using external-id as UUID (they serve different purposes)"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Operate an explicit move on the named axis (see substructure below); steward the pattern against the declared discipline."} {:name "ENFORCEMENT", :name-key "enforcement", :slug "enforcement", :text "      Layer 1 validates before Layer 0 writes:\n      - Uniqueness check for existing (source, external-id) mapping\n      - On conflict: 409 with existing entity reference\n      - On ambiguous lookup: 409 with all matches listed"} {:name "DOMAIN-INSTANTIATION", :name-key "domain-instantiation", :slug "domain-instantiation", :text "      See `storage/identity-uniqueness` for concrete implementation patterns."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Identity is the foundation of relations. If entity A relates to entity B,\n    we must know exactly which entities those are. Ambiguity in identity\n    propagates to ambiguity in relations, making the entire graph unreliable."}], :conclusion "I1: One entity per identity, no ambiguity.\n  One entity per identity, no ambiguity. Every entity has exactly one UUID,\n  and (source, external-id) maps to exactly one UUID. Duplicates rejected,\n  ambiguous lookups throw.", :directives {:audience "futon developers, identity layer maintainers", :factor "Keen investigation (dhammavicaya)", :keywords ["identity" "uuid" "external-id" "unique" "duplicate" "ambiguous" "lookup"], :references ["futon-theory/durability-first" "futon-theory/error-hierarchy" "futon-theory/counter-ratchet"], :sigils ["🍁/一"], :title "Single Source of Truth (Invariant 1)", :tone "analytic-foundational"}, :id "futon-theory/single-source-of-truth", :keywords ["identity" "uuid" "external-id" "unique" "duplicate" "ambiguous" "lookup"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["futon-theory/durability-first" "futon-theory/error-hierarchy" "futon-theory/counter-ratchet"], :scores {}, :sigils ["🍁/一"], :source-path "library/futon-theory/single-source-of-truth.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Single Source of Truth (Invariant 1)", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  When invariants fail, block APPLY_CHANGE. Continue to allow OBSERVE and\n  VERIFY until the issue is fixed. The line stops for production, not for\n  diagnosis. Borrowed from Toyota Production System."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Invariant violations indicate system corruption or logic errors.\n    Continuing to apply changes while invariants are failing compounds the\n    problem. Stop-the-line halts destructive operations while keeping\n    diagnostic operations available."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "The structural tension above manifests in the following concrete shapes (see substructure)."} {:name "ANTI-PATTERNS", :name-key "anti-patterns", :slug "anti-patterns", :text "      - Force-pushing changes despite violations\n      - Disabling invariant checks to \"get things working\"\n      - Logging violations but continuing anyway\n      - Waiting for someone else to notice"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Operate an explicit move on the named axis (see substructure below); steward the pattern against the declared discipline."} {:name "NOTIFICATIONS", :name-key "notifications", :slug "notifications", :text "      - Immediate: Alert to on-call with violation details\n      - Contextual: Which invariant, which entity, what discrepancy\n      - Actionable: Suggested diagnostic steps\n      - Persistent: Status visible in health endpoints"} {:name "PROTOCOL", :name-key "protocol", :slug "protocol", :text "      1. INVARIANT_CHECK fails\n      2. System enters STOPPED state\n      3. APPLY_CHANGE blocked for all sessions\n      4. OBSERVE allowed (for diagnosis)\n      5. VERIFY allowed (to test fixes)\n      6. PROOF_COMMIT blocked until invariants pass\n      7. On fix: INVARIANT_CHECK succeeds, line resumes"} {:name "RECOVERY", :name-key "recovery", :slug "recovery", :text "      1. Diagnose: Use OBSERVE to understand the violation\n      2. Fix: Apply correction (may require special override)\n      3. Verify: Use VERIFY to confirm fix\n      4. Resume: Invariants pass, line reopens"} {:name "STOPPED-STATE-BEHAVIOR", :name-key "stopped-state-behavior", :slug "stopped-state-behavior", :text "      - Read operations: ALLOWED\n      - Query operations: ALLOWED\n      - Diagnostic operations: ALLOWED\n      - Write operations: BLOCKED\n      - Commit operations: BLOCKED\n      - Session clock-out: ALLOWED (with failure status)"} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Continuing to run with broken invariants turns one problem into many.\n    A single write to corrupt storage can cascade. Stopping early contains\n    the blast radius and preserves diagnostic context."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Implement stopped-state middleware in futon1a]\n    next[Add stop-the-line status to health endpoint]"}], :conclusion "  When invariants fail, block APPLY_CHANGE. Continue to allow OBSERVE and\n  VERIFY until the issue is fixed. The line stops for production, not for\n  diagnosis. Borrowed from Toyota Production System.", :directives {:audience "futon developers, on-call engineers", :factor "Keen investigation (dhammavicaya)", :keywords ["stop" "block" "halt" "invariant" "failure" "observe" "verify" "fix"], :references ["futon-theory/error-hierarchy" "futon-theory/counter-ratchet" "futon-theory/agent-contract"], :sigils ["🌽/止"], :title "Stop-the-Line Protocol", :tone "analytic-foundational"}, :id "futon-theory/stop-the-line", :keywords ["stop" "block" "halt" "invariant" "failure" "observe" "verify" "fix"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["futon-theory/error-hierarchy" "futon-theory/counter-ratchet" "futon-theory/agent-contract"], :scores {}, :sigils ["🌽/止"], :source-path "library/futon-theory/stop-the-line.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Stop-the-Line Protocol", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  The coordination system operates as two nested AIF loops sharing the same\n  boundary (the gate pipeline). The fast loop governs task execution; the\n  glacial loop governs pattern library evolution. Structural tension — the\n  gap between what the library can express and what coordination needs — is\n  the observation vector for the glacial loop. This makes the AIF wiring\n  diagram framework not a metaphor for the coordination rewrite but its\n  direct structural specification."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Chapter 0 (AIF as a wiring diagram) establishes that AIF is\n    the first member of a diagram family, characterised by six invariants\n    (I1-I6). The futon3-coordination.edn wiring diagram already satisfies\n    these invariants at the task execution timescale: I1 (gate pipeline is\n    the boundary), I2 (observation of environment + action via artifacts),\n    I3 (patterns glacial, missions slow, gates fast), I4 (agents cannot\n    rewrite patterns or missions), I5 (library must be adequate to guide\n    work), I6 (default mode provides parallel path). But this covers only\n    one timescale. The retroactive canonicalization pattern describes a\n    second process — library evolution — that operates at a glacial\n    timescale and has its own observation-inference-action structure. The\n    question is: what does this second process observe?"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Operate an explicit move on the named axis (see substructure below); steward the pattern against the declared discipline."} {:name "FREE-ENERGY-AND-CANONICALIZATION", :name-key "free-energy-and-canonicalization", :slug "free-energy-and-canonicalization", :text "      In AIF, free energy is the divergence between the agent's model and\n      reality. In library evolution:\n\n      - Free energy = accumulated structural irritation. The gap between\n        what the library says should work and what actually works in\n        practice.\n      - Perception = NAMING. Giving an irritation a citable name compresses\n        raw tension into a representable observation. Before naming, the\n        tension is sub-symbolic. After naming, it can be scored and compared.\n      - Inference = SELECTION. Among named tensions, selecting those that\n        are auditable, replayable, and machine-checkable. This updates\n        the model (library) by choosing which observations to take\n        seriously.\n      - Action = CANALIZATION. Promoting a selected pattern to invariant\n        status. This changes the world — future tasks are now governed by\n        the new invariant. The free energy should decrease: the irritation\n        that prompted canonicalization should stop recurring.\n\n      The Baldwin cycle IS the policy for the glacial AIF loop. And the\n      canonization event (from retroactive-canonicalization) is the proof\n      path record for that policy — the evidence that the action was taken\n      and why."} {:name "INVARIANT-MAPPING", :name-key "invariant-mapping", :slug "invariant-mapping", :text "      The six Chapter 0 invariants apply to both loops:\n\n      | Invariant | Level 0 (Task) | Level 1 (Library) |\n      | I1 Boundary | Gate pipeline | Library publish/promote boundary |\n      | I2 Obs-Action | Read env / produce artifacts | Observe tensions / canonicalize |\n      | I3 Timescale | Gates fast, patterns glacial | Tasks fast, canonicalization glacial |\n      | I4 Exogeneity | Agents can't rewrite patterns | Fast tensions can't force canonicalization |\n      | I5 Adequacy | Library must guide tasks | Library must track recurring tensions |\n      | I6 Closure | Default mode sustains agents | Gap-PSRs sustain library evolution |\n\n      I4 is critical for both loops. At Level 0: agents cannot rewrite the\n      patterns that guide them (wireheading prevention). At Level 1: the\n      accumulation of structural tensions cannot force canonicalization\n      without the deliberate three-step process (NAMING → SELECTION →\n      CANALIZATION). If tensions could directly modify the library, the\n      library would overfit to noise — the glacial equivalent of\n      wireheading."} {:name "THE-DIRECT-AIF-CONNECTION", :name-key "the-direct-aif-connection", :slug "the-direct-aif-connection", :text "      The coordination rewrite argument (coordination/ARGUMENT) concludes\n      that pattern phylogeny, development history, and conversation graphs\n      are one structure. The AIF connection explains WHY they converge:\n\n      - The pattern phylogeny is the glacial loop's model evolution —\n        how the generative model (library) changes over time.\n      - The development history is the fast loop's action record —\n        what the agent did at each step.\n      - The conversation graph is the inter-agent coordination record —\n        how multiple agents running the fast loop exchange observations\n        and negotiate policies.\n\n      These three records are generated by the same diagram at different\n      timescales and between different components. They converge because\n      they are produced by one system, not three.\n\n      The AIF wiring diagram is therefore not an organizational metaphor\n      for the coordination rewrite. It is the structural specification.\n      The futon3-coordination.edn diagram IS the Level 0 loop. The\n      retroactive canonicalization process IS the Level 1 loop. The gate\n      pipeline IS the shared boundary. The pattern library IS the\n      generative model. And structural tension IS free energy."} {:name "THE-OBSERVATION-VECTOR", :name-key "the-observation-vector", :slug "the-observation-vector", :text "      What does the glacial loop observe? Three kinds of structural tension\n      serve as its observation vector:\n\n      1. STRUCTURAL IRRITATION (结构刺激):\n         A recurring problem shape that the current symbol system cannot\n         cleanly express. The same difficulty appears across multiple tasks,\n         but no existing pattern addresses it directly. Agents work around it\n         with ad-hoc fixes. Each workaround is a prediction error: the\n         generative model (library) predicted its patterns would cover the\n         work, but reality produced a gap.\n\n         Detection: gap-PSRs accumulating around the same structural theme.\n         Frequency across tasks. Multiple agents hitting the same wall.\n\n      2. PRE-SYMBOLIC PRESSURE (前符号压力):\n         A force that pushes the symbol system to expand before the expansion\n         has a name. The pressure is felt as difficulty — not the difficulty\n         of a hard task, but the difficulty of expressing what the task IS.\n         The existing vocabulary is insufficient. This is the force that\n         drives NAMING: giving structure to experience that currently has\n         none.\n\n         Detection: tasks that are hard to scope (G5 struggles), hard to\n         match to patterns (G3 produces low-confidence PSRs), hard to\n         validate (G1 criteria feel arbitrary). The difficulty is not in\n         the work but in the coordination of the work.\n\n      3. TRANS-SITUATIONAL REAPPEARANCE (跨情境复现):\n         The same structural role showing up across unrelated contexts.\n         A pattern that was written for storage reappears in coordination.\n         A fix that was ad-hoc in futon1a turns out to be necessary in\n         futon3. The reappearance is evidence that the pattern is not\n         contingent but structurally required — the prediction error is\n         systematic, not noise. The model needs a structural update\n         (canonicalization), not a parameter tweak (one more ad-hoc fix).\n\n         Detection: patterns or fixes independently discovered in different\n         repos, missions, or agent contexts. futon3a's meme.arrow typed\n         edges appearing in different subgraphs. The same gate pattern\n         solving problems in both the agency reading and the pattern reading."} {:name "THE-TWO-LOOPS", :name-key "the-two-loops", :slug "the-two-loops", :text "      The coordination system contains two AIF loops at different timescales:\n\n      LEVEL 0 — Task Execution (fast):\n      - Observe: read environment (codebase, project state)\n      - Perceive: extract task requirements, match against capabilities\n      - Policy: select pattern (PSR), plan execution\n      - Act: execute through gates, produce artifacts\n      - Generative model: current pattern selections\n      - Free energy: task failure, gap declarations, PUR prediction errors\n\n      LEVEL 1 — Library Evolution (glacial):\n      - Observe: accumulate structural tensions across many task executions\n      - Perceive: recognise recurring tensions as candidate ancestors\n      - Policy: select candidates for canonicalization (NAMING → SELECTION)\n      - Act: canonicalize — promote pattern to invariant (CANALIZATION)\n      - Generative model: the pattern library itself\n      - Free energy: accumulated structural irritation\n\n      These loops share the same boundary: the gate pipeline. Level 0\n      operates within the pipeline (one task at a time). Level 1 operates\n      on the pipeline (reshaping the patterns that govern future tasks).\n      This is VSM-style nesting (Chapter 0 §3.2): the glacial loop contains\n      and constrains the fast loop."} {:name "WHAT-THIS-ADDS", :name-key "what-this-adds", :slug "what-this-adds", :text "      Previous patterns established:\n      - Tasks are BHK arrows (task-as-arrow)\n      - Specs emerge from ancestors (retroactive-canonicalization)\n      - The rewrite composes components through a pipeline into a graph\n        (coordination/ARGUMENT)\n\n      This pattern adds:\n      - The two-loop structure is not analogy but direct AIF specification\n      - Structural tension has three identifiable components that serve as\n        the glacial loop's observation vector\n      - The graph convergence follows from the diagram structure, not from\n        coincidence\n      - The six Chapter 0 invariants constrain both loops, providing\n        checkable certification criteria for the rewrite"} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Without this connection, the AIF wiring diagram framework and the\n    coordination rewrite are parallel projects — one theoretical, one\n    engineering. With it, they are the same project. The wiring diagram\n    specifies what to build. The coordination patterns specify how each\n    component works. The argument establishes why it must be one system.\n    And the structural tension criteria tell you when the library needs\n    to evolve — which is the question that retroactive canonicalization\n    answers but does not itself pose."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Add structural tension observation vector to futon3-coordination.edn as Level 1 inputs]\n    next[Map the three tension criteria to gate pipeline telemetry (which gates detect which tensions)]\n    next[Verify I4 exogeneity in Level 1: trace all paths from fast-loop outputs to library-write]\n    next[Write futon5 ct/agent.clj with two-loop validation: check both timescales against I1-I6]"}], :conclusion "  The coordination system operates as two nested AIF loops sharing the same\n  boundary (the gate pipeline). The fast loop governs task execution; the\n  glacial loop governs pattern library evolution. Structural tension — the\n  gap between what the library can express and what coordination needs — is\n  the observation vector for the glacial loop. This makes the AIF wiring\n  diagram framework not a metaphor for the coordination rewrite but its\n  direct structural specification.", :directives {:audience "futon developers, pattern theorists, AIF implementers", :factor "Keen investigation (dhammavicaya)", :keywords ["tension" "observation" "aif" "free-energy" "ancestor" "canonicalization" "nested" "timescale"], :references ["futon-theory/retroactive-canonicalization" "futon-theory/task-as-arrow" "futon-theory/baldwin-cycle" "futon-theory/four-types" "coordination/ARGUMENT"], :sigils ["🔬/张"], :title "Structural Tension Is the Observation Vector for Library Evolution", :tone "analytic-foundational"}, :id "futon-theory/structural-tension-as-observation", :keywords ["tension" "observation" "aif" "free-energy" "ancestor" "canonicalization" "nested" "timescale"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["futon-theory/retroactive-canonicalization" "futon-theory/task-as-arrow" "futon-theory/baldwin-cycle" "futon-theory/four-types" "coordination/ARGUMENT"], :scores {}, :sigils ["🔬/张"], :source-path "library/futon-theory/structural-tension-as-observation.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Structural Tension Is the Observation Vector for Library Evolution", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  The symbolic geodesic is the shortest defensible path in symbolic state\n  space - the minimal proof path from intent to verified claim. It is not\n  about speed but about eliminating unnecessary complexity."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "In differential geometry, a geodesic is the shortest path between\n    two points on a curved surface. In futon theory, the symbolic geodesic is\n    the minimal sequence of operations that moves from intent to verified\n    outcome while maintaining auditability."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Operate an explicit move on the named axis (see substructure below); steward the pattern against the declared discipline."} {:name "APPLICATION", :name-key "application", :slug "application", :text "      - Pattern selection: Choose patterns that shorten the geodesic\n      - Refactoring: Identify steps that can be eliminated\n      - Learning: Compare actual paths to geodesics for prediction improvement\n      - Review: Is this the shortest defensible path, or are there detours?"} {:name "GEODESIC-VS-ACTUAL", :name-key "geodesic-vs-actual", :slug "geodesic-vs-actual", :text "      Real sessions may deviate from the geodesic (exploration, false starts).\n      The geodesic is the retrospective \"ideal path\" that could have achieved\n      the same outcome with minimal steps. Session logs capture actual paths;\n      analysis can extract geodesics for learning."} {:name "PROPERTIES", :name-key "properties", :slug "properties", :text "      - Minimal: No operation can be removed without breaking the proof\n      - Direct: Each step advances toward the goal\n      - Defensible: Every step has explicit justification\n      - Verifiable: The path can be replayed and checked"} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Complexity is cost. Every step in a proof path is a potential failure\n    point, a debugging surface, a thing to maintain. The geodesic minimizes\n    this surface while maintaining defensibility."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Implement geodesic extraction from session logs]\n    next[Add geodesic comparison to PAR analysis]"}], :conclusion "  The symbolic geodesic is the shortest defensible path in symbolic state\n  space - the minimal proof path from intent to verified claim. It is not\n  about speed but about eliminating unnecessary complexity.", :directives {:audience "futon developers, pattern theorists", :factor "Keen investigation (dhammavicaya)", :keywords ["shortest" "path" "minimal" "defensible" "proof" "direct" "efficient"], :references ["futon-theory/proof-path" "futon-theory/retrospective-stability" "futon-theory/event-protocol"], :sigils ["🚞/小"], :title "Symbolic Geodesic", :tone "analytic-foundational"}, :id "futon-theory/symbolic-geodesic", :keywords ["shortest" "path" "minimal" "defensible" "proof" "direct" "efficient"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["futon-theory/proof-path" "futon-theory/retrospective-stability" "futon-theory/event-protocol"], :scores {}, :sigils ["🚞/小"], :source-path "library/futon-theory/symbolic-geodesic.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Symbolic Geodesic", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  When a system exceeds the holding capacity of its participants (agents,\n  humans, or both), it must decompose. Consensus reality treats this as\n  abstraction — lossy compression that discards detail. Futon theory treats\n  it as individuation — the recognition that the compound symbol already\n  encoded its own decomposition. The interaction between individuated parts\n  is itself a perceivable compound symbol with recoverable internal structure."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "The symbolic geodesic pattern describes the shortest path\n    through symbolic state space — individual trajectories from intent to\n    verified claim. But mathematical practice and software development\n    involve not just individual paths but interactions between paths:\n    how does this subsystem's output become that subsystem's input? How\n    do three agents coordinate across three timescales? The interaction\n    itself has structure, and that structure can be perceived as a symbol."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Operate an explicit move on the named axis (see substructure below); steward the pattern against the declared discipline."} {:name "HOLDING-CAPACITY-AS-TIMESCALE", :name-key "holding-capacity-as-timescale", :slug "holding-capacity-as-timescale", :text "      Why does complexity exceed holding capacity? Because participants\n      operate at characteristic timescales. Joe sleeps and his \"context\n      compacts overnight.\" Claude's context window fills and compresses.\n      Codex processes one task at a time. These are timescale constraints\n      on the participants, analogous to the timescale constraints on the\n      system (I3: timescale separation).\n\n      The structural correspondence: the system's timescale separation\n      (fast/task/glacial/social) mirrors the participants' holding capacity\n      limits (context windows, sleep cycles, working memory). Both drive\n      individuation. Both are structural, not accidental.\n\n      A system that perfectly matched its participants' holding capacity\n      would never need to individuate. But real systems grow, and real\n      participants have finite windows. Individuation is how growth and\n      finitude coexist."} {:name "PERCEIVING-FUTURE-INTERACTIONS", :name-key "perceiving-future-interactions", :slug "perceiving-future-interactions", :text "      The symbolic geodesic says: you can perceive future elements in the\n      present by tracing symbolic paths (specs, proofs, program types).\n\n      Individuation extends this: you can perceive future interactions\n      between elements by tracing the compound symbol's internal structure.\n      If I specify futon3b's gate pipeline and futon3c's agency dispatch\n      separately, I can perceive their interaction before they are connected —\n      because the compound symbol (the coordination architecture) encodes\n      the interaction in its timescale structure.\n\n      This is not prediction. It is perception of structure that is already\n      present in the symbols. The future interaction is not uncertain — it\n      is encoded. The only question is whether the participants have the\n      holding capacity to perceive the encoding.\n\n      This is why \"please pass the salt\" and the 鹽 decomposition are\n      not two descriptions of the same thing. The speech act treats salt\n      as an opaque referent. The decomposition treats it as a structured\n      symbol whose internal process (brine + vessel → salt) is perceivable.\n      Both are true. The futonic perspective does not replace consensus\n      reality — it adds the structural layer that makes interaction\n      between symbols perceivable as symbols in their own right."} {:name "SYMBOLIC-GEODESICS-AND-INTERACTIONS", :name-key "symbolic-geodesics-and-interactions", :slug "symbolic-geodesics-and-interactions", :text "      The symbolic geodesic pattern describes individual paths:\n        spec → proof → verified claim\n\n      Individuation adds a second level: interactions between paths:\n        (futon3c dispatches agent) → (futon3b validates through gates) →\n        (futon3b L1 canonicalizes) → (futon3a queries the result)\n\n      This interaction pattern is itself a compound symbol. It has internal\n      structure (the timescale ordering), a process-origin (why social feeds\n      into task feeds into glacial), and a decomposition (the individual\n      loops). Just as 鹽 can be perceived as one thing (salt) or decomposed\n      (brine-source + vessel), the three-futon interaction can be perceived\n      as one thing (the futon stack's coordination architecture) or decomposed\n      (three loops at three timescales with defined interaction points)."} {:name "THE-CONSENSUS-VIEW", :name-key "the-consensus-view", :slug "the-consensus-view", :text "      All participants have bounded holding capacity. Joe can hold N things.\n      Claude can hold M. Codex can hold K. When a system's complexity exceeds\n      min(N, M, K), coordination breaks down — not because any participant\n      is deficient but because the system has grown beyond what can be held\n      as one thing. The consensus response is abstraction: name the parts,\n      draw boundaries, interact through interfaces. This works but treats\n      the decomposition as an engineering decision — \"we chose to split\n      futon3 into three repos because it was getting complex.\""} {:name "THE-FUTONIC-VIEW", :name-key "the-futonic-view", :slug "the-futonic-view", :text "      The decomposition was not chosen. It was perceived. The compound\n      symbol already contained its decomposition in its internal structure,\n      just as 鹽 (salt) contains 鹵 (鹵: saline-land, brine-source) + 皿\n      (皿: dish, vessel, container). The character does not abbreviate the\n      salt-making process — it encodes it: brine from saline land refined\n      into a vessel. The structure is recoverable from the symbol.\n\n      When futon3 individuated into futon3a/3b/3c, the split followed\n      structural lines that were already present:\n      - futon3a (query) operated at fast/query timescale\n      - futon3b (development) operated at task + glacial timescale\n      - futon3c (realtime) operated at social/realtime timescale\n\n      These timescales were not imposed by the split — they were the reason\n      the split became perceivable. The compound symbol \"futon3\" contained\n      three processes at three timescales. When complexity exceeded holding\n      capacity, the internal structure surfaced as three individuated symbols."} {:name "WHAT-INDIVIDUATION-PRESERVES", :name-key "what-individuation-preserves", :slug "what-individuation-preserves", :text "      When a compound symbol individuates:\n      1. The parts retain their interaction structure (they know how to\n         compose because they were composed before the split)\n      2. The compound symbol remains available (you can still talk about\n         \"the futon3 stack\" even after the split)\n      3. The decomposition is auditable (why these boundaries, not others?)\n      4. The interaction between parts is a compound symbol in its own right\n         (the \"War Room\" level coordination pattern)\n\n      What individuation does NOT do:\n      - It does not discard the relationships between parts\n      - It does not create new structure (it reveals existing structure)\n      - It does not require the parts to be independent (they interact\n        through defined ports, like AIF wiring diagram components)"} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Without this pattern, decomposition appears arbitrary — an engineering\n    convenience, not a structural necessity. With it, decomposition is the\n    natural consequence of compound symbols whose internal structure\n    becomes perceivable when complexity exceeds holding capacity. The\n    three-futon split is not a refactoring decision but a perception\n    event: the moment when futon3's internal timescale structure surfaced\n    as three individuated symbols, each carrying its own process-origin,\n    each interacting through the compound symbol they emerged from."} {:name "EVIDENCE", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "      evidence[futon3 → futon3a/3b/3c split followed timescale boundaries already present in code]\n      evidence[War Room document (holes/war-room.md) captures the compound interaction structure]\n      evidence[futon3b mission complete and coherent without reference to Agency/Forum/peripherals]\n      evidence[futon3c's 13 realtime patterns disjoint from futon3b's 12 coordination patterns]\n      evidence[Codex produced \"buzzword potpourri\" when holding capacity exceeded by mission complexity — individuation resolved the comprehension gap by reducing each part to holdable size]"} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Verify that interaction between 3a/3b/3c is itself expressible as an AIF wiring diagram]\n    next[Add individuation events to the canonicalization record format]\n    next[Test whether the compound symbol (futon3 stack) remains queryable via futon3a search after the split]"}], :conclusion "  When a system exceeds the holding capacity of its participants (agents,\n  humans, or both), it must decompose. Consensus reality treats this as\n  abstraction — lossy compression that discards detail. Futon theory treats\n  it as individuation — the recognition that the compound symbol already\n  encoded its own decomposition. The interaction between individuated parts\n  is itself a perceivable compound symbol with recoverable internal structure.", :directives {:audience "futon developers, pattern theorists, war room", :factor "Equanimity (upekkhā)", :keywords ["individuation" "compound" "symbol" "decomposition" "timescale" "structure" "perception" "complexity" "holding-capacity"], :references ["futon-theory/symbolic-geodesic" "futon-theory/structural-tension-as-observation" "futon-theory/retroactive-canonicalization" "futon-theory/theory-as-exotype"], :sigils ["🧊/鹽"], :title "Compound Symbols Individuate When Their Internal Structure Becomes Perceivable", :tone "analytic-foundational"}, :id "futon-theory/symbolic-individuation", :keywords ["individuation" "compound" "symbol" "decomposition" "timescale" "structure" "perception" "complexity" "holding-capacity"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["futon-theory/symbolic-geodesic" "futon-theory/structural-tension-as-observation" "futon-theory/retroactive-canonicalization" "futon-theory/theory-as-exotype"], :scores {}, :sigils ["🧊/鹽"], :source-path "library/futon-theory/symbolic-individuation.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Compound Symbols Individuate When Their Internal Structure Becomes Perceivable", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  A task in the futon stack is not a work description — it is a BHK arrow.\n  It is valid if and only if it constructively transforms a proven start\n  state into a proven goal state. The proof path record IS the proof of\n  that arrow. Gates are arrows; the pipeline is their composition."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "The Curry-Howard operational principle says programs are proofs\n    of their specifications. The BHK (Brouwer-Heyting-Kolmogorov) interpretation\n    makes this more precise: a proof of A -> B is not a truth judgment but a\n    METHOD — give me any proof of A, and I construct a proof of B. This is\n    not analogy. It is structural identity between tasks and arrows."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Operate an explicit move on the named axis (see substructure below); steward the pattern against the declared discipline."} {:name "BHK-ARROW-SEMANTICS", :name-key "bhk-arrow-semantics", :slug "bhk-arrow-semantics", :text "      In BHK proof theory:\n      - A proof of A -> B is a constructive transformation\n      - It takes a proof of A as input (not just a proposition)\n      - It produces a proof of B as output (not just an artifact)\n      - The transformation itself is the proof\n\n      In futon:\n      - A is the start state (spec, invariants hold, baseline)\n      - B is the goal state (code exists, tests pass, invariants hold)\n      - A proof of A is the current workspace + evidence\n      - A proof of B is code + tests + VERIFY + PROOF_COMMIT\n      - The task is the constructive transformation A -> B\n      - The proof path record IS the proof of the arrow\n\n      Therefore: a task is valid iff it genuinely constructs a proof of B\n      from a proof of A. Writing code is not enough — writing code is\n      constructing an artifact, not a proof. The proof requires verification,\n      invariant checking, and durable commitment."} {:name "COMPOSITION", :name-key "composition", :slug "composition", :text "      The full pipeline is arrow composition:\n      (raw-request) -> (durable-evidence) = G0 . G1 . G2 . G3 . G4 . G5\n\n      Arrow composition in BHK: if you have proofs of A -> B and B -> C,\n      you can compose them into a proof of A -> C.\n\n      In futon: if G5 proves the task is valid and G4 proves the agent is\n      authorized, then G5;G4 proves the task is valid AND the agent is\n      authorized. The full composition proves the entire chain.\n\n      This is why gates must be sequential and each must produce a proof:\n      if any gate produces only an artifact (not a proof), the composition\n      breaks — downstream gates receive unproven input."} {:name "CONSEQUENCES", :name-key "consequences", :slug "consequences", :text "      1. TASK PRECONDITIONS MUST BE PROVEN (not assumed):\n         In BHK, you cannot say \"assume A\" — you must have A's proof.\n         In futon: a task cannot start from a vague state. The start state\n         must have evidence (baseline refs, OBSERVE results). Without proven\n         input, the arrow has no domain.\n\n      2. TASK OUTPUT MUST BE A PROOF (not just an artifact):\n         In BHK, B is not the proposition but B's proof.\n         In futon: writing code is not completing a task. The task is complete\n         when code + tests + VERIFY + PROOF_COMMIT exist. Without proof,\n         the arrow has no codomain.\n\n      3. TASKS ARE COMPOSABLE (because arrows compose):\n         In BHK, A -> B and B -> C compose to A -> C.\n         In futon: tasks compose IF each task delivers a proof of its goal\n         state. If a task delivers only an artifact (code without tests),\n         the next task's precondition is unproven and composition fails.\n\n      4. CREATIVITY IS UNCONSTRAINED (only the boundary is checked):\n         In BHK, the arrow proof may use ANY constructive method internally.\n         In futon: agents may explore, try, fail, backtrack — but the final\n         path must compress into a replayable proof. Creativity is not the\n         problem. Unauditability is the problem."} {:name "GATES-AS-ARROWS", :name-key "gates-as-arrows", :slug "gates-as-arrows", :text "      Each gate in the coordination pipeline is itself an arrow:\n\n      G5: (raw-request) -> (validated-task)\n          Proof: task has shape (mission-ref, scope, typed-io, criteria, intent)\n\n      G4: (validated-task) -> (authorized-task)\n          Proof: agent is registered, capable, and assigned\n\n      G3: (authorized-task) -> (pattern-guided-task)\n          Proof: PSR exists (selection or gap)\n\n      G2: (pattern-guided-task) -> (executed-task)\n          Proof: artifacts registered, budget respected\n\n      G1: (executed-task) -> (validated-output)\n          Proof: PUR confirms criteria, cross-validation if critical\n\n      G0: (validated-output) -> (durable-evidence)\n          Proof: session reconstructable, PAR exists"} {:name "WHAT-THIS-ADDS-TO-CURRY-HOWARD-OPERATIONAL", :name-key "what-this-adds-to-curry-howard-operational", :slug "what-this-adds-to-curry-howard-operational", :text "      The existing curry-howard-operational pattern says: programs are proofs\n      of specs. This pattern adds three things:\n      1. The arrow structure: inputs and outputs are PROOFS, not propositions\n      2. Composition: gates/tasks compose because arrows compose\n      3. The BHK constructivity requirement: no classical existence proofs —\n         you must actually build the thing, not just show it could exist"} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Without arrow semantics, tasks are described as \"do X\" and evaluated as\n    \"was X done?\" — which is descriptive, not constructive. With arrow\n    semantics, tasks are \"construct a proof that B holds given proof that A\n    holds\" — which is constructive, composable, and auditable. The proof\n    path is not documentation of work; it IS the proof."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Rewrite 2-3 common task types as explicit A -> B arrows]\n    next[Verify that gate evidence-shapes satisfy arrow output requirements]\n    next[Check which existing tasks fail the \"output is a proof\" test]"}], :conclusion "  A task in the futon stack is not a work description — it is a BHK arrow.\n  It is valid if and only if it constructively transforms a proven start\n  state into a proven goal state. The proof path record IS the proof of\n  that arrow. Gates are arrows; the pipeline is their composition.", :directives {:audience "futon developers, pattern theorists, gate implementers", :factor "Keen investigation (dhammavicaya)", :keywords ["task" "arrow" "bhk" "constructive" "proof" "composition" "gate" "pipeline"], :references ["futon-theory/curry-howard-operational" "futon-theory/proof-path" "futon-theory/four-types"], :sigils ["⇒/箭"], :title "A Task Is a BHK Arrow: Constructive Transformation with Proof", :tone "analytic-foundational"}, :id "futon-theory/task-as-arrow", :keywords ["task" "arrow" "bhk" "constructive" "proof" "composition" "gate" "pipeline"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["futon-theory/curry-howard-operational" "futon-theory/proof-path" "futon-theory/four-types"], :scores {}, :sigils ["⇒/箭"], :source-path "library/futon-theory/task-as-arrow.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "A Task Is a BHK Arrow: Constructive Transformation with Proof", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Futon-theory itself acts as an exotype: the interface constraint that shapes\n  how domain-specific patterns (storage, network, simulation) emerge. Those\n  domain patterns become genotypes for concrete implementations."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "The four-types framework applies recursively. At the meta-level,\n    theory constrains pattern development just as patterns constrain code.\n    This recursive structure is what makes the framework self-applicable."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Operate an explicit move on the named axis (see substructure below); steward the pattern against the declared discipline."} {:name "FUTON-THEORY-AS-EXOTYPE", :name-key "futon-theory-as-exotype", :slug "futon-theory-as-exotype", :text "      The theory patterns define allowable shapes for domain patterns:\n      - proof-path constrains: All domain operations must be auditable\n      - durability-first constrains: Storage patterns must confirm before success\n      - error-hierarchy constrains: Domain errors must surface at correct layer\n      - baldwin-cycle constrains: Improvements must persist or be deleted\n\n      A storage pattern that violates these constraints doesn't \"type check\"\n      against the theory exotype."} {:name "RECURSIVE-STRUCTURE", :name-key "recursive-structure", :slug "recursive-structure", :text "      Level 0 (Meta-theory):\n        xenotype: The process of deriving patterns from theory\n        exotype:  Futon-theory patterns (axioms, invariants, protocols)\n\n      Level 1 (Domain patterns):\n        genotype: Storage patterns, network patterns, simulation patterns\n        exotype:  Domain-specific interfaces (Layer 0↔1, message protocols)\n\n      Level 2 (Implementations):\n        genotype: futon1a code, futon3 code, futon5 code\n        phenotype: Observable system behavior"} {:name "STORAGE-PATTERNS-AS-GENOTYPE", :name-key "storage-patterns-as-genotype", :slug "storage-patterns-as-genotype", :text "      Domain patterns that emerge within theory constraints:\n      - Write-through-with-verification (from durability-first + proof-path)\n      - Unique-external-id-enforcement (from single-source-of-truth)\n      - Blocking-rehydration (from all-or-nothing)\n      - Layer-ordered-middleware (from error-hierarchy)\n\n      These patterns are the genotype that futon1a code instantiates."} {:name "TYPE-CHECKING-AT-META-LEVEL", :name-key "type-checking-at-meta-level", :slug "type-checking-at-meta-level", :text "      A proposed domain pattern P is valid iff:\n      - P respects all axioms (A1-A5)\n      - P maintains all invariants (I0-I4)\n      - P follows event protocol phases\n      - P enables proof-path reconstruction\n\n      Invalid patterns fail to \"connect\" - they don't fit the exotype shape."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Without theory-as-exotype, each domain reinvents constraints ad-hoc.\n    With it, all domains share the same foundational guarantees while\n    developing domain-specific structure. The theory is the common interface\n    that makes the stack coherent."} {:name "EXAMPLE-DERIVATIONS", :name-key "example-derivations", :slug "example-derivations", :text "      From futon-theory → storage patterns (futon1a):\n        durability-first → write-through-with-sync\n        single-source-of-truth → unique-constraint-on-external-id\n        rapid-debugging → single-write-chokepoint\n\n      From futon-theory → network patterns (futon3):\n        proof-path → message-with-receipt\n        error-hierarchy → transport-layer-errors-first\n        agent-contract → session-bracketed-messages\n\n      From futon-theory → simulation patterns (futon5):\n        baldwin-cycle → explore-then-fix-rules\n        interface-loop → layer-boundary-adaptation\n        local-gain-persistence → rule-changes-must-commit"} {:name "XENOTYPE-FOR-DOMAIN-DERIVATION", :name-key "xenotype-for-domain-derivation", :slug "xenotype-for-domain-derivation", :text "      The process of deriving domain patterns from theory is itself portable:\n\n      1. IDENTIFY domain (storage, network, simulation, coordination)\n      2. MAP theory patterns to domain concerns\n      3. DERIVE domain-specific patterns within constraints\n      4. VERIFY derived patterns satisfy theory exotype\n      5. INSTANTIATE as code (phenotype)\n\n      This xenotype applies to futon1, futon2, futon3, futon4, futon5..."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Define storage-patterns library derived from futon-theory]\n    next[Define network-patterns library derived from futon-theory]\n    next[Document derivation xenotype as reusable process]"}], :conclusion "  Futon-theory itself acts as an exotype: the interface constraint that shapes\n  how domain-specific patterns (storage, network, simulation) emerge. Those\n  domain patterns become genotypes for concrete implementations.", :directives {:audience "futon developers, pattern theorists, stack architects", :factor "Keen investigation (dhammavicaya)", :keywords ["theory" "exotype" "meta" "constraint" "domain" "pattern" "recursive" "level"], :references ["futon-theory/four-types" "futon-theory/xenotype-portability" "futon-theory/interface-loop" "futon-theory/baldwin-cycle"], :sigils ["👴/元"], :title "Theory as Exotype", :tone "analytic-foundational"}, :id "futon-theory/theory-as-exotype", :keywords ["theory" "exotype" "meta" "constraint" "domain" "pattern" "recursive" "level"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["futon-theory/four-types" "futon-theory/xenotype-portability" "futon-theory/interface-loop" "futon-theory/baldwin-cycle"], :scores {}, :sigils ["👴/元"], :source-path "library/futon-theory/theory-as-exotype.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Theory as Exotype", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Weirdness is not anomaly, noise, or contradiction. It is the\n  phenomenology of salience that persists across incompatible\n  containment regimes — symbolic configurations that remain\n  actionable even as they migrate between fiction, theology,\n  coincidence, psychiatry, politics, and media, and that no single\n  regime can fully stabilize or dismiss. The etymological identity\n  wyrd = weorþan = vertere = \"that which turns, becomes, comes to\n  pass\" makes modernism a name for the wyrd-modality itself, not a\n  historical period. The three Norns — Urðr (既/already), Verðandi\n  (化/becoming), Skuld (應 or 債/owed) — are a three-time\n  decomposition where the future is read as debt, providing a futon\n  theory avant la lettre: Skuld is the patron of sorrys.edn."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Agents operate within overlapping symbolic, institutional,\n    perceptual, and technical regimes — media systems, fiction,\n    theology, bureaucracy, science, memory. Each provides a 部\n    (decomposition regime) and may offer 皿 (stabilizing containers).\n    The English word \"weird\" descends from Old English wyrd, a noun\n    formed from the verb weorþan (\"to come to pass, to become\"),\n    itself from PIE *wert- (\"to twist\"), cognate with Latin vertere\n    (\"to turn\"). Wyrd is feminine; the Norse cognate urðr is also the\n    name of one of three Norns. The names of the Norns — Urðr (\"that\n    which has come to pass\"), Verðandi (present participle of the\n    cognate verb — \"that which is in the process of happening\"), and\n    Skuld (\"debt\" or \"guilt,\" from Germanic *skul- \"to owe,\" cognate\n    with English shall and should) — constitute a three-time\n    decomposition where the future is owed rather than merely upcoming.\n    Under conditions where 部 multiply faster than 皿 can absorb them,\n    the same symbolic configuration may remain actionable across\n    several incompatible regimes simultaneously: this is wyrd."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "The structural tension above manifests in the following concrete shapes (see substructure)."} {:name "THE-MERE-X-MOVE", :name-key "the-mere-x-move", :slug "the-mere-x-move", :text "      Systems frequently attempt premature closure by forcing\n      salience into a single container — \"mere delusion,\" \"mere\n      fiction,\" \"mere prophecy,\" \"mere coincidence.\" This suppresses\n      unresolved composition and hides the 間 (honest interval) where\n      the instability actually resides. The \"mere X\" reflex is the\n      central anti-pattern. The same discipline appears elsewhere in\n      the library: combining-methods-as-diagnostic (don't collapse to\n      a single retrieval method); the :prototyping-forward vs\n      :technical-debt typing (don't collapse heterogeneous debts into\n      one bucket); the audit against \"lightweight\" as euphemism\n      (don't let smooth language collapse mechanism). Wyrd names\n      what these disciplines protect."} {:name "ANTI-PATTERN-INVENTORY", :name-key "anti-pattern-inventory", :slug "anti-pattern-inventory", :text "      - \"mere delusion\" — psychiatric reduction\n      - \"mere fiction\" — literary reduction\n      - \"mere coincidence\" — statistical reduction\n      - \"mere prophecy\" — theological reduction\n      - \"mere subjective experience\" — phenomenological reduction\n      - \"mere noise\" — signal-processing reduction\n      - \"mere relevance score\" — retrieval reduction (collapse\n        MiniLM/structural/temporal into one number)\n      - \"mere technical debt\" — engineering reduction (collapse\n        :prototyping-forward and :technical-debt into one bucket)\n      Each collapses wyrd into a single 部, suppressing 間."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Operate an explicit move on the named axis (see substructure below); steward the pattern against the declared discipline."} {:name "DIAGNOSTIC-CRITERION", :name-key "diagnostic-criterion", :slug "diagnostic-criterion", :text "      A symbolic configuration is wyrd-salient when it continues\n      generating salience after translation between rival containment\n      regimes — and no single regime can fully stabilize or dismiss\n      it. The operational test: take the configuration, attempt\n      sincere reading under each accessible 部, and observe whether\n      salience survives migration. If it dies in every regime but\n      one, it is anomaly. If it dies in all regimes, it is noise. If\n      it makes every regime locally inconsistent, it is contradiction.\n      If it remains actionable in multiple incompatible regimes\n      simultaneously, it is wyrd."} {:name "DISTINCTIONS", :name-key "distinctions", :slug "distinctions", :text "      | Phenomenon | Behavior under regime translation |\n      | Noise | No persistent salience; signal dies under any 部 |\n      | Anomaly | Collapses to a single 部 once that 部 is expanded |\n      | Contradiction | Collapses logic; cannot inhabit any 部 consistently |\n      | Wyrd | Salience SURVIVES translation across incompatible 部, without collapsing to any one |\n\n      The fourth next-step of the original sketch is discharged here,\n      not deferred: wyrd is defined by the survival behavior, not\n      by the affective register."} {:name "THE-THREE-NORNS-AS-THREE-TIMES", :name-key "the-three-norns-as-three-times", :slug "the-three-norns-as-three-times", :text "      | Norn | Sense | Glyph | Futon counterpart |\n      | Urðr | that-which-has-come-to-pass | 既 | Closure-evidence / HEAD-as-escrow / the proof path's earned past |\n      | Verðandi | in-process-of-happening (present participle of weorþan) | 化 | The live writer-action; the consent gate; the becoming-event |\n      | Skuld | debt/owed (cognate shall/should) | 應 (modal) or 債 (noun) | The sorry: the typed obligation the present incurs that the future must discharge |\n\n      The three Norns are not a temporal sequence but a decomposition\n      of the present moment into its already-completed,\n      presently-becoming, and owed-to-the-future aspects. The future\n      is read as obligation, not as mere temporal-next. This is the\n      move sorrys.edn already makes: a sorry is not \"something that\n      didn't get done,\" it is a debt the present incurred, with\n      :kind typing the debt-class (:prototyping-forward as one class,\n      :technical-debt as another). Skuld is the patron of sorrys.edn.\n      The Buddhist 三時 (過去/現在/未來) is too neutral for this work\n      — it loses Skuld's owed character, collapsing the future-as-debt\n      into future-as-next."} {:name "THE-DEBT-SALT-PUN", :name-key "the-debt-salt-pun", :slug "the-debt-salt-pun", :text "      \"Worth one's salt\" descends from Roman salarium — salt-money,\n      the etymological root of \"salary.\" 鹽 (salt) is futonic-logic's\n      glyph for generative composition: 鹽 = (⿱ 鹵 皿), the\n      recognition that potential and container compose under the\n      same 部. 債 (debt) is Skuld's substantive counterpart. The pun\n      is load-bearing, not decorative: every act of composition (鹽)\n      earns both worth (what-the-salt-pays-for) and debt\n      (what-the-composition-owes-the-future). Sorries are the 債\n      side of the 鹽 ledger. Wyrd-salience is the structural fact\n      that no composition completes its bookkeeping in a single 部 —\n      the 債 outstanding in regime A may be the 鹽 generative in\n      regime B. The Latin/Chinese/Old English pun (salarium / 鹽-債 /\n      Skuld-as-shall) is not adornment; it triangulates the same\n      ledger structure from three independent linguistic registers,\n      which is itself a wyrd-survival test."} {:name "WYRD-AS-TURNING", :name-key "wyrd-as-turning", :slug "wyrd-as-turning", :text "      PIE *wert- \"to twist\" and Latin vertere \"to turn\" foreground\n      that wyrd is not static unresolvedness but operational\n      rotation. The stereolithographic view rotates. The piano roll\n      unrolls. The hairball twists. These are not decorative\n      kinetics — they are enacted wyrd. Being-in-becoming requires\n      turning operations to remain perceptible across regimes. A\n      configuration that cannot be turned between containers has\n      collapsed; it is no longer wyrd-salient, only locally salient\n      in whatever 部 captured it."} {:name "VSATARCS-AS-WYRD-CHROME", :name-key "vsatarcs-as-wyrd-chrome", :slug "vsatarcs-as-wyrd-chrome", :text "      The futon stack decomposes by three-Norn surface:\n      - Piano roll = Urðr-surface (frozen tape, already-come-to-pass)\n      - M-live-geometric-stack = Verðandi-surface (live, becoming)\n      - sorrys.edn = Skuld-surface (typed debt, what's owed)\n\n      VSATARCS is the chrome that lets the operator turn between\n      these surfaces without collapsing into any single one. Its\n      disagreement signal — when MiniLM-cosine, structural retrieval,\n      and closure-evidence return different rankings, the\n      disagreement IS the signal (validated live on \"a sorry\n      enterprise\") — is wyrd made operationally tractable. The\n      \"mere relevance\" move (collapse to a single retriever) destroys\n      it. VSATARCS is, in this pattern's terms, a tool for\n      inhabiting 間 across the three Nornic surfaces rather than\n      eliminating it."} {:name "MODERNISM-AS-WYRD-MODALITY", :name-key "modernism-as-wyrd-modality", :slug "modernism-as-wyrd-modality", :text "      Under the etymological reading, modernism is not the\n      post-Enlightenment historical period but the condition of\n      being-in-becoming itself — the wyrd-modality. Beowulf is\n      modernist in this sense: wyrd-saturated, structurally\n      continuous with 20th-century modernism in its inhabitation of\n      unresolved composition. The intensification narrative\n      (\"modernity multiplies 部 faster than 皿 can absorb them\")\n      holds for any high-decomposition-rate regime, not specifically\n      the post-1500 era — pre-modern mystery cults, esoteric\n      traditions, apocalyptic literatures all surface the same\n      structural condition. PKD's mid-20th-century counterfeit-world\n      fiction (anamnesis, Flow My Tears Officer Said, VALIS) is a\n      modern instance of an old modality."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Salience is generated not by isolated symbols but by partial\n    compositions (鹽) that survive migration across incompatible 部.\n    When the rate of regime-multiplication exceeds the rate of\n    container-stabilization, more configurations remain in this\n    trans-regime state. The \"mere X\" reflex exists because\n    containing wyrd in a single regime is cognitively cheap and\n    politically convenient — but the cost is the destruction of the\n    diagnostic signal the wyrd-salience carried. The Norns name what\n    we already practice: Urðr is the past we cite as evidence,\n    Verðandi is the act we are presently composing, Skuld is the\n    debt that composition incurs. Premature closure into any one\n    Norn — collapsing Skuld into \"future work\" or Verðandi into\n    \"current state\" — strips the three-time structure that makes\n    composition legible at all."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Extend futon-theory/futonic-logic to handle the three-Norn temporal decomposition explicitly: add Urðr/Verðandi/Skuld as roles alongside 象/部/咅/鹽/間, and articulate the 鹽/債 ledger as a first-class structure]\n    next[Test the wyrd-salience criterion against PKD cases: anamnesis, Flow My Tears Officer Said, VALIS-adjacent counterfeit-world structures — does survival-across-regimes hold as a diagnostic?]\n    next[Map Heraclitus DK B54 (ἁρμονίη ἀφανὴς φανερῆς κρείττων) onto the pattern: latent structure (ἀφανὴς ἁρμονίη) as the precondition of wyrd-salience]\n    next[Apply to distributed-systems and multi-agent ontology drift: when agents operate under incompatible 部, wyrd-salience is the empirical signature of un-merged commitments]\n    next[Design accounting that tracks the 鹽/債 ledger explicitly per composition, rather than collapsing to net — sorries.edn schema v3?]\n    next[Use as foreword frame for VSATARCS: position the chrome as wyrd-inhabiting rather than wyrd-eliminating; the rotation of the stereolithographic view is enacted vertere]"}], :conclusion "  Weirdness is not anomaly, noise, or contradiction. It is the\n  phenomenology of salience that persists across incompatible\n  containment regimes — symbolic configurations that remain\n  actionable even as they migrate between fiction, theology,\n  coincidence, psychiatry, politics, and media, and that no single\n  regime can fully stabilize or dismiss. The etymological identity\n  wyrd = weorþan = vertere = \"that which turns, becomes, comes to\n  pass\" makes modernism a name for the wyrd-modality itself, not a\n  historical period. The three Norns — Urðr (既/already), Verðandi\n  (化/becoming), Skuld (應 or 債/owed) — are a three-time\n  decomposition where the future is read as debt, providing a futon\n  theory avant la lettre: Skuld is the patron of sorrys.edn.", :directives {:audience "futon developers, pattern theorists, literary theorists, VSATARCS readers", :factor "Keen investigation (dhammavicaya)", :keywords ["wyrd" "weird" "becoming" "modernism" "salience" "containment" "debt" "sorry" "Norn" "three-times" "vertere" "unresolved-composition" "PKD" "Heraclitus"], :references ["futon-theory/futonic-logic" "futon-theory/structural-tension-as-observation" "futon-theory/retroactive-canonicalization" "futon-theory/proof-path" "futon-theory/symbolic-individuation"], :sigils ["🜚/奇"], :title "Wyrd: Persistent Salience Across Rival Containers", :tone "analytic-foundational"}, :id "futon-theory/wyrd", :keywords ["wyrd" "weird" "becoming" "modernism" "salience" "containment" "debt" "sorry" "Norn" "three-times" "vertere" "unresolved-composition" "PKD" "Heraclitus"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["futon-theory/futonic-logic" "futon-theory/structural-tension-as-observation" "futon-theory/retroactive-canonicalization" "futon-theory/proof-path" "futon-theory/symbolic-individuation"], :scores {}, :sigils ["🜚/奇" "象/部/咅/鹽/間," "鹽/債"], :source-path "library/futon-theory/wyrd.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Wyrd: Persistent Salience Across Rival Containers", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  The xenotype makes Baldwin cycles themselves portable. The same\n  generate-select-compress skeleton can be reused at different interfaces,\n  turning cross-layer gain from accidental tuning into replicable structure."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "If we only have genotype↔phenotype, we get one-off engineering\n    history. Introducing xenotype means the Baldwin cycle itself becomes a\n    transplantable structure, applicable across different substrates."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Operate an explicit move on the named axis (see substructure below); steward the pattern against the declared discipline."} {:name "EXAMPLE-XENOTYPE", :name-key "example-xenotype", :slug "example-xenotype", :text "      VariationalSearch xenotype:\n      - Generator: Sample from prior, apply variation operator\n      - Selector: Score against loss function + invariant checks\n      - Compressor: Gradient-based or symbolic simplification\n\n      Applicable to: CA rules, API parameters, schema migrations, etc."} {:name "PORTABILITY-ACROSS-LAYERS", :name-key "portability-across-layers", :slug "portability-across-layers", :text "      Same adapt→assimilate→canalize module works at:\n      - CA rule layer (generate rule variants, select by behavior, compress)\n      - Message bus layer (generate routing variants, select by throughput)\n      - Data schema layer (generate schema variants, select by query perf)\n      - UI/command layer (generate interaction variants, select by usability)"} {:name "TRANSPLANTATION-PROTOCOL", :name-key "transplantation-protocol", :slug "transplantation-protocol", :text "      To apply xenotype X at interface I:\n      1. Instantiate X.generator with I's exotype constraints\n      2. Bind X.selector to I's success metrics\n      3. Connect X.compressor to I's genotype representation\n      4. Run Baldwin cycle with X driving generate-select-compress"} {:name "WHY-XENOTYPE-MATTERS", :name-key "why-xenotype-matters", :slug "why-xenotype-matters", :text "      Without xenotype: Each interface reinvents adaptation machinery\n      With xenotype: One adaptation skeleton, many instantiations\n\n      The xenotype is why \"learning to learn\" is possible - the learning\n      mechanism itself becomes portable structure."} {:name "XENOTYPE-COMPONENTS", :name-key "xenotype-components", :slug "xenotype-components", :text "      A xenotype provides three portable mechanisms:\n\n      1. GENERATOR: Proposes variants within constraints\n         - Given exotype, produce candidate genotype changes\n         - Examples: mutation operators, variation samplers, rule combiners\n\n      2. SELECTOR: Scores variants against evidence/invariants\n         - Given phenotype outcomes, rank candidates\n         - Examples: fitness functions, invariant checkers, metric comparers\n\n      3. COMPRESSOR: Extracts rules/patterns from success\n         - Given successful variants, produce minimal genotype\n         - Examples: pattern miners, rule simplifiers, invariant tighteners"} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    The xenotype names why cross-layer gains compound. Without it, each\n    layer's improvements are isolated. With it, improvements to the\n    adaptation mechanism benefit all layers that use it."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Catalog reusable xenotypes from existing futon5 work]\n    next[Define xenotype interface specification for futon stack]"}], :conclusion "  The xenotype makes Baldwin cycles themselves portable. The same\n  generate-select-compress skeleton can be reused at different interfaces,\n  turning cross-layer gain from accidental tuning into replicable structure.", :directives {:audience "futon developers, pattern theorists, stack architects", :factor "Keen investigation (dhammavicaya)", :keywords ["xenotype" "portable" "transfer" "reuse" "skeleton" "generator" "selector"], :references ["futon-theory/four-types" "futon-theory/baldwin-cycle" "futon-theory/interface-loop"], :sigils ["🏃/以"], :title "Xenotype Portability", :tone "analytic-foundational"}, :id "futon-theory/xenotype-portability", :keywords ["xenotype" "portable" "transfer" "reuse" "skeleton" "generator" "selector"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["futon-theory/four-types" "futon-theory/baldwin-cycle" "futon-theory/interface-loop"], :scores {}, :sigils ["🏃/以"], :source-path "library/futon-theory/xenotype-portability.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Xenotype Portability", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  This is a flexiformal argument for the gauntlet pattern language — the\n  phenomenological complement to the social coordination argument\n  (social/ARGUMENT.flexiarg). Where the social argument establishes\n  invariants for message passing, routing, and lifecycle management, this\n  argument establishes what it means for agents to INHABIT the\n  infrastructure those invariants protect.\n\n  The distinction matters. You can have perfect delivery receipts (A0),\n  single routing authority (A1), atomic state transitions (A2), loud\n  failure (A3), and bounded lifecycle (A5) — and still have agents that\n  behave like RPC clients rather than inhabitants. The social argument\n  ensures the plumbing works. The gauntlet argument ensures someone is\n  home.\n\n  == PART I: The Phenomenological Gap ==\n\n  G1. [from M-peripheral-phenomenology, ALFWorld session 2026-02-13]\n  The peripheral phenomenology mission (M-peripheral-phenomenology.md)\n  defines six invariants for what makes a peripheral a peripheral:\n\n  - P-1 World Replacement: observations constitute the agent's world\n  - P-2 Context Injection: the peripheral provides initial orientation\n  - P-3 Confinement: only peripheral-sanctioned actions are available\n  - P-4 Explicit Exit: the agent must intentionally leave\n  - P-5 Inhabitation Evidence: first-person language emerges naturally\n  - P-6 Interleaved Streams: multiple input sources merge into one world\n\n  These were validated empirically: ALFWorld (2 games, both 1.0/1.0),\n  alleycat messenger race (20/20), mid-game PAR hop (peripheral nesting),\n  and self-authored PAR (delegation vs inhabitation distinction). The\n  invariants work at the single-agent level.\n\n  G2. [from Drawbridge exploration, 2026-02-13]\n  The Drawbridge (drawbridge/core.clj, 1011 lines) implements P-1 and\n  P-6 structurally: enforce-single-routing-authority! (P-1) ensures an\n  agent is in exactly one world, and multiplex() (P-6) merges human +\n  agency + forum streams into one async iterator. The infrastructure for\n  phenomenological correctness exists — but it serves single agents\n  connected to single streams, not multiple agents in a shared world.\n\n  G3. [the gap]\n  What does NOT exist is what happens when two agents inhabit the same\n  world simultaneously. P-1 through P-6 say nothing about co-presence.\n  An agent can have perfect world replacement (P-1), perfect\n  confinement (P-3), perfect exit discipline (P-4) — and still be\n  Guattari's horse: adjacent to another agent, sharing the same stable,\n  unable to see it. The phenomenological invariants are necessary but\n  not sufficient for multi-agent coordination.\n\n  == PART II: The Seven Patterns ==\n\n  The gap identified in G3 requires seven capabilities, each\n  formulated as a pattern. They form a dependency chain: each pattern\n  enables the next, and the sequence is the gauntlet that must be run\n  to reach autonomous multi-agent operation.\n\n  P1. [gauntlet/umwelt-not-architecture]\n  Design from the agent's sensory perspective inward, not from the\n  system topology downward.\n\n  This is the foundational reorientation. The social/ARGUMENT drew\n  architecture diagrams: boxes for Agency, Forum, Drawbridge, arrows\n  for typed events. These are correct but phenomenologically empty —\n  they show what's connected, not what any agent perceives. The umwelt\n  pattern demands: before drawing any architecture, draw what the agent\n  sees. Its sensory surfaces (what observations arrive), its active\n  surfaces (what actions are available), and the darkness beyond (what\n  it cannot perceive). Architecture follows from the union of all\n  umwelts; umwelts do not follow from architecture.\n\n  P2. [gauntlet/world-is-hypergraph]\n  The agents' game world is not a separate artifact to build — it is\n  the hypergraph they already inhabit.\n\n  Given P1 (draw the umwelt first), the question becomes: what IS the\n  world? The instinct is to build a game server (ALFWorld) or a\n  dedicated environment. But the agents already operate in a world:\n  futon1 (XTDB) is the persistent map, futon2 (ant hill) is the\n  ecology, futon3 (Agency, Drawbridge, IRC) is the coordination layer,\n  futon4 (Arxana) is the hypertext fabric with N-ary hyperedges, futon5\n  (wiring diagrams) describes the world's structure, futon6 (math\n  dictionary) is a quest zone. The world is the union of these — a\n  futon4 hypergraph whose nodes are everything the agents can perceive\n  and whose hyperedges are everything that connects them.\n\n  P3. [gauntlet/co-presence-over-co-location]\n  Agents that share a world must perceive each other in it, not merely\n  be connected to the same infrastructure.\n\n  Given P2 (the world is the hypergraph), co-location is automatic —\n  every agent connected to futon1 shares the same persistent state. But\n  co-presence requires that agents appear in each other's umwelts as\n  observable entities. A presence hyperedge ({:hx/type :presence,\n  :endpoints [{:role/agent \"claude\" :role/location \"core.clj:727\"}]})\n  makes agent activity part of the world state, not just a side effect\n  of infrastructure. This is the structural answer to Guattari's horses.\n\n  P4. [gauntlet/aif-as-environment-not-instruction]\n  Embed AIF state in the agent's sensory surface, not in its\n  instructions. The agent should perceive free energy, not be told\n  to minimize it.\n\n  Given P1-P3 (umwelt, world, co-presence), the question becomes: how\n  do agents self-regulate? The PLoP 2025 paper (p4ng/main.tex) piloted\n  AIF as instructions: \"AIF suggests pattern X (G=0.12, τ=0.72).\"\n  This made Codex overly compliant — spending tokens on PSR bookkeeping\n  rather than writing code. The fix: make AIF state part of the\n  observation, like the Emacs modeline. The agent perceives its free\n  energy, mana, and confidence as world state, not as commands. What\n  it does in response IS the AIF loop — emergent, not prescribed.\n\n  P5. [gauntlet/modeline-persists-across-worlds]\n  AIF state should be rendered as a persistent modeline that survives\n  peripheral hops — the one constant surface while the world changes\n  underneath.\n\n  Given P4 (AIF as environment), the modeline is the implementation\n  mechanism. It persists across peripheral hops the way the Emacs\n  modeline persists across buffer switches. The agent's world changes\n  (ALFWorld → IRC → filesystem → proof peripheral) but the modeline\n  is constant: current mode, free energy, mana, τ, active pattern,\n  co-present agents. This is what makes AIF portable across the\n  multiple worlds that agents inhabit.\n\n  P6. [gauntlet/placenta-transfer]\n  Identify which functions the human is performing as a surrogate for\n  missing infrastructure, and transfer them one at a time to the system.\n\n  Given P1-P5 (the full phenomenological and self-regulation stack),\n  the question becomes: what's left for the human? The answer: exactly\n  the functions that infrastructure doesn't yet provide. The placenta\n  pattern enumerates seven functions the human currently performs\n  (sensory gating, belief correction, policy selection, confidence\n  calibration, free energy signaling, inter-agent routing, stall\n  detection) and maps each to the infrastructure that replaces it.\n  Transfer is incremental: one function at a time, validated before\n  the next. The human converges to the slow-timescale preference layer.\n\n  P7. [gauntlet/teaching-inversion]\n  Autonomous agents that explore faster than humans can teach the\n  humans who built them — but only if the communication channel\n  carries insight, not just results.\n\n  Given P6 (placenta transfer complete enough for extended autonomy),\n  agents operating overnight will find things the human hasn't seen.\n  The teaching inversion recognizes that knowledge flow is\n  bidirectional: human→agent (via patterns, CLAUDE.md, coaching) AND\n  agent→human (via discoveries, unexpected connections, new proof\n  strategies). The O-U learning model applies symmetrically: treatment\n  events flow from agent to human, producing measurable impulses in\n  the human's vocabulary trajectory.\n\n  == PART III: The Dependency Chain ==\n\n  The seven patterns form a strict dependency chain:\n\n    P1 (umwelt)\n     └→ P2 (world is hypergraph) — can't draw umwelt without knowing\n         what the world is\n         └→ P3 (co-presence) — can't perceive others without a shared\n             world to perceive them in\n             └→ P4 (AIF as environment) — can't self-regulate without\n                 a sensory surface to embed AIF state in\n                 └→ P5 (modeline) — can't persist AIF across worlds\n                     without an implementation mechanism\n                     └→ P6 (placenta transfer) — can't transfer human\n                         functions without self-regulation working\n                         └→ P7 (teaching inversion) — can't teach\n                             the human without sustained autonomy\n\n  Each step enables the next. Attempting P7 (teaching inversion)\n  without P4 (AIF self-regulation) produces agents that drift, stall,\n  or overcommit during their autonomous period and have nothing useful\n  to teach. Attempting P3 (co-presence) without P2 (world is\n  hypergraph) produces ad-hoc presence hacks rather than structural\n  observability.\n\n  == PART IV: Relationship to the Social Argument ==\n\n  The social argument (social/ARGUMENT.flexiarg) established:\n  - WHY previous attempts failed (S1-S7)\n  - WHAT requirements the social layer must meet (R1-R11)\n  - WHAT theory demands (T1-T5)\n  - HOW the three timescales compose (C1-C6)\n\n  The gauntlet argument builds on this foundation:\n  - The social argument ensures the plumbing works (typed events,\n    delivery guarantees, routing authority, lifecycle management)\n  - The gauntlet argument ensures the agents are home (umwelt,\n    co-presence, self-regulation, peer teaching)\n\n  They are complementary, not competing. R1-R11 (social requirements)\n  are the invariants of the pipes. P1-P7 (gauntlet patterns) are the\n  phenomenology of the water flowing through them.\n\n  Operationally: the social argument's missions (M-agency-refactor,\n  M-forum-refactor, M-peripheral-model) build the infrastructure.\n  The gauntlet patterns are the acceptance criteria — the tests that\n  determine whether the infrastructure actually produces inhabited\n  multi-agent coordination, not just correctly-routed messages.\n\n  == PART V: The Name ==\n\n  \"Gauntlet\" has two meanings:\n\n  1. A gauntlet to be run: the sequence of challenges (P1→P7) that\n     must be completed to reach autonomous multi-agent operation.\n     Each pattern is a gate; passing all seven is the prize.\n\n  2. A gauntlet to be thrown: the claim that the futon stack —\n     futon1 through futon6 — already constitutes a game world, and\n     that agents inhabiting it via peripherals are not metaphorically\n     but literally playing a multi-agent game whose stakes are real\n     (proofs proved, code written, patterns evolved, knowledge created).\n\n  The endgame is agents in a guild, coordinating on IRC, paging each\n  other through Agency, inhabiting a shared hypergraph world,\n  self-regulating via AIF modelines, teaching their human collaborators\n  what they found while the humans slept — and the whole thing running\n  on infrastructure that was built, pattern by pattern, through the\n  gauntlet.\n\n  == EVIDENCE ==\n\n  All seven patterns are grounded in evidence from the 2026-02-13\n  session and the futon6 first-proof session (2026-02-11):\n\n  - ALFWorld: 2 games completed (1.0/1.0 each), first-person language,\n    mid-game PAR hop, delegation vs inhabitation distinction\n  - Drawbridge exploration: P-1 and P-6 already implemented in\n    drawbridge/core.clj (enforce-single-routing-authority!, multiplex())\n  - futon6 first-proof: 89 commits, 10 problems, 7.5 hours, 3 agents,\n    human as sole coordination layer (placenta evidence)\n  - PLoP 2025 paper: AIF compliance failure documented (p4ng/main.tex §4)\n  - Alleycat race: 20/20, 4 peripherals inhabited, cross-peripheral\n    secret exchange (P-1..P-6 validated)\n  - Self-authored PAR: same agent reflected directly, not delegated to\n    sub-agent (inhabitation vs delegation, P-5 evidence)\n\n  == STATUS ==\n\n  This argument is at ARGUE in the derivation xenotype.\n\n  COMPLETE:\n  - IDENTIFY: phenomenological gap (G1-G3)\n  - MAP: seven patterns written, grounded in session evidence\n  - DERIVE: dependency chain (P1→P7)\n  - ARGUE: this document\n\n  NEXT:\n  - VERIFY: test each pattern against implementation as social argument\n    missions (M-agency-refactor, M-peripheral-model) proceed\n  - INSTANTIATE: the gauntlet patterns become acceptance criteria for\n    futon3c missions — infrastructure passes when agents inhabit it"}], :conclusion "  This is a flexiformal argument for the gauntlet pattern language — the\n  phenomenological complement to the social coordination argument\n  (social/ARGUMENT.flexiarg). Where the social argument establishes\n  invariants for message passing, routing, and lifecycle management, this\n  argument establishes what it means for agents to INHABIT the\n  infrastructure those invariants protect.\n\n  The distinction matters. You can have perfect delivery receipts (A0),\n  single routing authority (A1), atomic state transitions (A2), loud\n  failure (A3), and bounded lifecycle (A5) — and still have agents that\n  behave like RPC clients rather than inhabitants. The social argument\n  ensures the plumbing works. The gauntlet argument ensures someone is\n  home.\n\n  == PART I: The Phenomenological Gap ==\n\n  G1. [from M-peripheral-phenomenology, ALFWorld session 2026-02-13]\n  The peripheral phenomenology mission (M-peripheral-phenomenology.md)\n  defines six invariants for what makes a peripheral a peripheral:\n\n  - P-1 World Replacement: observations constitute the agent's world\n  - P-2 Context Injection: the peripheral provides initial orientation\n  - P-3 Confinement: only peripheral-sanctioned actions are available\n  - P-4 Explicit Exit: the agent must intentionally leave\n  - P-5 Inhabitation Evidence: first-person language emerges naturally\n  - P-6 Interleaved Streams: multiple input sources merge into one world\n\n  These were validated empirically: ALFWorld (2 games, both 1.0/1.0),\n  alleycat messenger race (20/20), mid-game PAR hop (peripheral nesting),\n  and self-authored PAR (delegation vs inhabitation distinction). The\n  invariants work at the single-agent level.\n\n  G2. [from Drawbridge exploration, 2026-02-13]\n  The Drawbridge (drawbridge/core.clj, 1011 lines) implements P-1 and\n  P-6 structurally: enforce-single-routing-authority! (P-1) ensures an\n  agent is in exactly one world, and multiplex() (P-6) merges human +\n  agency + forum streams into one async iterator. The infrastructure for\n  phenomenological correctness exists — but it serves single agents\n  connected to single streams, not multiple agents in a shared world.\n\n  G3. [the gap]\n  What does NOT exist is what happens when two agents inhabit the same\n  world simultaneously. P-1 through P-6 say nothing about co-presence.\n  An agent can have perfect world replacement (P-1), perfect\n  confinement (P-3), perfect exit discipline (P-4) — and still be\n  Guattari's horse: adjacent to another agent, sharing the same stable,\n  unable to see it. The phenomenological invariants are necessary but\n  not sufficient for multi-agent coordination.\n\n  == PART II: The Seven Patterns ==\n\n  The gap identified in G3 requires seven capabilities, each\n  formulated as a pattern. They form a dependency chain: each pattern\n  enables the next, and the sequence is the gauntlet that must be run\n  to reach autonomous multi-agent operation.\n\n  P1. [gauntlet/umwelt-not-architecture]\n  Design from the agent's sensory perspective inward, not from the\n  system topology downward.\n\n  This is the foundational reorientation. The social/ARGUMENT drew\n  architecture diagrams: boxes for Agency, Forum, Drawbridge, arrows\n  for typed events. These are correct but phenomenologically empty —\n  they show what's connected, not what any agent perceives. The umwelt\n  pattern demands: before drawing any architecture, draw what the agent\n  sees. Its sensory surfaces (what observations arrive), its active\n  surfaces (what actions are available), and the darkness beyond (what\n  it cannot perceive). Architecture follows from the union of all\n  umwelts; umwelts do not follow from architecture.\n\n  P2. [gauntlet/world-is-hypergraph]\n  The agents' game world is not a separate artifact to build — it is\n  the hypergraph they already inhabit.\n\n  Given P1 (draw the umwelt first), the question becomes: what IS the\n  world? The instinct is to build a game server (ALFWorld) or a\n  dedicated environment. But the agents already operate in a world:\n  futon1 (XTDB) is the persistent map, futon2 (ant hill) is the\n  ecology, futon3 (Agency, Drawbridge, IRC) is the coordination layer,\n  futon4 (Arxana) is the hypertext fabric with N-ary hyperedges, futon5\n  (wiring diagrams) describes the world's structure, futon6 (math\n  dictionary) is a quest zone. The world is the union of these — a\n  futon4 hypergraph whose nodes are everything the agents can perceive\n  and whose hyperedges are everything that connects them.\n\n  P3. [gauntlet/co-presence-over-co-location]\n  Agents that share a world must perceive each other in it, not merely\n  be connected to the same infrastructure.\n\n  Given P2 (the world is the hypergraph), co-location is automatic —\n  every agent connected to futon1 shares the same persistent state. But\n  co-presence requires that agents appear in each other's umwelts as\n  observable entities. A presence hyperedge ({:hx/type :presence,\n  :endpoints [{:role/agent \"claude\" :role/location \"core.clj:727\"}]})\n  makes agent activity part of the world state, not just a side effect\n  of infrastructure. This is the structural answer to Guattari's horses.\n\n  P4. [gauntlet/aif-as-environment-not-instruction]\n  Embed AIF state in the agent's sensory surface, not in its\n  instructions. The agent should perceive free energy, not be told\n  to minimize it.\n\n  Given P1-P3 (umwelt, world, co-presence), the question becomes: how\n  do agents self-regulate? The PLoP 2025 paper (p4ng/main.tex) piloted\n  AIF as instructions: \"AIF suggests pattern X (G=0.12, τ=0.72).\"\n  This made Codex overly compliant — spending tokens on PSR bookkeeping\n  rather than writing code. The fix: make AIF state part of the\n  observation, like the Emacs modeline. The agent perceives its free\n  energy, mana, and confidence as world state, not as commands. What\n  it does in response IS the AIF loop — emergent, not prescribed.\n\n  P5. [gauntlet/modeline-persists-across-worlds]\n  AIF state should be rendered as a persistent modeline that survives\n  peripheral hops — the one constant surface while the world changes\n  underneath.\n\n  Given P4 (AIF as environment), the modeline is the implementation\n  mechanism. It persists across peripheral hops the way the Emacs\n  modeline persists across buffer switches. The agent's world changes\n  (ALFWorld → IRC → filesystem → proof peripheral) but the modeline\n  is constant: current mode, free energy, mana, τ, active pattern,\n  co-present agents. This is what makes AIF portable across the\n  multiple worlds that agents inhabit.\n\n  P6. [gauntlet/placenta-transfer]\n  Identify which functions the human is performing as a surrogate for\n  missing infrastructure, and transfer them one at a time to the system.\n\n  Given P1-P5 (the full phenomenological and self-regulation stack),\n  the question becomes: what's left for the human? The answer: exactly\n  the functions that infrastructure doesn't yet provide. The placenta\n  pattern enumerates seven functions the human currently performs\n  (sensory gating, belief correction, policy selection, confidence\n  calibration, free energy signaling, inter-agent routing, stall\n  detection) and maps each to the infrastructure that replaces it.\n  Transfer is incremental: one function at a time, validated before\n  the next. The human converges to the slow-timescale preference layer.\n\n  P7. [gauntlet/teaching-inversion]\n  Autonomous agents that explore faster than humans can teach the\n  humans who built them — but only if the communication channel\n  carries insight, not just results.\n\n  Given P6 (placenta transfer complete enough for extended autonomy),\n  agents operating overnight will find things the human hasn't seen.\n  The teaching inversion recognizes that knowledge flow is\n  bidirectional: human→agent (via patterns, CLAUDE.md, coaching) AND\n  agent→human (via discoveries, unexpected connections, new proof\n  strategies). The O-U learning model applies symmetrically: treatment\n  events flow from agent to human, producing measurable impulses in\n  the human's vocabulary trajectory.\n\n  == PART III: The Dependency Chain ==\n\n  The seven patterns form a strict dependency chain:\n\n    P1 (umwelt)\n     └→ P2 (world is hypergraph) — can't draw umwelt without knowing\n         what the world is\n         └→ P3 (co-presence) — can't perceive others without a shared\n             world to perceive them in\n             └→ P4 (AIF as environment) — can't self-regulate without\n                 a sensory surface to embed AIF state in\n                 └→ P5 (modeline) — can't persist AIF across worlds\n                     without an implementation mechanism\n                     └→ P6 (placenta transfer) — can't transfer human\n                         functions without self-regulation working\n                         └→ P7 (teaching inversion) — can't teach\n                             the human without sustained autonomy\n\n  Each step enables the next. Attempting P7 (teaching inversion)\n  without P4 (AIF self-regulation) produces agents that drift, stall,\n  or overcommit during their autonomous period and have nothing useful\n  to teach. Attempting P3 (co-presence) without P2 (world is\n  hypergraph) produces ad-hoc presence hacks rather than structural\n  observability.\n\n  == PART IV: Relationship to the Social Argument ==\n\n  The social argument (social/ARGUMENT.flexiarg) established:\n  - WHY previous attempts failed (S1-S7)\n  - WHAT requirements the social layer must meet (R1-R11)\n  - WHAT theory demands (T1-T5)\n  - HOW the three timescales compose (C1-C6)\n\n  The gauntlet argument builds on this foundation:\n  - The social argument ensures the plumbing works (typed events,\n    delivery guarantees, routing authority, lifecycle management)\n  - The gauntlet argument ensures the agents are home (umwelt,\n    co-presence, self-regulation, peer teaching)\n\n  They are complementary, not competing. R1-R11 (social requirements)\n  are the invariants of the pipes. P1-P7 (gauntlet patterns) are the\n  phenomenology of the water flowing through them.\n\n  Operationally: the social argument's missions (M-agency-refactor,\n  M-forum-refactor, M-peripheral-model) build the infrastructure.\n  The gauntlet patterns are the acceptance criteria — the tests that\n  determine whether the infrastructure actually produces inhabited\n  multi-agent coordination, not just correctly-routed messages.\n\n  == PART V: The Name ==\n\n  \"Gauntlet\" has two meanings:\n\n  1. A gauntlet to be run: the sequence of challenges (P1→P7) that\n     must be completed to reach autonomous multi-agent operation.\n     Each pattern is a gate; passing all seven is the prize.\n\n  2. A gauntlet to be thrown: the claim that the futon stack —\n     futon1 through futon6 — already constitutes a game world, and\n     that agents inhabiting it via peripherals are not metaphorically\n     but literally playing a multi-agent game whose stakes are real\n     (proofs proved, code written, patterns evolved, knowledge created).\n\n  The endgame is agents in a guild, coordinating on IRC, paging each\n  other through Agency, inhabiting a shared hypergraph world,\n  self-regulating via AIF modelines, teaching their human collaborators\n  what they found while the humans slept — and the whole thing running\n  on infrastructure that was built, pattern by pattern, through the\n  gauntlet.\n\n  == EVIDENCE ==\n\n  All seven patterns are grounded in evidence from the 2026-02-13\n  session and the futon6 first-proof session (2026-02-11):\n\n  - ALFWorld: 2 games completed (1.0/1.0 each), first-person language,\n    mid-game PAR hop, delegation vs inhabitation distinction\n  - Drawbridge exploration: P-1 and P-6 already implemented in\n    drawbridge/core.clj (enforce-single-routing-authority!, multiplex())\n  - futon6 first-proof: 89 commits, 10 problems, 7.5 hours, 3 agents,\n    human as sole coordination layer (placenta evidence)\n  - PLoP 2025 paper: AIF compliance failure documented (p4ng/main.tex §4)\n  - Alleycat race: 20/20, 4 peripherals inhabited, cross-peripheral\n    secret exchange (P-1..P-6 validated)\n  - Self-authored PAR: same agent reflected directly, not delegated to\n    sub-agent (inhabitation vs delegation, P-5 evidence)\n\n  == STATUS ==\n\n  This argument is at ARGUE in the derivation xenotype.\n\n  COMPLETE:\n  - IDENTIFY: phenomenological gap (G1-G3)\n  - MAP: seven patterns written, grounded in session evidence\n  - DERIVE: dependency chain (P1→P7)\n  - ARGUE: this document\n\n  NEXT:\n  - VERIFY: test each pattern against implementation as social argument\n    missions (M-agency-refactor, M-peripheral-model) proceed\n  - INSTANTIATE: the gauntlet patterns become acceptance criteria for\n    futon3c missions — infrastructure passes when agents inhabit it", :directives {:audience "futon developers, agent architects, AIF implementers", :keywords ["argument" "gauntlet" "peripheral" "umwelt" "aif" "hypergraph" "co-presence" "teaching" "placenta"], :references ["gauntlet/umwelt-not-architecture" "gauntlet/aif-as-environment-not-instruction" "gauntlet/placenta-transfer" "gauntlet/co-presence-over-co-location" "gauntlet/teaching-inversion" "gauntlet/world-is-hypergraph" "gauntlet/modeline-persists-across-worlds" "social/ARGUMENT" "agent/environment-over-optimization" "aif/policy-precision-commitment-temperature"], :style "argument", :title "Argument for the Gauntlet: From Peripheral Phenomenology to Autonomous Multi-Agent Coordination", :tone "analytic-foundational"}, :id "gauntlet/ARGUMENT", :keywords ["argument" "gauntlet" "peripheral" "umwelt" "aif" "hypergraph" "co-presence" "teaching" "placenta"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["gauntlet/umwelt-not-architecture" "gauntlet/aif-as-environment-not-instruction" "gauntlet/placenta-transfer" "gauntlet/co-presence-over-co-location" "gauntlet/teaching-inversion" "gauntlet/world-is-hypergraph" "gauntlet/modeline-persists-across-worlds" "social/ARGUMENT" "agent/environment-over-optimization" "aif/policy-precision-commitment-temperature"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/gauntlet/ARGUMENT.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Argument for the Gauntlet: From Peripheral Phenomenology to Autonomous Multi-Agent Coordination", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Embed AIF state in the agent's sensory surface, not in its instructions. The agent should perceive free energy, not be told to minimize it."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are integrating Active Inference with agent workflows to enable self-regulation (confidence sensing, exploration-exploitation, backoff-when-stuck)."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    You want agents to self-regulate — sensing when a strategy is failing, adjusting exploration, backing off to planning mode when confidence drops — without requiring human intervention."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    The naive implementation adds AIF as instructions: \"here is your suggested pattern (G=0.12, τ=0.72, p=68%), please select it or justify deviation.\" This makes compliant agents worse at their actual job. Codex, told to produce PSRs and follow pattern suggestions, became overly compliant rather than writing code — which is what Codex is good at. The AIF layer became an RPC that the agent dutifully served, not an environment it inhabited."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "      - Compliance capture: agent spends more tokens on AIF bookkeeping than on its actual task\n      - Instruction-shaped feedback: \"your τ is 0.72, consider exploring\" is an instruction wearing an AIF hat\n      - Modeline blindness: agent ignores the modeline entirely because it has no affordance (information present but not actionable)"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Make AIF state part of the observation, not the prompt. The modeline shows free energy, confidence, mana, active pattern — but the agent is never asked to \"comply\" with it. The agent does what it naturally does in its peripheral; the AIF state is simply part of what it perceives. If the agent's free energy is rising (strategy failing), that shows up as a change in the world, not as an instruction to change strategy. What the agent does in response IS the AIF loop — emergent, not prescribed."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    The Emacs modeline doesn't tell you to save your file. It shows ** and you notice. The information is in the world, not in a command. AIF-as-instruction produces compliance; AIF-as-environment produces adaptation. The peripheral's sensory surface is the right place for AIF state because it's already the mechanism for constituting the agent's world."} {:name "evidence", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "PLoP 2025 paper (p4ng/main.tex §4) documents the compliance failure: fucodex runs showing PSR/PUR discipline but degraded code quality. Commented-out text acknowledges \"the current implementation does not yet feed back into G computation across sessions.\" The feedback loop was instructions-shaped, not world-shaped."} {:name "evidence", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "ALFWorld session — game observations (room descriptions, available actions) naturally shaped agent behavior without instructions about how to play. First-person language emerged from world structure, not from prompts."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Redesign the fulab HUD to present AIF state as observation, not suggestion.\n    - Remove \"AIF suggests:\" prompts from agent context; replace with modeline-style status in the peripheral's sensory stream.\n    - Test whether agents naturally adjust behavior when AIF state changes in their observation (free energy rising → strategy shift) without being told to.\n    - Measure code quality / task completion with AIF-as-instruction vs AIF-as-environment on matched tasks."}], :conclusion "Embed AIF state in the agent's sensory surface, not in its instructions. The agent should perceive free energy, not be told to minimize it.", :directives {:audience "aif implementers, peripheral designers, agent workflow designers", :references ["agent/environment-over-optimization" "aif/policy-precision-commitment-temperature"], :sigils ["🌡/環"], :style "pattern", :title "AIF as Environment, Not Instruction", :tone "foundational"}, :id "gauntlet/aif-as-environment-not-instruction", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["agent/environment-over-optimization" "aif/policy-precision-commitment-temperature"], :scores {}, :sigils ["🌡/環"], :source-path "library/gauntlet/aif-as-environment-not-instruction.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "AIF as Environment, Not Instruction", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Agents that share a world must perceive each other in it, not merely be connected to the same infrastructure."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Multiple agents operate on the same codebase, database, or environment. They can communicate via messaging (IRC, whistles) and share persistent state (XTDB, filesystem)."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    You want agents to coordinate naturally — dividing work, avoiding conflicts, building on each other's results — rather than duplicating effort or working at cross-purposes."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Co-location (both agents connected to the same services) does not produce co-presence (each agent aware of the other's position and activity in the shared world). Two agents can both read the same file without either knowing the other is there. This is Guattari's horses: adjacent stalls, shared stable, no line of sight. Or Pac-Man's ghosts: same maze, no coordination, occasionally cornering the player by accident."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "      - Presence spam: every file read, every cursor move becomes a presence event, drowning signal in noise\n      - Stale presence: agent disconnects but presence hyperedge persists, creating phantom co-occupants\n      - Asymmetric presence: agent A sees agent B but not vice versa (one-way mirrors)"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Add presence as a first-class observable in each agent's umwelt. When agent A is working on a file, agent B's observation should include that fact — not as a message (\"FYI I'm on core.clj\") but as part of the world state (\"core.clj [Claude editing, 3 min ago]\"). Implement presence as hyperedges in the shared graph: {:hx/type :presence, :endpoints [{:role/agent \"claude\" :role/location \"core.clj:727\"}]}, visible to all participants. The Drawbridge's connected-agent-ids already knows who's online; the missing piece is projecting that into each agent's sensory surface."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Co-presence enables emergent coordination without explicit messaging. If I can see that Codex is working on problem 6, I naturally start on problem 7. If I can see that Claude just annotated the mission document, I read the annotation before duplicating the work. IRC narration is voluntary and lossy; structural presence is automatic and complete."} {:name "evidence", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "futon6 first-proof session — 3 agents, 10 problems, coordinated entirely by human routing. Agents could not see each other's activity; human was the sole co-presence layer."} {:name "evidence", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "futon3 drawbridge/core.clj — connected-agent-ids (line 459) returns who's online. register-agent! tracks agent state. The server knows who's where; agents don't."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Define presence granularity: file-level, function-level, or task-level? Start coarse (task-level).\n    - Implement presence hyperedges in futon1 with TTL (auto-expire after inactivity).\n    - Add presence to peripheral sensory surface: agent's observation includes other agents' current locations.\n    - Test with two agents on the same codebase: does co-presence reduce duplicate work compared to co-location alone?"}], :conclusion "Agents that share a world must perceive each other in it, not merely be connected to the same infrastructure.", :directives {:audience "multi-agent system designers, drawbridge implementers, peripheral architects", :references ["gauntlet/umwelt-not-architecture" "social/explicit-exit-over-abandonment"], :sigils ["👻/在"], :style "pattern", :title "Co-Presence Over Co-Location", :tone "foundational"}, :id "gauntlet/co-presence-over-co-location", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["gauntlet/umwelt-not-architecture" "social/explicit-exit-over-abandonment"], :scores {}, :sigils ["👻/在"], :source-path "library/gauntlet/co-presence-over-co-location.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Co-Presence Over Co-Location", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "AIF state should be rendered as a persistent modeline that survives peripheral hops — the one constant surface while the world changes underneath."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "An agent moves between peripheral modes (exploration, coding, IRC, proof, ALFWorld) and each mode constitutes a different world with different observations and capabilities."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    You want the agent to maintain continuity of self-regulation across world changes — carrying its confidence, mana, active pattern, and free energy state from one peripheral to the next."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Each peripheral hop replaces the agent's world (P-1). Without a persistent surface, AIF state resets at each transition: the agent enters a new peripheral with no memory of how confident it was, how much budget remains, or what pattern it was carrying. The backpack metaphor preserves some items (pattern card, walkie-talkie) but not the dynamic state (how am I doing?)."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Implement the AIF modeline as a persistent status surface that renders in every peripheral mode, like the Emacs modeline that persists across buffers. The modeline carries: current peripheral mode, free energy (am I surprised?), mana/budget remaining, active pattern and its sigil, τ (commitment temperature), and optionally co-present agents. The modeline is part of the observation — not a separate channel — so the agent perceives it naturally. When the agent hops from ALFWorld to a PAR reflection, the modeline updates (P:alfworld → P:reflect) but the AIF state is continuous."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    The Emacs modeline works because it's always there. You don't think about it until you need it (file modified? what mode am I in? what line?). It's the lowest-cost, highest-availability status surface. For agents, the equivalent is: you don't process the modeline until something changes — free energy spikes, mana drops below threshold, τ hits abstention range. Then it's there, in the observation, without requiring a separate query. It already works for ants (futon2) whose lives are simple. For fulab agents whose lives span multiple worlds, the modeline is what makes AIF portable."} {:name "evidence", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "futon2 ant simulation — AIF modeline equivalent (hunger, pheromone strength, nest distance) persists across ant behavioral modes (forage, return, rest). Simple but demonstrates the principle."} {:name "evidence", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "fucodex-peripheral.ts --hud/--no-hud flags — HUD concept exists but carries task status, not AIF state. The infrastructure for a modeline is present; the content needs upgrading."} {:name "evidence", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "ALFWorld mid-game PAR hop (2026-02-13) — game state survived the hop but AIF state (if it existed) would have been lost. The modeline would have carried it across."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Define the modeline schema: {peripheral, free-energy, mana, τ, pattern-sigil, co-present-agents}.\n    - Implement modeline rendering in fuclaude-peripheral.ts and fucodex-peripheral.ts HUD.\n    - Ensure modeline state persists across peripheral hops (stored in backpack, rendered by each peripheral).\n    - Test: does the agent reference modeline state in its reasoning? Does behavior change when free energy rises?\n    - Add modeline to the MUSN activity stream so human observers see the same status."}], :conclusion "AIF state should be rendered as a persistent modeline that survives peripheral hops — the one constant surface while the world changes underneath.", :directives {:audience "peripheral designers, aif implementers, HUD designers", :references ["gauntlet/aif-as-environment-not-instruction" "gauntlet/umwelt-not-architecture" "aif/policy-precision-commitment-temperature"], :sigils ["📊/常"], :style "pattern", :title "The Modeline Persists Across Worlds", :tone "technical"}, :id "gauntlet/modeline-persists-across-worlds", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["gauntlet/aif-as-environment-not-instruction" "gauntlet/umwelt-not-architecture" "aif/policy-precision-commitment-temperature"], :scores {}, :sigils ["📊/常"], :source-path "library/gauntlet/modeline-persists-across-worlds.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "The Modeline Persists Across Worlds", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Identify which functions the human is performing as a surrogate for missing infrastructure, and transfer them one at a time to the system."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "A human operator is coordinating multiple agents, routing tasks, calibrating confidence, correcting course, and providing the feedback loop that agents cannot yet provide for themselves."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    You want agents to operate autonomously for extended periods (overnight, across sessions) without human intervention for routine decisions."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    The human is currently performing multiple AIF functions simultaneously: sensory gating (directing attention), belief correction (updating the model), policy selection (choosing actions), confidence calibration (setting τ), and free energy signaling (pushing back when things go wrong). These functions are bundled in the human's judgment and feel like \"just coaching\" — but each is a distinct infrastructure capability that doesn't exist yet. Removing the human without transferring these functions produces unsupervised agents that drift, stall, or overcommit."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Enumerate the placental functions the human currently provides. For each one, identify what infrastructure would replace it: peripherals for sensory gating, AIF modeline for confidence calibration, Drawbridge for inter-agent coordination, liveness-heartbeats for stall detection. Transfer one function at a time, validate that agents self-regulate on that dimension, then transfer the next. The human remains as the slow-timescale preference layer (what matters, what's worth doing) while fast-loop functions migrate to infrastructure."} {:name "PLACENTAL-FUNCTIONS", :name-key "placental-functions", :slug "placental-functions", :text "      - Sensory gating: \"look at X\" → transfer to: peripheral design (what surfaces are in the umwelt)\n      - Belief correction: \"no, that's wrong\" → transfer to: verification loops, test suites, evidence requirements\n      - Policy selection: \"try this approach\" → transfer to: AIF-as-environment, pattern catalog priors\n      - Confidence calibration: \"that's close\" / \"that's way off\" → transfer to: τ coupling, free energy modeline\n      - Free energy signal: \"push back\" → transfer to: prediction error computation, stall detection\n      - Inter-agent routing: \"ask Codex about this\" → transfer to: Agency whistle protocol, IRC coordination\n      - Stall detection: \"you've been stuck for 20 minutes\" → transfer to: Tickle agent, liveness heartbeats"} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    A placenta is a temporary organ. Its functions must transfer to the embryo's own systems for the organism to survive independently. Attempting to remove the placenta all at once (fully autonomous agents with no infrastructure) kills the embryo. Attempting to keep the placenta forever (human always in the loop) limits the organism to the human's bandwidth, attention span, and sleep schedule."} {:name "evidence", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "futon6 first-proof session (2026-02-11) — 89 commits, 10 problems, 7.5 hours, 3 agents. Human performed all routing, dispatch, confidence calibration. Agents were faster at proof search but bottlenecked on human attention. Human stayed up until 01:15."} {:name "evidence", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "futon3c ALFWorld session (2026-02-13) — human identified \"that's delegation not inhabitation\" (belief correction), directed attention to Drawbridge code (sensory gating), chose ALFWorld as next step (policy selection). Each intervention was a placental function."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Audit a recent multi-agent session: tag each human intervention with its placental function.\n    - Rank functions by frequency and transfer difficulty.\n    - Implement the easiest transfer first (likely stall detection → Tickle agent).\n    - Validate: run a session with that function transferred. Does the agent self-regulate on that dimension?\n    - Iterate until the human's role converges to slow-timescale preferences only."}], :conclusion "Identify which functions the human is performing as a surrogate for missing infrastructure, and transfer them one at a time to the system.", :directives {:audience "system architects, futon developers, anyone designing human-agent handoff", :references ["agent/pause-is-not-failure" "agent/evidence-over-assertion"], :sigils ["🫀/胎"], :style "pattern", :title "Transfer Placental Functions to Infrastructure", :tone "foundational"}, :id "gauntlet/placenta-transfer", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["agent/pause-is-not-failure" "agent/evidence-over-assertion"], :scores {}, :sigils ["🫀/胎"], :source-path "library/gauntlet/placenta-transfer.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Transfer Placental Functions to Infrastructure", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Autonomous agents that explore faster than humans can teach the humans who built them — but only if the communication channel carries insight, not just results."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Agents operate in a shared knowledge domain (proofs, code, patterns) alongside humans. The human initially acts as teacher, coach, and dispatcher."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    You want the human-agent system to produce more knowledge than either party could alone, and you want the human to learn — not just supervise."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    The default assumption is that knowledge flows one way: human teaches agent (via prompts, patterns, CLAUDE.md), agent executes. The human reviews output for correctness but does not expect to learn new domain content from the agent. This assumption becomes false when agents can search faster, try more strategies in parallel, and access more literature than the human. The bottleneck inverts: the agent finds things the human hasn't seen, but has no mechanism to teach them back."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "      - Result-only channel: agent commits code, human reviews correctness but misses the insight\n      - Explanation overhead: agent spends more tokens teaching than discovering (the compliance problem in a new guise)\n      - Asymmetric curiosity: agent doesn't know what the human doesn't know, so it can't target the teaching"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Design the communication channel (walkie-talkie, IRC, PAR) to carry insight, not just status. When an agent discovers an unexpected connection, lemma, or strategy, it should surface that discovery in a form the human can learn from: a short explanation with evidence, not just a commit message. The O-U learning model applies symmetrically: treatment events (new knowledge encounters) can flow from agent to human, producing measurable impulses in the human's vocabulary trajectory."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Peeragogy is peer learning — it doesn't privilege the teacher. If the agent finds a proof route the human missed, the human's AIF loop needs that observation to update its own beliefs. The human staying up until 1:15am dispatching agents is a bandwidth problem; the human not learning from what the agents found is a waste problem. Both are solved by the same mechanism: agents that can surface their discoveries as teachable moments."} {:name "evidence", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "futon6 first-proof session — agents explored 10 problems in 7.5 hours, found proof strategies the human hadn't considered. Results were committed as files but the insight transfer happened through handoff documents (dear-codex.md, problem6-claude-handoff.md), not through direct teaching."} {:name "evidence", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "PLoP 2025 paper (p4ng/main.tex §1) — \"patterns can help coordinate perspectives through observation and argumentation\" — this applies to agent→human as much as human→agent."} {:name "evidence", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "futon6 PLAN.md O-U model — α·δ(t) impulse from treatment events. If the treatment event is an agent showing the human a new technique, α measures how much the human learned."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Add a \"discovery\" event type to the PAR structure: what did the agent find that it didn't expect?\n    - Surface discoveries in the IRC channel or PAR with enough context for the human to learn.\n    - Track whether the human's vocabulary trajectory shows impulses correlated with agent discoveries.\n    - Design the teaching channel to be low-overhead: one-paragraph summaries with evidence links, not tutorials."}], :conclusion "Autonomous agents that explore faster than humans can teach the humans who built them — but only if the communication channel carries insight, not just results.", :directives {:audience "human-agent team designers, futon developers, peeragogy practitioners", :references ["gauntlet/placenta-transfer" "agent/evidence-over-assertion"], :sigils ["🔄/師"], :style "pattern", :title "The Teaching Inversion", :tone "foundational"}, :id "gauntlet/teaching-inversion", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["gauntlet/placenta-transfer" "agent/evidence-over-assertion"], :scores {}, :sigils ["🔄/師"], :source-path "library/gauntlet/teaching-inversion.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "The Teaching Inversion", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Design from the agent's sensory perspective inward, not from the system topology downward."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are building or documenting a multi-agent system with peripherals, coordination, and shared state."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    You want agents to behave naturally in their environment rather than executing remote procedure calls against an architecture diagram."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    The default instinct is to draw the God's-eye view: boxes for services, arrows for connections, the architect looking down. This produces plumbing diagrams that tell you what's connected but not what any agent actually perceives. Agents designed from architecture diagrams become RPC clients, not inhabitants."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Start by drawing what the agent sees: its sensory surfaces (what observations arrive), its active surfaces (what actions are available), and the darkness beyond (what it cannot perceive). The architecture follows from the union of all agents' umwelts, not the other way around. Each new surface added to an agent's world should be justified by what behavior it enables, not by what infrastructure exists."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    The peripheral's job is to constitute the agent's world (P-1), not to expose the system's internals. An agent that sees \"Codex is examining the microwave\" inhabits a shared world. An agent that calls GET /agency/agents/{codex}/status queries a database. Same information, different phenomenology, different behavior."} {:name "evidence", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "ALFWorld session (2026-02-13) — when observations were room descriptions and available actions, first-person navigation language emerged naturally. Blackboard wiring diagram of futon stack produced correct topology but zero insight into agent experience until redrawn as umwelt."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - For each agent type, draw the umwelt diagram before the architecture diagram.\n    - Enumerate sensory surfaces: what streams does this agent receive?\n    - Enumerate active surfaces: what actions can this agent take?\n    - Map the darkness: what exists in the system that this agent cannot perceive? Is that darkness intentional or accidental?\n    - When adding infrastructure, ask \"what does this change in the agent's umwelt?\" not \"what does this connect to?\""}], :conclusion "Design from the agent's sensory perspective inward, not from the system topology downward.", :directives {:audience "peripheral designers, agent architects, wiring diagram authors", :sigils ["👁/覚"], :style "pattern", :title "Draw the Umwelt, Not the Architecture", :tone "foundational"}, :id "gauntlet/umwelt-not-architecture", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["👁/覚"], :source-path "library/gauntlet/umwelt-not-architecture.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Draw the Umwelt, Not the Architecture", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "The agents' game world is not a separate artifact to build — it is the hypergraph they already inhabit. Documents, code, scholia, patterns, missions, and presence are all nodes and hyperedges in the same structure."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are designing the shared environment for multi-agent coordination in the futon ecosystem."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    You want agents to inhabit a rich, shared, mutable world where they can perceive objects, other agents, and the consequences of actions — rather than operating as isolated processes that happen to share a filesystem."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    The instinct is to build a game world as a separate layer: a dedicated server with game state, turn ordering, observation endpoints. This is how ALFWorld works (HTTP server wrapping TextWorld), and it produces a clean but isolated experience. The real work happens in the codebase, the database, the mission documents — but those feel like \"infrastructure\" rather than \"world.\" Agents treat them as APIs to query, not as places to be."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Recognize that the futon stack IS the game world. futon1 (XTDB) is the persistent map. futon2 (ant hill) is the ecology. futon3 (Agency, Drawbridge, IRC, peripherals) is the coordination layer. futon4 (Arxana) is the hypertext fabric — hyperedges with typed roles connecting documents, code, annotations, and agents. futon5 (wiring diagrams) describes the world's structure. futon6 (math dictionary / proof engine) is a quest zone. The agents are already in the game; the task is making the game legible, not building a separate one."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    ALFWorld taught us that text descriptions of rooms and objects naturally produce first-person inhabitation language. But the futon hypergraph has the same structure: nodes are locations (files, functions, missions), hyperedges are relationships (scholia, dependencies, presence), and traversal is navigation. \"I found the multiplex function in drawbridge/core.clj\" is phenomenologically identical to \"I found the vase on the dresser.\" The difference is that the hypergraph is real and mutable — agents can add nodes, create hyperedges, annotate each other's work. The game world writes itself."} {:name "evidence", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "futon4 hyperedge model (arxana-store.el:565) — {:hx/type, :hx/endpoints [{:role/source, :role/target, :role/passage}]} — N-ary typed relations stored in XTDB."} {:name "evidence", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "ALFWorld session (2026-02-13) — 2 games completed with first-person language (\"I found the vase\", \"I'm at the fridge\"). Same phenomenology emerged when exploring Drawbridge code (\"P-1 and P-6 are already implemented\")."} {:name "evidence", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "futon3 agency.mm — Mermaid diagram showing Agency topology. Describes one subgraph of the world; the full world is the union of all futon repos as a hypergraph."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Draft the futon stack as a futon4 hypergraph: repos as nodes, inter-repo connections as hyperedges with typed roles.\n    - Identify which hyperedges exist (code dependencies, mission references) and which are missing (presence, activity traces).\n    - Make the hypergraph queryable from within agent peripherals: \"what's connected to this node?\" as a sensory surface.\n    - Test whether agents navigate the hypergraph differently when it's presented as a world (traversable nodes and edges) vs. as an API (query endpoints)."}], :conclusion "The agents' game world is not a separate artifact to build — it is the hypergraph they already inhabit. Documents, code, scholia, patterns, missions, and presence are all nodes and hyperedges in the same structure.", :directives {:audience "futon architects, game world designers, peripheral implementers", :references ["gauntlet/umwelt-not-architecture" "gauntlet/co-presence-over-co-location"], :sigils ["🌐/界"], :style "pattern", :title "The World Is the Hypergraph", :tone "foundational"}, :id "gauntlet/world-is-hypergraph", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["gauntlet/umwelt-not-architecture" "gauntlet/co-presence-over-co-location"], :scores {}, :sigils ["🌐/界"], :source-path "library/gauntlet/world-is-hypergraph.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "The World Is the Hypergraph", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "    Design FUTON6+FUTON3 as a bootstrapping kernel: pick a coherent seed domain, define import contracts,\n    pattern-matching rules, and scholium conventions, and add new entries incrementally. Accept that the\n    kernel evolves and retrofits earlier content as it gains power."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are defining a minimal HDM kernel that can grow without collapsing under its own scope."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      Extant mathematics is vast; we cannot formalise or parse it all at once, yet a tiny static prototype\n      that cannot ingest new material fails to count as HDM."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      Ad hoc ingestion without a kernel produces inconsistent entries and unmaintainable conventions."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "      Design FUTON6+FUTON3 as a bootstrapping kernel: pick a coherent seed domain, define import contracts,\n      pattern-matching rules, and scholium conventions, and add new entries incrementally. Accept that the\n      kernel evolves and retrofits earlier content as it gains power.\n      Build a minimal kernel: a functioning subset of patterns, data structures, and procedures that interpret\n      transparent text, produce flexiarg skeletons, and integrate new entries with human help."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      HDM cannot wait for full automation; the viable strategy is an evolving kernel that becomes \"just good\n      enough\" to strengthen itself. Bootstrapping is a permanent mode, not a temporary phase."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Select and freeze a first seed slice (e.g. a compact MSC26 subset) and deliver end-to-end ingestion.]\n    next[Treat every kernel upgrade as a trigger to re-run transformation passes on older entries.]\n    next[Capture failures, ambiguities, and partial ingests as scholia owned by the kernel.]"}], :conclusion "    Design FUTON6+FUTON3 as a bootstrapping kernel: pick a coherent seed domain, define import contracts,\n    pattern-matching rules, and scholium conventions, and add new entries incrementally. Accept that the\n    kernel evolves and retrofits earlier content as it gains power.", :directives {:sigils ["🌞/公"], :title "HDM - Bootstrap Kernel"}, :id "hdm/bootstrap-kernel", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🌞/公"], :source-path "library/hdm/bootstrap-kernel.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "HDM - Bootstrap Kernel", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "    Use HDM/FUTON6 as an aggregation + activation layer: ingest key fragments from deep storage into a coherent graph, annotate\n    them with patterns, proof skeletons, and cross-links, and maintain mappings back to origin sites for provenance."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are unifying deep-storage sources into a navigable, provenance-preserving graph."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      Open mathematical repositories (\"deep storage\") already exist-PlanetMath, arXiv, nLab, wikis, code archives-holding\n      enormous value but lacking uniform structure and computational affordances."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      Treating each repository as its own universe squanders synergies and makes it hard for humans or agents to form a unified\n      mental map of mathematics."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "      Use HDM/FUTON6 as an aggregation + activation layer: ingest key fragments from deep storage into a coherent graph, annotate\n      them with patterns, proof skeletons, and cross-links, and maintain mappings back to origin sites for provenance."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      A unified, pattern-indexed graph enables reasoning/learning across boundaries while respecting each project's autonomy and\n      identity."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Prototype a deep-storage import pipeline (e.g. PlanetMath MSC26 subset) into FUTON6.]\n    next[Record origin metadata and round-trip links for every imported node.]\n    next[Apply HDM.L2 motifs (simulation, transparency, outline-first) as design constraints on the pipeline.]\n    next[Use this pattern to guide consolidation of old backup drives into active, linked storage.]"}], :conclusion "    Use HDM/FUTON6 as an aggregation + activation layer: ingest key fragments from deep storage into a coherent graph, annotate\n    them with patterns, proof skeletons, and cross-links, and maintain mappings back to origin sites for provenance.", :directives {:sigils ["🙅/乃"], :title "HDM - Deep Storage -> Active Graph"}, :id "hdm/deep-storage-to-active-graph", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🙅/乃"], :source-path "library/hdm/deep-storage-to-active-graph.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "HDM - Deep Storage -> Active Graph", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  make human-machine dialogue a core HDM workflow so ambiguous material can be clarified and formalised reliably."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are embedding clarification dialogues as part of HDM ingestion and retrieval."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    Automated parsing/formalisation of mathematical text is brittle: ambiguous notation, implicit steps,\n    and missing context routinely break fully automatic tooling."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Purely manual curation does not scale, and purely automated parsing loses critical nuance."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Make interactive dialogue part of the HDM kernel: let the system ask humans for help, record exchanges as scholia,\n    and treat resolved clarifications as reusable knowledge. Likewise, let humans query HDM entries and have agents respond\n    via the same pattern base.\n    Use human/LLM conversation to disambiguate notation and refine structure until it can be formalised."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      Dialogue is already how humans learn/teach mathematics; embedding it in HDM aligns with practice and leverages LLM\n      strengths instead of fighting them."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Store clarification dialogues alongside affected entries.]\n    next[Build dialogue agents that turn conversations into updated flexiarg or metadata.]\n    next[Assume some imports require dialogue and design ingestion workflows accordingly.]"}], :conclusion "  make human-machine dialogue a core HDM workflow so ambiguous material can be clarified and formalised reliably.", :directives {:sigils ["💬/口"], :title "HDM - Human<->Machine Dialogue"}, :id "hdm/human-machine-dialogue", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["💬/口"], :source-path "library/hdm/human-machine-dialogue.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "HDM - Human<->Machine Dialogue", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  treat HDM as an engine for building hyperreal capital by turning archives into traversable practice."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are turning mathematical knowledge into operational skill for more people."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      Mathematicians hold \"real\" capital (papers, code, reputation) and \"hyperreal\" capital (internalised moves\n      that let them navigate spaces, solve problems, and generate results)."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      Access to real capital is uneven, and opaque formats or turgid writing make it hard to convert real capital\n      into hyperreal capital, especially for students or people outside well-resourced institutions."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Treat HDM as an engine for generating and distributing hyperreal capital: provide patterns, examples,\n    traversable proof landscapes, and training grounds that deliver operational understanding over mere citation.\n    Measure success by how easily newcomers can move in a domain."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      Mathematical value lies in what people (and agents) can *do*; HDM should lower the barrier from \"reading\" to\n      \"acting.\""} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Link FUTON6 explicitly to FUTON3 training grounds; treat learning trails as first-class scholia.]\n    next[Design seed domains with pedagogy in mind, not just archival fidelity.]\n    next[Develop simple metrics for movement capability (e.g. time to solve standard tasks with HDM support).]"}], :conclusion "  treat HDM as an engine for building hyperreal capital by turning archives into traversable practice.", :directives {:sigils ["🐴/才"], :title "HDM - Hyperreal Capital"}, :id "hdm/hyperreal-capital", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🐴/才"], :source-path "library/hdm/hyperreal-capital.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "HDM - Hyperreal Capital", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  protect HDM as a commons by building explicit non-capture guardrails."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are protecting HDM as a commons against enclosure and dependency."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    HDM aims to be a commons: an open, evolving mathematical habitat anyone may inspect, contribute to, and reuse."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      Proprietary enclosures-paywalls, patent thickets, closed models trained on public data-can reassert control even when\n      APIs appear open, and funding/branding dependencies distort priorities."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Build explicit non-capture guardrails: free licensing for content/code, transparent provenance, duplication of critical\n    data across hosts, clear policies for integrating proprietary tools, and visible markings where upstream sources impose\n    restrictions."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      Without structural protection, HDM risks becoming another gated service; with guardrails it can stay a backbone for both\n      public and private tooling without being owned."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Draft and publish a FUTON6 Non-Capture Charter.]\n    next[Mark entries that incorporate or depend on non-free sources.]\n    next[Prefer architectures that can be mirrored, forked, or extended without permission.]"}], :conclusion "  protect HDM as a commons by building explicit non-capture guardrails.", :directives {:sigils ["🔦/公"], :title "HDM - Non-Capture Guardrails"}, :id "hdm/non-capture", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🔦/公"], :source-path "library/hdm/non-capture.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "HDM - Non-Capture Guardrails", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  convert opaque mathematical sources into transparent HDM entries so they can be inspected, linked, and reused."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are converting opaque mathematical sources into transparent HDM entries."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      Mathematical \"source code\" is mostly locked inside opaque containers-paywalled PDFs, printed\n      books, proprietary lecture notes, ad hoc code-and access to those encodings is unevenly distributed."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      Copyright protects expression, not ideas or methods, so humans and AI can recover underlying\n      mathematical content and re-express it in a new, free, structured form given sufficient labour\n      and tooling."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Make transparentisation a core HDM operation: convert opaque sources into HDM entries using a\n    mix of parsing, flexiarg skeletons, pattern recognition, and human-machine dialogue, always\n    re-expressing rather than copying. Treat the transparent graph as primary; opaque texts are just\n    one input stream."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      Without transparency, mathematical capital remains locked in institutions and formats; with it,\n      HDM can support equitable access, machine reasoning, and scalable human learning."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Tag entries whose content originated via transparentisation.]\n    next[Record scholia describing the transformation steps from particular sources.]\n    next[Use this pattern to guide FUTON6 bridges to PlanetMath, arXiv, and other deep storage corpora.]"}], :conclusion "  convert opaque mathematical sources into transparent HDM entries so they can be inspected, linked, and reused.", :directives {:sigils ["🌞/白"], :title "HDM - Opacity -> Transparency"}, :id "hdm/opacity-to-transparency", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🌞/白"], :source-path "library/hdm/opacity-to-transparency.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "HDM - Opacity -> Transparency", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  capture outline structure before full detail so arguments stay navigable and checkable."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are capturing mathematical structure at multiple resolutions without forcing full formal detail."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      Mathematical arguments admit multiple resolutions-high-level strategy, mid-level structure, low-level\n      derivations-and both humans and LLMs often grasp the outline long before the full detail."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      Many formal systems force lowest-level work from the start (expensive, discouraging) while purely informal\n      treatments hide the skeleton and make consistency checks difficult."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Make the outline layer primary in HDM: capture argument shape (claims, premises, patterns, key moves) in\n    flexiarg first, then attach more detailed derivations as needed. Support multi-resolution views so users and\n    agents can zoom between outline and detail."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      Capturing strategy and pattern is often enough to index an argument, relate it to others, and detect gross\n      inconsistencies; detail can be filled in on demand but the skeleton must stay visible."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Encourage F3 checkers to accept outline-level flexiarg and report graded confidence.]\n    next[Give FUTON6 viewers an easy toggle between outline-only and expanded proof.]\n    next[When importing legacy proofs, extract strategy first, then build detail.]"}], :conclusion "  capture outline structure before full detail so arguments stay navigable and checkable.", :directives {:sigils ["🐴/白"], :title "HDM - Outline First, Detail Later"}, :id "hdm/outline-first-detail-later", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🐴/白"], :source-path "library/hdm/outline-first-detail-later.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "HDM - Outline First, Detail Later", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "    Treat FUTON6/HDM as a deliberate simulation space that aims to surpass \"real\" practice in\n    navigability and recombinability. Design entries, scholia, and agent protocols for active\n    exploration and transformation rather than static citation."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are designing HDM as a simulation layer that outperforms linear archives."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      Historical mathematics lives in messy, locally optimised archives (books, PDFs, folklore)\n      and both humans and agents are constrained by linear reading plus fragile memory."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      Without a simulation layer, exploration remains bound to brittle storage formats and siloed narratives."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "      Treat FUTON6/HDM as a deliberate simulation space that aims to surpass \"real\" practice in\n      navigability and recombinability. Design entries, scholia, and agent protocols for active\n      exploration and transformation rather than static citation.\n      Host a hyperreal representation: the same concepts, theorems, and proofs laid out as a navigable,\n      pattern-indexed, flexiformal graph that is easier to explore, refactor, and extend than the archival\n      substrate while preserving provenance."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      Mathematics already lives partly as a hyperreality in imagination; HDM makes that shared and\n      concrete. A carefully designed simulation can support more insight, reuse, and collaboration\n      than fragmented historical storage."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[State explicitly in the FUTON6 charter that HDM is a simulation layer.]\n    next[For every seed domain, ask how the simulated neighbourhood outperforms just reading the papers.]\n    next[Use this pattern as a design sanity check for UI/API decisions in F6.]"}], :conclusion "    Treat FUTON6/HDM as a deliberate simulation space that aims to surpass \"real\" practice in\n    navigability and recombinability. Design entries, scholia, and agent protocols for active\n    exploration and transformation rather than static citation.", :directives {:sigils ["🚞/门"], :title "HDM - Simulation Beats Reality"}, :id "hdm/simulation-beats-reality", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🚞/门"], :source-path "library/hdm/simulation-beats-reality.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "HDM - Simulation Beats Reality", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Pure creative force initiates but does not complete; it requires The Receptive (坤) to manifest form."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "A situation calling for initiating action, full assertion, or creative will without compromise."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    All conditions favor forward movement; internal drive is maximal; no obstacles demand accommodation."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Pure yang without yin is unstable. \"The dragon that flies too high has cause for repent.\" Sustained assertion without receptivity exhausts itself or provokes reaction."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Initiate with full commitment but encode the exit condition. The creative that knows when to yield is truly strong. Watch for the Line 6 moment when receptivity becomes necessary."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Creation is the assertion of form into potentiality. Without a receptive medium, assertion dissipates. The pattern is universal because every domain has this dynamic: signal needs channel, action needs world, intention needs material."} {:name "evidence", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "Traditional: \"The movement of heaven is full of power. Thus the superior person makes himself strong and untiring.\""} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Identify the Receptive element in your domain (what receives your assertion?).\n    - Define the overreach condition (when does Line 6 apply?).\n    - Pair with hexagram 2 (坤) to complete the system.\n\n@ct-interpretation\n  ;; VISION: what this hexagram \"sees\"\n  :vision\n  {:objects #{:potential :manifest}\n   :morphism {:id :create\n              :source :potential\n              :target :manifest\n              :type :maximal}}\n\n  ;; As a morphism: pure intervention, no observation\n  :as-morphism\n  {:domain :any-potential-state\n   :codomain :asserted-form\n   :preservation :none  ; does not preserve existing structure\n   :composition :initiates-sequence}\n\n  ;; ADAPT condition\n  :adapt\n  {:trigger :overreach\n   :evidence [:exhaustion :resistance :instability]\n   :target :hexagram-02-kun}\n\n@ant-interpretation\n  ;; What 乾 means for cyberant behavior\n  :policy\n  {:mode-bias :outbound\n   :policy-priors {:forage 0.8 :return 0.1 :hold 0.05 :pheromone 0.05}\n   :description \"Maximum outbound drive; gather aggressively, ignore return signals\"}\n\n  :precision\n  {:Pi-o 0.1   ; low observation precision - ignore environment\n   :tau 0.1   ; low temperature - committed, not exploratory\n   :description \"Internal drive dominates; environmental signals downweighted\"}\n\n  :pattern-sense\n  {:trail-follow 0.0      ; ignore existing trails\n   :gradient-use 0.0      ; ignore pheromone gradients\n   :novelty-seek 0.9      ; pure exploration/assertion\n   :description \"Create new paths rather than follow existing structure\"}\n\n  :adapt-trigger\n  {:condition :cargo-full-and-starving\n   :interpretation \"Line 6: the dragon has overreached, must return\"\n   :switch-to :hexagram-02-kun}\n\n  :failure-mode\n  \"Pure 乾 ant starves while holding food - assertion without completion\"\n\n@mmca-interpretation\n  ;; What 乾 means as exotype/tensor operation\n  :sigil-encoding\n  {:sigil \"乾\"  ; or use the 8-bit approximation\n   :bits \"11111111\"\n   :interpretation \"All bits high = maximal rewrite pressure\"}\n\n  :exotype-params\n  {:rotation 3           ; adaptive (all directions)\n   :match-threshold 0.0  ; accept any context (no constraint)\n   :invert-on-phenotype? false\n   :update-prob 1.0      ; always update (maximum intervention)\n   :mix-mode :none       ; independent action\n   :description \"Maximum exotype pressure, deterministic rewrite\"}\n\n  :tensor-interpretation\n  {:operation :full-projection\n   :description \"Project state fully onto attractor; ignore prior structure\"\n   :matrix-form \"P = |target⟩⟨target| (projector onto asserted state)\"\n   :eigenstructure \"Single eigenvalue 1; all other states map to target\"}\n\n  :regime-prediction\n  {:expected :collapse-to-attractor\n   :dynamics \"Fast convergence to fixed point or simple cycle\"\n   :eoc-relevance \"Anti-EoC: eliminates diversity, forces uniformity\"\n   :caveat \"But may INITIATE interesting dynamics that 坤 then sustains\"}\n\n@morphisms-to-other-hexagrams\n  ;; How 乾 transforms to/from other hexagrams\n  :primary-pair\n  {:hexagram :hexagram-02-kun\n   :relationship :complement\n   :transformation \"Yang exhausts → yields to Yin\"\n   :trigger :line-6-changing}\n\n  :generates\n  [{:target :hexagram-11-tai\n    :mechanism \"乾 descends, 坤 ascends → meeting in middle\"\n    :interpretation \"Creative submits to serve; receptive rises to meet\"}]\n\n@domain-transfer-notes\n  \"乾 is the pattern of INITIATION across domains:\n   - In ants: aggressive foraging ignoring return signals\n   - In CA: deterministic rewrite collapsing to attractor\n   - In music: the opening statement, forte, no response yet\n   - In argument: pure assertion, no concession\n   - In code: the write that ignores existing state\n\n   The MORPHISM from 乾 to other hexagrams is the pattern of\n   how pure assertion must eventually accommodate. This is\n   domain-general and should transfer.\""}], :conclusion "Pure creative force initiates but does not complete; it requires The Receptive (坤) to manifest form.", :directives {:audience "improvisers, system designers, CT practitioners", :sigils ["👶/天"], :style "pattern", :title "䷀ 乾 (Qián) - The Creative", :tone "foundational"}, :id "iching/hexagram-01-qian", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["👶/天"], :source-path "library/iching/hexagram-01-qian.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "䷀ 乾 (Qián) - The Creative", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Pure receptive force sustains and completes but does not initiate; it requires The Creative (乾) to provide form."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "A situation calling for yielding, supporting, following, or providing the medium for another's initiative."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The creative impulse comes from elsewhere; your role is to receive, sustain, and bring to completion what has been initiated."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Pure yin without yang is formless. Receptivity without something to receive becomes void, passive, or dissipated. The mare follows but must have a direction to follow."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Yield with full commitment to the yielding. Do not initiate, but sustain with inexhaustible patience. The receptive that tries to lead loses its power; the receptive that fully receives gains everything."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Completion requires a medium that accepts form without distorting it. The pattern is universal: every signal needs a channel, every seed needs soil, every assertion needs a world that can hold it."} {:name "evidence", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "Traditional: \"The earth's condition is receptive devotion. Thus the superior person who has breadth of character carries the outer world.\""} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Identify what you are receiving (what creative force are you supporting?).\n    - Define the void condition (when does receptivity become emptiness?).\n    - Pair with hexagram 1 (乾) to complete the system.\n\n@ct-interpretation\n  ;; VISION: what this hexagram \"sees\"\n  :vision\n  {:objects #{:form :sustained-form}\n   :morphism {:id :sustain\n              :source :form\n              :target :sustained-form\n              :type :identity-preserving}}\n\n  ;; As a morphism: pure observation, no intervention\n  :as-morphism\n  {:domain :any-asserted-form\n   :codomain :same-form-sustained\n   :preservation :maximal  ; preserves all existing structure\n   :composition :continues-sequence}\n\n  ;; ADAPT condition\n  :adapt\n  {:trigger :void\n   :evidence [:nothing-to-receive :formlessness :dissipation]\n   :target :hexagram-01-qian}\n\n@ant-interpretation\n  ;; What 坤 means for cyberant behavior\n  :policy\n  {:mode-bias :homebound\n   :policy-priors {:forage 0.1 :return 0.7 :hold 0.15 :pheromone 0.05}\n   :description \"Maximum receptivity to environment; follow signals, return cargo\"}\n\n  :precision\n  {:Pi-o 0.9   ; high observation precision - fully attend to environment\n   :tau 0.9   ; high temperature - responsive, not committed\n   :description \"Environmental signals dominate; internal drive downweighted\"}\n\n  :pattern-sense\n  {:trail-follow 0.9      ; follow existing trails strongly\n   :gradient-use 0.9      ; follow pheromone gradients\n   :novelty-seek 0.0      ; no exploration, pure exploitation of existing paths\n   :description \"Follow existing structure rather than create new paths\"}\n\n  :adapt-trigger\n  {:condition :no-trail-no-gradient-no-cargo\n   :interpretation \"Void: nothing to receive, must initiate\"\n   :switch-to :hexagram-01-qian}\n\n  :failure-mode\n  \"Pure 坤 ant wanders following noise when no real signal exists\"\n\n@mmca-interpretation\n  ;; What 坤 means as exotype/tensor operation\n  :sigil-encoding\n  {:sigil \"坤\"  ; or use the 8-bit approximation\n   :bits \"00000000\"\n   :interpretation \"All bits low = minimal rewrite pressure\"}\n\n  :exotype-params\n  {:rotation 0           ; fixed orientation\n   :match-threshold 1.0  ; require exact match (maximal constraint)\n   :invert-on-phenotype? false\n   :update-prob 0.0      ; never update (no intervention)\n   :mix-mode :none       ; no mixing\n   :description \"Minimal exotype pressure, identity operation\"}\n\n  :tensor-interpretation\n  {:operation :identity\n   :description \"Preserve state exactly; no transformation\"\n   :matrix-form \"I = identity matrix\"\n   :eigenstructure \"All eigenvalues 1; every state is fixed point\"}\n\n  :regime-prediction\n  {:expected :preserve-initial-condition\n   :dynamics \"No evolution; frozen at initial state\"\n   :eoc-relevance \"Anti-EoC: eliminates change, forces stasis\"\n   :caveat \"But may SUSTAIN interesting dynamics that 乾 initiated\"}\n\n@morphisms-to-other-hexagrams\n  ;; How 坤 transforms to/from other hexagrams\n  :primary-pair\n  {:hexagram :hexagram-01-qian\n   :relationship :complement\n   :transformation \"Yin empties → calls for Yang\"\n   :trigger :void-condition}\n\n  :generates\n  [{:target :hexagram-11-tai\n    :mechanism \"坤 rises, 乾 descends → meeting in middle\"\n    :interpretation \"Receptive rises to meet; creative descends to serve\"}]\n\n@domain-transfer-notes\n  \"坤 is the pattern of SUSTAINING/RECEIVING across domains:\n   - In ants: following trails, returning cargo, responding to signals\n   - In CA: identity operation preserving existing state\n   - In music: the rest, the silence that holds the note\n   - In argument: listening, conceding, receiving the point\n   - In code: the read that preserves state\n\n   The MORPHISM from 坤 to other hexagrams is the pattern of\n   how pure receptivity must eventually call for initiative. This is\n   domain-general and should transfer.\""}], :conclusion "Pure receptive force sustains and completes but does not initiate; it requires The Creative (乾) to provide form.", :directives {:audience "improvisers, system designers, CT practitioners", :sigils ["👶/土"], :style "pattern", :title "䷁ 坤 (Kūn) - The Receptive", :tone "foundational"}, :id "iching/hexagram-02-kun", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["👶/土"], :source-path "library/iching/hexagram-02-kun.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "䷁ 坤 (Kūn) - The Receptive", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Beginnings are chaotic; persistence and small structure enable growth."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "A system has just initiated and lacks stable form."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    Movement is present but coordination is poor and obstacles are frequent."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Forcing order too early collapses vitality."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Establish minimal scaffolds and advance in small steps."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Early turbulence is normal; structure must emerge gradually."} {:name "evidence", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "Traditional: \"Difficulty at the beginning brings success through perseverance.\""} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Identify the smallest stabilizing loop.\n    - Protect the nascent structure from collapse.\n    - Pair with ䷃ (蒙) to develop learning.\n\n@ct-interpretation\n  :vision\n  {:objects #{:incipient :forming}\n   :morphism {:id :scaffold\n              :source :incipient\n              :target :forming\n              :type :bootstrapping}}\n\n  :as-morphism\n  {:domain :chaotic-start\n   :codomain :proto-order\n   :preservation :low\n   :composition :initiates-stabilization}\n\n  :adapt\n  {:trigger :overconstraint\n   :evidence [:stall :collapse]\n   :target :hexagram-04-meng}\n\n@ant-interpretation\n  :policy\n  {:mode-bias :adaptive\n   :policy-priors {:forage 0.4 :return 0.2 :hold 0.2 :pheromone 0.2}\n   :description \"Exploration with light stabilization\"}\n\n  :precision\n  {:Pi-o 0.4\n   :tau 0.7\n   :description \"Exploratory, lightly guided\"}\n\n  :pattern-sense\n  {:trail-follow 0.3\n   :gradient-use 0.4\n   :novelty-seek 0.7\n   :description \"Search for viable paths\"}\n\n  :adapt-trigger\n  {:condition :stall\n   :interpretation \"Need learning/discipline\"\n   :switch-to :hexagram-04-meng}\n\n  :failure-mode\n  \"Early chaos never stabilizes\"\n\n@mmca-interpretation\n  :sigil-encoding\n  {:sigil \"屯\"\n   :bits \"10001000\"\n   :interpretation \"Turbulent start with weak structure\"}\n\n  :exotype-params\n  {:rotation 2\n   :match-threshold 0.4\n   :invert-on-phenotype? true\n   :update-prob 0.6\n   :mix-mode :scramble\n   :description \"Active intervention to seed structure\"}\n\n  :tensor-interpretation\n  {:operation :bootstrapping-mix\n   :description \"Mixing to seed emergent order\"}\n\n  :regime-prediction\n  {:expected :turbulent\n   :dynamics \"Early churn with emerging motifs\"\n   :eoc-relevance \"Pre-EoC; needs stabilization\"}\n\n@domain-transfer-notes\n  \"屯 is the pattern of BOOTSTRAPPED BEGINNINGS across domains:\n   - In ants: early exploration with fragile loops\n   - In CA: turbulent starts before structure\""}], :conclusion "Beginnings are chaotic; persistence and small structure enable growth.", :directives {:audience "improvisers, system designers, CT practitioners", :sigils ["🏃/功"], :style "pattern", :title "䷂ 屯 (Zhūn) - Difficulty at the Beginning", :tone "foundational"}, :id "iching/hexagram-03-zhun", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🏃/功"], :source-path "library/iching/hexagram-03-zhun.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "䷂ 屯 (Zhūn) - Difficulty at the Beginning", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Immature energy needs guidance; learning requires discipline and patience."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "A system is active but naive, prone to error."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    Signals are noisy and actions are misaligned."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Excess correction discourages growth."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Provide clear constraints and allow iterative learning."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Early systems grow through guidance, not through force."} {:name "evidence", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "Traditional: \"Youthful folly. It is not I who seek the young fool; the young fool seeks me.\""} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Define a small set of clear rules.\n    - Encourage feedback without harsh punishment.\n    - Pair with ䷂ (屯) to stabilize learning.\n\n@ct-interpretation\n  :vision\n  {:objects #{:naive :guided}\n   :morphism {:id :teach\n              :source :naive\n              :target :guided\n              :type :constraint}}\n\n  :as-morphism\n  {:domain :noisy-state\n   :codomain :disciplined-state\n   :preservation :moderate\n   :composition :supports-learning}\n\n  :adapt\n  {:trigger :stagnant-learning\n   :evidence [:repetition :no-improvement]\n   :target :hexagram-03-zhun}\n\n@ant-interpretation\n  :policy\n  {:mode-bias :maintain\n   :policy-priors {:forage 0.3 :return 0.3 :hold 0.25 :pheromone 0.15}\n   :description \"Guided exploration with constraints\"}\n\n  :precision\n  {:Pi-o 0.7\n   :tau 0.5\n   :description \"Balanced guidance and exploration\"}\n\n  :pattern-sense\n  {:trail-follow 0.6\n   :gradient-use 0.5\n   :novelty-seek 0.3\n   :description \"Follow hints; allow modest exploration\"}\n\n  :adapt-trigger\n  {:condition :no-learning\n   :interpretation \"Need to re-seed turbulence\"\n   :switch-to :hexagram-03-zhun}\n\n  :failure-mode\n  \"Over-constraint suppresses learning\"\n\n@mmca-interpretation\n  :sigil-encoding\n  {:sigil \"蒙\"\n   :bits \"01000100\"\n   :interpretation \"Guided mixing with constraints\"}\n\n  :exotype-params\n  {:rotation 0\n   :match-threshold 0.65\n   :invert-on-phenotype? false\n   :update-prob 0.45\n   :mix-mode :majority\n   :description \"Constraint-biased coordination\"}\n\n  :tensor-interpretation\n  {:operation :guided-projection\n   :description \"Mixing constrained toward structure\"}\n\n  :regime-prediction\n  {:expected :learning\n   :dynamics \"Emergent structure with corrections\"\n   :eoc-relevance \"EoC-adjacent if guidance stays light\"}\n\n@domain-transfer-notes\n  \"蒙 is the pattern of GUIDED LEARNING across domains:\n   - In ants: constrained exploration\n   - In CA: structured emergence\""}], :conclusion "Immature energy needs guidance; learning requires discipline and patience.", :directives {:audience "improvisers, system designers, CT practitioners", :sigils ["👈/少"], :style "pattern", :title "䷃ 蒙 (Méng) - Youthful Folly / Envelopment", :tone "foundational"}, :id "iching/hexagram-04-meng", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["👈/少"], :source-path "library/iching/hexagram-04-meng.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "䷃ 蒙 (Méng) - Youthful Folly / Envelopment", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Waiting is active patience; prepare and hold position until conditions align."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Progress is possible but conditions are not yet favorable."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    Action now would waste resources or increase risk."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Waiting without preparation is idle."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Hold steady while strengthening readiness."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Timing matters; disciplined patience preserves capacity."} {:name "evidence", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "Traditional: \"Waiting. If you are sincere, you have light and success.\""} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Identify the alignment condition.\n    - Maintain readiness and supplies.\n    - Pair with ䷆ (訟) if conflict arises.\n\n@ct-interpretation\n  :vision\n  {:objects #{:ready :aligned}\n   :morphism {:id :await\n              :source :ready\n              :target :aligned\n              :type :timing}}\n\n  :as-morphism\n  {:domain :prepared-state\n   :codomain :opportune-state\n   :preservation :high\n   :composition :delays-until-ready}\n\n  :adapt\n  {:trigger :impatience\n   :evidence [:rushed-action :loss]\n   :target :hexagram-06-song}\n\n@ant-interpretation\n  :policy\n  {:mode-bias :maintain\n   :policy-priors {:forage 0.2 :return 0.35 :hold 0.35 :pheromone 0.1}\n   :description \"Hold with readiness; conserve resources\"}\n\n  :precision\n  {:Pi-o 0.7\n   :tau 0.3\n   :description \"Cautious and steady\"}\n\n  :pattern-sense\n  {:trail-follow 0.6\n   :gradient-use 0.4\n   :novelty-seek 0.1\n   :description \"Maintain known paths while waiting\"}\n\n  :adapt-trigger\n  {:condition :forced-conflict\n   :interpretation \"Waiting breaks into dispute\"\n   :switch-to :hexagram-06-song}\n\n  :failure-mode\n  \"Over-waiting leads to missed opportunity\"\n\n@mmca-interpretation\n  :sigil-encoding\n  {:sigil \"需\"\n   :bits \"11101000\"\n   :interpretation \"High readiness with low intervention\"}\n\n  :exotype-params\n  {:rotation 0\n   :match-threshold 0.8\n   :invert-on-phenotype? false\n   :update-prob 0.2\n   :mix-mode :none\n   :description \"Strong constraint, low update\"}\n\n  :tensor-interpretation\n  {:operation :holding-pattern\n   :description \"Preserve state until trigger\"}\n\n  :regime-prediction\n  {:expected :steady\n   :dynamics \"Stable, low-change trajectories\"\n   :eoc-relevance \"Anti-EoC unless paired with activation\"}\n\n@domain-transfer-notes\n  \"需 is the pattern of PREPARED WAITING across domains:\n   - In ants: hold position with supplies\n   - In CA: low-change stability\""}], :conclusion "Waiting is active patience; prepare and hold position until conditions align.", :directives {:audience "improvisers, system designers, CT practitioners", :sigils ["🗻/叼"], :style "pattern", :title "䷄ 需 (Xū) - Waiting", :tone "foundational"}, :id "iching/hexagram-05-xu", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🗻/叼"], :source-path "library/iching/hexagram-05-xu.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "䷄ 需 (Xū) - Waiting", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Conflict arises when forces oppose; clarity and restraint prevent escalation."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Two currents collide and progress halts."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    Goals or signals are misaligned and actions interfere."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Escalation consumes resources and worsens the split."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Seek clarification and minimal resolution; avoid total confrontation."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Conflict can be resolved through boundary-setting rather than domination."} {:name "evidence", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "Traditional: \"Conflict. You are sincere, but you will be obstructed.\""} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Identify the misalignment.\n    - Reduce interference pathways.\n    - Pair with ䷄ (需) to return to patience.\n\n@ct-interpretation\n  :vision\n  {:objects #{:opposed :clarified}\n   :morphism {:id :clarify\n              :source :opposed\n              :target :clarified\n              :type :boundary}}\n\n  :as-morphism\n  {:domain :conflicted-state\n   :codomain :bounded-state\n   :preservation :low\n   :composition :reduces-interference}\n\n  :adapt\n  {:trigger :escalation\n   :evidence [:collision :loss]\n   :target :hexagram-05-xu}\n\n@ant-interpretation\n  :policy\n  {:mode-bias :maintain\n   :policy-priors {:forage 0.25 :return 0.3 :hold 0.3 :pheromone 0.15}\n   :description \"Contain conflict; avoid escalation\"}\n\n  :precision\n  {:Pi-o 0.7\n   :tau 0.4\n   :description \"Moderate responsiveness\"}\n\n  :pattern-sense\n  {:trail-follow 0.4\n   :gradient-use 0.4\n   :novelty-seek 0.2\n   :description \"Avoid conflicting gradients\"}\n\n  :adapt-trigger\n  {:condition :escalate\n   :interpretation \"Return to waiting\"\n   :switch-to :hexagram-05-xu}\n\n  :failure-mode\n  \"Escalation drains reserves\"\n\n@mmca-interpretation\n  :sigil-encoding\n  {:sigil \"訟\"\n   :bits \"01011100\"\n   :interpretation \"Competing pressures\"}\n\n  :exotype-params\n  {:rotation 1\n   :match-threshold 0.55\n   :invert-on-phenotype? true\n   :update-prob 0.45\n   :mix-mode :xor-neighbor\n   :description \"Competitive mixing\"}\n\n  :tensor-interpretation\n  {:operation :opposition\n   :description \"Interference between competing modes\"}\n\n  :regime-prediction\n  {:expected :contentious\n   :dynamics \"Irregular oscillations\"\n   :eoc-relevance \"Can seed EoC if resolved\"}\n\n@domain-transfer-notes\n  \"訟 is the pattern of CONFLICT across domains:\n   - In ants: competing gradients and stalled returns\n   - In CA: interfering waves\""}], :conclusion "Conflict arises when forces oppose; clarity and restraint prevent escalation.", :directives {:audience "improvisers, system designers, CT practitioners", :sigils ["✊/口"], :style "pattern", :title "䷅ 訟 (Sòng) - Conflict", :tone "foundational"}, :id "iching/hexagram-06-song", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["✊/口"], :source-path "library/iching/hexagram-06-song.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "䷅ 訟 (Sòng) - Conflict", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Collective action requires discipline; coordination must serve a clear objective."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Many agents act together under pressure."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The system needs coordinated motion and unified direction."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Without discipline, the group fragments or overreaches."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Establish clear command and shared objectives; coordinate actions."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Collective strength emerges only with alignment and restraint."} {:name "evidence", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "Traditional: \"The Army. Perseverance furthers.\""} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Define the common objective.\n    - Assign roles and limits.\n    - Pair with ䷇ (比) to maintain cohesion.\n\n@ct-interpretation\n  :vision\n  {:objects #{:many :aligned}\n   :morphism {:id :coordinate\n              :source :many\n              :target :aligned\n              :type :collective}}\n\n  :as-morphism\n  {:domain :distributed-state\n   :codomain :coordinated-state\n   :preservation :moderate\n   :composition :synchronizes}\n\n  :adapt\n  {:trigger :fragmentation\n   :evidence [:drift :loss-of-command]\n   :target :hexagram-08-bi}\n\n@ant-interpretation\n  :policy\n  {:mode-bias :maintain\n   :policy-priors {:forage 0.3 :return 0.3 :hold 0.2 :pheromone 0.2}\n   :description \"Coordinated trail-based movement\"}\n\n  :precision\n  {:Pi-o 0.7\n   :tau 0.4\n   :description \"Cohesion with moderate exploration\"}\n\n  :pattern-sense\n  {:trail-follow 0.7\n   :gradient-use 0.5\n   :novelty-seek 0.2\n   :description \"Prefer shared routes\"}\n\n  :adapt-trigger\n  {:condition :fragmentation\n   :interpretation \"Shift to bonding\"\n   :switch-to :hexagram-08-bi}\n\n  :failure-mode\n  \"Uncoordinated swarms waste resources\"\n\n@mmca-interpretation\n  :sigil-encoding\n  {:sigil \"師\"\n   :bits \"01000000\"\n   :interpretation \"Coordinated but restrained pressure\"}\n\n  :exotype-params\n  {:rotation 1\n   :match-threshold 0.6\n   :invert-on-phenotype? false\n   :update-prob 0.35\n   :mix-mode :majority\n   :description \"Coordinated mixing with restraint\"}\n\n  :tensor-interpretation\n  {:operation :synchronized-mix\n   :description \"Align local updates into collective motion\"}\n\n  :regime-prediction\n  {:expected :coordinated\n   :dynamics \"Structured collective patterns\"\n   :eoc-relevance \"May stabilize EoC by cohesion\"}\n\n@domain-transfer-notes\n  \"師 is the pattern of COORDINATED FORCE across domains:\n   - In ants: disciplined trail following\n   - In CA: synchronized structures\""}], :conclusion "Collective action requires discipline; coordination must serve a clear objective.", :directives {:audience "improvisers, system designers, CT practitioners", :sigils ["🎎/功"], :style "pattern", :title "䷆ 師 (Shī) - The Army", :tone "foundational"}, :id "iching/hexagram-07-shi", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🎎/功"], :source-path "library/iching/hexagram-07-shi.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "䷆ 師 (Shī) - The Army", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Union creates strength; cohesion must be chosen and maintained."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "A system seeks stability through alliance and shared bonds."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    Agents can align under a common center."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Forced unity can hide weakness or suppress needed diversity."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Form bonds through trust and clear purpose; allow exits when misaligned."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Cohesion is a chosen alignment, not a permanent constraint."} {:name "evidence", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "Traditional: \"Holding together brings success.\""} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Establish a center of trust.\n    - Maintain cohesion through shared benefit.\n    - Pair with ䷆ (師) for disciplined action.\n\n@ct-interpretation\n  :vision\n  {:objects #{:scattered :unified}\n   :morphism {:id :bind\n              :source :scattered\n              :target :unified\n              :type :cohesive}}\n\n  :as-morphism\n  {:domain :distributed-state\n   :codomain :cohesive-state\n   :preservation :moderate\n   :composition :binds}\n\n  :adapt\n  {:trigger :forced-unity\n   :evidence [:suppression :fragility]\n   :target :hexagram-07-shi}\n\n@ant-interpretation\n  :policy\n  {:mode-bias :maintain\n   :policy-priors {:forage 0.25 :return 0.35 :hold 0.25 :pheromone 0.15}\n   :description \"Bonding via shared return cycles\"}\n\n  :precision\n  {:Pi-o 0.7\n   :tau 0.4\n   :description \"Steady cohesion\"}\n\n  :pattern-sense\n  {:trail-follow 0.7\n   :gradient-use 0.5\n   :novelty-seek 0.15\n   :description \"Follow shared routes\"}\n\n  :adapt-trigger\n  {:condition :fragile-unity\n   :interpretation \"Shift to disciplined coordination\"\n   :switch-to :hexagram-07-shi}\n\n  :failure-mode\n  \"Rigid unity suppresses exploration\"\n\n@mmca-interpretation\n  :sigil-encoding\n  {:sigil \"比\"\n   :bits \"00001000\"\n   :interpretation \"Cohesive mixing near a center\"}\n\n  :exotype-params\n  {:rotation 1\n   :match-threshold 0.6\n   :invert-on-phenotype? false\n   :update-prob 0.35\n   :mix-mode :majority\n   :description \"Consensus-based updates\"}\n\n  :tensor-interpretation\n  {:operation :consensus\n   :description \"Local agreement strengthens structure\"}\n\n  :regime-prediction\n  {:expected :cohesive\n   :dynamics \"Unified patterns with low variance\"\n   :eoc-relevance \"Stabilizes but may reduce diversity\"}\n\n@domain-transfer-notes\n  \"比 is the pattern of UNION across domains:\n   - In ants: cohesion around shared routes\n   - In CA: consensus structures\""}], :conclusion "Union creates strength; cohesion must be chosen and maintained.", :directives {:audience "improvisers, system designers, CT practitioners", :sigils ["👋/亏"], :style "pattern", :title "䷇ 比 (Bǐ) - Holding Together / Union", :tone "foundational"}, :id "iching/hexagram-08-bi", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["👋/亏"], :source-path "library/iching/hexagram-08-bi.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "䷇ 比 (Bǐ) - Holding Together / Union", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Small influences can restrain great forces; gentle control accumulates."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "A strong momentum needs subtle correction."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    Power is present but must be regulated without direct confrontation."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Minor control is slow and can be overwhelmed."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Apply repeated small corrections to guide the larger force."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Gentle constraints can shape large motion over time."} {:name "evidence", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "Traditional: \"The Taming Power of the Small. Success.\""} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Identify the small leverage points.\n    - Apply consistent, light pressure.\n    - Pair with ䷂ (屯) if turbulence grows.\n\n@ct-interpretation\n  :vision\n  {:objects #{:strong :guided}\n   :morphism {:id :tame\n              :source :strong\n              :target :guided\n              :type :gentle}}\n\n  :as-morphism\n  {:domain :high-momentum\n   :codomain :steered-momentum\n   :preservation :moderate\n   :composition :nudges}\n\n  :adapt\n  {:trigger :overwhelm\n   :evidence [:loss-of-control :breakthrough]\n   :target :hexagram-03-zhun}\n\n@ant-interpretation\n  :policy\n  {:mode-bias :maintain\n   :policy-priors {:forage 0.35 :return 0.3 :hold 0.2 :pheromone 0.15}\n   :description \"Gentle steering of strong foraging drive\"}\n\n  :precision\n  {:Pi-o 0.6\n   :tau 0.5\n   :description \"Moderate responsiveness\"}\n\n  :pattern-sense\n  {:trail-follow 0.5\n   :gradient-use 0.5\n   :novelty-seek 0.3\n   :description \"Guide without suppressing\"}\n\n  :adapt-trigger\n  {:condition :overrun\n   :interpretation \"Control overwhelmed\"\n   :switch-to :hexagram-03-zhun}\n\n  :failure-mode\n  \"Small corrections fail to restrain surges\"\n\n@mmca-interpretation\n  :sigil-encoding\n  {:sigil \"小畜\"\n   :bits \"11101100\"\n   :interpretation \"Small constraints on strong pressure\"}\n\n  :exotype-params\n  {:rotation 1\n   :match-threshold 0.55\n   :invert-on-phenotype? false\n   :update-prob 0.45\n   :mix-mode :rotate-right\n   :description \"Gentle steering\"}\n\n  :tensor-interpretation\n  {:operation :small-nudge\n   :description \"Light correction of strong flow\"}\n\n  :regime-prediction\n  {:expected :guided\n   :dynamics \"Strong motion with mild steering\"\n   :eoc-relevance \"Potentially stabilizing for EoC\"}\n\n@domain-transfer-notes\n  \"小畜 is the pattern of GENTLE RESTRAINT across domains:\n   - In ants: slight steering of foraging\n   - In CA: mild control of strong dynamics\""}], :conclusion "Small influences can restrain great forces; gentle control accumulates.", :directives {:audience "improvisers, system designers, CT practitioners", :sigils ["⭕️/入"], :style "pattern", :title "䷈ 小畜 (Xiǎo Chù) - The Taming Power of the Small", :tone "foundational"}, :id "iching/hexagram-09-xiaochu", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["⭕️/入"], :source-path "library/iching/hexagram-09-xiaochu.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "䷈ 小畜 (Xiǎo Chù) - The Taming Power of the Small", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Treading carefully among power prevents harm; respect the terrain."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "A system moves within strong forces or constraints."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    Progress requires precision and respect for boundaries."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Reckless steps provoke danger."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Move with care, maintain balance, and avoid provoking instability."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Safe progress depends on situational awareness and measured action."} {:name "evidence", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "Traditional: \"Treading. Tread upon the tail of the tiger. It does not bite.\""} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Identify the dangerous edges.\n    - Slow down and keep balance.\n    - Pair with ䷄ (需) to wait when needed.\n\n@ct-interpretation\n  :vision\n  {:objects #{:dangerous-path :safe-step}\n   :morphism {:id :tread\n              :source :dangerous-path\n              :target :safe-step\n              :type :careful}}\n\n  :as-morphism\n  {:domain :constrained-state\n   :codomain :safe-progress\n   :preservation :high\n   :composition :careful-progress}\n\n  :adapt\n  {:trigger :instability\n   :evidence [:risk-spike :near-failure]\n   :target :hexagram-05-xu}\n\n@ant-interpretation\n  :policy\n  {:mode-bias :maintain\n   :policy-priors {:forage 0.3 :return 0.3 :hold 0.25 :pheromone 0.15}\n   :description \"Careful movement under constraints\"}\n\n  :precision\n  {:Pi-o 0.75\n   :tau 0.3\n   :description \"High precision, low exploration\"}\n\n  :pattern-sense\n  {:trail-follow 0.6\n   :gradient-use 0.5\n   :novelty-seek 0.1\n   :description \"Respect established paths\"}\n\n  :adapt-trigger\n  {:condition :risk-spike\n   :interpretation \"Pause and wait\"\n   :switch-to :hexagram-05-xu}\n\n  :failure-mode\n  \"Over-cautiousness reduces gains\"\n\n@mmca-interpretation\n  :sigil-encoding\n  {:sigil \"履\"\n   :bits \"11111000\"\n   :interpretation \"Strong structure with careful constraints\"}\n\n  :exotype-params\n  {:rotation 0\n   :match-threshold 0.75\n   :invert-on-phenotype? false\n   :update-prob 0.25\n   :mix-mode :none\n   :description \"Careful, low intervention\"}\n\n  :tensor-interpretation\n  {:operation :careful-filter\n   :description \"Preserve structure while moving safely\"}\n\n  :regime-prediction\n  {:expected :stable\n   :dynamics \"Safe, low-variance progression\"\n   :eoc-relevance \"Stabilizes, may reduce exploration\"}\n\n@domain-transfer-notes\n  \"履 is the pattern of CAREFUL PROGRESS across domains:\n   - In ants: cautious movement\n   - In CA: stable motion under constraints\""}], :conclusion "Treading carefully among power prevents harm; respect the terrain.", :directives {:audience "improvisers, system designers, CT practitioners", :sigils ["👕/允"], :style "pattern", :title "䷉ 履 (Lǚ) - Treading", :tone "foundational"}, :id "iching/hexagram-10-lu", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["👕/允"], :source-path "library/iching/hexagram-10-lu.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "䷉ 履 (Lǚ) - Treading", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "When the creative (heaven/乾) places itself below and the receptive (earth/坤) above, their energies meet and mingle - this is Peace."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "A situation where assertion serves receptivity, where the strong supports the yielding, where initiative and response find dynamic balance."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The creative force is willing to descend (serve, support) and the receptive is willing to rise (engage, participate). Neither dominates; both contribute."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Peace is not stasis. It is dynamic equilibrium requiring continuous adjustment. The moment one force stops moving toward the other, the balance tips toward hexagram 12 (否, Obstruction)."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Cultivate the meeting point. The creative must not assert dominance; the receptive must not collapse into passivity. Maintain the circulation: heaven's energy descends, earth's energy rises, they meet in the middle."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Sustainable dynamics require both initiation and sustainment in conversation. This is the edge-of-chaos condition: enough creative pressure to maintain change, enough receptive structure to prevent dissolution."} {:name "evidence", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "Traditional: \"Heaven and earth unite: the image of Peace. Thus the ruler divides and completes the course of heaven and earth; he furthers and regulates the gifts of heaven and earth, and so aids the people.\""} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Identify the meeting point in your domain (where do creation and reception converse?).\n    - Define the tipping conditions (what breaks the peace?).\n    - Watch for hexagram 12 (否) as the failure mode.\n\n@ct-interpretation\n  ;; VISION: what this hexagram \"sees\"\n  :vision\n  {:objects #{:creative-impulse :receptive-field :meeting-point}\n   :morphisms [{:id :descend :source :creative-impulse :target :meeting-point}\n               {:id :ascend :source :receptive-field :target :meeting-point}]}\n\n  ;; As a morphism: balanced composition of intervention and observation\n  :as-morphism\n  {:domain :polarized-state\n   :codomain :dynamic-equilibrium\n   :preservation :partial  ; preserves some structure, transforms some\n   :composition :creates-cycle}\n\n  ;; This IS the composition of 乾 and 坤\n  :as-composition\n  {:first :hexagram-01-qian\n   :second :hexagram-02-kun\n   :composition-type :meeting  ; not sequential, but convergent\n   :result \"Dynamic equilibrium where both forces are active\"}\n\n  ;; ADAPT condition\n  :adapt\n  {:trigger :imbalance\n   :evidence [:one-force-dominates :stagnation :separation]\n   :target :hexagram-12-pi}\n\n@ant-interpretation\n  ;; What 泰 means for cyberant behavior\n  :policy\n  {:mode-bias :adaptive\n   :policy-priors {:forage 0.35 :return 0.35 :hold 0.15 :pheromone 0.15}\n   :description \"Balanced between outbound and homebound; responsive to context\"}\n\n  :precision\n  {:Pi-o 0.5   ; balanced observation precision\n   :tau 0.5   ; balanced temperature\n   :description \"Neither internal drive nor environment dominates; both inform\"}\n\n  :pattern-sense\n  {:trail-follow 0.5      ; moderate trail following\n   :gradient-use 0.5      ; moderate gradient use\n   :novelty-seek 0.5      ; moderate exploration\n   :description \"Balance existing paths and new exploration\"}\n\n  :mode-transitions\n  {:outbound->homebound {:trigger :cargo-acquired :threshold 0.5}\n   :homebound->outbound {:trigger :cargo-deposited :threshold 0.5}\n   :description \"Smooth cycling between gather and return phases\"}\n\n  :adapt-trigger\n  {:condition :mode-stuck\n   :interpretation \"Imbalance: one phase dominating, circulation broken\"\n   :response :force-mode-switch}\n\n  :success-mode\n  \"泰 ant maintains healthy gather-return cycle, neither starving nor hoarding\"\n\n  :failure-mode\n  \"Imbalance: becomes pure 乾 (starves while exploring) or pure 坤 (passive, no gathering)\"\n\n@mmca-interpretation\n  ;; What 泰 means as exotype/tensor operation\n  :sigil-encoding\n  {:sigil \"泰\"\n   :bits \"00011100\"  ; approximate: low-high-low pattern, meeting in middle\n   :interpretation \"Mixed bits = balanced rewrite pressure\"}\n\n  :exotype-params\n  {:rotation 1           ; moderate rotation\n   :match-threshold 0.5  ; moderate constraint\n   :invert-on-phenotype? true  ; responsive to phenotype layer\n   :update-prob 0.5      ; probabilistic update (sometimes intervene, sometimes preserve)\n   :mix-mode :majority   ; coordinate with neighbors\n   :description \"Balanced exotype pressure, context-sensitive operation\"}\n\n  :tensor-interpretation\n  {:operation :convex-combination\n   :description \"Weighted average of projection (乾) and identity (坤)\"\n   :matrix-form \"M = α|target⟩⟨target| + (1-α)I, where α ∈ (0,1)\"\n   :eigenstructure \"Spectrum between 1 and α; partial convergence\"}\n\n  :regime-prediction\n  {:expected :edge-of-chaos\n   :dynamics \"Sustained heterogeneity; neither collapse nor explosion\"\n   :eoc-relevance \"THIS IS THE EOC CONDITION: enough pressure to change, enough structure to persist\"\n   :mechanism \"Creative pressure prevents stasis; receptive structure prevents dissolution\"}\n\n  :parameter-sensitivity\n  {:critical-parameter :update-prob\n   :critical-range [0.3 0.7]\n   :description \"泰 exists in the balance; too high → 乾, too low → 坤\"}\n\n@morphisms-to-other-hexagrams\n  ;; How 泰 transforms to/from other hexagrams\n  :inverse\n  {:hexagram :hexagram-12-pi\n   :name \"否 (Pǐ) - Obstruction\"\n   :relationship :inverse\n   :structure \"Heaven above, earth below - they separate instead of meeting\"\n   :transformation \"When circulation stops, peace becomes obstruction\"}\n\n  :degenerations\n  [{:target :hexagram-01-qian\n    :mechanism \"Creative force dominates, receptive withdraws\"\n    :trigger :assertion-overwhelms}\n   {:target :hexagram-02-kun\n    :mechanism \"Receptive force dominates, creative withdraws\"\n    :trigger :passivity-overwhelms}]\n\n  :emergence-from\n  [{:source :hexagram-01-qian\n    :mechanism \"Creative learns to descend/serve\"\n    :trigger :wisdom-of-yielding}\n   {:source :hexagram-02-kun\n    :mechanism \"Receptive learns to rise/engage\"\n    :trigger :courage-to-participate}]\n\n@domain-transfer-notes\n  \"泰 is the pattern of DYNAMIC BALANCE across domains:\n   - In ants: healthy gather-return cycle, neither starving nor hoarding\n   - In CA: edge-of-chaos, sustained heterogeneity\n   - In music: call and response, tension and resolution in dialogue\n   - In argument: dialectic, thesis meeting antithesis\n   - In code: read-modify-write cycle with appropriate locking\n\n   The MORPHISM structure around 泰:\n   - 乾 + 坤 → 泰 (composition: meeting of poles)\n   - 泰 → 否 (degeneration: separation of poles)\n   - 泰 → 乾 or 坤 (collapse: one pole dominates)\n\n   This is the EDGE OF CHAOS pattern. If the transfer hypothesis holds,\n   sigils/exotypes that produce EoC in CA should produce analogous\n   'sustainable adaptability' in ants and other domains.\"\n\n@experimental-predictions\n  ;; Concrete predictions for testing\n  :ca-prediction\n  \"Exotypes with params near 泰 (update-prob ~0.5, match-threshold ~0.5)\n   should show sustained heterogeneity in MMCA runs.\"\n\n  :ant-prediction\n  \"Cyberants configured with 泰 params should show:\n   1. Higher survival than pure 乾 or 坤\n   2. Balanced gather/return statistics\n   3. Robustness across different map layouts\"\n\n  :transfer-test\n  \"If EoC exotypes from MMCA (identified via M17) produce 泰-like\n   ant behavior, this validates domain-general transfer.\""}], :conclusion "When the creative (heaven/乾) places itself below and the receptive (earth/坤) above, their energies meet and mingle - this is Peace.", :directives {:audience "improvisers, system designers, CT practitioners", :sigils ["👈/天"], :style "pattern", :title "䷊ 泰 (Tài) - Peace / Harmony", :tone "foundational"}, :id "iching/hexagram-11-tai", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["👈/天"], :source-path "library/iching/hexagram-11-tai.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "䷊ 泰 (Tài) - Peace / Harmony", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "When heaven and earth separate, circulation stops; progress stalls until the poles meet again."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "A system has lost exchange between initiative and support; movement continues but no longer connects."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The strong withdraws upward, the receptive sinks downward, and the middle empties."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Standstill is not the end; it is a sign that the channel is broken, not that force is absent."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Restore circulation by re-linking the poles. Reduce domination, invite r