applies (the quantities are nonneg and finite)."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text ""} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      Unlike transport-across-isomorphism, you cannot freely move results\n      back and forth. The coercion is directed. ENNReal.toReal loses ⊤ → 0.\n      NNReal.coe is injective but NNReal.mk requires a proof of nonnegativity.\n      Each step requires an explicit compatibility hypothesis."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "      (a) Prove the core mathematical content in ℝ where the API is richest.\n      (b) Identify the coercion path: which coe/toReal/ofReal lemmas connect\n          your ℝ result to the ℝ≥0∞ goal?\n      (c) For each coercion step, discharge the compatibility side-condition\n          (nonnegativity, finiteness, ne_top).\n      (d) If the bridge is >5 steps, consider whether the lemma can be proved\n          directly in ℝ≥0∞ instead. Sometimes the Mathlib API has a parallel\n          version (e.g., ENNReal.rpow vs Real.rpow) that avoids the bridge."} {:name "LEAN", :name-key "lean", :slug "lean", :text "      api[ENNReal.ofReal] api[ENNReal.toReal] api[NNReal.coe_le_coe]\n      api[ENNReal.ofReal_le_ofReal] api[ENNReal.toReal_le_toReal]\n      tactic[rw [ENNReal.ofReal...] → exact ... → simp [ne_top_of_lt]]"} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      Lean's type system enforces the distinction between ℝ, ℝ≥0, and ℝ≥0∞\n      that informal mathematics elides. The coercion bridge is the price of\n      rigour. But the bridge is mechanical — once you know the path, each\n      step is a named lemma (ENNReal.ofReal_le_ofReal, NNReal.coe_le_coe,\n      ENNReal.toReal_le_toReal). The mathematical insight is in ℝ; the\n      engineering is in the bridge."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Identify which type the mathematical proof lives in.]\n    next[Map the coercion path to the goal's type.]\n    next[Discharge compatibility side-conditions at each step.]\n    next[If bridge is long, check for a parallel API in the target type.]"}], :conclusion "  When the mathematical proof lives in one type (ℝ) but the Mathlib API\n  operates in another (ℝ≥0∞), wire through the directed coercion chain\n  rather than trying to isomorphism-transport.", :directives {:audience "lean-provers, analysis-students", :keywords ["coercion" "cast" "lift" "ENNReal" "NNReal" "Real" "embedding" "transport" "directed"], :sigils ["🌉/渡"], :title "Coercion Bridge Between Type Universes", :tone "heuristic"}, :id "math-formalization/coercion-bridge", :keywords ["coercion" "cast" "lift" "ENNReal" "NNReal" "Real" "embedding" "transport" "directed"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🌉/渡"], :source-path "library/math-formalization/coercion-bridge.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Coercion Bridge Between Type Universes", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Bound polynomials or analytic functions on disks using Complex.norm_eq_abs + Metric.ball API."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "Goal involves bounding |f(z)| on a disk or circle in ℂ."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "Need to carefully distinguish open ball, closed ball, and frontier (sphere)."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Use Metric.ball/closedBall for the domain, Metric.frontier_ball for the boundary, Complex.norm_eq_abs for the norm."} {:name "LEAN", :name-key "lean", :slug "lean", :text "      api[Complex.norm_eq_abs] api[Metric.ball] api[Metric.closedBall] api[Polynomial.eval]\n      tactic[have → calc → mul_comm → classical]"} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "Mathlib's metric space API provides the topological scaffolding; the complex norm is just abs."}], :conclusion "  Bound polynomials or analytic functions on disks using Complex.norm_eq_abs + Metric.ball API.", :directives {:audience "lean-provers, analysis-students", :keywords ["complex" "polynomial" "norm" "ball" "closedBall" "frontier" "estimate"], :sigils ["⭕/球"], :title "Complex Polynomial Norm Estimates on Balls", :tone "heuristic"}, :id "math-formalization/complex-polynomial-bound", :keywords ["complex" "polynomial" "norm" "ball" "closedBall" "frontier" "estimate"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["⭕/球"], :source-path "library/math-formalization/complex-polynomial-bound.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Complex Polynomial Norm Estimates on Balls", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  When proving a union of connected sets sharing a common point is connected,\n  argue by contradiction via open separations."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You have a family of connected sets {C_α} and a common point\n    x ∈ ∩ C_α, and you need to show ⋃ C_α is connected."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      The goal involves IsConnected or IsPreconnected applied to a union,\n      and the hypothesis provides a common point in all components."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      \"Union of connected sets is connected\" is false in general — the\n      common point hypothesis is essential. Without it, disjoint connected\n      sets are a trivial counterexample."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "      Use isPreconnected_sUnion from Mathlib. The key step: rewrite the\n      indexed union as a set-indexed union via sUnion_range, then apply\n      the combinator with the common point and individual connectedness."} {:name "LEAN", :name-key "lean", :slug "lean", :text "      api[isPreconnected_sUnion]\n      api[Set.range]\n      api[Set.not_nonempty_iff]\n      tactic[classical → by_cases → simpa [sUnion_range]]"} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      Mathlib's isPreconnected_sUnion handles the separation argument\n      internally — you supply the common point and the per-component\n      connectedness, and it composes the contradiction."}], :conclusion "  When proving a union of connected sets sharing a common point is connected,\n  argue by contradiction via open separations.", :directives {:audience "lean-provers, topology-students", :factor "Determination (adhiṭṭhāna)", :keywords ["connected" "union" "common-point" "separation" "isPreconnected" "sUnion"], :sigils ["🔗/連"], :title "Connected Union via Common Point", :tone "heuristic"}, :id "math-formalization/connected-union-via-common-point", :keywords ["connected" "union" "common-point" "separation" "isPreconnected" "sUnion"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🔗/連"], :source-path "library/math-formalization/connected-union-via-common-point.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Connected Union via Common Point", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  When the mathematical insight is trivial but the Lean construction is\n  ~50+ lines of engineering, use witness templates rather than building\n  from scratch each time."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "The proof requires constructing an explicit mathematical\n    object (a family of functions, a counterexample, a partition of\n    unity). On paper, the construction is \"obvious\" — tent functions on\n    disjoint intervals, bump functions with prescribed support, etc.\n    In Lean, the construction requires defining the object, proving\n    continuity, verifying bounds, checking support properties, etc."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      Informal math: \"let f_n be tent functions with height 1/n\" (1 line).\n      Lean: define f_n, prove Continuous f_n, prove 0 ≤ f_n x, prove\n      support ⊆ I_n, prove sup f_n = 1/n (50+ lines). The asymmetry is\n      intrinsic — Lean demands proofs that paper elides. The amelioration\n      is not to make Lean easier but to make the engineering reusable."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      The goal is existential (∃ f, P f) or requires producing an\n      explicit mathematical object, and the object belongs to a common\n      family (piecewise-linear, bump, indicator, characteristic function)."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text ""} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      The Lean engineering cost for common constructions is high because\n      Mathlib provides the *pieces* (Continuous.piecewise, indicator,\n      Set.Icc) but not the *assembled objects* (tent function family on\n      disjoint intervals with prescribed heights). Each proof rebuilds\n      the assembly from scratch."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "      (a) Check if a witness template exists in ApmCanaries/Local/.\n      (b) If not, build the construction once, prove its properties\n          once, and add it to Local/ as a reusable module.\n      (c) Future proofs that need the same construction import it\n          rather than rebuilding.\n      (d) The template becomes a formalization pattern entry — the\n          construction IS the pattern."} {:name "LEAN", :name-key "lean", :slug "lean", :text "      api[Continuous.piecewise] api[Set.indicator] api[MeasurableSet.indicator]\n      tactic[refine ⟨fun n => ..., ?_, ?_, ?_⟩ → constructor → intro → simp]"} {:name "WITNESS-TEMPLATES", :name-key "witness-templates", :slug "witness-templates", :text "      template[tent function family on disjoint intervals]\n      template[bump function with prescribed support and integral]\n      template[partition of unity subordinate to a cover]\n      template[indicator function of a measurable set]\n      template[piecewise-linear interpolation]"} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      The mathematical insight (\"use tent functions\") is the easy part.\n      The Lean engineering (\"define tent, prove continuous, prove\n      nonneg, prove support disjoint, prove height\") is the hard part.\n      Amortizing the engineering across problems is the only way to\n      make the 15-minute budget realistic for construction-heavy proofs."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Identify which common family the witness belongs to.]\n    next[Check ApmCanaries/Local/ for existing templates.]\n    next[If missing, build once, prove once, add to Local/.]\n    next[Future proofs import and instantiate.]"}], :conclusion "  When the mathematical insight is trivial but the Lean construction is\n  ~50+ lines of engineering, use witness templates rather than building\n  from scratch each time.", :directives {:audience "lean-provers, analysis-students, topology-students", :keywords ["construction" "witness" "explicit" "piecewise" "engineering" "asymmetry" "trivial-hard"], :sigils ["🏗️/造"], :title "Construction Cost Asymmetry", :tone "heuristic"}, :id "math-formalization/construction-cost-asymmetry", :keywords ["construction" "witness" "explicit" "piecewise" "engineering" "asymmetry" "trivial-hard"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🏗️/造"], :source-path "library/math-formalization/construction-cost-asymmetry.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Construction Cost Asymmetry", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Prove properties of composed bounded operators via ContinuousLinearMap API."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "Goal involves composition of bounded linear operators."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "Operator composition in Lean is Module.End.mul, not func