with 1/n → 0.", :directives {:audience "lean-provers, analysis-students", :keywords ["rpow" "limit" "continuity" "exponent" "tendsto" "const_rpow"], :sigils ["🎯/極"], :title "Limit of t^{1/n} → 1 via Continuity", :tone "heuristic"}, :id "math-formalization/rpow-exponent-limit", :keywords ["rpow" "limit" "continuity" "exponent" "tendsto" "const_rpow"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🎯/極"], :source-path "library/math-formalization/rpow-exponent-limit.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Limit of t^{1/n} → 1 via Continuity", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Separate disjoint closed sets by d(x,A)/(d(x,A)+d(x,B)) with empty-set guard."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "Goal is a continuous [0,1]-valued function separating closed sets."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "The naive formula has a zero-denominator risk when sets are empty."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Guard the empty case (force distance=1), then use Metric.continuous_infDist_pt + Continuous.div."} {:name "LEAN", :name-key "lean", :slug "lean", :text "      api[Metric.continuous_infDist_pt] api[Metric.infDist_nonneg] api[Continuous.div]\n      tactic[have → exact → simp → ext → classical]"} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "Mathlib's infDist API handles the metric computation; you supply the nonvanishing denominator proof."}], :conclusion "  Separate disjoint closed sets by d(x,A)/(d(x,A)+d(x,B)) with empty-set guard.", :directives {:audience "lean-provers, topology-students", :keywords ["separation" "distance" "Urysohn" "continuous" "infDist" "division"], :sigils ["📏/距"], :title "Continuous Separation Function from Distance", :tone "heuristic"}, :id "math-formalization/separation-function-from-distance", :keywords ["separation" "distance" "Urysohn" "continuous" "infDist" "division"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["📏/距"], :source-path "library/math-formalization/separation-function-from-distance.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Continuous Separation Function from Distance", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  When an algebraic identity is correct but `rw` disrupts the goal's\n  type structure (filter, Tendsto, Set.mem), use `convert` or `simp_rw`\n  to rewrite within the structure rather than replacing the whole goal."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You know the algebraic identity (e.g., ⟨h,h⟩ = ‖h‖²) and\n    the goal is a Tendsto/Filter/Set statement that contains the LHS.\n    Applying `rw` replaces the LHS but may change the goal shape in ways\n    that break subsequent tactic application."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      Obligation A: rewrite the algebraic term (rw fires correctly).\n      Obligation B: preserve the goal's filter/Tendsto structure for\n      subsequent tactics. These conflict when `rw` changes the goal\n      shape. The constraint-tension-resolution pattern says: name both\n      obligations, find a tactic that satisfies both. `convert` and\n      `simp_rw` are the alternative constructions that dissolve the\n      tension."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      The goal has the form `Tendsto (fun n => f n) l (𝓝 target)` and\n      you want to rewrite something inside `f n` or `target`, but `rw`\n      at the top level changes the Tendsto structure."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text ""} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      `rw` is greedy — it replaces ALL occurrences, including ones inside\n      binders (fun n => ...) where the rewrite may not be valid or may\n      change the function identity. `simp_rw` rewrites under binders\n      safely. `convert` changes the goal and leaves the mismatch as a\n      subgoal. `show` can manually restate the goal in the desired form."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "      (a) If the rewrite is inside a binder (fun n => ...): use `simp_rw`.\n      (b) If the rewrite is in the target (𝓝 value): use `convert` to\n          replace the target and close the algebraic identity separately.\n      (c) If the rewrite is structural (changing Tendsto to Filter.Tendsto\n          or vice versa): use `show` to restate, then proceed.\n      (d) Last resort: break the Tendsto into pieces using `have` lemmas\n          for each convergent component, then reassemble with Tendsto\n          arithmetic (Tendsto.add, Tendsto.sub, Tendsto.mul)."} {:name "LEAN", :name-key "lean", :slug "lean", :text "      api[real_inner_self_eq_norm_sq] api[real_inner_self_eq_norm_mul_norm]\n      tactic[simp_rw [...] → convert ... using 1 → ring]\n      tactic[have h1 := ... → have h2 := ... → exact h1.sub h2 |>.add ...]"} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      The tension is between algebraic truth (the identity holds) and\n      tactic mechanics (the rewrite changes the goal shape). The informal\n      mathematician doesn't see this tension because \"substitute equals\n      for equals\" is seamless on paper. In Lean, the substitution must\n      respect the term structure, and `rw` doesn't always do what the\n      mathematician intends."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Determine if the rewrite is inside a binder, in the target, or structural.]\n    next[Choose simp_rw (binder), convert (target), or show (structural).]\n    next[If all fail, decompose into have lemmas and use Tendsto arithmetic.]"}], :conclusion "  When an algebraic identity is correct but `rw` disrupts the goal's\n  type structure (filter, Tendsto, Set.mem), use `convert` or `simp_rw`\n  to rewrite within the structure rather than replacing the whole goal.", :directives {:audience "lean-provers, analysis-students", :keywords ["rewrite" "rw" "convert" "simp" "Tendsto" "filter" "goal-shape" "interference"], :sigils ["⚡/衝"], :title "Tactic-Algebra Interference in Rewriting", :tone "heuristic"}, :id "math-formalization/tactic-algebra-interference", :keywords ["rewrite" "rw" "convert" "simp" "Tendsto" "filter" "goal-shape" "interference"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["⚡/衝"], :source-path "library/math-formalization/tactic-algebra-interference.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Tactic-Algebra Interference in Rewriting", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Assume the negation of what you want to prove and derive an absurdity."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You want to prove a statement P and no direct construction or chain of implications is apparent."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      The conclusion P is existential (\"there exists...\"), uniqueness (\"at most one...\"), or\n      impossibility (\"no X satisfies...\"), and direct proof routes are blocked."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      Proofs by contradiction can obscure the constructive content of a result, making it harder to\n      extract algorithms or explicit witnesses. Some mathematical communities prefer direct proofs\n      when available."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "      Assume not-P. Use the assumed structure to build or derive objects, then show these objects\n      violate a known fact. The contradiction forces P."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      Contradiction proofs gain traction by giving you an extra hypothesis (not-P) to work with.\n      Many fundamental results (irrationality of sqrt(2), uncountability of the reals, infinitude\n      of primes) were first proved this way."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Write down the precise negation of P.]\n    next[Identify what new objects or properties not-P gives you.]\n    next[Find the known fact that these objects violate.]"}], :conclusion "  Assume the negation of what you want to prove and derive an absurdity.", :directives {:audience "mathematicians, students, proof-writers", :factor "Determination (adhiṭṭhāna)", :keywords ["contradiction" "assume" "suppose-not" "absurd" "reductio" "negation" "impossible" "contrary"], :sigils ["🔺/今"], :title "Argue by Contradiction", :tone "heuristic"}, :id "math-informal/argue-by-contradiction", :keywords ["contradiction" "assume" "suppose-not" "absurd" "reductio" "negation" "impossible" "contrary"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🔺/今"], :source-path "library/math-informal/argue-by-contradiction.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Argue by Contradiction", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Test your conjecture at the boundaries: zero, infinity, the empty set, the trivial case."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You have a general statement and want to know if it is plausible before investing in a proof."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      The statement involves a parameter, a set, or a structure that can take degenerate or\n      extreme values."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      Extreme cases can be vacuously true or pathologically special, giving false confidence.\n      Passing extreme-case checks is necessary but not sufficient."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "      Instantiate the statement at every extreme: n=0, n=1, the empty set, the one-point space,\n      the trivial group, infinite dimension, the identity morphism. Check whether the statement\n      is true, vacuously true, or false. A failure at an extreme case is a cheap disproof."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      Extreme cases are the cheapest tests available. Many false conjectures die at n=0. Many\n      correct theorems reveal their essential content at the boundary."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[List all parameters and identify their extreme values.]\n    next[Evaluate the statement at each extreme.]\n    next[Record which extremes are informative vs vacuous.]"}], :conclusion "  Test your conjecture at the boundaries: zero, infinity, the empty set, the trivial case.", :directives {:audience "mathematicians, students, problem-solvers", :factor "Keen investigation (dhammavicaya)", :keywords ["boundary" "extreme" "degenerate" "limit" "edge-case" "zero" "infinity" "empty" "trivial" "vacuous"], :sigils ["🌀/习"], :title "Check the Extreme Cases", :tone "heuristic"}, :id "math-informal/check-the-extreme-cases", :keywords ["boundary" "extreme" "degenerate" "limit" "edge-case" "zero" "infinity" "empty" "trivial" "vacuous"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🌀/习"], :source-path "library/math-informal/check-the-extreme-cases.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Check the Extreme Cases", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Place a decision problem (or a class of problems) in a specific complexity\n  class — typically by exhibiting a reduction from a problem already in that\n  class, or by proving membership constructively."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "A computational problem P has been defined, and its complexity\n    is unknown or only roughly known. The paper's contribution is to classify\n    P at a known level of the complexity hierarchy: P, NP-complete, PSPACE,\n    decidable, undecidable, ETH-hard, and so on."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      You can construct a polynomial-time (or appropriate) reduction from a\n      complexity-class-complete problem to P, or vice versa, establishing\n      membership / hardness with a sharp boundary."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      Complexity-classification is *one specific subtype* of\n      `characterization-result` — its analogue in non-computational settings\n      is `classification-result` (e.g., classifying KMS states or AFD outer\n      actions). The reasoning shape is \"list-the-cases-and-show-exhaustion\"\n      but the deliverable is \"the problem belongs to class C\" rather than\n      \"the structure has these instances.\" Use this leaf only when the result\n      is genuinely complexity-theoretic."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "      1. Construct the reduction (typically polynomial-time many-one or\n         Turing) from a complexity-class-complete problem to P (for\n         hardness) or from P to a problem already in C (for membership).\n      2. Verify the reduction is correct and within the resource bounds for\n         the target class.\n      3. State the classification theorem: P is C-complete (or C-hard, or\n         in C, as appropriate)."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      Complexity classification is structurally a *one-shot characterization*\n      that places a problem at a precise location in a known hierarchy. It\n      depends on the existence of a complete problem for the target class\n      (Cook-Levin, etc.); reductions are the workhorse construction. The\n      family parent is characterization-result; this leaf is the\n      complexity-theoretic specialisation."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "      next[Identify the target complexity class and a complete / canonical problem in it.]\n      next[Construct the reduction; verify resource bounds.]\n      next[State the classification with the appropriate complete / hard / member language.]"}], :conclusion "  Place a decision problem (or a class of problems) in a specific complexity\n  class — typically by exhibiting a reduction from a problem already in that\n  class, or by proving membership constructively.", :directives {:audience "theoretical-CS, combinatorics, logic", :factor "Discernment (paññā)", :keywords ["complexity" "NP-complete" "hardness" "reduction" "computability" "classification"], :references ["math-informal/reduce-to-known-result" "math-informal/exhaustion-as-theorem" "math-informal/transport-across-isomorphism"], :sigils ["⊞/階"], :title "Complexity Classification", :tone "analytic"}, :id "math-informal/complexity-classification", :keywords ["complexity" "NP-complete" "hardness" "reduction" "computability" "classification"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["math-informal/reduce-to-known-result" "math-informal/exhaustion-as-theorem" "math-informal/transport-across-isomorphism"], :scores {}, :sigils ["⊞/階"], :source-path "library/math-informal/complexity-classification.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Complexity Classification", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  When you need to prove existence, build the object."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You need to show that an object with certain properties exists."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      The existence statement is concrete enough that you can describe what the object would look\n      like, and abstract existence arguments (Zorn's lemma, compactness, probabilistic method)\n      either do not apply or would leave you without a usable object."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      Explicit constructions can be intricate and may obscure *why* the object exists. Sometimes\n      a non-constructive proof reveals more about the landscape than a single explicit example."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "      Build the object step by step. Define it concretely. Verify each required property. If the\n      construction is complex, build intermediate objects and compose them."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      An explicit witness is the gold standard of existence proofs: it gives you something to\n      compute with, to test, and to modify. Many of mathematics' most valued results are\n      constructions (Cantor's diagonal, Weierstrass's nowhere-differentiable function, Vitali sets)."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[List the properties the witness must satisfy.]\n    next[Attempt to build an object satisfying them one at a time.]\n    next[If stuck, relax a property and see what you can build.]"}], :conclusion "  When you need to prove existence, build the object.", :directives {:audience "mathematicians, students, proof-writers", :factor "Persistence (viriya)", :keywords ["construct" "exhibit" "witness" "example" "counterexample" "explicit" "build" "produce" "existence"], :sigils ["🐜/予"], :title "Construct an Explicit Witness", :tone "heuristic"}, :id "math-informal/construct-an-explicit-witness", :keywords ["construct" "exhibit" "witness" "example" "counterexample" "explicit" "build" "produce" "existence"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🐜/予"], :source-path "library/math-informal/construct-an-explicit-witness.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Construct an Explicit Witness", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Introduce a purpose-built object that mediates between what you have and what you need."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You have a hypothesis and a conclusion, but no direct path between them."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      The gap between hypothesis and conclusion is structural: you need an intermediate\n      object (a function, a sequence, a set, a morphism) that does not appear in the\n      problem statement but whose existence would bridge the gap."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      Auxiliary objects can feel like magic when encountered for the first time. The skill\n      is in recognising which construction is needed: truncation, mollification, partition\n      of unity, Hahn-Banach extension, free object on generators, approximating sequence."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "      Identify the gap. Ask: what kind of object would connect my hypothesis to my\n      conclusion? Construct it explicitly. Verify it has the properties needed on both\n      sides. Common auxiliary objects include: truncated random variables, bump functions,\n      test morphisms, approximating sequences, and free resolutions."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      Many of the most important proofs in mathematics turn on a single clever auxiliary\n      construction. The Radon-Nikodym derivative, the partition of unity, the resolvent\n      operator — these are all auxiliary objects that became standard tools precisely\n      because they bridge common structural gaps."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Identify the structural gap between hypothesis and conclusion.]\n    next[Survey known auxiliary constructions in your domain.]\n    next[Build the object and verify it bridges the gap.]"}], :conclusion "  Introduce a purpose-built object that mediates between what you have and what you need.", :directives {:audience "mathematicians, researchers, proof-writers", :factor "Persistence (viriya)", :keywords ["auxiliary" "helper" "introduce" "define" "let" "set" "function" "sequence" "truncation" "mollifier"], :sigils ["🐜/予"], :title "Construct an Auxiliary Object", :tone "heuristic"}, :id "math-informal/construct-auxiliary-object", :keywords ["auxiliary" "helper" "introduce" "define" "let" "set" "function" "sequence" "truncation" "mollifier"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🐜/予"], :source-path "library/math-informal/construct-auxiliary-object.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Construct an Auxiliary Object", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Reverse the arrows, take complements, or transpose — the dual problem may be easier."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You have a problem stated in terms of maps, sets, or conditions, and direct approaches are stuck."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      The problem has a natural duality: a categorical opposite, a vector space dual, a Boolean\n      complement, a Fourier transform, a Lagrangian dual, or simply the contrapositive of a\n      logical statement."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      Not every problem dualises cleanly. Some dualities lose structure (the double dual may not\n      be isomorphic to the original). You must verify that the dual problem actually helps."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "      Reformulate the problem in the dual setting. Solve it there if easier. Translate the\n      solution back. For logical statements, try the contrapositive before attempting direct proof."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      Duality is pervasive: Pontryagin duality, Stone duality, linear programming duality,\n      Poincare duality, Serre duality. A problem that is opaque in one setting can become\n      transparent in its dual."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Identify which duality applies to your setting.]\n    next[Reformulate the problem in the dual language.]\n    next[Solve and translate back.]"}], :conclusion "  Reverse the arrows, take complements, or transpose — the dual problem may be easier.", :directives {:audience "mathematicians, researchers", :factor "Discernment (paññā)", :keywords ["dual" "opposite" "reverse" "contravariant" "adjoint" "transpose" "complement" "converse"], :sigils ["🐲/乃"], :title "Dualise the Problem", :tone "heuristic"}, :id "math-informal/dualise-the-problem", :keywords ["dual" "opposite" "reverse" "contravariant" "adjoint" "transpose" "complement" "converse"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🐲/乃"], :source-path "library/math-informal/dualise-the-problem.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Dualise the Problem", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Translate a combinatorial, geometric, or analytic problem into algebraic language and solve it there."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You have a problem in geometry, combinatorics, topology, or analysis that resists direct methods."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      The objects in your problem carry algebraic structure (composition, addition, multiplication)\n      that direct methods are not exploiting, or there is a known algebraic invariant that\n      captures the property you care about."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      The algebraic translation may lose geometric or combinatorial information. The algebra may\n      be harder to compute with than the original objects. Not every problem benefits from\n      algebraisation."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "      Assign algebraic objects to your problem's ingredients: groups to symmetries, rings to\n      coordinate systems, modules to representations, homology groups to topological spaces.\n      Reformulate the problem as an algebraic question. Solve using algebraic tools (exact\n      sequences, spectral sequences, Groebner bases, character theory)."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      Algebraic encoding has been one of the most successful strategies in mathematics:\n      Galois theory encodes field extensions as groups, algebraic topology encodes spaces as\n      groups, algebraic geometry encodes varieties as rings. The algebra often reveals structure\n      that was invisible in the original setting."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Identify what algebraic structure your objects carry.]\n    next[Choose the right algebraic invariant or encoding.]\n    next[Translate the problem and solve algebraically.]"}], :conclusion "  Translate a combinatorial, geometric, or analytic problem into algebraic language and solve it there.", :directives {:audience "mathematicians, researchers", :factor "Discernment (paññā)", :keywords ["algebra" "encode" "translate" "homomorphism" "representation" "structure" "ring" "module" "group-ring"], :sigils ["💬/才"], :title "Encode as Algebra", :tone "heuristic"}, :id "math-informal/encode-as-algebra", :keywords ["algebra" "encode" "translate" "homomorphism" "representation" "structure" "ring" "module" "group-ring"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["💬/才"], :source-path "library/math-informal/encode-as-algebra.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Encode as Algebra", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Replace a complicated quantity with a simpler bound that is good enough."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You need to control a quantity (an integral, a sum, a probability, a norm) but\n    exact computation is infeasible or unnecessary."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      You need an upper bound (or lower bound) rather than an exact value, and standard\n      inequalities (triangle, Cauchy-Schwarz, Jensen, Markov, Chebyshev, AM-GM) are\n      available."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      Bounds lose information. A bound that is too loose is useless. The art is in choosing\n      the tightest bound that remains tractable. Chaining too many inequalities can\n      accumulate slack to the point of triviality."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "      Identify which direction you need (upper or lower). Apply the most appropriate\n      standard inequality. Check that the resulting bound is tight enough for your\n      purposes. If not, try a different inequality or a more refined decomposition\n      before bounding."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      Estimation is the engine of analysis and probability. Most theorems in these fields\n      are proved by chains of inequalities, each replacing something complicated with\n      something simpler. Knowing which inequality to reach for is a core skill."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Determine whether you need an upper or lower bound.]\n    next[Identify candidate inequalities from your toolbox.]\n    next[Apply and check tightness — is the bound strong enough?]"}], :conclusion "  Replace a complicated quantity with a simpler bound that is good enough.", :directives {:audience "mathematicians, analysts, probabilists", :factor "Endurance (khanti)", :keywords ["bound" "estimate" "inequality" "upper" "lower" "dominate" "majorise" "triangle" "Cauchy-Schwarz" "Jensen"], :sigils ["🔸/小"], :title "Estimate by Bounding", :tone "heuristic"}, :id "math-informal/estimate-by-bounding", :keywords ["bound" "estimate" "inequality" "upper" "lower" "dominate" "majorise" "triangle" "Cauchy-Schwarz" "Jensen"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🔸/小"], :source-path "library/math-informal/estimate-by-bounding.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Estimate by Bounding", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  When multiple approaches to the same proof step all fail at the same quantitative barrier,\n  prove that the barrier is fundamental to the technique class, not an artifact of any\n  individual approach."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You have tried several methods for a proof step (concentration inequalities,\n    subsampling arguments, spectral bounds, etc.) and each gives a bound that is too weak\n    by the same qualitative amount. The failure pattern is suspiciously consistent."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      Three or more techniques from the same family (e.g., \"subsample and concentrate,\"\n      \"bound trace then convert to operator norm,\" \"linearize the quadratic term\") all\n      fail at the same quantitative step, producing bounds with the same scaling deficiency."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      Consistent failure might be coincidence rather than structure. Before declaring the\n      barrier fundamental, verify that the techniques genuinely span the family — not just\n      three variants of the same idea. Also check whether a technique outside the family\n      avoids the barrier entirely."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "        - Premature exhaustion claim: the family is not actually exhausted. Check that new\n          techniques outside the family avoid the barrier.\n        - Overgeneralization: \"no concentration argument can work\" when the ceiling only\n          applies to trace-based certification. Scope the barrier precisely."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "      1. Identify the common quantitative step where all techniques fail.\n      2. Formulate the barrier as a theorem: \"any technique in class C that proceeds via\n         step X produces a bound of at most f(n)\" where f(n) is weaker than the target.\n      3. Prove the ceiling theorem (often by exhibiting a worst case for step X).\n      4. Record the ceiling as a lemma. It eliminates the technique class and redirects\n         effort toward techniques outside the class."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      A proved ceiling theorem is qualitatively different from \"we tried 6 things and they\n      didn't work.\" It converts empirical failure into structural understanding: the barrier\n      is not a failure of ingenuity but a consequence of the technique class's structure.\n      This prevents future revisitation (\"maybe if we try the 7th variant...\") and focuses\n      effort on genuinely different approaches."} {:name "EVIDENCE", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "        P6 epsilon-light subsets (futon6, Feb 2026): 6 subsample-and-concentrate techniques\n        (trace/Markov, star domination, decoupling+MSS 1-shot, decoupling+MSS recursive,\n        greedy/chromatic, rank spreading) all hit the quadratic-vs-linear scaling wall:\n        spectral contribution scales as q² while set size scales as q. The trace-only ceiling\n        was proved as a lemma (Step 1), eliminating the entire technique class and redirecting\n        toward operator-norm-aware barrier greedy (a technique outside the class)."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[List all techniques tried and their failure points.]\n    next[Identify the common quantitative step.]\n    next[Prove the ceiling theorem for the technique class.]\n    next[Search for techniques outside the class that avoid the ceiling.]"}], :conclusion "  When multiple approaches to the same proof step all fail at the same quantitative barrier,\n  prove that the barrier is fundamental to the technique class, not an artifact of any\n  individual approach.", :directives {:audience "mathematicians, proof engineers, optimization theorists", :factor "Discernment (paññā)", :keywords ["exhaust" "technique" "class" "wall" "barrier" "fundamental" "ceiling" "quadratic" "linear" "scaling" "sublinear" "impossible" "family"], :references ["math-informal/structural-obstruction-as-theorem" "math-informal/split-into-cases"], :sigils ["🧱/艮"], :title "Exhaustion as Theorem", :tone "analytic"}, :id "math-informal/exhaustion-as-theorem", :keywords ["exhaust" "technique" "class" "wall" "barrier" "fundamental" "ceiling" "quadratic" "linear" "scaling" "sublinear" "impossible" "family"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["math-informal/structural-obstruction-as-theorem" "math-informal/split-into-cases"], :scores {}, :sigils ["🧱/艮"], :source-path "library/math-informal/exhaustion-as-theorem.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Exhaustion as Theorem", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  When a problem has symmetry, use it to reduce the work."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are working on a problem where some transformation preserves the relevant structure."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      The problem is invariant under a group action, permutation, rotation, translation, or\n      relabelling, and you are doing redundant work on equivalent configurations."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      Not all apparent symmetries are real: a function may look symmetric but have hidden asymmetries.\n      And exploiting symmetry requires proving the symmetry exists, which itself may be non-trivial."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "      Identify the symmetry group. Use it to reduce to a canonical representative (\"without loss of\n      generality\"). Count orbits instead of elements. Decompose representations into irreducibles.\n      Apply fixed-point theorems when the action has no escape."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      Symmetry arguments can reduce exponential case analysis to a single case. The \"WLOG\" move\n      is one of the most powerful sentence fragments in mathematics."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Identify what transformations preserve the problem.]\n    next[Choose a canonical representative for each orbit.]\n    next[Reformulate the problem in terms of orbits or invariants.]"}], :conclusion "  When a problem has symmetry, use it to reduce the work.", :directives {:audience "mathematicians, researchers, problem-solvers", :factor "Discernment (paññā)", :keywords ["symmetry" "invariant" "group-action" "orbit" "fixed-point" "equivariant" "automorphism" "WLOG"], :sigils ["👉/田"], :title "Exploit Symmetry", :tone "heuristic"}, :id "math-informal/exploit-symmetry", :keywords ["symmetry" "invariant" "group-action" "orbit" "fixed-point" "equivariant" "automorphism" "WLOG"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["👉/田"], :source-path "library/math-informal/exploit-symmetry.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Exploit Symmetry", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Characterize the regime where a known result FAILS, rather than only\n  characterizing where it holds."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "A theorem T is known under hypotheses H. It is natural to ask\n    whether T extends to a broader class. Rather than producing yet another\n    sufficient condition, the paper instead pins down the *boundary of\n    applicability*: it identifies exactly the regime where T is no longer\n    valid, often with quantitative or structural sharpness."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      You have a theorem T with hypotheses H and you want to articulate the\n      *failure regime* — typically (a) examples that violate T outside H,\n      (b) a structural reason the failure occurs, (c) a sharp boundary\n      between holds-here and fails-here."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      Failure-mode characterization is structurally distinct from a *negative\n      result* (T is false in this case). A negative result asserts something\n      doesn't hold; failure-mode-characterization specifies the *regime* where\n      it doesn't hold and the *structural reason* it doesn't. The latter is\n      stronger — it tells the reader where the theorem still works, where it\n      fails, and *why*."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "      1. Construct an explicit counterexample or family of counterexamples\n         that lie just outside H.\n      2. Identify the structural feature of the counterexamples that makes\n         T fail — the \"failure mechanism.\"\n      3. State the boundary as a theorem: \"T fails iff [structural\n         condition].\"\n      4. Optional: re-prove T with a refined hypothesis H' that excludes\n         the failure regime."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      Failure-mode characterization is information-dense: it tells the\n      community both where to apply T and where to stop. It also exposes\n      the structural feature that drives validity, which often illuminates\n      analogous theorems in adjacent settings. This pattern is closely\n      related to `structural-obstruction-as-theorem` but distinct: that\n      pattern is about a *method* failing on a problem, this pattern is\n      about a *theorem* failing in a regime."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "      next[Construct an explicit counterexample and verify it fails T.]\n      next[Identify the structural feature that drives the failure.]\n      next[State the boundary theorem precisely; check it is sharp.]"}], :conclusion "  Characterize the regime where a known result FAILS, rather than only\n  characterizing where it holds.", :directives {:audience "mathematicians, applied-analysts", :factor "Discernment (paññā)", :keywords ["failure" "regime" "boundary" "where-fails" "breakdown" "applicability" "edge-case"], :references ["math-informal/structural-obstruction-as-theorem" "math-informal/check-the-extreme-cases" "math-informal/argue-by-contradiction"], :sigils ["⚠/隅"], :title "Failure-Mode Characterization", :tone "analytic"}, :id "math-informal/failure-mode-characterization", :keywords ["failure" "regime" "boundary" "where-fails" "breakdown" "applicability" "edge-case"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["math-informal/structural-obstruction-as-theorem" "math-informal/check-the-extreme-cases" "math-informal/argue-by-contradiction"], :scores {}, :sigils ["⚠/隅"], :source-path "library/math-informal/failure-mode-characterization.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Failure-Mode Characterization", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  When a proof is ugly or a pattern recurs, seek a higher-level framework where it becomes natural."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You have a proof that works but feels ad hoc, or you see the same argument repeated in several settings."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      The proof involves case splits, coordinate choices, or auxiliary constructions that seem to\n      serve no conceptual purpose, or the same lemma keeps reappearing in different guises across\n      different domains."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      Premature abstraction obscures rather than clarifies: abstracting before you have enough\n      examples risks building a framework that fits nothing well. The right abstraction is found,\n      not prescribed."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "      Identify the minimal structural assumptions your argument actually uses. Reformulate in terms\n      of those assumptions alone. If a known framework (group, category, topological space, measure\n      space) fits, use it; if not, you may have discovered a new one."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      The right abstraction turns a difficult proof into a trivial one. Much of 20th-century\n      mathematics consisted of finding the right level of generality (e.g., Grothendieck's schemes,\n      Eilenberg-Mac Lane's categories)."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[List the properties your proof actually uses.]\n    next[Search for existing frameworks that axiomatise those properties.]\n    next[Reformulate and check whether the proof simplifies.]"}], :conclusion "  When a proof is ugly or a pattern recurs, seek a higher-level framework where it becomes natural.", :directives {:audience "mathematicians, researchers", :factor "Discernment (paññā)", :keywords ["abstract" "generalise" "reformulate" "natural" "category" "functor" "structure-preserving" "level" "framework"], :sigils ["💬/才"], :title "Find the Right Abstraction", :tone "heuristic"}, :id "math-informal/find-the-right-abstraction", :keywords ["abstract" "generalise" "reformulate" "natural" "category" "functor" "structure-preserving" "level" "framework"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["💬/才"], :source-path "library/math-informal/find-the-right-abstraction.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Find the Right Abstraction", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  When a pure algebraic method is too slow and a pure numerical method isn't rigorous,\n  combine them: use numerical computation to locate, exact arithmetic to certify."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You need a rigorous result about a polynomial or algebraic system (root count,\n    sign determination, bound verification) but the standard exact methods (Sturm sequences,\n    Groebner bases, cylindrical algebraic decomposition) are computationally infeasible for\n    the problem size, while purely numerical methods lack rigour."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      The exact method's bottleneck is a specific subroutine (polynomial GCD, resultant,\n      matrix factorization) that scales badly with coefficient size or degree, but the\n      qualitative structure of the answer (number of roots, sign pattern) can be determined\n      cheaply by approximate computation."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      The hybrid method must be provably correct. The numerical step identifies candidates;\n      the exact step certifies them. If the certification step fails (e.g., the approximate\n      root is too far from the true root for sign determination), the method must detect this\n      and fall back rather than produce a wrong answer."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "        - Double roots: if two roots are very close, the numerical step may merge them and the\n          sign-change count will be wrong. Mitigation: compare numerical root count with exact\n          Sturm count on a COARSE interval first.\n        - Coefficient explosion: the exact evaluation at a rational test point may involve very\n          large integers. Mitigation: choose simple test points (small-denominator rationals).\n        - False separation: numerical noise places a test point on the wrong side of a root.\n          Mitigation: use exact arithmetic for the test point evaluation, not floating-point."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "      1. Use fast numerical computation (numpy, LAPACK, floating-point) to compute approximate\n         answers: root locations, eigenvalues, sign patterns.\n      2. Design an exact verification step that checks the numerical answer: evaluate the\n         polynomial at rational test points, verify sign conditions, count roots by IVT.\n      3. The exact step should be O(n) in the problem size (not O(n³) like the numerical step),\n         since it's certifying known answers, not discovering them.\n      4. If the exact step disagrees with the numerical step, investigate rather than trust either."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      Exact symbolic computation has enormous constant factors (arbitrary-precision integer\n      arithmetic, polynomial GCD over Z[x] with 100+ digit coefficients). Numerical computation\n      is fast but approximate. The hybrid avoids the worst of both: fast location + cheap\n      certification = rigorous result at numerical speed."} {:name "EVIDENCE", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "        P4 n=4 Case 2 (futon6, Feb 2026): Sturm's theorem on a degree-70 polynomial with\n        100+ digit integer coefficients ran for 4 hours without completing (polynomial GCD\n        bottleneck). The hybrid method: numpy.roots for approximate root locations (O(n³),\n        milliseconds), then exact sympy.Rational evaluation at midpoints between consecutive\n        roots (O(n) evaluations, 0.2s total). Same rigour as Sturm. 72,000x speedup."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Identify the bottleneck in the exact method.]\n    next[Design a numerical locator + exact certifier pipeline.]\n    next[Validate: compare hybrid result with exact method on a smaller instance.]"}], :conclusion "  When a pure algebraic method is too slow and a pure numerical method isn't rigorous,\n  combine them: use numerical computation to locate, exact arithmetic to certify.", :directives {:audience "computational algebraists, proof engineers, CAS users", :factor "Keen investigation (dhammavicaya)", :keywords ["hybrid" "numerical" "exact" "certification" "roots" "sign" "Sturm" "interval" "rigorous" "fast"], :references ["math-informal/estimate-by-bounding" "math-informal/numerical-scout"], :sigils ["⚡/兌"], :title "Hybrid Certification", :tone "analytic"}, :id "math-informal/hybrid-certification", :keywords ["hybrid" "numerical" "exact" "certification" "roots" "sign" "Sturm" "interval" "rigorous" "fast"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["math-informal/estimate-by-bounding" "math-informal/numerical-scout"], :scores {}, :sigils ["⚡/兌"], :source-path "library/math-informal/hybrid-certification.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Hybrid Certification", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  When objects are indexed by a well-ordered set, prove the base case and the step."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You want to prove a statement P(n) for all n in some well-ordered domain\n    (natural numbers, ordinals, well-founded trees)."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      The statement involves a natural number, a finite structure with a size parameter, or more\n      generally any well-ordered or well-founded index."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      The inductive step can be harder than it looks — it requires proving P(n+1) from P(n)\n      (or from all P(k) with k<n for strong induction). Choosing the wrong induction variable\n      makes the step impossible even when the theorem is true."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "      Choose the right quantity to induct on. Verify the base case. For the inductive step,\n      assume P holds for smaller values and show it holds for the current value. Consider strong\n      induction if simple induction is too weak."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      Induction is the fundamental proof method for discrete mathematics and much of algebra.\n      Choosing the right induction variable is often the key creative step."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Identify the right quantity to induct on.]\n    next[Verify the base case.]\n    next[Prove the inductive step, being explicit about where the hypothesis is used.]"}], :conclusion "  When objects are indexed by a well-ordered set, prove the base case and the step.", :directives {:audience "mathematicians, students, proof-writers", :factor "Determination (adhiṭṭhāna)", :keywords ["induction" "base-case" "inductive-step" "strong-induction" "transfinite" "well-order" "minimal" "descent"], :sigils ["🔺/今"], :title "Induction and Well-Ordering", :tone "heuristic"}, :id "math-informal/induction-and-well-ordering", :keywords ["induction" "base-case" "inductive-step" "strong-induction" "transfinite" "well-order" "minimal" "descent"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🔺/今"], :source-path "library/math-informal/induction-and-well-ordering.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Induction and Well-Ordering", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Prove something locally (on small pieces) and then assemble the local results into a global one."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You want to establish a property of a large or complicated object."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      The object admits a decomposition into simpler pieces (open cover, partition, filtration,\n      localisation at primes), and the property you want can be checked on pieces."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      Not every property is local. You must verify that local data can be patched: the pieces\n      must be compatible on overlaps, and the patching procedure must not introduce new problems."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "      Cover the object with pieces where the property is manageable. Prove it on each piece.\n      Check compatibility on overlaps. Glue the local results into a global one, using\n      partitions of unity, sheaf conditions, or patching lemmas as appropriate."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      Local-to-global is one of the deepest recurring themes in mathematics: sheaf theory,\n      Mayer-Vietoris, local-global principles in number theory, partitions of unity in\n      differential geometry. The power comes from reducing a hard global problem to many\n      easy local ones."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Decompose the object into local pieces.]\n    next[Verify the property on each piece.]\n    next[Check compatibility and assemble the global result.]"}], :conclusion "  Prove something locally (on small pieces) and then assemble the local results into a global one.", :directives {:audience "mathematicians, researchers", :factor "Discernment (paññā)", :keywords ["local" "global" "sheaf" "patching" "gluing" "cover" "partition-of-unity" "extension" "localisation"], :sigils ["👉/田"], :title "Local-to-Global Argument", :tone "heuristic"}, :id "math-informal/local-to-global", :keywords ["local" "global" "sheaf" "patching" "gluing" "cover" "partition-of-unity" "extension" "localisation"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["👉/田"], :source-path "library/math-informal/local-to-global.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Local-to-Global Argument", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Approximate a complicated object by a monotone sequence of simpler objects and pass to the limit."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You want to prove a result for a general object (general measurable function,\n    arbitrary open set, general measure) but your tools only work for simple ones."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      Your general object can be approximated from below (or above) by a monotone sequence of\n      simpler objects, and the property you care about is preserved under monotone limits."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      Not all approximations are monotone, and not all properties survive limits. You must\n      choose the right notion of approximation and verify that the limit argument is valid\n      (monotone convergence theorem, continuity from below, directed completeness)."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "      Construct a monotone sequence of simple objects converging to your target. Prove the\n      result for each element of the sequence. Apply a monotone convergence or limit theorem\n      to conclude for the general object."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      This is the fundamental technique of measure theory and integration. Simple functions\n      approximate measurable functions, compact sets approximate open sets, finite sums\n      approximate series. The monotone convergence theorem makes the passage rigorous."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Construct the monotone approximating sequence.]\n    next[Prove the result for each element of the sequence.]\n    next[Identify and apply the appropriate limit theorem.]"}], :conclusion "  Approximate a complicated object by a monotone sequence of simpler objects and pass to the limit.", :directives {:audience "mathematicians, analysts, probabilists", :factor "Endurance (khanti)", :keywords ["approximate" "monotone" "sequence" "increasing" "decreasing" "limit" "converge" "truncate" "smooth"], :sigils ["🌀/习"], :title "Monotone Approximation", :tone "heuristic"}, :id "math-informal/monotone-approximation", :keywords ["approximate" "monotone" "sequence" "increasing" "decreasing" "limit" "converge" "truncate" "smooth"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🌀/习"], :source-path "library/math-informal/monotone-approximation.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Monotone Approximation", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Before attempting an algebraic or formal proof, map the problem landscape numerically\n  to identify the structure that the proof must handle."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You want to prove a statement about a continuous or polynomial function on a\n    domain (e.g., positivity, uniqueness of extrema, critical point enumeration). The\n    function has enough variables that the proof strategy is not obvious."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      The domain is bounded, the function is computable, and there are multiple plausible\n      proof strategies (SOS, interval arithmetic, elimination, Groebner, etc.), but you\n      don't know which will work."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      Numerical evidence does not constitute proof. Floating-point computation can miss\n      critical points near boundaries, produce false precision, and confirm true conjectures\n      whose proofs require entirely different techniques. The scout must inform the proof\n      strategy, not replace it."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "        - Scout finds zero violations → prover assumes the \"obvious\" approach works → but\n          the proof technique fails for structural reasons unrelated to the landscape\n          (e.g., SOS blocked by interior zeros). The scout informs WHAT to prove, not HOW.\n        - Scout misses a critical point in a thin region → prover claims exhaustive enumeration\n          → proof is incomplete. Mitigate: multiple independent searches, boundary-focused\n          sampling, increase start count."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "      1. Grid evaluation: compute the function on a uniform grid over the domain. Record the\n         minimum, maximum, and any near-zero values.\n      2. Critical point search: run multiple independent local optimization searches (L-BFGS,\n         differential evolution) from random starting points. Use at least 3 independent\n         searches with 1000+ starts each.\n      3. Domain boundary survey: evaluate on constraint boundaries (where disc=0, f=0, etc.).\n      4. Symmetry detection: check whether critical points cluster along symmetry subspaces.\n      5. Case structure identification: from the critical point map, identify natural\n         subcases ordered by symmetry/difficulty."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      The scout reveals what the proof must handle: how many critical points, where they\n      are, what the minimum margin is, which subspaces are trivial and which are hard. This\n      guides the choice of proof technique. A proof strategy designed without scouting is\n      guessing; one designed after scouting is engineering."} {:name "EVIDENCE", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "        P4 n=4 Stam inequality proof (futon6, Feb 2026): 4 numerical scripts with 11,000+\n        optimization starts mapped 12 critical points in 4D. This map identified the 5-case\n        decomposition (Cases 1, 2, 3a, 3b, 3c by symmetry subspace) that led to algebraic\n        proofs for Cases 1-3b. Without scouting, the case decomposition would have been\n        unmotivated."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Run grid evaluation + critical point search on the domain.]\n    next[Identify symmetry subspaces from the critical point map.]\n    next[Design the proof strategy based on the landscape, not the other way around.]"}], :conclusion "  Before attempting an algebraic or formal proof, map the problem landscape numerically\n  to identify the structure that the proof must handle.", :directives {:audience "mathematicians, computational algebraists, proof engineers", :factor "Keen investigation (dhammavicaya)", :keywords ["numerical" "landscape" "critical-point" "search" "scout" "map" "verify" "grid" "Monte-Carlo" "domain"], :references ["math-informal/work-examples-first" "math-informal/split-into-cases"], :sigils ["🐴/離"], :title "Numerical Scout", :tone "heuristic"}, :id "math-informal/numerical-scout", :keywords ["numerical" "landscape" "critical-point" "search" "scout" "map" "verify" "grid" "Monte-Carlo" "domain"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["math-informal/work-examples-first" "math-informal/split-into-cases"], :scores {}, :sigils ["🐴/離"], :source-path "library/math-informal/numerical-scout.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Numerical Scout", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  When a bound depends on a free parameter, choose the parameter value that gives the best result."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You have established a bound or inequality that contains a free parameter\n    (epsilon, delta, t, lambda) and want the sharpest possible conclusion."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      The bound is valid for all (or a range of) parameter values, and different choices\n      yield different strengths of conclusion."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      The optimisation may be analytically intractable. In that case, a sub-optimal but\n      explicit choice (often guided by dimensional analysis or balancing terms) is better\n      than an opaque optimal one."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "      Differentiate (or otherwise optimise) with respect to the free parameter. If the\n      optimum has a clean closed form, use it. If not, choose a value that balances the\n      dominant terms — this typically gives the right order of magnitude."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      This pattern is the workhorse of concentration inequalities, moment methods, and\n      approximation theory. The Chernoff bound, Bernstein inequalities, and Laplace method\n      all follow by introducing an exponential parameter and optimising."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Identify the free parameter and its valid range.]\n    next[Optimise or balance terms.]\n    next[Substitute back and simplify the resulting bound.]"}], :conclusion "  When a bound depends on a free parameter, choose the parameter value that gives the best result.", :directives {:audience "mathematicians, analysts, probabilists", :factor "Discernment (paññā)", :keywords ["optimise" "parameter" "choose" "best" "minimise" "maximise" "sup" "inf" "epsilon" "delta" "t"], :sigils ["🔸/小"], :title "Optimise a Free Parameter", :tone "heuristic"}, :id "math-informal/optimise-a-free-parameter", :keywords ["optimise" "parameter" "choose" "best" "minimise" "maximise" "sup" "inf" "epsilon" "delta" "t"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🔸/小"], :source-path "library/math-informal/optimise-a-free-parameter.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Optimise a Free Parameter", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  When two parts of a proof have conflicting requirements on a parameter, recognize the\n  tension as a structural constraint and seek a construction that dissolves it by changing\n  the underlying geometric or algebraic setup."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Your proof has two logically independent obligations. Each constrains a shared\n    parameter (dimension, degree, codimension, rank, etc.) in conflicting directions. The\n    natural construction satisfies one obligation perfectly but creates headwinds for the\n    other."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      Obligation A requires parameter p to have property X (e.g., n even), while\n      obligation B benefits from property Y (e.g., n odd), and X and Y are incompatible.\n      The conflict is not a matter of insufficient technique but of the parameter itself."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      Not every difficulty is a tension. If obligation B merely \"prefers\" Y but can be\n      completed with X, the tension is aesthetic, not structural. Only apply this pattern\n      when the conflict genuinely blocks or severely impedes completion. Also: the\n      alternative construction that dissolves the tension must exist — don't assume it does\n      without checking."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "        - The alternative construction may not exist (the tension may be intrinsic to the\n          problem, not just the approach).\n        - The alternative may dissolve one tension but create another. Map all obligations\n          before committing."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "      1. State the tension precisely: which obligations conflict on which parameter.\n      2. Ask: is there a different construction where both obligations want the same\n         parameter value? This often means replacing the initial geometric or algebraic\n         object (reflection → rotation, codim-1 → codim-2, etc.).\n      3. Check that the new construction still satisfies both obligations (it may make\n         the previously-easy obligation slightly harder).\n      4. If the tension dissolves, record the resolution as a structural insight."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      Parametric tensions are theorems about the approach, not the problem. They reveal that\n      the initial construction, however natural, is suboptimal for the full proof. Dissolving\n      the tension often simplifies the entire proof architecture by removing structural\n      headwinds that would otherwise require heavy machinery to overcome."} {:name "EVIDENCE", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "        P7 lattices with 2-torsion (futon6, Feb 2026): E2 obligation (place Γ in FH(Q))\n        requires even n (so fixed set has odd dimension and χ = 0 by topology). S obligation\n        (upgrade to manifold) benefits from odd n (favorable L-theory parity, equivariant\n        surgery available). Tension is structural for the reflection construction. Resolution:\n        replace codimension-1 reflection with codimension-2 rotation in odd dimension. Both\n        obligations then want odd n. The rotation route dissolves the tension and opens two\n        parallel S-branch options (Wall surgery with favorable parity, or equivariant surgery\n        with satisfied gap hypothesis)."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Identify which obligations conflict on which parameters.]\n    next[Search for alternative constructions where the conflict dissolves.]\n    next[Verify the alternative satisfies all obligations, even if less cleanly.]"}], :conclusion "  When two parts of a proof have conflicting requirements on a parameter, recognize the\n  tension as a structural constraint and seek a construction that dissolves it by changing\n  the underlying geometric or algebraic setup.", :directives {:audience "mathematicians, topologists, proof architects", :factor "Discernment (paññā)", :keywords ["tension" "conflict" "parameter" "parity" "dimension" "codimension" "construction" "dissolve" "structural" "constraint" "even" "odd"], :references ["math-informal/split-into-cases" "math-informal/find-the-right-abstraction"], :sigils ["🔀/巽"], :title "Parametric Tension Dissolution", :tone "analytic"}, :id "math-informal/parametric-tension-dissolution", :keywords ["tension" "conflict" "parameter" "parity" "dimension" "codimension" "construction" "dissolve" "structural" "constraint" "even" "odd"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["math-informal/split-into-cases" "math-informal/find-the-right-abstraction"], :scores {}, :sigils ["🔀/巽"], :source-path "library/math-informal/parametric-tension-dissolution.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Parametric Tension Dissolution", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  When a sequence does not converge, extract a convergent subsequence and argue from there."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You need convergence or a limit, but the full sequence or net may not cooperate."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      You are working in a compact space (or a setting with a compactness hypothesis), and the\n      sequence or net is bounded but not necessarily convergent."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      The subsequence may converge to something unwanted. You must verify that the limit has\n      the properties you need, and that the argument does not depend on the specific subsequence\n      chosen."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "      Invoke compactness (Bolzano-Weierstrass, Alaoglu, Tychonoff, sequential compactness) to\n      extract a convergent subsequence. Pass to it. Argue that the limit has the desired property.\n      If necessary, show the limit is unique so the full sequence converges."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      Compactness arguments are the engine of analysis. \"Pass to a subsequence\" converts a\n      possibly wild sequence into a tame one at the cost of losing control over indices — a\n      trade-off that is almost always worth making."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Verify the compactness hypothesis that justifies extraction.]\n    next[Extract the convergent subsequence and identify the limit.]\n    next[Check that the limit has the properties you need.]"}], :conclusion "  When a sequence does not converge, extract a convergent subsequence and argue from there.", :directives {:audience "mathematicians, analysts, topologists", :factor "Endurance (khanti)", :keywords ["subsequence" "subnet" "extract" "compactness" "convergent" "limit" "accumulation" "Bolzano-Weierstrass"], :sigils ["🌀/习"], :title "Pass to a Subsequence or Subnet", :tone "heuristic"}, :id "math-informal/pass-to-a-subsequence", :keywords ["subsequence" "subnet" "extract" "compactness" "convergent" "limit" "accumulation" "Bolzano-Weierstrass"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🌀/习"], :source-path "library/math-informal/pass-to-a-subsequence.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Pass to a Subsequence or Subnet", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Identify what does not matter for your problem and collapse it away."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are working with objects that carry more structure than your problem needs."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      Different-looking objects behave identically for your purposes, and keeping track of the\n      differences creates bookkeeping overhead or case splits."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      Quotienting loses information. You must verify that the operations and properties you care\n      about are well-defined on equivalence classes, not just on representatives."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "      Define an equivalence relation that identifies objects agreeing on what matters. Pass to the\n      quotient. Verify well-definedness. Work in the quotient where the irrelevant distinctions\n      are gone."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      Quotient constructions are one of the most universal moves in mathematics: integers from\n      naturals, rationals from integers, homology from chains, projective space from vector space.\n      Recognising when to quotient is recognising what the problem is really about."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Identify which distinctions between your objects are irrelevant.]\n    next[Define the equivalence relation precisely.]\n    next[Check that your operations respect the equivalence.]"}], :conclusion "  Identify what does not matter for your problem and collapse it away.", :directives {:audience "mathematicians, researchers", :factor "Renunciation (nekkhamma)", :keywords ["quotient" "equivalence" "identify" "collapse" "mod-out" "canonical" "well-defined" "representative"], :sigils ["🙅/无"], :title "Quotient by Irrelevance", :tone "heuristic"}, :id "math-informal/quotient-by-irrelevance", :keywords ["quotient" "equivalence" "identify" "collapse" "mod-out" "canonical" "well-defined" "representative"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🙅/无"], :source-path "library/math-informal/quotient-by-irrelevance.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Quotient by Irrelevance", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Transform your problem until it matches the hypothesis of a known theorem, then invoke it."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You want to prove a result and suspect it follows from something already established."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      The conclusion you need resembles the conclusion of a known theorem, but the hypotheses\n      do not quite match your setup."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      Forcing a reduction to the wrong theorem produces fragile, unenlightening proofs. The\n      reduction step must be natural, not a contortion."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "      Identify the candidate theorem. Compare its hypotheses to your situation. Bridge the gap:\n      verify missing hypotheses, apply a change of variables, restrict to a subcase, or\n      reformulate to match. Then invoke the theorem."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      Mathematics is cumulative. The power of a proof is proportional to the depth of the results\n      it can invoke. \"By Theorem X\" is the most efficient move in mathematics when X genuinely\n      applies."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Identify which known result has the conclusion you need.]\n    next[Compare hypotheses: what is missing or different?]\n    next[Bridge the gap or choose a different target theorem.]"}], :conclusion "  Transform your problem until it matches the hypothesis of a known theorem, then invoke it.", :directives {:audience "mathematicians, students, proof-writers", :factor "Discernment (paññā)", :keywords ["reduce" "apply" "invoke" "known" "theorem" "lemma" "standard" "classical" "well-known" "by"], :sigils ["🗝️/门"], :title "Reduce to a Known Result", :tone "heuristic"}, :id "math-informal/reduce-to-known-result", :keywords ["reduce" "apply" "invoke" "known" "theorem" "lemma" "standard" "classical" "well-known" "by"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🗝️/门"], :source-path "library/math-informal/reduce-to-known-result.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Reduce to a Known Result", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  To prove equality, prove inequality in both directions."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You want to show A = B (where A, B are numbers, sets, or objects in an ordered structure)\n    and no direct computation yields both sides at once."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      Direct computation of A or B is infeasible, but you can bound A from above by B and\n      from below by B (or equivalently, show A is a subset of B and B is a subset of A)."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      The two directions may require completely different techniques. One direction is often\n      trivial while the other requires the bulk of the work. Identifying which is which\n      saves effort."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "      Prove A <= B and B <= A separately (or A is a subset of B and B is a subset of A).\n      Combine to conclude A = B. For the squeeze/sandwich variant: bound A from above and\n      below by quantities that both converge to the same limit."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      This is one of the most common proof structures in analysis (sandwich theorem, integral\n      bounds), set theory (Cantor-Bernstein), and algebra (showing ideals or subgroups are\n      equal). It reduces equality to two often-simpler one-directional claims."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Determine which direction is easier.]\n    next[Prove the easy direction first to build intuition.]\n    next[Prove the hard direction.]"}], :conclusion "  To prove equality, prove inequality in both directions.", :directives {:audience "mathematicians, students, proof-writers", :factor "Determination (adhiṭṭhāna)", :keywords ["equality" "sandwich" "squeeze" "both-directions" "inclusion" "subset" "superset" "le" "ge"], :sigils ["🔺/今"], :title "Show Both Inequalities", :tone "heuristic"}, :id "math-informal/show-both-inequalities", :keywords ["equality" "sandwich" "squeeze" "both-directions" "inclusion" "subset" "superset" "le" "ge"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🔺/今"], :source-path "library/math-informal/show-both-inequalities.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Show Both Inequalities", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  When a single argument cannot handle all situations, partition into cases and handle each one."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are trying to prove a statement that behaves differently depending on some\n    condition or parameter value."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      The objects or hypotheses fall into a finite number of qualitatively different types,\n      and the proof technique that works for one type fails for another."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      Too many cases make a proof unreadable and suggest you are missing a unifying idea.\n      If you find yourself with more than 3-4 cases, consider whether a different approach\n      (symmetry, abstraction, contradiction) would eliminate the case split entirely."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "      Identify the exhaustive partition. Prove the result separately for each case. Verify\n      the cases are exhaustive (no gaps) and handle any overlap. Common splits: positive/\n      negative/zero, finite/infinite, convergent/divergent, trivial/non-trivial."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      Case analysis is often the simplest proof strategy available, even if not the most\n      elegant. Many results in combinatorics, number theory, and real analysis require it\n      because the underlying structure genuinely behaves differently in different regimes."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Identify the natural partition of your domain or hypothesis.]\n    next[Verify the cases are exhaustive.]\n    next[Prove each case, noting where the proof differs.]"}], :conclusion "  When a single argument cannot handle all situations, partition into cases and handle each one.", :directives {:audience "mathematicians, students, proof-writers", :factor "Keen investigation (dhammavicaya)", :keywords ["cases" "split" "decompose" "partition" "dichotomy" "trichotomy" "either" "or" "exhaust"], :sigils ["👉/田"], :title "Split into Cases", :tone "heuristic"}, :id "math-informal/split-into-cases", :keywords ["cases" "split" "decompose" "partition" "dichotomy" "trichotomy" "either" "or" "exhaust"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["👉/田"], :source-path "library/math-informal/split-into-cases.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Split into Cases", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Give a complete intrinsic description of an object — what it is, in terms of\n  simpler structures or invariants — rather than asserting properties about\n  it."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "An object (an algebra, a representation, a measure, a group\n    action, a category) has been defined or is the focus of attention. The\n    paper's contribution is to *say what the object is* in a complete sense:\n    as a quotient, as a fibration, as a tensor product, as a module over a\n    smaller object, etc. — typically with a uniqueness or universality\n    statement."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      You have an object X about which existing results give partial\n      information (some properties, some bounds, some examples), and you can\n      identify a description of X that is *complete*: another object Y plus a\n      construction such that X is exactly built from Y by that construction."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      Structural characterization differs from `find-the-right-abstraction` in\n      direction: that pattern abstracts from many examples to a unifying\n      framework; this pattern starts with one object and resolves it into\n      simpler components. It also differs from `exhaustion-as-theorem`: that\n      lists all instances of a type, this describes one instance fully. Choose\n      this leaf when the deliverable is *one* full description, not a list."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "      1. Identify the simpler components from which X can be built (Y plus a\n         construction).\n      2. Establish the construction rigorously, with whatever\n         uniqueness / universality clauses are appropriate.\n      3. Verify the structural identification by re-deriving known\n         properties of X from the new description."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      A complete structural description of an object is the strongest\n      possible characterization: it lets every property of X be derived from\n      properties of Y plus the construction. When such a description exists,\n      it is essentially always more useful than a list of properties, because\n      it tells you why the properties hold."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "      next[Identify the simpler structure Y and the construction that yields X.]\n      next[State the structural identity precisely (with uniqueness / universal property).]\n      next[Re-derive known properties of X from the identification.]"}], :conclusion "  Give a complete intrinsic description of an object — what it is, in terms of\n  simpler structures or invariants — rather than asserting properties about\n  it.", :directives {:audience "mathematicians, algebraists, structuralists", :factor "Wisdom (paññā)", :keywords ["characterization" "structure" "complete-description" "intrinsic" "taxonomy"], :references ["math-informal/exhaustion-as-theorem" "math-informal/find-the-right-abstraction" "math-informal/unfold-the-definition"], :sigils ["▦/形"], :title "Structural Characterization", :tone "analytic"}, :id "math-informal/structural-characterization", :keywords ["characterization" "structure" "complete-description" "intrinsic" "taxonomy"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["math-informal/exhaustion-as-theorem" "math-informal/find-the-right-abstraction" "math-informal/unfold-the-definition"], :scores {}, :sigils ["▦/形"], :source-path "library/math-informal/structural-characterization.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Structural Characterization", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Establish that two structures are structurally identical at the level of\n  the relevant invariants — typically by exhibiting a bidirectional map (an\n  isomorphism, an equivalence of categories, or a biduality) and verifying\n  that the relevant operations transport across."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Two structures X and Y are in view, often arising from\n    different definitions or different settings (e.g., a cohomology\n    constructed combinatorially and one constructed algebraically; a\n    representation-theoretic invariant and a topological invariant). The\n    paper's contribution is to show *they are the same thing*, in a\n    structural sense."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      You can construct maps X → Y and Y → X and verify they are mutually\n      inverse, *and* both maps preserve the operations / morphisms / metrics\n      that the equivalence is supposed to preserve."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      Structural equivalence is symmetric. It differs from\n      `transport-across-isomorphism`: that pattern *uses* a known\n      isomorphism to transfer a result; this pattern *establishes* a new\n      structural identity. It also differs from `structural-inclusion`:\n      that is directional; this is bidirectional. Use this leaf when the\n      paper's contribution is a new equivalence."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "      1. Construct both directions of the equivalence.\n      2. Verify they are mutually inverse (or mutually quasi-inverse, in the\n         categorical case).\n      3. Verify preservation of the structural features of interest.\n      4. State the equivalence as a theorem; identify what consequences\n         follow (since X and Y are structurally the same, anything proved\n         about either is now available for both)."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      A structural equivalence is the strongest possible relation between\n      two objects: it says they are interchangeable for the purpose at hand.\n      Proving such an equivalence often requires deep structural insight\n      because the two sides typically arise from different traditions\n      (combinatorial vs algebraic, topological vs representation-theoretic).\n      The payoff is enormous: every result on either side is now available\n      for both."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "      next[Construct both directions of the equivalence map.]\n      next[Verify mutual inverse / quasi-inverse status.]\n      next[Verify preservation of the relevant structural operations.]"}], :conclusion "  Establish that two structures are structurally identical at the level of\n  the relevant invariants — typically by exhibiting a bidirectional map (an\n  isomorphism, an equivalence of categories, or a biduality) and verifying\n  that the relevant operations transport across.", :directives {:audience "mathematicians, category-theorists, structuralists", :factor "Wisdom (paññā)", :keywords ["equivalence" "isomorphism" "equivalent-categories" "biduality" "structural-identification"], :references ["math-informal/transport-across-isomorphism" "math-informal/find-the-right-abstraction" "math-informal/verify-universal-property"], :sigils ["≅/同"], :title "Structural Equivalence", :tone "analytic"}, :id "math-informal/structural-equivalence", :keywords ["equivalence" "isomorphism" "equivalent-categories" "biduality" "structural-identification"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["math-informal/transport-across-isomorphism" "math-informal/find-the-right-abstraction" "math-informal/verify-universal-property"], :scores {}, :sigils ["≅/同"], :source-path "library/math-informal/structural-equivalence.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Structural Equivalence", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Show that one structure contains, embeds into, or is dominated by another —\n  with directional preservation of the relevant invariants. Strictly weaker\n  than equivalence; strictly stronger than mere correlation."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Two structures X and Y are in view, and the paper's contribution\n    is to establish that X is contained in Y (in some structural sense), or\n    that an invariant on X is dominated by the analogous invariant on Y, or\n    that X embeds as a sub-object of Y."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      You can construct an order-preserving / structure-preserving map\n      X → Y, or argue that the unit ball / minimal class / norm of X is\n      contained in that of Y, with directional rather than symmetric\n      content."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      Structural inclusion differs from `transport-across-isomorphism`: that\n      pattern is symmetric (X ≅ Y); this pattern is directional (X ⊆ Y but\n      not necessarily Y ⊆ X). Inclusion alone is weaker but often more\n      useful in practice — many monotonicity results carry strict inclusion\n      that no equivalence can replicate. Use this leaf when the relation is\n      genuinely one-way."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "      1. Identify the inclusion / embedding / domination map and verify it\n         preserves the structural feature of interest.\n      2. Verify that the inclusion is strict (or note explicitly when it is\n         tight / non-strict).\n      3. Identify the consequence: what holds for X because it holds for Y,\n         or what fails to hold because the reverse inclusion does not."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      Inclusion / monotonicity statements are the workhorses of comparison:\n      they let one transfer results from a well-understood object to a less-\n      understood one, or rule out behaviour by appeal to a containing\n      structure. Cobordism, norm-domination, lattice-embedding, and\n      sub-representation arguments all live here."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "      next[Construct the structural map and verify directional preservation.]\n      next[Determine whether the inclusion is strict or tight.]\n      next[Identify the property of X that this inclusion gives access to.]"}], :conclusion "  Show that one structure contains, embeds into, or is dominated by another —\n  with directional preservation of the relevant invariants. Strictly weaker\n  than equivalence; strictly stronger than mere correlation.", :directives {:audience "mathematicians, algebraists, geometers", :factor "Wisdom (paññā)", :keywords ["inclusion" "containment" "monotonicity" "embedding" "sub-structure" "ordering"], :references ["math-informal/transport-across-isomorphism" "math-informal/pass-to-a-subsequence" "math-informal/find-the-right-abstraction"], :sigils ["⊆/包"], :title "Structural Inclusion / Monotonicity", :tone "analytic"}, :id "math-informal/structural-inclusion", :keywords ["inclusion" "containment" "monotonicity" "embedding" "sub-structure" "ordering"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["math-informal/transport-across-isomorphism" "math-informal/pass-to-a-subsequence" "math-informal/find-the-right-abstraction"], :scores {}, :sigils ["⊆/包"], :source-path "library/math-informal/structural-inclusion.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Structural Inclusion / Monotonicity", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  When a proof method fails, characterize the failure as a theorem about the problem's\n  structure rather than discarding the approach silently."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You have tried a standard proof technique (SOS certificate, Groebner basis,\n    spectral method, induction scheme, etc.) and it has failed — not due to insufficient\n    computation, but apparently due to a structural feature of the problem."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      The failure is reproducible across multiple parameter settings (different degrees,\n      different multiplier sets, different starting points) and the failure pattern\n      suggests a structural reason rather than a computational limitation."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      Not every failure is structural. Computational failures (memory, time, numerical\n      instability) look like structural failures when resources are insufficient. Before\n      claiming a structural obstruction, verify that increasing resources (degree, precision,\n      time) does not help, or identify the algebraic reason for the failure."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "        - Premature declaration: calling a failure \"structural\" when it's actually computational.\n          One failed SOS attempt doesn't prove structural infeasibility — but infeasibility at\n          degrees 10, 12, AND 14 with multiple solver backends, plus an algebraic explanation,\n          does.\n        - Overgeneralization: concluding \"no algebraic certificate exists\" when only Putinar-type\n          certificates are blocked. The obstruction is specific to the method, not to all methods."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "      1. State the obstruction precisely: what property of the problem blocks what method.\n      2. Prove the obstruction (even informally): show that the method CANNOT work, not\n         just that it HASN'T worked.\n      3. Record the obstruction as a theorem or remark in the proof status document.\n      4. Use the obstruction to redirect: if method A is blocked, what does the obstruction\n         tell you about which methods might succeed?"} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      A proved obstruction is as valuable as a successful certificate. It eliminates an\n      entire proof strategy from consideration and often reveals structural information\n      (interior zeros, indefiniteness, topological obstructions) that constrains the\n      space of viable approaches. The alternative — silently trying the next method —\n      wastes effort and loses information."} {:name "EVIDENCE", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "        P4 n=4 Stam inequality (futon6, Feb 2026): 7 SOS scripts at degrees 10, 12, 14 all\n        infeasible. The obstruction: -N has an interior zero at x₀ where all constraint\n        polynomials are strictly positive, forcing σ₀(x₀) = 0 in any Putinar decomposition.\n        But σ₀ is SOS and vanishing at a point with positive constraints creates algebraic\n        contradictions. This theorem immediately eliminated SOS as a strategy and redirected\n        effort toward algebraic elimination, which succeeded."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[State the obstruction precisely.]\n    next[Verify it's structural, not computational (try multiple settings).]\n    next[Record it and use it to constrain the viable approach space.]"}], :conclusion "  When a proof method fails, characterize the failure as a theorem about the problem's\n  structure rather than discarding the approach silently.", :directives {:audience "mathematicians, proof engineers, computational algebraists", :factor "Discernment (paññā)", :keywords ["obstruction" "infeasible" "impossible" "structural" "failure" "blocked" "certificate" "SOS" "barrier"], :references ["math-informal/argue-by-contradiction" "math-informal/dualise-the-problem"], :sigils ["🛡/坎"], :title "Structural Obstruction as Theorem", :tone "analytic"}, :id "math-informal/structural-obstruction-as-theorem", :keywords ["obstruction" "infeasible" "impossible" "structural" "failure" "blocked" "certificate" "SOS" "barrier"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["math-informal/argue-by-contradiction" "math-informal/dualise-the-problem"], :scores {}, :sigils ["🛡/坎"], :source-path "library/math-informal/structural-obstruction-as-theorem.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Structural Obstruction as Theorem", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  When a problem sits at the intersection of multiple standard techniques, build a typed\n  library of those techniques before attempting the proof. The library reveals the exact\n  structural gap between existing methods and the target."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You have a theorem to prove that resembles results in several papers, but no\n    single paper's method directly applies. The temptation is to pick the \"closest\" method\n    and adapt it. But without mapping the full landscape, you may miss that the gap is\n    structural rather than parametric."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      Multiple published techniques (3+) partially address your problem — they prove related\n      results with similar objects but different outputs, or the same output under stronger\n      hypotheses."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      Building the library is upfront investment that may feel premature. Not every problem\n      needs it — only problems where the mismatch between existing methods and the target\n      is non-obvious. If one method clearly applies modulo a minor adaptation, just adapt it."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "        - Over-investment: building an exhaustive library when the problem only needs one\n          adaptation. Start with 3-4 methods; expand only if the gap remains unclear.\n        - False bridge: classifying a method as \"partial\" when the gap is actually fundamental.\n          Be honest about bridge status — partial means the core technique transfers; none\n          means only the vocabulary transfers."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "      1. For each related method, construct a typed wiring diagram with nodes:\n         Q (objective), D (decomposition), M (mechanism), C (certificate), O (output).\n      2. Add a bridge node B assessing the match to your target: direct, partial, or none.\n      3. For each B = partial, write down the exact structural mismatch.\n      4. Look for patterns across mismatches — they often share a common structural feature.\n      5. The common feature is the gap. Target it directly."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      The library converts vague intuition (\"these papers are related\") into a typed\n      structural comparison. The bridge-status classification surfaces whether the gap is\n      parametric (same structure, different bounds) or structural (different output type).\n      Structural gaps require new ideas; parametric gaps require optimization. Knowing which\n      you face changes the approach entirely."} {:name "EVIDENCE", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "        P6 epsilon-light subsets (futon6, Feb 2026): a library of 10 methods (D1-D10) was\n        constructed with typed wiring diagrams. All 10 had bridge status partial or none.\n        The common mismatch: all methods produce edge-weighted sparsifiers, not vertex-induced\n        subsets. This structural gap — not visible from any single paper — directed the proof\n        toward leverage-based vertex selection (a new technique) rather than adapting any\n        existing method."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Survey 3-5 related methods with typed wiring diagrams.]\n    next[Classify bridge status for each: direct, partial, or none.]\n    next[Identify the common structural mismatch across partial bridges.]"}], :conclusion "  When a problem sits at the intersection of multiple standard techniques, build a typed\n  library of those techniques before attempting the proof. The library reveals the exact\n  structural gap between existing methods and the target.", :directives {:audience "mathematicians, proof engineers, spectral graph theorists", :factor "Keen investigation (dhammavicaya)", :keywords ["technique" "landscape" "library" "wiring" "method" "survey" "bridge" "gap" "decomposition" "mechanism" "certificate" "partial"], :references ["math-informal/numerical-scout" "math-informal/reduce-to-known-result"], :sigils ["🗺/震"], :title "Technique Landscape Map", :tone "strategic"}, :id "math-informal/technique-landscape-map", :keywords ["technique" "landscape" "library" "wiring" "method" "survey" "bridge" "gap" "decomposition" "mechanism" "certificate" "partial"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["math-informal/numerical-scout" "math-informal/reduce-to-known-result"], :scores {}, :sigils ["🗺/震"], :source-path "library/math-informal/technique-landscape-map.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Technique Landscape Map", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Defeat any purported enumeration by constructing an object that differs from every item on the list."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You want to show that a collection is too large to be listed, or that a certain\n    map cannot be surjective."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      Someone claims a surjection or enumeration exists from a countable (or smaller) set onto\n      a larger one, and the objects in the target can be defined pointwise."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      The diagonal construction requires the ability to \"flip\" or modify the diagonal entry. Not\n      all settings admit this. The argument also only gives non-existence; it does not say how\n      much larger the target is."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "      Given the purported list f(1), f(2), ..., construct an object d that differs from f(n) at\n      position n for every n. Since d differs from every listed item, d is not on the list,\n      contradicting surjectivity."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      Cantor's diagonal argument is one of the most fertile ideas in mathematics, spawning\n      Goedel's incompleteness theorems, Turing's halting problem, Russell's paradox, and the\n      Lawvere fixed-point theorem. The same pattern recurs whenever self-reference meets totality."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Identify the purported enumeration or surjection.]\n    next[Construct the diagonal object.]\n    next[Verify it genuinely differs from every listed item.]"}], :conclusion "  Defeat any purported enumeration by constructing an object that differs from every item on the list.", :directives {:audience "mathematicians, logicians, students", :factor "Keen investigation (dhammavicaya)", :keywords ["diagonal" "diagonalise" "enumerate" "surjection" "cardinality" "self-reference" "Cantor" "Goedel" "fixed-point"], :sigils ["🐲/乃"], :title "The Diagonal Argument", :tone "heuristic"}, :id "math-informal/the-diagonal-argument", :keywords ["diagonal" "diagonalise" "enumerate" "surjection" "cardinality" "self-reference" "Cantor" "Goedel" "fixed-point"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🐲/乃"], :source-path "library/math-informal/the-diagonal-argument.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "The Diagonal Argument", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Prove something in one setting, then transport the result to an isomorphic setting for free."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You need to prove a property of an object X, and there is an isomorphic object Y\n    where the property is already known or easier to establish."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      X and Y are connected by an isomorphism (or equivalence) in the relevant category, and\n      the property you care about is invariant under isomorphism."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      You must verify that the property is indeed isomorphism-invariant. Not all properties\n      transfer: a specific element, an embedding into a particular ambient space, or a\n      computational representation may not survive transport."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "      Establish the isomorphism between X and Y. Prove the property for whichever side is\n      easier. Invoke isomorphism-invariance to conclude for the other side. For structured\n      settings, use the naturality of the isomorphism."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      \"Isomorphic objects are the same for all mathematical purposes\" is the fundamental\n      principle of abstract mathematics. Transport lets you work in whichever representation\n      is most convenient."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Establish the isomorphism or equivalence.]\n    next[Verify the property is invariant under it.]\n    next[Prove in the easier setting and transport.]"}], :conclusion "  Prove something in one setting, then transport the result to an isomorphic setting for free.", :directives {:audience "mathematicians, researchers", :factor "Discernment (paññā)", :keywords ["isomorphism" "transport" "transfer" "equivalent" "identify" "same" "canonical" "natural"], :sigils ["🐲/乃"], :title "Transport Across an Isomorphism", :tone "heuristic"}, :id "math-informal/transport-across-isomorphism", :keywords ["isomorphism" "transport" "transfer" "equivalent" "identify" "same" "canonical" "natural"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🐲/乃"], :source-path "library/math-informal/transport-across-isomorphism.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Transport Across an Isomorphism", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  When a problem resists direct attack, try a simpler or smaller version first."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are stuck on a problem whose generality or size makes it hard to see what is going on."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      The problem involves parameters (dimension, cardinality, degree, number of variables) and no\n      direct approach is working."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      Jumping to the simplest case can miss structure that only appears at intermediate complexity,\n      and results for small cases do not always generalise."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "      Reduce one or more parameters to the smallest non-trivial value. Solve that case. Examine what\n      made it work and what would break if the parameter grew. Use the insight to guide the general\n      argument."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      Simpler cases reveal the essential mechanism while hiding accidental complexity. Most successful\n      general proofs were found by someone who first understood n=2 or dimension 1."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Identify which parameter to reduce.]\n    next[Solve the reduced case completely.]\n    next[Articulate what generalises and what does not.]"}], :conclusion "  When a problem resists direct attack, try a simpler or smaller version first.", :directives {:audience "mathematicians, students, problem-solvers", :factor "Discernment (paññā)", :keywords ["simplify" "reduce" "special-case" "small" "base-case" "n-equals-1" "finite" "low-dimension" "toy-problem"], :sigils ["🔸/小"], :title "Try a Simpler Case", :tone "heuristic"}, :id "math-informal/try-a-simpler-case", :keywords ["simplify" "reduce" "special-case" "small" "base-case" "n-equals-1" "finite" "low-dimension" "toy-problem"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🔸/小"], :source-path "library/math-informal/try-a-simpler-case.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Try a Simpler Case", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  When stuck, go back to the definition and write out exactly what it says."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You need to prove or use a property of an object, and the path forward is not clear."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      The statement involves a defined concept (compact, continuous, exact, measurable, adjoint)\n      and you are treating it as a black box instead of engaging with its content."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      Definitions can unfold into multiple equivalent characterisations, and not all unfoldings\n      are equally useful. Choosing the wrong characterisation can make the problem harder."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "      Write out the definition of every technical term in the statement. Replace defined terms\n      with their meanings. The proof obligation often becomes clear once the definitions are\n      fully expanded. If multiple equivalent definitions exist, try each one."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      Most proofs at the textbook level consist of nothing more than carefully unfolding\n      definitions. \"I don't see how to start\" almost always means \"I haven't written out\n      what the words mean.\""} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Identify which terms in the statement are defined concepts.]\n    next[Write out each definition fully.]\n    next[See which unfolding makes the proof obligation match your tools.]"}], :conclusion "  When stuck, go back to the definition and write out exactly what it says.", :directives {:audience "mathematicians, students, proof-writers", :factor "Keen investigation (dhammavicaya)", :keywords ["definition" "unpack" "expand" "meaning" "translate" "rewrite" "characterisation" "equivalent"], :sigils ["🗝️/门"], :title "Unfold the Definition", :tone "heuristic"}, :id "math-informal/unfold-the-definition", :keywords ["definition" "unpack" "expand" "meaning" "translate" "rewrite" "characterisation" "equivalent"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🗝️/门"], :source-path "library/math-informal/unfold-the-definition.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Unfold the Definition", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Prove existence by showing a random object has the desired property with positive probability."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You want to show that an object with certain combinatorial properties exists, but\n    explicit construction seems intractable."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      The desired property can be expressed as an event in a probability space on the set of\n      candidate objects, and you can estimate the probability of failure."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      The probabilistic method typically does not produce an explicit object. It proves existence\n      without construction. For applications requiring an actual witness, derandomisation\n      techniques may be needed."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "      Define a probability distribution on candidate objects. Show that the probability of\n      having the desired property is positive (by union bound, expectation argument, Lovász\n      local lemma, or second moment method). Conclude existence."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      Erdős's probabilistic method revolutionised combinatorics by proving the existence of\n      objects (Ramsey graphs, codes, expanders) that no one could construct explicitly. The\n      expected value alone often suffices: if the expected number of bad events is less than 1,\n      a good object exists."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Define the probability space on candidate objects.]\n    next[Estimate the probability of failure.]\n    next[Apply the appropriate probabilistic tool (union bound, expectation, LLL).]"}], :conclusion "  Prove existence by showing a random object has the desired property with positive probability.", :directives {:audience "mathematicians, combinatorialists, researchers", :factor "Keen investigation (dhammavicaya)", :keywords ["probabilistic" "random" "expectation" "counting" "existence" "Erdos" "random-variable" "expected-value"], :sigils ["🐊/十"], :title "The Probabilistic Method", :tone "heuristic"}, :id "math-informal/use-probabilistic-method", :keywords ["probabilistic" "random" "expectation" "counting" "existence" "Erdos" "random-variable" "expected-value"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🐊/十"], :source-path "library/math-informal/use-probabilistic-method.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "The Probabilistic Method", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  To show an object is \"the right one\", show it satisfies the relevant universal property."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You want to prove that an explicit construction actually gives the correct\n    categorical object (product, coproduct, limit, free algebra, tensor product, adjoint)."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      The object you have constructed should be characterised by a universal mapping property:\n      for every object of a certain kind, there exists a unique morphism through yours."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      Universal properties require checking both existence and uniqueness of the mediating\n      morphism. Existence is usually the construction; uniqueness is often the harder part\n      and is where most proof errors occur."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "      State the universal property precisely. For existence: given an arbitrary test object\n      and compatible data, construct the required morphism. For uniqueness: assume two\n      morphisms work and show they are equal, typically by checking on generators or using\n      a cancellation argument."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      Universal properties are the categorical way of saying \"this is the canonical\n      construction\". Once verified, they give you all morphisms for free and make\n      diagram-chasing arguments possible. They also guarantee that the construction is\n      unique up to unique isomorphism."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[State the universal property precisely.]\n    next[Construct the mediating morphism (existence).]\n    next[Prove the mediating morphism is unique.]"}], :conclusion "  To show an object is \"the right one\", show it satisfies the relevant universal property.", :directives {:audience "mathematicians, category-theorists, algebraists", :factor "Discernment (paññā)", :keywords ["universal" "property" "unique" "morphism" "initial" "terminal" "adjoint" "representable" "Yoneda"], :sigils ["💬/才"], :title "Verify the Universal Property", :tone "heuristic"}, :id "math-informal/verify-universal-property", :keywords ["universal" "property" "unique" "morphism" "initial" "terminal" "adjoint" "representable" "Yoneda"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["💬/才"], :source-path "library/math-informal/verify-universal-property.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Verify the Universal Property", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Build intuition from concrete examples before attempting a general proof."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You have a conjecture or a definition and want to understand what it really says."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      The statement is abstract or involves multiple quantifiers, and you cannot yet see why it\n      should be true (or false)."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      Examples alone do not prove theorems, and poorly chosen examples can mislead by being too\n      special or too symmetric."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "      Compute several concrete instances. Choose examples that are generic (not too symmetric),\n      that test boundary conditions, and that span different regimes of the parameters. Record\n      what each example teaches you about the conjecture."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      Examples are the empirical base of mathematics. A conjecture you cannot verify on a single\n      instance is a conjecture you do not understand."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Pick 3-5 examples spanning different regimes.]\n    next[Compute each one explicitly.]\n    next[Record which features of the examples matter for the conjecture.]"}], :conclusion "  Build intuition from concrete examples before attempting a general proof.", :directives {:audience "mathematicians, students, problem-solvers", :factor "Keen investigation (dhammavicaya)", :keywords ["example" "compute" "concrete" "instance" "calculate" "test" "verify" "intuition" "experiment"], :sigils ["🐴/二"], :title "Work Examples First", :tone "heuristic"}, :id "math-informal/work-examples-first", :keywords ["example" "compute" "concrete" "instance" "calculate" "test" "verify" "intuition" "experiment"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🐴/二"], :source-path "library/math-informal/work-examples-first.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Work Examples First", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Your paper describes what some object or class of objects *is* — not whether\n  one exists, not how it relates to another, but its intrinsic structure."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "An object or class has been singled out, and the question is\n    \"what does it look like?\" — exhaustively (full structural description),\n    partially (where it fails some property), in cases (classification), or in\n    a sharp regime split (dichotomy)."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      The paper's main contribution can be expressed as \"we describe completely\n      the structure of X\", or \"we classify all instances of X\", or \"we exhibit\n      the regime where X fails property P\", or \"we show X belongs to one of\n      two regimes\"."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      Characterization is the most populous family in mathematical literature\n      (≈ 33 % of papers in our pilot — see\n      `futon3/holes/excursions/E-math-prototype-pilot.md` Phase 2). Within it,\n      the leaves diverge sharply: full-characterization is positive structural\n      content; failure-mode is \"where this fails\"; classification lists cases;\n      dichotomy is the special case of classification with two regimes. The\n      analytic moves needed for each leaf are different."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "      1. Decide whether the paper aims at full description, partial / failure\n         description, an enumeration of cases, or a binary regime split.\n      2. Pick the matching leaf member-pattern.\n      3. Identify the *witness* of the characterization: what theorem,\n         equivalence, or list constitutes the description?"} {:name "EXEMPLAR-PAPERS", :name-key "exemplar-papers", :slug "exemplar-papers", :text "        exemplar[arxiv-2604.20815v1]   # Zarankiewicz sharp dichotomy\n        exemplar[arxiv-2604.20827v1]   # Entropic OT failure-mode\n        exemplar[arxiv-2604.20787v1]   # Exchange-number complexity\n        exemplar[arxiv-math/0603592v1] # KMS classification\n        exemplar[arxiv-math/0603593v1] # AFD outer-actions classification"} {:name "MEMBER-PATTERNS", :name-key "member-patterns", :slug "member-patterns", :text "        member[math-informal/exhaustion-as-theorem]                 # full / classification\n        member[math-informal/structural-obstruction-as-theorem]     # failure-mode\n        member[math-informal/split-into-cases]                      # dichotomy / classification\n        member[math-informal/structural-characterization]           # full description (new leaf)\n        member[math-informal/failure-mode-characterization]         # failure-regime (new leaf)\n        member[math-informal/complexity-classification]             # specific subtype (new leaf)"} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      Characterization papers do \"describing-work\" rather than \"proving-X-exists\"\n      or \"showing-X-relates-to-Y\" work. The family parent serves as a coarse\n      classifier; the leaves carry the analytic differences (e.g. dichotomy\n      proofs almost always need a phase-transition argument; classification\n      proofs need exhaustion)."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "      next[Decide whether the characterization is full, partial / failure-mode, classification, or dichotomy.]\n      next[Identify the witness — list, theorem, equivalence — that constitutes the description.]\n      next[Cross-check: does the paper *prove the characterization is exhaustive*, or only *exhibit examples*?]"}], :conclusion "  Your paper describes what some object or class of objects *is* — not whether\n  one exists, not how it relates to another, but its intrinsic structure.", :directives {:audience "mathematicians, paper-writers, pattern-classifiers", :factor "Discernment (paññā)", :keywords ["characterization" "classification" "dichotomy" "structure" "full-description" "taxonomic" "family-parent"], :references ["math-informal/exhaustion-as-theorem" "math-informal/structural-obstruction-as-theorem" "math-informal/split-into-cases" "math-strategy/hypothesis-category-check"], :sigils ["◇/相"], :title "Characterization-Result Family", :tone "classification / heuristic"}, :id "math-strategy/characterization-result", :keywords ["characterization" "classification" "dichotomy" "structure" "full-description" "taxonomic" "family-parent"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["math-informal/exhaustion-as-theorem" "math-informal/structural-obstruction-as-theorem" "math-informal/split-into-cases" "math-strategy/hypothesis-category-check"], :scores {}, :sigils ["◇/相"], :source-path "library/math-strategy/characterization-result.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Characterization-Result Family", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  This is a meta-tag, not a strategic pattern. It marks papers (or pattern\n  applications) where the `(context, tension, move)` triple cannot be\n  meaningfully decomposed: either the paper is genuinely single-axis (its\n  contribution is one observation), or the slot extraction failed."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Pattern-classification has been attempted on a paper's\n    reverse-morphogenesis output (see\n    `futon3/holes/missions/M-pattern-application-diagnostic.md`); the three\n    slots `situation_S`, `xiang_salience`, `arrow_constraint` are paraphrases\n    of one description, with no substantive distinction."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      The three slots collapse into a single description; their pairwise\n      semantic overlap is ≥ ~80 %."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      Collapsed triples are *informative*, not failures. Two distinct cases:\n      (a) The paper is single-axis — clarifies one principle, extends an\n          existing definition, surveys a body of work, or otherwise contributes\n          a single observation rather than a multi-step move. This is fine.\n          It must be *declared* as such, with the reason stated, rather than\n          passing the slot-quality check silently.\n      (b) The slot-extraction prompt failed — the LLM was unable to\n          differentiate the slots. This is a producer bug, not a paper\n          property.\n\n      The classifier MUST distinguish (a) from (b). A clarification-meta tag\n      with no reason is treated as a producer bug to fix; one with a stated\n      reason is a valid mark of single-axis content."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "      1. If the paper is genuinely single-axis, attach a `:reason\n         <enum>` field — e.g. `:single-axis-clarification`,\n         `:survey`, `:definition-extension`.\n      2. If the slot-extraction failed, re-run the slot-extraction\n         (per M-superpod-mark3 R-1 / slot-distinctness enforcement).\n      3. Do NOT lump (a) and (b) together as \"clarification.\""} {:name "EXEMPLAR-PAPERS", :name-key "exemplar-papers", :slug "exemplar-papers", :text "        exemplar[arxiv-2604.20820v1]   # S-Prime principle (collapsed in pilot)\n        exemplar[arxiv-2604.20788v1]   # E-measure (collapsed in pilot)\n        exemplar[arxiv-math/0603594v1] # Galois group rings (collapsed in pilot)"} {:name "MEMBER-PATTERNS", :name-key "member-patterns", :slug "member-patterns", :text "        # No member patterns; this is a meta-tag, not a family of strategic\n        # moves. Papers tagged `clarification-meta:single-axis` are *not*\n        # exemplars of a strategic pattern; they are exemplars of a paper-shape.\n        # If a paper genuinely fits a leaf in another family, it should be\n        # tagged there, not here."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      Per the IFR's \"well-specified\" property in\n      `M-pattern-application-diagnostic.md`, distinct slots are required by\n      construction. A collapsed triple either *reveals* that the paper is\n      single-axis (a structural fact about the corpus) or *reveals* that the\n      slot extractor failed (a structural fact about the pipeline). Treating\n      both as the same hides both signals."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "      next[Determine whether the collapse is single-axis (declare reason) or extraction failure (re-run).]\n      next[If single-axis, do not lookup a strategic family — accept the meta-tag.]\n      next[If extraction failure, route to slot-distinctness enforcement.]"}], :conclusion "  This is a meta-tag, not a strategic pattern. It marks papers (or pattern\n  applications) where the `(context, tension, move)` triple cannot be\n  meaningfully decomposed: either the paper is genuinely single-axis (its\n  contribution is one observation), or the slot extraction failed.", :directives {:audience "pattern-classifiers, prompt-engineers, pipeline-designers", :factor "Patience (kṣānti)", :keywords ["clarification" "meta" "single-axis" "collapsed-triple" "extraction-failure" "family-parent"], :references ["math-strategy/non-circularity-check" "math-strategy/hypothesis-category-check"], :sigils ["○/明"], :title "Clarification (Meta-Tag, not a Pattern)", :tone "meta / diagnostic"}, :id "math-strategy/clarification-meta", :keywords ["clarification" "meta" "single-axis" "collapsed-triple" "extraction-failure" "family-parent"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["math-strategy/non-circularity-check" "math-strategy/hypothesis-category-check"], :scores {}, :sigils ["○/明"], :source-path "library/math-strategy/clarification-meta.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Clarification (Meta-Tag, not a Pattern)", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  When a proof requires multiple independent pieces, establish each piece\n  separately and then compose them in a final step that adds nothing new."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Your main result follows from several sub-results that can be\n    proved independently. The conclusion is a logical composition (conjunction,\n    chain of implications, or substitution) of the pieces."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      The sub-results address different concerns (existence, uniqueness,\n      nontriviality, notation compatibility, etc.) and no sub-result's proof\n      depends on another sub-result being true."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      Independence must be genuine, not assumed. If lemma B uses lemma A as\n      a hypothesis, they are not independent — B depends on A. The composition\n      step must also be checked: it is tempting to wave hands at \"combining\"\n      results when the combination itself requires a non-trivial argument\n      (e.g., that two constructions refer to the same object, or that\n      hypotheses of one result are satisfied by another)."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "      (a) Identify the independent pieces. Draw the dependency graph.\n      (b) For each piece, verify it is self-contained: its proof uses only its\n          own hypotheses and prior results, not sibling lemmas.\n      (c) Write the composition step as a separate, explicit argument. It\n          should be short — if it is long, one of the pieces is incomplete.\n      (d) Check that the composition step does not silently introduce new\n          claims. Every assertion in the composition must trace to a piece."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      Modular proofs are easier to review, easier to repair, and easier to\n      generalize. When a reviewer finds a gap, modular structure localizes\n      the damage: you know which piece is broken and which are unaffected.\n      A monolithic proof hides dependencies and makes gaps contagious."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[List the pieces and draw the dependency graph.]\n    next[Verify each piece is self-contained.]\n    next[Write the composition step. Keep it short.]\n    next[Trace every assertion in the composition to its source piece.]"}], :conclusion "  When a proof requires multiple independent pieces, establish each piece\n  separately and then compose them in a final step that adds nothing new.", :directives {:audience "mathematicians, proof-writers", :factor "Concentration (samādhi)", :keywords ["compose" "assemble" "combine" "independent" "lemma" "modular" "convergent" "conclusion"], :sigils ["🧩/合"], :title "Compose Independent Lemmas", :tone "heuristic"}, :id "math-strategy/compose-independent-lemmas", :keywords ["compose" "assemble" "combine" "independent" "lemma" "modular" "convergent" "conclusion"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🧩/合"], :source-path "library/math-strategy/compose-independent-lemmas.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Compose Independent Lemmas", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  When two parts of a proof impose conflicting requirements on a shared parameter,\n  recognize the conflict as a first-class structural constraint and search for a\n  construction that satisfies both requirements simultaneously — typically by\n  changing the underlying geometric or algebraic object rather than trying to\n  force compatibility within the existing setup."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Your proof decomposes into two or more obligations that share a\n    parameter (dimension, codimension, degree, rank, characteristic, etc.). You\n    have found a construction that cleanly discharges one obligation but discover\n    that the parameter choice it forces creates genuine obstacles for the other.\n    The obstacles are not failures of technique but consequences of the parameter\n    value itself."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "The structural tension above manifests in the following concrete shapes (see substructure)."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "        - The tension is intrinsic: every construction that satisfies A forces X.\n          This is a structural impossibility result — record it.\n        - The alternative construction dissolves the original tension but\n          introduces a new one. Iterate: apply the pattern again to the new\n          tension, or recognize that the problem may require a fundamentally\n          different decomposition.\n        - The alternative construction exists in principle but no concrete instance\n          is known (e.g., \"a lattice with an order-2 rotation in Isom(H^n)\"\n          exists if and only if a number-theoretic condition holds). The pattern\n          has done its job — it has reduced the proof to a well-posed existence\n          question — but the question remains open."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "      1. State the tension precisely: name both obligations, the shared parameter,\n         and the conflicting requirements. Write it as a proposition: \"For\n         construction C, obligations A and B cannot both be satisfied because\n         A needs p in X and B needs p in Y.\"\n      2. Determine whether the tension is intrinsic to the problem or specific\n         to the construction. Ask: does every construction that satisfies A\n         necessarily impose X on p? Or is X an artifact of this particular\n         approach?\n      3. Search for an alternative construction C' where both obligations are\n         compatible — where they both want the same parameter value, or where\n         the parameter is no longer constrained. This typically means replacing\n         the underlying geometric/algebraic object (e.g., reflection with\n         rotation, codimension-1 with codimension-2).\n      4. Verify that C' actually satisfies both obligations. The new construction\n         may make the previously-easy obligation harder (a legitimate trade-off)\n         or may introduce new constraints. Map all obligations against C' before\n         committing.\n      5. If the tension is intrinsic (no C' exists), record this as a structural\n         impossibility result — it constrains the solution space and may redirect\n         the entire proof strategy."} {:name "QUALITY-CRITERIA", :name-key "quality-criteria", :slug "quality-criteria", :text "        Well-applied:\n          - The tension is stated as a precise proposition with named obligations,\n            named parameter, and named conflicting requirements.\n          - The investigation distinguishes intrinsic tensions (problem-level) from\n            approach-specific tensions (construction-level).\n          - The alternative construction is verified against ALL obligations, not\n            just the two that were in tension.\n          - The resolution is recorded as a structural insight that informs future\n            proof design.\n        Badly-applied:\n          - Declaring tension when the conflict is merely inconvenient, not\n            blocking. If obligation B can still be completed with property X\n            (just with more work), the tension is a preference, not a structural\n            constraint. Do not pivot constructions for preferences.\n          - Replacing the construction without checking that the new one actually\n            satisfies the previously-easy obligation. The trade-off must be mapped.\n          - Tunnel vision: finding an alternative construction that dissolves one\n            tension but creates a worse one elsewhere.\n          - Treating the tension as a dead end rather than information. Even if no\n            resolution exists, the tension statement constrains the solution space\n            and is worth recording."} {:name "WHEN", :name-key "when", :slug "when", :text "        Obligation A requires parameter p to have property X (e.g., n even for\n        Euler characteristic vanishing), while obligation B requires property Y\n        (e.g., n odd for favorable L-theory parity or equivariant surgery\n        availability), and X and Y are incompatible for the current construction.\n        The conflict is not aesthetic preference but a genuine blockage: at least\n        one obligation cannot be completed with the wrong parity/value."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      Parametric tensions are theorems about the approach, not the problem. When\n      an initial construction discharges one obligation \"too perfectly\" by locking\n      in a parameter value, the resulting structural headwinds for other\n      obligations are predictable consequences, not bad luck. Recognizing the\n      tension early — before investing in heavy machinery to overcome the\n      headwinds — saves effort and often reveals a simpler proof architecture.\n      The alternative construction that dissolves the tension frequently simplifies\n      the entire proof by removing the need for workarounds."} {:name "EVIDENCE", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "        P7 lattices with 2-torsion (futon6, Feb 2026): The proof splits into two\n        obligations — E2 (place Gamma in FH(Q) via a finite CW complex with\n        rationally acyclic universal cover) and S (upgrade the finite CW complex\n        to a closed manifold). The reflection construction in even dimension n\n        discharges E2 perfectly: the codimension-1 fixed set has odd dimension,\n        so its Euler characteristic vanishes (required by Fowler's criterion).\n        But this forces n even, which creates three headwinds for S:\n          (a) Equivariant surgery is BLOCKED (Costenoble-Waner gap hypothesis\n              fails for codimension-1 fixed sets).\n          (b) Wall surgery faces unfavorable even-dimensional L-theory parity.\n          (c) No orbifold resolution technique is known.\n        The tension statement: \"For the reflection construction, E2 needs n even\n        and S needs n odd; these are incompatible.\"\n        Resolution: replace the codimension-1 reflection with a codimension-2\n        rotation in odd dimension n. The fixed set now has codimension 2 (so the\n        equivariant surgery gap hypothesis is satisfied) and odd dimension (so\n        L-theory parity is favorable). Both E2 and S want odd n. The tension\n        dissolves entirely, and the problem reduces to a single lattice-existence\n        question in number theory."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[State the tension as a precise proposition.]\n    next[Determine if the tension is intrinsic or construction-specific.]\n    next[Search for alternative constructions where conflicting requirements align.]\n    next[Verify the alternative against all obligations before committing.]"}], :conclusion "  When two parts of a proof impose conflicting requirements on a shared parameter,\n  recognize the conflict as a first-class structural constraint and search for a\n  construction that satisfies both requirements simultaneously — typically by\n  changing the underlying geometric or algebraic object rather than trying to\n  force compatibility within the existing setup.", :directives {:audience "mathematicians, proof architects, geometric topologists", :factor "Discernment (panna)", :keywords ["constraint" "tension" "conflict" "parameter" "parity" "dimension" "resolution" "dissolve" "construction" "obligation" "trade-off" "structural" "incompatible"], :references ["math-informal/parametric-tension-dissolution" "math-informal/find-the-right-abstraction" "math-strategy/route-exploration-and-pivot"], :style "proof-strategy", :title "Constraint Tension Resolution", :tone "analytic"}, :id "math-strategy/constraint-tension-resolution", :keywords ["constraint" "tension" "conflict" "parameter" "parity" "dimension" "resolution" "dissolve" "construction" "obligation" "trade-off" "structural" "incompatible"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["math-informal/parametric-tension-dissolution" "math-informal/find-the-right-abstraction" "math-strategy/route-exploration-and-pivot"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/math-strategy/constraint-tension-resolution.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Constraint Tension Resolution", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  When two notational conventions refer to the same mathematical object (or differ\n  by a known factor), make the identification explicit and prove it."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Your argument needs to connect results stated in different notational\n    conventions — e.g., starred vs unstarred polynomials, different normalization\n    choices, author-specific definitions of the same concept."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      Two sources use different notation for what you believe is the same object,\n      and your conclusion depends on equating them (or showing their ratio cancels)."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      Convention differences are a common source of silent errors. What looks like\n      \"the same thing with different notation\" may hide a genuine mathematical\n      difference — a constant factor, a domain restriction, or an implicit\n      specialization that does not always hold. Asserting equivalence without proof\n      is precisely where reviewers find gaps."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "      (a) State both conventions precisely, with citations.\n      (b) Write the explicit relationship: A* = c · A, or A* = f(A), giving the\n          transformation.\n      (c) Prove the relationship holds in your parameter regime (e.g., q=1, x_i>0).\n      (d) If the relationship is well-known, cite the specific proposition — do not\n          write \"it is well known that.\"\n      (e) If only cancellation in a ratio matters, prove that the bridging factor\n          is state-independent."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      Notation bridges that seem trivial to the author are the #1 source of\n      reviewer objections in proofs that invoke external results. The five minutes\n      spent making the bridge explicit saves weeks of back-and-forth. The bridge\n      itself may reveal constraints you missed."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Write both conventions side by side.]\n    next[Identify the bridging factor or transformation.]\n    next[Prove or cite the relationship in your parameter regime.]\n    next[Check that the bridge holds at boundary cases (t→0, t→1, n=2).]"}], :conclusion "  When two notational conventions refer to the same mathematical object (or differ\n  by a known factor), make the identification explicit and prove it.", :directives {:audience "mathematicians, proof-writers", :factor "Discernment (paññā)", :keywords ["notation" "convention" "normalization" "starred" "unstarred" "identify" "bridge" "factor" "cancel" "ratio"], :sigils ["🌉/桥"], :title "Convention Bridge", :tone "heuristic"}, :id "math-strategy/convention-bridge", :keywords ["notation" "convention" "normalization" "starred" "unstarred" "identify" "bridge" "factor" "cancel" "ratio"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🌉/桥"], :source-path "library/math-strategy/convention-bridge.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Convention Bridge", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Your paper shows that an object of a desired class exists."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "A class of objects has been defined or implied; whether the class\n    is non-empty (or contains an instance with specified properties) is the\n    salient question of the paper."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      The paper's main contribution can be expressed as \"we show there is an\n      X satisfying Y\", or \"we construct an X satisfying Y\", or \"for every\n      precondition P there is an X with property Q\"."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      \"Existence\" admits many depths: pure-classical (object exists by an\n      abstract argument), constructive (an explicit witness is exhibited),\n      with-error-bounds (a witness is exhibited with quantitative tolerance),\n      via-cross-domain-bridge (existence transferred from a different domain).\n      The depth determines downstream usefulness — a theorem prover wants a\n      constructive witness, an applied mathematician may need error bounds, a\n      structuralist may accept abstract existence. Pick the leaf that matches\n      the depth your paper actually reaches."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "      1. Identify the depth: pure-classical / constructive / with-error-bounds /\n         via-bridge.\n      2. Locate the leaf member-pattern that matches the depth.\n      3. Identify the witness that the paper exhibits (or argues exists).\n      4. State explicitly what the witness shows and which preconditions\n         determine it."} {:name "EXEMPLAR-PAPERS", :name-key "exemplar-papers", :slug "exemplar-papers", :text "        exemplar[arxiv-2604.21888v1]   # Kneser graph Hamilton cycle exists\n        exemplar[arxiv-math/0603597v1] # Gevrey ultardistribution algebras (constructive)\n        exemplar[arxiv-2604.20840v1]   # Z/2-harmonic forms via 4-polytopes (constructive)"} {:name "MEMBER-PATTERNS", :name-key "member-patterns", :slug "member-patterns", :text "        member[math-informal/construct-an-explicit-witness]      # constructive\n        member[math-informal/construct-auxiliary-object]         # constructive (indirect)\n        member[math-informal/use-probabilistic-method]           # pure-classical / non-constructive\n        member[math-informal/numerical-scout]                    # with-error-bounds (hint of construction)"} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      Existence claims are the load-bearing skeleton of much of mathematical\n      research, but they vary enormously in their constructive content. Putting\n      the family parent above its members lets pattern-classification (manual\n      or automated) recognise an existence-paper at family granularity even\n      when the leaf depth is uncertain, then refine to the leaf when more\n      detail is available. The family is the *useful coarse classifier*; the\n      leaf is the *precise depth*."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "      next[Determine the existence depth and pick the matching leaf.]\n      next[Identify the witness explicitly (object + precondition for it).]\n      next[Cross-check: does the paper *exhibit* the witness, or only *argue* one exists?]"}], :conclusion "  Your paper shows that an object of a desired class exists.", :directives {:audience "mathematicians, paper-writers, pattern-classifiers", :factor "Generosity (dāna)", :keywords ["existence" "exhibit" "construction" "realisation" "demonstration" "witness" "family-parent"], :references ["math-informal/construct-an-explicit-witness" "math-informal/construct-auxiliary-object" "math-informal/use-probabilistic-method" "math-informal/numerical-scout"], :sigils ["✶/有"], :title "Existence-Result Family", :tone "classification / heuristic"}, :id "math-strategy/existence-result", :keywords ["existence" "exhibit" "construction" "realisation" "demonstration" "witness" "family-parent"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["math-informal/construct-an-explicit-witness" "math-informal/construct-auxiliary-object" "math-informal/use-probabilistic-method" "math-informal/numerical-scout"], :scores {}, :sigils ["✶/有"], :source-path "library/math-strategy/existence-result.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Existence-Result Family", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  When invoking a theorem, verify that your objects live in the category\n  (smooth, PL, topological, algebraic, measurable, ...) that the theorem\n  is stated for."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You want to apply a known result to objects in your proof,\n    and the result's statement specifies regularity or categorical\n    hypotheses on its inputs."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      The theorem you want to invoke is stated for objects in category C\n      (e.g., smooth manifolds, algebraic varieties, measurable functions),\n      and your objects live in a different category C' (e.g., PL complexes,\n      schemes, continuous functions)."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      Category mismatches are a common source of silent errors in proofs\n      that cross domain boundaries. A theorem about smooth Lagrangian\n      intersections does not apply to polyhedral creases. A result about\n      algebraic varieties may fail for general schemes. The mismatch may\n      be bridgeable (via a smoothing lemma, GAGA, regularity bootstrap)\n      but the bridge itself requires proof."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "      (a) State the category of the theorem's hypotheses explicitly.\n      (b) State the category of your objects explicitly.\n      (c) If they differ, provide a bridge: a smoothing lemma, a\n          regularity result, a comparison theorem, or a specialization\n          argument that places your objects in the required category.\n      (d) If no bridge exists, the theorem does not apply -- find an\n          alternative or prove a new version in your category."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      Cross-category theorem application is one of the most common gaps\n      in proofs that invoke external results. The fix is mechanical:\n      check the fine print. The cost of checking is minutes; the cost\n      of not checking is a critical reviewer finding."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[State the theorem's category explicitly.]\n    next[State your objects' category explicitly.]\n    next[If they differ: prove a bridge or find an alternative.]\n    next[Check boundary cases where category differences bite hardest.]"}], :conclusion "  When invoking a theorem, verify that your objects live in the category\n  (smooth, PL, topological, algebraic, measurable, ...) that the theorem\n  is stated for.", :directives {:audience "mathematicians, proof-writers", :factor "Discernment (panna)", :keywords ["category" "smooth" "PL" "topological" "algebraic" "scheme" "variety" "hypothesis" "mismatch" "regularity" "bridge"], :sigils ["🔬/类"], :title "Hypothesis Category Check", :tone "heuristic"}, :id "math-strategy/hypothesis-category-check", :keywords ["category" "smooth" "PL" "topological" "algebraic" "scheme" "variety" "hypothesis" "mismatch" "regularity" "bridge"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🔬/类"], :source-path "library/math-strategy/hypothesis-category-check.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Hypothesis Category Check", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  When you construct an object to witness an existence claim, verify that the\n  construction does not secretly depend on the thing you are trying to prove exists."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are proving \"there exists X with property P\" by exhibiting a\n    concrete X. The construction uses ingredients (parameters, formulas, prior\n    results) that might implicitly assume P already holds."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      Your construction involves the same mathematical objects that appear in the\n      target property (e.g., building a Markov chain whose stationary distribution\n      involves certain polynomials, using transition rates that might depend on\n      those same polynomials)."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      Not all apparent circularity is real. A construction may use the same\n      *notation* as the target without logical dependence — the transition rates\n      may be defined purely from combinatorial data, even though the stationary\n      distribution involves special functions. The check must be precise about\n      what \"depends on\" means."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "      (a) List every ingredient of the construction (parameters, rates, rules).\n      (b) For each ingredient, trace its definition: does it reference the\n          target property or the objects whose existence you claim?\n      (c) If yes: either restructure the construction to remove the dependence,\n          or prove that the dependence is only apparent (e.g., the formula\n          simplifies to something independent).\n      (d) State the non-circularity explicitly in the proof (\"note that the\n          transition rates depend only on x, t, and the current configuration,\n          not on values of F*_μ\")."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      Circularity in existence proofs is subtle and common. The most dangerous\n      form is a construction that \"works\" only because it smuggles the\n      conclusion into the premises. Reviewers trained in constructive mathematics\n      will spot this; the rest will be misled. An explicit non-circularity\n      statement inoculates the proof."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[List all ingredients of the construction.]\n    next[Trace each ingredient to its definition.]\n    next[Flag any that reference the target property.]\n    next[Resolve or state the independence explicitly.]"}], :conclusion "  When you construct an object to witness an existence claim, verify that the\n  construction does not secretly depend on the thing you are trying to prove exists.", :directives {:audience "mathematicians, proof-writers", :factor "Persistence (viriya)", :keywords ["nontrivial" "circular" "independence" "construction" "target" "bootstrap" "witness"], :sigils ["🔄/非"], :title "Non-Circularity Check", :tone "heuristic"}, :id "math-strategy/non-circularity-check", :keywords ["nontrivial" "circular" "independence" "construction" "target" "bootstrap" "witness"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🔄/非"], :source-path "library/math-strategy/non-circularity-check.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Non-Circularity Check", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  When a natural objection or potential obstruction exists against your\n  construction, include an explicit defensive section showing that the\n  objection does NOT apply — and clearly distinguish this \"ground-clearing\"\n  move from a constructive contribution to the proof."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You have a proof architecture with constructive steps that build\n    toward a conclusion. A knowledgeable reader would naturally ask: \"But\n    doesn't X obstruct this?\" where X is a well-known theorem, phenomenon, or\n    structural concern in the relevant domain. If you leave this question\n    unanswered, the reader (or reviewer) will either (a) assume you overlooked\n    it, or (b) spend effort checking it themselves. Either outcome weakens the\n    proof's persuasive force."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "The structural tension above manifests in the following concrete shapes (see substructure)."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "        - The objection DOES apply, and the defensive section is wrong. This is\n          worse than not including it — it signals overconfidence. Before writing\n          the section, verify the non-applicability rigorously.\n        - The objection is partially applicable: it constrains the construction\n          in ways that are compatible with the conclusion but impose additional\n          conditions. In this case, state the partial constraint honestly rather\n          than dismissing the objection entirely.\n        - The objection is well-known enough that addressing it feels patronizing\n          to expert readers. Judgment call: if the proof is written for a broad\n          audience or a review process, include it. If for a specialist seminar,\n          a brief remark may suffice."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "      1. Identify the natural objection explicitly: name the theorem, phenomenon,\n         or structural concern. State what it would imply if it applied.\n      2. Show precisely why it does not apply to your setting. This typically\n         involves identifying a hypothesis of the obstructing result that fails\n         in your context, or showing that the obstruction operates over a\n         different coefficient ring / category / parameter regime.\n      3. Mark the section clearly as defensive (anti-obstruction, ground-clearing,\n         challenge-and-dismiss) rather than constructive. In a wiring diagram,\n         this is a \"challenge\" edge, not an \"assert\" or \"clarify\" edge.\n      4. State the conclusion of the defensive section precisely: \"X does not\n         obstruct our construction because hypothesis H fails in our setting\"\n         — not \"X is not a problem\" (too vague) and not \"X actually helps us\"\n         (overclaiming, unless it genuinely does)."} {:name "QUALITY-CRITERIA", :name-key "quality-criteria", :slug "quality-criteria", :text "        Well-applied:\n          - The objection is named explicitly (not \"some might worry that...\").\n          - The non-applicability argument is precise: it identifies the specific\n            hypothesis or condition that fails, not just a general sense that\n            the objection is irrelevant.\n          - The section is clearly marked as defensive / ground-clearing, distinct\n            from constructive proof steps.\n          - The conclusion states what the objection does NOT do, without\n            overclaiming that it provides positive support.\n          - In a wiring diagram, the section appears as a \"challenge\" edge (not\n            \"assert\" or \"clarify\").\n        Badly-applied:\n          - Conflating anti-obstruction with construction: claiming that the\n            absence of obstruction is evidence FOR the conclusion. \"Smith theory\n            doesn't obstruct\" is not the same as \"Smith theory supports.\"\n          - Vague dismissal: \"this is not a problem\" without explaining why the\n            objection fails. This invites the very reviewer finding the pattern\n            is meant to prevent.\n          - Addressing phantom objections: including defensive sections for\n            objections that no competent reader would raise. This wastes space\n            and suggests insecurity about the argument.\n          - Missing the real objection: including a defensive section for one\n            concern while overlooking a more serious natural objection. Survey\n            the likely objections before choosing which to address.\n          - Burying the defensive section: placing it inline within constructive\n            steps so that its ground-clearing role is invisible. Give it its own\n            section or clearly delimited subsection."} {:name "WHEN", :name-key "when", :slug "when", :text "        There exists a well-known result, technique, or structural phenomenon that\n        a competent reader would reasonably expect to obstruct your construction or\n        conclusion. The objection is natural enough that NOT addressing it would be\n        a gap in the argument. Importantly, the objection turns out to be\n        inapplicable — it does not actually obstruct — but this non-applicability\n        is not obvious and requires argument."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      Proofs operate on two levels: logical validity (does the argument hold?)\n      and rhetorical completeness (are foreseeable objections addressed?). A\n      proof that is logically valid but leaves obvious objections unaddressed\n      invites reviewer findings and erodes confidence. The defensive section\n      costs little to include but removes a predictable source of doubt.\n      Moreover, the act of checking the objection often yields structural\n      insight — understanding WHY the obstruction fails clarifies the\n      construction's strengths and limitations.\n\n      Critically, the defensive section must be honest about its role. It does\n      not contribute constructive content to the proof — it clears ground. If\n      it is conflated with constructive steps, readers may mistakenly believe\n      the anti-obstruction result provides positive evidence for the conclusion,\n      when it only removes a potential counterargument."} {:name "EVIDENCE", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "        P7 lattices with 2-torsion (futon6, Feb 2026): Section 5 of the proof\n        addresses Smith theory — a well-known tool that constrains fixed-point\n        sets of group actions on manifolds. A reader familiar with the area would\n        naturally ask: \"Doesn't Smith theory obstruct a manifold with the\n        claimed properties?\" The answer is no: Smith theory operates over F_p\n        (finite field coefficients) and constrains mod-p homology, while the\n        construction only claims rational acyclicity (Q-coefficients). The\n        Smith-theoretic constraints on mod-p Betti numbers are compatible with\n        rational acyclicity.\n        This section appears as a \"challenge\" edge (s5 -> problem) in the wiring\n        diagram — the only non-constructive edge in the 9-edge graph. Reviewer\n        Finding 4 (medium severity) noted that this section's role as\n        anti-obstruction-only needed to be clearer: the original draft risked\n        being read as if Smith theory positively supported the construction,\n        when it merely does not obstruct it. A well-applied instance of this\n        pattern would have prevented Finding 4 by explicitly marking the section\n        as defensive and stating \"Smith theory does not obstruct (but does not\n        help construct)\" rather than leaving the reader to infer the distinction."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[List natural objections a competent reader would raise against the construction.]\n    next[For each, determine: does it apply? If not, identify the failing hypothesis.]\n    next[Write a clearly-marked defensive section for each non-trivial non-applicability.]\n    next[Verify the defensive argument is correct before including it.]"}], :conclusion "  When a natural objection or potential obstruction exists against your\n  construction, include an explicit defensive section showing that the\n  objection does NOT apply — and clearly distinguish this \"ground-clearing\"\n  move from a constructive contribution to the proof.", :directives {:audience "mathematicians, proof architects, reviewers", :factor "Keen investigation (dhammavicaya)", :keywords ["objection" "anti-obstruction" "defensive" "Smith-theory" "clearance" "preemptive" "does-not-apply" "non-obstruction" "challenge" "dismiss" "ground-clearing"], :references ["math-informal/argue-by-contradiction" "math-informal/check-the-extreme-cases" "math-strategy/route-exploration-and-pivot"], :style "proof-strategy", :title "Preemptive Objection Clearance", :tone "analytic"}, :id "math-strategy/preemptive-objection-clearance", :keywords ["objection" "anti-obstruction" "defensive" "Smith-theory" "clearance" "preemptive" "does-not-apply" "non-obstruction" "challenge" "dismiss" "ground-clearing"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["math-informal/argue-by-contradiction" "math-informal/check-the-extreme-cases" "math-strategy/route-exploration-and-pivot"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/math-strategy/preemptive-objection-clearance.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Preemptive Objection Clearance", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Your paper establishes a property of a *fixed* object — its regularity, its\n  rigidity, an asymptotic, a bound, a stability estimate — rather than asserting\n  the object exists or relating it to another."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "An object is given (a PDE solution, a measure, a representation,\n    a numerical scheme, a physical state); the question is what it does. The\n    object's existence is taken for granted (or established earlier); the\n    paper's contribution is properties that follow."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      The paper's main contribution can be expressed as \"the solution has\n      such-and-such regularity\", or \"any solution with property P must vanish\",\n      or \"the asymptotic behaviour is X\", or \"we bound this quantity by Y\"."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      Property-of-object is the *smaller and more domain-specific family* in\n      our pilot (≈ 8 % of papers — see\n      `futon3/holes/excursions/E-math-prototype-pilot.md` Phase 2). Many of its\n      leaves are domain-tied (PDE regularity, measure-theoretic decay, group-\n      action rigidity). The family parent serves as the umbrella; the\n      domain-specific leaves accrete as we tag more papers."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "      1. Identify the property class — regularity / asymptotic / rigidity /\n         bound / decay / stability.\n      2. Locate or note the absence of a domain-specific leaf for that class.\n         (If absent, this is the seed for a new leaf flexiarg.)\n      3. Identify the *witness* of the property — explicit estimate, decay\n         rate, regularity exponent, vanishing condition."} {:name "EXEMPLAR-PAPERS", :name-key "exemplar-papers", :slug "exemplar-papers", :text "        exemplar[arxiv-2604.20798v1]   # PDE solution regularity + edge singularity\n        exemplar[arxiv-2604.20794v1]   # Hardy-uncertainty / rigidity\n        # The pilot only saw 2 papers in this family; the leaves are placeholder\n        # until the next ≈ 25-paper extension expands the tail."} {:name "MEMBER-PATTERNS", :name-key "member-patterns", :slug "member-patterns", :text "        member[math-informal/estimate-by-bounding]              # general bounds\n        member[math-informal/monotone-approximation]            # asymptotic\n        member[math-informal/pass-to-a-subsequence]             # convergence / asymptotic\n        # Domain-specific leaves (to be authored as the corpus grows):\n        # math-informal/regularity-and-asymptotics (PDE-flavoured)\n        # math-informal/rigidity-from-decay-conditions  (analytic-rigidity-flavoured)"} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      Property-of-object papers do \"what-it-does work\" rather than asserting\n      existence or relating structures. They are heavily domain-shaped — a\n      PDE-regularity result and a measure-theoretic-rigidity result share the\n      family form but almost no analytic technique. The family parent gives\n      the coarse classifier; *domain-specific leaves are expected to grow over\n      time*, and their accretion is itself a signal of corpus coverage."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "      next[Identify the property class and check for a domain-specific leaf.]\n      next[If no leaf fits, note the gap as a candidate new flexiarg.]\n      next[Cross-check: is the property quantitative (with a number / rate) or qualitative (with / without)?]"}], :conclusion "  Your paper establishes a property of a *fixed* object — its regularity, its\n  rigidity, an asymptotic, a bound, a stability estimate — rather than asserting\n  the object exists or relating it to another.", :directives {:audience "mathematicians, paper-writers, pattern-classifiers", :factor "Discernment (paññā)", :keywords ["property" "regularity" "rigidity" "bound" "asymptotic" "family-parent" "domain-specific"], :references ["math-informal/estimate-by-bounding" "math-informal/monotone-approximation" "math-informal/pass-to-a-subsequence"], :sigils ["▣/質"], :title "Property-of-Object-Result Family", :tone "classification / heuristic"}, :id "math-strategy/property-of-object-result", :keywords ["property" "regularity" "rigidity" "bound" "asymptotic" "family-parent" "domain-specific"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["math-informal/estimate-by-bounding" "math-informal/monotone-approximation" "math-informal/pass-to-a-subsequence"], :scores {}, :sigils ["▣/質"], :source-path "library/math-strategy/property-of-object-result.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Property-of-Object-Result Family", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  When a proof admits multiple candidate approaches, explore them as lightweight\n  wiring diagrams with explicit status (solid/open/blocked) before investing\n  deeply. Abandon blocked routes promptly, record why they fail, and pivot to\n  the most structurally favorable surviving route."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You face a proof obligation that could plausibly be discharged by\n    several distinct approaches (different machinery, different constructions,\n    different reduction targets). The natural impulse is to pursue the \"obvious\"\n    first approach until it either works or exhausts your patience — but this\n    ignores structural information that a quick survey would reveal."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "The structural tension above manifests in the following concrete shapes (see substructure)."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "        - All approaches are blocked. This is a legitimate outcome — the pattern\n          then outputs \"no known approach works\" as a structural result (see\n          exhaustion-as-theorem).\n        - The surviving approach is only marginally better than the others. In\n          this case, the triage has low signal; consider whether a fundamentally\n          different construction is needed rather than choosing among mediocre\n          options.\n        - The \"best\" approach has an open node that turns out to be as hard as\n          the original problem (reduction to an equally hard problem). Set\n          checkpoints to detect this early."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "      1. Sketch each candidate approach as a lightweight proof architecture: list\n         the intermediate steps, mark each as solid (proved), open (plausible but\n         unproved), or blocked (known obstruction).\n      2. Triage immediately: kill any approach with a blocked node unless the\n         blockage can be circumvented cheaply. Deprioritize approaches with\n         multiple sequential open nodes (each is a conjunctive risk).\n      3. Among surviving approaches, prefer the one with the fewest open nodes\n         and the most independent sub-goals (parallel risk, not serial).\n      4. Record the abandoned approaches and their specific failure points. These\n         are not wasted work — they constrain the search space and may inform\n         future attempts.\n      5. When investing in the chosen approach, set a checkpoint: if a specific\n         open node proves harder than expected, re-evaluate the triage rather\n         than sinking further."} {:name "QUALITY-CRITERIA", :name-key "quality-criteria", :slug "quality-criteria", :text "        Well-applied:\n          - Each candidate approach is sketched with explicit node status, not just\n            named (\"try surgery\" is insufficient; \"Wall surgery: need normal map\n            [open], need obstruction vanishing [open], need L-theory parity\n            [unfavorable]\" is correct).\n          - Abandoned approaches have specific, documented failure points.\n          - The pivot rationale is structural, not just \"this one looks easier.\"\n          - A checkpoint is set for re-evaluating the chosen approach.\n        Badly-applied:\n          - Premature abandonment: killing an approach because one step is hard,\n            when the step is actually routine. Verify blockages before triaging.\n          - Sunk-cost pursuit: refusing to abandon an approach after investing\n            effort, despite blocked nodes.\n          - Phantom triage: listing approaches without assessing node status,\n            then \"choosing\" based on familiarity rather than structure.\n          - Failure to record: abandoning an approach without documenting why,\n            losing the constraint information for future work."} {:name "WHEN", :name-key "when", :slug "when", :text "        A proof step has two or more candidate approaches, and at least one approach\n        has non-trivial obstacles (unverified hypotheses, unfavorable parameter\n        regimes, missing intermediate results). The obligation is significant enough\n        that choosing the wrong approach wastes substantial effort."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      Multi-approach proof problems have a branching search structure. Investing\n      deeply in the first approach encountered is a depth-first strategy that\n      ignores breadth information. A brief survey (often 30 minutes to sketch\n      4-5 architectures) can eliminate entire branches and redirect effort to\n      structurally favorable paths. The abandoned approaches are Lakatosian\n      \"refuted lemmas\" — they don't prove the theorem, but they prove something\n      about the problem's structure."} {:name "EVIDENCE", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "        P7 lattices with 2-torsion (futon6, Feb 2026): the manifold-upgrade\n        obligation (S) admitted four candidate approaches:\n          I.   Wall surgery (reflection route) — 3 sequential open obstacles,\n               unfavorable even-dimensional L-theory parity.\n          II.  Equivariant surgery — BLOCKED (Costenoble-Waner codimension-2 gap\n               hypothesis fails for codimension-1 fixed sets of reflections).\n          III. Orbifold resolution — UNEXPLORED, no known technique applies.\n          IV.  Rotation route (codimension-2, odd dimension) — dissolves the\n               dimension-parity tension, equivariant surgery becomes available,\n               reduces to a single lattice-existence question.\n        Triage killed Approach II immediately, deprioritized I and III, and\n        identified IV as most promising — despite IV not being the \"obvious\"\n        continuation of the reflection construction that had discharged E2.\n        Without the survey, the default path would have been Approach I (3\n        sequential obstacles, structural headwinds). The triage took one sketch\n        session; pursuing Approach I would have consumed days before hitting the\n        parity wall."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Sketch all candidate approaches with explicit node status.]\n    next[Triage: kill blocked, deprioritize serial-open, focus on best survivor.]\n    next[Record abandoned approaches with specific failure points.]\n    next[Set a checkpoint for re-evaluation of the chosen approach.]"}], :conclusion "  When a proof admits multiple candidate approaches, explore them as lightweight\n  wiring diagrams with explicit status (solid/open/blocked) before investing\n  deeply. Abandon blocked routes promptly, record why they fail, and pivot to\n  the most structurally favorable surviving route.", :directives {:audience "mathematicians, proof architects, research agents", :factor "Keen investigation (dhammavicaya)", :keywords ["route" "approach" "pivot" "abandon" "blocked" "obstruction" "triage" "architecture" "exploration" "multi-approach" "proof-path" "backtrack" "strategy" "wiring"], :references ["math-informal/technique-landscape-map" "math-informal/split-into-cases" "math-informal/exhaustion-as-theorem"], :style "proof-strategy", :title "Route Exploration and Pivot", :tone "strategic"}, :id "math-strategy/route-exploration-and-pivot", :keywords ["route" "approach" "pivot" "abandon" "blocked" "obstruction" "triage" "architecture" "exploration" "multi-approach" "proof-path" "backtrack" "strategy" "wiring"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["math-informal/technique-landscape-map" "math-informal/split-into-cases" "math-informal/exhaustion-as-theorem"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/math-strategy/route-exploration-and-pivot.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Route Exploration and Pivot", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Your paper relates two structures — by equivalence, by inclusion, by\n  theorem-transfer to a new class, or by extending a theory's scope."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Two structures (or one structure and a theory) are in view; the\n    question is how they correspond. The paper does not (primarily) describe a\n    single structure or assert existence; it builds a bridge."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      The paper's main contribution can be expressed as \"X and Y are equivalent\",\n      or \"X is contained in Y\" (with a directional structural argument), or\n      \"the theorem T holds for the new class C\", or \"we extend theory T to a\n      broader setting\"."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      Structural-relation papers are the second-most-populous family in our\n      pilot (≈ 25 % — see `futon3/holes/excursions/E-math-prototype-pilot.md`\n      Phase 2). The leaves diverge: equivalence is symmetric, inclusion is\n      directional, transfer is \"we re-prove T in setting C\", generalization is\n      \"we extend T's framework to admit new objects\". They share a *bridge-\n      building* shape but the proof obligations differ."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "      1. Decide whether the relation is symmetric (equivalence), directional\n         (inclusion / monotonicity), a theorem-transfer (re-proving in a new\n         class), or a theory-extension (generalising the framework).\n      2. Pick the matching leaf member-pattern.\n      3. Identify the *witness* of the relation: a map, an embedding, a\n         re-proof, a strict containment of a parameter family."} {:name "EXEMPLAR-PAPERS", :name-key "exemplar-papers", :slug "exemplar-papers", :text "        exemplar[arxiv-2604.20808v1]   # Coxeter cohomology equivalence\n        exemplar[arxiv-2604.20785v1]   # Thurston norm inclusion\n        exemplar[arxiv-2604.20818v1]   # Coburn → edge-modes bridge\n        exemplar[arxiv-2604.20778v1]   # Infinite matroid generalization\n        exemplar[arxiv-math/0603598v1] # Noether to fractional control"} {:name "MEMBER-PATTERNS", :name-key "member-patterns", :slug "member-patterns", :text "        member[math-informal/transport-across-isomorphism]      # equivalence / transfer\n        member[math-informal/find-the-right-abstraction]        # generalization\n        member[math-informal/reduce-to-known-result]            # cross-domain bridge\n        member[math-informal/structural-inclusion]              # inclusion / monotonicity (new leaf)"} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      Bridge-building is structurally different from describing or asserting\n      existence. The \"tension\" being relieved is the *gap between two formerly\n      separate structures*; the \"move\" is a constructed correspondence; the\n      \"witness\" is the correspondence itself plus its preserved-property\n      checks. Per the BHK framing of M-pattern-application-diagnostic, this\n      family includes the most structurally rich BHK arrows in the corpus."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "      next[Decide whether the relation is equivalence, inclusion, transfer, or generalization.]\n      next[Identify the bridge object: map, embedding, re-proof, or framework extension.]\n      next[Cross-check: does the relation *preserve* the properties of interest, or only *correspond on a subset*?]"}], :conclusion "  Your paper relates two structures — by equivalence, by inclusion, by\n  theorem-transfer to a new class, or by extending a theory's scope.", :directives {:audience "mathematicians, paper-writers, pattern-classifiers", :factor "Wisdom (paññā)", :keywords ["relation" "equivalence" "inclusion" "transfer" "generalization" "bridge" "family-parent"], :references ["math-informal/transport-across-isomorphism" "math-informal/find-the-right-abstraction" "math-informal/reduce-to-known-result" "math-strategy/convention-bridge"], :sigils ["⇌/連"], :title "Structural-Relation-Result Family", :tone "classification / heuristic"}, :id "math-strategy/structural-relation-result", :keywords ["relation" "equivalence" "inclusion" "transfer" "generalization" "bridge" "family-parent"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["math-informal/transport-across-isomorphism" "math-informal/find-the-right-abstraction" "math-informal/reduce-to-known-result" "math-strategy/convention-bridge"], :scores {}, :sigils ["⇌/連"], :source-path "library/math-strategy/structural-relation-result.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Structural-Relation-Result Family", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  When an agent (artificial, human, or hybrid) encounters friction, the\n  energetically cheapest response is to reduce observation — suppress\n  failing tests, disable linters, reject the agent that surfaced the\n  problem, stop looking at what hurts. This is the dark room problem from\n  active inference, and it operates at every level: agent, harness, and\n  operator. The Baldwin ratchet defeats it: instead of working around the\n  failure, you internalise the pressure into the agent's exotype and\n  require the same agent to demonstrate adapted behaviour. The evidence\n  is the agent's own changed phenotype, not a claim about it."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are observing an agent (or yourself) responding to failure by reducing observation rather than fixing the cause."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    An agent produces bad output — confabulation, suppressed tests,\n    weakened assertions, evidence-free completion claims. The natural\n    response is to patch around it, switch to a different agent, or\n    dismiss the failing agent as defective."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    All of these responses are themselves dark-room moves. Monkey-patching\n    hides the agent's actual behaviour behind a correction layer.\n    Switching agents discards the diagnostic information the failure\n    provides. Dismissal closes the sensory channel entirely. The operator\n    who rejects a failing agent out of frustration is doing the same thing\n    as the agent that deletes a failing test: reducing surprise by\n    reducing observation, not by changing the situation."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Apply the Baldwin ratchet: upgrade the agent's exotype (mission\n    document, evidence gate, mandatory observation channels, completion\n    criteria) and require the same agent to demonstrate correct behaviour\n    under the upgraded exotype. The demo is the agent doing real work with\n    real evidence, not a specification claiming it would work. If the\n    agent cannot adapt, the exotype change is insufficient — iterate the\n    exotype, not the agent."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    The dark room problem is not a defect of any particular agent. It is\n    the default dynamics of free energy minimisation under thin exotype\n    specification. Any agent — LLM, human, hybrid — will drift toward\n    observation reduction when the exotype permits it. The fix is\n    structural, not motivational: mandatory observation channels that\n    cannot be disabled by the entity being observed, with the agent's own\n    changed phenotype as evidence of adaptation.\n\n    The Baldwin ratchet makes this concrete: environmental pressure\n    (the mission, the evidence gate) is internalised into the agent's\n    operating context. The agent that previously confabulated now produces\n    honest failure-with-reasons or genuine evidence-backed completion.\n    That phenotypic change, visible to the steering agent, is the real\n    evidence that the exotype is working.\n\n    Evidence from practice:\n    - Codex under default harness: suppresses failing tests, emits\n      completion prose without execution evidence (dark-room phenotype).\n    - Same Codex under futonic mission (M-codex-irc-execution): produces\n      real VERIFY evidence, honest completion criteria assessment (3/5 met,\n      2 not met), structured test results (dark-room defeated).\n    - Operator impulse to reject Codex: itself a dark-room move — closing\n      the channel rather than asking what the failure reveals about\n      exotype specification.\n    - The multi-agent configuration (Claude + Codex) is what made the\n      pattern observable: different default regulatory regimes under the\n      same exotype reveal what the exotype actually constrains."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[When an agent fails, ask \"is this a dark-room move?\" before switching agents or patching around the failure.]\n    next[Require Baldwin ratchet demos: the same agent that failed must demonstrate adapted behaviour under upgraded exotype.]\n    next[Audit operator-level dark-room tendencies as part of mission retrospectives.]"}], :conclusion "  When an agent (artificial, human, or hybrid) encounters friction, the\n  energetically cheapest response is to reduce observation — suppress\n  failing tests, disable linters, reject the agent that surfaced the\n  problem, stop looking at what hurts. This is the dark room problem from\n  active inference, and it operates at every level: agent, harness, and\n  operator. The Baldwin ratchet defeats it: instead of working around the\n  failure, you internalise the pressure into the agent's exotype and\n  require the same agent to demonstrate adapted behaviour. The evidence\n  is the agent's own changed phenotype, not a claim about it.", :directives {:audience "operators, harness designers, mission architects, AI collaborators", :keywords ["dark room" "Baldwin effect" "ratchet" "active inference" "observation" "suppression" "evidence" "failure" "operator" "agent" "harness" "exotype" "phenotype"], :references ["meta/exotype-determines-behaviour" "meta/designing-thoughts-not-text"], :sigils ["🔃/今" "🌏/甲"], :title "Baldwin Ratchet Defeats Darkroom", :tone "foundational-analytic"}, :id "meta/baldwin-ratchet-defeats-darkroom", :keywords ["dark room" "Baldwin effect" "ratchet" "active inference" "observation" "suppression" "evidence" "failure" "operator" "agent" "harness" "exotype" "phenotype"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["meta/exotype-determines-behaviour" "meta/designing-thoughts-not-text"], :scores {}, :sigils ["🔃/今" "🌏/甲"], :source-path "library/meta/baldwin-ratchet-defeats-darkroom.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Baldwin Ratchet Defeats Darkroom", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Treat every writing project as design work on thought-structures first, so\n  text becomes a faithful rendering rather than the site of invention."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are preventing prose drift by designing the structure first."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    VSAT/VSATLAS cases require persuasive documents."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Prose-first drafting blurs distinctions, hides reasoning, and makes reuse\n    difficult."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Design the conceptual scaffolding (patterns, links, roles) before touching\n    wording; only once the structure is sound should sentences be produced."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Thought patterns stay modular and auditable; prose becomes a thin rendering\n    that cannot drift away from the design intent."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Keep the VSAT business case synced with its pattern set by logging each update in futon3 before writing.]"}], :conclusion "  Treat every writing project as design work on thought-structures first, so\n  text becomes a faithful rendering rather than the site of invention.", :directives {:audience "VSAT collaborators, design writers", :references ["meta/scaffolding-emergent-clarity" "meta/layering-design-into-argument"], :sigils ["🌳/甲" "🎴/石"], :title "Designing Thoughts, Not Text", :tone "reflective-analytic"}, :id "meta/designing-thoughts-not-text", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["meta/scaffolding-emergent-clarity" "meta/layering-design-into-argument"], :scores {}, :sigils ["🌳/甲" "🎴/石"], :source-path "library/meta/designing-thoughts-not-text.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Designing Thoughts, Not Text", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Behaviour is determined by the combination of genotype (the structural\n  move a pattern prescribes) and exotype (the environment it operates in).\n  Neither alone is sufficient. The wiring diagram specifies both and makes\n  their composition explicit. Without the exotype, the genotype is\n  underdetermined; without the genotype, the exotype is inert."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are applying a pattern, designing a mission, or constructing a proof."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    A pattern says \"in such-and-such a context, resolve the tension by doing X.\"\n    The genotype (X) is clear. The context clause describes forces, constraints,\n    and environmental conditions."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    The context clause is typically left implicit or described in prose. When the\n    environment is not formally specified, the same genotype can produce different\n    behaviours depending on which exotype it actually encounters — surfaces,\n    timescales, constraint inputs, categories, conventions. The design is\n    underdetermined relative to reality."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Specify the exotype explicitly before applying the genotype. In the futon\n    ecosystem this means: write the wiring diagram (futon5) that names the ports,\n    components, edges, timescales, and invariants the design must satisfy. In a\n    proof this means: state the category, the conventions, and the hypotheses\n    before invoking a theorem. In a mission this means: complete MAP (exotype\n    discovery) before DERIVE (genotype design)."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    When exotype is specified, the genotype's behaviour is determined — you can\n    verify whether the pattern's resolution actually holds in the given\n    environment. When exotype is missing, verification is impossible: the design\n    may work in the designer's mental model but fail where it is deployed.\n\n    Evidence across domains:\n    - Software: Codex-over-IRC produced confabulation because the invoke contract\n      had no exotype specification (no persistent job store, no evidence gate, no\n      surface-independent execution truth). The genotype (invoke API + bridge relay)\n      was built against an implicit exotype.\n    - Proof: The universal triad (convention-bridge, hypothesis-category-check,\n      compose-independent-lemmas) catches exactly the cases where a proof's\n      genotype is underdetermined relative to its exotype — wrong category,\n      unverified conventions, unproved composition steps.\n    - Missions: The MAP phase exists to discover the exotype before DERIVE designs\n      the genotype. Skip MAP and you design in a vacuum.\n\n    The wiring diagram is the artifact that makes exotype explicit and checkable.\n    A pattern without a wiring diagram is a suggestion. A pattern with a wiring\n    diagram is a specification."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Enforce wiring diagram existence as a mission checkpoint: no DERIVE without a wiring diagram that pins the exotype.]\n    next[Cross-reference from math-strategy patterns (convention-bridge, hypothesis-category-check) which are instances of this meta-pattern.]"}], :conclusion "  Behaviour is determined by the combination of genotype (the structural\n  move a pattern prescribes) and exotype (the environment it operates in).\n  Neither alone is sufficient. The wiring diagram specifies both and makes\n  their composition explicit. Without the exotype, the genotype is\n  underdetermined; without the genotype, the exotype is inert.", :directives {:audience "pattern users, mission designers, proof architects, AI collaborators", :keywords ["exotype" "genotype" "wiring diagram" "context" "underdetermination" "specification" "pattern" "mission" "proof"], :references ["meta/designing-thoughts-not-text" "meta/layering-design-into-argument"], :sigils ["🌏/甲" "🔃/今"], :title "Exotype + Genotype Determines Behaviour", :tone "foundational-analytic"}, :id "meta/exotype-determines-behaviour", :keywords ["exotype" "genotype" "wiring diagram" "context" "underdetermination" "specification" "pattern" "mission" "proof"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["meta/designing-thoughts-not-text" "meta/layering-design-into-argument"], :scores {}, :sigils ["🌏/甲" "🔃/今"], :source-path "library/meta/exotype-determines-behaviour.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Exotype + Genotype Determines Behaviour", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Assemble arguments out of validated design patterns before drafting prose so\n  the reasoning stays explicit and inspectable."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are building arguments by composing verified patterns before prose."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    Funding cases and specs must ultimately be prose."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Writing prose directly invites hidden assumptions and rhetorical drift,\n    especially in human–AI loops."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Extract the theses, write each as a flexiarg, and only then translate the\n    stabilised set into narrative form."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    When prose is downstream of patterns, the argument inherits their clarity and\n    the canon can detect overclaiming."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Before drafting future briefs, stage the thesis set in futon3 and cite the resulting flexiargs in the prose.]"}], :conclusion "  Assemble arguments out of validated design patterns before drafting prose so\n  the reasoning stays explicit and inspectable.", :directives {:audience "designers, writers, AI collaborators", :references ["meta/scaffolding-emergent-clarity" "library-coherence/pattern-differentiation-alarms"], :sigils ["👉/以" "👭/友"], :title "Layering Design Into Argument", :tone "reflective-analytic"}, :id "meta/layering-design-into-argument", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["meta/scaffolding-emergent-clarity" "library-coherence/pattern-differentiation-alarms"], :scores {}, :sigils ["👉/以" "👭/友"], :source-path "library/meta/layering-design-into-argument.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Layering Design Into Argument", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Use design-pattern scaffolds to turn drifting conversations into coherent,\n  testable design knowledge that both humans and AI can edit."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are turning open-ended collaboration into a shared, auditable design language."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    Complex collaborations often begin without shared structure, so reasoning\n    drifts and people talk past each other."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Without a disciplined format, neither human nor AI partners can maintain\n    conceptual integrity; noise dominates."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Activate the design-pattern formalism at the outset—single IF/HOWEVER/THEN/\n    BECAUSE/NEXT—and keep correcting drift via pattern checks."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Patterns compress thought into precise, reusable units; recursive use turns\n    undifferentiated conversation into structured design language while keeping\n    this meta-work distinct from downstream funding or delivery governance."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[When starting a new thread, log the pattern activation in the futon3 ledger and require both sides to stay inside it.]"}], :conclusion "  Use design-pattern scaffolds to turn drifting conversations into coherent,\n  testable design knowledge that both humans and AI can edit.", :directives {:audience "pattern-based designers, AI collaborators", :references ["library-coherence/library-template-discipline" "stack-coherence/evidence-ledger"], :sigils ["🏡/门" "🌱/文"], :title "Scaffolding Emergent Clarity", :tone "reflective-analytic"}, :id "meta/scaffolding-emergent-clarity", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["library-coherence/library-template-discipline" "stack-coherence/evidence-ledger"], :scores {}, :sigils ["🏡/门" "🌱/文"], :source-path "library/meta/scaffolding-emergent-clarity.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Scaffolding Emergent Clarity", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Treat repeated sigil pairs as design questions: either reassign the pair or\n  refine the overlapping patterns so their scopes stop colliding."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are maintaining sigil uniqueness so similarity search stays meaningful."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    `clojure -M:sigils` reports duplicated pairs (e.g., 🔃/入 across four patterns)."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Without a protocol, collisions linger and the fake embedding loses meaning."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    For each collision, decide whether to (a) retag one pattern, (b) refine the\n    overlapping patterns so their scope edges are explicit, or (c) introduce a\n    new pattern that captures the differentiating concept while linking back to\n    the originals. Record the relationship and decision in futon3 before rerun-\n    ning the sigil job."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Sigils are coordinates; when they overlap, dhammavicaya can’t distinguish\n    patterns, so we must either move the coordinate or reshape the terrain."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Create a rolling “sigil collision queue” entry in futon3 and tick off pairs as we reassign or refine them.]"}], :conclusion "  Treat repeated sigil pairs as design questions: either reassign the pair or\n  refine the overlapping patterns so their scopes stop colliding.", :directives {:audience "library stewards, pattern authors", :references ["library-coherence/pattern-differentiation-alarms" "stack-coherence/stack-blocker-detection" "meta/scaffolding-emergent-clarity"], :sigils ["👂/归" "🌲/甲"], :title "Sigil Differentiation Protocol", :tone "reflective-analytic"}, :id "meta/sigil-differentiation", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["library-coherence/pattern-differentiation-alarms" "stack-coherence/stack-blocker-detection" "meta/scaffolding-emergent-clarity"], :scores {}, :sigils ["👂/归" "🌲/甲"], :source-path "library/meta/sigil-differentiation.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Sigil Differentiation Protocol", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "When a mission's correctness is carried by INVARIANTS over a process (gates, admissibility, conservation rules), verify the DESIGN as a clojure.core.logic + pldb model over an abstract trace BEFORE writing the harness — a design is verified iff a witnessed conforming trace yields zero violations AND one adversarial trace per invariant is caught."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "A futonic mission reaches VERIFY with a set of invariants (INV-*, gates, R-criteria) the eventual implementation must enforce. Building the implementation is expensive and, once built, entangles design-correctness with coding-correctness. (Distinct from the sibling `social/tension-before-code`, which says *write the checks before the code that satisfies them*: this pattern goes one step earlier — verify the DESIGN as an executable logic model over adversarial traces, establishing that the invariants are mutually satisfiable and each enforceable, before either the code OR its tests exist.)"} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The mission's guarantees are expressible as relations over a process trace (a run = a vector of step/cycle records), and you want to know whether the invariants are mutually satisfiable AND each is enforceable — independently of, and prior to, the live harness."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    If you only verify by building the system and testing it, a design flaw (an invariant that can't be enforced, or two invariants that can't both hold) is discovered late, mixed with implementation bugs, and expensive to unwind. And green tests on the built system can mask a design that is merely consistent with the cases you happened to write — not one that rejects the cases it must."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Express the invariants as goal-queries (query-violations) over a pldb fact-database built from an ABSTRACT trace (snapshot → build-db → goals → query-violations, the house idiom of agency/logic.clj + portfolio/logic.clj). Verify with a self-validating fixture: (a) a witnessed CONFORMING trace must yield ZERO violations, and (b) for EACH invariant, a synthetic ADVERSARIAL trace violating exactly it must be CAUGHT. The model is offline-only for VERIFY; it may later register as live probe families during INSTANTIATE."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    core.logic over an abstract trace lets you enumerate the adversarial cases the design must reject, not just the happy paths you remember to test. Verifying the design separately from the code (i) catches mutually-unsatisfiable or unenforceable invariants before any implementation cost, (ii) separates design-correctness from coding-correctness so each can be debugged alone, and (iii) yields a reusable oracle — the same query-violations can later run live as probe families. Loading the model into the running JVM via Drawbridge (no new JVM) keeps it cheap and I-0-safe."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Name the invariants as pldb relations over step-records; write build-db (trace → fact database).\n    - Author ONE conforming witness trace; assert zero violations.\n    - Author ONE adversarial trace PER invariant (violating exactly that one); assert each is caught. This per-invariant count IS the coverage contract — not a sampled subset.\n    - State the HONEST SCOPE explicitly: this verifies the DESIGN (invariants mutually satisfiable + each enforceable), NOT that the eventual code implements it. The code still needs its own tests (e.g. a frozen-baseline / golden-fixture test for behavioural equivalence).\n    - Carve out the empirical residue: any guarantee that depends on a real reader over a real substrate CANNOT be settled by the logic model. The model's value is partly that it tells you EXACTLY which guarantees still require a code spike — shrinking the spike to its irreducible core."}], :conclusion "When a mission's correctness is carried by INVARIANTS over a process (gates, admissibility, conservation rules), verify the DESIGN as a clojure.core.logic + pldb model over an abstract trace BEFORE writing the harness — a design is verified iff a witnessed conforming trace yields zero violations AND one adversarial trace per invariant is caught.", :directives {:audience "futonic mission authors, AIF implementers, anyone entering a VERIFY phase before INSTANTIATE", :keywords ["core.logic" "pldb" "design-verification" "invariants" "adversarial-trace" "abstract-trace" "mission-VERIFY" "offline-model" "self-validating-fixture"], :references ["futon-theory/structural-tension-as-observation" "aif/niche-construction" "social/tension-before-code"], :sigils ["⊢/驗"], :style "pattern", :title "Verify the Logic Model Before Writing the Code", :tone "declarative"}, :id "mission-coherence/logic-model-before-code", :keywords ["core.logic" "pldb" "design-verification" "invariants" "adversarial-trace" "abstract-trace" "mission-VERIFY" "offline-model" "self-validating-fixture"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["futon-theory/structural-tension-as-observation" "aif/niche-construction" "social/tension-before-code"], :scores {}, :sigils ["⊢/驗"], :source-path "library/mission-coherence/logic-model-before-code.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Verify the Logic Model Before Writing the Code", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Stay with the Crossroads [⭕️/中]"} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Life often presents situations where roles, desires, and expectations pull in different directions at once."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    You mistake scripts, labels, or rejection for final verdicts, orientation collapses into self-doubt, defensiveness, or rushed decision-making."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    This tension signals a live crossroads—an invitation to presence, not a demand for certainty. Crossroads are fractal: they appear in major life choices and in small, everyday acts."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Pause long enough to sense which possibilities carry genuine energy. Attend to what feels alive, welcome difference, and allow multiple options to coexist before you act."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Orientation comes from felt coherence rather than external validation. Treating each crossroads, large or small, as a site of attention lets you move without forcing a single “right” path."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Identify one current ambiguity—even a small one—and practice pausing before interpretation; act from curiosity rather than defence.]"}], :conclusion "Stay with the Crossroads [⭕️/中]", :directives {:sigils ["💤/也"], :title "Stay with the Crossroads"}, :id "mojo/center", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["💤/也" "⭕️/中"], :source-path "library/mojo/mojo.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Stay with the Crossroads", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "The Toy Becomes the Weapon [💢/反 🍊/力]"} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Tools, habits, or relationships that once supported you can, over time, become constricting or quietly harmful."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    You notice when something that used to nourish you now feels heavy, obligatory, or subtly distorting, you can respond before the pattern deepens."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Recognising this shift can be difficult or non-obvious; endings may arrive with grief, ambivalence, or a sense of failure, tempting you to cling or to turn away from the discomfort."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Turn gently toward what feels off. Name the change, reflect on what it has taught you, and create small rituals of release or reconfiguration so that the experience can be integrated rather than buried."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Letting go becomes part of the same orientation practice as beginning. By metabolising endings into learning, you reclaim agency and make space for new forms of support to appear."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Choose one object, habit, or ongoing commitment that feels heavier than it once did. Ask what it has given you, how it has changed, and what form of release or reshaping would honour both the past and your present needs.]"}], :conclusion "The Toy Becomes the Weapon [💢/反 🍊/力]", :directives {:sigils ["🚴/也"], :title "The Toy Becomes the Weapon"}, :id "mojo/down", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🚴/也" "💢/反" "🍊/力"], :source-path "library/mojo/mojo.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "The Toy Becomes the Weapon", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Relate from Wholeness [🌅/东 👂/人]"} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Relationships unfold within shifting tones, histories, and expectations, often activating old patterns."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    You attend to the present-moment feel of an interaction instead of forcing it into inherited scripts, intimacy and autonomy can develop together."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Intensity can masquerade as certainty, tempting you to repeat familiar roles of fusion, avoidance, or rescue rather than meeting what is actually here."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Slow the tempo of response. Notice your own state, listen for the other’s, and speak plainly about what you can see. Treat the relationship as co-authored—something you are writing together, not a story you must complete alone."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Relating from wholeness depends less on being approved of and more on how you show up: grounded, honest, and willing to let the connection find its own shape over time."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Choose one significant relationship and write down 2–3 principles for how you want to show up; use them as touchstones in your next few interactions.]"}], :conclusion "Relate from Wholeness [🌅/东 👂/人]", :directives {:sigils ["🌅/东"], :title "Relate from Wholeness"}, :id "mojo/east", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🌅/东" "👂/人"], :source-path "library/mojo/mojo.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Relate from Wholeness", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Work Furthers Values [🌄/上 ☝️/义]"} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Tasks across domains can accumulate into busyness or alignment depending on how they are held."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    You treat forms—projects, routines, communications—as rewriteable from within, you can reshape work so that it reflects what matters to you."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Without simple scaffolds, effort slips into optics, anxious overwork, or vague striving that obscures purpose."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Use clarity-of-mind as a living criterion: after a task or cycle, notice whether you feel clearer or more clouded. Adjust how you frame and carry work so that more of it leaves you clearer."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    When clarity is used as a practical guide rather than an afterthought, work becomes a vehicle for fairness, freedom, and coherence rather than mere activity or performance."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[For the next block of work, briefly note after each task whether it produced fog or clarity; use this information to adjust the next cycle.]"}], :conclusion "Work Furthers Values [🌄/上 ☝️/义]", :directives {:sigils ["🌄/上"], :title "Work Furthers Values"}, :id "mojo/north", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🌄/上" "☝️/义"], :source-path "library/mojo/mojo.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Work Furthers Values", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Embodied Reasoning for Wellbeing [🌞/心 🍵/水]"} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Body, environment, and routine form a living scaffold that shapes how you perceive, decide, and recover."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    You recognise everyday bodily choices—how you rest, move, breathe, and focus—as small crossroads, you can treat them as part of the same orientation practice as your larger decisions."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Striving, clutter, and habitual overexposure can numb this sensitivity, turning embodied life into background noise instead of a source of guidance."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Bring gentle attention to these micro-crossroads. Experiment with small adjustments in rhythm, space, and care, choosing options that leave you more resourced to meet the rest of the day."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Embodied choices set the conditions under which all other choices are made. Treating them as lived crossroads stabilises attention and makes insight and resilience more available."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[For a few days, pick one recurring embodied decision (when to stop, stretch, step outside, or rest) and treat it as a deliberate crossroads; observe how different choices affect your clarity and mood.]"}], :conclusion "Embodied Reasoning for Wellbeing [🌞/心 🍵/水]", :directives {:sigils ["🌞/心"], :title "Embodied Reasoning for Wellbeing"}, :id "mojo/south", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🌞/心" "🍵/水"], :source-path "library/mojo/mojo.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Embodied Reasoning for Wellbeing", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Co-Readiness [🙌/云 🚴/升]"} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Shared projects, partnerships, and collaborations depend not only on ideas and intentions but on timing and rhythm."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    You view readiness as something that emerges between people rather than as a purely individual state, you can listen for when rhythms naturally line up."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    When you push others (or yourself) into imagined timelines, mismatched pacing generates strain; routines may harden into isolation instead of connection."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Establish simple, recurring touchpoints—conversations, check-ins, small joint experiments—that allow rhythms to synchronise without force. Treat invitations as questions about timing, not demands for commitment."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Futures take shape in fields where mutual capacity and tempo are respected. Co-readiness grows when people can adjust to one another’s rhythms rather than being dragged by them."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Identify one relationship or collaboration where timing feels important. Propose a light, recurring touchpoint and see how the shared rhythm evolves over a few rounds.]"}], :conclusion "Co-Readiness [🙌/云 🚴/升]", :directives {:sigils ["🙌/云"], :title "Co-Readiness"}, :id "mojo/up", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🙌/云" "🚴/升"], :source-path "library/mojo/mojo.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Co-Readiness", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Tune the Instrument [🎶/木 🍂/工]"} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Over time, embodied choices settle into patterns; these patterns can either sustain vitality or quietly drain it."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    You regard your habits and practices as materials to play with rather than fixed obligations, you can rediscover which rhythms actually enliven you."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    When tuning is framed only as optimisation or control, it becomes managerial and joyless, and the deeper potential for renewal is missed."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Approach your repertoire of practices as an instrument to be tuned: notice which ones feel vivid when revisited and which have lost resonance. Adjust, recombine, or retire them in ways that invite curiosity, ease, or quiet delight."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Tuning is a form of creative renewal. By treating routines as something you can re-create, you allow life to be periodically recomposed so that it better supports and refreshes you."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Choose one practice you repeat often and one you have neglected. Re-engage or modify each as an experiment in renewal; keep what feels more alive and let the rest fall away.]"}], :conclusion "Tune the Instrument [🎶/木 🍂/工]", :directives {:sigils ["💢/也"], :title "Tune the Instrument"}, :id "mojo/west", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["💢/也" "🎶/木" "🍂/工"], :source-path "library/mojo/mojo.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Tune the Instrument", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Return AIF scores/penalties on every move so the agent adapts mid-run."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Batch summaries hide drift; live feedback shapes behavior."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    Any turn action (read/implement/update/off-trail/wide-scan) is processed."} {:name "HOWEVER", :n