ent the mirroring contract and which fields must align.]\n    next[Add restart-cycle tests asserting mirror equivalence.]"}], :conclusion "  keep Datascript (fast) and XTDB (persistent) in reproducible alignment so the same world state appears across restarts", :directives {:audience "futon1a builders, storage maintainers", :factor "Keen investigation (dhammavicaya)", :keywords ["datascript" "xtdb" "mirroring" "reproducible" "alignment"], :references ["stack-coherence/evidence-ledger"], :sigils ["🍵/文"], :title "Reproducible Mirroring", :tone "formal-analytic"}, :id "storage/reproducible-mirroring", :keywords ["datascript" "xtdb" "mirroring" "reproducible" "alignment"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["stack-coherence/evidence-ledger"], :scores {}, :sigils ["🍵/文"], :source-path "library/storage/reproducible-mirroring.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Reproducible Mirroring", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  if you want schema evolution but also stable contracts, formalize migration rules and versioned descriptors"} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "futon1 introduced model registries and open-world invariants to stabilize schema evolution."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    you need to evolve the graph schema over time"} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    downstream tools require stable contracts and predictable migrations"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    define explicit migration protocols and versioned model descriptors"} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    unmanaged schema change causes compatibility drift and broken clients"} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Define schema versioning rules and migration checklists.]\n    next[Add tests that assert compatibility across descriptor versions.]"}], :conclusion "  if you want schema evolution but also stable contracts, formalize migration rules and versioned descriptors", :directives {:audience "storage maintainers, futon1a builders", :factor "Keen investigation (dhammavicaya)", :keywords ["schema" "evolution" "contract" "compatibility"], :references ["stack-coherence/evidence-ledger" "storage/graph-memory-contract"], :sigils ["📁/义"], :title "Schema Evolution vs Contract Stability", :tone "formal-analytic"}, :id "storage/schema-evolution-stability", :keywords ["schema" "evolution" "contract" "compatibility"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["stack-coherence/evidence-ledger" "storage/graph-memory-contract"], :scores {}, :sigils ["📁/义"], :source-path "library/storage/schema-evolution-stability.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Schema Evolution vs Contract Stability", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  if you want fast availability but also integrity, gate startup on full rehydration and explicit diagnostics"} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "futon1 added XTDB sync and startup diagnostics to prevent stale state from serving."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    you want the system to start quickly"} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    you also need integrity and full rehydration correctness"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    block startup until rehydration and invariant checks pass, and emit precise diagnostics on failure"} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    partial startup created hidden inconsistencies and required repairs"} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Define the rehydration checklist and required diagnostics output.]\n    next[Add failure-mode tests for incomplete rehydration.]"}], :conclusion "  if you want fast availability but also integrity, gate startup on full rehydration and explicit diagnostics", :directives {:audience "storage maintainers, futon1a builders", :factor "Keen investigation (dhammavicaya)", :keywords ["startup" "integrity" "availability" "diagnostics"], :references ["stack-coherence/evidence-ledger" "storage/all-or-nothing-startup"], :sigils ["🌅/久"], :title "Startup Integrity Gate", :tone "formal-analytic"}, :id "storage/startup-integrity-gate", :keywords ["startup" "integrity" "availability" "diagnostics"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["stack-coherence/evidence-ledger" "storage/all-or-nothing-startup"], :scores {}, :sigils ["🌅/久"], :source-path "library/storage/startup-integrity-gate.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Startup Integrity Gate", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  The core capability this namespace is trying to make repeatable is\n  a **pair of moves**, not a single question. Given an artefact,\n  induce the problem it solves (**backwards pass**). Given a\n  problem, propose the artefact shape that would solve it (**forward\n  pass**). The pair together is what \"finding ways into the heart of\n  a problem\" means operationally — as opposed to describing the\n  artefact, reporting its features, or responding to its marketing.\n\n  The backwards pass is diagnostic and interpretive. The forward pass\n  is generative and commercial. Both are required for the capability\n  to be useful; backwards alone is criticism, forward alone is guessing."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "The move shows up under different names across the\n    futon stack:\n    - **M-superpod-mark2** (futon6) — backwards: reconstruct a\n      paper's problem-class from its technique vocabulary and\n      argumentative skeleton, disregarding the author's stated\n      framing where it diverges. Forward: forward model that takes\n      a novel problem and proposes techniques + patterns + sketched\n      construction. Four-layer output schema: stated problem,\n      techniques deployed, reconstructed problem-class,\n      stated-vs-reconstructed gap.\n    - **M-ukrns-wp** — backwards: what problem does a working paper\n      on training reproducibility actually solve? (Making training\n      impact legible to institutions and regulators.) Forward: a WP\n      that performs that legibility-production for the reader,\n      rather than describing training impact as a topic.\n    - **M-trip-journal** — backwards: what is the mission concept\n      failing to do that the current apparatus addresses? (It\n      doesn't guarantee operator-capacity development.) Forward:\n      the hinge-log + question-pool + sidecar apparatus is\n      structurally shaped to fill that specific gap.\n    The pattern is the same; the domain and the artefact type change."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "The structural tension above manifests in the following concrete shapes (see substructure)."} {:name "ANTI-PATTERNS", :name-key "anti-patterns", :slug "anti-patterns", :text "      - Running only the backwards pass. This is criticism, not\n        capability. Joe is not selling criticism.\n      - Running only the forward pass. This is guessing at solutions\n        without understanding the problem's actual shape. Produces\n        artefacts that collapse under the reader's own backwards\n        pass.\n      - Taking the artefact's self-description as the problem. This\n        is what M-superpod-mark2 explicitly refuses: \"the paper is\n        evidence, not testimony.\"\n      - Treating the pair as a procedure. The pair is a capability,\n        which means it has to be inhabited and developed, not\n        executed. Cook Ting's blade, not the ordinary carver's."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Operate an explicit move on the named axis (see substructure below); steward the pattern against the declared discipline."} {:name "HOW-TO-APPLY", :name-key "how-to-apply", :slug "how-to-apply", :text "      **Backwards:**\n      1. List what the artefact actually contains — techniques,\n         vocabulary, argumentative moves, what it rejects, what it\n         emphasises. Evidence loci, not summary.\n      2. Induce the problem these contents would tightly constrain.\n         Prefer tighter constraint to broader accommodation.\n      3. Compare the induced problem to the artefact's stated\n         framing. The gap is diagnostic — the sharper the gap, the\n         more likely the real contribution differs from the stated\n         one.\n\n      **Forward:**\n      1. Take the problem-class (from the backwards pass, or from\n         the operator's own situation) as the specification.\n      2. Propose an artefact shape whose contents would constrain\n         to this problem-class under the backwards pass.\n      3. Check: would a reader doing the backwards pass on the\n         proposed artefact arrive at the same problem-class? If\n         not, the forward proposal is underspecified."} {:name "THE-TWO-PASSES", :name-key "the-two-passes", :slug "the-two-passes", :text "      **Backwards pass.** Given an artefact, derive the problem-class\n      it actually solves — not the one it claims to solve. For papers\n      this means reading technique vocabulary as a constraint on the\n      solvability envelope. For missions it means reading the\n      apparatus (code, structure, outputs) as a constraint on what\n      is actually being addressed. For working papers it means reading\n      the shape of the argument for what a reader could carry away.\n      The backwards pass is done well when the induced problem\n      constrains the artefact more tightly than the stated framing\n      does.\n\n      **Forward pass.** Given a problem-class, propose an artefact\n      that would solve it. For papers this is the forward model:\n      novel problem → proposed techniques → sketched construction.\n      For missions it is: given the gap named at IDENTIFY, what\n      apparatus has the shape that would close it? For working\n      papers it is: given the legibility-production required, what\n      argumentative structure would produce it in a reader?"} {:name "WHEN-TO-PLUCK", :name-key "when-to-pluck", :slug "when-to-pluck", :text "      - At any hinge where an artefact is in hand and the operator\n        is tempted to respond to the artefact's self-description\n        rather than to what it actually does.\n      - At any hinge where a problem is in hand and the operator\n        is tempted to propose what comes to mind first rather than\n        to specify the problem carefully before proposing.\n      - At any hinge where two superficially-disjoint artefacts are\n        sitting in the operator's work at once — check whether they\n        actually solve the same problem under the backwards pass.\n        This is how M-superpod-mark2 and M-trip-journal and\n        M-ukrns-wp got recognised as sharing a core move on\n        2026-04-17."} {:name "WHY-THE-PAIR-HAS-MARKET-VALUE", :name-key "why-the-pair-has-market-value", :slug "why-the-pair-has-market-value", :text "      The backwards pass yields a specification that is not the\n      artefact's own self-description. The forward pass can then\n      produce new artefacts against that specification. Clients pay\n      for the forward pass when their own forward pass is blocked —\n      they have a problem but the artefact-space they can imagine\n      is too narrow. What they cannot do is run the backwards pass\n      on their own situation to generate a better specification;\n      that's the skill. Selling the pair = selling the capacity to\n      move between problem and artefact fluently in either direction."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Making thinking legible-enough-to-teach requires making the\n    moves explicit. This pair is the move the operator performs\n    when the work goes well — in consulting pitches, in reviewing\n    papers, in designing missions, in reading the stack's own\n    artefacts. Naming the pair converts an implicit capability\n    into a repeatable one. The operator-capacity-development loop\n    M-trip-journal targets depends on having named moves to\n    practise; this is one of them, and probably the most general."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Apply the pair to at least one non-mission artefact\n         (e.g., a paper, a competitor product, a client situation)\n         and record the backwards/forward outputs in the relevant\n         mission's sidecar]\n    next[Watch for cases where the backwards pass finds the same\n         problem across superficially disjoint artefacts — those\n         are the edges to log in the connection graph]\n    next[Track whether the forward pass, when run, actually\n         produces artefacts that survive a second-party backwards\n         pass. That is the capability's validation criterion.]"}], :conclusion "  The core capability this namespace is trying to make repeatable is\n  a **pair of moves**, not a single question. Given an artefact,\n  induce the problem it solves (**backwards pass**). Given a\n  problem, propose the artefact shape that would solve it (**forward\n  pass**). The pair together is what \"finding ways into the heart of\n  a problem\" means operationally — as opposed to describing the\n  artefact, reporting its features, or responding to its marketing.\n\n  The backwards pass is diagnostic and interpretive. The forward pass\n  is generative and commercial. Both are required for the capability\n  to be useful; backwards alone is criticism, forward alone is guessing.", :directives {:audience "anyone confronting an artefact (paper, mission, working paper, code body, scan output) and wanting to find the heart of the problem it addresses; anyone with a problem and wanting to propose an artefact shape for it", :factor "Keen investigation (dhammavicaya)", :keywords ["problem" "induction" "artefact" "situation" "reconstruction" "forward-pass" "capability" "heart-of-the-problem"], :references ["structure/cook-ting" "structure/hinge-point" "structure/what-problem-is-this-actually-solving" "realtime/learn-as-you-go" "enrichment/rational-reconstruction"], :sigils ["🔙/逆"], :title "Backwards Induction and Forward Construction", :tone "structural-generative"}, :id "structure/backwards-induction", :keywords ["problem" "induction" "artefact" "situation" "reconstruction" "forward-pass" "capability" "heart-of-the-problem"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["structure/cook-ting" "structure/hinge-point" "structure/what-problem-is-this-actually-solving" "realtime/learn-as-you-go" "enrichment/rational-reconstruction"], :scores {}, :sigils ["🔙/逆"], :source-path "library/structure/backwards-induction.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Backwards Induction and Forward Construction", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  A block is one full revolution of the futonic loop:\n  象 (input) → 部 (choose decomposition regime) → 咅 (articulate) →\n  鹽 (compose; ⿱ 鹵 皿) → 香 (sense) + 🔮 (regulate) →\n  未知 (act-within transitional state) → 味 (evaluate). The git\n  commit is the 咅 → 鹽 step — articulation composing latent work\n  (鹵) into durable substrate (皿) as a stable whole. Block boundaries\n  are recognised at hinges (structure/cook-ting), not scheduled. The\n  block is the smallest unit the operator can attest to and the\n  system can record durably."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "    Multiple framings converge on the same structural unit:\n      - Bitcoin: a block is the miner-attested transaction set.\n      - Clojure STM: a `dosync` body is one transaction.\n      - Music: a bar (小節 / 節) is a unit of metrical structure —\n        the music does not pause at bar lines, but the bar gives a\n        framework for phrasing.\n      - Futonic methodology: PSR → apply → PUR is one block-shaped\n        revolution.\n    All four frame the same shape: a coherent, attestable unit of\n    transition between two stable states. Naming this convergence\n    \"block\" lets the operator and the system reason about the same\n    thing."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The operator has been editing a working tree (or composing a\n    mission section, or running a session, or otherwise doing\n    futonic work), and a hinge appears."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    A block does not close at a fixed schedule, and not every commit\n    is a complete block. A block-closure is complete only if all\n    five phases have run:\n\n      1. **Identification** — the operator perceives the hinge.\n         Cleavage in the work has become visible. (`structure/cook-ting`)\n      2. **Specification** — the included changes are nameable. The\n         operator can say \"this block contains X.\" Without\n         specification, the commit message is vague.\n      3. **Elaboration** — the structure is articulated (commit\n         message; optionally a wiring diagram, paired test, lab\n         note). The block has a *shape* in addition to its content.\n      4. **Passion** — the work has been *done*. Embodied effort has\n         been discharged. (Not a free closure; mana-allostasis\n         requires the passion phase to award real, not counterfeit,\n         mana.)\n      5. **Closure** — 鹽 forms: 鹵 (the latent work) and 皿 (the\n         durable substrate) compose into a stable whole. The git\n         commit lands.\n\n    Skipping any phase produces a forced cut, dulling the blade\n    (cook-ting). All five phases must be present for the revolution\n    to count as one block."} {:name "ANTI-PATTERNS", :name-key "anti-patterns", :slug "anti-patterns", :text "      - **jj-style auto-commit**: every working-tree state is a\n        change-id. Defeats the operator-attestation that defines a\n        block. The operator's commit message — the 咅 — is what makes\n        a block one block.\n      - **\"Commit early, commit often\" as a rule**: a discipline-style\n        injunction that forces cuts at non-hinges. Dulls the blade\n        when applied uniformly; useful only when phrased as\n        \"perceive hinges actively, do not let blocks grow indefinite.\"\n      - **One-commit-per-file**: each commit becomes a half-block;\n        metrical structure breaks. The bar disappears.\n      - **Closure without passion**: closing a block without the\n        work-phase (rubber-stamp commits, automated formatters as\n        \"blocks\", squash-merges of unfinished branches). Awards\n        counterfeit mana; produces hollow consonance."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Treat the block as the unit the system records. Pressure\n    dissipates at block-closure, not at file-level commits. The\n    operator's task is to perceive when a block is approaching\n    completion through the five phases — not to manage individual\n    file changes."} {:name "APPLICATION-TO-MISSIONS", :name-key "application-to-missions", :slug "application-to-missions", :text "      A mission-phase transition (IDENTIFY → MAP, MAP → DERIVE, etc.)\n      is a block closure. The phase output (the new section in the\n      mission doc) is the 鹽 of that revolution. Mission docs across\n      the stack already follow this rhythm; naming it \"block-shaped\"\n      lets the invariant family reason about it uniformly."} {:name "APPLICATION-TO-WORKING-TREE", :name-key "application-to-working-tree", :slug "application-to-working-tree", :text "      A working-tree block is the set of file changes the operator\n      includes in `git commit -m \"...\"`. The commit message IS the\n      block header. A block may produce 1+N commits if the operator\n      splits its scope for clarity (e.g., the futon3c sweep on\n      2026-05-03 produced 9 commits inside what was conceptually one\n      \"clear all WIP\" block); the block-id (in commit footer or git\n      notes) binds the commits."} {:name "COMPOSITION-WITH-SIBLINGS", :name-key "composition-with-siblings", :slug "composition-with-siblings", :text "      Blocks compose with PSR/PUR cycles, mission-phase blocks,\n      session-blocks, day-blocks, excursion-blocks. A single revolution\n      of the futonic loop may be wholly contained within (e.g.) a\n      session-block, or may span multiple session-blocks if the work\n      is genuinely large. The block-id (when assigned) is the binding\n      that holds the revolution coherent across its containers."} {:name "DIAGNOSTIC", :name-key "diagnostic", :slug "diagnostic", :text "      - Commits are large and infrequent: hinges have been missed.\n        Pressure rises; eventually a stop-the-line forces an awkward\n        multi-block close. The \"504 file commit\" pattern.\n      - Commits are tiny and constant: the operator is over-committing\n        at non-hinges. Each commit is a half-formed 鹽; cumulative\n        attention cost rises (mana drains via the *committing-itself*\n        channel).\n      - Commit messages are vague (\"WIP\", \"fixes\", \"stuff\"): 咅\n        (articulation) didn't form cleanly. The block isn't really\n        one block — it's an in-flight transaction the operator hasn't\n        finished perceiving.\n      - The operator's hand is hovering over `git commit`: blade in\n        wrong place. Step back. Look for the cleavage. Do not force.\n        (cook-ting)"} {:name "REFERENCES", :name-key "references", :slug "references", :text "      - futon-theory/futonic-logic — formal definition of 象/部/咅/鹽/香/🔮/未知/味.\n      - futon-theory/proof-path — the 8-phase auditable event chain that a block manifests at the substrate layer.\n      - structure/cook-ting — hinge-recognition; perceive cleavages, do not force.\n      - structure/hinge-point — the structural slot where a block can close.\n      - structure/mana-allostasis — the dynamic the block sits inside (each block-close is a mana award).\n      - invariant-coherence/drain-channel-shape — how the new working-tree drain channel and existing bounded-disposition siblings cohere under one faculty."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Without a coherent unit, every dirty file is its own anxiety.\n    With blocks, the operator's attention is on whether the *current*\n    block is approaching closure — a single coherent question rather\n    than N file-level questions. The futonic loop already names the\n    structure; \"block\" is the operational name for one of its\n    revolutions."} {:name "APPLICATION-TO-AGENT-EVIDENCE", :name-key "application-to-agent-evidence", :slug "application-to-agent-evidence", :text "      Each agent chat turn is automatically one block — evidence-per-\n      turn is the *automatic* form of block-discipline. The unit is\n      the same as a working-tree block; the recording cadence differs\n      (turn-based vs hinge-based). This makes block-discipline\n      symmetric across agent and operator activity."}], :conclusion "  A block is one full revolution of the futonic loop:\n  象 (input) → 部 (choose decomposition regime) → 咅 (articulate) →\n  鹽 (compose; ⿱ 鹵 皿) → 香 (sense) + 🔮 (regulate) →\n  未知 (act-within transitional state) → 味 (evaluate). The git\n  commit is the 咅 → 鹽 step — articulation composing latent work\n  (鹵) into durable substrate (皿) as a stable whole. Block boundaries\n  are recognised at hinges (structure/cook-ting), not scheduled. The\n  block is the smallest unit the operator can attest to and the\n  system can record durably.", :directives {:audience "mission operators, agents, anyone reasoning about the unit of attestable work", :factor "Concentration (samādhi) — a single revolution held coherently", :keywords ["block" "futonic-loop" "revolution" "commit" "articulation" "bar" "measure" "hinge" "lifecycle"], :references ["futon-theory/futonic-logic" "futon-theory/proof-path" "structure/cook-ting" "structure/hinge-point" "structure/mana-allostasis" "invariant-coherence/drain-channel-shape"], :sigils ["🥁/節"], :style "pattern", :title "A Block Is One Revolution of the Futonic Loop", :tone "analytic-foundational"}, :id "structure/block-as-futonic-revolution", :keywords ["block" "futonic-loop" "revolution" "commit" "articulation" "bar" "measure" "hinge" "lifecycle"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["futon-theory/futonic-logic" "futon-theory/proof-path" "structure/cook-ting" "structure/hinge-point" "structure/mana-allostasis" "invariant-coherence/drain-channel-shape"], :scores {}, :sigils ["🥁/節"], :source-path "library/structure/block-as-futonic-revolution.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "A Block Is One Revolution of the Futonic Loop", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Find the joints already in the structure; do not cut through gristle.\n  The hinge-point in a mission is a natural cleavage that is already\n  there. The correct action at a hinge is to recognise the cleavage\n  and slip into it — not to manufacture a transition, not to enforce\n  a schedule, not to ask a question that does not belong to the joint\n  one is at. The blade-of-no-thickness is the question-or-absence\n  that enters the cleavage without catching on anything."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Zhuangzi's Cook Ting had used the same blade for nineteen\n    years, unbroken, because he followed the natural markings of the\n    bulls he carved. The ordinary carver breaks a blade a month by\n    forcing it through gristle. A mission that asks stipulated questions\n    at stipulated gates is the ordinary carver. A mission that recognises\n    its own hinges and slips questions into them where cleavages are open\n    is Ting."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "The structural tension above manifests in the following concrete shapes (see substructure)."} {:name "ANTI-PATTERNS", :name-key "anti-patterns", :slug "anti-patterns", :text "      - Asking all questions at all gates (forced carving).\n      - Adding questions to the pool prescriptively rather than growing it\n        from what actually did useful holding-work at real hinges.\n      - Treating the pool as a checklist. It is a library of blades, not\n        a sequence of cuts.\n      - Confusing hinges with the seven-phase mission lifecycle\n        transitions. A lifecycle transition may be a hinge or may not."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Operate an explicit move on the named axis (see substructure below); steward the pattern against the declared discipline."} {:name "APPLICATION-TO-MISSIONS", :name-key "application-to-missions", :slug "application-to-missions", :text "      1. Hinge-points are natural cleavages in the mission's structure —\n         they are not scheduled, they are recognised.\n      2. An operator plucks a question from the pool only when a hinge is\n         open and a question belongs to it. Not every hinge carries a Q→A.\n         A blade that never touches tendon is a question that is never\n         asked when no question belongs.\n      3. A mission that forces questions at regular intervals is the\n         ordinary carver. A mission that recognises hinges and meets them\n         appropriately is Ting. The blade of mission-steering lasts if\n         used this way. It breaks if used the other way."} {:name "DIAGNOSTIC", :name-key "diagnostic", :slug "diagnostic", :text "      - If the operator's hand is hovering, or is making many small niggling\n        strokes, the blade is in the wrong place. Step back. Look for the\n        cleavage. Do not force.\n      - If questions from the pool feel imposed rather than recognised, the\n        hinge has not been reached. Wait.\n      - If the blade comes out \"as though fresh from the forge\" after many\n        missions, the practice is right."} {:name "THE-PARABLE (abridged)", :name-key "the-parable (abridged)", :slug "the-parable-abridged", :text "      \"When I first began to carve I fixed my gaze on the animal in front\n      of me. After three years I no longer saw it as a whole bull, but as\n      a thing already divided into parts. Nowadays I no longer see it with\n      the eye; I merely apprehend it with the soul. My sense-organs are in\n      abeyance, but my soul still works. Unerringly my knife follows the\n      natural markings, slips into the natural cleavages, finds its way\n      into the natural cavities.\""} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    The mission's structure contains the hinges already. The operator\n    does not create them. Skilled mission-steering is perception of\n    what is already present, not imposition of what the operator\n    imagines should be there. This is the same claim Ting makes about\n    bulls; it is the same claim the peripheral-gauntlet patterns make\n    about agent environments; it is the same claim the MVSG makes\n    about the octahedral void."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Grow the pool by logging which questions did useful holding-work\n         at real hinges, and which did not]\n    next[Track blade-state: if an operator finds missions difficult and\n         niggling, the practice is still developing; if the blade stays\n         sharp, the hinge-recognition is working]"}], :conclusion "  Find the joints already in the structure; do not cut through gristle.\n  The hinge-point in a mission is a natural cleavage that is already\n  there. The correct action at a hinge is to recognise the cleavage\n  and slip into it — not to manufacture a transition, not to enforce\n  a schedule, not to ask a question that does not belong to the joint\n  one is at. The blade-of-no-thickness is the question-or-absence\n  that enters the cleavage without catching on anything.", :directives {:audience "mission operators, agents at hinge-points, anyone tempted to force a transition", :factor "Equanimity (upekkhā) — the blade of no thickness", :keywords ["hinge" "cleavage" "structure" "skilled-action" "disponibilité" "wu-wei" "non-exertion"], :references ["structure/hinge-point" "futon-theory/structural-tension-as-observation" "gauntlet/aif-as-environment-not-instruction" "peripherals/inhabitation-feeds-evolution" "war-machine/inhabitation-threshold"], :sigils ["🔪/庖"], :title "Cook Ting Finds the Cleavages", :tone "foundational-poetic"}, :id "structure/cook-ting", :keywords ["hinge" "cleavage" "structure" "skilled-action" "disponibilité" "wu-wei" "non-exertion"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["structure/hinge-point" "futon-theory/structural-tension-as-observation" "gauntlet/aif-as-environment-not-instruction" "peripherals/inhabitation-feeds-evolution" "war-machine/inhabitation-threshold"], :scores {}, :sigils ["🔪/庖"], :source-path "library/structure/cook-ting.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Cook Ting Finds the Cleavages", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  A hinge-log entry has **five optional fields**, each corresponding\n  to a specific aggregate in the MVSG / five-skandhas mapping. When\n  Q→A is non-empty, these fields are available but not required. The\n  operator fills what is load-bearing for this specific hinge. Empty\n  fields do not invalidate the entry; they record that this\n  particular aggregate did not carry load at this cleavage."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "The journal register (parallel to the ledger/saṅkhāra\n    side) holds the other three aggregates that co-arise around the\n    void. The slot structure is the minimum shape that lets those\n    aggregates be addressed without collapsing them into each other\n    or into saṅkhāra-style ledger content."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "The structural tension above manifests in the following concrete shapes (see substructure)."} {:name "ANTI-PATTERNS", :name-key "anti-patterns", :slug "anti-patterns", :text "      - Filling all five fields performatively → schema becomes\n        checklist; drift toward life-logging.\n      - Writing saṅkhāra-content (what was done, what was produced)\n        into any slot → register collapse.\n      - Paraphrasing first-person articulation into agent-voice → I-1\n        violation.\n      - Summarising across entries → destruction of journal character.\n      - Using the slots as a template for generating synthetic content\n        where no phenomenology was actually present → fabricated\n        phenomenology is worse than empty."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Operate an explicit move on the named axis (see substructure below); steward the pattern against the declared discipline."} {:name "HOW-TO-FILL", :name-key "how-to-fill", :slug "how-to-fill", :text "      1. Pluck zero or more questions from the pool\n         (`futon3/library/structure/`) if any belong to this hinge.\n      2. For each field where the operator has something load-bearing\n         to say, write it in first person (I-1 applied to humans),\n         lightly edited at most if agent-transcribed.\n      3. Leave fields empty where they do not carry load. Empty is\n         structurally valid; performatively-filled is anti-pattern.\n      4. Record which learning-loop channels fired at this hinge\n         (pattern library, operator vocabulary, connection graph,\n         question pool)."} {:name "SAṄKHĀRA IS PRESENT — DISTINGUISH CONTENT-SAṄKHĀRA FROM OPERATIONAL-SAṄKHĀRA", :name-key "saṅkhāra is present — distinguish content-saṅkhāra from operational-saṅkhāra", :slug "sa-kh-ra-is-present-distinguish-content-sa-kh-ra-from-operational-sa-kh-ra", :text "      An earlier version of this pattern claimed \"saṅkhāra is NOT among\n      the slots — that's the ledger's territory.\" That framing was\n      wrong in the way it conflated two distinct things:\n\n      - **Content-saṅkhāra:** ledger-shaped records of what was done\n        in the world (code shipped, mission executed, scan ran, PAR\n        emitted). This belongs in the A→B saṅkhāra register via\n        `reflect.clj` / PAR infrastructure. Smuggling it into the\n        phenomenological slots DOES collapse registers — that\n        anti-pattern stands.\n\n      - **Operational-saṅkhāra:** the term-synthesis step that makes\n        any entry exist at all. Writing a tension is fabrication.\n        Naming a shift is fabrication. Constructing a learning-loop\n        update is fabrication. The synthesis that inhabits the\n        five-field type IS saṅkhāra. It is present in every hinge-log\n        entry — in the shift slot explicitly (as phenomenological\n        reconfiguration), and in the entry's bookkeeping (header,\n        learning-loop-updates section, outcome) as the synthesis that\n        holds the entry together.\n\n      So: the five-field schema covers all five aggregates correctly —\n      four content-aggregates (rūpa, vedanā, saññā, saṅkhāra)\n      co-arising around the viññāṇa void. That is the MVSG structure\n      applied as it should be. Saṅkhāra is inhabited, not excluded.\n\n      The ledger/journal distinction remains: ledger-content is\n      saṅkhāra-dense (verb-side records of what the system did);\n      journal-content is rūpa/vedanā/saññā/saṅkhāra-co-arising-around-void\n      (the phase's texture plus the synthesis that made the texture\n      articulable). The journal is not aggregate-exclusive; it is\n      register-distinct."} {:name "THE-FIVE-SLOTS", :name-key "the-five-slots", :slug "the-five-slots", :text "      **1. Entry state (rūpa — form).** The organisational/operational\n         condition the phase arose in. What was the material-institutional\n         state at the start? Examples: \"mission doc drafted but untested\",\n         \"three missions running in parallel\", \"operator came off a coach\n         session and a coffee\", \"the scan cache was empty.\"\n\n      **2. Dominant tension (saññā — perception/pattern-recognition).**\n         What was pulling against what? Saññā is the pattern-matching\n         aggregate; recognising a tension is saññā doing its work.\n         Examples: \"completeness vs speed\", \"ledger's formalism vs what\n         I actually wanted to say\", \"agent's framing vs operator's\n         voice\", \"three warm contacts pulling in different directions.\"\n\n      **3. Shift (saṅkhāra — fabrication, active reconfiguration).**\n         What was synthesised here that reconfigured the pattern? When\n         saññā's pattern-match changes, saṅkhāra did the work —\n         the shift slot holds the *phenomenological aspect* of that\n         synthesis. Examples: \"the mission's purpose snapped into\n         operator-capacity-development frame\", \"what I thought was\n         about reproducibility turned out to be about building AIQA\",\n         \"saṅkhāra as register was wrong — saṅkhāra as synthesis\n         step is right.\"\n\n      **4. Residual unknowns (viññāṇa as preserved void — consciousness\n         as space of co-arising).** What didn't settle, and shouldn't\n         be forced to settle? This is the sorry-preservation field —\n         the void that must remain open. Examples: \"whether the hinge-log\n         is training or just recording\", \"what the B→A model's shape\n         will be once use-data arrives.\"\n\n      **5. Energy signature (vedanā — feeling, hedonic tone).** The\n         quality of mind in which the phase happened. Pressured, open,\n         flat, locked-in, quietly flowing. Vedanā's gatekeeper work\n         made legible. Not self-report about the operator's general\n         state — specifically about the phase's texture. Examples:\n         \"profound but manageable\", \"crisp, satisfying, small-scale\",\n         \"tired but the find carried energy.\""} {:name "WHEN-TO-FILL (AND WHEN NOT)", :name-key "when-to-fill (and when not)", :slug "when-to-fill-and-when-not", :text "      - **Fill** when the hinge is a natural cleavage (cf. `structure/cook-ting`)\n        and at least one of the five fields has load-bearing content.\n      - **Fill lightly** (one or two fields only) when the hinge is\n        minor and most of the phase's phenomenology doesn't need\n        articulation. The five-field schema is a maximum, not a\n        quota.\n      - **Skip entirely** (empty Q→A, empty fields, just record the\n        crossing) when the hinge passed without any aggregate\n        carrying load. This is the blade-of-no-thickness passing\n        through the cleavage."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    The MVSG claim (TN-mvsg.md, TN-halliday-clines.md) is that the\n    mission emerges as a qualitative object when the registers are\n    held distinct without collapse. The five slots give addressable\n    structure to the other-three-aggregates register without\n    forcing content into it. Slot-filler is scaffolding for\n    inhabited articulation, not a survey form."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Record which slots get used most often across the first\n         five missions that adopt the hinge-log format — low-use\n         slots are candidates for simplification]\n    next[Watch for slot-drift (e.g., \"dominant tension\" accidentally\n         collecting saṅkhāra content) as a signal that slot\n         definitions need sharpening]\n    next[Applied-chaos-theory phase begins once the overt method\n         has been used in ≥3 missions: perturbations (different\n         hinge types, different question plucks, different agent\n         participations) reveal the method's actual attractor\n         basins, and the four-channel learning loop integrates\n         the lessons]"}], :conclusion "  A hinge-log entry has **five optional fields**, each corresponding\n  to a specific aggregate in the MVSG / five-skandhas mapping. When\n  Q→A is non-empty, these fields are available but not required. The\n  operator fills what is load-bearing for this specific hinge. Empty\n  fields do not invalidate the entry; they record that this\n  particular aggregate did not carry load at this cleavage.", :directives {:audience "operators filling hinge-log entries; agents scribing on behalf of operators", :factor "Keen investigation (dhammavicaya)", :keywords ["slot" "hinge" "entry" "MVSG" "skandha" "fill" "template" "five-field" "aggregate" "rūpa" "vedanā" "saññā"], :references ["structure/cook-ting" "structure/hinge-point" "structure/backwards-induction" "structure/whose-question-is-this" "structure/unresolved-tensions-at-closure"], :sigils ["🎰/栏"], :title "Hinge-Log Entry Slot Structure (MVSG-Aligned)", :tone "structural-minimal"}, :id "structure/hinge-entry-slots", :keywords ["slot" "hinge" "entry" "MVSG" "skandha" "fill" "template" "five-field" "aggregate" "rūpa" "vedanā" "saññā"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["structure/cook-ting" "structure/hinge-point" "structure/backwards-induction" "structure/whose-question-is-this" "structure/unresolved-tensions-at-closure"], :scores {}, :sigils ["🎰/栏"], :source-path "library/structure/hinge-entry-slots.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Hinge-Log Entry Slot Structure (MVSG-Aligned)", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  A hinge-point is a structural slot in a mission where direction can\n  shift. The slot exists whether or not a question is asked and whether\n  or not an answer is given. Empty Q→A at a hinge is structurally\n  meaningful: it records that the hinge was encountered, no question\n  fired, the mission proceeded. This is different from \"no hinge\n  existed.\" The slot holds the space."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Missions tend toward state-machine shapes — transitions\n    between phases, completion conditions, orchestrable ledger records.\n    This shape is useful for coordination but it does not by itself\n    produce the qualitative object the mission is ultimately for. The\n    hinge is the mechanism by which a mission can be steered, not only\n    progressed. Steering happens at hinges; progression happens between\n    them."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "The structural tension above manifests in the following concrete shapes (see substructure)."} {:name "ANTI-PATTERNS", :name-key "anti-patterns", :slug "anti-patterns", :text "      - Predicting hinge count in advance (\"this mission will have 3 hinges\")\n      - Stipulating which questions must fire at which hinge\n      - Treating a routine phase transition as a hinge just because it's\n        a named boundary\n      - Answering hinge questions in third-person or in someone else's voice\n      - Summarising hinge logs across missions (\"Joe's most common residual\n        unknowns are X\") — this is exactly the database-move M-trip-journal\n        is structurally protected against"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Operate an explicit move on the named axis (see substructure below); steward the pattern against the declared discipline."} {:name "PROTECTION-AGAINST-LIFE-LOGGING-DRIFT", :name-key "protection-against-life-logging-drift", :slug "protection-against-life-logging-drift", :text "      Hinge-Q→As are consumed as mission-steering input. Once the mission\n      closes, the log is preserved within the mission's directory but is\n      NOT indexed across missions, NOT queried as personal-state history,\n      NOT trended as operator data. If a pattern of entries across missions\n      starts looking like a personal-state dashboard, that is drift — the\n      hinge questions have leaked scope from mission-steering into\n      life-logging."} {:name "STRUCTURAL-ANALOGY-TO-TURN-TAGGING", :name-key "structural-analogy-to-turn-tagging", :slug "structural-analogy-to-turn-tagging", :text "      Agent-conversation turns (A(Joe)→B(agent)) carry a slot for\n      pattern-tagging. Whether a pattern fires at a given turn is emergent;\n      the slot is structural. Hinge-points are the mission-scale analogue:\n      structural slots whose Q→A content is emergent, drawn from a pool,\n      and may be empty."} {:name "THE-Q→A-CONVENTION", :name-key "the-q→a-convention", :slug "the-q-a-convention", :text "      At a hinge, the operator may:\n        a. Pluck one or more questions from the question-pool (library/structure/)\n        b. Answer them in first person (I-1 applied to humans — delegation\n           to an agent writing-as-the-operator is a category error)\n        c. Or leave the hinge empty, recording only that it was reached\n      The hinge-log entry preserves (a), (b), or (c) with equal dignity.\n      An empty hinge is data about the mission; it is not an absence."} {:name "WHAT-IS-NOT-A-HINGE", :name-key "what-is-not-a-hinge", :slug "what-is-not-a-hinge", :text "      1. Routine phase transitions where the next step is mechanically\n         determined (e.g., IDENTIFY exits when a human says \"yes, proceed\").\n      2. Scheduled review points. Schedules produce ordinary carvers.\n      3. Any point where the operator is tempted to check in on themselves\n         rather than the mission. That's life-logging drift."} {:name "WHAT-MAKES-A-HINGE", :name-key "what-makes-a-hinge", :slug "what-makes-a-hinge", :text "      1. A point at which the mission's direction is contingent on operator\n         state, operator meaning-making, or external input that wasn't\n         available at mission design time.\n      2. A point at which closing or proceeding without reflection would\n         risk the sorry-preservation invariant — risk collapsing a typed\n         void into a prematurely-settled answer.\n      3. A point at which the mission's phenomenological register (the\n         operator's or agent's experience of being inside the mission)\n         carries load-bearing information for the next segment."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    The qualitative object a mission produces depends on being steered\n    at its hinges — held in the right tensions long enough for thinking\n    to happen. A mission with no hinge-slots is a railroad. A mission\n    with stipulated-gate cadence is an ordinary carver. A mission with\n    hinge-slots and a pool of blades-of-no-thickness is Cook Ting's\n    kitchen: the blade lasts, the bull is carved, and the king learns\n    something about the conservation of vital forces."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Log hinges as they fire in M-trip-journal's own recursive instance]\n    next[Note which questions did useful holding-work — those prove\n         themselves by being drawn from the pool repeatedly]\n    next[If the pool grows beyond ~15 entries without use-frequency\n         data, prune to what has actually held]"}], :conclusion "  A hinge-point is a structural slot in a mission where direction can\n  shift. The slot exists whether or not a question is asked and whether\n  or not an answer is given. Empty Q→A at a hinge is structurally\n  meaningful: it records that the hinge was encountered, no question\n  fired, the mission proceeded. This is different from \"no hinge\n  existed.\" The slot holds the space.", :directives {:audience "mission designers, trip-journal implementers, agents at mission transitions", :factor "Keen investigation (dhammavicaya)", :keywords ["hinge" "slot" "mission" "steering" "Q-and-A" "emergence" "inhabitation"], :references ["structure/cook-ting" "futon-theory/structural-tension-as-observation" "agent/state-is-hypothesis" "social/scope-bounded-handoff" "peripherals/inhabitation-feeds-evolution" "war-machine/inhabitation-threshold"], :sigils ["🦴/関"], :title "Hinge-Point as Structural Slot", :tone "analytic-structural"}, :id "structure/hinge-point", :keywords ["hinge" "slot" "mission" "steering" "Q-and-A" "emergence" "inhabitation"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["structure/cook-ting" "futon-theory/structural-tension-as-observation" "agent/state-is-hypothesis" "social/scope-bounded-handoff" "peripherals/inhabitation-feeds-evolution" "war-machine/inhabitation-threshold"], :scores {}, :sigils ["🦴/関"], :source-path "library/structure/hinge-point.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Hinge-Point as Structural Slot", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Mission checkpoints update the interest-network not by mutating EoI\n  entities directly but by emitting typed state-transition events\n  that target them. This flexiarg declares the v1 vocabulary: eight\n  event types (seven `state/*` events plus `link/asserted`) and a\n  payload schema. The event log is append-only and\n  provenance-bearing; the posterior projection derives entity\n  standing by replay. The vocabulary is neutral — the same eight\n  events apply to essay annotations, institution-objects, basins,\n  and composite-arrow edges without entity-class-specific slang."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "The EoI corpus declares interests as a prior. Lived\n    evidence from missions (M-interim-director during design\n    2026-05-14 → ~2026-05-28; downstream validation execution\n    ~June–August 2026; EoI engine runs in any session) constitutes\n    the likelihood. The interest-network surface renders the\n    posterior. Without an explicit event vocabulary, mission outputs\n    sit alongside the EoI corpus as parallel documents rather than\n    as posterior updates; the network either ossifies (silent\n    updates) or churns incoherently (overwrites without provenance).\n    The vocabulary closes this gap by giving every state-transition\n    a citable name."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "Three design choices the vocabulary makes, and why each\n    beats its alternative."} {:name "EIGHT-EVENTS-NOT-FREE-FORM", :name-key "eight-events-not-free-form", :slug "eight-events-not-free-form", :text "      A free-form rationale field per checkpoint would be simpler\n      but loses the structure that lets the projection compute\n      coverage, detect inconsistencies, and render the network\n      without re-parsing prose. Eight types is enough to\n      discriminate (a) entity-lifecycle moves (spawned / refined /\n      strengthened) from (b) closure moves (addressed / falsified /\n      foreclosed / reopened) from (c) relational moves\n      (link/asserted), while staying small enough that an operator\n      can hold them in mind without consulting a sheet."} {:name "STATE-SLASH-PREFIX-NEUTRAL", :name-key "state-slash-prefix-neutral", :slug "state-slash-prefix-neutral", :text "      Namespace is `state/*` — not `eoi/*`, `essay/*`, `basin/*` —\n      so the same events apply to essay annotations,\n      institution-objects, basins, and composite-arrow edges.\n      Granularity is a separate field (`:target/granularity`), not\n      part of the event-type. Cross-entity-class queries work\n      naturally (\"show me every entity that got addressed this\n      week\"); per-entity-class queries filter by granularity."} {:name "LINK-AS-SEPARATE-EVENT", :name-key "link-as-separate-event", :slug "link-as-separate-event", :text "      `link/asserted` is its own event type, not a special case of\n      `state/spawned`, because \"new relation exists\" and \"new entity\n      exists\" have different downstream uses (relation surfaces as\n      edge in the network view; entity surfaces as node).\n      Overloading `state/spawned` would force the projection to\n      discriminate by `:target/granularity` to know whether to\n      render as node or edge — fine for the projection, opaque to\n      the operator authoring the checkpoint."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "The eight event types, the payload schema, and the\n    emission discipline."} {:name "EVENT-VOCABULARY-V1", :name-key "event-vocabulary-v1", :slug "event-vocabulary-v1", :text "      - `state/spawned`     — Target entity is newly created.\n      - `state/refined`     — Target entity's framing has been\n        sharpened (re-anchor, scoped).\n      - `state/strengthened` — Target entity's standing has gained\n        support from evidence.\n      - `state/addressed`   — Target entity (typically a tension)\n        has been resolved by evidence; not falsified.\n      - `state/falsified`   — Target entity has been contradicted by\n        evidence.\n      - `state/foreclosed`  — Target entity (typically a basin) has\n        been deliberately closed off.\n      - `state/reopened`    — Target entity that was\n        addressed/falsified/foreclosed has had its standing reset\n        to `:live`. First-class so retraction isn't terminal.\n      - `link/asserted`     — A new cross-essay relation exists.\n        Separate from `state/spawned` to avoid overloading \"new\n        target\" with \"new relation\"."} {:name "PAYLOAD-SCHEMA", :name-key "payload-schema", :slug "payload-schema", :text "      Each event record carries these fields (EDN, keyword keys):\n      - `:event/id`           — unique within checkpoint\n      - `:event/type`         — keyword; one of the eight above\n      - `:checkpoint/ref`     — back-pointer to the checkpoint\n      - `:target/entity-id`   — the EoI entity this event targets\n      - `:target/granularity` — keyword; one of `:essay`, `:section`,\n        `:annotation`, `:institution-object`, `:basin`,\n        `:composite-arrow`\n      - `:prior-state`        — keyword; standing before this event.\n        Required for `state/*` events; optional/omitted for\n        `link/asserted`.\n      - `:posterior-state`    — keyword; standing after this event.\n        Required for `state/*` events; optional/omitted for\n        `link/asserted`.\n      - `:evidence/refs`      — `[String]`; pointers to evidence\n        artefacts (interview notes, go/nogo memos, plan drafts,\n        EoI strawman sections, etc.)\n      - `:operator/rationale` — `String`; the operator's\n        plain-language reason. This is the load-bearing\n        provenance field; the projection's posterior always\n        traces back to an operator-asserted *because*.\n      - `:link/src`           — `String`; source entity. Only for\n        `link/asserted` events.\n      - `:link/dst`           — `String`; destination entity. Only\n        for `link/asserted` events."} {:name "EMISSION-DISCIPLINE", :name-key "emission-discipline", :slug "emission-discipline", :text "      - APPEND-ONLY. Events are never edited or deleted. Mistakes\n        are corrected by emitting a new event (typically\n        `state/refined` or `state/reopened`) that updates the\n        target's standing. The log is the authoritative source;\n        the projection derives current state from log replay.\n      - NO-OP DAYS EMIT NO CHECKPOINT. Daily cadence is fine when\n        there's a real state delta — even a single `state/refined`\n        on a single annotation counts. But synthetic checkpoints\n        (an empty `:emitted-events` vector emitted just to satisfy\n        a daily rhythm) are forbidden: they pollute the log with\n        non-events and degrade the projection's signal-to-noise.\n      - OPERATOR-ASSERTED BOUNDARY. Events are emitted at\n        checkpoint boundaries by an operator, not inferred from\n        prose by a processor. The event log is authored, not\n        extracted. (Pre-checkpoint events — interview notes, go/nogo\n        memos as they happen — are out of scope for v1.)\n      - VALIDATION BLOCKS, NOT WARNS. Validation errors against\n        this vocabulary block the checkpoint; the operator fixes\n        the EDN and re-runs. Silent acceptance of malformed events\n        would let drift land in the projection."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Provenance is the load-bearing part of completeness-not-closure\n    (the parent EoI mission's framing: the network becomes more\n    complete under use because evidence accumulates, not because\n    documents multiply). The projection's render answers \"what is\n    the standing of every EoI entity right now?\", but the trail it\n    carries answers \"how did it get there?\". Both are needed:\n    without standing, the operator can't act on current state;\n    without trail, the operator can't tell whether progress is real\n    or a confabulation. The eight-event vocabulary fixes a\n    discrimination-shape the projection can compute against; the\n    append-only discipline preserves the trail; the\n    explicit-emission rule keeps the authoring surface honest. The\n    neutral `state/*` namespace makes the vocabulary reusable\n    across entity classes — which is what lets substrate-2's\n    sorry-typing, M-a-sorry-enterprise's mining-track, and\n    `:sorry/r3d-per-entity-attribution` adopt the same vocabulary\n    rather than re-inventing it per consumer."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Step (b) commit: this flexiarg IS the deliverable. The\n         vocabulary, payload, and emission discipline are committed\n         here as the canonical design substrate. M-INC's\n         INSTANTIATE step (b) is satisfied by this file's\n         existence.]\n    next[Step (c) projection: derive an EDN schema file from this\n         flexiarg as needed by XTDB validators\n         (`state/<spawned|refined|...>` as a keyword schema, the\n         payload as a `clojure.spec`/`malli` schema, etc.). The\n         flexiarg remains authoritative; EDN is procedural\n         adaptation. War-machine vocabulary EDNs\n         (`futon5a/data/war-machine-*-vocabulary.edn`) are\n         structural references for the EDN form.]\n    next[First emission: M-interim-director CP-1 (futon7) is the\n         first concrete planned emitter, per M-INC §1 IDENTIFY.\n         The EoI engine\n         (`~/code/algorithms/eoi-engine.md`) is a candidate\n         secondary emitter — `eoi_instances[]` writes from engine\n         runs map cleanly onto `state/spawned` + `link/asserted`\n         events (e.g. parent/child instance relations as observed\n         in `vsat-poc-2026-q2-q3-scenario-c-vsatlatarium` 2026-05-25).]\n    next[Cross-mission consumers (per claude-1 downstream finding\n         2026-05-26): substrate-2 sorry-typing\n         (M-action-cost-modelling §3.2) adopts the 7-status\n         `state/*` set as `:sorry`-entity-status vocabulary;\n         M-a-sorry-enterprise mining-track resumes emit-into-substrate\n         using this vocabulary; `:sorry/r3d-per-entity-attribution`\n         (`futon2/data/sorrys.edn`) re-types as\n         `:mission`-scale, consuming this vocabulary as the shared\n         status surface.]\n    next[Granularity-enum extension (deferred to step (c)): EoI\n         engine runs produce carry-forward entries on parent\n         instances that fit closest to `:annotation` but are not\n         text-annotations. Resolution candidates: extend the enum\n         (`:tension`, `:tech-debt-entry`) OR add an\n         `:annotation/subtype` discriminator. Non-blocking for\n         step (b); resolved at projection time when XTDB schemas\n         materialise.]"}], :conclusion "  Mission checkpoints update the interest-network not by mutating EoI\n  entities directly but by emitting typed state-transition events\n  that target them. This flexiarg declares the v1 vocabulary: eight\n  event types (seven `state/*` events plus `link/asserted`) and a\n  payload schema. The event log is append-only and\n  provenance-bearing; the posterior projection derives entity\n  standing by replay. The vocabulary is neutral — the same eight\n  events apply to essay annotations, institution-objects, basins,\n  and composite-arrow edges without entity-class-specific slang.", :directives {:audience "operators authoring mission checkpoints that need to update the standing of EoI entities; engine code that emits state-transition events; projection code that derives posterior state from the event log; downstream consumers (substrate-2 sorry-typing, M-a-sorry-enterprise mining-track, :sorry/r3d-per-entity-attribution) that need a shared entity-status vocabulary", :factor "Investigation (dhammavicaya)", :keywords ["vocabulary" "state-transition" "checkpoint" "interest-network" "posterior-update" "EoI" "event-log" "append-only" "provenance" "state-spawned" "state-refined" "state-strengthened" "state-addressed" "state-falsified" "state-foreclosed" "state-reopened" "link-asserted"], :references ["structure/unresolved-tensions-at-closure" "structure/whose-question-is-this" "writing-coherence/name-what-you-drop"], :sigils ["⌒/態"], :title "What state-transition is this checkpoint asserting against an interest-network entity?", :tone "declarative; vocabulary first"}, :id "structure/interest-event-vocabulary", :keywords ["vocabulary" "state-transition" "checkpoint" "interest-network" "posterior-update" "EoI" "event-log" "append-only" "provenance" "state-spawned" "state-refined" "state-strengthened" "state-addressed" "state-falsified" "state-foreclosed" "state-reopened" "link-asserted"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["structure/unresolved-tensions-at-closure" "structure/whose-question-is-this" "writing-coherence/name-what-you-drop"], :scores {}, :sigils ["⌒/態"], :source-path "library/structure/interest-event-vocabulary.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "What state-transition is this checkpoint asserting against an interest-network entity?", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  The operator's metabolic state in the stack is the integral of\n  (block-closures-at-hinges − allostatic-load-symptoms) over time.\n  Block-closures award mana; allostatic-load symptoms drain it.\n  The invariant is that net balance remains non-negative under\n  sustained operation. Symptom channels are plural — working-tree\n  dirt, mission-proliferation, session-sprawl, ahead-of-origin-\n  without-disposition, untriaged-evidence — but they share a shape\n  and an antidote: **perceive the hinge, close the block**."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "    Allostasis is the cumulative cost of physiological adaptation\n    to stressors. It is not a defect — it is the theoretical\n    precursor to resolution. Dissonance in music is not noise; it is\n    the structure that gives consonance its meaning. In a futon\n    stack, allostatic load is the rising tension that makes block-\n    closure satisfying and noticeable. Without load, closure is\n    unmotivated; without closure, load becomes degradation.\n\n    Complexity, similarly, is not a fault to be minimised. A working\n    tree in flux is the right state to be in *while doing the work*.\n    What the invariant guards is not the absence of complexity but\n    the timely return to a state where the substrate's transactional\n    discipline holds."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The operator is operating a futon stack with pluri-channel work\n    (working trees, missions, sessions, branches, evidence streams),\n    and is at risk of either (a) accumulating load past the point of\n    coherent resolution, or (b) discharging load prematurely into\n    rubber-stamp closures."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    No single channel is the whole story. Working-tree dirtiness is\n    one symptom-channel; mission-proliferation is another; session-\n    sprawl is another. Each drains by its own dynamic. Aggregating\n    them into a single \"you're tired\" signal preserves the felt\n    sense; separating them allows targeted resolution. Both readings\n    are needed.\n\n    Equally: not every block can or should close immediately.\n    Some load is the *passion* phase of a block in flight — the\n    operator is doing the actual work that the block will record.\n    Pressure is welcome there; the invariant is not \"minimise\n    pressure\" but \"perceive when pressure becomes overdue.\""} {:name "ANTI-PATTERNS", :name-key "anti-patterns", :slug "anti-patterns", :text "      - **Automation-of-closure**: forcing block-closure on a timer.\n        Resolves dissonance without it being earned; produces hollow\n        consonance. (jj-style; rubber-stamp commits.)\n      - **Single-channel obsession**: tracking only working-tree\n        pressure while letting mission-proliferation accumulate\n        quietly. Drain is multi-channel; perception must be too.\n      - **Mana-hoarding**: closing blocks for the award without the\n        work. Each block-closure must rest on actual passion;\n        otherwise the award is counterfeit, the system's metabolic\n        sense miscalibrates, and real load goes unflagged.\n      - **Dissonance-aversion**: forcing premature consonance. The\n        operator commits to \"feel done\" rather than because the work\n        is done. Music-theoretically: trite.\n      - **Dissonance-luxuriance**: holding load indefinitely\n        because \"the work isn't ready.\" At some point dissonance\n        becomes noise; the block must close even if imperfectly,\n        then the next block can refine."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Track the per-channel drain rate. Surface to the operator when\n    a channel approaches hinge-cadence overdue. Award mana at block-\n    closure-at-recognised-hinge (only with the passion phase\n    completed). Visualise the balance trajectory so the operator\n    can see whether the system is accumulating or recovering.\n    Distinguish \"open block in passion\" from \"open block past\n    hinge-cadence.\""} {:name "DIAGNOSTIC", :name-key "diagnostic", :slug "diagnostic", :text "      - Many open missions, many open sessions, dirty working trees:\n        multi-channel drain. Operator likely feels tired; confirm via\n        proprioceptive check. Targeted hinge-perception, channel by\n        channel.\n      - All channels low, blocks closing regularly: net mana positive;\n        the system is in healthy oscillation.\n      - One channel pinned high, others quiet: targeted resolution is\n        called for — perceive the specific hinge that's overdue and\n        slip the blade.\n      - Mana awards landing without prior load: counterfeit. Closures\n        are happening without real work. Investigate (the awards may\n        be from the wrong phase — e.g., closing a block before the\n        passion phase has run).\n      - Operator reports tiredness but futon-sync looks clean: the\n        drain may be coming from a channel not currently measured\n        (untriaged-evidence, attention-on-too-many-PRs, etc.).\n        Widen the drain-channel inventory."} {:name "ON-DISSONANCE-AS-PRECURSOR", :name-key "on-dissonance-as-precursor", :slug "on-dissonance-as-precursor", :text "      Consonance without prior dissonance is unmoving. A block-closure\n      without prior accumulation is a non-event. The pressure function\n      does not say \"minimise load\"; it says: when load approaches the\n      natural resolution-point (a hinge), the operator should perceive\n      it and act. The pleasure of the resolution scales with the load\n      that preceded it. This is one reason cleanup blocks (after a\n      long open passage) feel satisfying in a way that a hundred\n      half-formed commits cannot."} {:name "ON-PASSION-AS-A-PHASE", :name-key "on-passion-as-a-phase", :slug "on-passion-as-a-phase", :text "      The five-phase block lifecycle (identification → specification →\n      elaboration → passion → closure; structure/block-as-futonic-\n      revolution) names *passion* explicitly. This is the embodied-\n      effort phase — where adenosine actually accumulates, where work\n      actually gets done. Without naming this phase, the model would\n      suggest that closure happens through pure perception. Real\n      work has cost; the cost is the precondition for real\n      resolution."} {:name "ON-PLURI-CHANNEL-HARMOLODICS", :name-key "on-pluri-channel-harmolodics", :slug "on-pluri-channel-harmolodics", :text "      Working-tree dirt, mission-proliferation, session-sprawl,\n      branch-bloat, untriaged-evidence — each a voice in a multi-\n      voice composition. Harmolodics (Ornette Coleman) holds that\n      harmony, melody, and rhythm are equally weighted; no single\n      line dominates. The futonic invariant family has the same\n      structure. Watching only one drain (e.g., working-tree\n      dirtiness) while the others accumulate is monophonic\n      listening."} {:name "REFERENCES", :name-key "references", :slug "references", :text "      - structure/block-as-futonic-revolution — defines the unit that awards mana on close.\n      - structure/cook-ting — the hinge-recognition that makes the close effortless.\n      - structure/hinge-point — the structural slot where a hinge appears.\n      - invariant-coherence/drain-channel-shape — how multiple bounded-disposition siblings share the drain-channel shape.\n      - futon-theory/futonic-logic — the recognition loop the closure traverses."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Sleep pressure is real. Music-theoretically, dissonance held\n    too long without resolution becomes noise; resolved too early,\n    music becomes trite. The same in stacks: allostatic load held\n    too long without block-closure becomes substrate corruption\n    (the M-live-geometric-stack manifold can't read it; agents and\n    other operators see stale state). Resolved too early, blocks\n    become hollow — work is \"committed\" without being done. The\n    mana economy is the felt-sense interface to that physical\n    reality."}], :conclusion "  The operator's metabolic state in the stack is the integral of\n  (block-closures-at-hinges − allostatic-load-symptoms) over time.\n  Block-closures award mana; allostatic-load symptoms drain it.\n  The invariant is that net balance remains non-negative under\n  sustained operation. Symptom channels are plural — working-tree\n  dirt, mission-proliferation, session-sprawl, ahead-of-origin-\n  without-disposition, untriaged-evidence — but they share a shape\n  and an antidote: **perceive the hinge, close the block**.", :directives {:audience "mission operators, anyone watching their own metabolic state in the stack", :factor "Equanimity (upekkhā) — neither force closure nor over-extend the open block", :keywords ["mana" "allostasis" "balance" "drain" "award" "dissonance" "resolution" "complexity" "harmolodics"], :references ["structure/block-as-futonic-revolution" "structure/cook-ting" "structure/hinge-point" "invariant-coherence/drain-channel-shape" "futon-theory/futonic-logic"], :sigils ["⚖️/熱"], :style "pattern", :title "Mana Allostasis: Dissonance Is the Precursor to Resolution", :tone "analytic-poetic"}, :id "structure/mana-allostasis", :keywords ["mana" "allostasis" "balance" "drain" "award" "dissonance" "resolution" "complexity" "harmolodics"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["structure/block-as-futonic-revolution" "structure/cook-ting" "structure/hinge-point" "invariant-coherence/drain-channel-shape" "futon-theory/futonic-logic"], :scores {}, :sigils ["⚖️/熱"], :source-path "library/structure/mana-allostasis.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Mana Allostasis: Dissonance Is the Precursor to Resolution", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "When two apparently-distinct quantities consistently covary or read as duals, model them as two PROJECTIONS of one underlying quantity — π₁(Q) and π₂(Q) — so their relationship becomes a structural fact about Q and its projection maps, not a coincidence to explain or a causal arrow to assert."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You hold two named observables — two metrics, two phenomena, two affects — that keep\n    moving together, trading off, or appearing as opposite faces of the same thing. You are tempted\n    to either (a) draw a causal arrow between them, or (b) shrug the covariance off as incidental."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "      Two (or more) quantities A and B covary, trade off, or present as a duality across many\n      instances, and you need a model that predicts their joint behaviour and tells you where to act."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "      A causal arrow A→B (or B→A) usually over-fits — it breaks on the cases where they decouple, and\n      it misnames the structure. Treating the covariance as coincidence is worse: it discards a real\n      regularity and leaves you intervening on shadows. Neither \"A causes B\" nor \"A and B are\n      unrelated\" is the right shape."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "      Posit a single latent quantity Q and two projection maps π₁, π₂ such that A = π₁(Q) and\n      B = π₂(Q). Model Q and the projections explicitly. Read the A–B relationship off the geometry of\n      (π₁, π₂): where the projections align, A and B covary; where they are orthogonal, A and B\n      decouple — and that decoupling is now PREDICTED, not anomalous. Route interventions and\n      predictions through Q: moving Q moves both projections coherently; acting on A alone is acting on\n      a shadow."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "      A shared latent source is the parsimonious explanation of covariance (one quantity, two shadows)\n      and turns an apparent duality into structure: the two faces are guaranteed related because they\n      are views of one thing, and guaranteed to decouple exactly where the projections diverge.\n      Crucially it relocates agency — you intervene on Q (which the projections obey) instead of\n      fighting one observable while the other springs back. The canonical instance: EOI, AIF surprise,\n      anamnesis (un-discharged tension), the operator's T, Alexander's wholeness, and Salingaros'\n      geometric tension are not six measures to reconcile pairwise but six projections of one\n      *aliveness*-quantity — so mana flows when anamnesis discharges by construction, not by lucky\n      correlation.\n\n@references-extra [\"Origin: the aliveness-synthesis (EOI/AIF/anamnesis/T/Alexander-wholeness/Salingaros-tension as projections of one aliveness-quantity). Cited as `structure/two-projections-of-one-quantity` in M-memes-arrows / E-mission-head ARGUE (2026-06) before it existed — a laundering instance the cascade caught (futon6 early-closures.md). Minted via WM pilot Turn 3, 2026-06-10, addressing sorry/pattern-two-projections-of-one-quantity.\"]"}], :conclusion "When two apparently-distinct quantities consistently covary or read as duals, model them as two PROJECTIONS of one underlying quantity — π₁(Q) and π₂(Q) — so their relationship becomes a structural fact about Q and its projection maps, not a coincidence to explain or a causal arrow to assert.", :directives {:audience "modellers, mission authors, theory-builders, anyone reconciling two measures that covary", :keywords ["projection" "latent quantity" "duality" "covariance" "aliveness" "mana" "anamnesis" "one-source" "unification" "shadow" "intervene-at-the-source"], :sigils ["☀/影"], :style "pattern", :title "Two Projections of One Quantity", :tone "foundational"}, :id "structure/two-projections-of-one-quantity", :keywords ["projection" "latent quantity" "duality" "covariance" "aliveness" "mana" "anamnesis" "one-source" "unification" "shadow" "intervene-at-the-source"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["☀/影"], :source-path "library/structure/two-projections-of-one-quantity.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Two Projections of One Quantity", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  A hinge-pool question. Plucked when a mission is approaching\n  closure. The question guards against premature closure — the\n  failure mode where the mission is closed because its state machine\n  is at its final node, not because the tensions it arose to hold\n  have finished doing their work. Sorry-preservation applied to\n  process: some tensions are supposed to stay unresolved, and the\n  mission's job is to preserve them, not to force them."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "The mission lifecycle makes closure feel mandatory. A\n    mission at DOCUMENT is supposed to DOCUMENT and finish. This is\n    fine when the tensions that motivated the mission have actually\n    been addressed. It is a category error when the tensions are\n    still live and the mission is being closed because closing is\n    the next state, not because the work is done. Closing a mission\n    whose tensions are live is the same move as canonicalising a\n    sorry into a proof before the sorry has been encountered as\n    a sorry — it destroys the very object the mission was for."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "The structural tension above manifests in the following concrete shapes (see substructure)."} {:name "ANTI-PATTERNS-IN-ANSWERS", :name-key "anti-patterns-in-answers", :slug "anti-patterns-in-answers", :text "      - \"No unresolved tensions\" — almost always a sign the question\n        has not been inhabited; go back to structure/whose-question-is-this\n      - Listing every minor loose end equally — (a), (b), and (c) are\n        structurally different and need different handling\n      - Treating (c) as a failure of the mission rather than as its gift\n      - Exporting everything as a sorry into a future mission that will\n        never be run — the sorry is structural, not a TODO"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Operate an explicit move on the named axis (see substructure below); steward the pattern against the declared discipline."} {:name "HOW-TO-ANSWER", :name-key "how-to-answer", :slug "how-to-answer", :text "      1. First person. What is still pulling against what that, if\n         resolved, would force a claim I cannot support from inside\n         this mission?\n      2. Distinguish three kinds of unresolved tension:\n         a. SOLVABLE-BUT-NOT-YET: the mission could resolve this if\n            it continued, and closing now would lose that chance. Do\n            not close; extend.\n         b. NOT-SOLVABLE-HERE: the tension is real but belongs to a\n            different mission or scope. Name it, export it as a sorry,\n            close this mission cleanly.\n         c. STRUCTURALLY-VOID: the tension is the sorry the mission\n            is supposed to preserve — collapsing it would destroy the\n            mission's qualitative object. Name it, do not force\n            resolution, close the mission with the void preserved in\n            the journal register.\n      3. Each kind wants a different action at the hinge. Misclassifying\n         (a) as (b) loses work; misclassifying (b) as (c) preserves\n         voids that should have been solved; misclassifying (c) as (a)\n         or (b) kills the Halliday object."} {:name "WHAT-IT-GUARDS-AGAINST", :name-key "what-it-guards-against", :slug "what-it-guards-against", :text "      - Schedule-driven closure (\"we've been at this long enough\")\n      - Artifact-equals-mission conflation (\"the WP is written, therefore\n        the mission is done\")\n      - Loss of sorry-preservation: collapsing the typed gap into a\n        prematurely-closed statement\n      - \"Success at the wrong exit\" — mission passed its state machine's\n        final node while producing no qualitative object"} {:name "WHEN-TO-PLUCK", :name-key "when-to-pluck", :slug "when-to-pluck", :text "      - At any hinge within two hinges of planned closure\n      - When the mission has produced its primary artifact and the\n        temptation is to declare victory\n      - When the operator notices relief at the prospect of closing,\n        which is sometimes appropriate and sometimes a drift signal\n      - When a reviewer or stakeholder is pressing for closure on a\n        schedule external to the mission's actual progress"} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    The mission that closes correctly is the mission that stops when\n    its work is done, where \"work\" includes tension-preservation as\n    a legitimate terminal state. Cook Ting's blade does not force a\n    final cut that the structure does not offer. A mission that has\n    reached a joint where the tensions are (a) still resolvable in\n    scope, (b) properly exported, or (c) structurally preserved —\n    and acts according to which of these it is — carves the bull\n    correctly."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[If (a): extend the mission, do not close]\n    next[If (b): export the tensions as new-mission seeds, close cleanly]\n    next[If (c): write the journal register's closure entry to name\n         the preserved tension, close the ledger cleanly, preserve\n         the void in the mission's permanent record]"}], :conclusion "  A hinge-pool question. Plucked when a mission is approaching\n  closure. The question guards against premature closure — the\n  failure mode where the mission is closed because its state machine\n  is at its final node, not because the tensions it arose to hold\n  have finished doing their work. Sorry-preservation applied to\n  process: some tensions are supposed to stay unresolved, and the\n  mission's job is to preserve them, not to force them.", :directives {:audience "operators at closure-adjacent hinges (ARGUE-exit, DOCUMENT-entry, or any hinge where the mission is being considered for completion)", :factor "Equanimity (upekkhā)", :keywords ["sorry" "closure" "void" "residual" "premature" "tension-preservation"], :references ["structure/cook-ting" "structure/hinge-point" "structure/what-problem-is-this-actually-solving" "futon-theory/sorry-preservation"], :sigils ["🕳️/隙"], :title "What Tensions Would We Leave Unresolved If We Closed Here?", :tone "void-preserving"}, :id "structure/unresolved-tensions-at-closure", :keywords ["sorry" "closure" "void" "residual" "premature" "tension-preservation"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["structure/cook-ting" "structure/hinge-point" "structure/what-problem-is-this-actually-solving" "futon-theory/sorry-preservation"], :scores {}, :sigils ["🕳️/隙"], :source-path "library/structure/unresolved-tensions-at-closure.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "What Tensions Would We Leave Unresolved If We Closed Here?", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  A hinge-pool question. Plucked when a mission is approaching a\n  significant commitment of resources or closure on a phase whose\n  framing may have drifted from the problem the mission originally\n  arose to address. The question guards against proxy-problem\n  substitution — the failure mode where the mission is progressing\n  efficiently at the wrong goal."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Missions naturally drift from their founding problem\n    into problems that are better-defined, better-tooled, or more\n    proximate to the operator's current competence. This drift is\n    often productive — the original problem may not have been\n    accurately described. But it is also the failure mode of \"success\n    at the wrong goal,\" which is indistinguishable from success\n    internally while being worthless externally."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "The structural tension above manifests in the following concrete shapes (see substructure)."} {:name "ANTI-PATTERNS-IN-ANSWERS", :name-key "anti-patterns-in-answers", :slug "anti-patterns-in-answers", :text "      - Describing the artifact, not the problem (\"We are producing a\n        pattern library\" — no, what problem does a pattern library solve?)\n      - Citing the mission doc's original Motivation verbatim. If the\n        mission has been running for any length of time, the Motivation\n        section is frozen at IDENTIFY time; the actual problem has\n        evolved (or should have). A verbatim citation is a drift signal.\n      - Moral claims as problem statements (\"We are doing this because\n        it is important\"). Importance is not a problem."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Operate an explicit move on the named axis (see substructure below); steward the pattern against the declared discipline."} {:name "HOW-TO-ANSWER", :name-key "how-to-answer", :slug "how-to-answer", :text "      1. First person. I-1 applied to humans: you answer, not an agent\n         writing as you.\n      2. One sentence if possible. If the answer takes a paragraph,\n         either the problem has drifted or the answer is a list of\n         several problems and the mission needs splitting.\n      3. Say what problem the mission is solving, not what it is doing.\n         \"We are building an X\" is not an answer. \"We are building an\n         X because the problem of Y cannot be addressed without it\" is.\n      4. If the honest answer is \"I no longer know,\" that is a valid\n         answer. The mission needs to stop at this hinge and re-identify\n         before proceeding."} {:name "WHAT-IT-GUARDS-AGAINST", :name-key "what-it-guards-against", :slug "what-it-guards-against", :text "      - Drift from founding problem to tractable proxy\n      - Mission-as-artefact-production rather than mission-as-problem-solution\n      - DOCUMENT-phase writing that reads well but makes no claim about\n        what changed because of the mission\n      - The hermit-trap: missions that become self-sustaining practices\n        rather than problem-solving vehicles"} {:name "WHEN-TO-PLUCK", :name-key "when-to-pluck", :slug "when-to-pluck", :text "      - At IDENTIFY-exit: is the problem as-described in the mission\n        doc the problem we are actually working on, or has the writing\n        process already substituted a more tractable proxy?\n      - At MAP-entry: the mapping exercise is about to consume real\n        effort. Is the target of the map the actual problem?\n      - At any hinge where the mission feels easy in a way the founding\n        tension did not warrant. Ease at a hinge often signals that the\n        hard problem has been silently replaced.\n      - When presenting the mission to a reader (UKRN-S WP insight): the\n        reader's first question will be \"what problem is this actually\n        solving?\" If the mission can't answer in one sentence without\n        hedging, the problem has drifted."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    The UKRN-S Working Paper reader's first question is always \"what\n    problem is this actually solving.\" Asking it inside the mission,\n    repeatedly, at hinges, is the practice that makes the eventual\n    Working Paper (or deliverable, or artifact) able to answer it\n    when the reader arrives. The question is not a check on the\n    mission; it is a shape of its competence."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[If this question has fired at three hinges with three\n         different answers, the mission has drifted and should split\n         or re-IDENTIFY]\n    next[If this question has fired at three hinges with the same\n         answer, the mission's problem-definition is stable and the\n         question can retire until the next major phase transition]"}], :conclusion "  A hinge-pool question. Plucked when a mission is approaching a\n  significant commitment of resources or closure on a phase whose\n  framing may have drifted from the problem the mission originally\n  arose to address. The question guards against proxy-problem\n  substitution — the failure mode where the mission is progressing\n  efficiently at the wrong goal.", :directives {:audience "mission operators at IDENTIFY-exit, MAP-entry, or any hinge where a proxy-problem risks substituting for the real one", :factor "Keen investigation (dhammavicaya)", :keywords ["problem" "proxy" "drift" "identification" "purpose" "scope"], :references ["structure/cook-ting" "structure/hinge-point" "structure/whose-question-is-this"], :sigils ["❓/問"], :title "What Problem Is This Actually Solving?", :tone "probing-diagnostic"}, :id "structure/what-problem-is-this-actually-solving", :keywords ["problem" "proxy" "drift" "identification" "purpose" "scope"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["structure/cook-ting" "structure/hinge-point" "structure/whose-question-is-this"], :scores {}, :sigils ["❓/問"], :source-path "library/structure/what-problem-is-this-actually-solving.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "What Problem Is This Actually Solving?", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Before answering any hinge-pool question, pause and ask whose\n  question it is. The question was drawn from a pool and is therefore\n  structurally available to anyone. The answer must be inhabited —\n  given in the operator's first person, about the mission the operator\n  is in, at the hinge the operator is at. An answer given in anyone\n  else's voice is a category error (I-1 applied to humans). An answer\n  given as if from outside the mission is extractive mode."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "The question-pool is public. The answers are not. A pool\n    question like \"what problem is this actually solving\" can be\n    plucked at any hinge by any operator in any mission — but the\n    answer is specific to this operator, this mission, this hinge.\n    The failure mode: an operator plucks a question and produces a\n    \"correct-sounding\" answer that would be defensible in a review\n    meeting but does not come from inside the mission. That answer\n    is extractive — it has used the question to produce a record\n    rather than to let the question do its holding-work."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "The structural tension above manifests in the following concrete shapes (see substructure)."} {:name "ANTI-PATTERNS-IN-ANSWERS", :name-key "anti-patterns-in-answers", :slug "anti-patterns-in-answers", :text "      - Generic best-practice answers (\"the stakeholders are X, Y, Z...\")\n      - Answers that could have been given before the mission started\n      - Answers written for a future reviewer rather than for the\n        mission's current direction\n      - Delegation: \"let me ask the agent to draft this and I'll edit\"\n        — this is the I-1 violation and the hinge-log must not contain\n        agent-drafted answers attributed to the operator"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Operate an explicit move on the named axis (see substructure below); steward the pattern against the declared discipline."} {:name "HOW-TO-ANSWER", :name-key "how-to-answer", :slug "how-to-answer", :text "      1. Notice whose voice is composing the proposed answer. Yours?\n         An imagined reader's? A previous consultant's? A template's?\n      2. If not yours: discard the proposed answer and sit with the\n         question again. The question was plucked to hold you in a\n         tension; if you have reached for someone else's voice, the\n         tension has escaped and needs re-engaging.\n      3. The answer may be shorter, less articulate, less defensible\n         than the voice-not-yours version. That is correct. Inhabited\n         answers are often smaller.\n      4. \"I don't know\" in your own voice is a better answer than any\n         articulate construction in someone else's."} {:name "WHAT-IT-GUARDS-AGAINST", :name-key "what-it-guards-against", :slug "what-it-guards-against", :text "      - Extractive-mode mission steering: producing records instead of\n        thinking\n      - Agent-impersonation at the journal register (the PAR/ledger\n        side is agent-legible; the hinge-log side is not, by I-1)\n      - The \"consultant voice\" failure: answers that would pass external\n        review but do no holding-work inside the mission\n      - Polishing over inhabitation: answers that are too finished to\n        be human data (cf. \"only an unfinished object is human data\")"} {:name "WHEN-TO-PLUCK-THIS-QUESTION", :name-key "when-to-pluck-this-question", :slug "when-to-pluck-this-question", :text "      - Before answering any other hinge-pool question that is drawn\n        from the pool and feels \"standard.\"\n      - When an operator notices themselves composing an answer rather\n        than finding one.\n      - When an agent is present in the mission and there is a temptation\n        to let the agent phrase the answer. (I-1 forbids. The agent can\n        scaffold the question; it cannot inhabit the answer.)\n      - When the answer, if read aloud, sounds like something a\n        consultant would say rather than something the operator would say."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    The MVSG claim is that the mission emerges as a qualitative object\n    when the registers are held distinct without collapse. The hinge-log\n    is the journal register; answers in it must be inhabited. Without\n    this question gating the others, \"what problem is this solving\"\n    degrades into boilerplate and the journal register collapses into\n    the ledger. With this question gating the others, the journal\n    stays protected, and the mission has a chance of producing the\n    qualitative object."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[If an operator finds this question firing at every hinge, the\n         inhabitation posture may be unstable — consider whether the\n         mission is being done in inhabited mode at all]\n    next[If this question never fires, either inhabitation is habituated\n         (good) or the pool is being filled with consultant-voice\n         answers without notice (bad) — audit by reading a recent\n         hinge-log entry aloud in your own voice and checking whether\n         it sounds like you]"}], :conclusion "  Before answering any hinge-pool question, pause and ask whose\n  question it is. The question was drawn from a pool and is therefore\n  structurally available to anyone. The answer must be inhabited —\n  given in the operator's first person, about the mission the operator\n  is in, at the hinge the operator is at. An answer given in anyone\n  else's voice is a category error (I-1 applied to humans). An answer\n  given as if from outside the mission is extractive mode.", :directives {:audience "operators at any hinge; especially when a question is being plucked that \"feels obvious\" or that the operator is reluctant to answer first-person", :factor "Mindfulness (sati)", :keywords ["inhabitation" "extraction" "voice" "posture" "delegation" "I-1"], :references ["structure/cook-ting" "structure/hinge-point" "agent/inhabited-mode" "gauntlet/aif-as-environment-not-instruction"], :sigils ["👤/誰"], :title "Whose Question Is This?", :tone "inhabited-reflective"}, :id "structure/whose-question-is-this", :keywords ["inhabitation" "extraction" "voice" "posture" "delegation" "I-1"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["structure/cook-ting" "structure/hinge-point" "agent/inhabited-mode" "gauntlet/aif-as-environment-not-instruction"], :scores {}, :sigils ["👤/誰"], :source-path "library/structure/whose-question-is-this.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Whose Question Is This?", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "At any stage where claims are first being put on the record — bootstrap stages of a system, ARGUE-phase missions, foundational sections of papers — claims must be derived from reasonable assumptions and present-tense observations, not speculated forward as downstream effects.  \"May foster X\" is not a claim; it is a hope dressed as one.  The discipline is: a claim belongs at the bootstrap if it follows from the bootstrap's own action under reasonable assumptions, not if it depends on a chain of effects the bootstrap has not yet produced.  The remedy when such a claim is detected is to delete it (most often), demote it to an open question or future-work note (sometimes), or replace it with the what-must-follow restatement it was gesturing at (rarely — usually the speculation was masking the absence of any derivable claim)."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "AI drafts and human first-drafts both tend to dress speculation as claim.  The hedge \"may\", the modifier \"potentially\", the verb \"foster\" or \"could lead to\" — each does argumentative work without paying for it.  In ARGUE-phase missions and in bootstrap-stage system descriptions, speculative claims pile up because the writer is working at the edge of what the system has done so far, and the speculation is filling space that empirical claim cannot yet fill.  Distinct from `unearned-essentially` (about unsupported strength claims) and `hedged-lift` (about hedging that does the lifting): here the failure is *temporal* — the claim depends on stages the document has not yet brought into being."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The pattern operates on the axis: claim ambition ↔ what the action under reasonable assumptions actually delivers.\n    Irreducible: a claim either follows from the action under reasonable assumptions, or it depends on a chain the document has not yet built.  No amount of hedging changes which of these is true."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Real costs: a strong document may say less in early sections than the writer wants — empirically derivable claims at bootstrap are often modest and obvious.  Restraining the claim to the empirical scope at hand can feel anticlimactic; permitting speculation feels generous, but creates load-bearing reliance on claims the document has not paid for.\n    Active dynamic: the temptation grows whenever the writer wants the early section to feel weighty.  The cure for the section feeling thin is not speculation; it is moving more empirically derivable content into the early section, or accepting that the section is short."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "      - May-fostering: \"may foster\" / \"could lead to\" / \"has the potential to\" — each is the present participle of a verb the document has not earned.\n      - Downstream borrowing: claims at stage N depend on stages N+1 or N+2 having occurred; when challenged, the writer points forward rather than at what the action has produced.\n      - Hedge as warrant: a hedge (\"perhaps\", \"in principle\") is doing the work an argument would do — the reader is supposed to read the hedge as care rather than as the absence of derivation.\n      - Persuasion-by-aspiration: the claim is rhetorically attractive and the writer treats this attractiveness as warrant."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Delete or restrict the claim; replace with what must follow; or demote to open question."} {:name "COMPOSITIONS", :name-key "compositions", :slug "compositions", :text "      (1) Delete and stop: the simplest move.  If the claim does not follow from what the action does, remove it.  The remaining sentence is often short and sufficient.  \"This produces trainer-level visibility.\" is an entire claim.\n      (2) Restate what must follow: replace \"may foster X\" with the empirical claim it was reaching for.  If sharing evidence \"may foster ground-up demand for community infrastructure\", what *must* follow under reasonable assumptions is that trainers see findings; the demand-for-infrastructure claim depends on additional unbuilt machinery and should not be made here.\n      (3) Demote to open question: if the speculation flags a genuine downstream variable the work needs to track, move it to a separate \"open questions\" or \"what we will be watching\" section.  The demotion preserves the writer's intuition without letting the speculation pose as a derived claim.\n      (4) Audit by must-follow test: read the claim and ask, \"what would have to be the case for this to follow from the action just described, under reasonable assumptions?\"  If the answer requires additional steps the document has not specified, the claim is speculative.  If the answer is \"only the action just described, and nothing more\", the claim is empirical and stays."} {:name "CHECK", :name-key "check", :slug "check", :text "      Engaged when every claim in the section can be derived from the action just described, under reasonable assumptions, with no appeal to stages or machinery the document has not yet built.  Failing when claims use \"may\", \"could\", \"has the potential to\", or \"may foster\" as warrant, or when the writer would, if challenged, defend the claim by pointing forward rather than by pointing at what the action makes inevitable."}], :conclusion "At any stage where claims are first being put on the record — bootstrap stages of a system, ARGUE-phase missions, foundational sections of papers — claims must be derived from reasonable assumptions and present-tense observations, not speculated forward as downstream effects.  \"May foster X\" is not a claim; it is a hope dressed as one.  The discipline is: a claim belongs at the bootstrap if it follows from the bootstrap's own action under reasonable assumptions, not if it depends on a chain of effects the bootstrap has not yet produced.  The remedy when such a claim is detected is to delete it (most often), demote it to an open question or future-work note (sometimes), or replace it with the what-must-follow restatement it was gesturing at (rarely — usually the speculation was masking the absence of any derivable claim).", :directives {:audience "system designers, mission authors, working-paper drafters, ARGUE-phase reviewers", :keywords ["argue-phase" "empirical-claim" "must-follow" "reasonable-assumptions" "bootstrap-discipline" "futon-mission-lifecycle" "may-fostering"], :references ["futon-theory/futonic-logic," "futon-theory/mission-lifecycle," "writing-coherence/unearned-essentially," "writing-coherence/hedged-lift"], :style "pattern", :title "Argue Empirically, Not Persuasively", :tone "technical"}, :id "system-coherence/argue-empirically-not-persuasively", :keywords ["argue-phase" "empirical-claim" "must-follow" "reasonable-assumptions" "bootstrap-discipline" "futon-mission-lifecycle" "may-fostering"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["futon-theory/futonic-logic," "futon-theory/mission-lifecycle," "writing-coherence/unearned-essentially," "writing-coherence/hedged-lift"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/system-coherence/argue-empirically-not-persuasively.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Argue Empirically, Not Persuasively", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "An argument that supports a decision must be specified as a system: a named outside (the reader and the decision the argument couples to), an inside (the claim+evidence structure), a boundary (what the argument settles versus what it hands off), and an active surface (what action the argument licenses once it has landed).  The antipattern is to specify the argument as a coverage matrix — layered enumeration of open questions, findings, and closure-paths that all point inward at other items in the same enumeration.  An argument with no outside has no decision to couple to; the paper drafted from such a spec inherits the same shape, becoming a section-per-item exercise in internal consistency rather than a coupled support for a live choice."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "An argument-spec is the upstream artifact from which a paper, memo, or recommendation gets drafted.  If the spec is shaped as a layered enumeration — questions inside layers inside argument-layers, each item carrying a `:closure-path` to other items in the same spec — the drafting agent will faithfully reproduce that shape in prose.  The result is the surface failures observed downstream in `plan-as-enumeration` (counted headings, calendar-paginated milestones, lists where the count is the perceptual unit) and `triad-inflation` (rule-of-three padding).  Those are symptoms; the upstream cause is a spec that does not distinguish the regions of a coherent system from items in a checklist.\n\nThe positive frame draws on the Active Inference wiring diagram (see `futon5/docs/chapter0-aif-as-wiring-diagram`): a system has five regions — environment, sensory surface, internal states, active surface, preferences — joined by typed interfaces, with timescale separations that prevent fast dynamics from corrupting slow structure.  Applied to argumentation (the AIF-squared move: Active Inference Framework × Argumentation Interchange Framework):\n- Outside (environment): the reader-and-the-decision the argument is addressed to.\n- Sensory surface: what the reader brings in — prior beliefs, sector context, existing commitments.\n- Internal states: the claim+evidence structure; this is what coverage-checklist specs already capture.\n- Active surface: what operational move the argument licenses the reader to make.\n- Preferences/structure: the glacial commitments (audience identity, sector frame, funder identity) that shape what counts as a viable conclusion.\n\nA coverage-checklist spec captures only the third region and treats it as the whole.  Specifying as a system means making the other four explicit."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The pattern operates on the axis: internal coverage completeness <-/-> coupling to the external decision.\n    Irreducible: either the argument's structure makes its boundary and active surface visible as named regions, or the structure consists of internal items with closure-paths pointing only inward, and the boundary becomes implicit."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Real costs: specifying the boundary forces the writer to declare what the argument cannot settle — which feels like ceding ground before the argument even starts.  Specifying the active surface forces the writer to name what action the reader should take, which exposes the recommendation to challenge before the warrant has been laid out.  The coverage-checklist form preserves a comfortable ambiguity: every question is \"in scope\" and every closure-path lands internally, so the argument cannot be charged either with omission or with over-reach.\n    Active dynamic: when the argument-spec is built collaboratively or evolved across drafts, items accumulate.  Each new finding becomes a new layer entry; each new objection becomes a new question.  Without an explicit boundary, there is no principled rule for excluding an item, so the spec keeps growing — and the paper drafted from it keeps growing in proportion.  After-the-fact cut manifests (records of which items were dropped from the prose between drafts) are what an external boundary looks like once it has been imposed retroactively; a boundary specified at spec-time would have prevented most of those items from entering in the first place."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "      - Layers all the way down: nested layers (questions inside layers inside argument-layers) with no rule for what stops the recursion.\n      - Closure-paths point inward only: every `:closure-path` references other items in the same spec; nothing points at the reader's decision or at an external evidence source the reader could check independently.\n      - Boundary by implicature: what's in scope is named, but what's out of scope is left implicit.  The reader cannot tell what the argument refuses to settle.\n      - Active surface conflated with internal layer: the recommended action is named as another layer item (\"year-1 launch\") rather than as the system's coupling-to-the-world.\n      - Timescale flattening: fast-changing evidence (quarterly delivery counts) and glacial commitments (sector identity) are treated as co-equal layer items rather than as different-rate inputs to the system.\n      - Coverage-as-warrant: the spec implies that having an item for every question means the argument is sound, when it only means the questions have been listed.\n      - Spec form propagates downstream: when the spec is enumerative, the paper is enumerative; when the spec is system-shaped, the paper has a chance of being system-shaped.  The reverse repair is harder — fixing prose after enumeration has set in is mostly cosmetic; fixing the spec changes what gets drafted from it."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Specify the argument as a five-region wiring diagram before the layered content accumulates."} {:name "COMPOSITIONS", :name-key "compositions", :slug "compositions", :text "      (1) Name the outside first: who the argument is addressed to, what decision they face, what they will do with the argument once they have read it.  This is the Markov-blanket cut between argument and world.\n      (2) Name the active surface explicitly: declare what operational moves the argument licenses (e.g., \"adopt the five-role design\", \"fund a three-year bridge\", \"stand the operation down at Year 2\").  The active surface is not a layer item; it is the argument's coupling to the world, and should be named as its own region of the spec.\n      (3) Declare the boundary: what does the argument settle, and what does it hand off to other arguments (companion papers, indicators work, future evidence waves)?  An argument with no declared boundary is an argument that tries to cover everything, which is the spec-level source of Borgesian taxonomic accretion in prose.\n      (4) Separate timescales: tag evidence-inputs by timescale (fast / medium / slow / glacial).  When the argument depends on slow-moving structure (sector frame, funder identity), name that dependency rather than burying it in a layer alongside fast-moving evidence.\n      (5) Let closure-paths point outward where appropriate: some closure-paths land internally (the design closes question Q2 via role configuration), but at least some must land externally (the recipient-level evaluation wave closes Q1 via empirical evidence the reader can independently check).  An argument whose closure-paths point only inward is internally complete but externally suspended.\n      (6) Apply downstream prose-side discipline second: once the spec is system-shaped, patterns like `plan-as-enumeration`, `triad-inflation`, and `name-the-decision-before-the-framework` become much easier to keep clean, because the prose now has a non-list shape to mirror.  Prose-only de-AI-ification passes (see `algorithms/de-ai-ify.md`) cannot recover what the spec form did not provide."} {:name "CHECK", :name-key "check", :slug "check", :text "      Engaged when the argument-spec's structure makes the outside, the boundary, and the active surface visible as named regions — not as layer items competing for attention with internal claim-evidence content.  A reader inspecting the spec should be able to answer, without consulting the prose: who is this addressed to, what does it license them to do, what does it refuse to settle.  Failing when the spec consists of layered enumeration with closure-paths pointing inward, and the boundary and active surface are recoverable only by reading the prose drafted from the spec.  Diagnostic question: if a reader saw the argument-spec but not the paper, could they tell what the paper licenses the reader to do?  If the only answer is \"ratify the recommendation listed in the spec's `(recommendation-the-paper-supports)` slot\", the argument has no active surface — the recommendation is a static label, not a coupling."}], :conclusion "An argument that supports a decision must be specified as a system: a named outside (the reader and the decision the argument couples to), an inside (the claim+evidence structure), a boundary (what the argument settles versus what it hands off), and an active surface (what action the argument licenses once it has landed).  The antipattern is to specify the argument as a coverage matrix — layered enumeration of open questions, findings, and closure-paths that all point inward at other items in the same enumeration.  An argument with no outside has no decision to couple to; the paper drafted from such a spec inherits the same shape, becoming a section-per-item exercise in internal consistency rather than a coupled support for a live choice.", :directives {:audience "system designers, mission authors, working-paper drafters, programme leads, reviewers", :keywords ["argument" "argument-spec" "coverage-checklist" "system-specification" "markov-blanket" "active-inference" "AIF" "AIF-squared" "argumentation-interchange" "generative-model" "active-surface" "boundary" "closure-path"], :references ["system-coherence/plan-as-enumeration" "system-coherence/name-the-decision-before-the-framework" "system-coherence/bind-open-questions-to-closure-mechanisms" "system-coherence/turn-design-into-checks" "writing-coherence/triad-inflation" "writing-coherence/meta-title"], :style "pattern", :title "Specify the Argument as a System, Not a Coverage Matrix", :tone "technical"}, :id "system-coherence/argument-as-coverage-checklist-vs-system-specification", :keywords ["argument" "argument-spec" "coverage-checklist" "system-specification" "markov-blanket" "active-inference" "AIF" "AIF-squared" "argumentation-interchange" "generative-model" "active-surface" "boundary" "closure-path"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["system-coherence/plan-as-enumeration" "system-coherence/name-the-decision-before-the-framework" "system-coherence/bind-open-questions-to-closure-mechanisms" "system-coherence/turn-design-into-checks" "writing-coherence/triad-inflation" "writing-coherence/meta-title"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/system-coherence/argument-as-coverage-checklist-vs-system-specification.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Specify the Argument as a System, Not a Coverage Matrix", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "An open question belongs in a decision-supporting document only if it is bound to a named closure mechanism.  \"This remains to be determined\" is not enough.  The document must say how the uncertainty closes: by a role, an evidence wave, a governance event, a threshold, or some other explicit mechanism.  Otherwise the question is not being managed; it is being parked."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Operational writing cannot eliminate uncertainty, but it can type it.  The failure is not that a document contains unresolved questions; the failure is that the reader cannot tell whether those questions are expected to close through ordinary system operation or whether they are simply deferred indefinitely.  Unbound uncertainty accumulates as ambient fog around the decision and makes the design look softer than it is."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The pattern operates on the axis: acknowledged uncertainty <-/-> resolvable uncertainty.\n    Irreducible: a question is either attached to a mechanism that can close it, or it is only being named as a gesture of conscientiousness."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Real costs: binding a question to a closure mechanism forces the writer to expose ownership, timing, and evidence standards.  This can reveal that the system does not yet know how it would learn what it claims it needs to know.  Writers often prefer to preserve optionality by leaving the question open-ended.\n    Active dynamic: every \"we will need to learn\", \"future work\", or \"this depends on context\" sentence is a likely site of slippage from typed uncertainty into unmanaged uncertainty."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "      - Parked uncertainty: a live question is named but no actor or process is assigned to close it.\n      - Future-work haze: the document says the issue will be examined later without saying by whom or through what event.\n      - Governance vacuum: the need for a decision is acknowledged, but no forum or cadence exists in which the decision would actually be made.\n      - Evidence without landing zone: the document calls for data collection but never says what decision that evidence would unlock or revise."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Type the uncertainty and attach it to closure."} {:name "COMPOSITIONS", :name-key "compositions", :slug "compositions", :text "      (1) Name the closure mechanism: say \"resolved by annual review\", \"closed by evaluator evidence wave\", \"owned by the bearer role\", or equivalent.\n      (2) Couple question to consequence: make explicit what becomes decidable once the question closes, so the reader sees why the uncertainty matters.\n      (3) State the holding rule: if the question is unresolved now, say what the system does in the meantime rather than leaving action suspended.\n      (4) Delete false openness: if no closure mechanism can be named, either redesign the system so one exists or stop presenting the issue as a managed open question."} {:name "CHECK", :name-key "check", :slug "check", :text "      Engaged when every open question in the document is paired with a visible closure path and an interim operating rule.  Failing when uncertainty is acknowledged rhetorically but not wired into any role, cadence, threshold, or decision path."}], :conclusion "An open question belongs in a decision-supporting document only if it is bound to a named closure mechanism.  \"This remains to be determined\" is not enough.  The document must say how the uncertainty closes: by a role, an evidence wave, a governance event, a threshold, or some other explicit mechanism.  Otherwise the question is not being managed; it is being parked.", :directives {:audience "system designers, mission authors, working-paper drafters, reviewers", :keywords ["open-question" "closure" "governance" "evidence-wave" "role" "resolution-path"], :references ["system-coherence/name-the-decision-before-the-framework" "system-coherence/argue-empirically-not-persuasively" "writing-coherence/hedged-lift"], :style "pattern", :title "Bind Open Questions to Closure Mechanisms", :tone "technical"}, :id "system-coherence/bind-open-questions-to-closure-mechanisms", :keywords ["open-question" "closure" "governance" "evidence-wave" "role" "resolution-path"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["system-coherence/name-the-decision-before-the-framework" "system-coherence/argue-empirically-not-persuasively" "writing-coherence/hedged-lift"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/system-coherence/bind-open-questions-to-closure-mechanisms.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Bind Open Questions to Closure Mechanisms", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Raw delivery counts do not warrant institutional-effect claims until they are corrected by denominators matched to the population that would need to be reached for the effect to be plausible.  A numerator without a relevant denominator tells you activity, not reach; and reach, not activity alone, is the minimum precondition for institution-level change claims."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Training reports, programme updates, and working papers often slide from \"we delivered N sessions\" or \"N people attended\" to implications about culture change, community uptake, or institutional effect.  This is a scale error.  The same numerator can mean saturation in one setting and trivial penetration in another.  The denominator is not a statistical ornament; it is what gives the number argumentative meaning."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The pattern operates on the axis: activity count <-/-> plausible reach.\n    Irreducible: either the denominator is relevant to the affected population and the effect claim can be scaled accordingly, or the claim outruns what the count can support."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Real costs: relevant denominators are often harder to gather, messier than the delivery count, and may reveal that a proud-looking numerator is organisationally thin.  Writers therefore prefer counts because counts are clean and flattering.  But effect claims built on flattering numerators are precisely what denominator discipline exists to stop.\n    Active dynamic: the slippage appears whenever a programme wants to infer institutional consequence from centrally visible delivery metrics."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "      - Numerator triumphalism: a large count is presented as self-interpreting.\n      - Denominator mismatch: the denominator used is too broad, too narrow, or simply unrelated to the population whose change is being claimed.\n      - Reach/effect collapse: modest exposure is narrated as if it already constituted institutional transformation.\n      - Unique-person ambiguity: attendance or touch counts are used as if they were confirmed counts of distinct individuals reached."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Re-express activity as proportionate reach before making effect claims."} {:name "COMPOSITIONS", :name-key "compositions", :slug "compositions", :text "      (1) Identify the affected population: academic staff, PGRs, the combined research community, or another population that matches the claim being made.\n      (2) Compute the reach proportion on that denominator and state any uncertainty about uniqueness or completeness.\n      (3) Use the corrected number to narrow the claim: low reach may still justify local learning claims, but not institution-shaping ones.\n      (4) Preserve asymmetry: low reach can often falsify strong effect claims; high reach does not by itself prove effect, but it clears one necessary precondition."} {:name "CHECK", :name-key "check", :slug "check", :text "      Engaged when delivery metrics are consistently paired with relevant denominators and the prose distinguishes activity from plausible reach.  Failing when counts are left to imply significance on their own or when the denominator does not match the level of effect being claimed."}], :conclusion "Raw delivery counts do not warrant institutional-effect claims until they are corrected by denominators matched to the population that would need to be reached for the effect to be plausible.  A numerator without a relevant denominator tells you activity, not reach; and reach, not activity alone, is the minimum precondition for institution-level change claims.", :directives {:audience "system designers, mission authors, working-paper drafters, reviewers", :keywords ["denominator" "reach" "effect-claim" "scale" "exposure" "institutional-change"], :references ["system-coherence/argue-empirically-not-persuasively" "writing-coherence/unearned-essentially" "writing-coherence/hedged-lift"], :style "pattern", :title "Correct Effect Claims with Relevant Denominators", :tone "technical"}, :id "system-coherence/correct-effect-claims-with-relevant-denominators", :keywords ["denominator" "reach" "effect-claim" "scale" "exposure" "institutional-change"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["system-coherence/argue-empirically-not-persuasively" "writing-coherence/unearned-essentially" "writing-coherence/hedged-lift"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/system-coherence/correct-effect-claims-with-relevant-denominators.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Correct Effect Claims with Relevant Denominators", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "When a measure aggregates across substantively different sub-categories — training topic, audience community, discipline, intervention type, recipient role — the aggregate measure conceals the actionable heterogeneity that the design recommendation actually depends on.  Effect claims and design prescriptions should be made at the facet level where the underlying causal story holds, and aggregated only when the aggregation is interpretively defensible.  Aggregating before faceting flatters the weaker case, ranks unlike entities as if they were comparable, and produces uniform recommendations across facets that need different interventions."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Aggregate measures are easier to gather, easier to present, and easier to compare than facet-level ones.  A single number per institution feels orderly; a vector of numbers per institution feels messy.  But the orderliness is paid for in actionable lift: the design that follows from a flat per-institution number is necessarily blind to within-institution heterogeneity.  Two institutions with the same headline reach can have entirely different facet structures (5% reach in one community with strong uptake vs 40% reach in another with weak uptake), and any recommendation that treats them alike is silently betting that the facet structure does not matter.  When the rest of the document's evidence says it does, that bet is incoherent."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The pattern operates on the axis: aggregate convenience <-/-> facet-level fidelity.\n    Irreducible: either the measure is genuinely homogeneous within the aggregate, or the aggregate is concealing facet-level structure that the design recommendation depends on."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Real costs: faceting requires more data structure, more interpretive care, and longer analysis cycles.  It also exposes uncertainty that the aggregate hid.  Writers and analysts often resist faceting because aggregate numbers look cleaner and are easier to put into a slide.  But the cost of *not* faceting is paid downstream as recommendations that don't fit any actual sub-population.\n    Active dynamic: the slippage appears whenever a programme's reporting infrastructure was set up before the design recommendation was sharpened.  The reporting infrastructure produces aggregates because they were what the earlier purpose required.  When the design recommendation later turns on facet-level questions, the aggregates are pressed into service anyway."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "      - Aggregate-saturation conflation: a strong outcome in one facet plus a weak outcome in another are reported as a single average that flatters the weaker case and misrepresents the stronger.\n      - Like-with-unlike comparison: institutions or interventions are ranked on aggregates without checking whether their underlying facet populations are comparable; the ranking misleads.\n      - Facet-blind recommendation: the design's prescription is uniform across facets that need different interventions, because the aggregate cannot see them.\n      - Reporting-infrastructure inertia: the team continues to report at the aggregate level because that is what the existing template captures, even after the design recommendation has shifted to a facet-level question.\n      - Premature roll-up in dashboards: monitoring tooling collapses facet-level signals into a single index for display purposes, then that index becomes the operational measure."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Facet the measure where the causal story differs; aggregate only after interpretive verification."} {:name "COMPOSITIONS", :name-key "compositions", :slug "compositions", :text "      (1) Identify the relevant facets: which sub-categories does the design recommendation actually distinguish between?  Common candidates are intervention type, recipient population, discipline area, organisational sub-unit, or temporal phase.\n      (2) Restructure measurement at the facet level: data capture, reporting templates, and analysis units should carry facet tags from the point of capture.  Retrofitting aggregates after the fact is much harder than tagging at source.\n      (3) Make recommendations at the facet level: when the design's claim is \"this works for X but not Y\", state that explicitly rather than as a population-level average.  When the claim genuinely is population-level, defend why aggregation is interpretively warranted.\n      (4) Test aggregation defensibility: before rolling facet-level signals up to a single number, ask whether a reader would draw the same operational conclusion from the aggregate as from the facet-level data.  If not, the aggregate is hiding the actionable signal.\n      (5) Compose with denominator-discipline: facet-level reach claims still need facet-level denominators (per audience, per discipline) to carry effect claims.  Facet-before-aggregating and `correct-effect-claims-with-relevant-denominators` operate together: the first asks \"what unit\", the second asks \"what scale\"."} {:name "CHECK", :name-key "check", :slug "check", :text "      Engaged when measurement, reporting, and design recommendations operate at the facet level matching the causal story, and aggregation is reserved for cases where the homogeneity assumption can be defended.  Failing when the document presents aggregate numbers (per-institution reach, programme-wide attendance, sector-level satisfaction) without checking whether the facets within those aggregates would point to different recommendations."}], :conclusion "When a measure aggregates across substantively different sub-categories — training topic, audience community, discipline, intervention type, recipient role — the aggregate measure conceals the actionable heterogeneity that the design recommendation actually depends on.  Effect claims and design prescriptions should be made at the facet level where the underlying causal story holds, and aggregated only when the aggregation is interpretively defensible.  Aggregating before faceting flatters the weaker case, ranks unlike entities as if they were comparable, and produces uniform recommendations across facets that need different interventions.", :directives {:audience "system designers, mission authors, working-paper drafters, reviewers", :keywords ["facet" "aggregation" "heterogeneity" "unit-of-analysis" "comparison" "like-with-like" "denominator"], :references ["system-coherence/correct-effect-claims-with-relevant-denominators" "system-coherence/argue-empirically-not-persuasively" "writing-coherence/scope-mush"], :style "pattern", :title "Facet Before Aggregating", :tone "technical"}, :id "system-coherence/facet-before-aggregating", :keywords ["facet" "aggregation" "heterogeneity" "unit-of-analysis" "comparison" "like-with-like" "denominator"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["system-coherence/correct-effect-claims-with-relevant-denominators" "system-coherence/argue-empirically-not-persuasively" "writing-coherence/scope-mush"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/system-coherence/facet-before-aggregating.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Facet Before Aggregating", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Each region of an essay's AIF² wiring — outside (reader, decision), sensory (orienting context, evidence), internal (claim-and-evidence structure), active-surface (the operational move the argument licenses), preferences (glacial commitments and sector frame) — should occupy page-weight proportional to its argumentative load.  The internal region (the design's claims and warrants) is typically the most load-bearing and should be the page-weight winner.  When a low-load region — most often preferences (glacial context) or sensory (evidence summary) — runs longer than the internal region, the reader's perceptual unit becomes the heaviest thing on the page rather than the most load-bearing thing.  Compress the over-developed region, shift the load to a sibling, mark it as a digression, or promote the region's status (if it's actually doing more work than the argument acknowledged)."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "AIF² treats the essay as a typed open cover by regions, with each region playing a structural role analogous to a part of an Active Inference wiring diagram.  In a well-balanced essay, the internal region (the system specification, claims, evidence-as-warrant) takes the most space because that's where the argument lives.  Preferences (the glacial context that motivates the argument — sector frame, funder identity, audience) should be present but compact; the reader does not need a sector survey to be reminded that an institution is one of many actors.  Sensory (orienting prose, evidence presentation) should scale with its support function — enough to ground the internal claims, not enough to dominate them.  When these proportions invert — when, say, a section establishing \"open research is one of many priorities\" runs sixteen items of enumeration to land a one-sentence claim — the page-weight inversion confuses the reader about what the paper is actually arguing."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The pattern operates on the axis: page-weight ↔ argumentative load.\n    Irreducible: the reader's perceptual unit is what occupies the most page-weight; if the most page-weight is not the most load-bearing, the perceptual unit is misleading."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Real costs: compressing an over-developed region risks losing detail that the argument actually needed.  Shifting load to a footnote or annex moves the content out of the reader's primary scan path.  Marking as digression weakens the section's relationship to the argument.  Promoting a region requires admitting that the argument's scope was larger than declared.  Each composition gives something up; the pattern's discipline is to choose explicitly rather than to leave the imbalance implicit and hope the reader gives the writer credit.\n    Active dynamic: the pull toward over-development is strongest in evidence-citation work (a 2022 survey cited the eight themes; the writer reproduces all eight even though the claim only needs two), and in preferences-region work (the sector frame is invoked to motivate the argument, but the invocation grows into a survey of the frame rather than a brief gesture).  The over-development feels like rigour at writing time; it reads as imbalance at reading time."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "      - Preferences overrun: glacial context occupies more page-weight than the design region whose claims that context is supposed to motivate.\n      - Sensory inflation: evidence-presentation dominates the claim it supports; the reader spends more time on the data than on what the data shows.\n      - Exhaustive citation for incidental claim: a sub-claim that is genuinely supporting is given a 50-line evidence presentation as if it were load-bearing.\n      - Enumeration without enumerative content: an N-item list is exhibited where the structural claim is that there exist many items, not that any specific item is doing work.  This is `triad-inflation` scaled up — a sixteen-item list where two would suffice.\n      - Meta-lede closing on overweight region: an over-developed region requires a \"what this evidence tells us is...\" summary at the end (because the reader's perceptual unit has drifted), which is itself a meta-lede failure on top of the imbalance."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Match region weight to argumentative load.  Pick one composition."} {:name "COMPOSITIONS", :name-key "compositions", :slug "compositions", :text "      (1) Compress in place.\n          Augmentation schema: {:region <region-name> :compression-target <span-or-entity-id> :compression-ratio <approximate factor> :preserved-content <list>}\n          Entity types produced: :compressed-region (the new tight version), optionally :source-reference-note (citing where the full content can be found if needed)\n          Effect: the over-developed region shrinks to fit its argumentative load.  Specific details that were over-presented are reduced to a sentence or a citation.  Most appropriate when the over-developed content is incidental and the reader can take the structural claim without the enumerated specifics.\n      (2) Shift load to a sibling region.\n          Augmentation schema: {:from-region <overdeveloped-region> :to-region <footnote | annex | references> :shifted-content <list>}\n          Entity types produced: :shifted-content-reference (the residual cross-reference in the original region)\n          Effect: the heavy content moves to a footnote, annex, or references section; the original region retains only a brief gesture with a cross-reference.  Most appropriate when the content has long-term value as reference material but does not belong in the argument's main scan path.\n      (3) Mark as digression.\n          Augmentation schema: {:digression-marker <prefix | sidebar | explicit-disclaimer> :return-point <span-or-entity-id>}\n          Entity types produced: :digression-prefix, :return-marker\n          Effect: the over-developed region is explicitly typed as a digression that the reader can skip without losing the argument.  Weaker than (1) or (2) because the imbalance remains; appropriate when the content is genuinely interesting and the writer wants to keep it visible despite the imbalance.\n      (4) Promote the region.\n          Augmentation schema: {:promotion-target <region> :new-load-justification <claim that justifies the heavier weight>}\n          Entity types produced: :promoted-region-claim (the new claim that makes the region's weight argumentatively load-bearing)\n          Effect: the region's weight is retained, but its role in the argument is upgraded so the weight is justified.  Appropriate when the over-development reveals that the writer was actually trying to make a heavier claim than the argument-spec captured."} {:name "CHECK", :name-key "check", :slug "check", :text "      Engaged when each AIF² region's page-weight is proportional to its argumentative load, and when over-developed regions are either compressed, shifted, marked as digression, or promoted.  A skeptical reader scrolling the section can tell from page-weight alone which region carries the most argumentative load.  Failing when a preferences region (or any low-load region) is the heaviest unit on the page and the heaviest unit is not the load-bearing one."}], :conclusion "Each region of an essay's AIF² wiring — outside (reader, decision), sensory (orienting context, evidence), internal (claim-and-evidence structure), active-surface (the operational move the argument licenses), preferences (glacial commitments and sector frame) — should occupy page-weight proportional to its argumentative load.  The internal region (the design's claims and warrants) is typically the most load-bearing and should be the page-weight winner.  When a low-load region — most often preferences (glacial context) or sensory (evidence summary) — runs longer than the internal region, the reader's perceptual unit becomes the heaviest thing on the page rather than the most load-bearing thing.  Compress the over-developed region, shift the load to a sibling, mark it as a digression, or promote the region's status (if it's actually doing more work than the argument acknowledged).", :directives {:audience "system designers, working-paper drafters, reviewers", :keywords ["region-balance" "AIF" "preferences" "sensory" "internal" "active-surface" "page-weight" "overdeveloped" "underdeveloped" "enumeration"], :references ["system-coherence/argument-as-coverage-checklist-vs-system-specification" "system-coherence/plan-as-enumeration" "writing-coherence/triad-inflation"], :style "pattern", :title "Match Region Weight to Argumentative Load", :tone "technical"}, :id "system-coherence/match-region-weight-to-load", :keywords ["region-balance" "AIF" "preferences" "sensory" "internal" "active-surface" "page-weight" "overdeveloped" "underdeveloped" "enumeration"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["system-coherence/argument-as-coverage-checklist-vs-system-specification" "system-coherence/plan-as-enumeration" "writing-coherence/triad-inflation"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/system-coherence/match-region-weight-to-load.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Match Region Weight to Argumentative Load", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "When a document exists to support an operational choice, it must name the choice before it unfolds the framework.  The framework is there to warrant the decision, not to delay it.  A reader who has to reconstruct the decision from pages of apparatus is being asked to do argumentative integration work the document should already have done."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Writers often begin with models, taxonomies, and framing layers because those are easier to stabilise than a decision.  The result is a document that feels thoughtful but leaves the live question implicit.  This is not neutral ordering.  It changes the reader's task from \"evaluate this choice\" to \"infer what choice is even being proposed\".  In operational writing, that inversion is a coherence failure because the reader cannot tell what the framework is for."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The pattern operates on the axis: framework-display <-/-> decision-clarity.\n    Irreducible: either the document names the choice early enough for the reader to evaluate the rest as warrant, or it makes the reader infer the choice from surrounding machinery."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Real costs: naming the decision early exposes it to challenge before all the nuances have been introduced.  Writers resist this exposure by prefacing, layering, and framing.  But a delayed decision makes every subsequent section harder to parse because the reader cannot tell which distinctions are load-bearing.\n    Active dynamic: the temptation is strongest in papers that are partly analytical and partly programmatic.  The writer fears sounding too blunt, so the bluntness is postponed until after the reader has already spent effort."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "      - Framework-first drift: the document opens with layers, views, or distinctions while the operational choice remains unnamed.\n      - Decision-by-implication: the recommendation is only recoverable from accumulations of evidence or from the conclusion.\n      - Apparatus prestige: the framework is displayed as if its mere sophistication were itself a warrant.\n      - Late thesis reveal: the section that finally states the choice reads like a surprise rather than like the point the paper has been prosecuting."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Name the choice, then subordinate the framework to it."} {:name "COMPOSITIONS", :name-key "compositions", :slug "compositions", :text "      (1) State the decision in one sentence: say what should be built, adopted, rejected, or handed off.  Do this before the explanatory framework expands.\n      (2) Recast early sections as warrants: after the decision is named, let each framework component answer a visible question about why that choice follows.\n      (3) Title for claim, not furniture: section headings should tell the reader what argumentative work is being done, not merely how many parts are coming.\n      (4) Audit by inversion: ask whether a reader could delete the framework section and still say what choice the paper supports.  If not, the decision has been hidden inside its warrant."} {:name "CHECK", :name-key "check", :slug "check", :text "      Engaged when the reader can identify the operational choice in the opening movement and can read the rest of the document as support for that choice.  Failing when the framework is intelligible but its bearing on a specific decision is still implicit until late in the paper."}], :conclusion "When a document exists to support an operational choice, it must name the choice before it unfolds the framework.  The framework is there to warrant the decision, not to delay it.  A reader who has to reconstruct the decision from pages of apparatus is being asked to do argumentative integration work the document should already have done.", :directives {:audience "system designers, mission authors, working-paper drafters, reviewers", :keywords ["decision-first" "framing" "operational-choice" "framework" "warrant" "document-architecture"], :references ["system-coherence/argue-empirically-not-persuasively" "writing-coherence/throat-clearing-close" "writing-coherence/meta-title"], :style "pattern", :title "Name the Decision Before the Framework", :tone "technical"}, :id "system-coherence/name-the-decision-before-the-framework", :keywords ["decision-first" "framing" "operational-choice" "framework" "warrant" "document-architecture"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["system-coherence/argue-empirically-not-persuasively" "writing-coherence/throat-clearing-close" "writing-coherence/meta-title"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/system-coherence/name-the-decision-before-the-framework.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Name the Decision Before the Framework", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "A capability should be placed at the scale where its absence is systematic and where its value can compound across cases.  If the capability is locally absent in many institutions and only becomes economical, legible, or learnable when carried across cases, that is a warrant for network-scale provision rather than institution-by-institution expectation."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Design arguments often slide from \"institutions need X\" to \"therefore each institution should provide X\".  That step is not automatic.  Many capabilities are precisely the ones local institutions are least able to sustain because they are thinly distributed, episodic, politically awkward, or only visible when evidence is aggregated across sites.  The right question is not who needs the capability, but at what scale the capability can realistically exist and learn."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The pattern operates on the axis: local ownership <-/-> viable bearing scale.\n    Irreducible: either the capability can be expected to arise and persist locally, or the local absence is systematic enough that a higher-scale bearer is warranted."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Real costs: moving a capability to network scale can look centralising, can increase coordination demands, and can obscure local variation if done crudely.  But forcing a structurally absent capability back onto local actors does not preserve autonomy; it produces empty boxes in an organisational chart.\n    Active dynamic: the pressure to localise comes from institutional norm and from the wish that every university should already be able to do the thing.  The empirical test is whether they do."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "      - Wishful localisation: the design assigns responsibility locally because local ownership sounds tidy, despite evidence of repeated absence.\n      - Scale-blind diagnosis: the argument notes cross-case repetition of the same gap but still treats each case as an isolated deficit.\n      - Economy denial: the capability requires comparison, aggregation, or specialist continuity that cannot be sustained in each site separately, but the document ignores this.\n      - Bearer vagueness: the capability is said to matter, but no scale-specific bearer is named."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Place the capability where it can exist, learn, and pay."} {:name "COMPOSITIONS", :name-key "compositions", :slug "compositions", :text "      (1) Diagnose the absence pattern: distinguish occasional local weakness from recurrent cross-site absence.\n      (2) Ask what the capability needs to function: continuity, specialist expertise, comparative evidence, convening authority, or another property that may itself imply the right scale.\n      (3) Name the bearer at that scale: if the answer is network-level, say so explicitly and defend why that bearer is better placed than each local institution.\n      (4) Keep local interfaces explicit: higher-scale provision still needs local touchpoints, but those touchpoints are interfaces to the capability, not proof that the capability is locally borne."} {:name "CHECK", :name-key "check", :slug "check", :text "      Engaged when the paper can show that capability placement follows from the pattern of absence and the conditions of viability, not from administrative preference.  Failing when responsibilities are localised by default even though the argument's own evidence shows repeated local inability to carry them."}], :conclusion "A capability should be placed at the scale where its absence is systematic and where its value can compound across cases.  If the capability is locally absent in many institutions and only becomes economical, legible, or learnable when carried across cases, that is a warrant for network-scale provision rather than institution-by-institution expectation.", :directives {:audience "system designers, mission authors, working-paper drafters, reviewers", :keywords ["capability-placement" "scale" "bearer" "network-level" "local-absence" "cross-case"], :references ["system-coherence/name-the-decision-before-the-framework" "system-coherence/argue-empirically-not-persuasively" "writing-coherence/unearned-essentially"], :style "pattern", :title "Place Capabilities at the Scale of Their Absence", :tone "technical"}, :id "system-coherence/place-capabilities-at-the-scale-of-their-absence", :keywords ["capability-placement" "scale" "bearer" "network-level" "local-absence" "cross-case"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["system-coherence/name-the-decision-before-the-framework" "system-coherence/argue-empirically-not-persuasively" "writing-coherence/unearned-essentially"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/system-coherence/place-capabilities-at-the-scale-of-their-absence.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Place Capabilities at the Scale of Their Absence", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "A plan must name the commitments that are load-bearing, the decision points that branch the path, the observations that license the next step, and the conditions that would cause abandonment.  A calendar-paginated bullet list (\"Q0–Q4\", \"five-year roadmap\", \"monthly checkpoints\") satisfies the form of a plan because succession-in-time already imposes ordering — but ordering is not planning, it is chronology.  When the count of items becomes the perceptual unit, the decisions inside the plan are hidden in plain sight: five paragraphs of equal weight, of which perhaps two carry the actual branching."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Plans accumulate calendar granularity because the calendar is the easiest scaffold.  \"Q0–Q4\" satisfies the demand for a structured forward-look without forcing the writer to identify which moments are commitments and which are operational consequences of earlier commitments.  The compulsion to enumerate every calendar slot reads as planning thoroughness, but it conceals which slots carry the real decisions.  The reader cannot tell what would change if Q1 went badly versus if Q4 went badly — the items look symmetric on the page.  Adjacent surface failure: when several sections in the same document carry counted headings (\"Five operational roles\", \"Three things change each quarter\", \"Five quarterly milestones\", \"Five projects, five offer domains\"), the rhetorical pattern itself becomes the perceptual unit; see `writing-coherence/triad-inflation` and `writing-coherence/structural-style-inconsistency` for the prose-side surface of the same compulsion."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The pattern operates on the axis: activity-enumeration <-/-> decision-structure visibility.\n    Irreducible: either the plan exposes its decision points and commitments as the primary structural units, or it presents activities in temporal order and asks the reader to infer where the decisions sit."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Real costs: exposing the decision structure makes the plan look smaller than the bullet-count suggests.  \"Two decisions across a year, with operational consequences in between\" reads less impressive than \"five quarterly milestones\", and stakeholders who expect a roadmap may read the smaller shape as under-planned.  Naming decisions also exposes the gap between commitment and contingency — many \"milestones\" turn out to be operational consequences of earlier decisions rather than independent commitments — and that exposure is uncomfortable because it concentrates risk into a small number of identifiable moments rather than diffusing it across the calendar.\n    Active dynamic: the pull toward enumeration recurs every time a plan must be presented to a stakeholder, funder, or board that expects a \"roadmap\"; the calendar grid is a ready-made format and asking \"what are the decisions?\" is harder than asking \"what happens in each quarter?\".  The pull also recurs when the writer is themselves uncertain about the decision structure and the calendar offers a way to look planned without having to commit to which moments are the pivots."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "      - Calendar-imposed granularity: the plan has N items because the year has N quarters or the programme has N months, not because there are N structural beats.  The grid's count, not the design's, sets the item count.\n      - Decision points disguised as milestones: a milestone like \"first evaluation wave\" hides the actual decision (which instrument; centralised vs distributed configuration; how findings get fed back) inside an activity label.\n      - Activity-not-commitment confusion: each bullet describes what gets *done* in a period without saying what is being *committed to* by doing it, so the reader cannot tell what failing to do it would forfeit.\n      - Symmetric-list flatness: every period gets a paragraph of equal weight, so the reader cannot tell which periods are pivots and which are operational.  This is the form-side analogue of `triad-inflation`'s third-item freeloading, but at the section level.\n      - Abandon-condition silence: no statement of \"if X has not happened by Y, we [pause / re-plan / stand down]\"; the plan reads as if every step is presumed to succeed.\n      - To-do-list-as-plan: a flat list of items presented as a plan, with no internal structure beyond enumeration — common in \"follow-on work\", \"what we would still need\", \"future directions\" sections.  Distinguishable from a genuine plan by the absence of any decision, observation, or abandon condition attached to any item.\n      - Pre-announced count in heading: a section titled \"Five quarterly milestones\" or \"Seven checks\" or \"Three walks\" tells the reader that the count is what is being delivered, which is a tell that the items are not load-bearing in their own right."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Subordinate the calendar to the decision structure."} {:name "COMPOSITIONS", :name-key "compositions", :slug "compositions", :text "      (1) Locate the decision points first: identify the moments where the plan branches (continue / vary / stand down; centralised / distributed; bridge / self-finance) and make those the primary structural units.  The number of decision points sets the plan's shape, not the number of calendar slots.\n      (2) Demote calendar to scaffold: keep dates where they constrain (deadlines, dependencies, evidence-readiness) but stop treating every calendar slot as a planning unit.  Quarters without a decision or commitment can be summarised in a sentence rather than given a paragraph.\n      (3) Distinguish commitments from activities: \"we will hire a Training Manager by Q0\" is a commitment; \"the Training Manager schedules trainers\" is an activity that follows from the commitment.  Plans should foreground commitments; activities follow.\n      (4) Couple decisions to closure mechanisms: each decision point names its evidence-input and its possible outcomes.  This is `bind-open-questions-to-closure-mechanisms` operating inside the plan rather than around it.\n      (5) State abandon conditions: each commitment that could plausibly fail names what would trigger reconsideration.  A plan with no abandon conditions is a plan that has not been pressure-tested.\n      (6) Title for the decision, not the count: section headings should name what argumentative or operational work the section does, not how many items follow.  \"Quarterly milestones through to the Year-2 decision\" beats \"Five quarterly milestones\"."} {:name "CHECK", :name-key "check", :slug "check", :text "      Engaged when the plan's decision-and-commitment structure is recoverable in fewer items than the calendar-grid version would suggest, and when each decision point has a named evidence-input, possible outcomes, and an abandon condition.  Failing when the count of bullets matches the count of calendar slots (or, more generally, when the item count is a property of the presentation format rather than of the underlying design).  Diagnostic question: if a reader saw only the section's headings and item counts, could they tell which moments are pivots and which are operational consequences?  If no, the plan is operating as an enumeration."}], :conclusion "A plan must name the commitments that are load-bearing, the decision points that branch the path, the observations that license the next step, and the conditions that would cause abandonment.  A calendar-paginated bullet list (\"Q0–Q4\", \"five-year roadmap\", \"monthly checkpoints\") satisfies the form of a plan because succession-in-time already imposes ordering — but ordering is not planning, it is chronology.  When the count of items becomes the perceptual unit, the decisions inside the plan are hidden in plain sight: five paragraphs of equal weight, of which perhaps two carry the actual branching.", :directives {:audience "system designers, mission authors, working-paper drafters, programme leads, reviewers", :keywords ["plan" "planning" "enumeration" "milestone" "calendar" "decision-point" "roadmap" "commitment" "abandon-condition"], :references ["system-coherence/name-the-decision-before-the-framework" "system-coherence/bind-open-questions-to-closure-mechanisms" "system-coherence/turn-design-into-checks" "writing-coherence/triad-inflation" "writing-coherence/structural-style-inconsistency"], :style "pattern", :title "A Plan Is Not a List of Activities", :tone "technical"}, :id "system-coherence/plan-as-enumeration", :keywords ["plan" "planning" "enumeration" "milestone" "calendar" "decision-point" "roadmap" "commitment" "abandon-condition"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["system-coherence/name-the-decision-before-the-framework" "system-coherence/bind-open-questions-to-closure-mechanisms" "system-coherence/turn-design-into-checks" "writing-coherence/triad-inflation" "writing-coherence/structural-style-inconsistency"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/system-coherence/plan-as-enumeration.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "A Plan Is Not a List of Activities", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "When the load-bearing structural claim of a section is the *topology* of a small typed graph — the typed exchanges between five roles, the dependency couplings between seven capabilities, the message-passing contract between three layers — the section must exhibit the edges, not list the per-node outgoing-edge stacks.  Per-node adjacency listings (\"Node A hands to B; hands to C; hands to D.  Node B hands to A; hands to D.  ...\") give the reader N paragraphs from which the graph's E edges must be reconstructed by inference.  The page-weight scales with N (the number of nodes), not with E (the number of edges); the reader's perceptual unit becomes \"list of role-paragraphs each ending in a hand-off stack\" rather than \"graph of typed contracts.\"  The cure is to exhibit the edges directly — as a table, an explicit edge-list, or a topology paragraph — and let the role descriptions carry only their per-node identity work."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "AIF² and similar wiring-diagram framings treat systems as typed graphs.  When a system has 4–8 nodes and 6–12 typed edges, the topology is genuinely the load-bearing structure: the design's contribution is *which edges exist between which nodes carrying what data*.  Prose has two natural ways to present a small graph: (a) walk the nodes, listing each node's outgoing edges as the node is introduced; (b) introduce the nodes briefly and then exhibit the edge set as its own structural unit.  Mode (a) is locally readable (each role-paragraph stands on its own) but globally illegible (the topology is only recoverable by mental aggregation).  Mode (b) is structurally honest — the edges are visible as edges.  The pattern recurs in role-design sections, layer-architecture sections, and message-passing-contract sections."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The pattern operates on the axis: per-node locality ↔ topology visibility.\n    Irreducible: a prose presentation either lists outgoing edges under each source node (and forces the reader to reconstruct the graph), or exhibits the edge set as a structural unit visible at a glance.  The same edges, two presentation modes; the reader's perceptual unit differs."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Real costs: per-node adjacency lists are easier to write — they preserve the natural narrative order (\"Here is role A.  Role A does X.  Role A hands to B, C, D.\").  Exhibiting the edge set as a unit requires the writer to factor the topology out of the role descriptions, which can read as more abstract or as breaking the role-by-role flow.  Some readers (those who think node-first) find adjacency listings easier to absorb; others (those who think edge-first) find topology exhibits clearer.  The pattern's discipline is to match the presentation to the *claim's* load: if the claim is \"the design's contribution is the contracts between roles,\" exhibit the contracts; if the claim is \"each role has its own work,\" exhibit the roles.\n    Active dynamic: the pull toward adjacency listing is strongest when the role descriptions and the hand-offs are mutually informative — the role's identity partly IS the hand-offs it owns.  The writer reaches for \"describe the role; then say what it does in the network\" without realising that the topology has now been distributed across N paragraphs."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "      - Adjacency stacks: each node-paragraph ends in a stack of \"hands to X; hands to Y; hands to Z\" clauses; the reader sees N stacks, must mentally union them into the edge set.\n      - Page-weight scales with N, not E: if N=5 and E=8, the section has 5 role-paragraphs (page-weight ~5 × paragraph) instead of 5 short role-identities + 1 edge-list (page-weight ~5 × sentence + 1 × short-list).\n      - Redundant edge mention: edge X→Y appears in node X's \"outgoing\" list, but the reader can't easily verify that Y's \"incoming\" obligation matches.  Reciprocal edges (X→Y plus Y→X) get listed twice in two different paragraphs.\n      - Topology only inferable, not visible: a skeptical reader cannot answer \"how many edges does this design have?\" without reading every node-paragraph and aggregating.  This is the structural analogue of `plan-as-enumeration`'s \"decision points disguised as milestones\" failure.\n      - Triad-on-every-node inflation: each role-paragraph ends in three or four hand-offs even when some roles only have one or two genuine outgoing edges — the writer pads to maintain stylistic parallelism, inflating the apparent edge count."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Match the presentation to the load-bearing claim.  Pick one composition."} {:name "COMPOSITIONS", :name-key "compositions", :slug "compositions", :text "      (1) Exhibit edges as a table.\n          Augmentation schema: {:edge-columns <list of column names: From, To, Data-on-wire, etc.> :edge-rows <list of typed exchanges>}\n          Entity types produced: :edge-table, :typed-exchange-row (one per edge)\n          Effect: the topology is visible at a glance.  Per-node descriptions become brief (one sentence each) since the hand-offs no longer live inside the role-paragraphs.  Most appropriate when the edges have multiple typed properties worth showing (data shape, cadence, criticality).\n      (2) Exhibit edges as a labelled list.\n          Augmentation schema: {:edge-format (:source-arrow-target | :pair-symbol-data) :edge-items <list>}\n          Entity types produced: :typed-exchange-item (one per edge)\n          Effect: lighter touch than a table; reads as a focused prose-list rather than a structured display.  Most appropriate when the edges are easy to describe in a phrase each and a table would feel heavy.\n      (3) Exhibit topology in a paragraph.\n          Augmentation schema: {:topology-style :flow-narrative | :coupling-summary :grouping-axis <by-cadence | by-criticality | by-direction>}\n          Entity types produced: :topology-paragraph (one paragraph describing the graph as a whole)\n          Effect: most prose-natural; loses some specificity but preserves narrative flow.  Most appropriate when the edges are few enough to summarise in one paragraph and the writer wants to keep the reading-pace prose-y."} {:name "CHECK", :name-key "check", :slug "check", :text "      Engaged when the section's edge set is visible as its own structural unit — a table, a labelled list, or a topology paragraph — and per-node descriptions carry only per-node identity work.  A skeptical reader scrolling the section can answer \"how many typed edges does this design have, and what does each edge carry?\" without aggregating across multiple paragraphs.  Failing when each node-paragraph ends in a hand-off-stack and the topology is only inferable by union."}], :conclusion "When the load-bearing structural claim of a section is the *topology* of a small typed graph — the typed exchanges between five roles, the dependency couplings between seven capabilities, the message-passing contract between three layers — the section must exhibit the edges, not list the per-node outgoing-edge stacks.  Per-node adjacency listings (\"Node A hands to B; hands to C; hands to D.  Node B hands to A; hands to D.  ...\") give the reader N paragraphs from which the graph's E edges must be reconstructed by inference.  The page-weight scales with N (the number of nodes), not with E (the number of edges); the reader's perceptual unit becomes \"list of role-paragraphs each ending in a hand-off stack\" rather than \"graph of typed contracts.\"  The cure is to exhibit the edges directly — as a table, an explicit edge-list, or a topology paragraph — and let the role descriptions carry only their per-node identity work.", :directives {:audience "system designers, working-paper drafters, reviewers", :keywords ["graph" "topology" "edges" "nodes" "adjacency" "hand-offs" "exchanges" "presentation" "plan-as-enumeration"], :references ["system-coherence/plan-as-enumeration" "system-coherence/argument-as-coverage-checklist-vs-system-specification" "writing-coherence/triad-inflation" "writing-coherence/structural-style-inconsistency"], :style "pattern", :title "Present the Graph as Edges, Not Per-Node Adjacency Lists", :tone "technical"}, :id "system-coherence/present-graph-topology-not-adjacency-lists", :keywords ["graph" "topology" "edges" "nodes" "adjacency" "hand-offs" "exchanges" "presentation" "plan-as-enumeration"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["system-coherence/plan-as-enumeration" "system-coherence/argument-as-coverage-checklist-vs-system-specification" "writing-coherence/triad-inflation" "writing-coherence/structural-style-inconsistency"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/system-coherence/present-graph-topology-not-adjacency-lists.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Present the Graph as Edges, Not Per-Node Adjacency Lists", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Robust system arguments distinguish between what may vary and what cannot be broken without changing the system's character.  Configurable choices are not the same as load-bearing couplings.  If the document treats both alike, it cannot tell adaptability from drift or experimentation from self-cancellation."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Once a design is described, everything in it can look equally optional.  That flattening is attractive because it makes the system seem flexible.  But real flexibility depends on knowing which parts are safely adjustable and which relationships are carrying the design's causal load.  Without that distinction, adaptation becomes indistinguishable from erosion."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The pattern operates on the axis: permissible variation <-/-> constitutive coupling.\n    Irreducible: some elements can vary without changing the system's identity, while others are load-bearing enough that breaking them yields a different system, not merely a local variant."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Real costs: declaring couplings to be load-bearing narrows room for local tailoring and invites challenge about whether the system is over-specified.  But failing to declare them shifts the cost into execution, where every local variation can claim legitimacy and no principled line exists between robust adaptation and silent abandonment.\n    Active dynamic: the temptation is strongest when the writer wants the system to look portable across heterogeneous contexts.  The pressure then is to describe everything as configurable."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "      - Flat optionality: every component is presented as if locally selectable, leaving no visible system identity.\n      - Hidden brittleness: a coupling is in fact essential, but the document only reveals that after failure.\n      - Adaptation laundering: a change that removes a causal dependency is described as context-sensitive tailoring.\n      - Scope mush: the paper blurs core architecture, local implementation choices, and cosmetic differences into one undifferentiated bundle."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Mark the load-bearing couplings and fence the configuration space."} {:name "COMPOSITIONS", :name-key "compositions", :slug "compositions", :text "      (1) Name the couplings that make the system the system: these are the relationships whose removal would void the design's logic.\n      (2) Separate them from tunable parameters: delivery cadence, staffing pattern, local framing, or tooling may be adjustable without touching the core coupling.\n      (3) Use checks to police the boundary: if a local variation severs a load-bearing relation, classify it as system change rather than as mere adaptation.\n      (4) Document the reason for each declared coupling: say what causal work it does, so the constraint is legible rather than arbitrary."} {:name "CHECK", :name-key "check", :slug "check", :text "      Engaged when the paper clearly distinguishes adjustable design choices from constitutive relations and can explain why the latter matter.  Failing when local variation is celebrated in the abstract but no principled boundary protects the system from dissolving into unrelated local practices."}], :conclusion "Robust system arguments distinguish between what may vary and what cannot be broken without changing the system's character.  Configurable choices are not the same as load-bearing couplings.  If the document treats both alike, it cannot tell adaptability from drift or experimentation from self-cancellation.", :directives {:audience "system designers, mission authors, working-paper drafters, reviewers", :keywords ["robustness" "coupling" "configuration" "identity" "brittle" "variation" "invariants"], :references ["system-coherence/turn-design-into-checks" "system-coherence/argue-empirically-not-persuasively" "writing-coherence/scope-mush"], :style "pattern", :title "Separate Configurable Choices from Load-Bearing Couplings", :tone "technical"}, :id "system-coherence/separate-configurable-choices-from-load-bearing-couplings", :keywords ["robustness" "coupling" "configuration" "identity" "brittle" "variation" "invariants"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["system-coherence/turn-design-into-checks" "system-coherence/argue-empirically-not-persuasively" "writing-coherence/scope-mush"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/system-coherence/separate-configurable-choices-from-load-bearing-couplings.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Separate Configurable Choices from Load-Bearing Couplings", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "A stochastic simulation's output under a single random seed is not, by itself, a finding.  Apparent contrasts between configurations may be sampling artefacts of the particular seed sequence rather than substantive differences in the dynamics.  Reportable per-parameter contrasts must be averaged across multiple independent seeds, with cross-seed mean and variance disclosed; single-seed runs are reserved for development and clearly labelled if shown.  This is true even when the simulation is deterministic given a seed — determinism establishes reproducibility, not generality."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Stochastic simulations are common substrates for design-supporting claims, especially in active-inference, agent-based, and Monte-Carlo work.  The simulation's output depends on a random-number-generator state.  When configurations are compared without proper RNG isolation between them, apparent contrasts can arise from where in the seed-space the simulation happened to land rather than from the parameter difference being tested.  Even with proper RNG resets, a single seed produces only one path through a distribution of possible trajectories; without sampling that distribution, the reader cannot tell whether an observed contrast is a real signal or an artefact of one fortunate or unfortunate trajectory.  The discipline is harder than it looks: it requires explicit seed-reset infrastructure, multi-seed runners, cross-seed aggregation, and a reporting convention that disciplines the writer to disclose variance whenever they report contrast."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The pattern operates on the axis: single-trajectory observation <-/-> distribution-grounded claim.\n    Irreducible: a stochastic simulation either has its results characterised across enough seeds to estimate the distribution they came from, or its claimed contrasts may be artefacts of one trajectory's particular random walk."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Real costs: multi-seed runs multiply simulation time by N, and cross-seed aggregation adds reporting overhead.  Researchers under deadline often reach for single-seed runs, then write up their contrasts as if they were findings.  Reviewers without simulation experience may not notice that variance is missing.  The temptation is sharpest when a single-seed contrast looks compelling — exactly the situation where the contrast is most likely to be partially or wholly artefactual.\n    Active dynamic: the failure recurs whenever the writer wants to claim a per-parameter difference (configuration A produces different outcome from configuration B) without first verifying that the difference survives across-seed averaging."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "      - Sampling-artefact-as-finding: a contrast observed under one seed (or one seed sequence) is reported as a finding without checking whether it survives multi-seed averaging.\n      - Seed-leakage: RNG state evolves across configurations during sequential testing, so apparent contrasts between configurations are partially driven by where in the seed-space each test happened to land.  This is *worse* than single-seed-per-configuration because the contrasts can be generated by the shared evolving state rather than by the parameter difference.\n      - Variance-blindness: only point estimates are reported across configurations; the reader cannot tell whether a 0.13 difference is meaningful or noise.\n      - Premature publication of artefactual contrasts: a single-seed result becomes a quoted finding in subsequent work or in a paper, and only later (sometimes years later) is exposed as not surviving replication.\n      - Replication-by-coincidence: a re-run of the same code with the same seed reproduces the result and is mistaken for replication; what is needed is reproduction across *different* seeds.\n      - Determinism-as-warrant: deterministic-given-seed simulation behaviour is mistaken for generalisable claim; \"we got the same number\" is conflated with \"this number is robust\".\n      - Audit absence: there is no record of how many seeds were run, what their CIs look like, or whether any contrast survives multi-seed averaging."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Sample the distribution before claiming the contrast."} {:name "COMPOSITIONS", :name-key "compositions", :slug "compositions", :text "      (1) Reset RNG state explicitly between configurations.  Inherit nothing from the prior run; seeds are not just initial conditions but full simulation state.  Configurations should be testable in any order without the result depending on order.\n      (2) Run each configuration under N independent seeds.  Default thresholds: ≥20 seeds for sensitivity sweeps and parameter robustness checks, ≥50 seeds for headline findings that will appear in reports or papers.  Document the seed range used.\n      (3) Aggregate per-configuration: report cross-seed mean and confidence interval, not point estimate from a single seed.  When the underlying distribution is non-Gaussian, prefer empirical CIs (quantile-based) over standard-error approximations.\n      (4) For per-parameter contrasts: verify that confidence intervals do not overlap before claiming differentiation.  If they overlap, the contrast is not robustly differentiated under the seed regime tested; either run more seeds or reduce the contrast claim's strength.\n      (5) Distinguish statistical from illustrative runs.  Single-seed runs used for development, debugging, or pedagogic figure generation should be explicitly labelled \"illustrative\" or \"development run\"; never reported as findings.\n      (6) Compose with `aif/term-to-channel-traceability`.  Each reportable claim discloses its seed range, configuration parameters, and the time window over which the score is integrated; this turns the seed discipline into an audit-trail discipline.\n      (7) When the simulation is deterministic given seed, document this as reproducibility, not generality.  Reproduction requires *different* seeds producing consistent ranges; same-seed reruns establish determinism only."} {:name "CHECK", :name-key "check", :slug "check", :text "      Engaged when every per-parameter contrast in the simulation's reportable output appears with a cross-seed mean and CI, the seed range used is documented, and contrast claims are made only when CIs do not overlap.  Failing when single-seed numbers appear in tables or text without variance disclosure, or when contrast claims are stated without checking whether the difference survives multi-seed averaging, or when the simulation's seed-reset discipline cannot be verified by reading the code."}], :conclusion "A stochastic simulation's output under a single random seed is not, by itself, a finding.  Apparent contrasts between configurations may be sampling artefacts of the particular seed sequence rather than substantive differences in the dynamics.  Reportable per-parameter contrasts must be averaged across multiple independent seeds, with cross-seed mean and variance disclosed; single-seed runs are reserved for development and clearly labelled if shown.  This is true even when the simulation is deterministic given a seed — determinism establishes reproducibility, not generality.", :directives {:audience "system designers, mission authors, simulation builders, working-paper drafters, reviewers", :keywords ["stochastic-simulation" "rng" "seed" "multi-seed" "sampling-artifact" "statistical-claim" "confidence-interval" "variance" "replication"], :references ["system-coherence/argue-empirically-not-persuasively" "system-coherence/turn-design-into-checks" "aif/term-to-channel-traceability" "writing-coherence/hedged-lift"], :style "pattern", :title "Single-Seed Results Need Multi-Seed Validation", :tone "technical"}, :id "system-coherence/single-seed-results-need-multi-seed-validation", :keywords ["stochastic-simulation" "rng" "seed" "multi-seed" "sampling-artifact" "statistical-claim" "confidence-interval" "variance" "replication"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["system-coherence/argue-empirically-not-persuasively" "system-coherence/turn-design-into-checks" "aif/term-to-channel-traceability" "writing-coherence/hedged-lift"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/system-coherence/single-seed-results-need-multi-seed-validation.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Single-Seed Results Need Multi-Seed Validation", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Evidence presented in prose has a timescale.  Quarterly returns are fast (the value can move within months); focus-group findings are medium (slow to gather, stable over a year or two); sector surveys are slow (a one-time snapshot, expensive to refresh); institutional identities and funder structures are glacial.  When a section synthesizes evidence from multiple timescales without distinguishing them, the reader cannot tell whether a verdict is fast-changing news that will look different next quarter or long-settled state that has been stable for years.  Synthesis sentences in particular (\"this evidence tells us X\") are I3 failure sites because they fuse co-equal sentence-level claims across timescales the underlying evidence does not actually treat as co-equal."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Working papers often draw on evidence from several timescales at once — quarterly delivery counts (fast), a one-time priorities survey (slow), a recent indicators pilot (medium), the sector's standing identity (glacial).  Each is legitimate; together they form the evidence base.  But the prose tends to flatten them, presenting each as one more sentence-level fact alongside the others.  The flattening is most acute in the synthesis paragraph at the end of a section — where the writer needs to draw conclusions and reaches for \"in short, this evidence tells us...\" or similar, fusing facts that were measured on different timescales into one verdict the reader can't decompose.  This is the section-scope analogue of `argument-as-coverage-checklist`'s timescale-flattening failure mode, but localised to a specific synthesis sentence rather than the argument-spec as a whole."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The pattern operates on the axis: synthesis legibility ↔ timescale fidelity.\n    Irreducible: either each evidence claim carries enough timescale context for the reader to know how fast its truth-value moves, or the synthesis fuses incompatible rates into a verdict that hides what it stands on."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Real costs: adding timescale context to every evidence claim is wordy and pedagogical; the reader doesn't need \"(fast, quarterly returns, 2024–26)\" on every sentence.  The cost is paid where the timescales actually matter — at synthesis points, at moments of cross-timescale comparison, and where a slow-changing fact and a fast-changing fact bear on the same conclusion.\n    Active dynamic: the pull toward flattening is strongest in the synthesis paragraph.  The writer wants to draw a single conclusion (\"delivery happened at network scale but didn't settle practice change\"); the underlying evidence supports multi-rate sub-conclusions (\"delivery, measured fast, happened at scale; measurability of practice change, measured medium, is mixed; further evidence, on a longer horizon, is expected\").  The fast verdict is honest about what the fast evidence shows; the synthesis fuses it with medium and longer-horizon notes and the reader can't tell which part is settled and which is still moving."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "      - Timescale-flatten in synthesis: \"this evidence tells us X\" where the evidence is multi-rate.\n      - Temporal ambiguity: a finding is named without saying when it was measured (\"a final report reports a mixed verdict\") so the reader can't tell if it's settled or fresh.\n      - Historical-and-current coordinated by \"and\": a slow fact and a fast fact joined as if co-equal claims, when they actually belong to different epistemic categories.\n      - Forward-looking promise without timescale: \"various efforts are expected to contribute evidence\" without saying on what horizon, by when, with what update cadence.\n      - In short opener: \"In short\" announcing a synthesis without typing the synthesis's evidence — most common failure surface."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Tag evidence by timescale at the points where the timescales matter.  Pick one composition based on the section's structure."} {:name "COMPOSITIONS", :name-key "compositions", :slug "compositions", :text "      (1) Inline timescale tags.\n          Augmentation schema: {:tag-locations <list of clauses to tag> :tag-format (:parenthetical | :prefix | :em-dash)}\n          Entity types produced: :timescale-tagged-claim\n          Effect: each relevant claim gains a small parenthetical or prefix tagging its timescale.  Light-touch; preserves the existing structure.\n      (2) Reorder by timescale.\n          Augmentation schema: {:order :fast-first | :slow-first | :recent-first :grouping-style <by-paragraph | by-sentence>}\n          Entity types produced: :timescale-grouped-section\n          Effect: paragraphs (or sentences) reorder so all fast evidence groups together, then medium, then slow.  Heavier rewrite; produces strong visual segregation of timescales.\n      (3) Distinguish in synthesis.\n          Augmentation schema: {:synthesis-target <span-or-entity-id> :timescale-types-distinguished <list of timescales>}\n          Entity types produced: :timescale-tagged-synthesis-claim, optionally :forward-looking-evidence-note (separate sentence for forward-looking content)\n          Effect: the synthesis sentence is rewritten to name each timescale's contribution separately rather than fusing them.  Most appropriate when the body of the section is well-organised but the closing synthesis fuses what the body distinguished."} {:name "CHECK", :name-key "check", :slug "check", :text "      Engaged when every synthesis sentence and every cross-evidence comparison names the timescales of the evidence it draws on; a skeptical reader can tell which part of the verdict will look different next quarter and which has been stable for years.  Failing when \"this evidence tells us X\" or \"in short, X\" coordinates evidence from incompatible rates without distinguishing them."}], :conclusion "Evidence presented in prose has a timescale.  Quarterly returns are fast (the value can move within months); focus-group findings are medium (slow to gather, stable over a year or two); sector surveys are slow (a one-time snapshot, expensive to refresh); institutional identities and funder structures are glacial.  When a section synthesizes evidence from multiple timescales without distinguishing them, the reader cannot tell whether a verdict is fast-changing news that will look different next quarter or long-settled state that has been stable for years.  Synthesis sentences in particular (\"this evidence tells us X\") are I3 failure sites because they fuse co-equal sentence-level claims across timescales the underlying evidence does not actually treat as co-equal.", :directives {:audience "system designers, working-paper drafters, reviewers", :keywords ["timescale" "evidence" "fast" "slow" "glacial" "I3" "AIF" "synthesis" "mixed-undeclared"], :references ["system-coherence/argument-as-coverage-checklist-vs-system-specification" "system-coherence/argue-empirically-not-persuasively" "writing-coherence/meta-lede"], :style "pattern", :title "Tag Evidence by Timescale When the Section Mixes Rates", :tone "technical"}, :id "system-coherence/tag-evidence-by-timescale", :keywords ["timescale" "evidence" "fast" "slow" "glacial" "I3" "AIF" "synthesis" "mixed-undeclared"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["system-coherence/argument-as-coverage-checklist-vs-system-specification" "system-coherence/argue-empirically-not-persuasively" "writing-coherence/meta-lede"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/system-coherence/tag-evidence-by-timescale.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Tag Evidence by Timescale When the Section Mixes Rates", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "When the main constraints on outcomes are institutional conditions such as time, incentives, leadership support, workflow fit, and role ownership, redesigning training content is a second-order lever and should be argued as such.  Content refresh may improve fit and uptake at the margin, but it should not be presented as the principal mechanism of change when the evidence says the binding constraints lie elsewhere."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Content is the most visible and most controllable part of a training system, so it attracts disproportionate reform energy.  This is understandable but often incoherent.  If institutions are not giving staff time, reward, reinforcement, or practical routes to enact what training teaches, then even well-designed content will hit a ceiling set by those missing conditions.  The causal story must follow the binding constraint, not the convenience of the intervention."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The pattern operates on the axis: content optimisation <-/-> condition-setting leverage.\n    Irreducible: if the limiting factor is mainly the surrounding institutional condition, then content redesign cannot honestly be described as the primary route to materially different outcomes."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Real costs: saying content is second-order can feel anticlimactic, especially to teams whose main tool is curriculum design.  It can also seem to under-value real improvements in relevance, disciplinary fit, or pedagogy.  The point is not that content does not matter; it is that it matters within a larger causal envelope whose boundaries are set elsewhere.\n    Active dynamic: the slippage recurs whenever demand signals about preferred topics or formats are treated as if they had already identified the dominant outcome lever."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "      - Content solutionism: dissatisfaction with the current offer is treated as proof that a better offer would solve the larger problem.\n      - Constraint displacement: evidence about incentives, recognition, or workflow barriers is acknowledged and then sidelined while the argument returns to curriculum.\n      - Marginal-gain inflation: local improvements in fit are narrated as if they implied system-level change.\n      - Instrument myopia: the intervention is defined by what the programme can easily change, rather than by what the evidence shows to be causally limiting."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Argue content as a subordinate lever inside the binding conditions."} {:name "COMPOSITIONS", :name-key "compositions", :slug "compositions", :text "      (1) State the binding constraints first: name the institutional conditions that are doing the real limiting work.\n      (2) Describe content refresh in its proper role: better fit, less alienation, improved uptake, or stronger local usefulness.\n      (3) Link content gains to condition-setting mechanisms: show what additional structures would be required for better training to convert into changed practice.\n      (4) Refuse overclaiming: if the surrounding conditions remain unchanged, say that content refresh alone is unlikely to produce materially different institutional outcomes."} {:name "CHECK", :name-key "check", :slug "check", :text "      Engaged when the paper treats content redesign as one component of a wider system and explicitly subordinates it to the binding institutional conditions.  Failing when the document's own evidence shows that incentives and support dominate, yet the recommendations still imply that curriculum revision is the main route to change."}], :conclusion "When the main constraints on outcomes are institutional conditions such as time, incentives, leadership support, workflow fit, and role ownership, redesigning training content is a second-order lever and should be argued as such.  Content refresh may improve fit and uptake at the margin, but it should not be presented as the principal mechanism of change when the evidence says the binding constraints lie elsewhere.", :directives {:audience "system designers, mission authors, working-paper drafters, reviewers", :keywords ["content" "institutional-conditions" "incentives" "support" "leverage" "binding-constraint"], :references ["system-coherence/argue-empirically-not-persuasively" "system-coherence/correct-effect-claims-with-relevant-denominators" "writing-coherence/hedged-lift"], :style "pattern", :title "Treat Content Refresh as Second-Order When Conditions Bind", :tone "technical"}, :id "system-coherence/treat-content-refresh-as-second-order-when-conditions-bind", :keywords ["content" "institutional-conditions" "incentives" "support" "leverage" "binding-constraint"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["system-coherence/argue-empirically-not-persuasively" "system-coherence/correct-effect-claims-with-relevant-denominators" "writing-coherence/hedged-lift"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/system-coherence/treat-content-refresh-as-second-order-when-conditions-bind.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Treat Content Refresh as Second-Order When Conditions Bind", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "A design is not operational when it is merely describable.  It becomes operational when it is accompanied by checks that can tell whether it is being upheld, drifting, or failing.  Invariants, indicators, threshold rules, scenario tests, and handoff conditions are not optional implementation detail; they are part of the design's coherence."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Writers often stop at architecture.  They name roles, flows, and principles, and the design sounds complete because it can be narrated.  But a narrated design without checks cannot distinguish faithful enactment from decorative adoption.  The missing move is from \"what the system is supposed to be\" to \"how we would know whether that remains true in operation\"."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The pattern operates on the axis: described design <-/-> testable design.\n    Irreducible: either the system includes ways to detect whether its critical properties hold, or those properties remain aspirational."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Real costs: checks can feel reductive, can expose the weakness of concepts that sounded rich in prose, and can create obligations to gather evidence.  But without them, the document has no disciplined way to tell robust configuration from ceremonial language.\n    Active dynamic: principles are easier to write than thresholds; roles are easier to name than to validate; handoffs are easier to describe than to monitor."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "      - Principle without witness: the document states an invariant but gives no observable sign by which it could be checked.\n      - Threshold avoidance: action is said to depend on \"sufficient\" evidence, uptake, or readiness, but no threshold logic is named.\n      - Scenario fragility: a design sounds coherent in the happy path but has not been pressure-tested against foreseeable failure cases.\n      - Handoff opacity: transitions between phases or roles are described without entry or exit conditions."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Express critical design claims as checks."} {:name "COMPOSITIONS", :name-key "compositions", :slug "compositions", :text "      (1) Extract invariants: identify which properties must remain true for the system still to count as itself.\n      (2) Attach observables: for each invariant, specify what evidence, indicator, or event would count as a witness.\n      (3) Define thresholds or triggers where possible: name the conditions under which the system changes phase, escalates, hands off, or revises.\n      (4) Add at least one hostile scenario: show how the checks discriminate success from a plausible failure or mimic configuration."} {:name "CHECK", :name-key "check", :slug "check", :text "      Engaged when the design's most important claims have corresponding observables, thresholds, or scenario tests.  Failing when the reader can describe the system elegantly but still cannot tell what evidence would show that it is functioning as intended."}], :conclusion "A design is not operational when it is merely describable.  It becomes operational when it is accompanied by checks that can tell whether it is being upheld, drifting, or failing.  Invariants, indicators, threshold rules, scenario tests, and handoff conditions are not optional implementation detail; they are part of the design's coherence.", :directives {:audience "system designers, mission authors, working-paper drafters, reviewers", :keywords ["design" "invariants" "checks" "indicators" "thresholds" "operationalisation"], :references ["system-coherence/argue-empirically-not-persuasively" "system-coherence/bind-open-questions-to-closure-mechanisms" "writing-coherence/hedged-lift"], :style "pattern", :title "Turn Design Into Checks", :tone "technical"}, :id "system-coherence/turn-design-into-checks", :keywords ["design" "invariants" "checks" "indicators" "thresholds" "operationalisation"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["system-coherence/argue-empirically-not-persuasively" "system-coherence/bind-open-questions-to-closure-mechanisms" "writing-coherence/hedged-lift"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/system-coherence/turn-design-into-checks.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Turn Design Into Checks", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Rewriting a diagnostic annotation into a verified improvement is a typed transformation f:(A, B) → C, not a textual edit.  A is the typed diagnostic; B is the rewrite plan (composition-id chosen from the cited pattern's COMPOSITIONS menu, plus any augmentation for judgment calls); C is the post-rewrite tuple of new prose, updated annotation graph, and invariant-delta.  The rewrite is *verified* iff no invariant in the post-state is weaker than in the pre-state AND the cited pattern's CHECK clause holds on the new span.  Without verification, \"fixing the diagnostic\" is hope; with verification, the same typed problem cannot recur because the typed precondition that surfaced it no longer holds."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "When a diagnostic survey produces a list of structural problems and a rewriter sets out to address them, the typical move is \"read the prose, fix what reads badly, move on.\"  This works locally but accumulates failure modes: (1) the rewrite may close one diagnostic at the cost of opening another (a fix that compresses a region-balance issue may flatten timescale separation as a side effect); (2) the rewrite's *reason* is not recorded, so when the prose later drifts back toward the original failure, the prior rewrite cannot be cited or built on; (3) the rewriter's choices are not typed against the pattern that motivated the diagnostic, so \"good prose\" can drift away from the structural property the pattern was guarding.  Typed rewriting solves all three by making the rewrite a function of (diagnostic, composition-choice) with verification against the invariant audit.  The closure trace records the transformation so it can be inspected, reverted, or pattern-mined.\n\nThe type signature, concretely:\n\n```\nA : a diagnostic annotation (:hx-type :annotation/comment) carrying\n    {:endpoints [{:annotated <section, passage>}\n                 {:source :pattern-name <flexiarg-path>\n                          :passage <field-from-pattern>}]\n     :severity <level>\n     :status :open}\n\nB : a rewrite plan\n    {:composition-id <id-from-pattern's-COMPOSITIONS-menu>\n     :augmentation   <judgment calls for ambiguity, optional>}\n\nC : a post-rewrite tuple — refined for entity-level discipline (the\n    sheaf-of-rewrite reading; see below)\n    {:prose            <:after text — global section, redundant given operations + glue>\n     :entities-before  [{:id :eN :type <entity-type> :fate <keep|drop|replace|keep-with-glue-adjustment> :span \"...\"} ...]\n     :operations       [{:op <drop|keep|add|replace|move>\n                         :target :eN\n                         :rationale \"...\"\n                         :pattern-ref <optional flexiarg path>\n                         :with-entities <list of new entities for :replace and :add>}\n                        ...]\n     :glue-adjustments [{:effect <effect-kind>\n                         :on :eN\n                         :caused-by [:op :target ...]\n                         :note \"...\"}\n                        ...]\n     :annotations      <new/updated annotations on the new prose>\n     :invariant-delta  {invariant [before-status after-status] ...}\n     :verification     {:check-clause-passes? <bool-or-quantified>\n                        :invariants-weakened []\n                        :invariants-unchanged [...]\n                        :status :verified | :unverified}}\n```\n\nThe entity-level decomposition treats the affected paragraph as a typed open cover, each entity carrying its type (`:meta-lede-transition`, `:question`, `:empirical-observation`, `:normative-claim`, `:hedged-closure-mechanism-gesture`, `:parking-closer`, `:closure-owner-reference`, etc.) and its fate under the rewrite.  Each operation is a *local section* — a typed transformation on one entity.  Glue-adjustments are the *compatibility conditions on overlaps* — derived effects on entities adjacent to a local operation (e.g., dropping a meta-lede forces the following clause to capitalize as sentence-initial; dropping a parenthetical hedge forces the preceding clause to grow its own terminal period).  The sheaf-theoretic reading is not metaphor: local sections that satisfy compatibility on overlaps uniquely glue to the global section (the new prose).  A rewrite is *verified* when (i) each operation is well-typed against its target entity's type, (ii) glue-adjustments are exhibited rather than left implicit, (iii) the invariant-delta has no [pass fail] entries, and (iv) the pattern's CHECK clause holds on the new entities at their declared types."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The pattern operates on the axis: prose-correctness <-/-> structural-property-preservation.\n    Irreducible: a rewrite either preserves the typed graph the diagnostic was annotating, or it changes the graph in a way that may invalidate other typed properties.  There is no \"neutral\" textual rewrite — every change to the prose either updates the typed annotation graph or leaves the graph stale."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Real costs: typed rewriting requires that the diagnostic be properly annotated (with a pattern citation, not a free-text complaint), that the cited pattern have explicit COMPOSITIONS, and that the post-state audit be computable.  Each of these prerequisites is itself work.  For one-off prose passes the overhead may exceed the value; the discipline pays off on artifacts that will be rewritten repeatedly, that have evolving structure, or that need to be auditable later.\n    Active dynamic: the pull toward untyped rewriting is the temptation of \"just fix it.\"  Especially in writing work, the rewriter's skill is exactly the ability to fix prose without a spec — typed rewriting can feel like it's slowing the rewriter's strength.  The right framing: typed rewriting doesn't replace the skill; it audits the skill's outputs so they accumulate rather than churn."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "      - Closure without verification: a :before/:after record with no invariant-delta — looks like typed rewriting, isn't.  Common in hand-rolled sweep logs.\n      - Composition-id implicit: :scaffolding-used recorded too coarsely (e.g., the v6 sweep's `(:pattern :annotation)`) without naming which composition from the pattern's menu was applied.  The choice is not auditable.\n      - Invariant-delta absent: the rewrite changes the prose but the audit block isn't updated.  No way to tell whether the rewrite strengthened the typed graph, weakened it, or left it unchanged.\n      - Pattern-citation absent or wrong: a diagnostic without a `:source.pattern-name`, or one citing a pattern whose COMPOSITIONS don't actually fit the diagnostic, is not actionable by typed rewrite.  The diagnostic is a complaint until a fitting pattern exists.\n      - Verification by self-report: the rewriter declares the rewrite \"clean\" without an external check (machine-evaluated CHECK clause, or peer review of the invariant-delta).  `:sweep-status :clean` is not by itself a verification result; it's an assertion.\n      - Diagnostic deleted, not closed: the annotation is removed from the graph after the rewrite.  Provenance lost; future drifts back toward the original failure cannot be detected because the typed precondition that surfaced it is no longer in the graph to be re-evaluated.\n      - Blob-rewrite: the closure records a `:before` paragraph and an `:after` paragraph and treats the substitution as atomic, even when the rewrite plan internally names several structural moves.  The typed plan (\"drop the hedge, replace the parking-closer with a closure-mechanism reference, capitalize the following clause\") is hidden inside a single string swap.  This is the case the entity/operations/glue decomposition exists to refuse.  A blob-rewrite is detectable by the absence of `:entities-before` / `:operations` / `:glue-adjustments` in the closure — even if the textual change is correct, the structural transformation is not exhibited and so cannot be inspected or composed.\n      - Glue-adjustments left implicit: an operation has derived effects on adjacent entities (sentence-initial capitalization, missing terminal period, broken cross-reference) and the rewrite produces the textually-correct result without surfacing these as named adjustments.  The reader cannot tell which textual changes are *operations* (load-bearing) and which are *forced compatibility moves* (consequential).  Under the sheaf reading: the global section appears in the prose but the local sections that produced it are not visible."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Structure each rewrite as f:(A, B) → C with explicit type signature."} {:name "COMPOSITIONS", :name-key "compositions", :slug "compositions", :text "      (1) Type the diagnostic at survey time: every `:annotation/comment` cites a pattern.  If no pattern fits, either find one in the library, write a new flexiarg, or downgrade to a typeless complaint so it's clear the diagnostic isn't yet actionable.\n      (2) Make composition-id explicit: the closure block names which item from the pattern's COMPOSITIONS list was applied.  Compositions are stable per-pattern (their numbering survives flexiarg edits); the closure cites by composition number or canonical name.\n      (3) Record the augmentation: any judgment call the composition required (which words to drop, which live misreadings to preserve, which cross-reference to bind to) is captured in :augmentation.  The composition + augmentation together specify the rewrite uniquely.\n      (4) Compute the invariant-delta: snapshot the audit before applying the rewrite, recompute after, record the delta as `{invariant [before-status after-status]}`.  An empty delta means the rewrite was textual only (which may be fine, but should be visible).  A non-empty delta is the rewrite's structural contribution.\n      (5) Verify before closing: check the cited pattern's CHECK clause holds on the new span.  Check no invariant weakened in the audit.  Only then mark `:status :closed` and stamp `:verification :verified`.\n      (6) Retain the diagnostic, don't delete it: closure preserves provenance.  Future drifts back toward the original failure can be detected because the closed annotation is still in the graph, and the typed precondition can be re-evaluated against the (possibly drifted) prose.\n      (7) Decompose the affected paragraph into typed entities before applying operations.  Each entity carries an `:id`, a `:type` from a registered vocabulary (`:meta-lede-transition`, `:question`, `:empirical-observation`, `:normative-claim`, `:hedged-closure-mechanism-gesture`, `:parking-closer`, `:closure-owner-reference`, `:closure-support-reference`, `:closure-event-reference`, etc. — extensible per pattern domain), and a `:span` quoting its verbatim text.  The entity vocabulary is the typed open cover of the paragraph.\n      (8) Express the rewrite as a sequence of operations on entities, not as a blob substitution.  Each operation names one of `{:drop :keep :add :replace :move}`, the target entity id, a rationale, and (for `:replace` and `:add`) the new entities being introduced — each itself typed and id'd.  The augmentation fields from B map 1-to-1 to entity types in the `:with-entities` of `:replace`/`:add` operations (e.g., `:closure-owner` in augmentation produces a `:closure-owner-reference` entity).  The operations list is the sequence of local sections; the global section (the new prose) is recoverable by applying the operations.\n      (9) Surface glue-adjustments as explicit derived effects.  When an operation forces compatibility moves on adjacent entities (capitalization, punctuation, cross-reference repair), record each as a `:glue-adjustment` with the effect kind, the affected entity, and the operation that caused it.  This is the compatibility-on-overlaps condition made first-class; without it, the reader cannot distinguish operations (load-bearing) from glue (consequential), and rewrites cannot be composed cleanly."} {:name "CHECK", :name-key "check", :slug "check", :text "      Engaged when every closed diagnostic has a complete f:(A, B) → C trace including composition-id, augmentation if any, invariant-delta, and verification status.  A skeptical reader can inspect any closed annotation and reconstruct (i) what the original typed problem was, (ii) what rewrite plan was applied, (iii) what typed property the rewrite preserved or strengthened.  Failing when closure records the textual change but not the structural transformation — i.e., when \"fixed\" cannot be distinguished from \"fixed in a way that compensates for the cost it imposed elsewhere.\""}], :conclusion "Rewriting a diagnostic annotation into a verified improvement is a typed transformation f:(A, B) → C, not a textual edit.  A is the typed diagnostic; B is the rewrite plan (composition-id chosen from the cited pattern's COMPOSITIONS menu, plus any augmentation for judgment calls); C is the post-rewrite tuple of new prose, updated annotation graph, and invariant-delta.  The rewrite is *verified* iff no invariant in the post-state is weaker than in the pre-state AND the cited pattern's CHECK clause holds on the new span.  Without verification, \"fixing the diagnostic\" is hope; with verification, the same typed problem cannot recur because the typed precondition that surfaced it no longer holds.", :directives {:audience "system designers, mission authors, working-paper drafters, tool builders", :keywords ["rewrite" "rewriting-system" "verification" "diagnostic" "annotation" "invariant-delta" "closure" "f-of-A-B-equals-C"], :references ["system-coherence/argument-as-coverage-checklist-vs-system-specification" "system-coherence/turn-design-into-checks" "system-coherence/bind-open-questions-to-closure-mechanisms" "system-coherence/plan-as-enumeration"], :style "pattern", :title "Verified Rewrite from Diagnostic Annotation", :tone "technical"}, :id "system-coherence/verified-rewrite-from-diagnostic-annotation", :keywords ["rewrite" "rewriting-system" "verification" "diagnostic" "annotation" "invariant-delta" "closure" "f-of-A-B-equals-C"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["system-coherence/argument-as-coverage-checklist-vs-system-specification" "system-coherence/turn-design-into-checks" "system-coherence/bind-open-questions-to-closure-mechanisms" "system-coherence/plan-as-enumeration"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/system-coherence/verified-rewrite-from-diagnostic-annotation.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Verified Rewrite from Diagnostic Annotation", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Interaction time is protected when the delivery structure absorbs local variation. Where sessions try to cover everything uniformly, interaction shrinks, core concepts get diluted, and trainers feel unprepared to deliver outside their expertise."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "T3 sessions cover a broad range of open research topics; trainers have diverse disciplinary backgrounds and cannot realistically deliver all content with equal confidence."} {:name "if", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "You want trainers to tailor content to local needs without losing coherence or confidence."} {:name "does-not-apply", :name-key "does-not-apply", :slug "does-not-apply", :text "A pilot with a single, fixed module and no need for local tailoring.\n\nSessions vary in length and audience, and trainers need guidance on what to trim or expand."} {:name "however", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "One-size-fits-all agendas create fatigue, reduce interaction, and leave trainers unable to cover core material; impostor syndrome compounds the problem for professional staff delivering outside their expertise."} {:name "signals", :name-key "signals", :slug "signals", :text "Trainers regularly overrun, report dropping content ad hoc, or describe impostor syndrome about breadth."} {:name "then", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Define a delivery spine: a minimal core (two to three modules) plus named elective modules and a required co-working block for local adaptation. Provide time budgets and \"drop-in\" variants for short sessions."} {:name "because", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "A spine creates predictable structure while legitimising adaptation; it protects interaction time and reduces overload; trainers who focus on what they know deliver more confidently."} {:name "evidence", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "\"My top tips are to either give it more time than you expect\" (T1); \"I didn't feel prepared to talk about all the areas\" (T2A); \"There's a little bit of impostor syndrome, because it's still new to me and no one's taught me it!\" (T3); \"Probably 80% of the stuff I learned... I don't then replicate\" (T2B). COM-B: \"I am psychologically able\" M=4.85."} {:name "mechanism", :name-key "mechanism", :slug "mechanism", :text "A fixed core preserves coherence while electives absorb local variance, keeping interaction time intact and reducing the breadth-versus-depth tension."} {:name "counterfactual", :name-key "counterfactual", :slug "counterfactual", :text "If we deliver the full deck uniformly, interaction time shrinks, core concepts get diluted, and trainers feel unprepared."} {:name "next-steps", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "      - Publish three spine variants (90-min, half-day, full-day).\n      - Add a template for local adaptation notes and decisions.\n      - Include a standard Q&A and co-working segment in every variant."}], :conclusion "  Interaction time is protected when the delivery structure absorbs local variation. Where sessions try to cover everything uniformly, interaction shrinks, core concepts get diluted, and trainers feel unprepared to deliver outside their expertise.", :directives {:audience "trainers, curriculum-designers, cohort-leads", :sigils ["🪡/式"], :style "design-pattern", :title "Protected Interaction", :tone "formal-analytic"}, :id "t3/adaptive-delivery-spine", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🪡/式"], :source-path "library/t3/adaptive-delivery-spine.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Protected Interaction", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Whether training inputs translate into sustained collective practice depends on the institutional architecture that receives them — specifically, whether structures exist to convert individual participation into shared stewardship and to generate evidence of impact that routes back to practitioners and decision-makers. These architectural conditions are both the most important determinants of normalisation and the least developed in the programme studied."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "The systematic weakness of NPT's Collective Action and Reflexive Monitoring domains in the T3 data points beyond input quality to the institutional structures that determine whether training normalises."} {:name "if", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "You want training to produce collective, sustained practice change rather than individual, fragile goodwill."} {:name "does-not-apply", :name-key "does-not-apply", :slug "does-not-apply", :text "Institutions that already have functioning feedback and co-ownership structures for training programmes.\n\nThe programme operates across institutions with no guaranteed local infrastructure for sustaining training outcomes."} {:name "however", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "Architecture-level improvements require changes to the institutional environment — workload models, feedback systems, co-ownership pathways — that the programme can advocate for but cannot unilaterally enact."} {:name "signals", :name-key "signals", :slug "signals", :text "Trainers report isolation after the programme ends; formal sharing channels go unused; impact evidence is anecdotal or absent."} {:name "then", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Prioritise the architectural pair: design co-ownership structures that convert participation into shared stewardship, and feedback systems that generate visible evidence of impact. Recognise that these require institutional partnership, not just programme redesign."} {:name "because", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "Without architecture, training inputs are absorbed by individual effort and decay when that effort is withdrawn; with architecture, the same inputs are multiplied into institutional capacity."} {:name "evidence", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "Feedback-Activation Loop — COM-B item \"effects are being measured\" M=3.35 (lowest across all waves); Co-Ownership Engine — qualitative evidence of sustained engagement only where co-ownership was present (T1 t1-19, T1 t1-25, T3 t3-4)."} {:name "mechanism", :name-key "mechanism", :slug "mechanism", :text "Co-ownership creates distributed maintenance of the practice; feedback creates the evidence base that justifies institutional investment in maintaining the architecture."} {:name "counterfactual", :name-key "counterfactual", :slug "counterfactual", :text "If both patterns remain weak, even excellent training produces the \"absorbed input\" mode — individual goodwill carrying an unsupported practice."} {:name "next-steps", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "      - Map current co-ownership and feedback infrastructure at participating institutions.\n      - Identify minimal viable architectural interventions that do not depend on institutional mandate.\n      - Test whether participatory evaluation designs (Corneli, 2023) can close the feedback loop using the programme's own open research practices."}], :conclusion "  Whether training inputs translate into sustained collective practice depends on the institutional architecture that receives them — specifically, whether structures exist to convert individual participation into shared stewardship and to generate evidence of impact that routes back to practitioners and decision-makers. These architectural conditions are both the most important determinants of normalisation and the least developed in the programme studied.", :directives {:audience "programme-architects, institutional-leads, funders", :sigils ["🏛️/构"], :style "design-pattern", :title "Architectural Pair", :tone "formal-analytic"}, :id "t3/architectural-pair", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🏛️/构"], :source-path "library/t3/architectural-pair.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Architectural Pair", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Training produces capabilities — real freedoms to act — only when conversion factors are institutional, not individual. Where the programme produces functionings (attended, delivered, felt motivated) without conversion factors (workload allocation, feedback infrastructure, contribution pathways), outcomes depend on individual goodwill and do not survive the individual's departure."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Sen (1999) distinguishes functionings (achieved states) from capabilities (real freedoms to achieve those states sustainably), with conversion factors as the conditions that turn resources into capabilities. Open research training gives participants resources — knowledge, materials, motivation — but the question is whether those resources become durable capabilities or transient functionings."} {:name "if", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "You want training outcomes that persist beyond the initial event and do not depend on individual goodwill to sustain them."} {:name "does-not-apply", :name-key "does-not-apply", :slug "does-not-apply", :text "A programme with direct control over all institutional conditions (e.g., a single-site pilot with dedicated staffing and mandated participation).\n\nA programme operates across institutions with heterogeneous infrastructure and cannot control local conditions."} {:name "however", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "Most programme evaluation measures functionings — satisfaction scores, self-reported motivation, delivery counts — without asking whether the conditions exist for those functionings to persist as capabilities. A trainer who has motivation and knowledge but no workload allocation, no feedback system, and no contribution pathway has a functioning; they do not yet have a capability. The three implementation modes observed in this programme are distinguished precisely by their conversion-factor profiles: full (multiplied input), partial (absorbed input), absent (architectural mismatch)."} {:name "signals", :name-key "signals", :slug "signals", :text "High motivation coupled with low institutional support; delivery that depends on individual champions; outcomes that vary by institution despite identical training inputs."} {:name "then", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Design programmes around conversion factors, not just training content. Evaluate whether the institutional architecture creates the conditions under which individual readiness becomes collective capability. Use the five patterns identified in this study as a conversion-factor checklist: Confident Adaptation (clarity), Protected Interaction (structure), Shared Maintenance (distributed stewardship), Contribution Pathways (co-production), Visible Impact (feedback)."} {:name "because", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "The practice gap in open research is a capability gap in Sen's sense — participants have the resources but lack the conversion factors. The intervention-level patterns (Confident Adaptation, Protected Interaction, Shared Maintenance) improve the resource; the architecture-level patterns (Contribution Pathways, Visible Impact) build the conversion factors. Without the latter, the former produce better-prepared individuals in unchanged institutions."} {:name "evidence", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "COM-B survey — motivation M>5.3, institutional measurement M=3.35 (functioning without capability). Qualitative: \"At some point I potentially leave with that knowledge and it doesn't get used as much as it could have\" (T3) — a functioning that does not survive the individual. Multiplied-input mode shows what capability looks like: \"I have integrated it into existing structures of content delivery\" (T1) — the practice operates through structures, not individual holders."} {:name "mechanism", :name-key "mechanism", :slug "mechanism", :text "Conversion factors operate at the institutional level — they are not properties of the individual but of the environment. A trainer in a multiplied-input institution has the same knowledge as one in an architectural-mismatch institution; the difference is whether structures exist that convert individual readiness into collective practice."} {:name "counterfactual", :name-key "counterfactual", :slug "counterfactual", :text "If we evaluate only functionings (did they attend? did they deliver? are they motivated?), we conclude the programme is working even where it is producing goodwill-dependent delivery that will not survive staff turnover."} {:name "next-steps", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "      - Evaluate programmes using capability indicators (are conversion factors present?) not just functioning indicators (did participants report satisfaction?).\n      - Map conversion-factor profiles across institutions before assuming uniform programme impact.\n      - Design the operational signals from each pattern as embedded capability tracking (see t3/participatory-evaluation)."}], :conclusion "  Training produces capabilities — real freedoms to act — only when conversion factors are institutional, not individual. Where the programme produces functionings (attended, delivered, felt motivated) without conversion factors (workload allocation, feedback infrastructure, contribution pathways), outcomes depend on individual goodwill and do not survive the individual's departure.", :directives {:audience "implementation-researchers, programme-architects, funders, evaluators", :sigils ["🌱/能"], :style "design-pattern", :title "Capability, Not Functioning", :tone "formal-analytic"}, :id "t3/capability-not-functioning", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🌱/能"], :source-path "library/t3/capability-not-functioning.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Capability, Not Functioning", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Engagement sustains because participants become co-producers, not just recipients. Where contribution pathways are absent, delivery depends on individual goodwill and decays when that goodwill is exhausted or the individual moves on."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "T3 is designed as a cascade: national training produces local trainers. But cascade models risk treating trainers as delivery endpoints rather than co-owners of the practice."} {:name "if", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "You want training impact to spread beyond individual sessions and sustain over time."} {:name "does-not-apply", :name-key "does-not-apply", :slug "does-not-apply", :text "A centrally delivered programme with no expectation of local trainer autonomy.\n\nTrainers want to share adaptations and sustain activity beyond their first session."} {:name "however", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "Without explicit co-ownership structures — peer cohorts, contribution pathways, mentoring — community benefits are uneven and some trainers feel isolated after the programme ends."} {:name "signals", :name-key "signals", :slug "signals", :text "Trainers ask for peer support, express uncertainty about what happens after the course, or describe isolation."} {:name "then", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Build co-ownership into the programme: dedicated peer cohorts, shared adaptation reviews, and a visible pathway for trainers to contribute improvements and mentor new cohorts."} {:name "because", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "Co-ownership turns individual participation into collective momentum and reduces drop-off after the initial training wave; it is the engine of normalisation."} {:name "evidence", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "\"That ownership felt really important to me; and so then going on and sharing it and integrating it into my own practice\" (T1); \"I wouldn't have delivered that training if I hadn't had this framework\" (T1); \"At some point I potentially leave with that knowledge and it doesn't get used as much as it could have\" (T3). COM-B: \"I see the value\" M=5.37 (high), \"I feel positive\" M=4.21 (moderate — motivation present but not fully activated into ownership)."} {:name "mechanism", :name-key "mechanism", :slug "mechanism", :text "Peer cohorts and contribution pathways convert one-off participation into ongoing shared stewardship, creating distributed maintenance of the practice."} {:name "counterfactual", :name-key "counterfactual", :slug "counterfactual", :text "If contribution pathways are absent, engagement decays after initial delivery and the practice depends on individual goodwill."} {:name "next-steps", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "      - Establish rotating peer cohorts with monthly check-ins.\n      - Create a contribution pathway for trainers to propose updates.\n      - Pair new trainers with a local or regional mentor."}], :conclusion "  Engagement sustains because participants become co-producers, not just recipients. Where contribution pathways are absent, delivery depends on individual goodwill and decays when that goodwill is exhausted or the individual moves on.", :directives {:audience "programme-leads, community-managers, trainers", :sigils ["🤝/共"], :style "design-pattern", :title "Contribution Pathways", :tone "formal-analytic"}, :id "t3/co-ownership-engine", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🤝/共"], :source-path "library/t3/co-ownership-engine.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Contribution Pathways", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Quantitative COM-B survey data (T3 immediate n=52, T3 six-month follow-up n=7, T1 recipient baseline n=15) converge with the qualitative NPT findings: trainers report high motivation and perceived value (M>5.3) but consistently low institutional support for measuring effects (M=3.35), removing obstacles to uptake (M=3.38), and sustaining delivery (M=3.78). The bottleneck is not capability or motivation but opportunity — specifically, the institutional opportunity structures that would convert individual readiness into collective practice."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Qualitative findings identified architectural deficits through focus group analysis; quantitative data from COM-B-aligned Likert surveys provide independent triangulation."} {:name "if", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "You want to confirm that the qualitative patterns are not artefacts of sampling or interpretation."} {:name "does-not-apply", :name-key "does-not-apply", :slug "does-not-apply", :text "Purely qualitative studies or programmes without COM-B-aligned instruments.\n\nA programme has both qualitative and quantitative evaluation data and wants to integrate them."} {:name "however", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "Survey response rates are low (especially n=7 for the six-month follow-up); quantitative findings are directional and triangulating, not inferential."} {:name "signals", :name-key "signals", :slug "signals", :text "Survey items on value and motivation score high; survey items on institutional support and measurement score low; the gap is persistent across survey waves."} {:name "then", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Use the COM-B profile diagnostically: motivation and capability are not the intervention targets; institutional opportunity is. Design programme improvements that target the weakest COM-B component rather than reinforcing what is already strong."} {:name "because", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "When motivation is high and institutional infrastructure is low, adding more motivation-focused interventions (inspirational talks, value messaging) wastes resources; the leverage point is opportunity."} {:name "evidence", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "T3 immediate (n=52): \"I see the value\" M=5.37, \"effects are being measured\" M=3.35; T3 follow-up (n=7): motivation items increase, institutional items static; T1 (n=15): same pattern — value high, effort distribution and measurement low."} {:name "mechanism", :name-key "mechanism", :slug "mechanism", :text "COM-B identifies the binding constraint; NPT explains the social processes through which that constraint operates."} {:name "counterfactual", :name-key "counterfactual", :slug "counterfactual", :text "If we interpret high motivation as programme success and stop there, we miss the architectural deficit that prevents motivation from translating into practice."} {:name "next-steps", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "      - Present the COM-B profile to programme stakeholders as a diagnostic, not a scorecard.\n      - Target interventions at the weakest component (institutional opportunity), not the strongest (motivation).\n      - Repeat the survey after architectural interventions to test whether the profile shifts."}], :conclusion "  Quantitative COM-B survey data (T3 immediate n=52, T3 six-month follow-up n=7, T1 recipient baseline n=15) converge with the qualitative NPT findings: trainers report high motivation and perceived value (M>5.3) but consistently low institutional support for measuring effects (M=3.35), removing obstacles to uptake (M=3.38), and sustaining delivery (M=3.78). The bottleneck is not capability or motivation but opportunity — specifically, the institutional opportunity structures that would convert individual readiness into collective practice.", :directives {:audience "implementation-researchers, programme-evaluators, funders", :sigils ["📊/合"], :style "design-pattern", :title "COM-B Convergence", :tone "formal-analytic"}, :id "t3/com-b-convergence", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["📊/合"], :source-path "library/t3/com-b-convergence.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "COM-B Convergence", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Trainers can adapt confidently because they know what is fixed and what is flexible. Where this clarity is absent, delivery fragments and trainers cannot coordinate their actions or assess whether they are doing enough."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "National T3 programmes attract diverse participants with uneven prior knowledge and shifting institutional mandates; ambiguity about what is required versus optional undermines delivery."} {:name "if", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "You want trainers to act confidently and consistently while still adapting locally."} {:name "does-not-apply", :name-key "does-not-apply", :slug "does-not-apply", :text "A single local team with an agreed curriculum and direct supervision already has alignment.\n\nMultiple cohorts, diverse mandates, and distributed delivery teams need shared expectations."} {:name "however", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "Vague participation rules and undefined completion criteria leave trainers unsure what they can legitimately promise, and delivery fragments across institutions."} {:name "signals", :name-key "signals", :slug "signals", :text "Repeated questions about \"what counts\" or inconsistent module selection across trainers."} {:name "then", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Publish a minimal expectation scaffold: required sessions, optional pathways, and a stated \"ready-to-deliver\" threshold. Provide a short, plain-language briefing on what T3 is and is not, plus a self-check checklist for trainers."} {:name "because", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "Clarity lowers cognitive load, reduces mismatch between expectations and capacity, and improves coordination across distributed trainers."} {:name "evidence", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "\"It wasn't very clear to us how many sessions we needed to have attended, which ones were fundamental\" (T2A); \"I didn't realise when I first signed up for it that these courses were part of this research project\" (T2B); \"It was useful to have a template of what was expected\" (T3). COM-B: \"I understand what this requires of me\" M=4.90."} {:name "mechanism", :name-key "mechanism", :slug "mechanism", :text "Clear requirements reduce ambiguity, which increases trainer confidence and yields more consistent delivery choices."} {:name "counterfactual", :name-key "counterfactual", :slug "counterfactual", :text "If we only add more content or sessions without clarifying requirements, confusion persists and delivery remains fragmented."} {:name "next-steps", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "      - Create a one-page \"T3 in Brief\" with required/optional components.\n      - Define a minimal competency checklist with examples of acceptable adaptations.\n      - Add an onboarding call focused on expectations and scope."}], :conclusion "  Trainers can adapt confidently because they know what is fixed and what is flexible. Where this clarity is absent, delivery fragments and trainers cannot coordinate their actions or assess whether they are doing enough.", :directives {:audience "programme-leads, trainers, policy-owners", :sigils ["🧭/规"], :style "design-pattern", :title "Confident Adaptation", :tone "formal-analytic"}, :id "t3/expectation-scaffold", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🧭/规"], :source-path "library/t3/expectation-scaffold.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Confident Adaptation", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  The programme learns because feedback is structured, visible, and shared. Where feedback mechanisms are thin — optional surveys yielding only positive or absent responses, informal signals like reading the room — the programme lacks evidence of its downstream impact, cannot systematically improve, and cannot make the case for institutional support."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Trainers note that engagement and feedback are crucial for improving delivery and sustaining motivation, but current mechanisms provide almost no usable signal; cross-institutional sharing channels exist but go unused."} {:name "if", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "You want long-term impact and evidence of cultural change beyond one-off sessions."} {:name "does-not-apply", :name-key "does-not-apply", :slug "does-not-apply", :text "Programmes with robust, continuous evaluation already in place.\n\nTrainers report low survey response and lack of evidence about reach; institutional stakeholders ask for impact data."} {:name "however", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "Reliance on optional surveys misses signal; trainers lack feedback on impact and cannot easily demonstrate reach; the absence of a feedback loop means the programme cannot learn or justify itself to institutional stakeholders."} {:name "signals", :name-key "signals", :slug "signals", :text "Trainers cannot point to concrete impact; rely on \"nobody says this was terrible\" as evidence; formal sharing channels unused."} {:name "then", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Establish a lightweight feedback loop: short post-session pulse, 3-6 month follow-up, and a visible impact log. Pair this with a rotation of \"impact stories\" that travel across cohorts."} {:name "because", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "Regular feedback sustains motivation, reveals adaptation needs, supports accountability, and — crucially — generates the evidence base that justifies institutional investment in the architectural conditions the programme needs."} {:name "evidence", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "\"I have no other ways of getting feedback short of people filling in the feedback form\" (T2B); \"As we get like a few answers and nobody says this was terrible, I generally think it's gone quite well\" (T2B); \"Even if you don't get the feedback survey, you can kind of tell whether people are engaged\" (T2B). COM-B: \"effects are being measured\" M=3.35 (lowest item across all waves); T3 follow-up M=3.17 (still lowest at 6 months)."} {:name "mechanism", :name-key "mechanism", :slug "mechanism", :text "Frequent, low-friction feedback plus shared impact stories builds momentum and surfaces improvement needs early; visible impact evidence creates a case for institutional support."} {:name "counterfactual", :name-key "counterfactual", :slug "counterfactual", :text "If feedback is limited to optional end-of-course surveys, impact remains invisible, motivation erodes, and the programme cannot argue for the workload allocation and institutional backing it needs."} {:name "next-steps", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "      - Add a two-question pulse survey after each session.\n      - Run a quarterly impact story round-up shared across cohorts.\n      - Pilot participatory evaluation design (see t3/participatory-evaluation)."}], :conclusion "  The programme learns because feedback is structured, visible, and shared. Where feedback mechanisms are thin — optional surveys yielding only positive or absent responses, informal signals like reading the room — the programme lacks evidence of its downstream impact, cannot systematically improve, and cannot make the case for institutional support.", :directives {:audience "programme-leads, evaluators, trainers", :sigils ["🔁/促"], :style "design-pattern", :title "Visible Impact", :tone "formal-analytic"}, :id "t3/feedback-activation-loop", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🔁/促"], :source-path "library/t3/feedback-activation-loop.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Visible Impact", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Three implementation modes — multiplied input, absorbed input, and architectural mismatch — are distinguished not by training quality or trainer motivation but by the institutional architecture that receives the training. In multiplied-input mode, existing structures amplify the training into sustained collective practice. In absorbed-input mode, individual goodwill sustains delivery without institutional support, creating fragile dependence. In architectural-mismatch mode, motivated trainers face confusion and fragmentation because the institutional environment offers no receiving structure for what they have learned."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Conventional programme evaluation treats success and failure as properties of the training; this pattern reframes them as properties of the institutional architecture."} {:name "if", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "You want to explain why the same training produces divergent outcomes across institutions."} {:name "does-not-apply", :name-key "does-not-apply", :slug "does-not-apply", :text "A programme with direct control over institutional conditions at all delivery sites.\n\nA programme operates across institutions with varying infrastructure and observes divergent outcomes from similar inputs."} {:name "however", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "The modes are not permanent features of institutions; they describe a relationship between input and architecture that can shift if architectural conditions change."} {:name "signals", :name-key "signals", :slug "signals", :text "Same training, different outcomes; motivation high but delivery fragile or absent; trainers describe institutional barriers rather than content gaps."} {:name "then", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Diagnose the implementation mode at each institution before attributing success or failure to the training itself. Invest in architectural conditions (workload models, managerial backing, feedback systems, co-ownership pathways) rather than solely in better inputs."} {:name "because", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "When motivated, capable trainers fail to sustain delivery, the cause is almost never motivational or cognitive — it is architectural. Reframing the problem redirects intervention design from \"better training\" to \"better receiving conditions.\""} {:name "evidence", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "Qualitative implementation modes across four focus groups; COM-B motivation items consistently high (M>5.0) while institutional items consistently low (M<3.8); trainers in failure mode report \"I struggled to fit everything together\" (T2A) despite high engagement."} {:name "mechanism", :name-key "mechanism", :slug "mechanism", :text "Institutional architecture acts as a multiplier, absorber, or deflector of training inputs depending on the presence of workload allocation, feedback infrastructure, and co-ownership pathways."} {:name "counterfactual", :name-key "counterfactual", :slug "counterfactual", :text "If we treat failure as a training problem and respond with better inputs into the same architecture, we repeat the cycle."} {:name "next-steps", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "      - Classify participating institutions by implementation mode.\n      - Design differentiated support: architectural investment for mismatch sites, maintenance support for absorbed sites, peer learning from multiplied sites.\n      - Track mode transitions over time as architectural conditions change."}], :conclusion "  Three implementation modes — multiplied input, absorbed input, and architectural mismatch — are distinguished not by training quality or trainer motivation but by the institutional architecture that receives the training. In multiplied-input mode, existing structures amplify the training into sustained collective practice. In absorbed-input mode, individual goodwill sustains delivery without institutional support, creating fragile dependence. In architectural-mismatch mode, motivated trainers face confusion and fragmentation because the institutional environment offers no receiving structure for what they have learned.", :directives {:audience "programme-evaluators, institutional-leads, implementation-researchers", :sigils ["🔀/态"], :style "design-pattern", :title "Implementation Modes", :tone "formal-analytic"}, :id "t3/implementation-modes", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🔀/态"], :source-path "library/t3/implementation-modes.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Implementation Modes", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Improving the training input — its clarity, structure, and maintenance — reduces confusion, overload, and rework at the point of delivery, but these intervention-level improvements are necessary, not sufficient, for normalisation; a well-designed input delivered into an unreceptive institutional environment still fails to embed."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "Programme evaluation typically focuses on improving the training input; these three patterns capture the intervention-level conditions that the programme can directly control."} {:name "if", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "You want training inputs to be clear, usable, and maintainable across diverse institutional contexts."} {:name "does-not-apply", :name-key "does-not-apply", :slug "does-not-apply", :text "A single trainer with a fixed audience and stable, well-maintained content.\n\nMultiple trainers deliver across institutions with varying local conditions."} {:name "however", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "Even well-designed inputs fail to normalise when the institutional architecture cannot receive them; intervention-level improvements are necessary but not sufficient."} {:name "signals", :name-key "signals", :slug "signals", :text "Trainers report confusion about requirements, overstuffed sessions, or outdated materials."} {:name "then", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Ensure the intervention triad is in place — clear expectations, adaptive structure, and maintained materials — while recognising that these address the input, not the transformation space."} {:name "because", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "The triad reduces friction at the point of delivery; without it, trainers waste effort on ambiguity, overload, and content drift, leaving no capacity for the architectural work that sustains practice."} {:name "evidence", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "Patterns 1-3 each supported by 3+ transcript sources across all four focus groups; COM-B items on psychological capability (M=4.85) and institutional resource appropriateness (M=3.78) corroborate."} {:name "mechanism", :name-key "mechanism", :slug "mechanism", :text "Clarity, modularity, and maintenance free trainer cognitive resources for the harder work of institutional embedding."} {:name "counterfactual", :name-key "counterfactual", :slug "counterfactual", :text "If the intervention triad is absent, trainers exhaust themselves on input-level problems and never reach architectural engagement."} {:name "next-steps", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "      - Audit the programme against each pattern in the triad.\n      - Address intervention-level gaps before expecting architecture-level engagement.\n      - Document which intervention-level improvements are within programme control versus requiring institutional action."}], :conclusion "  Improving the training input — its clarity, structure, and maintenance — reduces confusion, overload, and rework at the point of delivery, but these intervention-level improvements are necessary, not sufficient, for normalisation; a well-designed input delivered into an unreceptive institutional environment still fails to embed.", :directives {:audience "programme-designers, curriculum-leads, trainers", :sigils ["🔧/入"], :style "design-pattern", :title "Intervention Triad", :tone "formal-analytic"}, :id "t3/intervention-triad", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🔧/入"], :source-path "library/t3/intervention-triad.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Intervention Triad", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Materials stay current because stewardship is distributed, not individual. Where maintenance falls to whoever happens to notice a problem, content drifts from practice, local rework multiplies, and trust in the programme's resources erodes."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "The Carpentries lessons are frequently cited as a \"gold standard,\" but trainers rework content locally without a shared process for maintaining or contributing changes."} {:name "if", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "You want materials to remain credible and easy to use across contexts."} {:name "does-not-apply", :name-key "does-not-apply", :slug "does-not-apply", :text "Content that is intentionally static or time-bound with no expected revisions.\n\nTrainers report outdated sections or make repeated local edits that are not shared."} {:name "however", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "Static resources drift from practice; updating is ad hoc, and trainers bear the cost of reworking content alone, adding to the workload burden that is already the most prevalent theme (25% of annotated turns)."} {:name "signals", :name-key "signals", :slug "signals", :text "Recurrent comments that modules are out of date, or multiple divergent local versions exist."} {:name "then", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Treat materials as a living product: assign stewards, publish update cadence, and provide a lightweight change log. Add an adaptation log so local changes can be upstreamed or documented."} {:name "because", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "Named stewardship and cadence reduce drift and enable safe reuse, so trainers spend less time reinventing updates and more time delivering."} {:name "evidence", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "\"You'll see like a gold standard of how to run an intro to reproducible research session\" (T1); \"every time I've done it, I haven't given enough time\" (T1); COM-B: \"policies, procedures, and resource allocation are appropriate\" M=3.78."} {:name "mechanism", :name-key "mechanism", :slug "mechanism", :text "Stewardship distributes maintenance work and creates a feedback channel between local adaptation and central content."} {:name "counterfactual", :name-key "counterfactual", :slug "counterfactual", :text "If updates remain ad hoc, content trust declines, local rework multiplies, and the programme's \"gold standard\" erodes."} {:name "next-steps", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "      - Create a quarterly update rhythm with named maintainers.\n      - Add a one-page adaptation log template to each module.\n      - Prioritise updates for sections flagged as outdated by trainers."}], :conclusion "  Materials stay current because stewardship is distributed, not individual. Where maintenance falls to whoever happens to notice a problem, content drifts from practice, local rework multiplies, and trust in the programme's resources erodes.", :directives {:audience "maintainers, curriculum-owners, trainers", :sigils ["🌱/料"], :style "design-pattern", :title "Shared Maintenance", :tone "formal-analytic"}, :id "t3/living-materials-stewardship", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🌱/料"], :source-path "library/t3/living-materials-stewardship.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Shared Maintenance", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  The programme teaches open research practices — pre-registration, transparent documentation, peer review — yet its own evaluation infrastructure does not use them. A participatory evaluation design in which training events are treated as open research objects could close the Feedback-Activation Loop through the very practices the programme teaches, simultaneously generating evidence of impact and modelling the behaviour it advocates (Corneli, 2023)."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "The Feedback-Activation Loop is the programme's weakest architecture-level pattern; conventional evaluation methods (optional surveys, informal signals) have failed to close it."} {:name "if", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "You want to close the feedback gap without adding a separate evaluation burden."} {:name "does-not-apply", :name-key "does-not-apply", :slug "does-not-apply", :text "Programmes with functioning, well-resourced external evaluation; contexts where open documentation of training is prohibited.\n\nThe programme already teaches documentation, pre-registration, and open sharing, and the feedback loop is demonstrably weak."} {:name "however", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "Participatory evaluation adds complexity and depends on trainer willingness to document and share; institutional compliance requirements may constrain open publication."} {:name "signals", :name-key "signals", :slug "signals", :text "Feedback surveys produce low response and thin data; trainers cannot demonstrate impact; formal sharing channels go unused."} {:name "then", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Pilot a participatory evaluation design: trainers pre-register training rationale and expected outcomes, document delivery, and invite structured peer commentary using open infrastructure (e.g., Octopus.ac). Each training event becomes a small, documented experiment that generates impact evidence as a by-product of delivery."} {:name "because", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "Embedding evaluation in the training activity itself, using the same open practices the programme promotes, addresses the feedback deficit, models the target behaviour, and generates reusable evidence — three functions for the cost of one."} {:name "evidence", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "Present study findings — Feedback-Activation Loop is weakest pattern; COM-B \"effects are being measured\" M=3.35; cross-institutional sharing channels unused. Corneli (2023) proposed this design prior to the present evaluation; the present findings independently validate the need."} {:name "mechanism", :name-key "mechanism", :slug "mechanism", :text "Participatory evaluation converts the feedback loop from a separate administrative task into an integral part of the practice, reducing the additional burden and increasing alignment."} {:name "counterfactual", :name-key "counterfactual", :slug "counterfactual", :text "If evaluation remains external to the training activity, it competes with delivery for scarce time and continues to produce thin, optional data."} {:name "next-steps", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "      - Pilot with 2-3 willing trainers in the next cohort.\n      - Use Octopus.ac or equivalent open platform for documentation.\n      - Compare feedback quality and volume with conventional survey methods."}], :conclusion "  The programme teaches open research practices — pre-registration, transparent documentation, peer review — yet its own evaluation infrastructure does not use them. A participatory evaluation design in which training events are treated as open research objects could close the Feedback-Activation Loop through the very practices the programme teaches, simultaneously generating evidence of impact and modelling the behaviour it advocates (Corneli, 2023).", :directives {:audience "programme-architects, evaluators, open-research-advocates", :sigils ["🔬/开"], :style "design-pattern", :title "Participatory Evaluation as Architecture", :tone "formal-analytic"}, :id "t3/participatory-evaluation", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🔬/开"], :source-path "library/t3/participatory-evaluation.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Participatory Evaluation as Architecture", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Open research training operates within a transformation space — a set of institutional structures, incentive systems, workload models, and feedback mechanisms — that determines its outcomes regardless of input quality. The programme's primary constraint is not the quality of its training inputs but the institutional architecture through which those inputs are — or are not — translated into routine practice."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "A persistent practice gap separates attending open research training from routinely working differently; this is compounded by a research gap — the literature evaluates training content and satisfaction but not the implementation processes through which training does or does not translate into sustained practice change. Because we have not studied implementation, we lack the concepts and evidence needed to diagnose why the practice gap persists."} {:name "if", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "You want to understand why well-designed training does not reliably produce practice change, and why studying training inputs alone cannot answer that question."} {:name "does-not-apply", :name-key "does-not-apply", :slug "does-not-apply", :text "A single-site pilot with full institutional backing and dedicated resources.\n\nA programme operates across multiple institutions with heterogeneous infrastructure and no direct control over local conditions."} {:name "however", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "Most programme evaluation focuses on improving the training input (content, pedagogy, materials) without examining the institutional architecture that receives it; train-the-trainer models add further implementation challenges — translation, institutional support, workload, materials maintenance — each a potential point of failure."} {:name "signals", :name-key "signals", :slug "signals", :text "Motivated, capable trainers report inability to sustain delivery; the same training produces divergent outcomes across institutions."} {:name "then", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Analyse the transformation space: the institutional structures, incentive systems, workload models, and feedback mechanisms that determine whether any training input — however well-designed — produces lasting collective change. Use NPT to map social processes and COM-B to diagnose individual-level bottlenecks."} {:name "because", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "Improving the input is necessary but not sufficient; without architectural capacity to receive, amplify, and sustain what training produces, impact depends on individual goodwill and decays when that goodwill is exhausted."} {:name "evidence", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "T3 immediate survey — motivation items M>5.3, institutional measurement items M<3.4; qualitative implementation modes show architecture, not motivation, as differentiator."} {:name "mechanism", :name-key "mechanism", :slug "mechanism", :text "Institutional architecture multiplies or absorbs training inputs depending on the presence of receiving structures (workload allocation, feedback systems, co-ownership pathways)."} {:name "counterfactual", :name-key "counterfactual", :slug "counterfactual", :text "If we only improve training design without addressing architecture, we produce better-trained individuals in unchanged institutions, and normalisation fails."} {:name "next-steps", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "      - Distinguish intervention-level from architecture-level findings in programme evaluation.\n      - Map institutional receiving structures before designing training inputs.\n      - Design evaluation frameworks that assess architectural capacity alongside training quality."}], :conclusion "  Open research training operates within a transformation space — a set of institutional structures, incentive systems, workload models, and feedback mechanisms — that determines its outcomes regardless of input quality. The programme's primary constraint is not the quality of its training inputs but the institutional architecture through which those inputs are — or are not — translated into routine practice.", :directives {:audience "implementation-researchers, programme-architects, funders", :sigils ["🏗️/场"], :style "design-pattern", :title "Transformation Space", :tone "formal-analytic"}, :id "t3/transformation-space", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🏗️/场"], :source-path "library/t3/transformation-space.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Transformation Space", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "talk4real augments existing doctoral training by embedding meta-learning infrastructure that lets scholars observe, model, and re-engineer the systems they inhabit. [🚢/氏]"} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are positioning talk4real as additive infrastructure rather than a competing DTP."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    Existing DTPs need cross-cohort learning without extra administrative load."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Meta-learning often gets treated as optional or external."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Embed the REPL method inside the programme so it scales across cohorts."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Most doctoral programmes teach disciplinary technique but rarely teach how disciplines learn from one another.\n    talk4real combines ethnography, computer science, and design so scholars can treat conversation, computation, and intervention as one method.\n    The Supplement pilot treats conversation as data and prototype as publication, keeping every scholar inside a continuous REPL loop.\n    The programme operates as a portable connective tissue for other DTPs, enabling cross-cohort self-correction without extra training burden."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Map one concrete integration point with a partner DTP to illustrate fit.]\n    next[Use this pattern to structure the research task on how doctoral training currently runs.]"}], :conclusion "talk4real augments existing doctoral training by embedding meta-learning infrastructure that lets scholars observe, model, and re-engineer the systems they inhabit. [🚢/氏]", :directives {:audience "research funders, doctoral-training consortia, university R&I leads", :sigils ["🚢/氏"], :style "Clear, concrete funding-case prose", :title "2 Relationship to existing doctoral training", :tone "formal-analytic"}, :id "t4r/dtp-fit", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🚢/氏"], :source-path "library/t4r/dtp-fit.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "2 Relationship to existing doctoral training", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "talk4real reimagines doctoral training as a shared experimental learning infrastructure that continually refines itself through reflexive, collective practice. [🏡/工]"} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are framing the executive summary as a compact, inspectable pattern."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The proposal must persuade funders while staying aligned with the pattern canon."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Narrative prose can obscure the reasoning and make claims drift."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Anchor the summary in explicit pattern logic before drafting the final prose.\n    Apply a REPL-style cycle—read, evaluate, print, loop—so ethnography, computational analysis, design, and collective reflection remain linked.\n    Run coordinated doctoral projects that instantiate the same learning architecture in distinct domains.\n    Treat each doctoral scholar as both a researcher and an agent of collective intelligence."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Most doctoral programmes operate within disciplinary or thematic silos, limiting cross-boundary learning and system-level improvement.\n    Collective-intelligence frameworks can capture feedback between researchers, institutions, and communities so research systems learn from their own activity.\n    The model is structured by ethical reflexivity (co-authored representation), lightweight infrastructure (portable method), and polyphonic governance (synthesised voices).\n    Related precedents include initiatives such as realtalk@MIT, PlanetMath.org, and Peeragogy."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Refine the exemplars and verify each claim against the supporting pattern set.]\n    next[Insert 1-2 current, funder-recognisable exemplars of collective-intelligence infrastructure.]"}], :conclusion "talk4real reimagines doctoral training as a shared experimental learning infrastructure that continually refines itself through reflexive, collective practice. [🏡/工]", :directives {:audience "research funders, doctoral-training consortia, university R&I leads", :keywords ["collective-intelligence" "doctoral-training" "reflexivity" "infrastructure"], :sigils ["🚴/了"], :style "Clear, concrete, funding-application English", :title "Executive Summary", :tone "analytic-visionary"}, :id "t4r/exec-summary-tree", :keywords ["collective-intelligence" "doctoral-training" "reflexivity" "infrastructure"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🚴/了" "🏡/工"], :source-path "library/t4r/exec-summary.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Executive Summary", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "talk4real leaves a durable legacy by coupling open, modular infrastructure with graduates who act as agents of collective intelligence. [🌱/友 ➰/互]"} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are explaining how the programme sustains itself beyond the funding window."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    Long-term impact requires both infrastructure and trained stewards."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Single-host programmes often collapse when the grant ends."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Build modular assets and graduate cohorts who can replicate the method elsewhere."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    The programme yields software components, data schemas, and design templates alongside researchers fluent in listening, analysis, and redesign.\n    Each doctoral project produces a self-contained yet interoperable prototype, so sustainability never depends on a single host institution.\n    The Supplement embodies this ethos as a collaboratively authored artefact that is simultaneously document, dataset, and performance.\n    Graduates seed the method elsewhere while the open infrastructure supports new nodes linking universities, civic organisations, and cultural institutions into an evolving ecosystem."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Add a concrete legacy pathway showing how a cohort would seed a new node.]"}], :conclusion "talk4real leaves a durable legacy by coupling open, modular infrastructure with graduates who act as agents of collective intelligence. [🌱/友 ➰/互]", :directives {:audience "research funders, doctoral-training consortia, university R&I leads", :sigils ["🚴/了"], :style "Clear, concrete funding-case prose", :title "5 Legacy and sustainability", :tone "formal-analytic"}, :id "t4r/legacy", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🚴/了" "🌱/友" "➰/互"], :source-path "library/t4r/legacy.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "5 Legacy and sustainability", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "talk4real is justified as an infrastructure for collective intelligence because it converts accumulative, non-reflective research systems into learning systems that can redesign themselves. [🚴/了]"} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are establishing the foundational justification for the programme."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The research system lacks built-in reflexive learning loops."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Existing audits emphasise compliance rather than adaptation."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Introduce a REPL-style infrastructure that turns research into a learning system."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Research policies, tools, and incentives currently pile up without coherent mechanisms for reflection.\n    Institutions generate knowledge faster than they can interpret, while prevailing audits and surveys prioritise compliance over learning.\n    The Supplement’s simulated dialogue, participatory annotation, and iterative synthesis show the research system studying itself in real time.\n    Formalising a social-psychological REPL makes reflexive learning habitual from doctoral study to institutional governance."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Align this rationale with current policy language used by target funders.]"}], :conclusion "talk4real is justified as an infrastructure for collective intelligence because it converts accumulative, non-reflective research systems into learning systems that can redesign themselves. [🚴/了]", :directives {:audience "research funders, doctoral-training consortia, university R&I leads", :sigils ["🚴/了"], :style "Clear, concrete funding-case prose", :title "1 Rationale for the choice of research subject area", :tone "formal-analytic"}, :id "t4r/rationale", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🚴/了"], :source-path "library/t4r/rationale.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "1 Rationale for the choice of research subject area", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "The Supplement operationalises talk4real’s REPL by staging a multi-agent workshop that prototypes listening infrastructure, governance, and design tensions in real time. [👄/口]"} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are using the Supplement as a concrete proof of the REPL method."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    Readers need evidence that the method works in practice."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Abstract descriptions can feel speculative without a live prototype."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Point to the workshop transcript as the operational demonstration."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Facilitators run five segments mapped to Read, Evaluate, Print, Loop with defined roles—facilitator, recorder, reporter, persona agents—to interrogate the brief.\n    Persona statements from researchers, doctoral scholars, developer-scholars, supervisors, administrators, and the talk4real team surface strengths, blind spots, and initial stances.\n    Participants co-design minimal listening captures, consent protocols, tagging vocabularies, and assessment artefacts that treat stories as analyzable yet textured data.\n    Live debate over anonymisation, governance boards, licensing, and preserving dissent keeps the Print phase accountable to what Read reveals, demonstrating the reflexive loop in action."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Publish a short excerpted walkthrough highlighting the REPL phases.]"}], :conclusion "The Supplement operationalises talk4real’s REPL by staging a multi-agent workshop that prototypes listening infrastructure, governance, and design tensions in real time. [👄/口]", :directives {:audience "research funders, doctoral-training consortia, university R&I leads", :sigils ["🚴/了"], :style "Clear, concrete funding-case prose", :title "Supplement workshop transcript", :tone "formal-analytic"}, :id "t4r/supplement", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🚴/了" "👄/口"], :source-path "library/t4r/supplement.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Supplement workshop transcript", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "talk4real can deliver scalable, ethical, self-renewing collective research development with modest resources by leveraging lightweight assets and distributed partnerships. [👫/女 🌞/工]"} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are justifying resource efficiency and partnership leverage in funding prose."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    Funders need to see that support costs stay proportionate to outcomes."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Budget narratives can hide dependencies or inflate required resources."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Ground the support case in concrete partner assets and shared infrastructure."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    The main assets are conversation, computation, and coordination already present in partner institutions, so core costs focus on stipends, workshops, and hosting.\n    Partners such as the UK Reproducibility Network, PathOS, and the Oxford Schwartzman Centre plus allied studios and labs contribute mentoring and in-kind expertise.\n    Open-source software, shared cloud infrastructure, and cross-university collaboration keep financial overhead minimal while maximising reach.\n    Outputs remain freely available, ensuring continuing value beyond the funding window."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Confirm partner commitments and update the support section with named in-kind contributions.]"}], :conclusion "talk4real can deliver scalable, ethical, self-renewing collective research development with modest resources by leveraging lightweight assets and distributed partnerships. [👫/女 🌞/工]", :directives {:audience "research funders, doctoral-training consortia, university R&I leads", :sigils ["👫/女"], :style "Clear, concrete funding-case prose", :title "6 Additional financial or other support resources", :tone "formal-analytic"}, :id "t4r/support", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["👫/女" "🌞/工"], :source-path "library/t4r/support.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "6 Additional financial or other support resources", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "talk4real’s supervisory and training model turns the cohort into a distributed research operating system that learns how learning itself can be designed. [👭/友]"} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are describing how supervision and training uphold the shared REPL method."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The cohort needs a common protocol to keep projects aligned."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Dispersed supervision can fragment practice and standards."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Anchor training in shared rituals and artefacts that keep learning collective."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Supervision is collaborative and polyphonic, pairing disciplinary mentors with methodological companions in ethnography, data science, and design.\n    Training follows the shared REPL rhythm demonstrated in the Supplement, grounding every project in a common protocol.\n    Cohort practices such as listening clinics and signal-boost workshops share data, analyse overlaps, and co-design interventions.\n    Portfolio assessment with open datasets, code notebooks, design artefacts, and open peer review makes each scholar a co-investigator refining the collective infrastructure."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Document the training cadence and attach a sample cohort calendar.]"}], :conclusion "talk4real’s supervisory and training model turns the cohort into a distributed research operating system that learns how learning itself can be designed. [👭/友]", :directives {:audience "research funders, doctoral-training consortia, university R&I leads", :sigils ["👭/友"], :style "Clear, concrete funding-case prose", :title "4 Supervision, training, and cohort-building activities", :tone "formal-analytic"}, :id "t4r/training", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["👭/友"], :source-path "library/t4r/training.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "4 Supervision, training, and cohort-building activities", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "talk4real delivers added value by supplying replicable infrastructural DNA that universities and disciplines can use to build a federated commons for adaptive governance. [🌱/以]"} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are articulating the added value delivered to universities and the field."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    Stakeholders need to see concrete benefits beyond individual projects."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Benefits can seem abstract without visible shared artefacts."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Emphasise the commons outputs and the governance method they enable."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Ten linked doctoral projects—talk4maths, talk4health, talk4policy, talk4arts, and others—test the same REPL architecture in different domains.\n    Each project contributes stories, datasets, and tools to an evolving commons that lets institutions reflect collectively.\n    The Supplement demonstrates cross-voice dialogue, thematic tagging, consent protocols, and reflective synthesis working as a live prototype.\n    Universities gain a practical method for adaptive governance, and the wider field gains a way to study and iteratively improve epistemic systems."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Name 2–3 exemplar domains and link their outputs to the shared commons.]"}], :conclusion "talk4real delivers added value by supplying replicable infrastructural DNA that universities and disciplines can use to build a federated commons for adaptive governance. [🌱/以]", :directives {:audience "research funders, doctoral-training consortia, university R&I leads", :sigils ["🌱/以"], :style "Clear, concrete funding-case prose", :title "3 Added value to the field and the universities", :tone "formal-analytic"}, :id "t4r/value", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🌱/以"], :source-path "library/t4r/value.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "3 Added value to the field and the universities", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Separate FUTON0 Signals from FUTON4 Footholds"} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are constructing a long-term working life oriented around exploration, sustainability, and alignment, rather than throughput or extraction."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    FUTON0 is responsible for keeping you alive and oriented (health, energy, sleep, progress, oxygen levels), and FUTON4 is responsible for making movement possible (conceptual footholds, links, tools, affordances)."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    There is a temptation to blur these roles, which risks either false reassurance (instrumentation without traction) or unsafe exertion (traction without instrumentation)."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Enforce the boundary: FUTON0 only tracks signals that answer \"is continued ascent safe and worthwhile?\", while FUTON4 only records structures that answer \"what movements are possible from here?\""} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Safe exploration depends on separating situational awareness from action affordance; when those collapse, judgement degrades under fatigue and complexity."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[For FUTON0, explicitly enumerate the questions you need answered to stay safe (energy, sleep debt, progress signal, recovery trend) and reject any data that does not clearly serve one of those questions.]\n    next[For FUTON4, treat every hypergraph edge as a potential foothold, not a metric; its value is that it can be used later, not that it reflects current state.]\n    next[Periodically check that FUTON0 never tells you how to climb and FUTON4 never tells you whether you should; if either starts doing the other’s job, you have found a fault line.]"}], :conclusion "Separate FUTON0 Signals from FUTON4 Footholds", :directives {:sigils ["👐/生" "👋/广"], :title "Separate FUTON0 Signals from FUTON4 Footholds"}, :id "transition/f0-f4-boundary", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["👐/生" "👋/广"], :source-path "library/transition/prototype1-transition.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Separate FUTON0 Signals from FUTON4 Footholds", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Persistence Requires Infrastructure"} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are building foundational infrastructure (prototype 1A) intended to support long-term exploratory work rather than to directly achieve outcomes."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    Persistence is interpreted as continuous effort or repeated pushing upward regardless of conditions."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    In real terrain this behaviour is indistinguishable from foolhardiness and reliably prevents both safe summits and safe descents."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Treat prototype 1A as the construction of conditions under which persistence later becomes possible and sane."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Validated persistence requires the capacity to stop, recover, resume, and change route without loss of viability, which cannot exist without prior infrastructure."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Evaluate prototype 1A only against enabling criteria (can future work start, pause, and resume safely?) rather than output metrics.]\n    next[Treat any urge to push just a bit further on the infrastructure as a signal to stop and consolidate.]\n    next[Assess persistence only after the workbench or base camp is used in ordinary conditions, not during its construction.]"}], :conclusion "Persistence Requires Infrastructure", :directives {:sigils ["🏃/功" "👎/引"], :title "Persistence Requires Infrastructure"}, :id "transition/persistence-conditions", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["🏃/功" "👎/引"], :source-path "library/transition/prototype1-transition.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Persistence Requires Infrastructure", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Prototype 1A as Dedicated Workbench"} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are using a bounded high-agency interval to construct foundational tooling intended to support many distinct future lines of work over months and years."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The immediate goal is to enable sustained work on FUTON-5/6/7 rather than to directly produce their outputs."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    There is a persistent risk of mistaking the enabling infrastructure (base camp, workbench, prototype 1A) for either a finished product or a general-purpose appliance."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Frame success as the existence and usability of a dedicated workbench: a tool explicitly designed to make later work tractable, modifiable, and repairable under real conditions."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Long-arc productivity depends less on early outputs than on having a stable locus where work can reliably begin, pause, resume, and be re-tooled without re-inventing foundations."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Articulate in one place what the workbench is for and what it explicitly is not for (no furniture, no appliances, no finished artifacts).]\n    next[Treat FUTON0 instrumentation as the safety system for using the workbench, not as part of the bench itself.]\n    next[Let prototype 1B be evidence-driven: repairs, reinforcements, and extensions only in response to observed strain during real FUTON-5/6/7 work, not anticipated perfection.]\n    next[Use this pattern to frame Release Prototype 1 as a workbench milestone, not a finished product.]"}], :conclusion "Prototype 1A as Dedicated Workbench", :directives {:sigils ["👍/支" "👓/已"], :title "Prototype 1A as Dedicated Workbench"}, :id "transition/prototype1a-workbench", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["👍/支" "👓/已"], :source-path "library/transition/prototype1-transition.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Prototype 1A as Dedicated Workbench", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "The worked-contrast pair carries the multiplicativity claim concretely; it is load-bearing rhetoric, not illustration.  But the pair ages: the cases chosen when the paper was written may no longer span the model's discriminative dimensions as the evidence base evolves.  The service states which composition it is running on the axis between stable anchors (the current pair persists) and evolving anchors (cases replaced as the profile landscape shifts), and binds itself to the discipline that the current pair is named with a date and selection rationale, a succession plan exists, and each cycle reviews the pair's continued validity."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "This implementation pattern specifies the stewardship mechanic behind ukrns/worked-contrast.  The meta-pattern makes a rhetorical claim about paired cases; this pattern addresses the operational question of how the pair is maintained as the programme evolves.  Without a maintenance discipline, the paper's concrete cases drift into stale illustrations that contradict the current model."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "The worked-contrast pair (currently FG2A-P1 + FG3-A) is the concrete grounding of the multiplicativity claim that ukrns/diagnose-before-prescribing rests on; readers and institutions trained on it return to the paper expecting these anchors to remain legible across cycles AND the pair must continue to represent the model's discriminative extremes under the current evidence base."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "A single pair cannot simultaneously be maximally stable across cycles and maximally accurate under evolving evidence.  Stable anchors cost accuracy — the pair may no longer reflect current evidence.  Evolving anchors cost comparability — claims made with one pair are not directly comparable to claims made with another.  The active pull shifts over time: early, when the paper's claims are newly established, stability is load-bearing; later, as evidence accrues and new extremes emerge, rotation becomes more honest."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "      - Stale pair: current pair no longer represents the model's extremes but continues to be cited\n      - Silent replacement: the pair changes without announcement; readers trained on the old pair are disoriented\n      - Succession-plan-missing: no stated plan for when replacement happens or how\n      - Rationale-missing: pair is named but the reason for the selection is not explicit\n      - Case-drift: the scores for the named pair shift under evidence updates but the pair's role as representative is not reviewed"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "The service operates an explicit composition on the axis between stable and evolving anchors, declares it, and stewards the pair against that declaration."} {:name "COMPOSITIONS", :name-key "compositions", :slug "compositions", :text "      (1) Fixed pair: FG2A-P1 + FG3-A remain canonical indefinitely; evolution of the profile landscape is described in annotations.  Gives up accuracy over time.\n      (2) Rotating pair: every cycle (or when a significantly better contrast emerges), the pair is updated.  Gives up cross-cycle comparability; claims in different cycles rest on different cases.\n      (3) Canonical + archive: the canonical pair is named with a date (\"as of Cycle N, the canonical contrast is FG2A-P1 + FG3-A\"); superseded pairs are archived with their cycle for historical comparability.  Gives up simplicity.\n      The paper currently operates at (1) — the pair is named but no stewardship discipline is declared."} {:name "CHECK", :name-key "check", :slug "check", :text "      Engaged when: the current pair is named with a date and a selection rationale (why these two); a succession plan exists (when and how replacement happens); the pair's continued validity is reviewed each cycle.\n      Failing when: the pair is used without review across cycles; the current pair no longer represents the model's extremes but has not been replaced; replacement happens silently without announcement."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Multiplicativity claim is concrete: ukrns/diagnose-before-prescribing rests on the multiplicative D × A gate.  The worked-contrast pair makes this claim inspectable — FG2A-P1 shows delivery-gating, FG3-A shows multi-constraint.  If the pair no longer represents these extremes, the concrete grounding of the multiplicativity claim degrades.\n\n    Rhetorical continuity vs empirical accuracy: readers returning to the paper across versions expect the same anchor cases; institutions that have built mental models around FG2A-P1 and FG3-A will not appreciate silent replacement.  But the paper's empirical honesty depends on the cases being good contrasts.  The composition choice names this tension.\n\n    Succession planning: even under Composition (1), the pair will eventually become untenable.  The pattern requires that a succession plan exist — when and how replacement happens — even if the current pair is fixed.\n\n    Cross-pattern: the pair is downstream of ukrns/assumptions-commons (pair changes require provenance); upstream of ukrns/design-state-lineage (pair changes are versioned events).\n\n    Mechanic, in short: this pattern stewards the worked-contrast pair across cycles; it serves worked-contrast's concrete-grounding claim by holding the axis between stable and evolving anchors explicit; without it, the paper's concrete cases eventually contradict its current claims."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Declare the current composition (most likely (1) for paper time, (3) for operational use)\n    - For each cycle, review the current pair's validity against the current evidence base\n    - If replacement is needed, announce it with the rationale (which new pair, why superseded)\n    - Archive superseded pairs with their cycle identifier for historical comparison\n    - Cross-reference with ukrns/assumptions-commons (pair changes are evidence events) and ukrns/design-state-lineage (changes are versioned)"}], :conclusion "The worked-contrast pair carries the multiplicativity claim concretely; it is load-bearing rhetoric, not illustration.  But the pair ages: the cases chosen when the paper was written may no longer span the model's discriminative dimensions as the evidence base evolves.  The service states which composition it is running on the axis between stable anchors (the current pair persists) and evolving anchors (cases replaced as the profile landscape shifts), and binds itself to the discipline that the current pair is named with a date and selection rationale, a succession plan exists, and each cycle reviews the pair's continued validity.", :directives {:audience "UKRN-S design, working-paper maintainers, diagnostic practitioners, case-authors", :keywords ["implementation-pattern" "worked-contrast" "anchor-cases" "FG2A-P1" "FG3-A" "stewardship" "case-currency" "rotation" "succession"], :references ["ukrns/worked-contrast" "ukrns/diagnose-before-prescribing" "ukrns/assumptions-commons"], :style "implementation-pattern", :title "Anchor Case Maintenance — Stewarding the Worked-Contrast Pair (Implementation Pattern)", :tone "operational"}, :id "ukrns/anchor-case-maintenance", :keywords ["implementation-pattern" "worked-contrast" "anchor-cases" "FG2A-P1" "FG3-A" "stewardship" "case-currency" "rotation" "succession"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["ukrns/worked-contrast" "ukrns/diagnose-before-prescribing" "ukrns/assumptions-commons"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/ukrns/anchor-case-maintenance.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Anchor Case Maintenance — Stewarding the Worked-Contrast Pair (Implementation Pattern)", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "The file assumptions-v2.edn is not a configuration file; it is the declarative record of the programme's evidence state and the input to every computational claim the paper makes.  Its governance is a commons-governance decision: who can change what, who reviews, how versions are tracked, and how contributors earn recognised standing.  The service states which composition it is running on the axis between governed-commons and open-editable.  Every value in assumptions-v2.edn has a traceable provenance (commit, rationale, reviewer if applicable).  The current composition on the governance axis is declared publicly.  Rollback is supported."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "This implementation pattern specifies the mechanics behind ukrns/self-application and ukrns/computation-as-exploration.  Both meta-patterns require assumptions-v2.edn to be declarative, inspectable, and re-runnable; neither specifies who is allowed to change it.  Without an assumptions-commons discipline, the meta-patterns are stances without operational reality."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The pattern operates on the axis: governed-commons (formal change process with review) ↔ open-editable (low-friction contribution with post-hoc audit).\n    Irreducible: a single file cannot be simultaneously maximally low-friction to contribute to AND maximally integrity-preserving."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Real costs: governed costs contribution speed and lowers the friction-gradient that invites participation; open-editable costs integrity of the shared record and risks silent divergence.\n    Active dynamic: early, when contributors are few and the evidence base is small, open-editable suffices; as the network grows and the file becomes consequential for operational decisions, governed-commons becomes more load-bearing."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "      - Silent drift: values change without a rationale message; readers cannot reconstruct the change's purpose\n      - Governance opacity: the current composition is not declared; contributors do not know what process applies\n      - Rollback unavailable: changes are made via live-edit without version history; bad changes cannot be reverted\n      - Commit-message theatre: every change has a message but the messages are uninformative\n      - Contributor invisibility: contributors not credited; their standing in the commons not recognised"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Operate an explicit move on the named axis (see substructure below); steward the pattern against the declared discipline."} {:name "COMPOSITIONS", :name-key "compositions", :slug "compositions", :text "      (1) Single maintainer + pull-request review: one named maintainer owns the file; contributors open PRs; maintainer reviews and merges.  Gives up contributor autonomy; simplest to operate.\n      (2) Working-group review: a small committee reviews all proposals; changes announced on merge.  Gives up speed; distributes authority.\n      (3) Open-editable with audit: anyone may edit; every change logged with contributor identity and rationale; rollback on challenge by any participant.  Gives up veto; places trust in transparency + rollback.\n      These compositions are not ranked; the paper currently operates as (1).  Scaling to network operations may require (2) or (3)."} {:name "CHECK", :name-key "check", :slug "check", :text "      Engaged when: every value in assumptions-v2.edn traces to an identifiable proposal or log entry; contributors can see the change history; rollback is possible.\n      Failing when: assumptions change with no traceable provenance; contributors cannot audit recent changes; the file drifts silently between publications."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Ostrom collective-choice principle: those who sustain the commons earn recognised rights to shape it.  Assumptions-v2.edn is a commons in Ostrom's sense — its maintenance benefits the whole network, its governance decides whose evidence counts.  Per equity/contribution-pathways, governance standing follows contribution, not the reverse.\n\n    Integrity of self-application: if the paper recomputes from assumptions-v2.edn, then uncaptured changes to the file produce argument changes that readers cannot trace.  ukrns/self-application's claim depends on the file's integrity; the commons governance protects that claim.\n\n    Projection into main patterns: ukrns/design-as-function-of-evidence requires that service changes trace to evidence updates.  If the evidence file itself has no update trace, design-state changes cannot be explained.  The commons governance is the precondition for that traceability.\n\n    Mechanic, in short:\n    Mechanic: who can change what in assumptions-v2.edn, under what process.\n    Serves: ukrns/self-application, ukrns/computation-as-exploration, ukrns/design-as-function-of-evidence.\n    Axis: governed-commons ↔ open-editable.\n    Without this pattern: the meta-patterns' integrity claims are aspirational."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Declare the current composition (most likely (1) single maintainer + PR review) in the project README\n    - Ensure every commit to assumptions-v2.edn carries a rationale message (not just a numeric diff)\n    - Announce scale transitions: if the commons moves from (1) to (2) as the network grows, publish the change-of-governance\n    - Cross-reference with equity/contribution-pathways: contributors to the assumptions file earn recognised standing"}], :conclusion "The file assumptions-v2.edn is not a configuration file; it is the declarative record of the programme's evidence state and the input to every computational claim the paper makes.  Its governance is a commons-governance decision: who can change what, who reviews, how versions are tracked, and how contributors earn recognised standing.  The service states which composition it is running on the axis between governed-commons and open-editable.  Every value in assumptions-v2.edn has a traceable provenance (commit, rationale, reviewer if applicable).  The current composition on the governance axis is declared publicly.  Rollback is supported.", :directives {:audience "UKRN-S design, working-paper maintainers, contributors to assumptions-v2.edn, futon developers", :keywords ["implementation-pattern" "assumptions-v2.edn" "commons-governance" "contributor-rights" "rollback" "traceability" "Ostrom" "declarative-data"], :references ["ukrns/self-application" "ukrns/computation-as-exploration" "ukrns/design-as-function-of-evidence"], :style "implementation-pattern", :title "Assumptions Commons — Governance of the Shared Evidence File (Implementation Pattern)", :tone "operational"}, :id "ukrns/assumptions-commons", :keywords ["implementation-pattern" "assumptions-v2.edn" "commons-governance" "contributor-rights" "rollback" "traceability" "Ostrom" "declarative-data"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["ukrns/self-application" "ukrns/computation-as-exploration" "ukrns/design-as-function-of-evidence"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/ukrns/assumptions-commons.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Assumptions Commons — Governance of the Shared Evidence File (Implementation Pattern)", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "A service should only promise demonstration where the measurement base exists to support the claim. Where indicator capacity is partial, the promise must narrow with it."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "The current paper's indicators track distinguishes between practices that can already be monitored at pilot scale and practices that still depend on immature standards, incomplete workflows, or manual checking."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The pattern operates on the axis: broad impact promise ↔ currently demonstrable impact promise."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Real costs: narrower promises can sound less ambitious, but broader promises outrun the measurement substrate and weaken trust."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    State which practices are currently demonstrable, which are still development work, and align service claims with that distinction."}], :conclusion "A service should only promise demonstration where the measurement base exists to support the claim. Where indicator capacity is partial, the promise must narrow with it.", :directives {:audience "UKRN Services design, funders, institutional leads, evaluators", :keywords ["sketch" "indicators" "impact" "measurement" "demonstration" "calibration" "promises" "infrastructure"], :references ["system-coherence/argue-empirically-not-persuasively" "system-coherence/correct-effect-claims-with-relevant-denominators"], :style "sketch", :title "Calibrate Impact Promises to Current Indicator Capacity (Sketch)", :tone "analytic-foundational"}, :id "ukrns/calibrate-impact-promises-to-current-indicator-capacity", :keywords ["sketch" "indicators" "impact" "measurement" "demonstration" "calibration" "promises" "infrastructure"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["system-coherence/argue-empirically-not-persuasively" "system-coherence/correct-effect-claims-with-relevant-denominators"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/ukrns/calibrate-impact-promises-to-current-indicator-capacity.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Calibrate Impact Promises to Current Indicator Capacity (Sketch)", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "The numerical scores in assumptions-v2.edn did not arise spontaneously; they were derived from qualitative NPT coding of focus-group transcripts and then upgraded from textual judgements to [0, 1] values.  Any computational claim in the paper rests on this upgrade.  The service states which composition it is running on the axis between full claim-to-transcript traceability (every numerical claim walks back to specific transcript passages) and aggregate claim-to-score provenance (claims walk back to scored dimensions and coder identity, but not to individual passages).  The coding protocol is documented.  Coder identities are recorded.  The qualitative-to-numerical upgrade is described at the declared granularity.  The composition on the traceability axis is declared.  The data cache is accessible under a declared policy."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "This implementation pattern specifies the empirical-provenance mechanic behind ukrns/computation-as-exploration.  The meta-pattern claims the model makes conditional dependencies inspectable; this pattern addresses the upstream question — are the inputs themselves inspectable back to the qualitative evidence?  Without a provenance discipline, the model's outputs are computable but the inputs are effectively oracle; the claim 'this is what the evidence shows' becomes 'this is what someone coded'."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The pattern operates on the axis: full traceability (every NPT score walks back to specific transcript passages and coder judgements) ↔ aggregate provenance (scores walk back to scored dimensions and coder identity; specific passages not linked).\n    Irreducible: provenance cannot be simultaneously maximally detailed AND maximally lightweight to maintain."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Real costs: full traceability costs curation — passages must be cross-linked, coder notes preserved, access maintained; aggregate costs reverse-checkability — a reviewer asking 'how do you know this score is 0.40?' gets a general answer rather than a specific one.\n    Active dynamic: early, when the scores are newly derived and claims are being actively contested, full traceability is load-bearing; later, when scores have stabilised and been cross-referenced, aggregate provenance may suffice for many readers."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "      - Oracle scores: numerical values in assumptions-v2.edn with no traceable derivation\n      - Lost coder identity: who scored what is not recorded\n      - Undocumented protocol: the upgrade from qualitative code to [0, 1] value has no stated rubric\n      - Cache inaccessibility: transcripts or coded passages exist somewhere but cannot be located\n      - Review-request failure: a reviewer asking for provenance receives an unstructured answer\n      - Granularity-confusion: the paper cites passages for some claims but not others with no declared principle"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Operate an explicit move on the named axis (see substructure below); steward the pattern against the declared discipline."} {:name "COMPOSITIONS", :name-key "compositions", :slug "compositions", :text "      (1) Full claim-to-transcript traceability: every score in assumptions-v2.edn is linked to specific transcript passages with coder notes.  Gives up lightweight maintenance.\n      (2) Aggregate claim-to-score provenance: assumptions-v2.edn states the score, the coder(s), the date, and the scoring protocol; passages are archived but not linked per score.  Gives up reverse-traceability at passage grain.\n      (3) Differential granularity: indicator-level claims (the paper's specific quantitative points) trace to passages; general claims trace to scored dimensions.  Gives up simplicity.\n      The paper currently operates somewhere between (1) and (2) informally — transcript evidence is cited in prose but the assumptions-v2.edn entries do not carry explicit back-links.  The proposed Annex C would make the operating composition explicit."} {:name "CHECK", :name-key "check", :slug "check", :text "      Engaged when: any claim in the paper can be walked back to its empirical source at the declared granularity; coder identity and scoring protocol are recorded; the composition is declared.\n      Failing when: a reviewer asks 'how do you know?' and the answer is unstructured; scores exist in assumptions-v2.edn with no linked provenance; the coding protocol is not documented; coder identity is lost."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Qualitative-to-numerical is load-bearing: the upgrade from textual NPT codes to [0, 1] values is the single most important epistemic step in the paper's empirical chain.  All downstream computation rests on it.  Provenance is the audit trail for that step.\n\n    Reviewer challenge defence: when a reviewer asks 'how do you know that FG2A-P1 has communal-specification 0.20?', the minimum adequate answer names the transcript passages, the coder, and the protocol.  Without provenance, the answer is 'someone coded it' — which is not a defence.\n\n    Coder-in-the-loop: provenance preserves coder identity and judgements.  This serves two purposes: accountability (coders stand behind their work) and governance (future coders can trace how earlier judgements were made, which informs calibration).\n\n    Commons-adjacent: the provenance record is itself a commons artefact.  Its governance (who can add, who can audit, how retention works) mirrors ukrns/assumptions-commons.\n\n    Projection into main patterns: without coding provenance, ukrns/diagnose-before-prescribing's profile signatures cannot be contested by institutions.  A profile's classification depends on its NPT scores; an institution challenging its classification needs access to the provenance to mount a defence.\n\n    Mechanic, in short:\n    Mechanic: how the qualitative-to-numerical upgrade is traced.\n    Serves: computation-as-exploration's inspectability claim at the upstream end.\n    Axis: full traceability ↔ aggregate provenance.\n    Without this pattern: the empirical base is oracle, the upgrade is private, and the paper's computational claims rest on unauditable judgements."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Produce Annex C (proposed in the writing plan) that documents the coding protocol, coder identities, and the qualitative-to-numerical upgrade\n    - Declare the current composition on the traceability axis\n    - Establish the data cache: where are the transcripts, the codebook, the coded passages, the upgrade mapping stored, and who has access\n    - For composition (1) or (3): add back-links in assumptions-v2.edn (via a sidecar file or embedded metadata) pointing to passages\n    - Cross-reference with ukrns/assumptions-commons (provenance is part of evidence governance)"}], :conclusion "The numerical scores in assumptions-v2.edn did not arise spontaneously; they were derived from qualitative NPT coding of focus-group transcripts and then upgraded from textual judgements to [0, 1] values.  Any computational claim in the paper rests on this upgrade.  The service states which composition it is running on the axis between full claim-to-transcript traceability (every numerical claim walks back to specific transcript passages) and aggregate claim-to-score provenance (claims walk back to scored dimensions and coder identity, but not to individual passages).  The coding protocol is documented.  Coder identities are recorded.  The qualitative-to-numerical upgrade is described at the declared granularity.  The composition on the traceability axis is declared.  The data cache is accessible under a declared policy.", :directives {:audience "UKRN-S design, working-paper maintainers, reviewers auditing claims, future coders", :keywords ["implementation-pattern" "NPT-coding" "provenance" "traceability" "qualitative-to-numerical" "inter-rater" "cache" "audit" "empirics"], :references ["ukrns/computation-as-exploration" "ukrns/assumptions-commons"], :style "implementation-pattern", :title "Coding Provenance — Traceability of NPT Qualitative Coding (Implementation Pattern)", :tone "operational"}, :id "ukrns/coding-provenance", :keywords ["implementation-pattern" "NPT-coding" "provenance" "traceability" "qualitative-to-numerical" "inter-rater" "cache" "audit" "empirics"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["ukrns/computation-as-exploration" "ukrns/assumptions-commons"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/ukrns/coding-provenance.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Coding Provenance — Traceability of NPT Qualitative Coding (Implementation Pattern)", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Use computation to expose what the design depends on, not to pretend certainty the evidence does not support.  In the current paper, the model is doing four main jobs: showing how delivery and institutional architecture interact, checking what happens when key roles are removed, testing whether the design can plausibly pay for itself, and naming what would falsify its claims.  That is a viability-checking use of computation, not a forecast.  The discipline is to keep the model inspectable, to keep its assumptions separate from its conclusions, and to say plainly when a result is exploratory rather than predictive."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "The current working paper no longer uses computation as a general aura of rigour.  It uses it where prose alone would be too loose.  The simulation anchors delivery in measured training activity and architecture in qualitative evidence, then asks concrete questions: where do institutions sit under the design, what breaks if the Evaluator disappears, what changes if the Researcher-Advisor disappears, and does the unit economics look plausible inside the serviceable market.  Those are design-checking questions.  They help the paper move from argument into launch without claiming to know the future."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The pattern operates on the axis: computation-as-exploration ↔ computation-as-prediction.\n    Irreducible: a model that keeps its assumptions open and contestable is honest about uncertainty, but it cannot also speak with the false certainty of a forecast.  A model that speaks as forecast usually hides more of its own construction."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Real costs: exploratory computation is more honest and more inspectable, but it is rhetorically weaker than a clean prediction.  Predictive computation can support sharper commitments, but if the evidence base is still thin it becomes overclaiming dressed as method.\n    Active dynamic: the right position can change over time.  Early in a service's life, exploration and stress-testing are the honest use.  Later, after Year-1 evidence accumulates, some narrower predictive claims may become defensible."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "      - Model-as-oracle: simulation output is cited as if it were observed fact\n      - Decorative quantification: numbers are added for authority, not because they answer a specific design question\n      - Hidden assumptions: the reader cannot tell what inputs or structural choices the result depends on\n      - Unsupported precision: exact-looking outputs are narrated more confidently than the evidence warrants\n      - Single-score collapse: a rich design question is flattened into one number that silently acts like a recommendation\n      - Detached computation: the model produces outputs that are not tied to refutation conditions, launch checks, or operational decisions"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Use computation where it sharpens design judgement, and stop before it starts pretending to be more than that."} {:name "COMPOSITIONS", :name-key "compositions", :slug "compositions", :text "      (1) Design-checking only: use the model to expose dependencies, stress-test roles, and test viability claims, while keeping future predictions off the table.  This is the current paper's position.\n      (2) Design-checking plus bounded planning scenarios: keep the model exploratory, but also use it to compare a few explicit operating scenarios whose assumptions are published.  Gives up some simplicity.\n      (3) Predictive reporting: use the model to make forward claims on which commitments directly rest.  Gives up much of the epistemic caution and should be reserved for a later evidence state.\n      These compositions are not ranked in the abstract.  The point is to say which one the service is using and why."} {:name "CHECK", :name-key "check", :slug "check", :text "      Engaged when: each figure or result answers a specific design question; assumptions are inspectable; sensitivity or verification is reported where it matters; and any predictive language is clearly marked as such.\n      Failing when: simulation output substitutes for evidence; the reader cannot see what the result depends on; or the computation produces no operational consequence beyond looking impressive."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Some dependencies are hard to hold in prose alone: The design depends on interactions between delivery, institutional architecture, role placement, and timing.  Computation helps make those interactions inspectable instead of leaving them implicit.\n\n    Verification needs more than assertion: The role-removal asymmetry, the boundary-band result, and the economics crossover are all examples where the model is useful because it checks the design's logic against a formal structure rather than merely restating the author's belief.\n\n    Falsifiability improves when the model is tied to explicit failure conditions: The strongest use of computation in the current paper is not that it produces numbers.  It is that those numbers are linked to claims that could be shown false in Year 1.\n\n    Exploration is not anti-rigour: A prior, a simulation, or a scenario walk can still be rigorous if the assumptions are clear, the question is well posed, and the result is not oversold.  What matters is not avoiding computation, but keeping it proportionate to the evidence.\n\n    Mechanic, in short:\n    Derived from the paper's use of simulation to test viability, role dependence, and affordability without treating the model as a forecast engine.\n    Describes one way the service can make design reasoning inspectable when the question is too interdependent for prose alone.\n    Mapped to the learning loop between design and verification: the model helps check whether the proposed system hangs together before the service commits to instantiate it.\n    Coordination requirement: Low to medium at paper time, higher if the service later turns the model into a live operational tool."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Keep assumptions and code paths inspectable enough that a reader can see what each result depends on\n    - Tie every computational output to a concrete design question or refutation condition\n    - Re-run the model after substantive Year-1 evidence arrives, and publish what changed in the service-facing interpretation\n    - Keep operational reporting plainer than the underlying harness: the code can be detailed while the service story stays decision-facing\n    - Avoid making predictive claims until the evidence base is strong enough to justify them explicitly"}], :conclusion "Use computation to expose what the design depends on, not to pretend certainty the evidence does not support.  In the current paper, the model is doing four main jobs: showing how delivery and institutional architecture interact, checking what happens when key roles are removed, testing whether the design can plausibly pay for itself, and naming what would falsify its claims.  That is a viability-checking use of computation, not a forecast.  The discipline is to keep the model inspectable, to keep its assumptions separate from its conclusions, and to say plainly when a result is exploratory rather than predictive.", :directives {:audience "UKRN-S design, working-paper authors, evaluators, futon developers, readers of the WP", :keywords ["simulation" "viability-check" "inspectability" "prior-not-posterior" "exploration-vs-prediction" "role-removal" "unit-economics" "refutation"], :references ["ukrns/diagnose-before-prescribing" "ukrns/measure-where-least-sure" "ukrns/design-as-function-of-evidence" "ukrns/self-application"], :style "meta-pattern", :title "Use Computation to Expose Dependencies", :tone "analytic-foundational"}, :id "ukrns/computation-as-exploration", :keywords ["simulation" "viability-check" "inspectability" "prior-not-posterior" "exploration-vs-prediction" "role-removal" "unit-economics" "refutation"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["ukrns/diagnose-before-prescribing" "ukrns/measure-where-least-sure" "ukrns/design-as-function-of-evidence" "ukrns/self-application"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/ukrns/computation-as-exploration.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Use Computation to Expose Dependencies", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "The service's current composition on the openness/governance Pareto is not a retrospective characterisation; it is a stated position the service can point to before events run and institutions can contest.  The service states which composition it is running on the axis between cycle-level declaration (one statement per annual cycle, events read through it) and event-level declaration (every service event carries its two-axis costing at the point of commitment).  The current cycle's Pareto composition is publicly declared.  Each event within the cycle carries its two-axis costing.  Institutions have a declared contestation channel."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "This implementation pattern specifies the publication mechanic behind ukrns/open-the-triangle.  The main pattern's invariant requires every service event to be explicitly costed on two axes and a stopping point stated.  Without a publication discipline, costing happens internally and the stopping point is a post-hoc reading of what the service happened to do."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The pattern operates on the axis: cycle-level declaration (one position published per cycle, events run under it) ↔ event-level declaration (each event carries its own two-axis costing at the point of commitment).\n    Irreducible: cannot be simultaneously maximally event-flexible and maximally cycle-coherent."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Real costs: cycle-level costs event-specific flexibility — events within the cycle are constrained by the declared stopping point; event-level costs coherence — the cycle's overall shape is only reconstructible post hoc.\n    Active dynamic: early in a programme, events are few and cycle-level suffices; as events multiply and become heterogeneous, event-level becomes more accurate."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "      - Post-hoc characterisation: the composition is reported as what happened rather than declared as what will happen\n      - Silent events: service events run without costing\n      - Cycle-declared, event-divergent: events within the cycle violate the declared position without announcement\n      - No contestation channel: institutions cannot engage the composition choice before events run\n      - Declaration-as-boilerplate: the declaration is published but carries no specific reasoning"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Operate an explicit move on the named axis (see substructure below); steward the pattern against the declared discipline."} {:name "COMPOSITIONS", :name-key "compositions", :slug "compositions", :text "      (1) Per-cycle declaration: at the start of each annual (or other) cycle, the service publishes its current Pareto composition — which of the three compositions from ukrns/open-the-triangle it is running (low-governance loop / governance-first / subset pilot).  Events run under the declared position.  Gives up within-cycle flexibility.\n      (2) Per-event declaration: every service event (T1 wave, peer-evaluation pilot, scaffold publication) carries its openness gain and governance cost at the point of commitment; the cycle's overall position is the aggregate.  Gives up cycle-level coherence.\n      (3) Both cycle-level + event-level: the cycle declares the stopping position; events reconfirm or flag divergence.  Gives up simplicity.\n      The paper currently describes the pattern and includes a Pareto table (§4) but does not declare an operational composition."} {:name "CHECK", :name-key "check", :slug "check", :text "      Engaged when: the current composition is statable and reference-able at any time; institutions know which composition applies to a given event; contestation is possible before events run.\n      Failing when: composition is a post-hoc characterisation; institutions discover what happened after the fact; events run under no declared position; the paper describes the axis but operational practice ignores it."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Contestability before the fact: the purpose of declaration is to allow institutions to engage the composition choice before events run — so network input can shape the position rather than merely observe it.  A post-hoc characterisation cannot be contested.\n\n    Governance-cost as commons good: the governance costs listed in the Pareto table are borne by institutions (consent extensions, data-sharing infrastructure).  Declaration makes those costs visible to the institutions that will pay them, and allows negotiation about who pays and when.\n\n    Two-axis costing is asymmetric: openness gains often accrue to the network; governance costs often accrue to specific institutions.  Declaration exposes this asymmetry so the network can see who is being asked to bear what.\n\n    Coordination with design-state lineage: cycle and event declarations are candidate entries in ukrns/design-state-lineage.  The declaration mechanic feeds the versioning mechanic.\n\n    Mechanic, in short:\n    Mechanic: how the current openness/governance composition is declared and contestable.\n    Serves: open-the-triangle's invariant.\n    Axis: cycle-level ↔ event-level declaration.\n    Without this pattern: the axis exists in the paper but not in operations."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - At each cycle boundary, publish the current composition (low-governance loop / governance-first / subset pilot) and the reasoning\n    - For each event within the cycle, cost it on the two axes and note whether it is consistent with the cycle declaration\n    - Provide a contestation channel: institutions can flag events they think violate the declared position\n    - Cross-reference with ukrns/design-state-lineage (cycle declarations are versioned design-state changes)"}], :conclusion "The service's current composition on the openness/governance Pareto is not a retrospective characterisation; it is a stated position the service can point to before events run and institutions can contest.  The service states which composition it is running on the axis between cycle-level declaration (one statement per annual cycle, events read through it) and event-level declaration (every service event carries its two-axis costing at the point of commitment).  The current cycle's Pareto composition is publicly declared.  Each event within the cycle carries its two-axis costing.  Institutions have a declared contestation channel.", :directives {:audience "UKRN-S design, participating institutions, funders tracking cycle decisions", :keywords ["implementation-pattern" "Pareto-position" "openness-governance" "cycle-declaration" "event-level-costing" "composition-publication"], :references ["ukrns/open-the-triangle"], :style "implementation-pattern", :title "Cycle Position Publication — Declaring the Current Pareto Composition (Implementation Pattern)", :tone "operational"}, :id "ukrns/cycle-position-publication", :keywords ["implementation-pattern" "Pareto-position" "openness-governance" "cycle-declaration" "event-level-costing" "composition-publication"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["ukrns/open-the-triangle"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/ukrns/cycle-position-publication.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Cycle Position Publication — Declaring the Current Pareto Composition (Implementation Pattern)", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "A learning system should change because evidence says it should, not because habit, enthusiasm, or fashion pushes it around.  But it should not change so often that the institutions inside it cannot plan against a stable offer.  The current paper turns that tension into an operating discipline: quarterly checks, annual indicators, stakeholder walks, and a Year-2 commitment decision in which continue, vary, and stand down are all genuinely live.  A design change should be traceable to evidence; the absence of a design change should also be defensible."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "The current working paper no longer frames this mainly as a model-update problem.  It frames it as an operational commitment.  Year 1 is a real launch, not a symbolic pilot.  The service runs seven quarterly yes/no checks on itself, performs three annual stakeholder walks to see whether the operation can actually be used without the writing team in the room, and then makes a Year-2 decision on the basis of what was learned.  The point of the pattern is to make sure evidence terminates in consequences rather than accumulating harmlessly at the edge of the system."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The pattern operates on the axis: responsiveness ↔ stability.\n    Irreducible: a service cannot be maximally responsive to each new signal and maximally stable for the institutions trying to use it.  Every operating model chooses a position on that axis."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Real costs: responsiveness catches problems early and can improve fit quickly, but too much of it turns the service into a moving target.  Stability makes planning easier, but too much of it leaves a weak design in place after evidence has already shown the weakness.  Neither end is free.\n    Active dynamic: the right balance changes over time.  Early in a new service, the case for adjustment is stronger because many uncertainties are still open.  Later, once the operating rhythm and evidence chain are established, stability deserves more weight."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "      - Evidence-without-change: checks and evaluation happen, but nothing downstream ever moves\n      - Change-without-evidence: the service changes shape, scope, or emphasis without a clear evidential basis\n      - Permanent-pilot: Year 1 produces learning but no real commitment decision ever arrives\n      - Hidden variation: operational changes are made informally and never surfaced as explicit choices\n      - No-stand-down option: continuation is assumed, so verification has no real teeth\n      - Quarterly thrash: every small signal produces operational rework before anyone can tell whether it matters"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Choose an explicit position on the axis and make the consequences visible."} {:name "COMPOSITIONS", :name-key "compositions", :slug "compositions", :text "      (1) Quarterly adjustment inside an annual frame: run regular checks and allow limited in-year variation, but reserve the largest commitments for the annual decision point.  This is the position closest to the current paper.  It gives up perfect immediacy in exchange for manageability.\n      (2) Annual-batch revision: hold the design largely stable through the year and revise mainly at year-end.  Gives up speed of correction.\n      (3) Continuous operational revision: treat each new signal as a potential live change to the service.  Gives up predictability and makes governance harder.\n      These compositions are not ranked in the abstract.  The point is that the service should be able to say which one it is using and why."} {:name "CHECK", :name-key "check", :slug "check", :text "      Engaged when: each material operational change can be tied back to evidence; the seven quarterly checks are actually run; the annual stakeholder walks expose whether the service is usable by the actors who must carry it; the Year-2 decision can continue, vary, or stop.\n      Failing when: learning is gathered but has no consequences; the operation changes for reasons no one can inspect; stand-down is named but never really available."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Evidence only matters if it changes consequences: The paper's live claim is not just that evaluation should happen, but that it should terminate in decisions.  Otherwise the design is not a learning system; it is a reporting system.\n\n    Year-1 launch makes the discipline operational: The quarterly checks, the annual indicators, and the stakeholder walks are the mechanism by which evidence enters the design.  They are not side documentation.  They are the operating discipline that prevents both complacency and panic.\n\n    Stand-down is part of the pattern: A Year-2 choice between continue and vary is not enough if stopping was never a real option.  The stand-down path is what makes the verification posture credible.  If the service cannot fail in principle, it cannot learn honestly in practice.\n\n    Relationship to participation and visible impact: This pattern still answers the same structural concern as the older library language: if people contribute evidence or downstream experience, that contribution should matter.  The service demonstrates that it matters by letting the evidence chain end in visible operational consequence rather than in a dead archive.\n\n    Mechanic, in short:\n    Derived from the paper's Year-1 launch discipline: quarterly checks, annual stakeholder walks, and an explicit Year-2 commitment decision.\n    Answers the general problem of evidence that never changes the system it describes.\n    Mapped to the learning loop at the update step: after diagnosis, provisioning, delivery, and evaluation, something must be allowed to change.\n    Coordination requirement: High.  The service, institutions, and partners all need to know what can change when, and on what basis."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Publish the seven quarterly checks as part of the operating model, not as an internal afterthought\n    - Record any material in-year variation in the service and the evidence that prompted it\n    - Use the three annual stakeholder walks as decision tests rather than as presentational exercises\n    - Keep continue, vary, and stand down genuinely live at the Year-2 decision point\n    - Use this pattern downstream of measurement and cross-institutional comparison, where evidence is mature enough to justify operational consequence"}], :conclusion "A learning system should change because evidence says it should, not because habit, enthusiasm, or fashion pushes it around.  But it should not change so often that the institutions inside it cannot plan against a stable offer.  The current paper turns that tension into an operating discipline: quarterly checks, annual indicators, stakeholder walks, and a Year-2 commitment decision in which continue, vary, and stand down are all genuinely live.  A design change should be traceable to evidence; the absence of a design change should also be defensible.", :directives {:audience "UKRN-S design, UKRN leadership, Research England, participating institutions, trainer community", :keywords ["responsiveness" "stability" "quarterly-checks" "annual-indicators" "stakeholder-walks" "year-2-decision" "continue-vary-stand-down" "thrash" "ossification"], :references ["equity/contribution-pathways" "equity/visible-impact" "ukrns/open-the-triangle" "ukrns/measure-where-least-sure"], :style "pattern", :title "Treat Design as a Function of Evidence", :tone "analytic-foundational"}, :id "ukrns/design-as-function-of-evidence", :keywords ["responsiveness" "stability" "quarterly-checks" "annual-indicators" "stakeholder-walks" "year-2-decision" "continue-vary-stand-down" "thrash" "ossification"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["equity/contribution-pathways" "equity/visible-impact" "ukrns/open-the-triangle" "ukrns/measure-where-least-sure"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/ukrns/design-as-function-of-evidence.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Treat Design as a Function of Evidence", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "A service that claims design updates as a function of evidence must be able to show, for any point in time, (a) the design state that was active then, (b) the evidence that produced it, and (c) the update-policy clause that authorised the update.  The service states which composition it is running on the axis between fine-grained versioning (every change versioned) and coarse-grained versioning (changes batched into versioned releases).  Every design-state change is versioned and traces to (evidence update identifier, policy clause).  The composition on the versioning axis is declared.  Past design states are reconstructible."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "This implementation pattern specifies the versioning mechanic behind ukrns/design-as-function-of-evidence.  The pattern's invariant-statement requires every service change to trace to an evidence update and policy clause; without a lineage mechanism, that requirement is unverifiable.  Design-state lineage is what makes the main pattern's traceability claim operational."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The pattern operates on the axis: fine-grained versioning (every change versioned separately, full evidence-trace per change) ↔ coarse-grained versioning (changes batched into periodic releases).\n    Irreducible: a single versioning strategy cannot simultaneously maximise per-change traceability and minimise release noise."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Real costs: fine-grained costs overhead — more version tags, more release noise; coarse-grained costs traceability — readers cannot see individual changes within a release.\n    Active dynamic: early in a programme, changes are few and coarse-grained suffices; as update cadence rises (under a continuous or threshold policy from ukrns/design-as-function-of-evidence), fine-grained becomes more informative."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "      - Untraced change: design state updates without a recorded reason\n      - Policy-clause-missing: change traces to evidence but not to the policy clause that authorised it\n      - Reconstruction-impossible: past design states cannot be recovered from the lineage\n      - Fine-grained-overload: every recompute gets a version; release noise overwhelms readers\n      - Coarse-grained-opaque: cycle-release rolls up so many changes that nothing specific is traceable\n      - Ad hoc versioning: version numbers exist but carry no consistent meaning"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Operate an explicit move on the named axis (see substructure below); steward the pattern against the declared discipline."} {:name "COMPOSITIONS", :name-key "compositions", :slug "compositions", :text "      (1) Per-change versioning: every update gets its own version tag and evidence-trace.  Gives up version-count simplicity; highest traceability.\n      (2) Per-cycle versioning: quarterly or per-T1-wave releases batch internal changes into a single version tag; internal changes logged separately but not versioned.  Gives up per-change traceability.\n      (3) Dual-track: canonical versions (per-cycle, citable) + internal log (per-change, for reconstruction).  Gives up simplicity; both audiences served.\n      The paper currently operates informally through git history; operational use at UKRN-S requires a declared composition."} {:name "CHECK", :name-key "check", :slug "check", :text "      Engaged when: the design state at any past date is reconstructible; every change traces to an evidence update identifier and an update-policy clause; the chosen composition (fine / coarse / dual) is declared.\n      Failing when: past design states cannot be reconstructed; changes trace to \"updated 2026-05\" with no evidence identifier; the policy clause that authorised each change is not recorded."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Traceability is the main pattern's invariant: ukrns/design-as-function-of-evidence requires every service change to trace to evidence + policy clause.  Lineage makes this trace constructible for any historical moment, not only the present.\n\n    Lineage vs log: a log records changes; a lineage preserves the ability to reconstruct the state at any past point.  Institutions that planned around the design state at time T need to be able to retrieve that state, not just read the change log.\n\n    Cross-pattern coordination: this pattern operates downstream of ukrns/assumptions-commons (evidence provenance) and ukrns/model-recompute-schedule (rerun events); it operates upstream of ukrns/publication-cadence (which versions get canonicalised).  A coherent lineage mechanism is the backbone of the implementation-pattern stack.\n\n    Commons accountability: institutions contributing evidence should be able to see their observation's effect on the design state.  Without lineage, the contribution-pathway (equity/contribution-pathways) terminates in opacity.\n\n    Mechanic, in short:\n    Mechanic: how design-state changes are versioned and traced.\n    Serves: design-as-function-of-evidence's traceability invariant.\n    Axis: fine-grained ↔ coarse-grained.\n    Without this pattern: the main pattern's invariant cannot be checked."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Declare the current composition in project documentation\n    - Make each design-state change carry: version identifier, evidence revision, policy clause, date\n    - Expose the lineage as a queryable history (even a simple chronological log is better than none)\n    - Cross-reference with ukrns/assumptions-commons (evidence provenance) and ukrns/publication-cadence (which versions are canonical)\n    - Provide a reconstruction example: \"here is how to retrieve the design state as of 2026-Q1\""}], :conclusion "A service that claims design updates as a function of evidence must be able to show, for any point in time, (a) the design state that was active then, (b) the evidence that produced it, and (c) the update-policy clause that authorised the update.  The service states which composition it is running on the axis between fine-grained versioning (every change versioned) and coarse-grained versioning (changes batched into versioned releases).  Every design-state change is versioned and traces to (evidence update identifier, policy clause).  The composition on the versioning axis is declared.  Past design states are reconstructible.", :directives {:audience "UKRN-S design, working-paper maintainers, institutions tracking service-state changes, funders auditing design history", :keywords ["implementation-pattern" "versioning" "design-state" "lineage" "traceability" "change-history" "evidence-trace" "fine-grained" "coarse-grained"], :references ["ukrns/design-as-function-of-evidence" "ukrns/assumptions-commons" "ukrns/model-recompute-schedule" "ukrns/publication-cadence"], :style "implementation-pattern", :title "Design-State Lineage — Versioned Service-Design Changes (Implementation Pattern)", :tone "operational"}, :id "ukrns/design-state-lineage", :keywords ["implementation-pattern" "versioning" "design-state" "lineage" "traceability" "change-history" "evidence-trace" "fine-grained" "coarse-grained"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["ukrns/design-as-function-of-evidence" "ukrns/assumptions-commons" "ukrns/model-recompute-schedule" "ukrns/publication-cadence"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/ukrns/design-state-lineage.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Design-State Lineage — Versioned Service-Design Changes (Implementation Pattern)", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "A training service should diagnose the main constraint before choosing its response.  In the current paper's terms, the important distinction is whether an institution is mainly constrained by delivery, by institutional architecture, by both, or by a substrate problem the service cannot lift within scope.  A single offer for everyone is easy to run and easy to explain, but it misfits a visible part of the network.  A diagnosed offer states what seems to be binding, says what that implies for the service response, and leaves room for revision when later evidence shows the diagnosis was wrong or incomplete."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "The current working paper separates two main dimensions of the problem.  Delivery asks whether training is actually reaching people and being taken up.  Institutional architecture asks whether the setting around the training can hold and use what is being delivered.  The design also distinguishes what it can affect directly, what it can affect only indirectly, and what sits outside its scope.  Verification then shows that institutions do not all sit in the same place: some are mainly delivery-constrained, some mainly architecture-constrained, some constrained on both, and some blocked by wider substrate conditions that the service alone cannot shift."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The pattern operates on the axis: universal offer ↔ diagnosed offer.\n    Irreducible: a service cannot maximise both uniformity and fit at the same time.  The more the offer is standardised, the easier it is to communicate and run.  The more it is diagnosed, the more it can respond to real differences across the network."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Real costs: a universal offer is cheaper, simpler, and easier to explain; a diagnosed offer is harder to run, harder to justify quickly, and more exposed to disagreement about the diagnosis itself.  Both ends of the axis carry real cost.\n    Active dynamic: the right position can change over time.  Early in a service cycle, diagnosis may be rough and the case for a common baseline may be stronger.  Later, once evaluation and comparison are in place, the service has more reason to differentiate."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "      - One-offer-for-all: every institution receives the same response regardless of what is actually blocking progress\n      - Variance-as-effort: uneven outcomes are explained as trainer quality or local enthusiasm rather than as differences in constraint structure\n      - Boundary-blindness: the service keeps promising lift where the blocking condition is outside its scope\n      - Scope-denial: a substrate problem is treated as if a training-service adjustment can solve it\n      - Frozen diagnosis: the initial judgement is never revised after new evaluation evidence arrives\n      - Hidden diagnosis: the service acts on a diagnosis internally but never states it to the institutions affected by it"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Operate an explicit move on the named axis; make the diagnosis inspectable; revise it when evidence warrants."} {:name "COMPOSITIONS", :name-key "compositions", :slug "compositions", :text "      (1) Diagnostic-first: use the early wave of delivery and evaluation to establish what is mainly constraining each institution or cluster before differentiating the response.  Gives up speed at the start.\n      (2) Universal baseline plus selective escalation: begin with a common offer, then add differentiated support where evidence shows a stronger delivery or architecture constraint.  Gives up simplicity of message.\n      (3) Mostly universal offer: keep the service largely standardised and accept that some institutions will be served imperfectly.  Gives up fit for reach and ease.\n      These compositions are not ranked.  The point of the pattern is to make the trade visible and to stop the service pretending that one common offer is automatically the right answer everywhere."} {:name "CHECK", :name-key "check", :slug "check", :text "      Engaged when: the service can say, for any institution or cluster, what seems to be binding and what evidence supports that judgement; the response follows from that judgement; later evidence can revise it.\n      Failing when: every institution gets the same answer by default; differences in outcome are explained away as effort or enthusiasm; no one can say what the service thinks is actually in the way."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Central finding of the paper: The paper's headline claim is that the institutional setting matters at least as much as the training content itself.  That is exactly why diagnosis matters.  If the main problem is not the same everywhere, the same response will not work everywhere.\n\n    Evidence from the model and verification: The design and verification work distinguish boundary cases, floor cases, and higher-throughput cases rather than treating the whole network as flat.  That difference is what a diagnosed service is trying to respect.  The point is not to claim certainty; it is to stop making undifferentiated promises where the evidence already shows meaningful differences.\n\n    Relationship to direct, indirect, and out-of-scope levers: Diagnosis also disciplines ambition.  If the problem looks directly serviceable, the service can act.  If the problem can only be shifted indirectly, the service should say so.  If the main blockage is outside scope, the honest response is not to overclaim.\n\n    Governance value: A stated diagnosis can be challenged.  An unstated diagnosis cannot.  By naming what it thinks is binding, the service gives institutions something they can contest, refine, or confirm with better evidence.\n\n    Mechanic, in short:\n    Derived from the paper's distinction between delivery and institutional architecture, the verification work's separation of different institution types, and the later distinction between direct, indirect, and out-of-scope effects.\n    Answers equity/confident-adaptation at service scale: just as trainers need to know what is fixed around them, the service needs to know what is actually blocking progress before prescribing a response.\n    Mapped to the learning loop at the diagnose step: upstream of capability placement, evidence gathering, and operational differentiation.\n    Coordination requirement: Moderate.  The service can make an initial diagnosis centrally, but it needs institutions to contest and refine it."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - State, for each participating institution or cluster, whether the main current constraint appears to be delivery, institutional architecture, both, or something outside scope\n    - Tie service choices back to that diagnosis rather than treating them as free-standing preferences\n    - Let institutions challenge the diagnosis with their own evidence\n    - Re-run the diagnosis after the first evaluation wave and again at the Year-2 commitment point\n    - Use this pattern upstream of capability placement and evidence-prioritisation decisions"}], :conclusion "A training service should diagnose the main constraint before choosing its response.  In the current paper's terms, the important distinction is whether an institution is mainly constrained by delivery, by institutional architecture, by both, or by a substrate problem the service cannot lift within scope.  A single offer for everyone is easy to run and easy to explain, but it misfits a visible part of the network.  A diagnosed offer states what seems to be binding, says what that implies for the service response, and leaves room for revision when later evidence shows the diagnosis was wrong or incomplete.", :directives {:audience "UKRN-S design, programme commissioners, Research England, institutional leads", :keywords ["diagnosis" "binding-constraint" "delivery" "institutional-architecture" "direct-indirect-out-of-scope" "universal-vs-diagnosed" "service-differentiation"], :references ["equity/confident-adaptation" "ukrns/provision-at-network-scale" "ukrns/measure-where-least-sure"], :style "pattern", :title "Diagnose Before Prescribing", :tone "analytic-foundational"}, :id "ukrns/diagnose-before-prescribing", :keywords ["diagnosis" "binding-constraint" "delivery" "institutional-architecture" "direct-indirect-out-of-scope" "universal-vs-diagnosed" "service-differentiation"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["equity/confident-adaptation" "ukrns/provision-at-network-scale" "ukrns/measure-where-least-sure"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/ukrns/diagnose-before-prescribing.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Diagnose Before Prescribing", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Attendance proves reach into rooms; it does not prove changed research practice afterwards. A training system that reports delivery as if it were impact will systematically overstate what it knows."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "The current paper's training track already makes this distinction explicit: 2,816 trainees can establish network-scale delivery, but not whether those researchers subsequently changed how they work."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The pattern operates on the axis: delivery evidence ↔ outcome evidence."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Real costs: delivery is visible sooner and more cheaply than practice change, so programmes are persistently tempted to let the first stand in for the second."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Use delivery counts to claim delivery, and reserve impact claims for downstream evidence that actually tracks changed practice."}], :conclusion "Attendance proves reach into rooms; it does not prove changed research practice afterwards. A training system that reports delivery as if it were impact will systematically overstate what it knows.", :directives {:audience "UKRN Services design, funders, institutional leads, evaluators", :keywords ["sketch" "delivery" "attendance" "impact" "practice-change" "training" "counts" "outcome"], :references ["system-coherence/correct-effect-claims-with-relevant-denominators" "writing-coherence/plain-language-thesis"], :style "sketch", :title "Distinguish Delivery from Practice Change (Sketch)", :tone "analytic-foundational"}, :id "ukrns/distinguish-delivery-from-practice-change", :keywords ["sketch" "delivery" "attendance" "impact" "practice-change" "training" "counts" "outcome"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["system-coherence/correct-effect-claims-with-relevant-denominators" "writing-coherence/plain-language-thesis"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/ukrns/distinguish-delivery-from-practice-change.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Distinguish Delivery from Practice Change (Sketch)", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "The breadth of open-research topics exceeds what any single trainer can confidently cover.  A programme that requires uniform coverage from every trainer produces breadth overload, impostor syndrome, and absorbed-mode delivery.  A programme that treats coverage as a network property — each trainer declaring the modules they will own, each institution committing an explicit coverage profile, the programme maintaining an aggregate view — converts the same breadth from an individual burden into a distributed responsibility.  The service states which composition it is running on the axis between uniform-coverage-per-trainer and distributed-coverage-across-the-network.  Each trainer has a declared module subset.  Each participating institution has a committed coverage profile.  The programme maintains a current network-level coverage map.  Coverage gaps are visible; institutional commitments are contestable."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "This pattern addresses the hole (H5) that the five main design patterns left open: protected-interaction (breadth overload) is only partially answered by network-scale provisioning of coordination support (ukrns/provision-at-network-scale).  Network-scale coordination helps trainers find each other but does not itself distribute the coverage burden.  The equity-domain protected-interaction pattern diagnoses the institutional failure (trainers silently absorb a coordination problem that extends beyond their remit); this design pattern specifies a service-level response that makes coverage distribution explicit.  The paper-v2.3 resolution for protected-interaction — declared delivery profiles, institutional coverage commitments, coverage as network property — is formalised here as its own design pattern."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The pattern operates on the axis: uniform coverage per trainer (every trainer expected to cover all topics) ↔ distributed coverage across the network (each trainer owns a subset, the network guarantees totality).\n    Irreducible: coverage cannot be simultaneously uniform-per-trainer and distributed-across-network.  The same module either is expected of every trainer or is owned by a declared subset."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Real costs: uniform is communicatively simple ('all our trainers deliver the full open-research curriculum') and requires no coordination infrastructure; distributed requires declaration, aggregation, institutional commitment, and active maintenance of the coverage map.  Both carry real costs.\n    Active dynamic: the right position shifts with the territory.  A narrow curriculum favours uniform; the wide, evolving territory of open-research favours distributed.  As the territory grows (AI for research, new replication methods, new data-governance requirements), the case for distributed strengthens."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "      - Breadth overload: trainers silently cover topics outside their primary expertise without team support\n      - Impostor-driven delivery: session held while trainer under-prepared because declaration infrastructure was absent\n      - Un-profiled institution: institution participates without naming what it covers or what it resources\n      - Uniform-communication with distributed-reality: programme is described as if coverage is uniform but actually absorbed by a subset of trainers\n      - Coverage-map drift: declared profiles exist but the aggregate map is stale\n      - Institutional-refusal unacknowledged: some institutions cannot commit but Composition (3)'s honesty is not declared; gaps are hidden\n      - Absorbed-mode as individual failure: breadth overload read as trainer motivation problem rather than architectural absence"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Operate an explicit move on the named axis (see substructure below); steward the pattern against the declared discipline."} {:name "COMPOSITIONS", :name-key "compositions", :slug "compositions", :text "      (1) Declared delivery profiles: each trainer declares the modules they will own; each participating institution commits an explicit coverage profile with resourcing; the programme maintains an aggregate coverage view.  Coverage is a network property; gaps are visible.  Gives up communicative simplicity; demands declaration infrastructure.\n      (2) Uniform expectation: every trainer expected to cover the full curriculum; breadth overload and impostor syndrome become individual problems; absorbed-mode delivery is the predictable outcome.  Gives up the distributed trainer experience; easy to describe to funders.\n      (3) Curriculum triage per institution: each institution picks which subset to deliver based on its profile; coverage gaps are flagged rather than hidden; the programme does not guarantee totality at the network level.  Gives up network-level totality; gains institution-level honesty about what is delivered.\n      These compositions are not ranked; the paper-v2.3 (companion journal article) resolution for protected-interaction favours Composition (1) with institutional coverage commitments.  Composition (3) is a realistic fallback when institutional capacity is uneven."} {:name "CHECK", :name-key "check", :slug "check", :text "      Engaged when: every trainer can point to their declared module subset; each participating institution has a committed coverage profile; the programme maintains a current aggregate coverage map; coverage gaps (modules not owned by anyone at sufficient depth) are visible.\n      Failing when: individual trainers silently absorb breadth overload; no institutional coverage commitment exists; coverage is described uniformly ('our trainers deliver the full curriculum') without distribution evidence; impostor syndrome is widespread and its structural cause is not named."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Breadth-vs-depth tension (equity/protected-interaction link): the equity-domain pattern names the tension at institutional scale: the territory is wider than any single trainer's expertise, so uniform expectation produces either superficial coverage or impostor-mode delivery.  Distributed coverage resolves the tension structurally rather than by asking individuals to work harder.\n\n    Network-property framing: coverage as a network property is the move that reframes the problem.  Under uniform expectation, a gap in a trainer's competence is a trainer problem.  Under distributed coverage, the same gap is a network problem ('this module is owned by trainers X, Y, Z; the network needs to ensure their resourcing') — which locates the problem where the resources exist to resolve it.\n\n    Absorbed-mode signature: absorbed input (companion paper) is the implementation mode where individuals carry what the architecture should distribute.  Breadth overload is a specific absorbed-mode signature; distributed coverage is the architectural response.\n\n    Profile-contingency: institutions differ in what they can resource.  Composition (1) presumes institutional commitment; Composition (3) acknowledges that some institutions cannot commit to full modules.  The choice is itself a profile-contingent decision (linked: ukrns/diagnose-before-prescribing).\n\n    Cross-pattern: the coverage map is a specific instance of ukrns/scale-register — coverage is a capability whose scale is 'distributed across declared trainers/institutions'.  Maintaining the map is an operational act governed by the scale-register mechanics."} {:name "EVIDENCE", :name-key "evidence", :slug "evidence", :text "      Observation (2026-04, UKRN Services recruitment): UKRN Services issued a call for expressions of interest seeking trainers to deliver 'open research and related topics to the wider sector and beyond'.  Despite addressing a population of recently-trained, motivated open-research trainers (the ORP Training Community of Practice, ~200-strong), the response rate was disappointingly low.  UKRN-S itself has acknowledged the finding and wants to understand why the transition from 'trainer on open research within your institution' to 'providing training to others via UKRN Services' has not happened at the rate they hoped.\n\n      Reading through the pattern: the call asks candidates to volunteer for delivery of an unspecified subset of a wide territory, without a scaffold for what the service will deliver (confident-adaptation, at UKRN-S scale) and without a mechanism for declaring which modules a trainer will own (distributed-coverage, at trainer scale).  The pattern predicts that breadth-overload and impostor effects — already documented at ORP delivery stage — will recur at the recruitment stage.  Observed low response is consistent with this prediction.\n\n      Implication for operational use: adoption of the pattern's discipline — declared delivery profiles, institutional (or trainer-level) coverage commitments, a network-level coverage map, and an explicit expectation scaffold for UKRN-S delivery — would be expected to reduce this transition friction.  The argument generalises the paper's ORP-era findings into UKRN-S's own recruitment experience; the same architectural gap produces the same absorbed-mode signature at a different point in the pipeline.\n\n    Mechanic, in short:\n    Derived from paper-v2.3 protected-interaction resolution (declared delivery profiles, institutional coverage as network property) and from the hole (H5) in the original five-pattern set where ukrns/provision-at-network-scale answered equity/protected-interaction only partially.\n    Answers equity/protected-interaction fully at service scale: network provisioning of coordination (ukrns/provision-at-network-scale) + distributed coverage (this pattern) + declared delivery profiles (trainer-level declaration).\n    Mapped to the learning loop: this pattern operates alongside provision-at-network-scale during the 'provision' step.  Once diagnosis (ukrns/diagnose-before-prescribing) has named a profile, both scale choice (ukrns/provision-at-network-scale) and coverage distribution (this pattern) are chosen.\n    Axis framing follows the paper-draft-16-april Tension / Composition / Check structure.\n    Coordination requirement: Medium (trainer + institution + programme) to High (network-scale coverage map maintenance).\n    Sixth main pattern: the original five covered the openness, diagnostic, scale, measurement, and update disciplines; this sixth pattern covers the coverage-distribution discipline that completes the response to equity/protected-interaction."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Ask each participating trainer to declare the modules they will own\n    - Ask each participating institution to commit an explicit coverage profile with resourcing\n    - Maintain an aggregate network-level coverage map (linked: ukrns/scale-register)\n    - Audit the map for gaps (modules without owners or with only under-resourced owners)\n    - Where Composition (3) applies: flag coverage gaps as institutional honesty rather than hiding them\n    - Use this pattern alongside ukrns/diagnose-before-prescribing (which profile can commit to which composition?) and ukrns/provision-at-network-scale (what coordination does distributed coverage require?)"}], :conclusion "The breadth of open-research topics exceeds what any single trainer can confidently cover.  A programme that requires uniform coverage from every trainer produces breadth overload, impostor syndrome, and absorbed-mode delivery.  A programme that treats coverage as a network property — each trainer declaring the modules they will own, each institution committing an explicit coverage profile, the programme maintaining an aggregate view — converts the same breadth from an individual burden into a distributed responsibility.  The service states which composition it is running on the axis between uniform-coverage-per-trainer and distributed-coverage-across-the-network.  Each trainer has a declared module subset.  Each participating institution has a committed coverage profile.  The programme maintains a current network-level coverage map.  Coverage gaps are visible; institutional commitments are contestable.", :directives {:audience "UKRN-S design, Training Project, Institutional Leads, individual trainers, Research England", :keywords ["main-pattern" "distributed-coverage" "delivery-profile" "breadth-overload" "impostor-syndrome" "network-property" "curriculum-triage" "coverage-register"], :references ["equity/protected-interaction" "ukrns/provision-at-network-scale" "ukrns/scale-register" "ukrns/diagnose-before-prescribing"], :style "pattern", :title "Distributed Coverage — Coverage as a Network Property, Not an Individual Requirement (Sixth Main Pattern)", :tone "analytic-foundational"}, :id "ukrns/distributed-coverage", :keywords ["main-pattern" "distributed-coverage" "delivery-profile" "breadth-overload" "impostor-syndrome" "network-property" "curriculum-triage" "coverage-register"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["equity/protected-interaction" "ukrns/provision-at-network-scale" "ukrns/scale-register" "ukrns/diagnose-before-prescribing"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/ukrns/distributed-coverage.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Distributed Coverage — Coverage as a Network Property, Not an Individual Requirement (Sixth Main Pattern)", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "A paper that invites readers to pick positions on axes has a choice about what to do when they do.  The service states which composition it is running on the axis between passive exercises (reader picks privately, no capture) and captured responses (positions logged, with consent, and aggregated).  Capture without consent is a privacy failure; capture followed by silence is a trust failure.  The current composition on the capture axis is declared.  If responses are captured, consent is explicit and feedback is provided.  Reader privacy is preserved under the declared composition."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "This implementation pattern specifies the response mechanic behind ukrns/reader-as-participant.  The meta-pattern invites readers into the diagnosis; this pattern addresses the practical question of what happens after they accept the invitation.  Without a declared response-capture policy, exercises are either purely rhetorical (reader picks privately) or covertly data-producing (captured without visible consent)."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The pattern operates on the axis: passive exercises (reader picks privately; no capture mechanism) ↔ captured responses (positions logged with consent; distribution aggregated and fed back).\n    Irreducible: an exercise cannot be both maximally frictionless and maximally data-producing."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Real costs: passive costs the aggregate signal readers' responses could produce — the service never learns what distribution of positions its readers hold; captured costs reader friction, privacy risk, and the overhead of maintaining the capture infrastructure.\n    Active dynamic: early in a paper's life, passive suffices; as the exercise set matures, captured responses become informative (and as operational UKRN-S moves from paper to service, capture becomes a natural fit)."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "      - Covert capture: responses captured without visible consent\n      - Capture without feedback: responses logged but never surfaced; readers contribute to a black box\n      - Passive exercises framed as captured: the paper implies responses matter operationally but has no channel for them to land\n      - Over-engineered capture: interactive infrastructure built but exercises do not produce responses worth capturing\n      - Consent-creep: initial consent is narrow but response use expands silently"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Operate an explicit move on the named axis (see substructure below); steward the pattern against the declared discipline."} {:name "COMPOSITIONS", :name-key "compositions", :slug "compositions", :text "      (1) Passive-only: exercises invite but no response mechanism exists; reader picks privately.  Paper's current state.  Gives up aggregate signal.\n      (2) Opt-in capture: invitation to submit responses via a declared channel (form, email, repo issue); responses aggregated and distribution published; consent explicit at point of response.  Gives up universal participation.\n      (3) Embedded capture: interactive notebook (or live HTML with JavaScript) where positions are captured by default; consent must be architected into the interface, not assumed.  Gives up low-tech accessibility; requires privacy infrastructure.\n      The paper currently operates at (1).  Composition (3) is a future state requiring specific infrastructure."} {:name "CHECK", :name-key "check", :slug "check", :text "      Engaged when: the composition is declared; if responses are captured, consent is explicit and responses are fed back in some form; if passive, the exercises make clear that no capture is occurring.\n      Failing when: responses are captured without visible consent; captured responses are never surfaced back; the paper invites participation but there is no channel for responses to land anywhere."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Consent-first discipline: capture without visible consent is coercive — readers who thought their responses were private discover they have been counted.  Any composition above (1) requires explicit consent architecture, and the consent must be present at the point the response is given.\n\n    Feedback-or-don't: capturing responses and never surfacing them back to readers is a trust failure.  If the service asks readers to respond, the minimal contract is that the service reports what the responses were.  Composition (2) requires aggregated feedback; composition (3) benefits from live visibility.\n\n    Pedagogical vs diagnostic purpose: the exercises serve two purposes in ukrns/reader-as-participant: they teach pattern-reading (pedagogical) and they invite the reader's position (diagnostic).  The pedagogical purpose is served by passive exercises; the diagnostic purpose requires some form of capture.  The composition choice names which purpose is being served.\n\n    Commons-adjacent: if responses are captured, the aggregate distribution is itself a commons artefact.  Governance of that data (who can see it, how it is curated, how long it is retained) mirrors ukrns/assumptions-commons' concerns.\n\n    Mechanic, in short:\n    Mechanic: what happens to reader positions on exercise axes.\n    Serves: reader-as-participant's invitation claim.\n    Axis: passive ↔ captured responses.\n    Without this pattern: invitations are rhetorical, and any capture risks coercion."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Declare the current composition in the paper or project documentation\n    - If (2) or (3): write a consent statement; declare retention policy; set up a feedback mechanism for aggregates\n    - If (1): make it explicit that exercises are for the reader alone\n    - Cross-reference with ukrns/assumptions-commons if responses feed into evidence"}], :conclusion "A paper that invites readers to pick positions on axes has a choice about what to do when they do.  The service states which composition it is running on the axis between passive exercises (reader picks privately, no capture) and captured responses (positions logged, with consent, and aggregated).  Capture without consent is a privacy failure; capture followed by silence is a trust failure.  The current composition on the capture axis is declared.  If responses are captured, consent is explicit and feedback is provided.  Reader privacy is preserved under the declared composition.", :directives {:audience "UKRN-S design, working-paper maintainers, readers who respond to exercises, evaluators", :keywords ["implementation-pattern" "reader-exercises" "capture" "consent" "aggregation" "feedback" "participation" "reader-response"], :references ["ukrns/reader-as-participant" "ukrns/assumptions-commons"], :style "implementation-pattern", :title "Exercise Response Capture — What Happens When Readers Pick Positions (Implementation Pattern)", :tone "operational"}, :id "ukrns/exercise-response-capture", :keywords ["implementation-pattern" "reader-exercises" "capture" "consent" "aggregation" "feedback" "participation" "reader-response"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["ukrns/reader-as-participant" "ukrns/assumptions-commons"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/ukrns/exercise-response-capture.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Exercise Response Capture — What Happens When Readers Pick Positions (Implementation Pattern)", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Training without indicators cannot show what changed. Indicators without sector positioning do not reach the bodies that could act on them. Sector positioning without a live training offer is positioning without product. The three lines only pay fully when they are joined up."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "The current paper's background section identifies these three lines of evidence separately, then concludes that their parallel treatment is one of the main current failure modes."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The pattern operates on the axis: parallel evidence lines ↔ operationally joined evidence lines."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Real costs: joining the lines up requires more coordination and more role clarity than running each stream on its own."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Design the exchanges so training generates indicators-relevant evidence, indicators feed sector translation, and sector signals feed the next training and evaluation wave."}], :conclusion "Training without indicators cannot show what changed. Indicators without sector positioning do not reach the bodies that could act on them. Sector positioning without a live training offer is positioning without product. The three lines only pay fully when they are joined up.", :directives {:audience "UKRN Services design, funders, institutional leads, sector partners", :keywords ["sketch" "joined-up" "training" "indicators" "sector-positioning" "coupling" "policy-traction" "design"], :references ["system-coherence/turn-design-into-checks" "ukrns/provision-at-network-scale"], :style "sketch", :title "Join Up Training, Indicators, and Sector Positioning (Sketch)", :tone "analytic-foundational"}, :id "ukrns/join-up-training-indicators-and-sector-positioning", :keywords ["sketch" "joined-up" "training" "indicators" "sector-positioning" "coupling" "policy-traction" "design"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["system-coherence/turn-design-into-checks" "ukrns/provision-at-network-scale"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/ukrns/join-up-training-indicators-and-sector-positioning.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Join Up Training, Indicators, and Sector Positioning (Sketch)", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "A service with limited evaluation capacity should measure where the next observation is most likely to matter.  Sometimes that means measuring where the evidence gap is biggest.  Sometimes it means measuring where board attention, funder attention, or sector attention is already concentrated.  The key discipline is to choose deliberately and to say which principle is selecting the next wave of work.  Measurement should not drift toward whatever is easiest, most flattering, or most familiar."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "The current paper makes two things clear.  First, some open-research indicators can already be measured reasonably well, while others still cannot.  Second, the wider sector conversation and funder cycle do not attend evenly to every topic, institution type, or signal.  Because evaluation capacity is bounded, UKRN Services cannot treat every possible question as equally urgent.  It needs a rule for deciding where the next piece of measurement effort goes."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The pattern operates on the axis: uncertainty reduction ↔ stakeholder attention.\n    Irreducible: the place where the service is least sure is not always the place where outside attention is already waiting.  A single wave of evaluation often has to choose which question it is trying to answer first."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Real costs: measuring where uncertainty is highest sharpens the design fastest, but it may miss the places where visible accountability is most needed.  Measuring where attention already sits may help with governance, legitimacy, and partnership, but it may tell the service little that it does not broadly know already.\n    Active dynamic: both maps move.  Uncertainty should shrink if the service is learning.  Attention should shift as sector priorities, funder cycles, and public pressures change.  So the selection rule cannot be fixed once and forgotten."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "      - Convenience drift: the same easy-to-reach settings are measured repeatedly because they are easy\n      - Reassurance drift: measurement is aimed mainly at places most likely to produce good news\n      - Uncertainty neglect: the service keeps talking confidently about areas it still has weak evidence on\n      - Attention neglect: the service learns things internally but cannot answer the questions external stakeholders actually ask\n      - Hidden mix: a wave is really selected on mixed grounds, but only one reason is published\n      - Measure-everything fiction: a bounded service pretends it can observe everything at once and ends up observing none of it well"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Choose the selection principle for each wave before the work starts, and publish it."} {:name "COMPOSITIONS", :name-key "compositions", :slug "compositions", :text "      (1) Uncertainty-first: use the wave mainly to tighten the weakest parts of the evidence base.  Gives up some immediate external legibility.\n      (2) Attention-first: use the wave mainly to answer the question that funders, partners, or member institutions are already pressing on.  Gives up some learning efficiency.\n      (3) Split wave: reserve part of the budget for the biggest evidence gaps and part for the most watched questions.  Gives up some reach in order to do both honestly.\n      These compositions are not ranked in the abstract.  The current paper leans toward uncertainty-first when the aim is to improve the design, but it also makes clear that attention cannot simply be ignored."} {:name "CHECK", :name-key "check", :slug "check", :text "      Engaged when: each evaluation wave has a stated reason for selection; the service can say which open question or implementation decision the wave is meant to inform; the next wave reflects what the last one learned.\n      Failing when: measurement choices are made post hoc; the same terrain is measured repeatedly for convenience; or no one can explain why this wave happened here rather than somewhere else."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Indicator capacity is uneven: The paper is explicit that some indicators are already tractable and some are not.  That means measurement discipline is not a luxury.  It is how the service avoids promising to demonstrate things it still cannot actually demonstrate.\n\n    Learning depends on selective observation: If evidence is supposed to shape design, then the service has to decide where an additional observation will change its judgement most.  Otherwise evaluation becomes diffuse activity rather than decision support.\n\n    External attention is real: Member institutions, funders, and partners do not care about all questions equally.  A service that never measures where scrutiny already sits may become internally coherent but externally unpersuasive.\n\n    Governance benefit: Stating the selection principle before fieldwork makes the service's reasoning inspectable.  It allows institutions and partners to challenge not only the findings, but the choice of what was measured in the first place.\n\n    Mechanic, in short:\n    Derived from the paper's distinction between what can currently be measured, where uncertainty still lives, and where sector attention already sits.\n    Answers the service-level version of the visible-impact problem: not just whether evidence exists, but where the service chooses to look next.\n    Mapped to the learning loop at the measure step: after diagnosis and provisioning, bounded evaluation effort is placed where it will matter most.\n    Coordination requirement: Medium, rising when a wave depends on distributed data collection across institutions."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Publish, for each evaluation wave, whether it is mainly uncertainty-reducing, attention-facing, or a stated mix\n    - Tie each wave explicitly to one of the paper's open questions or implementation decisions\n    - Reassess the selection rule after each wave rather than repeating the same pattern by habit\n    - Keep a visible list of questions the service still cannot yet measure well, so silence is not mistaken for closure\n    - Use this pattern upstream of design revision, where evidence is meant to produce operational consequence"}], :conclusion "A service with limited evaluation capacity should measure where the next observation is most likely to matter.  Sometimes that means measuring where the evidence gap is biggest.  Sometimes it means measuring where board attention, funder attention, or sector attention is already concentrated.  The key discipline is to choose deliberately and to say which principle is selecting the next wave of work.  Measurement should not drift toward whatever is easiest, most flattering, or most familiar.", :directives {:audience "UKRN-S design, evaluators, Research England, trainer community", :keywords ["uncertainty-reduction" "stakeholder-attention" "indicator-capacity" "evaluation-wave" "measurement-selection" "open-questions" "hand-off-decisions"], :references ["equity/visible-impact" "ukrns/open-the-triangle" "ukrns/design-as-function-of-evidence"], :style "pattern", :title "Measure Where Uncertainty Is Highest", :tone "analytic-foundational"}, :id "ukrns/measure-where-least-sure", :keywords ["uncertainty-reduction" "stakeholder-attention" "indicator-capacity" "evaluation-wave" "measurement-selection" "open-questions" "hand-off-decisions"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["equity/visible-impact" "ukrns/open-the-triangle" "ukrns/design-as-function-of-evidence"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/ukrns/measure-where-least-sure.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Measure Where Uncertainty Is Highest", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "A notebook that loads evidence and recomputes the model is inert until someone triggers a rerun.  The service states when and why the model recomputes — on evidence arrival (event-driven), on a calendar (cadence-driven), or in a declared hybrid — and which recomputes produce published design-state changes.  Every rerun output carries a timestamp and an evidence-revision identifier; no output circulates without those.  The current recompute composition is declared.  Every output carries a timestamp and evidence-revision identifier.  A recompute log is maintained."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "This implementation pattern specifies the operational mechanics behind ukrns/self-application (the paper is a re-runnable notebook) and ukrns/design-as-function-of-evidence (updates trace to evidence).  Without a declared recompute schedule, self-application is a property of the artefact that nobody exercises, and design-state updates happen silently."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The pattern operates on the axis: event-driven recompute (triggered by evidence arrival or assumptions change) ↔ cadence-driven recompute (triggered on a published calendar).\n    Irreducible: a single policy cannot be both maximally event-responsive and maximally predictable-by-calendar."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Real costs: event-driven costs predictability — readers do not know when a change will happen; cadence-driven costs latency — evidence sits unprocessed until the next scheduled rerun.\n    Active dynamic: early in a programme, evidence arrives slowly and cadence suffices; as measurement waves multiply, event-driven becomes more defensible; as the posterior tightens, cadence may again be appropriate."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "      - Silent recompute: outputs change without readers being notified\n      - Schedule-only, no events: major evidence waits for cadence; urgency of significant observations is lost\n      - Events-only, no cadence: recomputes only when the author remembers; baseline continuity suffers\n      - Timestamp-missing: outputs circulate without the information that would let readers correlate them with evidence\n      - Log-as-theatre: recompute log exists but is uninformative (\"rerun on 2026-05-01\")"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Operate an explicit move on the named axis (see substructure below); steward the pattern against the declared discipline."} {:name "COMPOSITIONS", :name-key "compositions", :slug "compositions", :text "      (1) Scheduled-only: weekly or monthly recompute on a declared cadence; all evidence arriving between recomputes waits.  Gives up latency.\n      (2) Event-driven: each T1 wave completion and each assumptions-v2.edn change triggers a recompute; no fixed calendar.  Gives up predictability.\n      (3) Hybrid: cadence for routine (monthly baseline recompute); event-driven for major waves (cross-institutional evaluation completion, significant assumption revisions).  Gives up simplicity.\n      The paper currently operates informally — recomputes happen when the author reruns.  Operational use at UKRN-S requires a declared composition."} {:name "CHECK", :name-key "check", :slug "check", :text "      Engaged when: every published recompute output carries a timestamp and evidence-revision identifier; the current schedule (or declared mix) is published; readers can see which evidence triggered the most recent recompute.\n      Failing when: recomputes happen silently; readers cannot tell which version they are looking at; design-state changes propagate without recompute traceability."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Self-application requires rerun: ukrns/self-application's claim is that the artefact instantiates its argument.  Every rerun is a micro-instance of the cycle; a rerun that never happens leaves the claim aspirational.\n\n    Measurement-wave pairing: ukrns/measure-where-least-sure commits to observations whose results will move the model.  If those observations complete but the model does not recompute, the observational commitment is broken.  The recompute schedule is how observations become model updates.\n\n    Design-state versioning: ukrns/design-as-function-of-evidence requires that service changes trace to evidence updates.  Every recompute is a candidate version-event; the schedule determines how the version lineage accrues.\n\n    Predictability as commons good: institutions plan around the design state.  Event-driven changes produce planning friction; cadence-driven changes produce planning predictability.  The schedule is a service commitment to the network.\n\n    Mechanic, in short:\n    Mechanic: when and why the model reruns.\n    Serves: self-application, computation-as-exploration, design-as-function-of-evidence, measure-where-least-sure.\n    Axis: event-driven ↔ cadence-driven.\n    Without this pattern: the notebook form is packaging, not method."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Declare the current composition in project documentation\n    - Timestamp every notebook output with assumptions-v2.edn revision at point of compute\n    - Publish a recompute log: when the model was rerun, what triggered it, what changed\n    - Cross-reference with ukrns/design-state-lineage (the versioning mechanic downstream)\n    - For paper time: even if informal, record the compute-date and evidence-revision in each published version"}], :conclusion "A notebook that loads evidence and recomputes the model is inert until someone triggers a rerun.  The service states when and why the model recomputes — on evidence arrival (event-driven), on a calendar (cadence-driven), or in a declared hybrid — and which recomputes produce published design-state changes.  Every rerun output carries a timestamp and an evidence-revision identifier; no output circulates without those.  The current recompute composition is declared.  Every output carries a timestamp and evidence-revision identifier.  A recompute log is maintained.", :directives {:audience "UKRN-S design, working-paper maintainers, evaluators, trainers receiving design-state updates", :keywords ["implementation-pattern" "recompute" "event-driven" "cadence-driven" "hybrid" "trigger" "self-application" "model-operations"], :references ["ukrns/self-application" "ukrns/computation-as-exploration" "ukrns/design-as-function-of-evidence" "ukrns/measure-where-least-sure"], :style "implementation-pattern", :title "Model Recompute Schedule — When Computation Runs Operationally (Implementation Pattern)", :tone "operational"}, :id "ukrns/model-recompute-schedule", :keywords ["implementation-pattern" "recompute" "event-driven" "cadence-driven" "hybrid" "trigger" "self-application" "model-operations"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["ukrns/self-application" "ukrns/computation-as-exploration" "ukrns/design-as-function-of-evidence" "ukrns/measure-where-least-sure"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/ukrns/model-recompute-schedule.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Model Recompute Schedule — When Computation Runs Operationally (Implementation Pattern)", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "A distributed training service does not become a learning system just because good work happens locally.  Local success has to become shareable evidence: something that other institutions, partners, and funders can inspect, reuse, or act on.  Every service event therefore sits on an axis between shareability gain and governance cost.  The service should be explicit about what is worth sharing, what it costs to share it responsibly, and when the extra governance burden is justified."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "The current paper's background and synthesis point to the same problem from two directions.  First, the three lines of evidence — training delivery, what can currently be measured, and sector priorities — are often not joined up.  Second, funder and policy cycles matter to whether local work becomes visible beyond the institution that did it.  A service that leaves training outcomes, evaluation findings, and operational learning trapped locally will keep recreating the same work in parallel and will struggle to build wider recognition for what it does."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The pattern operates on the axis: shareability gain ↔ governance cost.\n    Irreducible: making evidence portable, reusable, and externally legible usually requires extra governance work.  Avoiding that work keeps things easy locally, but it also keeps the learning trapped."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Real costs: some things are cheap to share, such as a training plan, a scaffold, or a summary of what was tried.  Other things, such as downstream evaluation, cross-institution comparison, or evidence that could affect institutional reputation, require much more consent, handling, and trust.\n    Active dynamic: the governance cost of sharing should fall as the network builds routine practice, trusted formats, and repeatable agreements.  But it never falls to zero, and the service still has to choose where the higher-cost sharing really pays."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "      - Local-success trap: good work happens, but no one outside the host institution can learn from it\n      - Governance-free sharing: evidence is pushed outward without the consent, attribution, or handling needed to do it responsibly\n      - Governance-without-reuse: governance effort is paid, but the resulting evidence is too thin or too obscure to matter\n      - Post-dependent memory: the shared layer depends on a specific funded role and disappears when that role ends\n      - Sector disconnect: the service shares internally but never in forms that connect to funder or partner conversations\n      - Archive-without-consequence: evidence is technically deposited, but nothing downstream uses it"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Choose what to make shareable, and cost the governance honestly."} {:name "COMPOSITIONS", :name-key "compositions", :slug "compositions", :text "      (1) Low-governance shared layer: make planning materials, role definitions, routine findings, and annual scenario outputs shareable first, while holding back heavier cross-institution evidence until the governance surface is ready.  Gives up some comparative power.\n      (2) Governance-first build-out: invest early in the agreements and processes needed for higher-value cross-institution evidence sharing.  Gives up speed.\n      (3) Subset sharing: build the fuller shared layer with a smaller group of institutions willing to carry the burden first, and let the rest of the network inherit what is learned from that arrangement.  Gives up uniformity.\n      These compositions are not ranked in the abstract.  The right one depends on what evidence the service most needs to move from local usefulness to network usefulness."} {:name "CHECK", :name-key "check", :slug "check", :text "      Engaged when: the service can say what each event leaves behind for the network; the governance burden of sharing is named rather than assumed away; and the shared layer persists beyond the immediate funding cycle or project team.\n      Failing when: every institution has to rediscover the same things for itself; governance work is performed ceremonially; or the shared artefacts disappear with the post or grant that created them."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Network learning requires a shared layer: The paper's central move is from isolated training activity toward a coordinated learning system.  That move fails if evidence never leaves the place where it was produced.\n\n    Wider recognition depends on shareable evidence: Funders and policy actors cannot act on local success they cannot see.  A service that wants sector-level standing needs forms of evidence that travel beyond the originating institution.\n\n    Persistence matters as much as publication: A one-off shared output is not yet infrastructure.  The pattern is about creating deposits that later cycles can inherit, revise, and build on.\n\n    Governance is part of the work, not an afterthought: The question is not whether evidence should be shared in the abstract, but which evidence is worth sharing at which governance cost.  Making that trade explicit is what keeps the pattern from becoming either secrecy by default or openness as a slogan.\n\n    Mechanic, in short:\n    Derived from the paper's need to connect local delivery, usable evidence, and sector recognition into one operating story.\n    Answers the service-level version of the visible-impact and shared-maintenance problems: what one institution learns should become part of the network's usable memory.\n    Mapped to the learning loop at the deposit step: evidence produced locally enters a shared layer so later actors can build on it.\n    Coordination requirement: Low for routine artefacts, high for cross-institution evaluation and anything carrying reputational consequence."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Decide, for each class of event, what the network-level deposit is meant to be\n    - State the governance cost of that deposit before treating it as routine\n    - Prefer deposits that can survive role turnover and funding-cycle turnover\n    - Make at least some outputs legible not only internally but to funders, partners, and incoming member institutions\n    - Use this pattern alongside measurement selection and design revision, so shared evidence is both chosen deliberately and acted on"}], :conclusion "A distributed training service does not become a learning system just because good work happens locally.  Local success has to become shareable evidence: something that other institutions, partners, and funders can inspect, reuse, or act on.  Every service event therefore sits on an axis between shareability gain and governance cost.  The service should be explicit about what is worth sharing, what it costs to share it responsibly, and when the extra governance burden is justified.", :directives {:audience "UKRN-S design, programme coordinators, network-services leads", :keywords ["shareable-evidence" "governance-cost" "persistence" "local-success" "sector-conversation" "funder-recognition" "evidence-deposit"], :references ["equity/visible-impact" "equity/shared-maintenance" "ukrns/measure-where-least-sure" "ukrns/design-as-function-of-evidence"], :style "pattern", :title "Make Local Success Shareable", :tone "analytic-foundational"}, :id "ukrns/open-the-triangle", :keywords ["shareable-evidence" "governance-cost" "persistence" "local-success" "sector-conversation" "funder-recognition" "evidence-deposit"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["equity/visible-impact" "equity/shared-maintenance" "ukrns/measure-where-least-sure" "ukrns/design-as-function-of-evidence"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/ukrns/open-the-triangle.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Make Local Success Shareable", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Put a capability at network scale only when it genuinely pays at network scale.  The current paper's five-role design makes this concrete: evaluator, convener, researcher-advisor, and parts of training management create cross-institution value that a single institution cannot easily recreate for itself, while institutional leads remain local because local uptake, local relationships, and local priorities cannot be centralised without losing the point.  The discipline is not to centralise everything, and not to leave everything local, but to place each capability where its gains are real."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "The paper's design argument depends on cross-institution learning.  That means some capabilities need a home above the level of any one institution: comparing evidence across sites, maintaining a common operational picture, carrying sector relationships, and stewarding the parts of the analytical toolkit that should not be reinvented locally.  At the same time, institutions still need their own people to translate, adapt, and carry the work in context.  The question is therefore not whether UKRN Services should act centrally or locally in general.  It is which roles and functions only pay once they are placed at network scale."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The pattern operates on the axis: local fit ↔ network leverage.\n    Irreducible: the more a role is embedded in local context, the less it can serve many institutions at once.  The more a role is built for comparison, shared maintenance, or sector coordination, the more it benefits from being held once above the institutional level."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Real costs: network-scale provision gains comparability, continuity, and one-to-many efficiency, but it can lose local nuance and can become a single point of capacity risk.  Local provision gains trust, context, and institutional fit, but it can duplicate work, compress too many functions into one person, and leave cross-institution learning under-provisioned.\n    Active dynamic: the right placement can change as the service matures.  A function that needs to be central in Year 1 may later support stronger local uptake.  A function that is safely local in a small network may need more central support once the network grows."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "      - Centralise-everything: UKRN Services absorbs roles whose value depends on local ownership\n      - Leave-everything-local: cross-institution comparison, evaluation, and shared maintenance remain too thin to function\n      - Role-compression return: local actors are expected to carry too many distinct functions at once\n      - Hidden scale choice: a role is placed centrally or locally by habit, not by published reasoning\n      - Context-blind centre: a network role operates without a route for local realities to reshape its work\n      - Post-dependent centre: a central capability disappears with the grant or post that created it"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Place each role or capability where its main gain is real, and say why."} {:name "COMPOSITIONS", :name-key "compositions", :slug "compositions", :text "      (1) Capability-by-capability placement: keep institutional leads local, hold the evaluator and convener at network scale, keep methodological support above the day-to-day rhythm, and place training management where common coordination genuinely helps.  This is closest to the current paper.  It gives up the simplicity of one blanket rule.\n      (2) Strong centre: place most capabilities in UKRN Services and use local actors mainly as contact points.  Gives up local ownership and some contextual responsiveness.\n      (3) Light centre: use UKRN Services mainly for convening and leave most capabilities local.  Gives up much of the cross-institution learning that justifies the service in the first place.\n      These compositions are not ranked in the abstract.  The pattern exists to force an explicit argument role by role."} {:name "CHECK", :name-key "check", :slug "check", :text "      Engaged when: the service can say for each role or capability what is gained by its current placement, what is lost, and why that loss is acceptable; the placement is revisited as the network grows.\n      Failing when: centralisation or localisation is treated as a default ideology; institutions cannot see why a role sits where it does; or the same person is quietly carrying several roles because the scale choice was never really made."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Cross-institution roles only pay at network scale: Evaluation comparison, convening across institutions, and stewardship of shared analytical or operational infrastructure all depend on seeing more than one institution at once.  Those functions lose much of their value if they are fragmented into local copies.\n\n    Context-bearing roles still need local ownership: Institutional uptake does not happen abstractly.  Someone in or near the institution still has to carry the local relationships, timing, and translation work that the centre cannot honestly substitute for.\n\n    The paper's evidence already points to under-provisioned shared capability: One of the design pressures the paper is responding to is that some forms of evaluation and cross-institution learning were simply not happening reliably at local level.  Network scale is justified where the alternative is not a rich local version, but absence.\n\n    Persistence matters: A network-scale capability is only worth building if it can survive the initial bootstrap and the funding cycle that enabled it.  Otherwise the centre briefly carries the role and then drops it, leaving no durable gain behind.\n\n    Mechanic, in short:\n    Derived from the paper's five-role system and its claim that some cross-institution capabilities only pay once they are held above the single-institution level.\n    Answers the service-level question of where shared capability should sit if the aim is real cross-institution learning rather than symbolic central coordination.\n    Mapped to the learning loop at the provision step: once the service knows what needs to happen, it has to decide where that capability lives.\n    Coordination requirement: Medium to high, depending on how many institutions depend on the capability and how much local variation it must absorb."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Maintain an explicit role-and-capability register stating what sits centrally, what sits locally, and why\n    - Test each central role against a persistence question: would this capability survive beyond the bootstrap year and the post currently carrying it?\n    - Keep a visible route by which institutional leads can reshape the work of central roles\n    - Revisit placement at the Year-2 decision rather than assuming the bootstrap arrangement is permanent\n    - Use this pattern downstream of diagnosis and alongside the evidence-selection pattern, since capability placement determines what the service can actually observe and compare"}], :conclusion "Put a capability at network scale only when it genuinely pays at network scale.  The current paper's five-role design makes this concrete: evaluator, convener, researcher-advisor, and parts of training management create cross-institution value that a single institution cannot easily recreate for itself, while institutional leads remain local because local uptake, local relationships, and local priorities cannot be centralised without losing the point.  The discipline is not to centralise everything, and not to leave everything local, but to place each capability where its gains are real.", :directives {:audience "UKRN-S design, UKRN leadership, Research England, participating institutions", :keywords ["scale-choice" "network-scale" "local-context" "five-roles" "cross-institution-learning" "evaluator" "convener" "institutional-leads" "selective-provisioning"], :references ["equity/protected-interaction" "equity/contribution-pathways" "equity/visible-impact" "ukrns/diagnose-before-prescribing"], :style "pattern", :title "Provision at Network Scale, Selectively", :tone "analytic-foundational"}, :id "ukrns/provision-at-network-scale", :keywords ["scale-choice" "network-scale" "local-context" "five-roles" "cross-institution-learning" "evaluator" "convener" "institutional-leads" "selective-provisioning"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["equity/protected-interaction" "equity/contribution-pathways" "equity/visible-impact" "ukrns/diagnose-before-prescribing"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/ukrns/provision-at-network-scale.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Provision at Network Scale, Selectively", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "A paper that is re-computable from evidence can be published in two forms: the living document (continuously updated, reflecting the current evidence state) and the snapshot (frozen at a moment, citable, DOI-bearing).  The service states which composition it is running on the axis between living-only, snapshot-only, and both, and resolves the citation ambiguity that arises when a paper has multiple states.  The publication composition is declared.  Readers can find both the current state and a citable snapshot.  Each snapshot declares its evidence-revision and compute-date.  Citation paths are explicit."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "This implementation pattern specifies the artefact-publication mechanic behind ukrns/self-application.  The meta-pattern claims the artefact instantiates its argument; this pattern addresses the practical question of how that artefact is published — as a moving target, a series of frozen snapshots, or both.  Without a declared cadence, \"which version am I reading\" becomes unanswerable."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The pattern operates on the axis: living document (continuously-updated canonical version) ↔ frozen snapshots (versioned fixed publications with citable identifiers).\n    Irreducible: a single publication cannot be simultaneously maximally current AND maximally citable as a fixed object."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Real costs: living costs archival durability — a snapshot citable today may differ from the same URL tomorrow; frozen costs currency — citable versions drift further from the current evidence state over time.\n    Active dynamic: early drafts favour living (claims are forming); as claims stabilise, snapshots become appropriate; archival norms for academic work favour snapshots."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "      - Ambiguous citation: \"the paper\" cited by URL changes silently; citing reader sees different content than intended\n      - Living-without-history: current version has no revision history; prior states irrecoverable\n      - Snapshots-without-revisions: snapshots exist but do not declare their evidence-revision; cannot be reproduced\n      - Stale-snapshot-only: the most recent snapshot is years old; the self-application claim is unverifiable\n      - Citation-ambiguity-unowned: the project has not decided what readers should cite; different citations proliferate"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Operate an explicit move on the named axis (see substructure below); steward the pattern against the declared discipline."} {:name "COMPOSITIONS", :name-key "compositions", :slug "compositions", :text "      (1) Living-only: current HTML (or notebook) with visible revision history; no frozen snapshots.  Gives up archival citability.\n      (2) Snapshot-only: versioned PDFs or archived HTML with DOIs at each publication event; intermediate states not published.  Gives up currency.\n      (3) Both: living current version + periodic frozen snapshots, each snapshot declaring its evidence-file revision and compute-date.  Gives up simplicity; serves both readerships.\n      Working papers typically need (3); the paper-draft-16-april is an instance of an intermediate snapshot (marked by date in filename)."} {:name "CHECK", :name-key "check", :slug "check", :text "      Engaged when: readers can find both the current state and the most recent citable snapshot; each snapshot declares its evidence-file revision and compute-date; the composition is declared.\n      Failing when: \"the paper\" is ambiguous — readers citing different URLs think they are citing the same document; snapshots exist but do not declare their evidence-revision; the living document drifts without visible history."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Citation integrity: an academic artefact that readers cite must be citable as a specific object.  Snapshots give citation integrity; living documents do not.  A paper that is both a living document AND cited in grant applications requires snapshots for the citation paths.\n\n    Currency integrity: a paper that argues for design-as-function-of-evidence and is five years out of date undercuts its own argument.  The living document is what demonstrates the self-application claim.\n\n    Reader-need differentiation: different readers want different things.  A funder reviewing a proposal wants the current state; a peer reviewer citing the work in a rebuttal wants a fixed target.  Composition (3) serves both.\n\n    Cross-pattern: publication-cadence is downstream of ukrns/design-state-lineage (snapshots are checkpoints in the lineage) and a sibling of ukrns/cycle-position-publication (both publish design states at chosen intervals).\n\n    Mechanic, in short:\n    Mechanic: how the artefact is published (living, snapshot, both).\n    Serves: self-application's artefact-instantiation claim.\n    Axis: living document ↔ frozen snapshots.\n    Without this pattern: \"which paper\" is ambiguous; self-application cannot be demonstrated."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Declare the current composition (likely (3) living + periodic snapshots)\n    - Each snapshot carries: compute-date, assumptions-v2.edn revision, DOI or equivalent identifier\n    - Living version carries a visible \"current compute-date\" and \"current assumptions revision\"\n    - A clear statement: \"to cite this paper in academic work, cite [snapshot DOI]; to read the current state, see [living URL]\"\n    - Cross-reference with ukrns/design-state-lineage and ukrns/cycle-position-publication"}], :conclusion "A paper that is re-computable from evidence can be published in two forms: the living document (continuously updated, reflecting the current evidence state) and the snapshot (frozen at a moment, citable, DOI-bearing).  The service states which composition it is running on the axis between living-only, snapshot-only, and both, and resolves the citation ambiguity that arises when a paper has multiple states.  The publication composition is declared.  Readers can find both the current state and a citable snapshot.  Each snapshot declares its evidence-revision and compute-date.  Citation paths are explicit.", :directives {:audience "UKRN-S design, working-paper maintainers, readers citing the paper, archivists", :keywords ["implementation-pattern" "publication" "canonical-version" "snapshot" "DOI" "archival" "living-document" "citation"], :references ["ukrns/self-application" "ukrns/design-state-lineage"], :style "implementation-pattern", :title "Publication Cadence — Canonical vs Snapshot Artefacts (Implementation Pattern)", :tone "operational"}, :id "ukrns/publication-cadence", :keywords ["implementation-pattern" "publication" "canonical-version" "snapshot" "DOI" "archival" "living-document" "citation"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["ukrns/self-application" "ukrns/design-state-lineage"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/ukrns/publication-cadence.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Publication Cadence — Canonical vs Snapshot Artefacts (Implementation Pattern)", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "A paper that states a tension, names two or three compositions, and refuses to rank them is addressing the reader as a participant in the decision, not a consumer of a conclusion.  The working paper's reader exercises are not decoration; they are the points at which the reader is explicitly invited to pick a position on an axis and justify it.  The paper teaches its reader how to read design patterns.  The service states which composition it is running on the axis between reader-as-consumer and reader-as-diagnostic-participant.  For each axis in the working paper, the reader is explicitly invited to pick a position and justify it.  The exercises are load-bearing: the paper's structure teaches pattern-reading through them.  For UKRN-S operational documents, the composition on the reader-as-participant axis is a deliberate choice per document and per audience, not a default."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "This meta-pattern is about the address of the working paper — whom it is written for and in what posture — and, by projection, about how UKRN-S could address its own network of institutions, trainers, and funders.  The five main patterns follow a Tension / Composition / Check structure that deliberately makes axes visible without prescribing a single answer.  The reader exercises (Reader — diagnose your current programme; Reader — which of these five would you commission first; Reader — what does this table let you say, and what does it explicitly not support?; Reader — if you had resources for Step 1 only, would you still progress?) are the places where the reader is invited into the diagnostic itself.  Take the exercises out and the axes revert to statements; the reader becomes a consumer again."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The pattern operates on the axis: reader-as-consumer (receives conclusions; reads for information) ↔ reader-as-diagnostic-participant (is invited to pick a position; reads for decision).\n    Irreducible: the same section cannot simultaneously deliver a closed conclusion and invite an open choice.  A reader confronted with 'you must decide which composition you would run' has a different reading experience than one shown 'the authors recommend composition X'."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Real costs: a consumer-oriented paper is faster to read, easier to cite, and respects the reader's time; a participant-oriented paper demands more of the reader and produces more friction.  Both orientations carry genuine costs.\n    Active dynamic: the right orientation depends on the reader and the moment.  Early in a paper's lifecycle, when the argument is forming and the decision is genuinely open, participation is productive.  As claims harden and decisions are made, participation becomes performative (the reader is invited to agree with a choice already taken).  Audiences also differ: funders may want conclusions; institutional leads may need to participate; trainers may need to see themselves in the diagnosis."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "      - Conclusory exercises: the 'Reader — pick a position' is answered by the paper two sentences later\n      - Decorative exercises: exercises present but unrelated to the pattern's axis; ornamental rather than pedagogical\n      - Hidden recommendation: three compositions stated, one discreetly preferred by diction; the reader reads through the preference despite the non-ranking\n      - Uniform consumer address: the paper addresses every reader as a consumer regardless of what is being decided; no participation invited\n      - Uniform participant address: the paper demands participation from every reader including those who need closure (e.g. funders under decision pressure); produces frustration rather than engagement\n      - Context-less invitation: 'Reader — pick a composition' without the context the reader would need to answer; invitation is formal rather than real"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Operate an explicit move on the named axis (see substructure below); steward the pattern against the declared discipline."} {:name "COMPOSITIONS", :name-key "compositions", :slug "compositions", :text "      (1) Exercises at section boundaries: each section ends with a Reader exercise that asks the reader to pick a position on the section's axis; the answer is yours, the paper provides the framework.  This is the working paper's current approach.  Gives up reading speed; demands more of each reader.\n      (2) Exercises consolidated at end: one 'working with this paper' section at the end collects all the diagnostic invitations; the body of the paper reads as a consumer paper; the exercises are a separate, opt-in layer.  Gives up the axis-by-axis participation; keeps the consumer reading path clean.\n      (3) No exercises, maximum consumer-paced: the paper states the tensions and compositions but does not explicitly invite the reader to pick; the reader may infer the invitation or not.  Gives up the pedagogical move of teaching pattern-reading; keeps the paper's surface the shortest.\n      These compositions are not ranked; the paper is at Composition (1) by deliberate choice and the exercises are load-bearing for the pedagogical claim (see BECAUSE)."} {:name "CHECK", :name-key "check", :slug "check", :text "      Engaged when: the paper (or service document) explicitly names axes, unranked compositions, and invites the reader to pick; exercises are load-bearing rather than decorative; the reader who skips the exercises has a legible but diminished reading.\n      Failing when: the paper names tensions but quietly recommends one composition; exercises present but conclusory (their 'answers' are given by the paper); the reader is addressed uniformly as a consumer regardless of the decision being described; the participatory surface is ornamental rather than pedagogical."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Pattern-reading is learned: The Tension / Composition / Check structure is not self-explanatory on first encounter.  A reader meeting it for the first time has to be shown, not told, that the compositions are genuinely unranked and that picking among them is the reader's work.  The exercises are the teaching moments.  Take them out and the Tension / Composition / Check structure reads as a presentation of options from which the authors have discreetly chosen one; put them in and the structure reads as a framework for shared decision.\n\n    Unranked compositions require invitation: A paper that names three compositions and stops leaves the reader to guess the authors' preference.  A paper that names three and invites the reader to pick one makes the non-ranking explicit.  Without the invitation, the reader's default is to hunt for the authorial preference; with it, the reader's default is to consider their own position.  The invitation changes the reading posture.\n\n    Refuses authoritative closure: The exercises are not merely participatory in form; they are epistemically humble in content.  'The answer is yours; the point is that the answer lets UKRN-S justify the next T1 budget on uncertainty-reduction grounds, not on general interest' explicitly refuses to tell the reader what to do.  This is the same epistemic stance as ukrns/computation-as-exploration (prior, not posterior) applied to the paper's rhetoric.\n\n    Diagnostic reading teaches diagnostic acting: A funder or institutional lead who has read the paper participatorily has already done some of the work ukrns/diagnose-before-prescribing asks UKRN-S to do.  The reading rehearsal transfers: having named a binding constraint on paper, they are readier to recognise one in practice.  This is the meta-pattern's projection into the main patterns.\n\n    Projection into main patterns: UKRN-S's own address to its network could maintain the same posture.  Commissioned service documents, profile diagnoses, measurement-wave justifications could all invite the institution to pick a position rather than delivering a service recommendation.  The meta-pattern is thus not only about reading the paper; it is about addressing the network.\n\n    Audience-shifting caveat: The right address depends on audience.  A funder's ingestion path may need more closure than a trainer's.  A paper (or service) that invites participation uniformly will be read as indecisive by some and respectful by others.  The service composes — per document, per audience — the right participation level.\n\n    Mechanic, in short:\n    Derived from the working paper's explicit reader-exercise scaffold and the Tension / Composition / Check structure those exercises animate.\n    Meta-pattern character: describes the paper's address to its reader and, by projection, UKRN-S's address to its network.\n    Projection into main patterns: participatory reading rehearses participatory decision-making; ukrns/diagnose-before-prescribing, ukrns/measure-where-least-sure, and ukrns/open-the-triangle all describe decisions that would benefit from institutional participation in the same posture the paper rehearses with its readers.\n    Axis framing follows the same Tension / Composition / Check structure as the main patterns.  The self-referential character — a pattern about addressing the reader that uses the same pattern form to address its own reader — is deliberate.\n    Coordination requirement: Low at paper time (single author team can choose the posture); Medium to High at service time (consistent participatory address across the network's communications requires coordination)."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - For each axis in the working paper, maintain a reader exercise that invites the reader to pick a position on that axis\n    - Make the exercises genuinely open: the paper provides the framework for answering, not the answer\n    - Distinguish axes where the authors do take a baseline position (e.g. ukrns/measure-where-least-sure favours VoI-first) from axes where they do not — the reader should know which is which\n    - For UKRN-S operational documents: consider the same posture for service communications; where appropriate, invite the recipient to pick a position rather than delivering a recommendation\n    - Audience-check each exercise: does this audience have the context to answer? if not, provide the context before the invitation\n    - Track which exercises land: which are read, which are skipped, which produce discussion"}], :conclusion "A paper that states a tension, names two or three compositions, and refuses to rank them is addressing the reader as a participant in the decision, not a consumer of a conclusion.  The working paper's reader exercises are not decoration; they are the points at which the reader is explicitly invited to pick a position on an axis and justify it.  The paper teaches its reader how to read design patterns.  The service states which composition it is running on the axis between reader-as-consumer and reader-as-diagnostic-participant.  For each axis in the working paper, the reader is explicitly invited to pick a position and justify it.  The exercises are load-bearing: the paper's structure teaches pattern-reading through them.  For UKRN-S operational documents, the composition on the reader-as-participant axis is a deliberate choice per document and per audience, not a default.", :directives {:audience "UKRN-S design, working-paper authors, readers, funders, institutional leads", :keywords ["meta-pattern" "reader-exercise" "reader-participation" "diagnostic-invitation" "axis-visible" "reader-consumer" "reader-co-diagnostician" "media-theory" "pattern-reading"], :references ["ukrns/diagnose-before-prescribing" "ukrns/measure-where-least-sure" "ukrns/open-the-triangle" "ukrns/computation-as-exploration"], :style "meta-pattern", :title "Reader as Diagnostic Participant (Meta-Pattern)", :tone "analytic-foundational"}, :id "ukrns/reader-as-participant", :keywords ["meta-pattern" "reader-exercise" "reader-participation" "diagnostic-invitation" "axis-visible" "reader-consumer" "reader-co-diagnostician" "media-theory" "pattern-reading"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["ukrns/diagnose-before-prescribing" "ukrns/measure-where-least-sure" "ukrns/open-the-triangle" "ukrns/computation-as-exploration"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/ukrns/reader-as-participant.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Reader as Diagnostic Participant (Meta-Pattern)", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "A paper that claims self-application is only as strong as the infrastructure an actual reader needs to rerun it.  The service states which composition it is running on the axis between full reproducibility (a reader runs the notebook and reproduces the current outputs) and accessible readout (the paper declares enough in an annex for a reader to reimplement the computation in any language).  Both endpoints serve different readers; neither alone is sufficient for long-horizon reproducibility.  The current reader-run composition is declared.  At least one path works for its declared audience.  A fallback for tooling decay is maintained."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "This implementation pattern specifies the reader-facing reproducibility mechanic behind ukrns/self-application.  The meta-pattern claims the artefact is re-computable; this pattern addresses the practical question of what infrastructure a reader needs.  Without a declared reader-run path, the self-application claim is aspirational for most readers and will decay as the compute environment ages."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The pattern operates on the axis: full reproducibility (reader runs the notebook in a declared environment) ↔ accessible readout (the paper's annex contains enough equations, data, and protocols for first-principles reconstruction in any language).\n    Irreducible: a single infrastructure cannot simultaneously be maximally one-click-runnable in today's tooling AND maximally independent of any specific tooling for long-term preservation."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Real costs: reproducibility costs tooling maintenance — a declared environment will decay as dependencies age; readout costs rerun fidelity — a reader reconstructing from first principles may reproduce the approach but not bit-for-bit match.\n    Active dynamic: technical readers in the near term appreciate reproducibility; non-technical readers need readout; archival readers in the far future need readout (tooling will have changed)."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "      - Unspecified environment: reader attempts to rerun, discovers dependencies are not declared, gives up\n      - Environment-decay: the Docker image stops building; the rerun path is broken and nobody notices\n      - Annex-incomplete: readout is claimed but the annex is missing details needed for first-principles reconstruction\n      - Fallback-missing: primary path depends on specific tooling; when tooling decays, self-application is lost\n      - No reader-feedback channel: failures are unreported; the maintainer never learns the rerun is broken"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Operate an explicit move on the named axis (see substructure below); steward the pattern against the declared discipline."} {:name "COMPOSITIONS", :name-key "compositions", :slug "compositions", :text "      (1) Notebook + environment: fully-reproducible containerised environment (Docker, Nix, or similar) that locks dependencies; reader runs the notebook and reproduces current outputs.  Gives up low-barrier access.\n      (2) Static + reimplementation annex: the paper publishes equations, levers, uncertainty model, sampling protocol, and declarative assumptions file in enough detail that a reader can reconstruct the computation in any language.  Gives up one-click rerun.\n      (3) Both: containerised environment for near-term rerun + reimplementation annex for long-term preservation and first-principles access.  Gives up simplicity; double maintenance.\n      The paper-draft-16-april has Annex B, which satisfies Composition (2); the notebook (Clojure/Clay) satisfies Composition (1) partially.  Operational use would benefit from declaring the composition as (3) with both paths declared."} {:name "CHECK", :name-key "check", :slug "check", :text "      Engaged when: the composition is declared; at least one of the rerun paths works for the declared audience; the fallback composition (typically readout) is maintained even if the primary is available.\n      Failing when: the paper claims self-application but no reader can actually rerun; the notebook runs but only in an undeclared environment; the annex is missing details needed for reconstruction; there is no fallback for when the primary path decays."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Near-term vs long-term reproducibility: ukrns/self-application wants re-computability.  Near-term: readers today want to rerun.  Long-term: readers in 20 years want to reconstruct when the current tooling is obsolete.  These are different audiences served by different compositions.\n\n    Reader posture differentiation: technical readers can use a containerised environment; non-technical readers cannot but may want to understand what the computation does.  Composition (2) is accessible to any reader who reads mathematics; Composition (1) is accessible only to readers with the tooling.\n\n    Graceful degradation: the compute environment will eventually decay.  When Docker images stop building, what happens?  Composition (2) is the standing fallback: the reimplementation annex remains readable even when the environment is obsolete.  Projects with only Composition (1) lose their self-application property when tooling decays.\n\n    Declared access level: readers should know before attempting what level of reproducibility the paper supports.  A paper that claims self-application but whose rerun fails on first attempt undermines trust.  Declaration manages expectation.\n\n    Mechanic, in short:\n    Mechanic: what infrastructure a reader needs to rerun or reconstruct the computation.\n    Serves: self-application's re-computability claim.\n    Axis: full reproducibility ↔ accessible readout.\n    Without this pattern: self-application is aspirational and decays with the compute environment."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Declare the current composition in the paper or project documentation\n    - If (1): specify the environment (Docker image, Nix flake, or equivalent); test the rerun from scratch periodically\n    - If (2): ensure Annex B is complete enough for reimplementation (equations, factor lists, lever definitions, uncertainty model, sampling protocol)\n    - If (3): maintain both paths; declare which is primary and which is fallback\n    - Invite reader feedback: someone who tries to rerun and fails should have a channel\n    - Cross-reference with ukrns/publication-cadence (snapshots need rerun-path documentation at snapshot time)"}], :conclusion "A paper that claims self-application is only as strong as the infrastructure an actual reader needs to rerun it.  The service states which composition it is running on the axis between full reproducibility (a reader runs the notebook and reproduces the current outputs) and accessible readout (the paper declares enough in an annex for a reader to reimplement the computation in any language).  Both endpoints serve different readers; neither alone is sufficient for long-horizon reproducibility.  The current reader-run composition is declared.  At least one path works for its declared audience.  A fallback for tooling decay is maintained.", :directives {:audience "UKRN-S design, working-paper maintainers, readers attempting to rerun, peer reviewers, archivists", :keywords ["implementation-pattern" "reproducibility" "reader-rerun" "containerisation" "reimplementation" "degradation-path" "accessibility" "compute-environment"], :references ["ukrns/self-application" "ukrns/computation-as-exploration" "ukrns/publication-cadence"], :style "implementation-pattern", :title "Reader Run Path — Minimum Reproducibility Infrastructure (Implementation Pattern)", :tone "operational"}, :id "ukrns/reader-run-path", :keywords ["implementation-pattern" "reproducibility" "reader-rerun" "containerisation" "reimplementation" "degradation-path" "accessibility" "compute-environment"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["ukrns/self-application" "ukrns/computation-as-exploration" "ukrns/publication-cadence"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/ukrns/reader-run-path.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Reader Run Path — Minimum Reproducibility Infrastructure (Implementation Pattern)", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Every capability the network could provide — evaluation analysis, coordination/liaison, scaffold maintenance, any future addition — has a scale choice (per-institution vs network-scale) that must be inspectable.  The service maintains a register of these choices with rationale, date, and revision history, and states which composition it is running on the axis between central register and distributed per-capability documentation.  The register lists every capability the network could provide.  Each entry has scale, rationale, date, revision history.  Contestation channel is declared."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "This implementation pattern specifies the publication mechanic behind ukrns/provision-at-network-scale.  The main pattern's invariant requires scale choices to be published and revisable; without a register, those choices exist in project-memory rather than as public artefacts institutions can contest."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The pattern operates on the axis: central register (one maintained document listing every capability and its scale decision) ↔ distributed per-capability documentation (each capability described in its own document, with scale decision inline).\n    Irreducible: a single documentation strategy cannot simultaneously maximise centralised coherence and distributed depth."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Real costs: central costs coherence maintenance — keeping the register aligned with reality — and creates a single point of obsolescence; distributed costs findability — readers may not know every capability exists or may not find its scale decision easily.\n    Active dynamic: small capability sets favour central; large sets favour distributed; mixed strategies become attractive as the set grows past roughly 10 capabilities."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "      - Incomplete register: capabilities exist operationally but are not registered\n      - Rationale absence: scale stated without reasoning; choice appears arbitrary\n      - Stale revision: rationale dated years ago; reality has moved\n      - No contestation channel: institutions have no declared way to flag mis-scaling\n      - Register-as-table-only: the capability trade-off table in the paper is treated as the register; the table is a snapshot, a register is a living artefact"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Operate an explicit move on the named axis (see substructure below); steward the pattern against the declared discipline."} {:name "COMPOSITIONS", :name-key "compositions", :slug "compositions", :text "      (1) Central register: a single artefact (markdown, EDN, or similar) listing every capability with scale, rationale, date, revision history.  Gives up per-capability depth.\n      (2) Capability-by-capability docs: each capability has its own document including scale decision and rationale.  Gives up single-point findability.\n      (3) Both — central index + per-capability deep docs: index lists names and current scale; each entry links to the capability's own doc.  Gives up simplicity; doubles maintenance.\n      The paper currently operates at a proto-(1) level via the capability trade-off table in §2; operational use requires a living register."} {:name "CHECK", :name-key "check", :slug "check", :text "      Engaged when: every current capability is findable in the register (or via the index); each entry has scale, rationale, date, revision history; institutions can contest choices via a declared channel.\n      Failing when: some capabilities exist but are not in the register; entries have scale without rationale; rationale exists but is dated years ago without revision; contestation has no channel."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Contestability: ukrns/provision-at-network-scale's invariant requires institutions to be able to contest scale choices.  Contestation is only possible if the choice is findable and its rationale inspectable.  The register is the publication mechanic that makes contestation possible.\n\n    Asymmetric trade-offs: the main pattern is built on the observation that capabilities have different trade-off shapes.  The register is where those shapes are compared side by side; losing the register collapses the asymmetry argument.\n\n    Revision history: as the network grows, scale choices may shift — a capability viable at one scale today may shift tomorrow.  The register's revision history is the mechanism that makes scale evolution legible rather than silent.\n\n    Cross-pattern: the register is a particular kind of deposit (ukrns/open-the-triangle); it is itself a service-event with openness gain (institutions can see the current provisioning map) and governance cost (someone must maintain it).\n\n    Mechanic, in short:\n    Mechanic: how capability scale choices are published and revised.\n    Serves: provision-at-network-scale's invariant.\n    Axis: central register ↔ distributed per-capability docs.\n    Without this pattern: scale choices exist in project memory, not as contestable public artefacts."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Declare the current composition (most likely (1) central register at start) in project documentation\n    - Create the register as an artefact (not merely a table inside the working paper); make it findable from the project root\n    - Every entry: name, current scale, rationale, date, revision history, contestation channel\n    - Cross-reference from the working paper's §3 capability trade-off table to the register\n    - At network scale: allow institutions to flag capabilities they think are mis-scaled"}], :conclusion "Every capability the network could provide — evaluation analysis, coordination/liaison, scaffold maintenance, any future addition — has a scale choice (per-institution vs network-scale) that must be inspectable.  The service maintains a register of these choices with rationale, date, and revision history, and states which composition it is running on the axis between central register and distributed per-capability documentation.  The register lists every capability the network could provide.  Each entry has scale, rationale, date, revision history.  Contestation channel is declared.", :directives {:audience "UKRN-S design, institutional leads, capability owners, auditors", :keywords ["implementation-pattern" "capability-register" "scale-decisions" "central-register" "distributed-docs" "findability" "subsidiarity"], :references ["ukrns/provision-at-network-scale"], :style "implementation-pattern", :title "Scale Register — Publishing Capability Scale Choices (Implementation Pattern)", :tone "operational"}, :id "ukrns/scale-register", :keywords ["implementation-pattern" "capability-register" "scale-decisions" "central-register" "distributed-docs" "findability" "subsidiarity"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["ukrns/provision-at-network-scale"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/ukrns/scale-register.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Scale Register — Publishing Capability Scale Choices (Implementation Pattern)", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "A paper that argues for 'design as a function of evidence' and is itself a static, frozen PDF undermines its own claim by example.  The working paper is a notebook that loads the evidence file, recomputes the model, and emits the argument — so a reader with updated evidence can see the design shift.  Form instantiates claim.  The service states which composition it is running on the axis between static-artefact and instantiating-artefact.  The working paper is re-computable from a declarative evidence file and a documented computational environment.  Every published version states its evidence-file revision.  The artefact's lineage is preserved so a reader can see the history of argument shifts against evidence shifts."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "This meta-pattern is about the construction of the working paper and, by projection, about how UKRN-S could publish its own design state.  If the main patterns describe a learning system that updates design as evidence accrues (ukrns/design-as-function-of-evidence), then an artefact that describes such a system without being one makes a weaker argument than an artefact that is one.  The meta-pattern is media-theoretic: medium as message.  The paper can be re-run because the medium supports it."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The pattern operates on the axis: static artefact (frozen document, snapshot of claims) ↔ instantiating artefact (re-computable, argument emitted from evidence on each run).\n    Irreducible: a single artefact cannot be maximally static (frozen, universally readable, dependency-free) AND maximally instantiating (re-computable from live data, reflecting evidence-state-of-today).  These are different kinds of object."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Real costs: static artefacts are cheaper to produce, easier to archive, familiar to readers, and survive infrastructural decay; instantiating artefacts require computational infrastructure, active maintenance, and readers (or institutions) with the technical capacity to rerun.  Both carry real cost.\n    Active dynamic: at publication time, the instantiating form has high production and reader cost; as notebook tooling matures, cost drops and instantiation becomes viable for more artefacts.  A decade from now, the current paper's tooling will likely be obsolete; the static record is the durable one.  The right balance shifts over the lifetime of the medium."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "      - Claim-without-instance: argues for evidence-driven design but is itself a frozen document with no re-runnable lineage\n      - Embedded evidence: assumptions hard-coded in the document body; changes require manual editing rather than updating a data file\n      - Inert computation: the notebook runs but its outputs do not depend on the evidence file; reruns produce identical results\n      - Untracked lineage: published versions do not declare which evidence-file revision they were computed from; the reader cannot tell what has changed\n      - Tooling lock-in: the artefact depends on specific infrastructure that will evaporate; no fallback composition is maintained\n      - Performative self-application: the artefact is packaged as runnable but no reader actually reruns; form without function"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Operate an explicit move on the named axis (see substructure below); steward the pattern against the declared discipline."} {:name "COMPOSITIONS", :name-key "compositions", :slug "compositions", :text "      (1) Notebook-as-paper: the artefact is a live notebook; a reader with the evidence file and the computational environment reruns to see the current design state.  Gives up durability and frictionless reading; requires active tool maintenance.\n      (2) Static paper + declarative EDN: the paper is published as a static document but accompanied by the assumptions file (assumptions-v2.edn) and a reimplementation pointer (Annex B); a motivated reader can reconstruct the computation from first principles.  Gives up the live-rerun experience; keeps the archival property of static papers.\n      (3) Companion repository: a static paper + a separate runnable notebook in a versioned repository, with an explicit mapping from paper sections to notebook cells.  Gives up unified presentation; keeps both audiences served (archival readers + technical rerunners).\n      These compositions are not ranked; the paper's current form is closest to Composition (1) with Composition (2) as fallback.  The choice may shift as tooling and archival norms evolve."} {:name "CHECK", :name-key "check", :slug "check", :text "      Engaged when: the artefact can be re-run with an updated evidence file and the argument shifts accordingly; the re-run process is documented and reproducible; the relationship between the published version and the live state is itself inspectable (e.g. version history, git log).\n      Failing when: the artefact claims to be a function of evidence but cannot actually be re-run; the evidence file is embedded in the document body rather than separable; re-runs produce the same output regardless of input changes (computation inert); the artefact is published as a static snapshot without a re-runnable lineage."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    REPL discipline (linked: t4r/ pattern library): Read-Evaluate-Print-Loop is the computational form the paper borrows.  Each run of the notebook is: read the assumptions; evaluate the model; print the argument; loop back when evidence changes.  This is not decorative: the loop is what makes the design-as-function-of-evidence claim concrete rather than aspirational.\n\n    Ontogenetic accretion (linked: ukrns/open-the-triangle): The working paper is itself a deposit.  Each version captures the design state at a point in time; the notebook lineage traces how the design evolved as evidence accrued.  The artefact IS a growth ring in the programme's architecture.\n\n    Medium as message: The paper does what it says.  A reader who sees the notebook rerunning after an evidence update sees the argument's claim instantiated rather than described.  This is stronger evidence than prose: the claim is demonstrated rather than asserted.\n\n    Projection into main patterns: UKRN-S's design state could be published in the same form.  Institutions could see the current population model, the current diagnosis for their profile, the current service-design cycle, as output of a live computation from the current evidence file.  The update-policy choice (ukrns/design-as-function-of-evidence) becomes visible in the versioning of the artefact: every re-run is a potential update; every skipped re-run is a qualifying absence.\n\n    Pre-empts self-undercutting: A paper arguing for 'services should update as evidence arrives' that is itself a frozen snapshot creates the strongest available objection to its own thesis.  Self-application is the method for dissolving that objection.  The inverse objection (why should anyone trust a moving target?) is real but not symmetric: the trust objection applies to services and artefacts alike and is answered by the update-policy discipline (ukrns/design-as-function-of-evidence).\n\n    Mechanic, in short:\n    Derived from the working paper's self-application stance: the notebook form is not a packaging choice but a methodological one.\n    Meta-pattern character: describes how the paper is made and, by projection, how UKRN-S could publish its design state.\n    Projection into main patterns: ukrns/design-as-function-of-evidence specifies that the service updates on evidence; this meta-pattern specifies that the service's artefacts can themselves be instances of that update.  ukrns/open-the-triangle specifies that deposits persist; this meta-pattern specifies the form those deposits take.\n    Axis framing follows the same Tension / Composition / Check structure as the main patterns.\n    Coordination requirement: Medium (single-author team can adopt at paper time; institutional operationalisation requires shared tooling and maintenance)."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - Maintain the working paper as a rerunnable notebook; any published version (PDF, HTML, archive) declares which evidence-file revision it was computed from\n    - When publishing a new version, surface what changed: which evidence updates triggered which argument shifts\n    - Package the notebook and the assumptions file together so a reader can reproduce the computation\n    - For operational use at UKRN-S: consider publishing the design state as a live notebook that updates when measurement waves complete\n    - Maintain a mapping from paper sections to notebook cells (Annex B already partially does this)\n    - Record a deprecation plan: when the current tooling becomes obsolete, how will the artefact's claim survive? (Composition 2 is the standing fallback.)"}], :conclusion "A paper that argues for 'design as a function of evidence' and is itself a static, frozen PDF undermines its own claim by example.  The working paper is a notebook that loads the evidence file, recomputes the model, and emits the argument — so a reader with updated evidence can see the design shift.  Form instantiates claim.  The service states which composition it is running on the axis between static-artefact and instantiating-artefact.  The working paper is re-computable from a declarative evidence file and a documented computational environment.  Every published version states its evidence-file revision.  The artefact's lineage is preserved so a reader can see the history of argument shifts against evidence shifts.", :directives {:audience "UKRN-S design, working-paper authors, readers who want to rerun, futon developers", :keywords ["meta-pattern" "self-application" "artefact-as-instance" "REPL" "notebook-as-paper" "form-as-claim" "reproducibility" "media-theory" "recomputable" "reflexivity"], :references ["ukrns/design-as-function-of-evidence" "ukrns/open-the-triangle" "ukrns/computation-as-exploration"], :style "meta-pattern", :title "Self-Application — The Artefact Instantiates Its Claim (Meta-Pattern)", :tone "analytic-foundational"}, :id "ukrns/self-application", :keywords ["meta-pattern" "self-application" "artefact-as-instance" "REPL" "notebook-as-paper" "form-as-claim" "reproducibility" "media-theory" "recomputable" "reflexivity"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["ukrns/design-as-function-of-evidence" "ukrns/open-the-triangle" "ukrns/computation-as-exploration"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/ukrns/self-application.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Self-Application — The Artefact Instantiates Its Claim (Meta-Pattern)", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Not every institutional condition is equally movable by a network service. A design that types its own levers is more honest, more governable, and easier to evaluate than one that implies uniform control."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "The current paper's design section already distinguishes between conditions UKRN Services can move by contract or routine, conditions it can influence only through evidence and pressure, and conditions that remain prior constraints."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The pattern operates on the axis: undifferentiated influence claim ↔ typed leverage claim."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Real costs: typing leverage narrows the design's apparent power and exposes where it depends on action by others."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Name the direct levers, the indirect levers, and the out-of-scope conditions explicitly, and keep the out-of-scope ones visible in later verification."}], :conclusion "Not every institutional condition is equally movable by a network service. A design that types its own levers is more honest, more governable, and easier to evaluate than one that implies uniform control.", :directives {:audience "UKRN Services design, funders, institutional leads, board readers", :keywords ["sketch" "leverage" "direct" "indirect" "out-of-scope" "honesty" "service-design" "institutional-conditions"], :references ["system-coherence/separate-configurable-choices-from-load-bearing-couplings" "writing-coherence/plain-language-thesis"], :style "sketch", :title "Separate Direct, Indirect, and Out-of-Scope Levers (Sketch)", :tone "analytic-foundational"}, :id "ukrns/separate-direct-indirect-and-out-of-scope-levers", :keywords ["sketch" "leverage" "direct" "indirect" "out-of-scope" "honesty" "service-design" "institutional-conditions"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["system-coherence/separate-configurable-choices-from-load-bearing-couplings" "writing-coherence/plain-language-thesis"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/ukrns/separate-direct-indirect-and-out-of-scope-levers.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Separate Direct, Indirect, and Out-of-Scope Levers (Sketch)", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Some uncertainties belong to the evidence base and must be resolved or explicitly carried forward; others are implementation choices that properly belong to UKRN Services once the design is accepted. A paper that mixes the two makes its own gaps look larger than they are and makes implementation ownership harder to see."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "The current working paper now distinguishes open questions about impact and learning from hand-off questions about resourcing, evaluator configuration, partnerships, and topic priority. That separation is doing real governance work for the reader."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The pattern operates on the axis: unresolved evidence need ↔ owned implementation choice."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Real costs: separating the two exposes where the paper cannot yet close a claim, and it also exposes where the implementing team cannot defer a judgment by asking for more analysis."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Name the evidence gaps explicitly; name the implementation decisions explicitly; and refuse to let one masquerade as the other."}], :conclusion "Some uncertainties belong to the evidence base and must be resolved or explicitly carried forward; others are implementation choices that properly belong to UKRN Services once the design is accepted. A paper that mixes the two makes its own gaps look larger than they are and makes implementation ownership harder to see.", :directives {:audience "UKRN Services design, working-paper authors, board readers, implementers", :keywords ["sketch" "open-questions" "hand-off" "implementation" "evidence-gap" "ownership" "decision-boundary"], :references ["system-coherence/bind-open-questions-to-closure-mechanisms" "system-coherence/separate-configurable-choices-from-load-bearing-couplings" "writing-coherence/meta-title"], :style "sketch", :title "Separate Evidence Gaps from Implementation Decisions (Sketch)", :tone "analytic-foundational"}, :id "ukrns/separate-evidence-gaps-from-implementation-decisions", :keywords ["sketch" "open-questions" "hand-off" "implementation" "evidence-gap" "ownership" "decision-boundary"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["system-coherence/bind-open-questions-to-closure-mechanisms" "system-coherence/separate-configurable-choices-from-load-bearing-couplings" "writing-coherence/meta-title"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/ukrns/separate-evidence-gaps-from-implementation-decisions.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Separate Evidence Gaps from Implementation Decisions (Sketch)", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Periods of active ringfenced funding and periods without it produce different patterns of institutional movement. A design that ignores that rhythm will misread the environment as random variation."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "The current paper's sector-priorities track and its closing finding both identify ringfenced funding as a decisive enabling condition for collaborative research-culture work."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The pattern operates on the axis: funding cycle as background ↔ funding cycle as explicit operating condition."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Real costs: acknowledging the funding rhythm narrows what can honestly be promised in quieter periods and makes the design more contingent on external timing than a cleaner abstract model would prefer."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Build plans, expectations, and interpretation around the funding rhythm explicitly rather than treating it as noise outside the design."}], :conclusion "Periods of active ringfenced funding and periods without it produce different patterns of institutional movement. A design that ignores that rhythm will misread the environment as random variation.", :directives {:audience "UKRN Services design, funders, institutional leads, evaluators", :keywords ["sketch" "funding-cycle" "ringfenced-funding" "operational-rhythm" "sector-scale" "planning" "external-conditions"], :references ["system-coherence/treat-content-refresh-as-second-order-when-conditions-bind" "writing-coherence/meet-the-reader-where-they-are"], :style "sketch", :title "Treat Funder Cycles as Operational Rhythm (Sketch)", :tone "analytic-foundational"}, :id "ukrns/treat-funder-cycles-as-operational-rhythm", :keywords ["sketch" "funding-cycle" "ringfenced-funding" "operational-rhythm" "sector-scale" "planning" "external-conditions"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["system-coherence/treat-content-refresh-as-second-order-when-conditions-bind" "writing-coherence/meet-the-reader-where-they-are"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/ukrns/treat-funder-cycles-as-operational-rhythm.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Treat Funder Cycles as Operational Rhythm (Sketch)", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "An abstract model states conditional dependencies; a single case instantiates them.  Two contrasting cases that occupy different positions in the model's space — and that therefore fall into different modes — show the model biting differently.  The working paper's FG2A-P1 (delivery-gated) and FG3-A (multiply-constrained) are the paired worked contrast; neither case alone would have produced the same legibility.  The service states which composition it is running on the axis between abstraction-at-population-scale and concreteness-at-case-scale.  For each major modelling claim in the working paper (and in UKRN-S operational diagnosis), at least one pair of worked cases is traced through the model; the pair is chosen to show the claim biting differently; the selection rationale is explicit; worked cases are updated as evidence updates."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "This meta-pattern is about the rhetoric of the working paper and, by projection, about how UKRN-S could maintain diagnostic anchors in its operational work.  The population model is abstract (D × A, gate/support, mode tree).  The Table 1 ten-profile sweep is intermediate.  The worked examples are concrete.  FG2A-P1 is traced through the model to show delivery-gating; FG3-A is traced through to show multi-constraint.  The pairing is not redundant — each case reveals what the other does not — and the contrast is load-bearing for the diagnose-before-prescribing pattern (ukrns/diagnose-before-prescribing)."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The pattern operates on the axis: abstraction (population-level, model-level statements) ↔ concreteness (case-level, profile-level instantiations).\n    Irreducible: a single statement cannot be simultaneously maximally abstract (covering all cases) and maximally concrete (tracing one case through in full detail).  They answer different reading needs."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Real costs: abstraction without concrete leaves the reader unable to see what the model claims means operationally; concreteness without abstraction is anecdote and does not generalise.  Both carry real costs when taken alone.\n    Active dynamic: a reader unfamiliar with the model needs more concrete; a reader deep in the data needs less.  As the paper's audience shifts over time — from authors and peer reviewers to funders, institutional leads, trainers — the right abstraction/concreteness balance shifts.  Early cycles may favour concreteness; later cycles, with trained readers and accumulated evidence, may favour abstraction."} {:name "FAILURE-MODES", :name-key "failure-modes", :slug "failure-modes", :text "      - Single-case-as-representative: one profile treated as 'the case' without contrast; the reader cannot see what the model discriminates\n      - Catalogue drift: every profile traced without selection; loses the discriminative power of contrast\n      - Politically-chosen cases: worked cases picked because they carry institutional weight rather than because they expose model behaviour\n      - Rhetorical-only concreteness: worked cases present for legibility but not linked explicitly to abstract claims; reader can read either layer but not bridge them\n      - Stale worked cases: cases not updated as evidence base shifts; current claim and worked example contradict\n      - Abstraction-only: claims stated without concrete instantiation; reader without the model in hand cannot follow"} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Operate an explicit move on the named axis (see substructure below); steward the pattern against the declared discipline."} {:name "COMPOSITIONS", :name-key "compositions", :slug "compositions", :text "      (1) Paired contrast: two worked cases positioned to show the model biting differently (FG2A-P1 delivery-gated + FG3-A multiply-constrained).  Gives up the depth of a single-case deep dive; gains legibility of the model's differential behaviour.\n      (2) Single deep case: one profile traced through in exhaustive detail — every NPT factor, every lever effect, every mode-probability update.  Gives up the contrast that shows the model's differential behaviour; gains density of evidence for one institution.\n      (3) Population overview + one representative: the ten-profile table + one 'typical' case.  Gives up the contrast and the depth; gains communicative simplicity.\n      These compositions are not ranked; the paper is at Composition (1) deliberately, and the pairing is what makes the multiplicativity claim (ukrns/diagnose-before-prescribing BECAUSE) concrete."} {:name "CHECK", :name-key "check", :slug "check", :text "      Engaged when: at least two worked cases are traced through the model, and the pair is chosen to show the model biting differently (different binding constraints, different modes, different lever-response profiles).  The selection rationale is explicit.\n      Failing when: a single case is offered as representative without a contrast; all cases are traced without selection (catalogue drift); the paired cases are chosen for political salience rather than to show the model's differential behaviour; the relationship between abstract claims and concrete cases is not made explicit."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Contrast reveals the axis: A single worked case illustrates the model's machinery; two contrasting cases illustrate what the model discriminates between.  FG2A-P1 has strong architecture (A = 0.63) but weak delivery (D = 0.46) — the D × A gate is delivery-gated, and a scaffold intervention (Step 0) would move it toward multiplied.  FG3-A has weak both dimensions (D = 0.33, A = 0.21); no single-dimension lever suffices.  The two cases together show WHY diagnosis matters: the same lever bundle would produce different effects on each.  This is the multiplicativity claim made concrete.\n\n    Mode-space legibility: The three modes (Multiplied, Absorbed, Mismatch) are abstract until a case falls into each.  The paired contrast maps concrete profiles to mode-space positions: FG2A-P1 falls closest to mismatch at baseline (low D, architecture stronger than delivery); FG3-A falls deeper in mismatch (both dimensions below threshold).  A reader who has traced both can read the Table 1 profile sweep without further translation.\n\n    Abstraction ladder: The paper's rhetoric uses a three-rung ladder — abstract model (Annex B equations) → intermediate population sweep (Table 1/2) → concrete paired cases.  Each rung supports the next.  Removing the concrete rung collapses the ladder; a reader jumping from Annex B to Table 2 has no handhold.\n\n    Projection into main patterns: UKRN-S's operational diagnostic work could maintain paired anchor cases that represent the span of the profile space.  When a new institution joins the network, matching it against the anchors is the first step; the anchors are the operational instance of ukrns/diagnose-before-prescribing's binding-constraint-signature claim.  The meta-pattern thus projects into service practice, not only paper rhetoric.\n\n    Not every case is worth tracing: The paired contrast's discipline is selective — two cases chosen because they expose different model behaviours.  A paper that traces every case collapses to a catalogue (Composition variant: population overview, but without overview-level claims).  The selection itself is part of the pattern: which two cases, and why this pair.\n\n    Mechanic, in short:\n    Derived from the working paper's use of the FG2A-P1 / FG3-A paired worked example to make the multiplicative-gate claim concrete.\n    Meta-pattern character: describes a rhetorical discipline in the paper and, by projection, an operational discipline for UKRN-S's diagnostic work.\n    Projection into main patterns: ukrns/diagnose-before-prescribing's binding-constraint signature is abstract; anchor cases make it operational.  As a meta-pattern, worked contrast is how the paper teaches the reader to diagnose.\n    Axis framing follows the same Tension / Composition / Check structure as the main patterns.\n    Coordination requirement: Low at paper time; Medium at service time (anchor cases require maintenance as the profile map evolves)."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    - For each major modelling claim, maintain at least one worked pair that shows the claim biting differently in different profile positions\n    - Choose worked cases to span the model's discriminative dimensions (e.g. delivery-gated vs. multiply-constrained), not to represent 'typical' cases\n    - State the selection rationale: why these two, not two others\n    - For operational use at UKRN-S: maintain a small set of anchor profiles that new institutions can be matched against during diagnosis (ukrns/diagnose-before-prescribing)\n    - Update worked cases as the evidence base updates; stale worked cases are worse than no worked cases (they suggest a diagnosis that no longer holds)\n    - Preserve the abstraction ladder: abstract → intermediate → concrete; do not skip the concrete rung"}], :conclusion "An abstract model states conditional dependencies; a single case instantiates them.  Two contrasting cases that occupy different positions in the model's space — and that therefore fall into different modes — show the model biting differently.  The working paper's FG2A-P1 (delivery-gated) and FG3-A (multiply-constrained) are the paired worked contrast; neither case alone would have produced the same legibility.  The service states which composition it is running on the axis between abstraction-at-population-scale and concreteness-at-case-scale.  For each major modelling claim in the working paper (and in UKRN-S operational diagnosis), at least one pair of worked cases is traced through the model; the pair is chosen to show the claim biting differently; the selection rationale is explicit; worked cases are updated as evidence updates.", :directives {:audience "UKRN-S design, working-paper authors, readers who want to see the model work, evaluators", :keywords ["meta-pattern" "worked-example" "diagnostic-concrete" "paired-cases" "delivery-gated" "multiply-constrained" "contrast" "abstraction-concreteness" "FG2A-P1" "FG3-A"], :references ["ukrns/diagnose-before-prescribing" "ukrns/computation-as-exploration"], :style "meta-pattern", :title "Worked Contrast — Paired Concrete Cases That Show the Model Biting Differently (Meta-Pattern)", :tone "analytic-foundational"}, :id "ukrns/worked-contrast", :keywords ["meta-pattern" "worked-example" "diagnostic-concrete" "paired-cases" "delivery-gated" "multiply-constrained" "contrast" "abstraction-concreteness" "FG2A-P1" "FG3-A"], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["ukrns/diagnose-before-prescribing" "ukrns/computation-as-exploration"], :scores {}, :sigils [], :source-path "library/ukrns/worked-contrast.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Worked Contrast — Paired Concrete Cases That Show the Model Biting Differently (Meta-Pattern)", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Interpretive notes should be able to graduate from observations into\n  commitments — named actions with owners, timelines, and completion\n  criteria — so that collective sense-making produces accountable\n  follow-through."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are designing the bridge between interpretation and action within a facilitated programme. This does not apply when annotations are purely scholarly or archival."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    A pilot's interpretive notes surface actionable patterns —\n    \"these three stories describe the same unmet need\" or \"this\n    contrast link reveals a policy gap.\""} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Notes that remain observations create a culture of noticing\n    without acting. The gap between insight and response is where\n    community trust erodes."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Allow any interpretive note to be promoted to a commitment:\n    an action item with an owner, a deadline, a success criterion,\n    and a link back to the note and its source stories. Commitments\n    appear in a steward-visible tracker alongside the constellation."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    When annotation becomes commitment, the interpretive layer stops\n    being a commentary system and becomes a planning tool. The\n    constellation is no longer only a picture of the past — it is a\n    surface for coordinating the future."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Extend the interpretive note schema with optional commitment fields (owner, deadline, criterion, status).]\n    next[Design the steward dashboard view that shows open commitments alongside the constellation.]\n    next[Test with one pilot: do promoted commitments actually get completed at a higher rate than unpromoted notes?]"}], :conclusion "  Interpretive notes should be able to graduate from observations into\n  commitments — named actions with owners, timelines, and completion\n  criteria — so that collective sense-making produces accountable\n  follow-through.", :directives {:audience "facilitators, programme designers, cooperative learning practitioners", :references ["vsatlas/cooperative-learning-environment" "vsatlas/interpretation-offering" "vsatelier/decision-provenance"], :sigils ["✍️/约"], :style "design-pattern", :title "Annotation as Commitment", :tone "analytic"}, :id "vsatelier/annotation-as-commitment", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["vsatlas/cooperative-learning-environment" "vsatlas/interpretation-offering" "vsatelier/decision-provenance"], :scores {}, :sigils ["✍️/约"], :source-path "library/vsatelier/annotation-as-commitment.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Annotation as Commitment", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Allow participants to flag spatial clusters or arc concentrations as\n  agenda items so that patterns visible in the constellation become\n  inputs to structured deliberation."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are bridging spatial perception and collective decision-making. This does not apply when the constellation is used purely for exhibition or archival purposes."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    Participants notice a recurring pattern — several stories linked by\n    causal arcs, a dense cluster around a shared harm, an isolated story\n    that should be connected."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Noticing is not the same as acting. Without a mechanism to capture\n    the observation and route it to a deliberation process, spatial\n    insight dissipates when the viewer looks away."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Provide a \"flag this pattern\" gesture (click, long-press, voice,\n    annotation) that captures the cluster, its constituent stories and\n    links, and a free-text framing question. The flag becomes an agenda\n    item in a facilitation queue visible to stewards."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Agendas grounded in visible spatial patterns carry their evidence\n    with them. The facilitator does not need to re-explain the\n    observation — they can point at it."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Define the minimal data a flag must carry (cluster IDs, framing question, flagger identity).]\n    next[Prototype a flag gesture in one projection (VR controller, web click, or terminal command).]\n    next[Design how flags surface in the stewardship layer — queue, dashboard, or meeting agenda.]"}], :conclusion "  Allow participants to flag spatial clusters or arc concentrations as\n  agenda items so that patterns visible in the constellation become\n  inputs to structured deliberation.", :directives {:audience "facilitators, community organisers, deliberation designers", :references ["vsatlatarium/cluster-as-story" "vsatlas/facilitation-scaffolds" "vsatlas/community-problem-solving-substrate"], :sigils ["📋/议"], :style "design-pattern", :title "Cluster as Agenda Item", :tone "analytic"}, :id "vsatelier/cluster-as-agenda", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["vsatlatarium/cluster-as-story" "vsatlas/facilitation-scaffolds" "vsatlas/community-problem-solving-substrate"], :scores {}, :sigils ["📋/议"], :source-path "library/vsatelier/cluster-as-agenda.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Cluster as Agenda Item", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Every decision that emerges from deliberation should trace back to the\n  stories, links, and flagged patterns that motivated it, creating an\n  auditable chain from lived experience to collective action."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are designing accountability into a system where community stories inform real decisions. This does not apply to purely exploratory or artistic uses of the constellation."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    A facilitated session produces a decision — fund a programme, change\n    a policy, allocate resources, create a new pilot."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    If the decision is recorded without its evidential context, it\n    becomes an assertion rather than a conclusion. Participants who\n    were not present cannot verify the reasoning. Future stewards\n    cannot learn from the process."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Link each decision record to the agenda flag that prompted it, the\n    stories and arcs that constituted the evidence, the session notes,\n    and the participants involved. Store this chain as a first-class\n    object alongside stories and links."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Provenance is how deliberation becomes trustworthy. Communities\n    that can trace \"we decided X because stories A, B, C showed\n    pattern P\" are better equipped to revisit, revise, and defend\n    their choices."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Define the decision record schema (decision text, linked flags, session ID, participants, date).]\n    next[Sketch how decision records appear in the constellation — a new node type? an annotation on existing arcs?]\n    next[Identify one real deliberation case to model retroactively as a provenance chain.]"}], :conclusion "  Every decision that emerges from deliberation should trace back to the\n  stories, links, and flagged patterns that motivated it, creating an\n  auditable chain from lived experience to collective action.", :directives {:audience "governance designers, stewards, programme evaluators", :references ["vsatlas/three-layer-architecture" "vsatlas/stewardship-layer" "vsatelier/cluster-as-agenda"], :sigils ["🔗/溯"], :style "design-pattern", :title "Decision Provenance", :tone "analytic"}, :id "vsatelier/decision-provenance", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["vsatlas/three-layer-architecture" "vsatlas/stewardship-layer" "vsatelier/cluster-as-agenda"], :scores {}, :sigils ["🔗/溯"], :source-path "library/vsatelier/decision-provenance.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Decision Provenance", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Multiple independent constellations should be able to federate — each\n  visible as a node in a meta-level view — so that separate organisations,\n  programmes, or communities can maintain sovereignty while discovering\n  cross-boundary patterns."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are designing for a network of organisations that each run their own story worlds but want selective interoperability. This does not apply to single-organisation deployments with no external connections."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    Several organisations each maintain their own VSAT/VSATLAS\n    installations — a university, a community group, an NGO, a\n    farming cooperative."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Isolated installations cannot reveal patterns that span organisational\n    boundaries. A migrant story in one installation and a policy story in\n    another may describe the same systemic failure, but neither community\n    sees the connection."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Define a federation protocol: each installation publishes a summary\n    (story count, cluster structure, link types, anthology list) without\n    exposing raw content. A meta-constellation renders each installation\n    as a single node — click to enter that cosmos. Opt-in cross-boundary\n    links allow specific stories to bridge cosmoses when both parties\n    consent."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Federation preserves sovereignty (each community owns its data and\n    governance) while enabling the emergent patterns that only appear\n    at scale. This is the digital-library equivalent of inter-library\n    loan: you do not merge the libraries; you create a protocol for\n    sharing what each is willing to share."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Define the minimal federation summary schema (what an installation publishes about itself).]\n    next[Prototype the meta-constellation view with two synthetic installations.]\n    next[Design the consent workflow for cross-boundary linking (both stewards must approve).]\n    next[Evaluate whether existing protocols (ActivityPub, Solid, IIIF) could carry the federation data.]"}], :conclusion "  Multiple independent constellations should be able to federate — each\n  visible as a node in a meta-level view — so that separate organisations,\n  programmes, or communities can maintain sovereignty while discovering\n  cross-boundary patterns.", :directives {:audience "platform architects, network organisers, digital library architects", :references ["vsatlatarium/anthology-as-filter" "vsatlas/open-progression-ecosystem" "vsatlas/open-infrastructure-expert-practice"], :sigils ["🌐/宇"], :style "design-pattern", :title "Federated Cosmoses", :tone "analytic"}, :id "vsatelier/federated-cosmoses", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["vsatlatarium/anthology-as-filter" "vsatlas/open-progression-ecosystem" "vsatlas/open-infrastructure-expert-practice"], :scores {}, :sigils ["🌐/宇"], :source-path "library/vsatelier/federated-cosmoses.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Federated Cosmoses", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Deliberation patterns should be defined independently of any specific\n  rendering technology so the same structures work across VR domes,\n  AR overlays, 2D graphs, hypertext pages, and terminal interfaces."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are designing for longevity across hardware generations and interaction paradigms. This does not apply when the system is permanently bound to a single platform."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    The patterns of connection, clustering, anthology, and deliberation\n    outlive the specific technology used to render them."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Coupling patterns to a particular renderer (A-Frame, Three.js, a\n    specific headset SDK) means they die when the technology does. The\n    Quest 2 is already dated; the Steam Frame is not yet shipping; AR\n    glasses are nascent; Nelsonian hypertext requires no 3D at all."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Define the deliberation layer (flags, decisions, commitments, return\n    loops) as data structures and interaction contracts, not as rendering\n    code. Each projection surface implements the contract:\n    — VR dome: flag by pointing, decision appears as a glowing node\n    — AR glasses: flag by gaze, decision pins to a physical wall\n    — 2D web: flag by click, decision appears in a sidebar\n    — Terminal: flag by command, decision appears in a ledger\n    The pattern library describes the contract; implementations vary."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    Projection independence is how a pattern library outlives its first\n    implementation. The VSATLATARIUM flexiargs describe one projection;\n    the VSATELIER flexiargs describe what must be true across all of them."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Extract the interaction contracts from the VSATLATARIUM patterns into projection-neutral specs.]\n    next[Prototype one deliberation pattern (cluster-as-agenda) in two different projections to verify independence.]\n    next[Evaluate futon4 (Nelsonian hypertext) as a second projection surface for the same pattern set.]"}], :conclusion "  Deliberation patterns should be defined independently of any specific\n  rendering technology so the same structures work across VR domes,\n  AR overlays, 2D graphs, hypertext pages, and terminal interfaces.", :directives {:audience "platform architects, pattern library maintainers", :references ["vsatlatarium/dome-projection" "vsatlatarium/embodied-browsing" "vsatlatarium/sky-disk-continuum"], :sigils ["🪞/映"], :style "design-pattern", :title "Projection Independence", :tone "analytic"}, :id "vsatelier/projection-independence", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["vsatlatarium/dome-projection" "vsatlatarium/embodied-browsing" "vsatlatarium/sky-disk-continuum"], :scores {}, :sigils ["🪞/映"], :source-path "library/vsatelier/projection-independence.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Projection Independence", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Outcomes of actions taken from the constellation should re-enter it as\n  new stories, closing the loop between seeing, deciding, acting, and\n  witnessing."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are designing a living system where action feeds back into shared knowledge. This does not apply to one-off analyses where no follow-up is expected."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    A community acts on a pattern surfaced by the constellation — they\n    run a programme, change a practice, allocate a resource."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    If the outcome of that action is never recorded as a story, the\n    constellation becomes a static snapshot of the past. The community\n    cannot see whether their actions changed anything."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Encourage (or structure) the creation of outcome stories: what\n    happened after the decision, what changed, what surprised. These\n    stories enter the constellation as new nodes. Links from the\n    decision record to the outcome story close the loop visually —\n    an arc from \"we decided\" to \"this is what happened.\""} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    The return loop transforms the constellation from a diagnostic\n    instrument into a learning cycle. Each pass through\n    see → deliberate → act → witness deepens the community's\n    understanding of its own situation and its capacity to change it."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Define an \"outcome story\" template that prompts for before/after, surprises, and unresolved questions.]\n    next[Design the visual representation: how does a return-loop arc differ from an interpretive link?]\n    next[Identify one completed action from an existing pilot and retroactively model the return loop.]"}], :conclusion "  Outcomes of actions taken from the constellation should re-enter it as\n  new stories, closing the loop between seeing, deciding, acting, and\n  witnessing.", :directives {:audience "programme designers, community organisers, action researchers", :references ["vsatelier/decision-provenance" "vsatelier/annotation-as-commitment" "vsatlas/capacity-building-sustainability"], :sigils ["🔄/返"], :style "design-pattern", :title "Return Loop (Outcomes as New Stories)", :tone "analytic"}, :id "vsatelier/return-loop", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["vsatelier/decision-provenance" "vsatelier/annotation-as-commitment" "vsatlas/capacity-building-sustainability"], :scores {}, :sigils ["🔄/返"], :source-path "library/vsatelier/return-loop.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Return Loop (Outcomes as New Stories)", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "  Treat each facilitated session with the constellation as a rehearsal —\n  a bounded, low-stakes space where participants practice the skills of\n  collective interpretation, deliberation, and commitment before applying\n  them to consequential decisions."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are introducing deliberation practices to groups that have not previously done collective sense-making. This does not apply to experienced deliberation teams who need no scaffolding."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "    Participants are new to interpreting linked stories and producing\n    shared decisions from spatial patterns."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "    Jumping straight to consequential decisions in an unfamiliar medium\n    risks poor outcomes and eroded trust."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "    Structure early sessions as rehearsals: use a small anthology, set\n    a low-stakes question, walk through the full cycle (explore the\n    constellation → flag a pattern → discuss → decide → record the\n    decision). Debrief the process itself, not just the content. Graduate\n    to consequential sessions once the group has practised the cycle."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "    An atelier is a place of practice before it is a place of production.\n    The deliberation skills — reading space, articulating patterns,\n    translating observations into commitments — are learned through\n    doing, not through instruction. Rehearsal lowers the cost of error\n    and builds the group's confidence in its own capacity."} {:name "NEXT-STEPS", :name-key "next-steps", :slug "next-steps", :text "    next[Design a 90-minute rehearsal session plan using a 3-story anthology.]\n    next[Define the debrief questions that distinguish process learning from content learning.]\n    next[Test whether groups that rehearse produce better-provenance decisions than groups that do not.]"}], :conclusion "  Treat each facilitated session with the constellation as a rehearsal —\n  a bounded, low-stakes space where participants practice the skills of\n  collective interpretation, deliberation, and commitment before applying\n  them to consequential decisions.", :directives {:audience "facilitators, cooperative learning practitioners, workshop designers", :references ["vsatlas/facilitation-scaffolds" "vsatlas/cooperative-learning-environment" "vsatelier/cluster-as-agenda"], :sigils ["🎭/排"], :style "design-pattern", :title "Session as Rehearsal", :tone "analytic"}, :id "vsatelier/session-as-rehearsal", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references ["vsatlas/facilitation-scaffolds" "vsatlas/cooperative-learning-environment" "vsatelier/cluster-as-agenda"], :scores {}, :sigils ["🎭/排"], :source-path "library/vsatelier/session-as-rehearsal.flexiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Session as Rehearsal", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Interpretive notes should be able to graduate from observations into commitments — named actions with owners, timelines, and completion criteria."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are designing the bridge between interpretation and action within a facilitated programme."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "A pilot's interpretive notes surface actionable patterns."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "Notes that remain observations create a culture of noticing without acting."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Allow any note to be promoted to a commitment with an owner, deadline, and success criterion."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "When annotation becomes commitment, the constellation becomes a planning surface for the future."} {:name "STATUS", :name-key "status", :slug "status", :text "status[sketch]"}], :conclusion "Interpretive notes should be able to graduate from observations into commitments — named actions with owners, timelines, and completion criteria.", :directives {:sigils ["✍️/约"], :title "Annotation as Commitment"}, :id "vsatelier/annotation-as-commitment", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["✍️/约"], :source-path "library/vsatelier/vsatelier.multiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Annotation as Commitment", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Allow participants to flag spatial clusters or arc concentrations as agenda items so that patterns visible in the constellation become inputs to structured deliberation."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are bridging spatial perception and collective decision-making."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "Participants notice a recurring pattern — dense causal links, an isolated story, a shared harm."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "Without a mechanism to capture the observation and route it to deliberation, spatial insight dissipates."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Provide a \"flag this pattern\" gesture that captures the cluster and a framing question. The flag becomes an agenda item in a facilitation queue."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "Agendas grounded in visible spatial patterns carry their evidence with them."} {:name "STATUS", :name-key "status", :slug "status", :text "status[sketch]"}], :conclusion "Allow participants to flag spatial clusters or arc concentrations as agenda items so that patterns visible in the constellation become inputs to structured deliberation.", :directives {:sigils ["📋/议"], :title "Cluster as Agenda Item"}, :id "vsatelier/cluster-as-agenda", :keywords [], :projection-version "flexiarg-v0", :references [], :scores {}, :sigils ["📋/议"], :source-path "library/vsatelier/vsatelier.multiarg", :sources [], :status :ok, :title "Cluster as Agenda Item", :typed-slots nil} #:pattern{:clauses [{:name "conclusion", :name-key "conclusion", :slug "conclusion", :text "Every decision should trace back to the stories, links, and flagged patterns that motivated it, creating an auditable chain from lived experience to collective action."} {:name "context", :name-key "context", :slug "context", :text "You are designing accountability into a system where community stories inform real decisions."} {:name "IF", :name-key "if", :slug "if", :text "A facilitated session produces a decision — fund a programme, change a policy, create a new pilot."} {:name "HOWEVER", :name-key "however", :slug "however", :text "Decisions recorded without evidential context become assertions rather than conclusions."} {:name "THEN", :name-key "then", :slug "then", :text "Link each decision record to its agenda flag, source stories and arcs, session notes, and participants."} {:name "BECAUSE", :name-key "because", :slug "because", :text "Provenance is how deliberation becomes trustworthy."} {:name "STATUS", :name-key "status", :slug "status", :text "status[sketch]"}], :conclusion "Every decision should trace back to the stories, links, and flagged patterns that motivated it, creating an auditable chain from lived experience to collective action.", :directives {:sigils ["🔗/溯"], :title "Decision Provenance"}, :i